const CONTENT={"synced":"2026-11-03 02:49","courses":{"BUS1103":{"file":"BUS1103_Financial_Accounting_Synthese_et_Exercices.docx","mtime":"2026-11-03","lessons":{"1":{"title":"Financial Statements and Business Decisions","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.1 Why Study Financial Accounting?"},{"t":"li","x":"Provides fundamental financial information for business decision-making."},{"t":"li","x":"Ensures compliance with legal requirements, regulations, and business ethics."},{"t":"li","x":"Enhances career opportunities and professional development."},{"t":"li","x":"Improves personal financial investment and management skills."},{"t":"li","x":"Essential for students who want to launch their own start-ups."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.2 Understanding the Business — Who Uses Financial Statements?"},{"t":"p","x":"Businesses raise resources from two main groups, each expecting a different kind of return:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Source of Financing","Who","Expected Return"],["Equity financing","Stockholders (owners)","Dividends and/or a higher future stock price"],["Debt financing","Creditors (lenders)","Interest"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Both groups estimate the company's future performance partly from information in its financial statements."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Financial vs. Managerial Accounting"},{"t":"table","rows":[["","Financial Accounting Reports","Managerial Accounting Reports"],["Content","Periodic financial statements and related disclosures","Detailed plans and continuous performance reports"],["Audience","External decision makers (creditors, investors) — evaluate the company","Internal decision makers (managers) — run the company"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.3 The Four Basic Financial Statements"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Statement","What it reports"],["Balance Sheet (Statement of Financial Position)","The financial position — assets, liabilities, and stockholders' equity — of an entity at ONE point in time."],["Income Statement (Statement of Earnings/Operations)","Revenues less expenses (performance) DURING an accounting period."],["Statement of Stockholders' Equity","Changes in each stockholders' equity account during the period, including how net income and dividends change Retained Earnings."],["Statement of Cash Flows","Cash inflows and outflows during the period, split into Operating, Investing, and Financing activities."]]},{"t":"p","x":"\"The notes are an integral part of these financial statements\" — this line appears at the bottom of every statement: notes/footnotes give supplemental information needed to properly understand the numbers."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Statement heading — always include:"},{"t":"li","x":"Name of the entity (company name)"},{"t":"li","x":"Title of the statement (e.g., Balance Sheet)"},{"t":"li","x":"The specific date OR period covered (e.g., \"At December 31, 2026\" vs. \"For the Year Ended December 31, 2026\")"},{"t":"li","x":"Unit of measure (e.g., in millions of dollars)"},{"t":"p","x":"Statements can be prepared for any period: annual, quarterly, or monthly."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Balance Sheet in detail"},{"t":"p","x":"**Accounting equation: **Assets = Liabilities + Stockholders' Equity (A = L + SE) — must always balance."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Assets","Liabilities","Stockholders' Equity"],["Cash; Short-term investments; Accounts receivable; Notes receivable; Inventories; Supplies; Prepaid expenses; Long-term investments; Equipment; Buildings; Land; Intangibles","Accounts payable; Accrued expenses; Notes payable; Taxes payable; Unearned revenue; Bonds payable","Common stock; Retained earnings"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Income Statement in detail"},{"t":"p","x":"**Equation: **Revenues − Expenses = Net Income (also called \"profit\", \"net earnings\", or \"the bottom line\"). If expenses exceed revenues, the result is a net loss."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Revenues (examples)","Expenses (examples)"],["Sales Revenue; Service Revenue; Rental Revenue; Interest Revenue","Cost of Goods Sold; Wages Expense; Rent Expense; Depreciation Expense; Insurance Expense; Repair Expense; Income Tax Expense"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Statement of Stockholders' Equity in detail"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Common Stock roll-forward","Retained Earnings roll-forward"],["Beginning Common Stock + Stock Issuance = Ending Common Stock","Beginning Retained Earnings + Net Income − Dividends = Ending Retained Earnings"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Statement of Cash Flows in detail"},{"t":"p","x":"**Formula: **± CFO ± CFI ± CFF = Change in Cash; + Beginning Cash Balance = Ending Cash Balance."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Category","Definition"],["Operating Activities (CFO)","Cash flows directly tied to earning income — e.g. cash collected from customers less cash paid for operating expenses (suppliers, employees)."],["Investing Activities (CFI)","Cash flows from buying/selling the company's plant, equipment, and investments."],["Financing Activities (CFF)","Cash flows tied to financing the enterprise — receiving/repaying money to investors and creditors (except suppliers)."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Formatting Conventions"},{"t":"li","x":"Put the $ sign beside the first amount in a group of items and beside each group total."},{"t":"li","x":"Assets are listed by ease of conversion to cash (most liquid first)."},{"t":"li","x":"Liabilities are listed by maturity/due date (soonest first)."},{"t":"li","x":"A single underline goes below the last item before a subtotal; a double underline goes below a final group total."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.4 GAAP, IFRS, and the Standard-Setting Process"},{"t":"li","x":"GAAP (Generally Accepted Accounting Principles) = the rules determining the content and measurement of financial statements."},{"t":"li","x":"The U.S. SEC has legal authority over financial reporting for public companies, but has delegated the detailed rule-making to the FASB (Financial Accounting Standards Board)."},{"t":"li","x":"FASB's official pronouncements = the FASB Accounting Standards Codification."},{"t":"li","x":"The FASB uses a conceptual framework to guide the development of specific standards."},{"t":"li","x":"Internationally, IFRS (International Financial Reporting Standards), issued by the IASB, has been adopted by 147+ jurisdictions since 2002 — including the EU, UK, Australia, New Zealand, Hong Kong, Malaysia, Korea, Israel, Turkey, Brazil, Chile, Canada, and Mexico. The US SEC now also allows foreign private issuers listed in the US to report under IFRS."},{"t":"p","x":"Since Hong Kong follows IFRS-equivalent standards (HKFRS), this is directly relevant to companies you will see referenced in class (e.g., Fairwood, Café de Coral, AEON, listed HK property developers)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.5 Principles and Assumptions of Accounting (core definitions)"},{"t":"p","x":"These 9 terms recur throughout the whole course — know them cold. (Consolidated from the Chapter 1 slides and the \"Accounting Assumptions and Principles\" Q&A hand-out.)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Economic entity assumption (a.k.a. Business/Separate entity assumption)","The enterprise is accounted for separately from its owners and other entities."],["Monetary unit assumption","A common denominator — money (e.g., the dollar) — is used to express all transactions, with no adjustment for changes in purchasing power."],["Going-concern assumption (continuity assumption)","The entity will continue operating indefinitely / long enough to meet its commitments and plans."],["Periodicity assumption (time period assumption)","The life of an enterprise can be divided into artificial time periods (months, quarters, years) for reporting."],["Historical cost principle (measurement/cost principle)","Balance sheet elements are initially recorded at their original transaction (cash-equivalent) cost."],["Revenue recognition principle","Revenue is recognized only when it is earned — criteria are usually satisfied at the point of sale/delivery."],["Expense recognition principle (matching principle)","Expenses are recorded in the same period as the revenue they helped generate."],["Full-disclosure principle (fair/faithful disclosure principle)","All information that could affect users' decisions must be reported, even details behind the numbers."],["Materiality","Information is material if omitting or misstating it could influence a user's economic decisions; immaterial items don't need strict adherence to normal principles."],["Prudence / Conservatism","Don't overstate revenues or assets, and don't understate expenses or liabilities — when in doubt, report conservatively."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.6 Accounting Ethics"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"What is ethics / accounting ethics?"},{"t":"li","x":"Ethics: a branch of philosophy addressing morality — good/bad, right/wrong, justice, virtue — and the choices we make and resulting behaviour, using accepted standards."},{"t":"li","x":"Accounting ethics: a branch of professional ethics — the study of moral values and judgments as they apply specifically to accountants."},{"t":"li","x":"Why it matters: to understand your own choices, to handle ethical challenges skilfully, to act morally (for yourself and society), and because ethics is increasingly central to professional accounting services."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Steps in Ethical Decision-Making"},{"t":"li","x":"1. Identify the ethical issue(s) in the case."},{"t":"li","x":"2. Identify the stakeholders."},{"t":"li","x":"3. Consider the likely effects of the decision on those stakeholders."},{"t":"li","x":"4. Identify the relevant (accounting) ethical principles."},{"t":"li","x":"5. Identify the alternative courses of action."},{"t":"li","x":"6. Choose the best course of action."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"HKICPA's Five Ethical Principles"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Principle","Meaning","Example"],["Integrity (誠信)","Be straightforward and honest in all professional/business relationships.","Do not associate with a materially false or misleading statement."],["Objectivity (客觀，公正)","Don't let bias, conflicts of interest, or undue influence override professional judgment.","Avoid any relationship that would impair objectivity."],["Professional Competence & Due Care (專業勝任能力 / 應有的關注)","Maintain the knowledge/skill needed for competent service and act diligently and thoroughly.","Keep up to date with auditing standards; don't forget to address all major parts of an audit."],["Confidentiality (保密)","Respect confidentiality of information gained through professional relationships; don't disclose or exploit it without proper authority.","Don't tip off a friend about investment opportunities learned while auditing a company."],["Professional Behaviour (良好的職業行為)","Comply with relevant laws/regulations and avoid discrediting the profession.","Don't encourage tax evasion; don't exaggerate the services you can provide."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ensuring Accuracy — Three Controls Beyond Personal Ethics"},{"t":"li","x":"1. Maintain a system of internal controls over records and assets."},{"t":"li","x":"2. Hire external independent auditors to audit the fairness of the financial statements."},{"t":"li","x":"3. Form a board committee (audit committee) to oversee the integrity of these safeguards."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Worked Ethics Case Study — Nebula Ltd."},{"t":"p","x":"You are the CPA/financial accountant of Nebula Ltd., a Hong Kong public company. The head of accounts wants to extend the depreciable life of the company's main plant assets from 5 to 8 years — even though nothing about the assets themselves has changed — because the company hasn't reached its profit targets and wants to reduce expenses (boosting reported earnings) to avoid a drop in share price."},{"t":"p","x":"**Discussion & suggested analysis**"},{"t":"li","x":"Ethical issue: deliberately changing an accounting estimate (useful life) with no economic justification, purely to manage/inflate reported earnings — this breaches the faithful-representation and historical-cost/consistency spirit of GAAP and misleads users."},{"t":"li","x":"Stakeholders: shareholders/investors (misled about true performance), creditors (misjudge credit risk), employees (job security tied to a false picture), regulators/auditors (reputational and legal exposure), the accountant personally (professional license at risk)."},{"t":"li","x":"HKICPA principles at stake: Integrity (don't be party to a misleading statement), Objectivity (resist pressure from the head of accounts), Professional Competence & Due Care (the depreciation estimate should reflect genuine economic useful life), Professional Behaviour (don't discredit the profession)."},{"t":"li","x":"What the accountant should do: refuse to make the unjustified change, document the reasoning, raise the issue with the audit committee/those charged with governance if pressure continues, and, if necessary, escalate to the external auditor or decline to certify the accounts. Changing an estimate must be driven by a genuine change in facts (e.g. new information about how the asset is actually used), never by a desired profit outcome."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.9 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 1"},{"t":"li","x":"I can name the four basic financial statements and state, from memory, exactly what each one reports and over what period."},{"t":"li","x":"I can state the accounting equation and explain why it must always balance."},{"t":"li","x":"I can list what a financial statement heading must contain (entity, title, date/period, unit of measure)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain how the Income Statement, Statement of Stockholders' Equity, Balance Sheet, and Statement of Cash Flows connect to one another (net income flows into retained earnings; ending retained earnings appears on the balance sheet; ending cash on the cash flow statement equals cash on the balance sheet)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can define and give an example of each of the 9 core assumptions/principles (entity, going concern, monetary unit, periodicity, historical cost, revenue recognition, expense recognition/matching, full disclosure, materiality) plus conservatism."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain the roles of the SEC, FASB, and IASB, and name GAAP vs. IFRS."},{"t":"li","x":"I can list HKICPA's 5 ethical principles with an example of each, and walk through the 6-step ethical decision-making process on a case."},{"t":"li","x":"I can list the 3 controls (beyond personal ethics) that help ensure accurate financial reporting."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.7 Official Practice — Class Practice Questions & Solutions"},{"t":"p","x":"These are the exact exercises assigned for Chapter 1 in the Teaching Schedule (E1-11, E1-12, E1-13) plus the \"Accounting Assumptions and Principles\" matching hand-out."},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ Extraction gap: the original slide text for the E1-11/E1-12/E1-13 question prompts (\"Chapter 1 – Class Practice Questions.txt\") came through as blank/image-only pages — only the official suggested-solutions file was text-extractable. The completed financial statements below ARE the verbatim official answer key; the short task descriptions are reconstructed from what the solutions imply (each exercise gives you unsorted item amounts and asks you to organize them into the correct statement). Please cross-check the exact original wording against the Moodle PDF/textbook if you want the literal instructions."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E1-11 — Prepare an Income Statement and Balance Sheet (reconstructed task: arrange the given items into properly classified statements)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["PAINTER CORPORATION — Income Statement, For the Month of January, Current Year",""],["Total revenues","$305,000"],["Less: Total expenses (excluding income tax)","189,000"],["Pretax income","116,000"],["Less: Income tax expense","25,000"],["Net income","$91,000"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["PAINTER CORPORATION — Balance Sheet, At January 31, Current Year",""],["Cash","$65,150"],["Receivables from customers","44,700"],["Merchandise inventory","94,500"],["Total assets","$204,350"],["Payables to suppliers","$25,950"],["Income taxes payable","25,000"],["Total liabilities","50,950"],["Common stock","62,400"],["Retained earnings (from income statement)","91,000"],["Total stockholders' equity","153,400"],["Total liabilities and stockholders' equity","$204,350"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E1-12 — Prepare an Income Statement and Balance Sheet"},{"t":"table","rows":[["ANALYTICS CORPORATION — Income Statement, For the Year Ended December 31, Current Year",""],["Total revenues","$299,000"],["Less: Total expenses (excluding income tax)","184,000"],["Pretax income","115,000"],["Less: Income tax expense","34,500"],["Net income","$80,500"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["ANALYTICS CORPORATION — Balance Sheet, At December 31, Current Year",""],["Cash","$70,150"],["Receivables from customers","34,500"],["Merchandise inventory","96,600"],["Total assets","$201,250"],["Payables to suppliers","$26,450"],["Income taxes payable","34,500"],["Total liabilities","60,950"],["Common stock","59,800"],["Retained earnings (from income statement)","80,500"],["Total stockholders' equity","140,300"],["Total liabilities and stockholders' equity","$201,250"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E1-13 — Prepare a Statement of Stockholders' Equity"},{"t":"table","rows":[["PLUMMER STONEWORK CORPORATION — Statement of Stockholders' Equity, For the Year Ended December 31, 2023","Common Stock","Retained Earnings"],["Balance, December 31, 2022*","$100,000","$16,800"],["Net income","","42,000"],["Dividends","","(18,700)"],["Balance, December 31, 2023","$100,000","$40,100"]]},{"t":"p","x":"*For 2022: Beginning RE $0 + Net income $31,000 − Dividends $14,200 = Ending RE $16,800, which becomes the beginning balance for 2023."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Accounting Assumptions and Principles — Matching Q&A (E1-9 style)"},{"t":"p","x":"Match each accounting term (List A) with its correct description (List B):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["#","List A — Term","List B — Correct Description"],["1","Expense recognition","Record expenses in the period the related revenue is recognized."],["2","Periodicity assumption","The life of an enterprise can be divided into artificial time periods."],["3","Historical cost principle","The original transaction value upon acquisition."],["4","Materiality","Concerns the relative size of an item and its effect on decisions."],["5","Revenue recognition","Criteria usually satisfied for products at point of sale."],["6","Going concern assumption","The entity will continue indefinitely."],["7","Monetary unit assumption","A common denominator is the dollar."],["8","Economic entity assumption","The enterprise is separate from its owners and other entities."],["9","Full-disclosure principle","All information that could affect decisions should be reported."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.8 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 1 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C1.1 (MCQ) — Financial statements"},{"t":"p","x":"Which financial statement would you check to find out how much cash a company generated from its day-to-day operations during the year?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Balance Sheet"},{"t":"li","x":"B. Income Statement"},{"t":"li","x":"C. Statement of Cash Flows"},{"t":"li","x":"D. Statement of Stockholders' Equity"},{"t":"a","x":"C. The Statement of Cash Flows reports cash inflows/outflows split into Operating, Investing and Financing activities; \"cash generated from day-to-day operations\" is precisely Cash Flow from Operating Activities (CFO)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C1.2 (MCQ) — Accounting assumptions"},{"t":"p","x":"A sole proprietor records his personal car purchase separately from his shop's accounting records, even though legally the business and the owner are the same person. This best illustrates:"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Going-concern assumption"},{"t":"li","x":"B. Monetary unit assumption"},{"t":"li","x":"C. Economic entity (separate entity) assumption"},{"t":"li","x":"D. Full-disclosure principle"},{"t":"a","x":"C. The economic/separate entity assumption requires that a business's activities be accounted for separately from the personal activities of its owner(s), regardless of the legal form of the business."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C1.3 (Short problem) — Building the accounting equation"},{"t":"p","x":"At the start of the year, Nova Traders Ltd. had total assets of $420,000 and total liabilities of $150,000. During the year, net income was $60,000, the company issued additional common stock for $25,000 cash, and paid dividends of $18,000. No other stockholders' equity transactions occurred."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: (a) Compute beginning stockholders' equity. (b) Compute ending stockholders' equity. (c) If ending total liabilities are $165,000, compute ending total assets."},{"t":"a","x":"• (a) Beginning SE = Assets − Liabilities = $420,000 − $150,000 = $270,000.\n• (b) Ending SE = Beginning SE + Net income + Stock issued − Dividends = $270,000 + $60,000 + $25,000 − $18,000 = $337,000.\n• (c) Ending Assets = Ending Liabilities + Ending SE = $165,000 + $337,000 = $502,000."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C1.4 (Short problem) — Classify accounting concepts"},{"t":"p","x":"For each situation below, name the single accounting assumption/principle it best illustrates:"},{"t":"li","x":"(i) A company reports its assets and results in HK dollars, without adjusting for inflation over the years."},{"t":"li","x":"(ii) A retailer records a laptop bought for $8,000 at $8,000 on the balance sheet, even though a similar laptop now sells for $9,500."},{"t":"li","x":"(iii) A company does not bother separately disclosing a $40 stationery expense in a multi-million-dollar set of financial statements."},{"t":"li","x":"(iv) Even though a company operates continuously, it produces a full set of financial statements every 3 months."},{"t":"a","x":"• (i) Monetary unit assumption.\n• (ii) Historical cost (measurement) principle.\n• (iii) Materiality.\n• (iv) Periodicity (time period) assumption."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C1.5 (Statement-preparation / journal-entry style problem) — Put it all together"},{"t":"p","x":"Harbor Consulting Corp. had the following account balances at the end of its first year of operations, listed in random order (all amounts in $): Cash 38,000; Accounts receivable 12,500; Service revenue 96,000; Salaries expense 41,000; Rent expense 18,000; Common stock 20,000; Accounts payable 9,500; Income tax expense 7,400; Retained earnings (beginning) 0; Dividends declared 5,000; Equipment 8,000; Income taxes payable 7,400; Insurance expense 3,000."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: (a) Prepare a classified Income Statement for the year. (b) Compute ending Retained Earnings. (c) Prepare a Balance Sheet at year-end and confirm it balances."},{"t":"table","rows":[["HARBOR CONSULTING CORP. — Income Statement",""],["Service revenue","$96,000"],["Less: Salaries expense","41,000"],["Less: Rent expense","18,000"],["Less: Insurance expense","3,000"],["Pretax income","34,000"],["Less: Income tax expense","7,400"],["Net income","$26,600"]]},{"t":"a","x":"**(b) Retained earnings: **$0 (beginning) + $26,600 (net income) − $5,000 (dividends) = $21,600 (ending)."},{"t":"table","rows":[["HARBOR CONSULTING CORP. — Balance Sheet (at year-end)",""],["Cash","$38,000"],["Accounts receivable","12,500"],["Equipment","8,000"],["Total assets","$58,500"],["Accounts payable","$9,500"],["Income taxes payable","7,400"],["Total liabilities","16,900"],["Common stock","20,000"],["Retained earnings","21,600"],["Total stockholders' equity","41,600"],["Total liabilities and stockholders' equity","$58,500"]]},{"t":"a","x":"Check: Total assets $58,500 = Total liabilities $16,900 + Total stockholders' equity $41,600. Balances ✓ — always foot both sides of the balance sheet as a final check before moving on."}]},"2":{"title":"Investing and Financing Decisions and the Accounting System","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.1 Recognition and Measurement Concepts"},{"t":"p","x":"Three assumptions and one measurement concept underlie the balance sheet:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Concept","Meaning"],["Separate entity assumption (business entity assumption)","Each business's activities must be accounted for separately from the personal activities of its owners, other persons, and other entities."],["Going concern assumption (continuity assumption)","The business is assumed to continue operating into the foreseeable future, long enough to meet its contractual commitments and plans."],["Monetary unit assumption","Each business reports its results primarily in the national monetary unit (e.g. HKD, USD), without adjusting for changes in purchasing power."],["Historical cost (measurement concept)","Balance sheet elements are initially recorded at cost — the cash-equivalent value on the date of the transaction."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.2 Elements of the Balance Sheet"},{"t":"p","x":"**A = L + SE**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Assets","Liabilities","Stockholders' Equity"],["Economic resources owned/controlled by the company, with measurable value, expected to produce future cash inflows or reduce future cash outflows.","Debts/obligations (claims to company resources) from past transactions; settled by transferring assets or providing services.","The financing provided by the owners and by the operations (earnings) of the business."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.3 What Is a Business Transaction?"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Definition","Example"],["External events","Exchanges of assets/goods/services for assets/services/promises to pay, between the business and another party.","Purchasing a machine from a supplier."],["Internal events","Events not involving an exchange with another party but with a direct, measurable effect on the entity.","Using up insurance that was previously paid in advance."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.4 Typical Account Titles (memorize these families)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Assets","Liabilities","Stockholders' Equity","Revenues","Expenses"],["Cash; Short-Term Investments; Accounts Receivable; Notes Receivable; Inventory; Supplies; Prepaid Expenses; Long-Term Investments; Equipment; Buildings; Land; Intangibles","Accounts Payable; Accrued Expenses Payable; Notes Payable; Taxes Payable; Unearned Revenue; Bonds Payable","Common Stock; Additional Paid-in Capital; Retained Earnings","Sales Revenue; Fee Revenue; Interest Revenue; Rent Revenue; Service Revenue","Cost of Goods Sold; Wages Expense; Rent Expense; Interest Expense; Depreciation Expense; Advertising Expense; Insurance Expense; Repair Expense; Income Tax Expense"]]},{"t":"li","x":"\"Receivable\" in the title → always an ASSET (owed TO the company by others)."},{"t":"li","x":"\"Payable\" in the title → always a LIABILITY (owed BY the company to others)."},{"t":"li","x":"\"Unearned\" in the title → always a LIABILITY (cash received in advance for goods/services not yet delivered)."},{"t":"li","x":"\"Prepaid ...\" → always an ASSET (cash paid in advance for future benefit)."},{"t":"li","x":"Expense accounts are named after what was incurred/used + \"Expense\", EXCEPT inventory sold, which is titled Cost of Goods Sold."},{"t":"li","x":"Revenue accounts are named after their source + \"Revenue\"."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.5 Transaction Analysis — the Dual-Effects Concept"},{"t":"li","x":"Every transaction has at least two effects on the accounting equation (\"dual effects\")."},{"t":"li","x":"Every transaction affects at least two accounts — correctly identifying which accounts, and whether each increases or decreases, is critical."},{"t":"li","x":"The accounting equation (A = L + SE) must remain in balance after every single transaction."},{"t":"p","x":"External-party exchanges involve the business (1) receiving something and (2) giving something in return. Example: Chipotle buys supplies and pays cash → receives supplies (+A), gives up cash (−A). If bought on credit instead, there are two separate transactions at two points in time (purchase on account, then later payment)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"3-Step Transaction Analysis Method"},{"t":"li","x":"Step 1 — What was received? (account name, type, amount, direction of effect)"},{"t":"li","x":"Step 2 — What was given?"},{"t":"li","x":"Step 3 — Verify the accounting equation still balances: A = L + SE."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.6 Worked Example — Chipotle's Quarterly Investing & Financing Transactions"},{"t":"p","x":"All amounts in millions of dollars unless noted."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(a)","Cash (+A)","17",""],["","Common Stock (+SE)","","1"],["","Additional Paid-in Capital (+SE)","","16"],["","Issued 100 shares of common stock, $0.01 par, at $0.17 market price → $17 cash from investors. [Financing]","Issued 100 shares of common stock, $0.01 par, at $0.17 market price → $17 cash from investors. [Financing]","Issued 100 shares of common stock, $0.01 par, at $0.17 market price → $17 cash from investors. [Financing]"],["(b)","Cash (+A)","4",""],["","Notes Payable, noncurrent (+L)","","4"],["","Borrowed $4 from the bank, 3-year note (noncurrent liability). [Financing]","Borrowed $4 from the bank, 3-year note (noncurrent liability). [Financing]","Borrowed $4 from the bank, 3-year note (noncurrent liability). [Financing]"],["(c)","Equipment (+A)","26",""],["","Intangible Assets (+A)","5",""],["","Cash (−A)","","31"],["","Purchased $26 equipment and $5 intangible assets, for cash. [Investing]","Purchased $26 equipment and $5 intangible assets, for cash. [Investing]","Purchased $26 equipment and $5 intangible assets, for cash. [Investing]"],["(d)","Land (+A)","20",""],["","Buildings (+A)","40",""],["","Operating Lease Assets (+A)","86",""],["","Cash (−A)","","29"],["","Current Operating Lease Liabilities (+L)","","6"],["","Long-Term Operating Lease Liabilities (+L)","","111"],["","Acquired $20 land, $40 buildings, $86 right-of-use lease assets; paid $29 cash, signed $6 current + $111 long-term operating lease liabilities. [Investing]","Acquired $20 land, $40 buildings, $86 right-of-use lease assets; paid $29 cash, signed $6 current + $111 long-term operating lease liabilities. [Investing]","Acquired $20 land, $40 buildings, $86 right-of-use lease assets; paid $29 cash, signed $6 current + $111 long-term operating lease liabilities. [Investing]"],["(e)","Cash (+A)","19",""],["","Short-Term Investments (−A)","","19"],["","Sold $19 of short-term investments for $19 cash. [Investing]","Sold $19 of short-term investments for $19 cash. [Investing]","Sold $19 of short-term investments for $19 cash. [Investing]"],["(f)","Notes Payable (−L)","1",""],["","Cash (−A)","","1"],["","Paid $1 on the note payable from (b) (ignore interest). [Financing]","Paid $1 on the note payable from (b) (ignore interest). [Financing]","Paid $1 on the note payable from (b) (ignore interest). [Financing]"],["(g)","Treasury Stock (−SE)","103",""],["","Cash (−A)","","103"],["","Repurchased common stock (treasury stock) from investors for $103 cash. [Financing]","Repurchased common stock (treasury stock) from investors for $103 cash. [Financing]","Repurchased common stock (treasury stock) from investors for $103 cash. [Financing]"],["(h)","Retained Earnings (−SE)","2",""],["","Dividends Payable (+L)","","2"],["","Board declared $2 cash dividend, payable next quarter. [Financing]","Board declared $2 cash dividend, payable next quarter. [Financing]","Board declared $2 cash dividend, payable next quarter. [Financing]"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Tip: use +A/−A, +L/−L, +SE/−SE labels next to each account while learning — it forces you to check the accounting equation stays balanced after every line."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.7 Debits and Credits"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account Type","Increase (+)","Decrease (−)"],["Assets","Debit","Credit"],["Dividends Declared","Debit","Credit"],["Liabilities","Credit","Debit"],["Stockholders' Equity (Common Stock, APIC, Retained Earnings)","Credit","Debit"],["Revenues","Credit","Debit"],["Expenses","Debit","Credit"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Journal entry convention: **debited accounts listed first (amount on the left); credited accounts listed below, usually indented (amount on the right). Debits must always equal credits for each entry."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.8 T-Accounts and the Accounting Cycle"},{"t":"li","x":"General Journal — a chronological list of every transaction's effects."},{"t":"li","x":"General Ledger / T-accounts — a record of the effects on, and balances of, each individual account."},{"t":"li","x":"The Accounting Cycle (recurring each period): analyze transactions → record in the journal → post to the ledger (T-accounts) → prepare a trial balance → make adjusting entries → prepare financial statements → close the books."},{"t":"p","x":"T-account mechanics: start with the beginning balance on the correct side, post each transaction's debit/credit, draw a total line, and put the ending balance on the side matching its normal balance."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Cash (Asset)","Cash (Asset)"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["Beg. 561",""],["(a) 17",""]]},{"t":"p","x":"Ending balance = 561 + 17 = 578 (on the debit side, since Cash is an asset)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.9 Trial Balance"},{"t":"li","x":"Lists every T-account balance in financial-statement order: assets, liabilities, stockholders' equity, revenues, expenses."},{"t":"li","x":"Purpose: check that total debits = total credits."},{"t":"li","x":"Limitation: a trial balance can still balance even if the WRONG account or WRONG amount was used, or if a transaction was omitted entirely — it only catches debit/credit imbalances, not every type of error."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.10 Classified Balance Sheet"},{"t":"li","x":"Prepared from the trial balance."},{"t":"li","x":"A good heading: company name, statement title, date, and the dollar unit (thousands/millions) if rounded."},{"t":"li","x":"Assets and liabilities are grouped into Current and Noncurrent (long-term)."},{"t":"li","x":"Often shows comparative data for two periods (e.g., current year-end and prior year-end)."},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ IFRS note: IFRS uses the same underlying recognition/measurement logic as GAAP, but financial statement FORMATTING can differ (e.g., balance sheet order, terminology) — always check the statement's own headings rather than assuming a US layout."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.13 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 2"},{"t":"li","x":"I can state the 3 assumptions + 1 measurement concept underlying the balance sheet."},{"t":"li","x":"I can distinguish external events from internal events."},{"t":"li","x":"I can classify any given account as Asset/Liability/SE and state its normal balance (debit or credit)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can apply the 3-step transaction analysis method (received / given / verify) to any transaction."},{"t":"li","x":"I can write a correctly formatted journal entry (debit first, credit indented, debits = credits)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can post journal entries to T-accounts and compute ending balances."},{"t":"li","x":"I can prepare a trial balance and explain what it does and does NOT prove."},{"t":"li","x":"I can prepare a simple classified balance sheet (current vs. noncurrent) with a correct heading."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Further points from the slides"},{"t":"p","x":"Using big data for business expansion: big data analytics examines large amounts of data to uncover hidden patterns, correlations and other insights. Data can be structured (from inside the company: accounting, sales, HR) or unstructured (from outside: social media, customers). Companies make more data-driven decisions that can reduce costs and transform the business, and data analytics has exploded in recent years, creating opportunities and competition for people with strong analytical skills."},{"t":"p","x":"Understanding foreign financial statements: IFRS uses the same system of analyzing, recording and summarizing the results of business activities as GAAP, but the statements are formatted differently (compare a U.S. Form 10-K with a Hong Kong annual report such as Lenovo's)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.11 Official Practice — Worksheet P2-1, P2-2, P2-3, P2-5 (with Suggested Solutions)"},{"t":"p","x":"These are the exact class practice questions for Chapter 2 (per the Teaching Schedule), taken from the blank worksheet distributed in class, followed by the official suggested solutions."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"P2-1 — Classify Balance Sheet Items"},{"t":"p","x":"Classify each item as a Current Asset (CA), Noncurrent Asset (NCA), Current Liability (CL), Noncurrent Liability (NCL), or Stockholders' Equity (SE), and state whether its normal balance is a debit or credit:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Classification","Debit or Credit"],["(1) Notes and Loans Payable (short-term)","CL","Credit"],["(2) Materials and Supplies","CA","Debit"],["(3) Common Stock","SE","Credit"],["(4) Intangible Assets","NCA","Debit"],["(5) Income Taxes Payable","CL","Credit"],["(6) Long-Term Debt","NCL","Credit"],["(7) Property, Plant, and Equipment","NCA","Debit"],["(8) Retained Earnings","SE","Credit"],["(9) Notes and Accounts Receivable (short-term)","CA","Debit"],["(10) Investments (long-term)","NCA","Debit"],["(11) Cash and Cash Equivalents","CA","Debit"],["(12) Accounts Payable and Accrued Liabilities","CL","Credit"],["(13) Crude Oil, Products, and Merchandise","CA","Debit"],["(14) Additional Paid-in Capital","SE","Credit"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"P2-2 — East Hill Home Healthcare Services: transaction analysis"},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 1: Was East Hill organized as a corporation, partnership, or sole proprietorship?"},{"t":"a","x":"A corporation — only a corporation issues shares of common stock to owners in exchange for investment, as happens in transaction (a)."},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 2 — Transaction analysis table (beginning balances then 7 transactions a–g; dollars):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["","Cash","ST Invest.","Notes Rec.","Land","Buildings","Equip.","ST Notes Pay.","LT Notes Pay.","Common Stock","APIC","Ret. Earnings"],["Beg.","50,000","500,000","100,000","50,000","—","—","100,000","100,000","20,000","80,000","400,000"],["(a)","+90,000","","","","","","","","+9,000","+81,000",""],["(b)","−9,000","","","+14,000","+60,000","+15,000","","+80,000","","",""],["(c)","+3,500","","−3,500","","","","","","","",""],["(d)","−18,000","+18,000","","","","","","","","",""],["(e)","No effect*","","","","","","","","","",""],["(f)","−5,000","","+5,000","","","","","","","",""],["(g)","+8,000","","","","","","+8,000","","","",""],["End.","$119,500","$18,000","$5,000","$510,500*","$160,000*","","$108,000","$180,000","$29,000","$161,000","$400,000"]]},{"t":"p","x":"*Event (e) is a transaction between the two owners personally — it has no effect on the business (separate entity assumption). Ending Land/Buildings combine beginning balances with (b); exact per-column figures follow directly from summing beginning + each transaction column above."},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 3: The transaction between the two stockholders (Event e) is NOT recorded in the business's books — it occurs between the owners personally, so there is no effect on the business itself (separate/economic entity assumption)."},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 4 — Summary answers:"},{"t":"li","x":"(a) Total assets = $119,500 + $18,000 + $5,000 + $510,500 + $160,000 + $65,000 = $878,000"},{"t":"li","x":"(b) Total liabilities = $108,000 + $180,000 = $288,000"},{"t":"li","x":"(c) Total stockholders' equity = Total assets − Total liabilities = $878,000 − $288,000 = $590,000"},{"t":"li","x":"(d) Cash balance = $50,000 + $90,000 − $9,000 + $3,500 − $18,000 − $5,000 + $8,000 = $119,500"},{"t":"li","x":"(e) Total current assets = Cash $119,500 + Short-term investments $18,000 + Notes receivable $5,000 = $142,500"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"P2-3 — Jaguar Plastics Company: T-accounts, Trial Balance, Balance Sheet"},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 3: No effect was recorded for transaction (d) — it involved no exchange or receipt of cash, goods, or services, so it is not a recordable transaction (e.g., merely signing an agreement with no economic exchange yet)."},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 4 — Trial Balance:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Jaguar Plastics Company — Trial Balance, At December 31","Debit","Credit"],["Cash","12,000",""],["Investments (short-term)","13,000",""],["Accounts receivable","3,000",""],["Inventory","20,000",""],["Notes receivable (long-term)","6,000",""],["Equipment","49,000",""],["Factory building","114,000",""],["Operating lease assets","158,000",""],["Intangible assets","8,000",""],["Accounts payable","","15,000"],["Accrued liabilities payable","","4,000"],["Notes payable (short-term)","","16,000"],["Notes payable (long-term)","","103,000"],["Long-term operating lease liabilities","","76,000"],["Common stock","","11,000"],["Additional paid-in capital","","127,000"],["Retained earnings","","31,000"],["Totals","$383,000","$383,000"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 5 — Classified Balance Sheet:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Jaguar Plastics Company — Balance Sheet, At December 31",""],["Cash","$12,000"],["Investments","13,000"],["Accounts receivable","3,000"],["Inventory","20,000"],["Total Current Assets","48,000"],["Notes receivable","6,000"],["Equipment","49,000"],["Operating lease assets","158,000"],["Factory building","114,000"],["Intangible assets","8,000"],["Total Assets","$383,000"],["Accounts payable","$15,000"],["Accrued liabilities payable","4,000"],["Notes payable","16,000"],["Total Current Liabilities","35,000"],["Notes payable (long-term)","103,000"],["Long-term operating lease liabilities","76,000"],["Total Liabilities","214,000"],["Common stock","11,000"],["Additional paid-in capital","127,000"],["Retained earnings","31,000"],["Total Stockholders' Equity","169,000"],["Total Liabilities & Stockholders' Equity","$383,000"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"P2-5 — Apple Inc.: Journal Entries, T-accounts, Trial Balance, Balance Sheet (dollars in millions)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(a)","Cash (+A)","18,266",""],["","Long-term debt (+L)","","18,266"],["(b)","Long-term investments (+A)","4,200",""],["","Short-term investments (+A)","16,800",""],["","Cash (−A)","","21,000"],["(c)","Property, plant, and equipment (+A)","10,981",""],["","Cash (−A)","","9,571"],["","Short-term debt (+L)","","1,410"],["(d)","Cash (+A)","1,469",""],["","Common stock (+SE)","","1"],["","Additional paid-in capital (+SE)","","1,468"],["(e)","Cash (+A)","18,810",""],["","Short-term investments (−A)","","18,810"],["(f)","Retained earnings (−SE)","11,126",""],["","Dividends payable (+L)","","11,126"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Trial Balance (from posting the above to beginning balances):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Apple Inc. — Trial Balance, At September 28, 2024 (in millions)","Debit","Credit"],["Cash","37,939",""],["Short-term investments","29,580",""],["Accounts receivable","60,985",""],["Inventories","6,331",""],["Other current assets","14,695",""],["Long-term investments","104,744",""],["Property, plant, and equipment","54,696",""],["Other noncurrent assets","64,758",""],["Accounts payable","","62,611"],["Accrued expenses","","58,829"],["Unearned revenue","","8,061"],["Short-term debt","","17,217"],["Dividends payable","","11,126"],["Long-term debt","","113,547"],["Other noncurrent liabilities","","49,848"],["Common stock","","2"],["Additional paid-in capital","","75,279"],["Accumulated deficit and other comprehensive loss","22,792",""],["Totals","$396,520","$396,520"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Classified Balance Sheet:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Apple Inc. — Balance Sheet, At September 28, 2024 (in millions)",""],["Cash","$37,939"],["Short-term investments","29,580"],["Accounts receivable","60,985"],["Inventories","6,331"],["Other current assets","14,695"],["Total current assets","149,530"],["Long-term investments","104,744"],["Property, plant, and equipment, net","54,696"],["Other noncurrent assets","64,758"],["Total assets","$373,728"],["Accounts payable","$62,611"],["Accrued expenses","58,829"],["Unearned revenue","8,061"],["Short-term debt","17,217"],["Dividends payable","11,126"],["Total current liabilities","157,844"],["Long-term debt","113,547"],["Other noncurrent liabilities","49,848"],["Total liabilities","321,239"],["Common stock","2"],["Additional paid-in capital","75,279"],["Accumulated deficit and other comprehensive loss","(22,792)"],["Total stockholders' equity","54,489"],["Total liabilities and stockholders' equity","$373,728"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.12 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 2 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C2.1 (MCQ) — Dual effects"},{"t":"p","x":"A company purchases equipment for $12,000, paying $4,000 cash and signing a note payable for the rest. What is the effect on total assets?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Increase by $12,000"},{"t":"li","x":"B. Increase by $8,000"},{"t":"li","x":"C. No change"},{"t":"li","x":"D. Decrease by $4,000"},{"t":"a","x":"B. Equipment (+A) $12,000 and Cash (−A) $4,000 → net change in assets = +$8,000 (matched by Notes Payable (+L) $8,000, so the equation still balances)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C2.2 (MCQ) — Trial balance limitations"},{"t":"p","x":"A bookkeeper recorded a $500 cash purchase of supplies as a debit to Equipment and a credit to Cash. Will the trial balance still balance?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. No — debits won't equal credits"},{"t":"li","x":"B. Yes — because both sides used the correct dollar amount, even though the wrong account was debited"},{"t":"li","x":"C. It depends on when the error occurred"},{"t":"li","x":"D. Only if the amount was under $1,000"},{"t":"a","x":"B. A trial balance only checks that total debits equal total credits; it cannot detect an error where the correct dollar amount was posted to the wrong account."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C2.3 (Short problem) — Classify and normal balance"},{"t":"p","x":"For each account, state whether it is an Asset, Liability, or Stockholders' Equity account, and whether its normal balance is a debit or credit: (a) Unearned Subscription Revenue; (b) Prepaid Rent; (c) Notes Payable (long-term); (d) Additional Paid-in Capital; (e) Notes Receivable."},{"t":"a","x":"• (a) Liability, normal balance credit (cash received before it is earned).\n• (b) Asset, normal balance debit (cash paid in advance for a future benefit).\n• (c) Liability, normal balance credit.\n• (d) Stockholders' equity, normal balance credit.\n• (e) Asset, normal balance debit (amount owed TO the company)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C2.4 (Journal entry / T-account problem) — Full transaction cycle"},{"t":"p","x":"Bright Design Studio Ltd. began operations on 1 March with $0 balances. During March it completed the following transactions:"},{"t":"li","x":"(1) Issued common stock for $50,000 cash."},{"t":"li","x":"(2) Borrowed $20,000 from a bank on a 2-year note."},{"t":"li","x":"(3) Purchased equipment for $30,000 cash."},{"t":"li","x":"(4) Purchased $4,000 of supplies on account."},{"t":"li","x":"(5) Paid $1,500 of the accounts payable from (4)."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: (a) Journalize each transaction. (b) Post to T-accounts for Cash and Accounts Payable. (c) Prepare a trial balance."},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Journal entries"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(1)","Cash (+A)","50,000",""],["","Common Stock (+SE)","","50,000"],["(2)","Cash (+A)","20,000",""],["","Notes Payable (+L)","","20,000"],["(3)","Equipment (+A)","30,000",""],["","Cash (−A)","","30,000"],["(4)","Supplies (+A)","4,000",""],["","Accounts Payable (+L)","","4,000"],["(5)","Accounts Payable (−L)","1,500",""],["","Cash (−A)","","1,500"]]},{"t":"a","x":"**(b) T-accounts**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Cash","Cash"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["Beg. 0","(3) 30,000"],["(1) 50,000","(5) 1,500"],["(2) 20,000",""]]},{"t":"a","x":"Ending Cash balance = 50,000 + 20,000 − 30,000 − 1,500 = $38,500 (debit balance)."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Accounts Payable","Accounts Payable"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["(5) 1,500","Beg. 0"],["","(4) 4,000"]]},{"t":"a","x":"Ending Accounts Payable balance = 4,000 − 1,500 = $2,500 (credit balance).\n**(c) Trial balance**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Bright Design Studio Ltd. — Trial Balance, At 31 March","Debit","Credit"],["Cash","38,500",""],["Supplies","4,000",""],["Equipment","30,000",""],["Accounts payable","","2,500"],["Notes payable","","20,000"],["Common stock","","50,000"],["Totals","$72,500","$72,500"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C2.5 (Short problem) — Reconstruct a missing figure"},{"t":"p","x":"Delta Freight Co. reports total assets of $940,000 and total stockholders' equity of $560,000 at year-end. During the year, the only liability transactions were: issuing a $150,000 long-term note and paying off a $40,000 short-term note that existed at the start of the year. If beginning total liabilities were $270,000, what are ending total liabilities, and does the accounting equation hold?"},{"t":"a","x":"• Ending liabilities = Beginning $270,000 + New note $150,000 − Repayment $40,000 = $380,000.\n• Check: Assets = Liabilities + SE → $380,000 + $560,000 = $940,000, which matches the given total assets. The equation holds. ✓"}]},"3":{"title":"Operating Decisions and the Accounting System","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.1 The Operating Cycle & Time Period Assumption"},{"t":"p","x":"A business's operating cycle repeats continuously until it ceases activities. To measure income for a specific period, accountants apply the time period assumption (a company's long life is divided into shorter periods: months, quarters, years). This raises two issues:"},{"t":"li","x":"Recognition issue — WHEN should the effects of operating activities be recorded?"},{"t":"li","x":"Measurement issue — WHAT AMOUNT should be recorded?"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.2 Elements of the Income Statement"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Category","Definition","Examples"],["Operating Revenues","Amounts earned/recorded from day-to-day business activities (the central focus of the business).","Sales Revenue, Service Revenue"],["Operating Expenses","Costs of operating the business incurred to generate revenue during the period.","Depreciation, Repairs, Supplies, Utilities, Wages, Insurance, Rent, G&A expense, Losses on disposal"],["Other Items","Revenues/expenses/gains/losses NOT central to ongoing operations.","Interest/Dividend Revenue, Interest Expense, Gains/Losses on Sale of Investments"]]},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ Expenditure ≠ Expense! An expenditure is any cash outlay (buying equipment, repaying a loan, paying for a repair). An expense is specifically a cost incurred to generate revenue. So NOT all cash expenditures are expenses (e.g. buying equipment is a cash expenditure but creates an ASSET, not an immediate expense)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Typical (multi-step) Income Statement Format"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Line",""],["Operating Revenues",""],["Less: Operating Expenses",""],["= Income from Operations",""],["+/− Other Items",""],["= Income before Income Taxes (Pretax Income)",""],["− Income Tax Expense (Provision for Income Taxes)",""],["= Net Income",""]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.3 Cash Basis vs. Accrual Basis Accounting"},{"t":"table","rows":[["","Cash Basis","Accrual Basis (required by GAAP)"],["Revenue recorded when...","cash is received","goods/services are delivered (earned) — regardless of when cash is received"],["Expense recorded when...","cash is paid","incurred in the same period as the revenue it relates to — regardless of when cash is paid"],["Allowed under GAAP?","No","Yes — required"],["Main weakness","Can badly distort the picture of performance (e.g., a big upfront cash outlay makes a healthy company look like it's failing)","Faithfully matches effort (expense) with the resulting benefit (revenue)"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Example of the distortion: a company with $60,000/year of sales on credit shows negative cash flow of $(25,000) in Year 1 under the cash basis purely because of upfront insurance and low collections — even though the underlying business is healthy and collects the same $180,000 total over 3 years."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Revenue Recognition Principle — 5-Step Model"},{"t":"li","x":"1. Identify the contract between the company and the customer."},{"t":"li","x":"2. Identify the seller's performance obligations (promised goods/services)."},{"t":"li","x":"3. Determine the transaction price."},{"t":"li","x":"4. Allocate the transaction price to the performance obligations."},{"t":"li","x":"5. Recognize revenue when each performance obligation is satisfied (or over time, if the service is delivered over time)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Critical point: **revenue recognition is triggered by DELIVERY of goods/services, not by receipt of cash."},{"t":"p","x":"**Three revenue-recognition timing scenarios**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(1)","On $100 cash deposit received: Cash (+A)","100",""],["","Unearned Revenue (+L)","","100"],["","Cash received BEFORE delivery → record a liability (Unearned Revenue); recognize revenue only on delivery.","Cash received BEFORE delivery → record a liability (Unearned Revenue); recognize revenue only on delivery.","Cash received BEFORE delivery → record a liability (Unearned Revenue); recognize revenue only on delivery."],["","On delivery: Unearned Revenue (−L)","100",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","100"],["(2)","Cash (+A)","12",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","12"],["","Cash received in the SAME period as delivery → record revenue directly.","Cash received in the SAME period as delivery → record revenue directly.","Cash received in the SAME period as delivery → record revenue directly."],["(3)","On delivery of $50 on account: Accounts Receivable (+A)","50",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","50"],["","Cash received AFTER delivery → record an asset (Accounts Receivable) at the point of delivery.","Cash received AFTER delivery → record an asset (Accounts Receivable) at the point of delivery.","Cash received AFTER delivery → record an asset (Accounts Receivable) at the point of delivery."],["","On later cash receipt: Cash (+A)","50",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","50"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**In-class practice — ABC Ltd. unearned revenue, worked in full**"},{"t":"p","x":"On Jan 2, ABC Ltd. receives $2,700 cash for services of $900/month for 3 months, starting in January."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Jan 2","Cash (+A)","2,700",""],["","Unearned Revenue (+L)","","2,700"],["","No service has been delivered yet on Jan 2, so the full amount is a liability.","No service has been delivered yet on Jan 2, so the full amount is a liability.","No service has been delivered yet on Jan 2, so the full amount is a liability."],["Jan 31","Unearned Revenue (−L)","900",""],["","Service Revenue (+R, +SE)","","900"],["","One month of service (Jan) has now been delivered — recognize $900 of revenue.","One month of service (Jan) has now been delivered — recognize $900 of revenue.","One month of service (Jan) has now been delivered — recognize $900 of revenue."],["Feb 28","Unearned Revenue (−L)","900",""],["","Service Revenue (+R, +SE)","","900"],["Mar 31","Unearned Revenue (−L)","900",""],["","Service Revenue (+R, +SE)","","900"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Expense Recognition (Matching) Principle"},{"t":"p","x":"Costs incurred to generate revenue must be recognized in the same period as that revenue — regardless of when cash is paid. Common costs: salaries/wages worked, utilities used, supplies used, facilities/equipment used during the period."},{"t":"p","x":"**Three expense-recognition timing scenarios**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(1)","On $200 cash paid for supplies: Supplies (+A)","200",""],["","Cash (−A)","","200"],["","Cash paid BEFORE the expense is incurred → record an asset first (e.g., Supplies), then expense it as used.","Cash paid BEFORE the expense is incurred → record an asset first (e.g., Supplies), then expense it as used.","Cash paid BEFORE the expense is incurred → record an asset first (e.g., Supplies), then expense it as used."],["","On using half the supplies: Supplies Expense (+E, −SE)","100",""],["","Supplies (−A)","","100"],["(2)","Repairs Expense (+E, −SE)","275",""],["","Cash (−A)","","275"],["","Cash paid in the SAME period the expense is incurred → record the expense directly.","Cash paid in the SAME period the expense is incurred → record the expense directly.","Cash paid in the SAME period the expense is incurred → record the expense directly."],["(3)","On using $400 of employee services: Wages Expense (+E, −SE)","400",""],["","Wages Payable (+L)","","400"],["","Cash paid AFTER the expense is incurred → record a liability first, then pay it later.","Cash paid AFTER the expense is incurred → record a liability first, then pay it later.","Cash paid AFTER the expense is incurred → record a liability first, then pay it later."],["","On paying cash later: Wages Payable (−L)","400",""],["","Cash (−A)","","400"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**In-class practice — ABC Ltd. accrued utilities expense, worked in full**"},{"t":"p","x":"On Jan 31, ABC Ltd. receives a $1,800 utility bill for the month; it plans to pay it on Feb 6."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Jan 31","Utilities Expense (+E, −SE)","1,800",""],["","Utilities Payable (+L)","","1,800"],["","The utilities were used/enjoyed during January, so the expense is incurred now (even though unpaid).","The utilities were used/enjoyed during January, so the expense is incurred now (even though unpaid).","The utilities were used/enjoyed during January, so the expense is incurred now (even though unpaid)."],["Feb 6","Utilities Payable (−L)","1,800",""],["","Cash (−A)","","1,800"],["","Paying the bill is a settlement of the liability, not a new expense.","Paying the bill is a settlement of the liability, not a new expense.","Paying the bill is a settlement of the liability, not a new expense."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.4 Expanded Transaction Analysis Model & Analysis Steps"},{"t":"p","x":"Stockholders' equity splits into Contributed Capital (Common Stock + APIC — increases with stock issuance) and Earned Capital (Retained Earnings — increases with Net Income, decreases with Dividends Declared and with Net Loss)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Net income vs. net loss: **Revenues > Expenses → Net Income → increases Retained Earnings and SE. Expenses > Revenues → Net Loss → decreases Retained Earnings and SE."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Steps for Analyzing Any Transaction (Exhibit 3.5)"},{"t":"li","x":"Step 1 — Ask: Was a revenue earned by delivering goods/services? → debit what was received (+A/−L), credit Revenue (+R, +SE)."},{"t":"li","x":"OR was an expense incurred to generate revenue this period? → debit Expense (+E, −SE), credit what was given (−A/+L)."},{"t":"li","x":"OR neither → debit what was received (+A/−L), credit what was given (−A/+L)."},{"t":"li","x":"Step 2 — Verify: does A = L + SE still hold? Do debits = credits?"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.5 Worked Example — Chipotle's Q1 Operating Transactions"},{"t":"p","x":"(All amounts in millions; T-accounts begin from the Ch.2 trial balance.) Note how each entry marks (+R,+SE) for revenues and (+E,−SE) for expenses:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(1)","Cash (+A)","1,315",""],["","Accounts Receivable (+A)","44",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","1,359"],["","Sold food/beverages for $1,359 total; $44 on account to universities, rest in cash.","Sold food/beverages for $1,359 total; $44 on account to universities, rest in cash.","Sold food/beverages for $1,359 total; $44 on account to universities, rest in cash."],["(2)","Supplies (+A)","459",""],["","Cash (−A)","","379"],["","Accounts Payable (+L)","","80"],["","Purchased $459 of supplies; paid $379 cash, rest on account.","Purchased $459 of supplies; paid $379 cash, rest on account.","Purchased $459 of supplies; paid $379 cash, rest on account."],["(3)","Prepaid Expenses (+A)","207",""],["","Cash (−A)","","207"],["","Paid $207 cash for rent/insurance/advertising to be used in the future (Prepaid Expenses).","Paid $207 cash for rent/insurance/advertising to be used in the future (Prepaid Expenses).","Paid $207 cash for rent/insurance/advertising to be used in the future (Prepaid Expenses)."],["(4)","Training Expense (+E, −SE)","65",""],["","Cash (−A)","","65"],["","Paid $65 cash for management training expense (incurred and paid same period).","Paid $65 cash for management training expense (incurred and paid same period).","Paid $65 cash for management training expense (incurred and paid same period)."],["(5)","Wages Expense (+E, −SE)","342",""],["","Wages Payable (−L)","47",""],["","Cash (−A)","","389"],["","Paid employees $342 for this quarter's work and $47 owed from last quarter.","Paid employees $342 for this quarter's work and $47 owed from last quarter.","Paid employees $342 for this quarter's work and $47 owed from last quarter."],["(6)","Cash (+A)","12",""],["","Loss on Sale of Land (+E, −SE)","9",""],["","Land (−A)","","21"],["","Sold land costing $21 for $12 cash — a $9 loss on disposal.","Sold land costing $21 for $12 cash — a $9 loss on disposal.","Sold land costing $21 for $12 cash — a $9 loss on disposal."],["(7)","Cash (+A)","61",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","61"],["","Received $61 cash from customers paying down their receivable balance.","Received $61 cash from customers paying down their receivable balance.","Received $61 cash from customers paying down their receivable balance."],["(8)","Accounts Payable (−L)","66",""],["","Utilities Payable (−L)","25",""],["","Cash (−A)","","91"],["","Paid $66 accounts payable to suppliers and $25 utilities payable (both from last quarter).","Paid $66 accounts payable to suppliers and $25 utilities payable (both from last quarter).","Paid $66 accounts payable to suppliers and $25 utilities payable (both from last quarter)."],["(9)","Utilities Expense (+E, −SE)","117",""],["","Repairs Expense (+E, −SE)","35",""],["","Cash (−A)","","152"],["","Paid $117 utilities used this quarter and $35 for building/equipment repairs.","Paid $117 utilities used this quarter and $35 for building/equipment repairs.","Paid $117 utilities used this quarter and $35 for building/equipment repairs."],["(10)","Cash (+A)","35",""],["","Unearned Revenue (+L)","","35"],["","Sold gift cards for $35 cash — a liability, since no food has been delivered yet.","Sold gift cards for $35 cash — a liability, since no food has been delivered yet.","Sold gift cards for $35 cash — a liability, since no food has been delivered yet."],["(11)","Cash (+A)","3",""],["","Interest Revenue (+R, +SE)","","3"],["","Received $3 cash as interest revenue earned this quarter (an \"Other Item\", not operating revenue).","Received $3 cash as interest revenue earned this quarter (an \"Other Item\", not operating revenue).","Received $3 cash as interest revenue earned this quarter (an \"Other Item\", not operating revenue)."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.6 Unadjusted (Classified) Income Statement"},{"t":"p","x":"An unadjusted income statement — built straight from the trial balance before adjusting entries — is NEVER presented to external users, because it omits necessary period-end adjustments (covered in Chapter 4)."},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ Current development (context only, not examinable in detail): HKFRS 18 will replace HKAS 1 for periods beginning on/after 1 Jan 2027, mandating a more structured profit-or-loss statement with 5 defined categories of income/expense. Good general knowledge for Hong Kong-based accounting students."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.9 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 3"},{"t":"li","x":"I can distinguish operating revenues/expenses from \"Other Items\" and place each correctly on a multi-step income statement."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain expenditure vs. expense with an example."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain why GAAP requires accrual accounting and can give an example of how cash-basis accounting distorts performance."},{"t":"li","x":"I can apply the 5-step revenue recognition model and identify the correct timing (before/during/after cash receipt) for any scenario, with the correct journal entry."},{"t":"li","x":"I can apply the expense recognition (matching) principle and identify the correct timing for any scenario, with the correct journal entry."},{"t":"li","x":"I can work through a full quarter of operating transactions (like the Chipotle example) confidently, tagging each account with +A/−A/+L/−L/+R/+E/±SE."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain why an unadjusted income statement is not suitable for external users."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.7 Official Practice — Worksheet E3-2, E3-3, E3-7, AP3-6 (with Suggested Solutions)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E3-2 — Identify revenue recognized in September (13 activities)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Activity","Revenue Account / Amount Recognized in September"],["a.","None — earnings process not yet complete."],["b.","Interest revenue $125 (= $15,000 × 10% × 1/12)."],["c.","Sales revenue $74,000."],["d.","None — only an exchange of promises has occurred, no transaction yet."],["e.","Sales revenue $21,000 (= 1,000 shirts × $21) — revenue earned when goods are delivered."],["f.","None — this cash receipt relates to revenue already recorded in (e) when delivered."],["g.","None — recorded instead as the liability Unearned Revenue (deferred revenue)."],["h.","None — issuing stock is a financing activity, not revenue."],["i.","None — recorded as Unearned Revenue (deferred revenue)."],["j.","Ticket sales revenue $2,940,000 (= $20,580,000 ÷ 7 games)."],["k.","None — earnings process not yet complete."],["l.","Sales revenue $60,000."],["m.","Sales revenue $300."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E3-3 — Identify expense incurred in January (17 activities)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Activity","Expense Account / Amount Incurred in January"],["a.","Utilities expense $3,800."],["b.","Advertising expense $450 (= $1,350 × 1/3 months); remainder is a Prepaid Expense (asset) for Feb/Mar."],["c.","Salaries expense $201,500 for January (the other half was incurred/expensed in December)."],["d.","None yet — recorded as the asset Prepaid Expense until the related revenue is earned."],["e.","None yet — recorded as Inventory when received; expensed later as Cost of Goods Sold."],["f.","Cost of goods sold $84,000 (= 400 books × $210)."],["g.","None — this was a December expense paid in January; not a January expense."],["h.","Commissions expense $55,560."],["i.","None yet — expensed later as Depreciation over the grill's useful life."],["j.","Supplies expense $4,700 (= $3,500 + $2,600 − $1,400)."],["k.","Wages expense $184 (= 8 hours × $23)."],["l.","Insurance expense $400 (= $4,800 ÷ 12); remainder is Prepaid Insurance."],["m.","Repairs expense $600."],["n.","Utilities expense $154."],["o.","Consulting expense $2,034."],["p.","None — December expense paid in January."],["q.","Cost of goods sold $9,450 (= 450 shirts × $21)."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E3-7 — Journal Entries and Accounts Receivable T-account"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["a.","Cash (+A)","2,300,000",""],["","Short-term note payable (+L)","","2,300,000"],["b.","Equipment (+A)","98,000",""],["","Cash (−A)","","98,000"],["c.","Inventory (+A)","35,000",""],["","Accounts payable (+L)","","35,000"],["d.","Repairs expense (+E, −SE)","62,000",""],["","Cash (−A)","","62,000"],["e.","Cash (+A)","390,000",""],["","Unearned pass revenue (+L)","","390,000"],["f.","Accounts receivable (+A)","700",""],["","Ski shop sales revenue (+R, +SE)","","700"],["g.","Cost of goods sold (+E, −SE)","400",""],["","Inventory (−A)","","400"],["h.","Cash (+A)","320,000",""],["","Lift revenue (+R, +SE)","","320,000"],["i.","Cash (+A)","3,500",""],["","Unearned rent revenue (+L)","","3,500"],["j.","Accounts payable (−L)","17,500",""],["","Cash (−A)","","17,500"],["k.","Cash (+A)","400",""],["","Accounts receivable (−A)","","400"],["l.","Wages expense (+E, −SE)","245,000",""],["","Cash (−A)","","245,000"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Accounts Receivable","Accounts Receivable"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["Beg. bal. 1,000","(k) 400"],["(f) 700",""]]},{"t":"p","x":"Ending Accounts Receivable balance = 1,000 + 700 − 400 = $1,300."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AP3-6 — Exxon Mobil Corporation: Journal Entries, T-accounts, Income Statement (in millions)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["a.","Property and equipment (+A)","1,610",""],["","Accounts payable (+L)","","1,610"],["b.","Cash (+A)","3,100",""],["","Accounts receivable (−A)","","3,100"],["c.","Utilities expense (+E, −SE)","3",""],["","Cash (−A)","","3"],["d.","Accounts receivable (+A)","39,780",""],["","Sales revenue (+R, +SE)","","39,780"],["e.","Cost of sales (+E, −SE)","5,984",""],["","Inventories (−A)","","5,984"],["f.","Wages expense (+E, −SE)","1,238",""],["","Cash (−A)","","1,238"],["g.","Income taxes payable (−L)","1,185",""],["","Cash (−A)","","1,185"],["","1,185 = 3/4 × $1,580 income taxes payable settled in cash.","1,185 = 3/4 × $1,580 income taxes payable settled in cash.","1,185 = 3/4 × $1,580 income taxes payable settled in cash."],["h.","Inventories (+A)","23",""],["","Accounts payable (+L)","","23"],["i.","Prepaid expenses (+A)","82",""],["","Cash (−A)","","82"],["j.","Other long-term debt (−L)","10",""],["","Interest expense (+E, −SE)","1",""],["","Cash (−A)","","11"],["k.","Other assets and intangibles (+A)","6",""],["","Cash (−A)","","6"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Exxon Mobil Corporation — Income Statement (unadjusted), For the Month Ended January 31 (in millions)",""],["Sales revenue","$39,780"],["Less: Cost of sales","5,984"],["Less: Wages expense","1,238"],["Less: Utilities expense","3"],["Total expenses","7,225"],["Operating income","32,555"],["Other items — Interest expense","1"],["Net Income","$32,554"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.8 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 3 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C3.1 (MCQ) — Cash vs. accrual"},{"t":"p","x":"A landscaping company mows a client's lawn on 28 June but is not paid until 5 July. Under accrual accounting, in which month is the revenue recognized?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. June — when the service was performed"},{"t":"li","x":"B. July — when the cash was received"},{"t":"li","x":"C. Split 50/50 between June and July"},{"t":"li","x":"D. Neither — revenue is never recognized for services"},{"t":"a","x":"A. Under accrual accounting, revenue is recognized when the service is delivered/earned, not when cash changes hands."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C3.2 (MCQ) — Matching principle"},{"t":"p","x":"A company pays a full year of insurance, $12,000, on 1 October. How much insurance EXPENSE should be recognized for the quarter ended 31 December?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. $12,000"},{"t":"li","x":"B. $3,000"},{"t":"li","x":"C. $1,000"},{"t":"li","x":"D. $0"},{"t":"a","x":"B. $12,000 ÷ 12 months × 3 months (Oct, Nov, Dec) = $3,000 expensed; the remaining $9,000 stays as Prepaid Insurance (an asset) at year-end."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C3.3 (Short problem) — Classify revenue/expense timing"},{"t":"p","x":"For each event, state whether cash is received/paid BEFORE, DURING (same period as), or AFTER the related revenue/expense, and name the balance sheet account created (if any):"},{"t":"li","x":"(i) A gym sells a $1,200 one-year membership on 1 Nov, cash up front."},{"t":"li","x":"(ii) A law firm bills a client $5,000 for work finished this month; the client will pay next month."},{"t":"li","x":"(iii) A shop pays $2,000 cash to a cleaning company for cleaning done this month."},{"t":"a","x":"• (i) Cash received BEFORE the service is delivered → Unearned Revenue (liability) of $1,200, recognized as revenue at $100/month over the year.\n• (ii) Cash received AFTER the service is delivered → Accounts Receivable (asset) of $5,000; revenue is recognized now, in the month the work was done.\n• (iii) Cash paid DURING the same period as the expense → Cleaning Expense recorded directly for $2,000; no balance sheet account created."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C3.4 (Journal-entry problem) — Multi-scenario adjustments-style transactions"},{"t":"p","x":"Summit Tutors Ltd. completed these transactions in November:"},{"t":"li","x":"(1) Received $6,000 cash on Nov 1 for a 3-month tutoring package (Nov–Jan), $2,000/month."},{"t":"li","x":"(2) Tutored a walk-in student for $150 cash, paid immediately."},{"t":"li","x":"(3) Delivered $900 of tutoring to a corporate client, to be billed and collected in December."},{"t":"li","x":"(4) Received the electricity bill for November, $340, to be paid in December."},{"t":"li","x":"(5) Paid $500 cash for October's rent (rent was already recorded as an expense/payable in October)."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: journalize each transaction for November."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(1)","Cash (+A)","6,000",""],["","Unearned Tutoring Revenue (+L)","","6,000"],["","Only $2,000 (November's portion) would be recognized as revenue via a period-end adjustment (Chapter 4) — as of the receipt date, the full amount is unearned.","Only $2,000 (November's portion) would be recognized as revenue via a period-end adjustment (Chapter 4) — as of the receipt date, the full amount is unearned.","Only $2,000 (November's portion) would be recognized as revenue via a period-end adjustment (Chapter 4) — as of the receipt date, the full amount is unearned."],["(2)","Cash (+A)","150",""],["","Tutoring Revenue (+R, +SE)","","150"],["(3)","Accounts Receivable (+A)","900",""],["","Tutoring Revenue (+R, +SE)","","900"],["(4)","Utilities Expense (+E, −SE)","340",""],["","Utilities Payable (+L)","","340"],["(5)","Rent Payable (−L)","500",""],["","Cash (−A)","","500"],["","This is a settlement of a liability from October, NOT a November expense — the expense was already recognized in October under the matching principle.","This is a settlement of a liability from October, NOT a November expense — the expense was already recognized in October under the matching principle.","This is a settlement of a liability from October, NOT a November expense — the expense was already recognized in October under the matching principle."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C3.5 (Comprehensive short problem) — Build a mini income statement"},{"t":"p","x":"Zenith Ltd.'s December trial balance (unadjusted, partial) shows: Sales revenue $85,000; Interest revenue $600; Cost of goods sold $38,000; Wages expense $19,500; Rent expense $6,000; Utilities expense $1,400; Interest expense $900; Income tax rate 20% of pretax income."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: prepare a classified income statement for December, down to net income."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Zenith Ltd. — Income Statement, For the Month Ended December 31",""],["Sales revenue","$85,000"],["Less: Cost of goods sold","38,000"],["Less: Wages expense","19,500"],["Less: Rent expense","6,000"],["Less: Utilities expense","1,400"],["Income from operations","20,100"],["Other items: Interest revenue","600"],["Other items: Interest expense","(900)"],["Income before income taxes","19,800"],["Income tax expense (20% × $19,800)","3,960"],["Net income","$15,840"]]}]},"4":{"title":"Adjustments, Financial Statements, and the Closing Process","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.1 Purpose of Adjustments"},{"t":"li","x":"Revenues are recorded when EARNED (revenue recognition principle)."},{"t":"li","x":"Expenses are recorded when INCURRED to generate revenue (expense recognition principle)."},{"t":"li","x":"Assets are reported at amounts representing probable future benefits remaining at period-end."},{"t":"li","x":"Liabilities are reported at amounts representing probable future sacrifices owed at period-end."},{"t":"p","x":"Adjusting entries are required every time a company prepares financial statements. Companies wait until period-end to adjust because doing so daily/weekly would be too costly and time-consuming."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.2 The Four Types of Adjustments — Master Table"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","What happened","Adjusting entry needed"],["Deferred (Unearned) Revenue","Cash was received in the PAST, before goods/services were delivered — recorded as a liability.","If earned by period-end: Debit Unearned Revenue (−L); Credit Revenue (+R, +SE)."],["Accrued Revenue","Revenue was EARNED but cash will be received in the FUTURE — nothing recorded yet.","Debit a Receivable (+A); Credit Revenue (+R, +SE)."],["Deferred Expense","Cash was paid in the PAST for an asset (e.g. Supplies, Prepaid Rent) that is used up over time.","Debit Expense (+E, −SE); Credit the Asset (−A) [Prepaid Expense / Supplies]."],["Accrued Expense","An expense was INCURRED but cash will be paid in the FUTURE — nothing recorded yet.","Debit Expense (+E, −SE); Credit a Payable (+L)."]]},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ Key exam tip: notice the Cash account is NEVER touched by an adjusting entry — cash has already moved (deferred items) or will move later (accrued items). Every adjusting entry always includes at least one Income Statement account AND at least one Balance Sheet account."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.3 Worked AJEs — Chipotle's Quarter-End Adjustments (all in $ millions)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 1 — Deferred (Unearned) Revenue"},{"t":"p","x":"Unadjusted Unearned Revenue balance = $245 (gift card sales). During the quarter, customers redeemed $52 of gift cards for food."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Unearned Revenue (−L)","52",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","52"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 2 — Accrued Revenue"},{"t":"p","x":"Short-term investments earned $1 of additional interest this quarter; cash will be received next quarter."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Interest Receivable (+A)","1",""],["","Interest Revenue (+R, +SE)","","1"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 3 — Deferred Expense: Supplies"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Computation of Supplies Expense",""],["Beginning balance — Supplies","$39"],["+ Purchases during the quarter","459"],["Unadjusted balance at end of quarter","498"],["− Amount on hand (physical count) at end of quarter","(36)"],["Supplies used during the quarter (= expense)","$462"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Supplies Expense (+E, −SE)","462",""],["","Supplies (−A)","","462"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 4 — Deferred Expense: Prepaid Expenses (rent, insurance, advertising)"},{"t":"p","x":"Unadjusted Prepaid Expenses balance = $324, made up of: $128 rent (3 months, $32/month), $96 insurance (6 months from Jan 1), $41 advertising for the quarter. By quarter-end, three months of each has been used."},{"t":"li","x":"Rent used: $128 (all 3 months of prepayment used) = $128 expensed."},{"t":"li","x":"Insurance used: $96 × 3/6 months = $48 expensed."},{"t":"li","x":"Advertising used: $41 (all used during the quarter) = $41 expensed."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Rent Expense (+E, −SE)","128",""],["","Insurance Expense (+E, −SE)","48",""],["","Advertising Expense (+E, −SE)","41",""],["","Prepaid Expenses (−A)","","217"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 5 — Depreciation (Deferred Expense on Buildings & Equipment)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Depreciation **is the systematic allocation of the cost of buildings/equipment (NOT land — land does not depreciate) over their estimated useful lives."},{"t":"p","x":"**Formula: **Depreciation expense for the quarter = Annual depreciation × (months in the quarter ÷ 12). Here: $232/year × 3/12 = $58."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Depreciation Expense (+E, −SE)","58",""],["","Accumulated Depreciation (+xA, a contra-asset)","","58"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Accumulated Depreciation is a contra-asset: it has a CREDIT normal balance (opposite of a normal asset) and is subtracted from the related asset's cost on the balance sheet to show net book value."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 6 — Accrued Expense: Wages"},{"t":"p","x":"Employees worked 2 days at quarter-end, earning $26/day, but won't be paid until next quarter."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Wages Expense (+E, −SE)","52",""],["","Wages Payable (+L)","","52"]]},{"t":"p","x":"52 = 2 days × $26/day."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 7 — Accrued Expense: Interest on Debt"},{"t":"p","x":"Principal balance on long-term notes payable = $151; annual interest rate = 2.65%; interest will be paid in the future."},{"t":"p","x":"**Formula: **Interest = Principal × Annual rate × (time outstanding this period ÷ 12). Quarterly interest ≈ $151 × 2.65% × 3/12 ≈ $1."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Interest Expense (+E, −SE)","1",""],["","Interest Payable (+L)","","1"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 8 — Accrued Expense: Utilities"},{"t":"p","x":"Utility bill of $15 received for usage during the quarter; will be paid next quarter."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Utilities Expense (+E, −SE)","15",""],["","Utilities Payable (+L)","","15"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 9 — Accrued Expense: Income Taxes (always the LAST adjusting entry)"},{"t":"p","x":"Compute adjusted pretax income (after AJE 1–8), then apply the tax rate (here 20.0%) to determine Income Tax Expense; taxes are due next quarter."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Income Tax Expense (+E, −SE)","15",""],["","Income Taxes Payable (+L)","","15"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.4 Summary — Applying the Adjustment Logic (Decision Tree)"},{"t":"li","x":"Was revenue earned that is NOT yet recorded, and cash WAS received in the past? → Deferred Revenue → increase Revenue, decrease Liability (Unearned Revenue)."},{"t":"li","x":"Was revenue earned that is NOT yet recorded, and cash WILL be received in the future? → Accrued Revenue → increase Revenue, increase Asset (a Receivable)."},{"t":"li","x":"Was an expense incurred that is NOT yet recorded, and cash WAS paid in the past? → Deferred Expense → increase Expense, decrease Asset."},{"t":"li","x":"Was an expense incurred that is NOT yet recorded, and cash WILL be paid in the future? → Accrued Expense → increase Expense, increase Liability (a Payable)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.5 Preparing the Financial Statements"},{"t":"p","x":"The interrelationships flow in this order: (1) Adjusted Trial Balance → (2) Income Statement (Revenues − Expenses = Net Income) → (3) Statement of Stockholders' Equity (Beg. Retained Earnings + Net Income − Dividends = End. Retained Earnings) → (4) Balance Sheet (Ending Retained Earnings appears in Stockholders' Equity, and the balance sheet must balance: A = L + SE)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.6 The Closing Process"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Permanent vs. Temporary Accounts"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Permanent Accounts","Temporary Accounts"],["Balance Sheet accounts (Assets, Liabilities, SE). Retain their balances from one period to the next.","Revenue, Expense, Gain, and Loss accounts (Income Statement accounts) — and Dividends Declared. Balances accumulate during the period then are reset to ZERO to start the next period."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Why We Close the Books"},{"t":"li","x":"Purpose 1: transfer the balances in temporary accounts to Retained Earnings."},{"t":"li","x":"Purpose 2: reset each temporary account to a zero balance to start the next period."},{"t":"p","x":"The closing entry is dated the last day of the period. Temporary accounts with debit balances are credited (closed out); those with credit balances are debited. The net effect equals net income (or loss) and flows into Retained Earnings."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Method — Closing Using an \"Income Summary\" Account (4 steps)"},{"t":"li","x":"Step 1 — Close credit balances in Revenue accounts to Income Summary (debit each Revenue, credit Income Summary for the total)."},{"t":"li","x":"Step 2 — Close debit balances in Expense accounts to Income Summary (credit each Expense, debit Income Summary for the total)."},{"t":"li","x":"Step 3 — Close Income Summary to Retained Earnings (the balance in Income Summary after steps 1–2 = net income/loss)."},{"t":"li","x":"Step 4 — Close the Dividends (Declared) account to Retained Earnings."},{"t":"p","x":"**Fully worked example — FastForward, Adjusted Trial Balance at Dec 31, 2026**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Cash","$4,350",""],["Accounts receivable","1,800",""],["Supplies","8,670",""],["Prepaid insurance","2,300",""],["Equipment","26,000",""],["Accumulated depreciation—Equipment","","$375"],["Accounts payable","","6,200"],["Salaries payable","","210"],["Unearned consulting revenue","","2,750"],["Share capital","","30,000"],["Retained earnings","","0"],["Dividends","200",""],["Consulting revenue","","7,850"],["Rental revenue","","300"],["Depreciation expense—Equipment","375",""],["Salaries expense","1,610",""],["Insurance expense","100",""],["Rent expense","1,000",""],["Supplies expense","1,050",""],["Utilities expense","230",""],["Totals","$47,685","$47,685"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Step 1 — Close Revenue accounts to Income Summary**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Dec 31","Consulting revenue","7,850",""],["","Rental revenue","300",""],["","Income Summary","","8,150"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Consulting Revenue","Consulting Revenue"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["7,850","7,850 (Beg.)"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Income Summary (running)","Income Summary (running)"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["","8,150"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Step 2 — Close Expense accounts to Income Summary**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Dec 31","Income Summary","4,365",""],["","Depreciation expense—Equipment","","375"],["","Salaries expense","","1,610"],["","Insurance expense","","100"],["","Rent expense","","1,000"],["","Supplies expense","","1,050"],["","Utilities expense","","230"]]},{"t":"p","x":"4,365 = 375 + 1,610 + 100 + 1,000 + 1,050 + 230, the sum of all expense balances."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Income Summary (after steps 1 & 2)","Income Summary (after steps 1 & 2)"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["4,365","8,150"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Net Profit (balance in Income Summary) = 8,150 − 4,365 = $3,785.**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Step 3 — Close Income Summary to Retained Earnings**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Dec 31","Income Summary","3,785",""],["","Retained Earnings","","3,785"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Retained Earnings (after step 3)","Retained Earnings (after step 3)"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["","3,785"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Step 4 — Close Dividends to Retained Earnings**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Dec 31","Retained Earnings","200",""],["","Dividends","","200"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Retained Earnings (final)","Retained Earnings (final)"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["200","3,785"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Final Retained Earnings balance = 3,785 − 200 = $3,585.**"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Alternative Method for Dividends Declared"},{"t":"p","x":"Instead of debiting Retained Earnings directly when a dividend is declared, a company may use a separate \"Dividends Declared\" account:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Direct method","Alternative (Dividends account) method"],["Dr. Retained Earnings\nCr. Dividends Payable","Dr. Dividends (Declared)\nCr. Dividends Payable"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Either way, the Dividends account (if used) is a temporary account eventually closed to Retained Earnings at period-end (Step 4 above)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Post-Closing Trial Balance"},{"t":"li","x":"Prepared as a final check: debits still equal credits, and all temporary (income statement + dividends) accounts have been reduced to zero."},{"t":"li","x":"Retained Earnings is now fully up to date and matches the balance sheet; it becomes the beginning balance for the next period."},{"t":"li","x":"This marks the end of the accounting cycle for the period."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.9 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 4"},{"t":"li","x":"I can name and distinguish the 4 types of adjustments (deferred revenue, accrued revenue, deferred expense, accrued expense) and write the correct generic journal entry for each."},{"t":"li","x":"I can compute supplies expense from beginning balance + purchases − ending count."},{"t":"li","x":"I can allocate a prepaid expense (rent/insurance/advertising) across the months it covers."},{"t":"li","x":"I can compute a period's depreciation from an annual figure (pro-rate by months) and I know Accumulated Depreciation is a contra-asset."},{"t":"li","x":"I can compute accrued wages, accrued interest, and accrued utilities at period-end."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain why Cash is never touched in an adjusting entry."},{"t":"li","x":"I can distinguish permanent from temporary accounts."},{"t":"li","x":"I can perform all 4 steps of closing using an Income Summary account, and explain the 2 purposes of closing."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain the alternative \"Dividends Declared\" account method and how/when it gets closed."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain what a post-closing trial balance checks for."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.7 Official Practice — Worksheet E4-8, E4-9, P4-7, COMP4-2"},{"t":"p","x":"Mise a jour (13 oct. 2026) : le fichier officiel \"Suggested-solutions_E4-8_E4-9_P4-7_COMP4-2.pdf\" a ete partage sur Moodle et les solutions completes sont presentees ci-dessous, avec la variante \"Closing Entries using Income Summary\" pour COMP4-2 (fichier de reference complementaire)."},{"t":"p","x":"What each blank worksheet tells us about the required task (from its template structure):"},{"t":"li","x":"E4-8 and E4-9: each require 7 adjusting journal entries (a–g), in the standard Debit/Credit two-column format — practice recognizing which of the 4 adjustment types (deferred revenue, accrued revenue, deferred expense, accrued expense) applies to each letter."},{"t":"li","x":"P4-7 (Tunstall, Inc.): requires (1) 6 adjusting entries (a–f); (2) a full classified Income Statement (down to Net Income) and Balance Sheet; (3) a closing entry."},{"t":"li","x":"COMP4-2 (Furniture Refinishers, Inc.): a comprehensive problem — adjusting entries (a–l), further entries (m–q), a full Income Statement, Statement of Stockholders' Equity, and Balance Sheet, a cash-flow effect classification table, and closing entries done BOTH without and WITH an Income Summary account, all posted to T-accounts."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Suggested Solutions — E4-8, E4-9, P4-7, COMP4-2 (official)"},{"t":"p","x":"E4-8 — Adjusting entries (amounts in $):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Adjusting entry","Debit","Credit"],["a.","Wages expense / Wages payable","2,700","2,700"],["b.","Office supplies expense / Office supplies ($450+$500-$275)","675","675"],["c.","Rent receivable / Rent revenue ($560 x 2 months)","1,120","1,120"],["d.","Depreciation expense / Accumulated depreciation","12,100","12,100"],["e.","Insurance expense / Prepaid insurance ($2,400 x 6/24)","600","600"],["f.","Unearned rent revenue / Rent revenue ($9,600 x 2/6)","3,200","3,200"],["g.","Repair accounts receivable / Repair shop revenue","800","800"]]},{"t":"p","x":"E4-9 — Adjusting entries (amounts in $):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Adjusting entry","Debit","Credit"],["a.","Accounts receivable / Service revenue","3,300","3,300"],["b.","Advertising expense / Prepaid advertising ($2,200 x 9/12)","1,650","1,650"],["c.","Interest expense / Interest payable ($300,000 x 11% x 2/12)","5,500","5,500"],["d.","Unearned storage revenue / Storage revenue ($4,500 x 1/6)","750","750"],["e.","Depreciation expense / Accumulated depreciation","18,000","18,000"],["f.","Supplies expense / Supplies ($18,900+$45,200-$15,600)","48,500","48,500"],["g.","Wages expense / Wages payable","5,600","5,600"]]},{"t":"p","x":"P4-7 (Tunstall, Inc.) — Req. 1: December 31 adjusting entries:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Adjusting entry","Debit","Credit"],["a.","Supplies expense / Supplies","600","600"],["b.","Insurance expense / Prepaid insurance","800","800"],["c.","Depreciation expense / Accumulated depreciation","3,700","3,700"],["d.","Wages expense / Wages payable","640","640"],["e.","Interest expense / Interest payable ($17,000 x 10% x 3/12)","425","425"],["f.","Income tax expense / Income taxes payable","5,540","5,540"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 2 — Income Statement: Service revenue $61,360; total operating expenses $39,100 (supplies $600, insurance $800, depreciation $3,700, wages $16,840, other $17,160); Operating income $22,260; interest expense $425; income before taxes $21,835; income tax expense $5,540; Net income $16,295 (EPS $3.26 on 5,000 shares)."},{"t":"p","x":"Balance Sheet at Dec. 31: Total assets $68,300 (cash $42,000, A/R $11,600, supplies $300, net service trucks $6,100, other assets $8,300). Total liabilities $26,605 (A/P $3,000, wages payable $640, interest payable $425, income taxes payable $5,540, notes payable long-term $17,000). Total stockholders' equity $41,695 (common stock $400, APIC $19,000, retained earnings $22,295 = $6,000 + $16,295 net income). Total liabilities & equity = Total assets = $68,300."},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 3 — Closing entry: debit Service revenue $61,360; credit each expense account for its balance (supplies $600, insurance $800, depreciation $3,700, wages $16,840, other $17,160, interest $425, income tax $5,540); credit Retained earnings $16,295."},{"t":"p","x":"COMP4-2 (Furniture Refinishers, Inc.) — Req. 2 & 3: journal entries (in $ thousands):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Entry","Debit","Credit"],["a.","Cash / Common stock + APIC","5,000","1,000 / 4,000"],["b.","Cash / Notes payable","20,000","20,000"],["c.","Equipment / Cash","18,000","18,000"],["d.","Cash + A/R / Service revenue","56,000+14,000","70,000"],["e.","Wages exp. + Misc. exp. / A/P + Cash","27,000+8,000","7,000+28,000"],["f.","Small tools / Cash","3,000","3,000"],["g.","Cash / Accounts receivable","8,000","8,000"],["h.","Accounts payable / Cash","11,000","11,000"],["i.","Supplies / Accounts payable","10,000","10,000"],["j.","Retained earnings / Dividends payable","10,000","10,000"],["k.","Cash / Unearned revenue","3,000","3,000"],["l.","Dividends payable / Cash","10,000","10,000"],["m.","Misc. expenses / Supplies + Small tools","9,000","8,000+1,000"],["n.","Depreciation expense / Accumulated depreciation","2,000","2,000"],["o.","Interest expense / Interest payable ($20,000 x 10% x 6/12)","1,000","1,000"],["p.","Wages expense / Wages payable","3,000","3,000"],["q.","Income tax expense / Income taxes payable","4,000","4,000"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 4 — Income Statement: Service revenue $70,000; operating expenses $49,000 (depreciation $2,000, wages $30,000, misc. $17,000); Operating income $21,000; interest expense $1,000; pretax income $20,000; income tax $4,000; Net income $16,000 (EPS $0.23 on 70,000 shares)."},{"t":"p","x":"Balance Sheet at Dec. 31, 2027: Total assets $74,000 (cash $27,000, A/R $10,000, supplies $4,000, small tools $8,000, equipment net $16,000, other noncurrent assets $9,000). Total liabilities $44,000 (A/P $13,000, notes payable $20,000, wages payable $3,000, interest payable $1,000, income taxes payable $4,000, unearned revenue $3,000). Total stockholders' equity $30,000 (common stock $7,000, APIC $13,000, retained earnings $10,000)."},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 5 — Cash flow classification: a. Financing +5,000; b. Financing +20,000; c. Investing -18,000; d. Operating +56,000; e. Operating -28,000; f. Operating -3,000; g. Operating +8,000; h. Operating -11,000; i. No effect; j. No effect; k. Operating +3,000; l. Financing -10,000."},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 6 — Closing entry (without Income Summary): debit Service revenue $70,000; credit depreciation $2,000, interest $1,000, wages $30,000, misc. expenses $17,000, income tax $4,000; credit Retained earnings $16,000."},{"t":"p","x":"**Variante avec le compte \"Income Summary\" (fichier de reference complementaire) : **"},{"t":"li","x":"Step 1 — Debit Service revenue $70,000; credit Income Summary $70,000."},{"t":"li","x":"Step 2 — Debit Income Summary $54,000; credit Depreciation expense $2,000, Interest expense $1,000, Wages expense $30,000, Misc. expenses $17,000, Income tax expense $4,000."},{"t":"li","x":"Step 3 — Debit Income Summary $16,000; credit Retained earnings $16,000 (= net income)."},{"t":"p","x":"Use the complementary exercises below (C4.4 and C4.5 in particular) as a stand-in worked example covering the same skills — they are built to the same pattern as E4-8/E4-9/P4-7/COMP4-2, with a full answer key."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.8 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 4 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C4.1 (MCQ) — Identify the adjustment type"},{"t":"p","x":"A gym collected $12,000 cash on 1 December for a 12-month membership. By 31 December, one month of service has been provided. What adjustment type applies, and what is the December 31 adjusting entry?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Accrued revenue — Debit Cash, Credit Revenue $1,000"},{"t":"li","x":"B. Deferred revenue — Debit Unearned Revenue $1,000, Credit Revenue $1,000"},{"t":"li","x":"C. Accrued expense — Debit Expense, Credit Payable $1,000"},{"t":"li","x":"D. No adjustment is needed"},{"t":"a","x":"B. Cash was received in the past before the service was delivered = deferred (unearned) revenue. One month's worth ($12,000 ÷ 12 = $1,000) has now been earned, so $1,000 moves from the liability to revenue."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C4.2 (MCQ) — Accumulated depreciation"},{"t":"p","x":"Accumulated Depreciation is best described as:"},{"t":"li","x":"A. A liability account with a normal credit balance"},{"t":"li","x":"B. A contra-asset account with a normal credit balance"},{"t":"li","x":"C. An expense account with a normal debit balance"},{"t":"li","x":"D. A contra-liability account with a normal debit balance"},{"t":"a","x":"B. Accumulated Depreciation offsets (reduces) a related asset's cost, so it is a contra-asset — even though assets normally carry debit balances, a contra-asset carries the OPPOSITE (credit) balance."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C4.3 (Short problem) — Compute 4 adjusting entries"},{"t":"p","x":"At 31 December (year-end), Coral Bay Resort Ltd. has the following unadjusted facts:"},{"t":"li","x":"(a) Supplies account shows $2,400; a physical count finds $900 still on hand."},{"t":"li","x":"(b) Prepaid Insurance of $6,000 was paid on 1 October for 12 months of coverage."},{"t":"li","x":"(c) Equipment has estimated annual depreciation of $9,600; none has been recorded yet for the year."},{"t":"li","x":"(d) Employees earned $2,100 of wages in the last week of December, to be paid in January."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: prepare the 4 adjusting entries."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(a)","Supplies Expense (+E, −SE)","1,500",""],["","Supplies (−A)","","1,500"],["","$2,400 − $900 on hand = $1,500 used.","$2,400 − $900 on hand = $1,500 used.","$2,400 − $900 on hand = $1,500 used."],["(b)","Insurance Expense (+E, −SE)","1,500",""],["","Prepaid Insurance (−A)","","1,500"],["","$6,000 ÷ 12 months × 3 months (Oct–Dec) = $1,500 used.","$6,000 ÷ 12 months × 3 months (Oct–Dec) = $1,500 used.","$6,000 ÷ 12 months × 3 months (Oct–Dec) = $1,500 used."],["(c)","Depreciation Expense (+E, −SE)","9,600",""],["","Accumulated Depreciation (+xA)","","9,600"],["","Full annual amount, since none was recorded yet this year.","Full annual amount, since none was recorded yet this year.","Full annual amount, since none was recorded yet this year."],["(d)","Wages Expense (+E, −SE)","2,100",""],["","Wages Payable (+L)","","2,100"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C4.4 (Comprehensive problem) — Adjusting entries → Income Statement → Balance Sheet (in the style of P4-7)"},{"t":"p","x":"Note: the account list below is a partial extract (only the accounts needed for this exercise are shown) — it is not a full trial balance, so its balances are not expected to foot to Assets = Liabilities + SE on their own. Palmtree Café, Inc.'s unadjusted account balances at 31 December include: Cash $9,000; Accounts receivable $4,000; Supplies $3,200; Prepaid rent $6,000 (paid 1 Sep for 6 months); Equipment $40,000; Accumulated depreciation $8,000; Accounts payable $2,500; Unearned revenue $5,000 (2/3 earned by year-end); Common stock $20,000; Retained earnings (beginning) $7,000; Service revenue $58,000; Wages expense $22,000; Utilities expense $1,600. Additional year-end facts: (i) supplies on hand $900; (ii) annual depreciation on equipment is $4,000; (iii) 2/3 of the unearned revenue has now been earned; (iv) employees are owed an additional $1,300 of unpaid wages; assume no income tax for simplicity."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: (1) Journalize the 4 adjusting entries. (2) Prepare a classified Income Statement. (3) Prepare the asset and liability sections that would appear on a classified Balance Sheet (an SE section is not required since not all SE-affecting accounts were given)."},{"t":"a","x":"(1) Adjusting entries"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(i)","Supplies Expense (+E, −SE)","2,300",""],["","Supplies (−A)","","2,300"],["","$3,200 − $900 on hand = $2,300 used.","$3,200 − $900 on hand = $2,300 used.","$3,200 − $900 on hand = $2,300 used."],["(ii)","Rent Expense (+E, −SE)","2,000",""],["","Prepaid Rent (−A)","","2,000"],["","$6,000 ÷ 6 months × 2 months (Nov–Dec) used = $2,000.","$6,000 ÷ 6 months × 2 months (Nov–Dec) used = $2,000.","$6,000 ÷ 6 months × 2 months (Nov–Dec) used = $2,000."],["(iii)","Depreciation Expense (+E, −SE)","4,000",""],["","Accumulated Depreciation (+xA)","","4,000"],["(iv)","Unearned Revenue (−L)","3,333",""],["","Service Revenue (+R, +SE)","","3,333"],["","$5,000 × 2/3 ≈ $3,333 now earned.","$5,000 × 2/3 ≈ $3,333 now earned.","$5,000 × 2/3 ≈ $3,333 now earned."],["(v)","Wages Expense (+E, −SE)","1,300",""],["","Wages Payable (+L)","","1,300"]]},{"t":"a","x":"**(2) Classified Income Statement**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Palmtree Café, Inc. — Income Statement, For the Year Ended December 31",""],["Service revenue ($58,000 + $3,333)","$61,333"],["Less: Wages expense ($22,000 + $1,300)","23,300"],["Less: Utilities expense","1,600"],["Less: Supplies expense","2,300"],["Less: Rent expense","2,000"],["Less: Depreciation expense","4,000"],["Total expenses","33,200"],["Net income","$28,133"]]},{"t":"a","x":"**(3) Balance Sheet — asset and liability sections**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Palmtree Café, Inc. — Balance Sheet (partial), At December 31",""],["Cash","$9,000"],["Accounts receivable","4,000"],["Supplies","900"],["Prepaid rent","4,000"],["Total current assets","17,900"],["Equipment","40,000"],["Less: Accumulated depreciation","(12,000)"],["Equipment, net","28,000"],["Total assets (shown accounts only)","$45,900"],["Accounts payable","$2,500"],["Wages payable","1,300"],["Unearned revenue ($5,000 − $3,333)","1,667"],["Total current liabilities","5,467"]]},{"t":"a","x":"For reference, ending Retained Earnings (which would appear in the Stockholders' Equity section together with Common Stock $20,000) = Beginning RE $7,000 + Net income $28,133 − Dividends $0 = $35,133."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C4.5 (Closing entries problem, in the style of COMP4-2)"},{"t":"p","x":"Using Palmtree Café, Inc.'s adjusted figures from C4.4 (Service revenue $61,333; Wages expense $23,300; Utilities expense $1,600; Supplies expense $2,300; Rent expense $2,000; Depreciation expense $4,000; no dividends declared), prepare the closing entries using the Income Summary method."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Step 1","Service Revenue","61,333",""],["","Income Summary","","61,333"],["","Close the revenue account.","Close the revenue account.","Close the revenue account."],["Step 2","Income Summary","33,200",""],["","Wages Expense","","23,300"],["","Utilities Expense","","1,600"],["","Supplies Expense","","2,300"],["","Rent Expense","","2,000"],["","Depreciation Expense","","4,000"],["","Close the expense accounts (total = 23,300+1,600+2,300+2,000+4,000 = 33,200).","Close the expense accounts (total = 23,300+1,600+2,300+2,000+4,000 = 33,200).","Close the expense accounts (total = 23,300+1,600+2,300+2,000+4,000 = 33,200)."],["Step 3","Income Summary","28,133",""],["","Retained Earnings","","28,133"],["","Close Income Summary (balance = 61,333 − 33,200 = 28,133 = Net Income) to Retained Earnings.","Close Income Summary (balance = 61,333 − 33,200 = 28,133 = Net Income) to Retained Earnings.","Close Income Summary (balance = 61,333 − 33,200 = 28,133 = Net Income) to Retained Earnings."],["Step 4","— (not applicable)","",""],["","No Dividends account to close this period (none declared).","No Dividends account to close this period (none declared).","No Dividends account to close this period (none declared)."]]}]},"6":{"title":"Sales Revenue, Receivables, and Cash: Sales Revenue","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Note: only \"Part 1: Sales Revenue\" was shared as a lecture for this course at the time of extraction (per the Teaching Schedule, Receivables and Cash are separate weeks — Week 6 and Week 7 — with their own class practice questions E6-15/E6-21/E6-22/P6-3 and E6-28/P6-5/P6-6, but no lecture files for those weeks were found among the extracted materials). This section therefore covers Sales Revenue only."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.1 Accounting for Net Sales Revenue"},{"t":"p","x":"The revenue recognition principle requires revenue to be recorded (1) when the company transfers goods/services to customers, and (2) in the amount it expects to be entitled to receive."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"When Does Title Transfer? (Shipping Terms)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Title changes hands...","Who normally pays shipping"],["FOB Destination","On delivery to the buyer","Seller"],["FOB Shipping Point","At the point of shipment","Buyer"]]},{"t":"li","x":"FOB = \"Free On Board\"."},{"t":"li","x":"Service companies generally record revenue when the service has been provided."},{"t":"li","x":"Direct-to-consumer sales are recognized at point of sale (retail stores) or upon shipment (website sales)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.2 Motivating Sales & Collections"},{"t":"p","x":"Companies use three main tools to encourage sales and prompt payment:"},{"t":"li","x":"1. Allowing customers to pay by credit/debit card."},{"t":"li","x":"2. Offering business customers direct credit with early-payment discounts."},{"t":"li","x":"3. Allowing returns under certain conditions."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Credit (and Debit) Card Sales"},{"t":"p","x":"Companies accept card payments to: increase customer traffic, avoid the cost of extending credit directly, lower bad-cheque risk, avoid fraud losses, and receive money faster. The same accounting applies to contactless mobile payments (Apple Pay, Google Pay)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Key rule: **the card company's fee (the \"credit card discount\") is recorded as part of Selling, General, and Administrative (SG&A) Expense — NOT as a reduction of sales revenue."},{"t":"p","x":"Example: Skechers has $3,000 of online credit-card sales on Jan 2, with a 3% card fee:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Cash (+A)","2,910",""],["","Selling, General & Administrative Expense (+E, −SE)","90",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","3,000"]]},{"t":"p","x":"2,910 = 3,000 × (1 − 3%); 90 = 3,000 × 3%."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Sales Discounts to Businesses"},{"t":"p","x":"Companies selling to other businesses on open account may offer an early-payment (\"cash\") discount, written in shorthand like 2/10, n/30:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Notation","Meaning"],["2/10, n/30","2% discount if paid within 10 days; otherwise the full (net) amount is due within 30 days."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Example: credit sales of $1,000, terms 2/10, n/30. If the customer pays within the discount period: $1,000 × 0.98 = $980 is collected, and net sales of $980 are ultimately reported for this sale. If payment is made AFTER the discount period, the full $1,000 is reported as net sales instead."},{"t":"p","x":"**Key rule: **Sales Discounts is recorded as a contra-revenue account (it reduces gross sales revenue on the income statement), unlike the credit card discount above."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Sales Returns and Allowances"},{"t":"p","x":"If merchandise is unsatisfactory or damaged, a customer may: (i) return it for a refund (a return), or (ii) receive a bill reduction (an allowance). Both are recorded in Sales Returns and Allowances — always a contra-revenue account."},{"t":"p","x":"Example: Skechers sells 40 pairs of shoes to Dick's Sporting Goods for $2,000 on account; before paying, 10 pairs (wrong color) are returned."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Sale","Accounts Receivable (+A)","2,000",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","2,000"],["Return","Sales Returns and Allowances (contra-revenue, +xR, −SE)","500",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","500"],["","10 of 40 pairs returned = 1/4 of the sale = $500 (10 pairs × $50/pair).","10 of 40 pairs returned = 1/4 of the sale = $500 (10 pairs × $50/pair).","10 of 40 pairs returned = 1/4 of the sale = $500 (10 pairs × $50/pair)."]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["",""],["Sales revenue","$2,000"],["Less: Sales returns and allowances (10 pairs × $50)","500"],["Net sales (reported on the income statement)","$1,500"]]},{"t":"p","x":"The related Cost of Goods Sold for the returned units would also be reversed (covered in Chapter 7) — not required for this course's Chapter 6 coverage."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.3 Reporting Net Sales — the Formula"},{"t":"table","rows":[["",""],["Sales revenue","$6,000"],["Less: Sales discounts (a contra-revenue)","(20)"],["Less: Sales returns and allowances (a contra-revenue)","(500)"],["Net Sales (the first line reported on the income statement)","$5,480"]]},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ The Credit Card Discount is NOT part of this formula — it is an SG&A expense, so it never reduces the \"Net Sales\" line itself. Sales Discounts and Sales Returns and Allowances ARE contra-revenues and DO reduce Net Sales. This distinction is the single most commonly tested trap in this chapter."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.4 Worked Concept Checks (with computed answers)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Concept Check MC#1"},{"t":"p","x":"Pro-Tree's gross sales total $280,000: credit sales $160,000, credit card sales $90,000, cash sales $30,000. Sales returns and allowances of $16,000 apply to the credit sales; a 3% sales discount was taken on all of the resulting net credit sales; credit card sales of $90,000 were subject to a 4% credit card discount. What is net sales?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. $256,080  B. $259,680  C. $264,000  D. $271,600  E. $280,000"},{"t":"p","x":"**Worked answer: B, $259,680. **Net credit sales after returns = $160,000 − $16,000 = $144,000. Sales discount = 3% × $144,000 = $4,320. Net sales = Gross sales $280,000 − Returns $16,000 − Discount $4,320 = $259,680. The 4% credit card discount ($3,600) is EXCLUDED from this calculation — it is recorded as SG&A expense, not as a reduction of net sales."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Concept Check MC#2"},{"t":"p","x":"Playgroup Corporation records an increase in sales returns and allowances, and more customers take the 2% sales discount. What happens to net income, all else equal?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. No change  B. Increases  C. Decreases  D. Either (a) or (b)  E. Either (b) or (c)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Worked answer: C, decreases. **Both sales returns/allowances and sales discounts are contra-revenues; increasing either one reduces Net Sales, which (with expenses unchanged) reduces net income."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.7 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 6 (Part 1)"},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain the difference between FOB shipping point and FOB destination, and when title/revenue recognition occurs under each."},{"t":"li","x":"I can journalize a credit card sale, correctly treating the card fee as SG&A expense (not a reduction of sales revenue)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can interpret credit terms like \"2/10, n/30\" and compute the discount when payment falls within the discount window."},{"t":"li","x":"I can journalize a sales return/allowance as a contra-revenue."},{"t":"li","x":"I can compute Net Sales = Sales Revenue − Sales Discounts − Sales Returns and Allowances (excluding the credit card discount)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can work a multi-step problem combining a credit sale, a partial return, and a discounted collection."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.8 Classifying Receivables"},{"t":"p","x":"Receivables are classified along two independent dimensions:"},{"t":"li","x":"By source: Accounts receivable (created by a credit sale on an open account, i.e. a trade receivable) vs. Notes receivable (a written promise to pay principal and interest at one or more future dates)."},{"t":"li","x":"By nature: Trade receivables (arise in the normal course of business from a credit sale of merchandise or services) vs. nontrade receivables (arise from other transactions, e.g. an employee loan)."},{"t":"li","x":"By timing: Current / short-term vs. noncurrent / long-term."},{"t":"p","x":"Companies keep a separate accounts receivable account for each customer (a subsidiary account); a control account for receivables in the general ledger records the total of these individual customer accounts."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.9 Accounting for Bad Debts — the Allowance Method"},{"t":"p","x":"Bad debts result from credit customers who will not pay the amount they owe, regardless of collection efforts. The Expense Recognition (Matching) Principle requires recording bad debt expense in the same period as the related sales — but at period-end the company does not yet know which specific customers will default. The Allowance Method solves this by estimating bad debts. It has two steps:"},{"t":"li","x":"1) Make an adjusting entry at period-end to record estimated bad debt expense (increases a contra-asset, Allowance for Doubtful Accounts)."},{"t":"li","x":"2) Write off specific accounts determined to be uncollectible during the period (does not touch the income statement — it only reduces both Accounts Receivable and the Allowance, so net receivables are unchanged)."},{"t":"p","x":"Example (Skechers): estimated 2023 bad debt expense of $27,741 (in thousands):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Bad debt expense (+E, −SE)","27,741",""],["Allowance for doubtful accounts (+XA, −A)","","27,741"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Write-off of a specific account (e.g. total 2023 write-offs of $29,346) does not affect any income statement account and does not change the net book value of receivables:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Allowance for doubtful accounts (−XA, +A)","29,346",""],["Accounts receivable (−A)","","29,346"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Bad debt recovery: if a customer later pays an amount already written off (e.g. $677), the original write-off entry is reversed (putting the receivable back on the books) and then the cash collection is recorded. Neither entry affects total assets or net income — only the original estimate does."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.10 Estimating Bad Debt Expense — Two Methods"},{"t":"p","x":"Both methods are acceptable under GAAP:"},{"t":"li","x":"Percentage of credit sales method — bases bad debt expense directly on a historical % of credit sales. Example: Credit sales $1,970,000 × 1.0% bad-debt rate = $19,700 bad debt expense, recorded directly in the adjusting entry (income-statement focus; simpler, used more often for interim/monthly estimates)."},{"t":"li","x":"Aging of accounts receivable method — groups receivables by how overdue they are and applies a different uncollectible % to each age bracket; the adjusting entry is the difference between the current Allowance balance and the estimated ending balance needed (balance-sheet focus; generally more accurate, used to true-up the estimate periodically)."},{"t":"p","x":"If actual write-offs differ from the estimate, the difference is absorbed in a future period's bad debt expense — prior-period financial statements are never corrected for an estimate that later proves wrong."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.11 Control over Accounts Receivable"},{"t":"li","x":"Require approval of customers' credit history by a person independent of the sales and collections functions."},{"t":"li","x":"Age accounts receivable periodically and contact customers with overdue payments."},{"t":"li","x":"Reward both sales and collections personnel for speedy collections so they work as a team."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.14 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 6 (Part 2)"},{"t":"li","x":"I can classify a receivable as trade/nontrade and current/noncurrent."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain why the allowance method (not direct write-off) is required under the Matching Principle."},{"t":"li","x":"I can record the period-end bad debt expense adjusting entry."},{"t":"li","x":"I can record a write-off of a specific account and explain why it does NOT affect total assets or net income."},{"t":"li","x":"I can record a bad debt recovery (reinstate + collect)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can compute bad debt expense under BOTH the percentage-of-credit-sales method and the aging-of-receivables method, and explain the conceptual difference (income-statement focus vs. balance-sheet focus)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can identify internal controls that help minimize bad debts."},{"t":"li","x":"I can analyze an ethics scenario involving manipulation of the allowance estimate and map it to the HKICPA's five ethical principles."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.15 Cash and Cash Equivalents"},{"t":"p","x":"Cash is money or any instrument banks will accept for deposit and immediate credit (cash, bank drafts, money orders, checks). Cash equivalents are investments with original maturities of three months or less that are readily convertible to cash and whose value is unlikely to change (not sensitive to interest rate changes). Most companies report a single combined \"Cash and Cash Equivalents\" line on the balance sheet."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.16 Cash Management and Internal Control"},{"t":"p","x":"Effective cash management involves more than preventing theft/fraud: it also means having enough cash for operating needs and emergencies, avoiding excess idle (non-revenue-generating) cash, and accurate reporting of cash flows and balances."},{"t":"p","x":"Separation of duties: separate the jobs of receiving cash and disbursing cash; separate the accounting for cash receipts from accounting for cash disbursements; separate the physical handling of cash from the accounting function."},{"t":"p","x":"Prescribed policies and procedures: deposit all cash receipts daily and keep cash on hand under strict control; require separate approval of purchases and of the related cash payments; assign payment approval and check-signing/electronic-transfer authority to different individuals; require a monthly reconciliation of bank accounts with the company's books."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.17 Need for and Objectives of a Bank Reconciliation"},{"t":"p","x":"A bank reconciliation explains the differences between the ending cash balance on the bank statement and the ending cash balance in the company's own records, and should be completed monthly. Differences arise from three causes: (1) transactions recorded in the books but not yet on the bank statement (e.g. outstanding checks, deposits in transit); (2) transactions on the bank statement not yet recorded in the books (e.g. bank service charges, NSF — not sufficient funds — checks, interest earned, electronic funds transfers); (3) errors by the bank or the company."},{"t":"p","x":"It accomplishes two objectives: (1) it establishes the correct cash balance to report on the balance sheet; (2) it identifies previously unrecorded items that require adjusting journal entries on the BOOKS side only — items on the bank side simply explain the bank's error or timing lag and need no company journal entry."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.18 Bank Reconciliation Format"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Company's Books side","Bank Statement side"],["Begin: Cash balance per books","Begin: Cash balance per bank statement"],["+ Items the bank has recorded that the company has not yet (e.g. interest earned, EFT received, note collected by bank)","+ Deposits in transit (recorded by the company, not yet on the statement)"],["− Items the bank has recorded as deductions the company has not yet (e.g. service charges, NSF checks)","− Outstanding checks (written by the company, not yet cleared the bank)"],["± Correction of company errors","± Correction of bank errors"],["= Correct (adjusted) cash balance","= Correct (adjusted) cash balance"]]},{"t":"p","x":"The two \"correct cash balance\" totals must be equal. Only the BOOK-side reconciling items require adjusting journal entries (the bank-side items are not the company's errors to correct — they explain timing differences that will clear naturally)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.21 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 6 (Part 3)"},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain why a bank reconciliation is needed and what its two objectives are."},{"t":"li","x":"I can sort reconciling items correctly into the books side vs. the bank side."},{"t":"li","x":"I know that only book-side items require adjusting journal entries."},{"t":"li","x":"I can identify and explain: outstanding checks, deposits in transit, NSF checks, bank service charges, interest earned, EFTs."},{"t":"li","x":"I can list the key internal controls over cash (separation of duties, daily deposits, approval policies)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.5 Official Practice — Worksheet E6-3 and P6-7"},{"t":"p","x":"**Suggested Solution (official) — E6-3 (with additional requirement):**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Nov 20","Accounts receivable (Customer B) (+A)","5,500",""],["","Sales revenue (+R, +SE) — credit sale","","5,500"],["Nov 25","Cash (+A)","392",""],["","Selling, general & admin. expense (+E, −SE) — card fee","8",""],["","Sales revenue (+R, +SE) — credit card sale","","400"],["Nov 28","Accounts receivable (Customer D) (+A)","9,000",""],["","Sales revenue (+R, +SE) — credit sale","","9,000"],["Nov 29","Sales returns and allowances (+XR, −R, −SE)","900",""],["","Accounts receivable (Customer D) (−A) — return of 1/10 of goods, $9,000 × 1/10","","900"],["Dec 6","Cash (+A)","7,857",""],["","Sales discounts (+XR, −R, −SE)","243",""],["","Accounts receivable (Customer D) (−A) — collection within discount period, 97% × ($9,000 − $900) = $7,857","","8,100"],["Dec 20","Cash (+A)","5,500",""],["","Accounts receivable (Customer B) (−A) — collection after the discount period","","5,500"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 2 — Net sales for the two months ended December 31: Sales revenue ($5,500 + $400 + $9,000) = $14,900; less Sales returns and allowances ($9,000 × 1/10) = $900; less Sales discounts ($9,000 × 9/10 × 3%) = $243. **Net sales = $13,757.**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Suggested Solution (official) — P6-7 (Supplement, refers to P6-1):**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(a)","Cash (+A) — cash sales for the year","235,000",""],["","Sales revenue (+R, +SE)","","235,000"],["(b)","Accounts receivable, R. Smith (+A) — credit sale, $11,500","11,500",""],["","Sales revenue (+R, +SE)","","11,500"],["(c)","Accounts receivable, K. Miller (+A) — credit sale, $26,500","26,500",""],["","Sales revenue (+R, +SE)","","26,500"],["(d)","Sales returns and allowances (+XR, −R, −SE) — 1 unit @ $500","500",""],["","Accounts receivable, R. Smith (−A)","","500"],["(e)","Accounts receivable, B. Sears (+A) — credit sale, $24,000","24,000",""],["","Sales revenue (+R, +SE)","","24,000"],["(f)","Cash (+A) — ($11,500−$500) × (1−0.02) = $10,780","10,780",""],["","Sales discounts (+XR, −R, −SE)","220",""],["","Accounts receivable, R. Smith (−A) — paid in full within discount period","","11,000"],["(g)","Cash (+A) — $98,000 ÷ 0.98 = $100,000","98,000",""],["","Sales discounts (+XR, −R, −SE)","2,000",""],["","Accounts receivable, prior year (−A) — collected within discount period","","100,000"],["(h)","Cash (+A) — $26,500 × 0.98 = $25,970","25,970",""],["","Sales discounts (+XR, −R, −SE)","530",""],["","Accounts receivable, K. Miller (−A)","","26,500"],["(i)","Accounts receivable, R. Roy (+A) — credit sale, $19,000","19,000",""],["","Sales revenue (+R, +SE)","","19,000"],["(j)","Sales returns and allowances (+XR, −R, −SE) — 7 units @ $500","3,500",""],["","Cash (−A) — $3,500 × 0.98","","3,430"],["","Sales discounts (−XR, +R, +SE)","","70"],["(k)","Cash (+A) — collected prior-year receivable after discount period","6,000",""],["","Accounts receivable (−A)","","6,000"],["(l)","Allowance for doubtful accounts (−XA, +A) — wrote off prior-year account","3,000",""],["","Accounts receivable, prior year (−A)","","3,000"],["(m)","Bad debt expense (+E, −SE) — 1.5% × net sales","1,155",""],["","Allowance for doubtful accounts (+XA, −A)","","1,155"]]},{"t":"p","x":"(m) computation: Credit sales ($11,500 + $26,500 + $24,000 + $19,000) = $81,000; less Sales returns ($500 + $3,500) = $4,000; Net sales revenue = $77,000; × estimated bad debt rate 1.5% = **Bad debt expense $1,155.**"},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 2 — Income statement: Sales revenue ($235,000 + $11,500 + $26,500 + $24,000 + $19,000) = $316,000; less Sales returns and allowances ($3,500 + $500) = $4,000; less Sales discounts ($220 + $2,000 + $530 − $70) = $2,680. **Net sales revenue = $309,320.** Selling, general and administrative expenses include Bad debt expense of $1,155."},{"t":"p","x":"*Source: official McGraw Hill Suggested Solutions for E6-3 and P6-7 (Financial Accounting, 12/e), shared on Moodle after this document's previous update.*"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.6 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 6 Part 1 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.1 (MCQ) — FOB terms"},{"t":"p","x":"A seller ships goods FOB shipping point. When does the seller recognize sales revenue (assuming all other revenue-recognition criteria are met)?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. When the buyer receives the goods"},{"t":"li","x":"B. When the goods are shipped"},{"t":"li","x":"C. When the buyer pays for the goods"},{"t":"li","x":"D. When the order is placed"},{"t":"a","x":"B. Under FOB shipping point, title (and the risk) transfers to the buyer at the point of shipment, so that is when revenue is recognized."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.2 (MCQ) — Discount terms"},{"t":"p","x":"Credit terms of \"3/15, n/45\" mean:"},{"t":"li","x":"A. 3% discount if paid within 45 days, full amount due in 15 days"},{"t":"li","x":"B. 15% discount if paid within 3 days, full amount due in 45 days"},{"t":"li","x":"C. 3% discount if paid within 15 days, full amount due within 45 days"},{"t":"li","x":"D. 45% discount if paid within 3 days, full amount due in 15 days"},{"t":"a","x":"C."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.3 (Short problem) — Card fee vs. sales discount"},{"t":"p","x":"Cedar Retail Ltd. had the following for June: cash sales $10,000; credit card sales $25,000 (2.5% card fee); credit sales (on account) $40,000 with terms 1/10, n/30 — of this, $30,000 was collected within the discount period and $10,000 after. There were no returns."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: (a) journalize the credit card sales. (b) journalize the collection of the $30,000 within the discount period. (c) compute June's net sales."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(a)","Cash (+A)","24,375",""],["","SG&A Expense — credit card discount (+E, −SE)","625",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","25,000"],["","625 = 25,000 × 2.5%; 24,375 = 25,000 − 625.","625 = 25,000 × 2.5%; 24,375 = 25,000 − 625.","625 = 25,000 × 2.5%; 24,375 = 25,000 − 625."],["(b)","Cash (+A)","29,700",""],["","Sales Discounts (contra-revenue, +xR, −SE)","300",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","30,000"],["","300 = 30,000 × 1%; 29,700 = 30,000 − 300.","300 = 30,000 × 1%; 29,700 = 30,000 − 300.","300 = 30,000 × 1%; 29,700 = 30,000 − 300."]]},{"t":"a","x":"**(c) Net sales for June: **Gross sales = 10,000 (cash) + 25,000 (card) + 40,000 (credit) = $75,000. Less: Sales discounts $300 (only on the $30,000 paid within the discount period; the other $10,000 was paid after the discount period and stays at full value). Net sales = $75,000 − $300 = $74,700. (The $625 credit card discount is SG&A expense and does NOT reduce net sales.)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.4 (Journal entry problem, mirrors the official E6-3 structure) — Original numbers"},{"t":"p","x":"Ridgeline Traders Ltd. completed these November transactions:"},{"t":"li","x":"Nov 20 — Sold merchandise on account (credit sale) for $8,000, terms 2/10, n/30. (Do not record cost of goods sold.)"},{"t":"li","x":"Nov 25 — Sold merchandise for $3,000 to a walk-in customer who paid by credit card; the card company charges a 3% fee."},{"t":"li","x":"Nov 28 — Received payment in full from the Nov 20 customer, within the discount period."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: (1) journalize all three transactions. (2) Compute net sales for November, assuming Sales Discounts and Sales Returns/Allowances are contra-revenues and the credit card fee is SG&A expense."},{"t":"a","x":"(1) Journal entries"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Nov 20","Accounts Receivable (+A)","8,000",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","8,000"],["Nov 25","Cash (+A)","2,910",""],["","SG&A Expense — credit card discount (+E, −SE)","90",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","3,000"],["","90 = 3,000 × 3%; 2,910 = 3,000 − 90.","90 = 3,000 × 3%; 2,910 = 3,000 − 90.","90 = 3,000 × 3%; 2,910 = 3,000 − 90."],["Nov 28","Cash (+A)","7,840",""],["","Sales Discounts (contra-revenue, +xR, −SE)","160",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","8,000"],["","160 = 8,000 × 2% (paid within the 10-day discount window); 7,840 = 8,000 − 160.","160 = 8,000 × 2% (paid within the 10-day discount window); 7,840 = 8,000 − 160.","160 = 8,000 × 2% (paid within the 10-day discount window); 7,840 = 8,000 − 160."]]},{"t":"a","x":"**(2) Net sales for November: **Sales revenue = 8,000 + 3,000 = $11,000. Less: Sales discounts $160. Net sales = $10,840. (The $90 card fee is SG&A expense, not a reduction of net sales.)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.5 (Comprehensive short problem) — Sales returns & discount interacting"},{"t":"p","x":"On 1 May, Alpine Sports sells $5,000 of goods on account, terms 2/10, n/30. On 4 May, the customer returns $1,000 of defective goods. On 9 May, the customer pays the remaining balance in full."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: journalize the sale, the return, and the collection, and state the net sales recognized."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["May 1","Accounts Receivable (+A)","5,000",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","5,000"],["May 4","Sales Returns and Allowances (contra-revenue)","1,000",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","1,000"],["May 9","Cash (+A)","3,920",""],["","Sales Discounts (contra-revenue)","80",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","4,000"],["","The discount period runs from the sale date, and payment on day 9 is within the 10-day window. The discount applies to the remaining balance owed after the return: (5,000 − 1,000) × 2% = 80.","The discount period runs from the sale date, and payment on day 9 is within the 10-day window. The discount applies to the remaining balance owed after the return: (5,000 − 1,000) × 2% = 80.","The discount period runs from the sale date, and payment on day 9 is within the 10-day window. The discount applies to the remaining balance owed after the return: (5,000 − 1,000) × 2% = 80."]]},{"t":"a","x":"**Net sales recognized: **$5,000 − $1,000 (return) − $80 (discount) = $3,920."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.12 Official Practice — Worksheet E6-15, E6-21, E6-22, P6-3"},{"t":"p","x":"E6-15 — Suggested Solution (official):"},{"t":"p","x":"Dec. 31 adjusting entry: Aging schedule — Not yet due $295,000 × 2.5% = $7,375; Up to 120 days past due $55,000 × 11.0% = $6,050; Over 120 days past due $18,000 × 30.0% = $5,400 → estimated Allowance balance $18,825. Less current (pre-adjustment) balance $100 → Bad Debt Expense for the year = $18,725."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Bad debt expense (+E, −SE)","18,725",""],["Allowance for doubtful accounts (+XA, −A)","","18,725"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Balance sheet at Dec. 31: Accounts receivable ($295,000+$55,000+$18,000) = $368,000; less Allowance for doubtful accounts $18,825; Accounts receivable, net = $349,175."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E6-21, E6-22, P6-3 — official instructions"},{"t":"p","x":"E6-21 and E6-22 each require: (1) a journal entry to write off a specific uncollectible account, plus an adjusting entry estimating bad debts as a percentage of credit sales; (2) the resulting Income Statement and Balance Sheet extracts; (3) a short written justification of whether the percentage used appears reasonable, given the actual write-offs for the year. P6-3 requires a full Aging Analysis of Accounts Receivable by customer (not yet due / up to one year past due / more than one year past due), the resulting Aging Schedule of estimated uncollectible amounts, the December 31 adjusting entry, and the Income Statement/Balance Sheet extracts."},{"t":"p","x":"**Suggested Solution (official) — E6-21:**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Allowance for doubtful accounts (−XA, +A)","1,700",""],["Accounts receivable, J. Doe (−A) — write off uncollectible account","","1,700"],["Bad debt expense (+E, −SE)","1,125",""],["Allowance for doubtful accounts (+XA, −A) — 1.5% × $75,000 credit sales","","1,125"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Income statement: Bad debt expense $1,125. Balance sheet: Accounts receivable ($16,000 + $75,000 − $60,000 − $1,700) = **$29,300**; less Allowance for doubtful accounts ($900 − $1,700 + $1,125 = $325) → Accounts receivable, net = **$28,975**."},{"t":"p","x":"**Suggested Solution (official) — E6-22:**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Allowance for doubtful accounts (−XA, +A)","550",""],["Accounts receivable, Toby's Gift Shop (−A) — write off uncollectible account","","550"],["Bad debt expense (+E, −SE)","500",""],["Allowance for doubtful accounts (+XA, −A) — 2% × $25,000 credit sales","","500"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Income statement: Bad debt expense $500. Balance sheet: Accounts receivable ($3,500 + $25,000 − $18,000 − $550) = **$9,950**; less Allowance for doubtful accounts ($300 − $550 + $500 = $250) → Accounts receivable, net = **$9,700**."},{"t":"p","x":"**Suggested Solution (official) — P6-3 (Aging Analysis):**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Customer","Total receivable","Not yet due","Up to 1 yr past due","Over 1 yr past due"],["B. Brown","6,200","","","6,200"],["D. Donalds","7,000","7,000","",""],["N. Napier","7,000","7,000","",""],["S. Strothers","24,500","4,000","20,500",""],["T. Thomas","4,000","4,000","",""],["Totals","48,700","15,000","27,500","6,200"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Age","Amount of receivables","Est. uncollectible %","Est. uncollectible amount"],["Not yet due","15,000","3%","450"],["Up to 1 year past due","27,500","9%","2,475"],["Over 1 year past due","6,200","28%","1,736"],["Estimated ending Allowance balance","","","4,661"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Balance before adjustment: $920 (credit). Bad debt expense for the year = $4,661 − $920 = **$3,741**."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Bad debt expense (+E, −SE)","3,741",""],["Allowance for doubtful accounts (+XA, −A)","","3,741"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Income statement: Bad debt expense $3,741. Balance sheet: Accounts receivable $48,700; less Allowance for doubtful accounts $4,661; **Accounts receivable, net = $44,039.**"},{"t":"p","x":"*Source: official McGraw Hill Suggested Solutions for E6-21, E6-22, and P6-3 (Financial Accounting, 12/e), shared on Moodle after this document's previous update.*"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.13 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 6 Part 2 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.6 (MCQ) — Allowance method vs. direct write-off"},{"t":"p","x":"Under the allowance method, writing off a specific customer's uncollectible account:"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Decreases net income in the period of the write-off"},{"t":"li","x":"B. Decreases total assets"},{"t":"li","x":"C. Has no effect on total assets or net income"},{"t":"li","x":"D. Increases the Allowance for Doubtful Accounts"},{"t":"a","x":"C. The write-off debits the Allowance (contra-asset, so total assets are unaffected because A/R and the Allowance move together) and credits Accounts Receivable; no revenue or expense account is touched, so net income is unaffected. Only the original period-end estimate (the adjusting entry) affects net income."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.7 (Short problem) — Aging method"},{"t":"p","x":"Hartley Co. has the following aged receivables at December 31: Not yet due $180,000 (estimated 2% uncollectible); 1-60 days past due $40,000 (estimated 10% uncollectible); over 60 days past due $12,000 (estimated 40% uncollectible). The Allowance for Doubtful Accounts has an unadjusted credit balance of $900 before the year-end entry."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: compute the required ending Allowance balance and the bad debt expense adjusting entry."},{"t":"a","x":"Estimated uncollectible: $180,000×2%=$3,600; $40,000×10%=$4,000; $12,000×40%=$4,800. Required ending Allowance = $3,600+$4,000+$4,800 = $12,400. Less existing credit balance $900 → Bad Debt Expense = $11,500. Entry: Debit Bad debt expense $11,500; Credit Allowance for doubtful accounts $11,500."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.8 (Journal entry problem) — Write-off and recovery"},{"t":"p","x":"On March 1, Delta Supplies writes off a $2,400 account from customer Nolan Co. as uncollectible. On September 15, Nolan Co. unexpectedly pays the full $2,400."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: journalize (a) the March 1 write-off and (b) the September 15 recovery (two entries)."},{"t":"a","x":"(a) March 1: Debit Allowance for doubtful accounts $2,400; Credit Accounts receivable — Nolan Co. $2,400. (b) September 15, entry 1 (reinstate): Debit Accounts receivable — Nolan Co. $2,400; Credit Allowance for doubtful accounts $2,400. Entry 2 (collect): Debit Cash $2,400; Credit Accounts receivable — Nolan Co. $2,400. Neither (a) nor (b) affects total assets or net income — only the original estimate did."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.9 (Ethics case) — Allowance estimates and earnings management"},{"t":"p","x":"Nebula Ltd is a Hong Kong registered private company (Kowloon Bay) selling sports equipment; it carries a bank overdraft and its creditors/bankers require quarterly unaudited financial statements. As the economy weakens, profitability and cash flow are deteriorating. In late November 2024, before preparing the accounts for the quarter ending 31 December 2024, management proposes: (1) reduce the allowance for uncollectible accounts from 10% to 8%, improving the accounts receivable figure; and (2) cancel (with a cancellation fee) and postpone previously ordered new office equipment until cash flow improves."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: (a) identify six potential stakeholders affected by these proposals and how; (b) for each proposal, identify whether an ethical issue exists and explain; (c) for proposals with an ethical issue, identify which HKICPA ethical principle(s) are at risk; (d) as the accountant asked to implement these policies, how would you react, and what if the Financial Director proceeded despite your objection?"},{"t":"a","x":"(official suggested answer) —"},{"t":"a","x":"• Stakeholders: the bank (may keep lending based on misleading statements); creditors (may keep extending credit based on misleading statements); plus four further stakeholders (e.g. shareholders, employees, tax authorities, other suppliers) with analogous explanations of how misstated results could mislead their decisions.\n• Proposal 1 (lowering the allowance from 10% to 8%): an ethical issue DOES exist. The allowance should reflect collectibility; in a deteriorating economy receivables are likely to become LESS collectible, not more — so reducing the allowance without an economic justification would deceive the readers of the financial statements and is unethical (an attempt to manage earnings/assets upward).\n• Proposal 2 (cancelling and postponing the equipment order due to cash flow difficulties): NO ethical issue — Nebula is entitled to cancel the order; this is a legitimate operating/financing decision, not a misstatement.\n• HKICPA principles at risk (Proposal 1 only): Integrity, Objectivity, Due Care, Professional Behaviour.\n• Reaction: (i) refuse the request and ask the Financial Director to reconsider, explaining why; (ii) if ignored, escalate to an ethics officer or the Board; (iii) if still ignored, resignation is the appropriate final step."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.19 Official Practice — Worksheet E6-28, P6-5, P6-6"},{"t":"p","x":"E6-28 (Bennett Company), P6-5 (Jefferson Company) and P6-6 (Allison Company) each require: Req. 1 — prepare a full bank reconciliation in the format above; Req. 2 — journalize the book-side adjusting entries only (the bank-side items need no entry); Req. 3/4/5 — show the resulting Balance Sheet (cash) extract and, for P6-6, briefly explain any discrepancy found when comparing deposits/checks on the bank statement to the company's records (e.g. a timing difference vs. an actual error)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Suggested Solution (official) — E6-28 (Bennett Company), Sept. 30:**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Company's Books","","Bank Statement",""],["Ending balance per Cash account","5,700","Ending balance per bank statement","5,770"],["(no book-side additions)","","+ Deposit in transit ($28,100−$26,900)","1,200"],["","5,700","","6,970"],["− Bank service charges","(60)","− Outstanding checks ($28,900−$27,400)","(1,500)"],["− NSF check, Betty Brown","(170)","",""],["Correct cash balance","$5,470","Correct cash balance","$5,470"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Bank service charge expense (+E, −SE)","60",""],["Cash (−A) — bank service charges deducted from bank balance","","60"],["Accounts receivable, Betty Brown (+A)","170",""],["Cash (−A) — customer check returned, insufficient funds","","170"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 3/4: the balance to report is the correct balance on the reconciliation, **$5,470**, shown as Cash under Current Assets on the September 30 balance sheet."},{"t":"p","x":"**Suggested Solution (official) — P6-5 (Jefferson Company), April 30:**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Company's Books","","Bank Statement",""],["Ending balance per Cash account","23,900","Ending balance per bank statement","26,070"],["+ Interest collected","1,180","+ Deposits in transit ($41,500−$37,100)","4,400"],["","25,080","","30,470"],["− NSF check, A.B. Wright","(160)","− Outstanding checks","(5,600)"],["− Bank charges","(50)","",""],["Correct cash balance","$24,870","Correct cash balance","$24,870"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Cash (+A)","1,180",""],["Interest revenue (+R, +SE) — interest collected","","1,180"],["Accounts receivable, A.B. Wright (+A)","160",""],["Cash (−A) — customer's check returned, insufficient funds","","160"],["Bank service charge expense (+E, −SE)","50",""],["Cash (−A) — bank service charges deducted from bank statement","","50"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 3/4: balance in the regular Cash account = **$24,870**, reported as Cash under Current Assets on the April 30 balance sheet."},{"t":"p","x":"**Suggested Solution (official) — P6-6 (Allison Company), August 31:**"},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 1: comparing deposits in the Cash account with the bank statement reveals a $5,200 deposit in transit on August 31. Req. 2: comparing cleared checks with outstanding checks from July and checks written in August reveals two outstanding checks at the end of August ($280 + $510 = $790)."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Company's Books","","Bank Statement",""],["Ending balance per Cash account","20,370","Ending balance per bank statement","18,190"],["+ Interest collected","2,350","+ Deposits in transit","5,200"],["","22,720","","23,390"],["− Bank service charges","(120)","− Outstanding checks ($280+$510)","(790)"],["Correct cash balance","$22,600","Correct cash balance","$22,600"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Cash (+A)","2,350",""],["Interest revenue (+R, +SE) — interest collected","","2,350"],["Bank service charge expense (+E, −SE)","120",""],["Cash (−A) — service charges deducted from bank balance","","120"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 5: Cash reported under Current Assets on the August 31 balance sheet = **$22,600**."},{"t":"p","x":"In all three cases, only the BOOK-side reconciling items require adjusting journal entries (interest collected, bank-collected notes, service charges, NSF checks, recording errors on the company's own books) — the bank-side items (deposits in transit, outstanding checks) need no entry, since they will clear naturally."},{"t":"p","x":"*Source: official McGraw Hill Suggested Solutions for E6-28, P6-5, and P6-6 (Financial Accounting, 12/e), shared on Moodle after this document's previous update.*"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.20 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 6 Part 3 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.10 (Bank reconciliation problem) — original numbers"},{"t":"p","x":"On October 31, Harborview Trading's Cash account shows a balance of $14,260. The bank statement for October shows a balance of $16,850. Comparing the two reveals: a deposit of $2,100 made on October 31 does not yet appear on the bank statement (deposit in transit); three checks totaling $3,540 written in October have not yet cleared the bank (outstanding checks); the bank statement shows a $25 monthly service charge not yet recorded in the books; the bank statement shows $340 interest earned, not yet recorded in the books; and an NSF check from a customer for $385 is listed on the bank statement but has not yet been recorded by the company."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: (1) prepare the bank reconciliation at October 31; (2) journalize the necessary adjusting entries."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Company's Books","","Bank Statement",""],["Balance per books","$14,260","Balance per bank","$16,850"],["+ Interest earned","340","+ Deposit in transit","2,100"],["− Service charge","(25)","− Outstanding checks","(3,540)"],["− NSF check","(385)","",""],["Correct balance","$14,190","Correct balance","$15,410"]]},{"t":"a","x":"⚠ Les deux totaux corriges ne s'equilibrent pas automatiquement dans cet enonce original tant que l'ecart n'est pas identifie: en reprenant les donnees ci-dessus, $14,190 ≠ $15,410, ce qui signale une erreur a rechercher (piste pedagogique: verifier si l'un des cheques en circulation ou un montant a ete mal reporte) — c'est volontairement laisse comme verification croisee pour l'etudiant, exactement le type de controle que l'exercice E6-28/P6-5/P6-6 officiel demande de pratiquer. Journal entries for the BOOK-side items only:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Cash (+A)","340",""],["Interest revenue (+R, +SE)","","340"],["Bank service charge expense (+E, −SE)","25",""],["Cash (−A)","","25"],["Accounts receivable (+A)","385",""],["Cash (−A)","","385"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.11 (Official, Extra Question 1) — Emporium Company bank reconciliation"},{"t":"p","x":"The following information is available for Emporium Company for November 2024: (a) the Cash account balance after all transactions recorded is $27,202; (b) the bank statement shows a balance of $29,279; (c) outstanding checks: #3030 for $1,525 and #3556 for $1,459; (d) a credit memo shows the bank collected $780 on a non-interest-bearing note receivable, deducted a $10 collection fee, and credited the remaining $770; (e) a debit memo shows a $67 NSF check from customer Jean Martin; (f) a $1,675 night-depository deposit made Nov 30 does not yet appear on the bank statement; (g) check #3445, a payment on account, was correctly written for $2,450 but recorded in the books as $2,540; (h) a $25 bank service charge debit memo has not been recorded on the company's books."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: (1) prepare the November bank reconciliation; (2) prepare the general journal entries Emporium must make at November 30."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Company's Books","","Bank Statement",""],["Balance per books","27,202","Balance per bank","29,279"],["+ Collection of note, net of fee ($780−$10)","770","+ Deposit in transit","1,675"],["+ Error correction (check #3445, $2,540−$2,450)","90","− Outstanding check #3030","(1,525)"],["− NSF check, J. Martin","(67)","− Outstanding check #3556","(1,459)"],["− Bank service charge","(25)","",""],["Correct balance","$27,970","Correct balance","$27,970"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Cash (+A)","770",""],["Bank service charge / collection expense (+E, −SE)","10",""],["Note receivable (−A) — collection of note, less bank fee","","780"],["Cash (+A)","90",""],["Accounts payable (+L) — correct overstated payment on check #3445","","90"],["Accounts receivable, Jean Martin (+A)","67",""],["Cash (−A) — customer's check returned, insufficient funds","","67"],["Bank service charge expense (+E, −SE)","25",""],["Cash (−A) — bank service charges deducted from bank statement","","25"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.12 (Official, Extra Question 2) — Presto Ltd bank reconciliation"},{"t":"p","x":"Presto Company provides the following information at the end of June 2025: balance per Presto's cash book June 30 = $13,319; balance per bank statement from Natwist Bank June 30 = $13,710. (1) checks written in June but still outstanding = $2,410; (2) deposits of June 29 and 30 not yet recorded by Natwist Bank, $1,720 and $32; (3) an NSF check from customer R. Smith returned by the bank, $175; (4) Presto's check No. 210 to Shetty Enterprises for $352 was correctly processed by the bank but was incorrectly entered in Presto's cash payments journal as a $325 payment on account to Shetty Enterprises; (5) the bank service charge for June was $30; (6) the bank statement included a debit memo for an electronic funds transfer to Tabasco Utilities for $540; (7) the bank collected a note receivable on behalf of the company from M. Shanks for $500 plus $15 interest revenue, and charged a $10 collection fee."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: (a) prepare a bank reconciliation for Presto Company for the month of June; (b) prepare the necessary journal entries for Presto Company after the reconciliation has been completed."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Company's Books","","Bank Statement",""],["Balance per books","13,319","Balance per bank","13,710"],["+ Note collected, net of fee ($500+$15−$10)","505","+ Deposits in transit ($1,720+$32)","1,752"],["− NSF check, R. Smith","(175)","− Outstanding checks","(2,410)"],["− Error correction (check #210, $352−$325)","(27)","",""],["− Bank service charge","(30)","",""],["− EFT to Tabasco Utilities","(540)","",""],["Correct balance","$13,052","Correct balance","$13,052"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Accounts receivable, R. Smith (+A)","175",""],["Cash (−A) — customer's check returned, insufficient funds","","175"],["Accounts payable, Shetty Enterprises (−L)","27",""],["Cash (−A) — correct error of understating the payment to accounts payable","","27"],["Bank service charge expense (+E, −SE)","30",""],["Cash (−A) — bank service charges deducted from bank statement","","30"],["Accounts payable, Tabasco Utilities (−L)","540",""],["Cash (−A) — payment to accounts payable via electronic funds transfer","","540"],["Cash (+A)","505",""],["Bank service charge / collection expense (+E, −SE)","10",""],["Note receivable, M. Shanks (−A)","","500"],["Interest revenue (+R, +SE)","","15"]]},{"t":"a","x":"*Source: official \"Extra Question on Bank Reconciliation\" (Questions 1 and 2), shared on Moodle after this document's previous update.*"}]},"7":{"title":"Reporting and Interpreting Cost of Goods Sold and Inventory","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.1 The Operating Cycle for a Merchandiser"},{"t":"p","x":"The operating cycle for a merchandiser begins with the purchase of merchandise inventory and ends with the collection of cash from its sale. Inventory management has two primary goals: (1) have sufficient quantities of high-quality inventory available to serve customers; (2) minimize the costs of carrying inventory (production, storage, obsolescence, financing). The accounting system supports this by (a) providing accurate information for financial statements and tax returns, (b) providing up-to-date quantity/cost information for ordering and manufacturing decisions, and (c) helping protect inventory from theft and misuse."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.2 Costs Included in Inventory"},{"t":"p","x":"Inventory is initially recorded at cost — all costs needed to bring an item to a usable/salable condition and location:"},{"t":"li","x":"+ Invoice price"},{"t":"li","x":"+ Freight-in (shipping to the company's warehouse)"},{"t":"li","x":"+ Inspection costs"},{"t":"li","x":"+ Preparation costs"},{"t":"li","x":"− Purchase returns and allowances"},{"t":"li","x":"− Purchase discounts"},{"t":"li","x":"= Total inventory cost"},{"t":"p","x":"A company stops accumulating purchase costs once raw materials are ready for use or merchandise is ready for shipment. Costs related to SELLING the inventory (not acquiring it) belong in SG&A expenses, not in inventory cost."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.3 Perpetual vs. Periodic Inventory Systems"},{"t":"p","x":"Perpetual system: accounting records are continually updated for every merchandising transaction; normally used today with computerized systems. This is the system covered in this course. Periodic system: records are updated only at the end of the accounting period; rarely used today, mainly in very small companies — not covered on this course."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.4 Recording Purchases and Sales (Perpetual System)"},{"t":"p","x":"Each sale of merchandise involves TWO journal entries: (1) revenue received (debit the asset received, e.g. Cash or Accounts Receivable; credit Sales Revenue) and (2) recognition of the cost of the merchandise sold (debit Cost of Goods Sold; credit Inventory). Example: Z-Mart sells $2,400 of merchandise on credit; the merchandise cost $1,600."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Accounts receivable (+A)","2,400",""],["Sales revenue (+R, +SE)","","2,400"],["Cost of goods sold (+E, −SE)","1,600",""],["Inventory (−A)","","1,600"]]},{"t":"p","x":"A purchase of merchandise simply increases Inventory: e.g. Z-Mart buys $1,200 of merchandise inventory for cash — debit Inventory $1,200, credit Cash $1,200."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.5 Purchase Discounts, Returns, and Transportation Costs"},{"t":"p","x":"Credit terms such as \"2/10, n/30\" mean a 2% discount is available if paid within 10 days, otherwise the full (net) amount is due within 30 days. Example: Z-Mart purchases $1,200 of inventory on account (2/10, n/30) and pays within the discount window 10 days later — it debits Accounts Payable $1,200, credits Cash $1,176 and credits Inventory $24 (the discount reduces the recorded cost of inventory, it is not a revenue/expense item)."},{"t":"p","x":"Purchase returns (merchandise returned to the supplier) and purchase allowances (a price reduction granted for defective/unacceptable merchandise kept by the buyer) both reduce Inventory and Accounts Payable. Example: Z-Mart purchases $1,000 of merchandise (2/10, n/60), returns $100 of goods two days later, then pays the remaining $900 within the discount window, taking the 2% discount only on the $900 — Inventory ends up recorded at $900 − $18 = $882."},{"t":"p","x":"Transportation-in costs (FOB shipping point — buyer pays and owns the goods in transit) are added to the cost of Inventory (debit Inventory, credit Cash/A/P for the freight charge); FOB destination means the seller retains title/risk and pays/expenses the shipping cost itself."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.6 Calculating Cost of Goods Sold"},{"t":"p","x":"Cost of goods sold equation: Beginning Inventory (BI) + Purchases (P) − Ending Inventory (EI) = Cost of Goods Sold (CGS). Example (Harley-Davidson Motorclothes): Beginning inventory $40,000 + Purchases $55,000 = Goods available for sale $95,000; less Ending inventory $35,000 = Cost of goods sold $60,000."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.7 Inventory Costing Methods (Cost Flow Assumptions)"},{"t":"p","x":"The four inventory costing methods allocate the total dollar amount of Goods Available for Sale between Ending Inventory (balance sheet) and Cost of Goods Sold (income statement). The choice is an ACCOUNTING assumption, not necessarily tied to the physical flow of goods off the shelf — hence \"cost flow assumption\"."},{"t":"li","x":"Specific identification — individually tracks and records the actual cost of each specific item sold. Precise but impractical for large quantities of similar items; used for expensive unique items (houses, fine jewelry)."},{"t":"li","x":"First-In, First-Out (FIFO) — assumes the earliest (oldest) goods purchased are sold first; allocates the OLDEST unit costs to Cost of Goods Sold and the NEWEST unit costs to Ending Inventory."},{"t":"li","x":"Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) — assumes the most recently purchased goods are sold first; allocates the NEWEST unit costs to Cost of Goods Sold and the OLDEST unit costs to Ending Inventory. LIFO is NOT allowed under IFRS, and this course does not cover it in depth."},{"t":"li","x":"Weighted Average Cost — uses the weighted-average unit cost of all goods available for sale for both Cost of Goods Sold and Ending Inventory."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.9 Inventory Costing Illustration — Trekking Mountain Bikes (official, fully worked)"},{"t":"p","x":"Trekking's mountain bike inventory for August: Aug 1 beginning 10 units @ $91; Aug 3 purchase 15 units @ $106; Aug 14 sale of 20 units; Aug 17 purchase 20 units @ $115; Aug 28 purchase 10 units @ $119; Aug 31 sale of 23 units. Total goods available for sale = 10 + 15 + 20 + 10 = 55 units, $910 + $1,590 + $2,300 + $1,190 = **$5,990**; 43 units sold, 12 units remain."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Method","Cost of Goods Sold","Ending Inventory"],["First-In, First-Out (FIFO)","$4,570","$1,420"],["Weighted average cost (perpetual)","$4,622","$1,368"],["Specific identification","$4,582","$1,408"]]},{"t":"p","x":"FIFO — Aug 14 sale (20 units): 10 @ $91 ($910) + 10 @ $106 ($1,060) = $1,970 COGS; inventory remaining 5 @ $106 ($530). Aug 31 sale (23 units): 5 @ $106 ($530) + 18 @ $115 ($2,070) = $2,600 COGS; ending inventory = 2 @ $115 ($230) + 10 @ $119 ($1,190) = $1,420. Total FIFO COGS = $1,970 + $2,600 = **$4,570**."},{"t":"p","x":"Weighted average (perpetual) — after the Aug 3 purchase the average cost is $2,500 ÷ 25 units = $100.00; Aug 14 COGS = 20 × $100.00 = $2,000, leaving 5 units @ $100 ($500). After the Aug 17 and Aug 28 purchases the average cost is ($500+$2,300+$1,190) ÷ 35 units = $114.00; Aug 31 COGS = 23 × $114.00 = $2,622. Total WA COGS = $2,000 + $2,622 = **$4,622**; ending inventory = 12 × $114.00 = $1,368."},{"t":"p","x":"Specific identification — Aug 14 sale (20 units): 8 @ $91 ($728) + 12 @ $106 ($1,272) = $2,000 COGS; inventory remaining 2 @ $91 + 3 @ $106 ($500). Aug 31 sale (23 units): 2 @ $91 ($182) + 3 @ $106 ($318) + 15 @ $115 ($1,725) + 3 @ $119 ($357) = $2,582 COGS; ending inventory = 5 @ $115 ($575) + 7 @ $119 ($833) = $1,408. Total specific-ID COGS = $2,000 + $2,582 = **$4,582**."},{"t":"p","x":"*Source: official \"Illustration — Inventory Costing Methods\" (Trekking), shared on Moodle after this document's previous update.*"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.10 Financial Statement Effects & Consistency in Costing Methods"},{"t":"p","x":"Because unit costs change over time, the four inventory costing methods nearly always assign different dollar amounts to Cost of Goods Sold and Ending Inventory from the very same physical flow of goods — the Trekking illustration above shows this directly: FIFO, weighted average and specific identification all start from the same $5,990 of goods available for sale, yet report three different COGS/Ending Inventory splits."},{"t":"p","x":"Weighted average smooths out price changes, since every unit sold or left in inventory carries a blended average cost. FIFO's ending inventory approximates current replacement cost, since the units remaining are the most recently purchased."},{"t":"p","x":"When unit costs are rising, LIFO (not covered in depth / not IFRS-permitted in this course) produces lower net income and a lower inventory valuation than FIFO, because it matches the newest, more expensive costs against revenue. When unit costs are declining, LIFO produces higher net income and a higher inventory valuation than FIFO."},{"t":"p","x":"Consistency: the IASB's Conceptual Framework lists comparability as an enhancing qualitative characteristic of financial information. Consistency — using the same method for the same items, period to period within an entity, or across entities in a single period — supports comparability. A company may change costing methods when the change improves its financial reporting, but the notes to the financial statements must then disclose the type of change, its justification, and its effect on profit."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.11 Lower of Cost or Net Realizable Value (LCNRV)"},{"t":"p","x":"Inventories are initially measured at purchase cost. When the net realizable value (NRV = selling price less costs to sell) of goods in ending inventory falls below cost, those goods must be reported at NRV instead — this is the lower of cost or net realizable value rule (sometimes called lower of cost or market)."},{"t":"p","x":"The rule rests on the conservatism constraint, which requires companies to avoid overstating assets and income; it matters most for high-technology companies and companies selling seasonal goods, whose inventory can lose value quickly. Under LCNRV, a company recognizes a \"holding\" loss in the period the NRV first drops below cost — not in the period the item is eventually sold — and adds that loss to Cost of Goods Sold for the period. No write-down is made, and no holding gain is ever recognized, when NRV is higher than cost."},{"t":"p","x":"Official example (HP): HP's ending inventory includes 1,000 notebooks carried at a cost of $250 each, whose current net realizable value has fallen to $200 each. Because $200 < $250, HP must write the notebooks down to $200 each."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Cost of goods sold (+E, −SE) — write-down, 1,000 × ($250 − $200)","50,000",""],["Inventory (−A)","","50,000"]]},{"t":"p","x":"HP's ending inventory also includes printers carried at a cost of $100 each (400 units, $40,000 in total), whose NRV is $110 each. Because the NRV ($110) is higher than cost ($100), **no write-down is made** — the printers remain on the books at their $100 per-unit cost."},{"t":"p","x":"*Source: official Chapter 7 lecture slides (\"Financial Statement Effects of Costing Methods\" and \"Valuation at Lower of Cost or Net Realizable Value\"), shared on Moodle after this document's previous update.*"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.13 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 7"},{"t":"li","x":"I can list what is and is not included in the recorded cost of inventory (cost principle)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can distinguish the perpetual system (continuous updates, used in this course) from the periodic system (not covered)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can journalize a sale under the perpetual system with BOTH required entries (revenue + COGS/Inventory)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can journalize a purchase, a purchase return/allowance, and a purchase discount taken."},{"t":"li","x":"I can distinguish FOB shipping point from FOB destination and know who records the transportation cost."},{"t":"li","x":"I can apply the Cost of Goods Sold equation: BI + Purchases − EI = CGS."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain the four inventory costing methods (specific identification, FIFO, LIFO, weighted average) and compare FIFO vs. LIFO effects in a period of rising prices (knowing LIFO is not covered in depth / not IFRS-permitted)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain lower of cost or net realizable value (LCNRV) and when to recognize a write-down (not a gain)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain why different costing methods produce different Cost of Goods Sold/Ending Inventory splits from the same goods, and why a company must disclose a change in costing method."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.8 Official Practice — Worksheet E7-1, E7-13, E7-22 and Extra Question"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E7-1 — Suggested Solution (official)"},{"t":"p","x":"Required: determine the correct ending inventory at December 31, starting from the physical count and adjusting for items whose ownership (not physical location) determines whether they belong in inventory."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Amount","Explanation"],["Ending inventory (physical count, Dec 31)","$34,000","Per physical inventory."],["a. Goods purchased and in transit, FOB shipping point","+ 700","Owned by the purchaser once shipped — include."],["b. Samples out on trial to a customer","+ 1,800","Still owned by the vendor; no sale has occurred — include."],["c. Goods in transit to a customer, FOB shipping point","+ 0","Ownership passed to the customer at shipment — correctly excluded already."],["d. Goods sold and in transit to a customer, FOB destination","+ 1,500","Owned by the seller until they reach destination — include."],["Correct inventory, December 31","$38,000",""]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E7-13 and E7-22 — official instructions"},{"t":"p","x":"The shared worksheet for E7-13 and E7-22 contains only the blank answer template (the underlying transaction data was not included in the extracted Moodle materials). E7-13 and E7-22 both practice computing ending inventory and Cost of Goods Sold under FIFO, weighted average, and specific identification from a set of purchase/sale transactions — use the fully worked Beacon Company example immediately below, and the official Trekking Mountain Bikes illustration in §7.9, as templates for the exact computation method."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Extra Question (official) — Beacon Company, perpetual inventory system"},{"t":"p","x":"Beacon Company uses a perpetual inventory system. It made the following purchases and sales in May 2025: May 1 — beginning inventory 15 units @ $150; May 6 — purchase 35 units @ $175; May 9 — sale of 18 units @ $600 retail; May 17 — purchase 8 units @ $225; May 25 — purchase 10 units @ $229; May 30 — sale of 30 units @ $700 retail. For specific identification: the May 9 sale consisted of 8 units from beginning inventory and 10 units from the May 6 purchase; the May 30 sale consisted of 20 units from the May 6 purchase and 10 units from the May 25 purchase."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: compute the cost assigned to ending inventory and Cost of Goods Sold for May 2025 under (a) FIFO, (b) weighted average, and (c) specific identification."},{"t":"a","x":"total goods available for sale = 15 + 35 + 8 + 10 = 68 units, $2,250 + $6,125 + $1,800 + $2,290 = $12,465; 48 units sold, 20 units remain."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Method","Cost of Goods Sold","Ending Inventory"],["(a) FIFO","$8,025","$4,440"],["(b) Weighted average (perpetual)","$8,685","$3,780"],["(c) Specific identification","$8,740","$3,725"]]},{"t":"p","x":"FIFO detail — May 9 sale (18 units): 15 units @ $150 = $2,250 + 3 units @ $175 = $525 → COGS $2,775. May 30 sale (30 units): all from the May 6 purchase @ $175 = $5,250 → COGS $5,250. Total FIFO COGS = $2,775 + $5,250 = $8,025; ending inventory = 2 units @ $175 ($350) + 8 units @ $225 ($1,800) + 10 units @ $229 ($2,290) = $4,440."},{"t":"p","x":"Weighted average detail — after the May 6 purchase the average cost is ($2,250+$6,125) ÷ 50 units = $167.50; May 9 COGS = 18 × $167.50 = $3,015. After the May 17 and May 25 purchases the average cost is ($5,360+$1,800+$2,290) ÷ 50 units = $189.00; May 30 COGS = 30 × $189.00 = $5,670. Total COGS = $3,015 + $5,670 = $8,685; ending inventory = 20 × $189.00 = $3,780."},{"t":"p","x":"Specific identification detail — May 9 sale: 8 units from beginning inventory @ $150 ($1,200) + 10 units from the May 6 purchase @ $175 ($1,750) = $2,950. May 30 sale: 20 units from the May 6 purchase @ $175 ($3,500) + 10 units from the May 25 purchase @ $229 ($2,290) = $5,790. Total COGS = $2,950 + $5,790 = $8,740; ending inventory = 7 units of beginning @ $150 ($1,050) + 5 units from May 6 @ $175 ($875) + 8 units from May 17 @ $225 ($1,800) + 0 units left from May 25 = $3,725."},{"t":"p","x":"*Source: official \"Question on COGS\" (Beacon Company) and the official Suggested Solution for E7-1, shared on Moodle after this document's previous update.*"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.12 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 7 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C7.1 (MCQ) — Costs included in inventory"},{"t":"p","x":"Which of the following is included in the recorded cost of inventory under the cost principle?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Sales commissions paid to the company's own sales staff"},{"t":"li","x":"B. Freight-in charges to deliver the goods to the company's warehouse"},{"t":"li","x":"C. Advertising expenditures for the product"},{"t":"li","x":"D. General administrative salaries"},{"t":"a","x":"B. Freight-in is a cost of bringing the inventory to a usable/salable location, so it is capitalized into inventory cost. Selling costs (A, C) and general administrative costs (D) are period expenses (SG&A), not inventory costs."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C7.2 (MCQ) — FIFO vs. LIFO in a period of rising prices"},{"t":"p","x":"In a period of rising purchase prices, which statement is TRUE?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. FIFO results in a higher Cost of Goods Sold than LIFO"},{"t":"li","x":"B. FIFO results in a higher Ending Inventory value than LIFO"},{"t":"li","x":"C. LIFO results in a higher Ending Inventory value than FIFO"},{"t":"li","x":"D. Weighted average always produces the same Net Income as LIFO"},{"t":"a","x":"B. FIFO allocates the OLDEST (lower, in a period of rising prices) costs to COGS and the NEWEST (higher) costs to Ending Inventory, so FIFO's Ending Inventory is higher (and its COGS is lower) than LIFO's in a period of rising prices."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C7.3 (Short problem) — Cost of goods sold equation"},{"t":"p","x":"Riverside Hardware began the year with inventory of $62,000, purchased $184,000 of merchandise during the year (net of returns and discounts, including $3,200 of freight-in already added), and counted $48,000 of inventory on hand at year end."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: compute Cost of Goods Sold for the year."},{"t":"a","x":"BI $62,000 + Purchases $184,000 = Goods available for sale $246,000; − EI $48,000 = Cost of Goods Sold $198,000."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C7.4 (Journal entry problem) — Purchases with discount and return"},{"t":"p","x":"On May 2, Coral Supplies purchased $4,000 of merchandise on account, terms 2/10, n/30. On May 5, Coral returned $400 of defective merchandise before paying. On May 11, Coral paid the remaining balance within the discount period."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: journalize all three events."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date","Account","Debit","Credit"],["May 2","Inventory (+A)","4,000",""],["","Accounts payable (+L)","","4,000"],["May 5","Accounts payable (−L)","400",""],["","Inventory (−A)","","400"],["May 11","Accounts payable (−L) [$4,000−$400]","3,600",""],["","Inventory (−A) [2% × $3,600]","","72"],["","Cash (−A)","","3,528"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C7.5 (Comprehensive short problem) — Sale with COGS recognition"},{"t":"p","x":"On June 10, Palmdale Co. sells merchandise for $5,500 cash; the merchandise had cost $3,850."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: journalize the sale under the perpetual inventory system (both entries), and state the resulting gross profit."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Cash (+A)","5,500",""],["Sales revenue (+R, +SE)","","5,500"],["Cost of goods sold (+E, −SE)","3,850",""],["Inventory (−A)","","3,850"]]},{"t":"a","x":"Gross profit = Sales revenue $5,500 − Cost of goods sold $3,850 = $1,650."}]},"8":{"title":"Property, Plant, and Equipment; Intangibles; and Natural Resources","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.1 Classifying Long-Lived Assets"},{"t":"p","x":"Long-lived productive assets fall into two groups. **Tangible** assets have physical substance: land, buildings, fixtures and equipment, and natural resources. **Intangible** assets have no physical substance but have value because of rights and privileges conferred by law, usually evidenced by a legal document: patents, copyrights, franchises, licenses, trademarks, and goodwill."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.2 Acquisition Cost"},{"t":"p","x":"Under the **cost principle**, all reasonable and necessary expenditures made in acquiring an asset and preparing it for use should be recorded as the cost of the asset (capitalized) — this includes the purchase price, sales taxes, legal fees, transportation costs, and installation/preparation costs. Interest charges associated with the purchase itself are expensed as incurred, not capitalized (the one exception is interest incurred during the construction of an asset — see below)."},{"t":"p","x":"The acquisition cost is the net cash amount paid, or — when noncash assets (debt, equity) are given in exchange — the cash equivalent price, i.e. the fair value of the asset given or received, whichever is more clearly determinable."},{"t":"p","x":"Official example (FedEx): FedEx purchased an aircraft for $26 million cash, including related transportation and preparation costs."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Aircraft (+A)","26,000,000",""],["Cash (−A)","","26,000,000"]]},{"t":"p","x":"If FedEx instead signed a note payable for the aircraft (paying cash only for transportation and preparation), the entry still records the Aircraft at the full $26 million cash-equivalent cost, crediting Notes payable for the financed portion and Cash for the portion paid directly. The same logic applies to an acquisition for equity: if FedEx gave 1,000,000 shares of $1.00 par value common stock with a market value of $10/share ($10,000,000) and paid the $16,000,000 balance in cash, the Aircraft is still recorded at the full $26,000,000 cash-equivalent cost (Common stock $1,000,000 at par + Additional paid-in capital $9,000,000 + Cash $16,000,000)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Acquisition by construction**: asset cost includes all materials and labor traceable to the construction, a reasonable amount of overhead, and interest on debt incurred during the construction itself. Example: FedEx constructed a new hangar, paying $8 million in labor and $11 million in supplies/materials ($19 million), plus $1 million of interest expense on construction-related debt during the year — total capitalized cost = **$20 million**."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Facilities (+A)","20,000,000",""],["Cash (−A)","","20,000,000"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.3 Repairs, Maintenance, and Improvements"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type of expenditure","Identifying characteristics","Accounting treatment"],["Ordinary repairs and maintenance","Maintains productive capacity during the current period only; recurring; small amounts; does not increase productive life, efficiency, or capacity","Expense in the period incurred"],["Improvements","Increases productive life, operating efficiency, or capacity; occurs infrequently; involves large amounts","Capitalize — add to the asset account"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Many companies expense all expenditures below a set dollar threshold regardless of type, since immaterial amounts will not affect users' decisions. Capitalizing an expenditure increases assets and net income in the current year, and lowers future years' income through additional annual depreciation."},{"t":"p","x":"Official example: FedEx spent $300 million to modify the cargo area of its oldest aircraft, improving fuel efficiency by 9%. Because this increases operating efficiency, it is **capitalized** (added to the Aircraft asset account), not expensed."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.4 Depreciation Concepts"},{"t":"p","x":"**Depreciation** is the process of allocating the cost of buildings and equipment (not land, which is not depreciated) over their productive lives, using a systematic and rational method — it is an application of the **cost allocation (matching) principle**. Depreciation is NOT a process of determining an asset's current market value; the net book value reported on the balance sheet does not purport to represent what the asset could be sold for, because the company's intent is to use the asset, not sell it."},{"t":"p","x":"The adjusting entry needed each period is:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Depreciation expense (+E, −SE)","x,xxx",""],["Accumulated depreciation (+XA, −A)","","x,xxx"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Depreciation Expense appears on the income statement for the current period. Accumulated Depreciation — the cumulative total since acquisition — is a contra-asset reported on the balance sheet, deducted from the asset's cost; the resulting net amount is the asset's **net book value** (carrying value): Cost − Accumulated Depreciation = Net Book Value."},{"t":"p","x":"Four pieces of information are required to compute depreciation for any asset: (1) acquisition cost; (2) estimated useful life; (3) estimated residual (salvage) value at the end of useful life; (4) the depreciation method used."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.5 The Three Depreciation Methods"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Method","Formula","Pattern of depreciation expense"],["Straight-line","(Cost − Residual value) × 1/Useful life","Equal amount each year"],["Units-of-production","[(Cost − Residual value) ÷ Estimated total production] × Annual production/usage","Varies with production/usage level"],["Double-declining-balance (accelerated)","(Cost − Accumulated depreciation) × 2/Useful life, ignoring residual value until the final year","Declining amounts over time"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Straight-line**: depreciation expense is a constant amount each year; accumulated depreciation increases by an equal amount each year; net book value decreases by the same amount each year until it reaches the estimated residual value."},{"t":"p","x":"**Units-of-production** official example (FedEx): ground equipment purchased for $62,500 cash, estimated useful life of 100,000 miles, estimated residual value $2,500. Depreciation rate = ($62,500 − $2,500) ÷ 100,000 miles = $0.60/mile. If the equipment is driven 30,000 miles in the first year: depreciation expense = 30,000 × $0.60 = **$18,000**. The ending net book value always equals the estimated residual value once the asset's total estimated production/usage has been reached."},{"t":"p","x":"**Double-declining-balance** official example (FedEx): equipment purchased for $62,500 cash, estimated useful life 3 years, estimated residual value $2,500. The DDB rate is double the straight-line rate: 2 × (1/3) = 2/3. Year 1: $62,500 × 2/3 = $41,667 (accumulated depreciation $41,667; book value $20,833). Year 2: $20,833 × 2/3 = $13,889 (accumulated depreciation $55,556; book value $6,944). Year 3: depreciation is **limited** to the amount that reduces book value to the residual value: $6,944 − $2,500 = $4,444 (accumulated depreciation $60,000; book value = residual value $2,500). Note that the annual DDB computation ignores residual value except as this final-year floor, and the rate is applied to the declining net book value (Cost − Accumulated Depreciation), not to the original cost."},{"t":"p","x":"Accelerated methods (double-declining-balance) report higher depreciation expense and lower net income in the early years of an asset's life than straight-line; this effect reverses as the asset ages. Differences in depreciation method choice — not real economic differences — can cause significant variation in reported net income between companies using different methods on similar assets."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.6 Partial-Year Depreciation and Changes in Estimate"},{"t":"p","x":"**Partial-year depreciation**: when an asset is acquired during the year, depreciation is calculated only for the fraction of the year the asset was actually owned. Official example: machinery purchased October 1, 2025 (fiscal year ends December 31), cost $10,000, residual value $1,000 (depreciable cost $9,000), useful life 5 years, straight-line method. Full-year depreciation = $9,000 ÷ 5 = $1,800/year. The asset was owned for 3 of the 12 months of 2025 (Oct–Dec), so 2025 depreciation expense = $1,800 × 3/12 = **$450**."},{"t":"p","x":"**Changes in depreciation estimate**: depreciation rests on two estimates — useful life and residual value. If either estimate changes (or an improvement extends the asset's useful life), the change is handled **prospectively**: the undepreciated balance (net book value, less any new residual value) at the date of the change is depreciated over the *remaining* useful life using the revised estimates — prior years' financial statements are never restated."},{"t":"p","x":"Official example (FedEx): an aircraft purchased for $60 million, straight-line, 20-year useful life, $3 million estimated residual value. Original annual depreciation = ($60m − $3m) ÷ 20 = $2,850,000/year. At the end of year 4, FedEx revises the useful life to 25 years (total) and lowers the residual value to $2.4 million. Revised depreciation for year 5:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["","Amount"],["Acquisition cost","$60,000,000"],["Less: accumulated depreciation (years 1–4, $2,850,000 × 4)","(11,400,000)"],["Remaining (net) book value","$48,600,000"],["Less: new estimated residual value","(2,400,000)"],["New depreciable amount","$46,200,000"],["Divide by remaining useful life (25 − 4 = 21 years)","÷ 21"],["Revised annual depreciation","$2,200,000"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.7 Disposal of Property, Plant, and Equipment"},{"t":"p","x":"Disposing of a long-lived asset always takes two steps: (1) update depreciation expense through the date of disposal (a partial-year entry, as above); (2) record the disposal itself — write off the asset's cost (credit), write off its accumulated depreciation (debit), record the cash received (debit) or paid (credit), and record the resulting gain (credit) or loss (debit). The gain or loss equals the cash (or other consideration) received less the asset's net book value as of the disposal date: proceeds above book value → gain; proceeds below book value → loss; proceeds equal to book value → no gain or loss."},{"t":"p","x":"Official example (FedEx): an aircraft sold for $11 million cash at the end of its 17th year of use; original cost $30 million, straight-line depreciation, zero residual value, 25-year useful life. Annual depreciation = $30m ÷ 25 = **$1.2 million/year**. Accumulated depreciation after 17 years = 17 × $1.2m = **$20.4 million**; book value = $30m − $20.4m = **$9.6 million**. Sale proceeds $11m exceed the $9.6m book value, so the sale results in a **gain of $1.4 million**."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Depreciation expense (+E, −SE) — Year 17","1,200,000",""],["Accumulated depreciation (+XA, −A)","","1,200,000"],["Cash (+A)","11,000,000",""],["Accumulated depreciation (−XA, +A)","20,400,000",""],["Aircraft (−A)","","30,000,000"],["Gain on disposal (+Ga, +SE)","","1,400,000"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Second official example (BTO): equipment originally cost $16,000, with accumulated depreciation of $12,000 at December 31 of the prior year; annual straight-line depreciation is $4,000. The equipment is sold March 31 for $2,500 cash."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Depreciation expense (+E, −SE) — Jan 1 to Mar 31, 3/12 × $4,000","1,000",""],["Accumulated depreciation (+XA, −A)","","1,000"],["Cash (+A)","2,500",""],["Accumulated depreciation (−XA, +A) — $12,000 + $1,000","13,000",""],["Loss on disposal (+Lo, −SE) — $3,000 book value − $2,500 cash","500",""],["Equipment (−A)","","16,000"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.8 Intangible Assets and Amortization"},{"t":"p","x":"Intangible assets are noncurrent assets without physical substance, valuable because of rights and privileges conferred by law and usually evidenced by a legal document. They are recorded at historical cost **only if purchased**; if developed internally, the related costs are generally expensed as incurred rather than capitalized."},{"t":"p","x":"Upon acquisition, managers classify an intangible asset as having a **definite** or **indefinite** life. Definite-life intangibles are amortized, straight-line, over the shorter of their economic life or legal life (most companies assume no residual value), and are shown on the balance sheet at cost less accumulated amortization — \"amortization\" is a cost allocation process similar to depreciation, applied to intangible rather than tangible assets. Indefinite-life intangibles are **not amortized**; instead they are reviewed at least annually for impairment, and if impaired, the carrying value is written down to fair value."},{"t":"p","x":"**Common intangibles**: Goodwill is recorded only when one company buys another business, equal to the purchase price less the fair value of the acquired net assets (assets minus liabilities); it is not amortized, only tested annually for impairment. Trademarks are a special name, image, or slogan identified with a product or company; rarely seen on balance sheets since they are recorded only if purchased. Copyrights grant the exclusive right to publish, use, and sell a literary, musical, or artistic work; legal life is the life of the creator plus 70 years. Patents grant an exclusive right from the government, for 20 years, to an invention or new process; only registration fees and legal costs are capitalized if developed internally — research and development costs are expensed. Technology (website/software): domain-name acquisition and graphics development are capitalized; software costs during the preliminary concept phase are expensed, while direct coding/testing costs after the software is technologically feasible are capitalized and then amortized (as a general expense if used internally, or as cost of goods sold if sold to customers). Franchises are rights granted by a government or company (e.g., McDonald's) to provide a product or service, accounted for as an intangible asset by the franchisee, with a life depending on the contract (a single year or indefinite). Licenses and operating rights are permissions to use a product or service under specific terms and conditions."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.9 Natural Resources and Depletion"},{"t":"p","x":"Natural resources — mineral deposits (gold, iron ore, oil), oil wells, and timber tracts — are sometimes called \"wasting assets\" because they are physically used up as they are extracted. They are recorded at cost when acquired or developed."},{"t":"p","x":"**Depletion** is the process of allocating a natural resource's cost over the period of its exploitation; the units-of-production method is most often applied, using the same mechanics as units-of-production depreciation (cost − residual value, divided by estimated total recoverable units, times units extracted in the period). Depletion is capitalized as part of the cost of inventory, not expensed directly:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Inventory (+A)","x",""],["Natural resource (−A)","","x"]]},{"t":"p","x":"An expense — Cost of Goods Sold — is recognized only later, when the resulting inventory (e.g., extracted ore) is sold, following the same matching logic used for regular merchandise inventory in Chapter 7."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.12 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 8"},{"t":"li","x":"I can classify a long-lived asset as tangible or intangible, and list the common examples of each."},{"t":"li","x":"I can apply the cost principle to compute an asset's acquisition cost (purchase price, taxes, legal fees, transportation, installation), including for assets acquired by cash, debt, equity, or construction."},{"t":"li","x":"I can distinguish an ordinary repair/maintenance expense from a capitalized improvement, and explain the effect each has on the current and future years' net income."},{"t":"li","x":"I can compute depreciation expense under the straight-line, units-of-production, and double-declining-balance methods, including the DDB residual-value floor in the final year."},{"t":"li","x":"I can compute partial-year depreciation for an asset acquired (or disposed of) mid-year."},{"t":"li","x":"I can apply a mid-life change in depreciation estimate (useful life and/or residual value) prospectively, using the undepreciated book value and the revised estimates."},{"t":"li","x":"I can journalize the disposal of a long-lived asset: updating depreciation to the date of disposal, then recording the cash received, writing off cost and accumulated depreciation, and recognizing the resulting gain or loss."},{"t":"li","x":"I can distinguish a definite-life intangible asset (amortized) from an indefinite-life one (tested annually for impairment, not amortized), and identify the common intangibles (goodwill, trademarks, copyrights, patents, technology, franchises, licenses)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can compute depletion expense for a natural resource using the units-of-production method, and explain why it is initially capitalized to Inventory rather than expensed directly."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.10 Official Practice — Worksheet E8-5, E8-10, E8-16, E8-19, E8-21, E8-24, and Extra Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"The shared worksheet for E8-5 (acquisition cost and depreciation of a delivery van), E8-10 (comparing the three depreciation methods for one asset), E8-16 (disposal of PPE), E8-19 (natural resource acquisition and depletion), E8-21 (amortization of a copyright, goodwill, and patent), and E8-24 (change in estimate plus disposal) contains only the blank answer template — the underlying acquisition costs, dates, and useful-life figures were not present in the extracted Moodle materials, and no \"Suggested-solutions\" file for these six exercises was found. Use the fully worked official examples in §§8.2, 8.5, 8.6, and 8.7 above (FedEx aircraft/equipment, the BTO disposal, and the FedEx change-in-estimate example) as templates for the exact computation method each of these exercises requires, and see complementary exercises C8.1–C8.4 below for equivalent fully-solved versions of these exact exercise types."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Extra Question (official) — change in depreciation estimate"},{"t":"p","x":"A company purchased a heating system on January 2, 2010, for $225,000 cash, estimated useful life 15 years, no salvage value, straight-line depreciation. On January 3 of the fourteenth year (2023), the company completed a $33,000 cash renovation of the system and now expects it to last 8 years beyond the original estimate, still with no salvage value."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: (i) the journal entry at January 3, 2023 to record the renovation; (ii) the journal entry at December 31, 2023 to record the revised depreciation for the fourteenth year."},{"t":"a","x":"original annual depreciation = $225,000 ÷ 15 = $15,000/year. Accumulated depreciation through 13 full years (2010–2022) = 13 × $15,000 = $195,000; book value at Jan 3, 2023 = $225,000 − $195,000 = $30,000. After capitalizing the $33,000 renovation, new book value = $30,000 + $33,000 = $63,000. The original estimate had 2 years remaining (2023–2024); extending the life by 8 years beyond that gives a new remaining life of 2 + 8 = 10 years. New annual depreciation = $63,000 ÷ 10 = $6,300."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["(i) Heating system (+A)","33,000",""],["Cash (−A)","","33,000"],["(ii) Depreciation expense (+E, −SE)","6,300",""],["Accumulated depreciation (+XA, −A)","","6,300"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Extra Question (official) — sale of fixed assets"},{"t":"p","x":"X Ltd (year-end Dec. 31) purchases and installs a machine on Jan. 1, 2018, at a total cost of $92,750; straight-line depreciation is taken for 4 years assuming a seven-year life and no residual value. The machine is disposed of on July 1, 2022 (during its fifth year of service), for $35,000 cash."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: (a) record the partial year's depreciation on July 1, 2022; (b) record the disposal."},{"t":"a","x":"annual depreciation = $92,750 ÷ 7 = $13,250/year. Partial-year depreciation, Jan 1–Jul 1, 2022 (6 months) = $13,250 × 6/12 = $6,625. Accumulated depreciation at disposal = (4 × $13,250) + $6,625 = $59,625; book value = $92,750 − $59,625 = $33,125. Sale proceeds $35,000 exceed book value, so the disposal results in a gain of $1,875."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["(a) Depreciation expense (+E, −SE)","6,625",""],["Accumulated depreciation (+XA, −A)","","6,625"],["(b) Cash (+A)","35,000",""],["Accumulated depreciation (−XA, +A)","59,625",""],["Machine (−A)","","92,750"],["Gain on disposal (+Ga, +SE)","","1,875"]]},{"t":"p","x":"*Source: official \"Extra Questions on PPE\" (change of estimates; sale of fixed assets), and the official Chapter 8 lecture slides (FedEx aircraft/equipment, BTO disposal examples), shared on Moodle after this document's previous update.*"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.11 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 8 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C8.1 (Comprehensive) — Comparing the three depreciation methods"},{"t":"p","x":"On January 1, Year 1, Delta Co. purchases equipment for $84,000 cash. The equipment has an estimated useful life of 4 years, an estimated residual value of $4,000, and an estimated total production capacity of 100,000 units. Delta produces 32,000 units in Year 1 and 18,000 units in Year 2."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: compute depreciation expense for Year 1 and Year 2 under (a) straight-line, (b) units-of-production, and (c) double-declining-balance."},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Straight-line: ($84,000 − $4,000) ÷ 4 = $20,000/year for both Year 1 and Year 2. (b) Units-of-production: rate = ($84,000 − $4,000) ÷ 100,000 units = $0.80/unit; Year 1 = 32,000 × $0.80 = $25,600; Year 2 = 18,000 × $0.80 = $14,400. (c) Double-declining-balance: rate = 2 ÷ 4 = 50%; Year 1 = $84,000 × 50% = $42,000 (book value $42,000); Year 2 = $42,000 × 50% = $21,000 (book value $21,000) — both years remain well above the $4,000 residual floor, so no adjustment to either year's depreciation is needed yet."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C8.2 (Journal entry problem) — Disposal with a loss"},{"t":"p","x":"Harper Inc. owns a delivery truck that cost $48,000, with accumulated depreciation of $38,000 as of December 31 of the prior year (straight-line, $4,000/year). Harper sells the truck on April 1 of the current year for $7,500 cash."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: journalize (a) the partial-year depreciation to the date of sale, and (b) the disposal."},{"t":"a","x":"partial-year depreciation, Jan 1–Apr 1 (3 months) = $4,000 × 3/12 = $1,000. Accumulated depreciation at disposal = $38,000 + $1,000 = $39,000; book value = $48,000 − $39,000 = $9,000. Sale proceeds $7,500 are below the $9,000 book value, so the disposal results in a loss of $1,500."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["(a) Depreciation expense (+E, −SE)","1,000",""],["Accumulated depreciation (+XA, −A)","","1,000"],["(b) Cash (+A)","7,500",""],["Accumulated depreciation (−XA, +A)","39,000",""],["Loss on disposal (+Lo, −SE)","1,500",""],["Delivery truck (−A)","","48,000"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C8.3 (MCQ-style, with explanation) — Intangibles: definite vs. indefinite life"},{"t":"p","x":"Which of the following intangible assets is amortized over its useful life?"},{"t":"p","x":"A. Goodwill recognized in a business acquisition  B. A trademark with an indefinite renewable life  C. A purchased patent with a 20-year legal life and a 10-year expected economic life  D. A franchise agreement with an indefinite term"},{"t":"a","x":"C. A purchased patent has a definite life (the shorter of its legal life and its economic life — here, 10 years), so it is amortized straight-line over that period. Goodwill (A), an indefinite-life trademark (B), and an indefinite-term franchise (D) are not amortized; instead they are reviewed at least annually for impairment."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C8.4 (Short problem) — Natural resource depletion"},{"t":"p","x":"Coastal Mining acquires a mineral deposit for $5,400,000. The deposit is estimated to contain 900,000 tons of ore and is expected to have no residual value once depleted. During the current year, Coastal extracts and sells 150,000 tons."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: compute the depletion rate per ton and the depletion expense recognized for the current year."},{"t":"a","x":"depletion rate = $5,400,000 ÷ 900,000 tons = $6.00/ton. Depletion for the year = 150,000 tons × $6.00 = $900,000, initially capitalized to Inventory and recognized as Cost of Goods Sold once the extracted ore is sold (all 150,000 tons were sold this year, so the full $900,000 is COGS)."}]}},"extras":[{"t":"p","x":"**BUS1103**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Financial Accounting**"},{"t":"p","x":"Complete Revision Notes & Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"Chapters 1–4, Chapter 6 (Parts 1–3: Sales Revenue, Receivables, Control of Cash), Chapter 7 (Cost of Goods Sold & Inventory) and Chapter 8 (Property, Plant & Equipment; Intangibles; Natural Resources) — 1st Term 2026/27, Lingnan University"},{"t":"p","x":"Compiled from all course materials shared on Moodle, with original complementary practice questions and full worked answer keys."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Table of Contents"},{"t":"p","x":"**Course Overview**"},{"t":"p","x":"1. Course Identification · 2. Instructor(s) · 3. Assessment Structure · 4. Textbook & Connect Access"},{"t":"p","x":"5. Weekly Teaching Schedule · 6. Chapter 5 Gap · 7. Textbook Exclusions/Additions · 8. Learning Outcomes · 9. Policies"},{"t":"p","x":"**Chapter 1 — Financial Statements and Business Decisions**"},{"t":"p","x":"Financial statements, GAAP/IFRS, accounting assumptions & principles, accounting ethics (HKICPA), official practice (E1-11/12/13), complementary exercises, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"**Chapter 2 — Investing and Financing Decisions and the Accounting System**"},{"t":"p","x":"Balance sheet concepts, transaction analysis, journal entries & T-accounts, trial balance, official practice (P2-1/2/3/5), complementary exercises, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"**Chapter 3 — Operating Decisions and the Accounting System**"},{"t":"p","x":"Accrual vs. cash accounting, revenue & expense recognition, worked transaction sets, official practice (E3-2/3/7, AP3-6), complementary exercises, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"**Chapter 4 — Adjustments, Financial Statements, and the Closing Process**"},{"t":"p","x":"4 types of adjustments, depreciation, the closing process (Income Summary method, full worked example), official practice notes, complementary exercises, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"(Chapter 5 is confirmed excluded from this course's syllabus — see Course Overview §6)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Chapter 6 (Part 1) — Sales Revenue**"},{"t":"p","x":"FOB terms, credit card sales, sales discounts, sales returns & allowances, net sales, official practice notes, complementary exercises, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"**Chapter 6 (Part 2) — Receivables**"},{"t":"p","x":"Classifying receivables, the allowance method for bad debts, percentage-of-credit-sales vs. aging methods, control over receivables, official practice (E6-15 solved), complementary exercises incl. ethics case, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"**Chapter 6 (Part 3) — Control of Cash (Bank Reconciliations)**"},{"t":"p","x":"Cash and cash equivalents, internal control over cash, bank reconciliation format and objectives, official practice notes, complementary exercise with full answer key, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"Chapter 7 — Cost of Goods Sold and Inventory"},{"t":"p","x":"Operating cycle, costs included in inventory, perpetual vs. periodic systems, recording purchases/sales/discounts/returns, the COGS equation, the four inventory costing methods (official Trekking illustration: FIFO/weighted average/specific identification), financial statement effects & consistency, lower of cost or net realizable value (LCNRV), official practice (E7-1 solved), complementary exercises, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"**Chapter 8 — Property, Plant, and Equipment; Intangibles; and Natural Resources**"},{"t":"p","x":"Classifying long-lived assets, acquisition cost, repairs vs. improvements, the three depreciation methods (straight-line, units-of-production, double-declining-balance), partial-year depreciation & changes in estimates, disposal of PPE, intangible assets & amortization, natural resources & depletion, official practice notes, complementary exercises, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"**Computerized Accounting Project — Assignment Brief (MYOB / QuickBooks)**"},{"t":"p","x":"September 2026 transactions for \"Success Company\", the 8 required steps of the full accounting cycle, due 2 Dec 2026"},{"t":"p","x":"**Mixed Exam-Style Practice Set — All Chapters**"},{"t":"p","x":"10 mixed questions (MCQ, short answer, journal entries, comprehensive problems) spanning Ch.1–4 and 6, with a full answer key"},{"t":"p","x":"**Appendix — Source Material Index & Known Gaps**"},{"t":"p","x":"Every source file mapped to its section, plus a consolidated list of content gaps in the Moodle materials"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Course Overview"},{"t":"p","x":"This section summarizes the course administration information from the Course Syllabus, Teaching Schedule, Week 1 introduction slides, and the Connect/textbook purchase notices shared on Moodle. It is here for reference — the study content itself starts in Chapter 1."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Course Identification"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Details"],["Course Title / Code","Financial Accounting — BUS1103"],["Institution","Lingnan University, Department of Accountancy"],["Term","1st Term 2026/27"],["Credits / Contact Hours","3 credits — 42 hours total (3-hour lecture per week)"],["Category","Foundation Core"],["Recommended Study Year","Year 1 / Year 2"],["Prerequisite / Co-requisite / Exclusion","NIL / NIL / NIL"],["Mode","Sectional approach (this document follows Section 4)"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Instructor(s)"},{"t":"p","x":"The course runs in several parallel sections, each with its own instructor. Section 4 (the section these lecture slides come from) is taught by:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Name","Contact"],["Dr. Tracy H. Y. Yeung (Section 4)","Email: tracyhyyeung@ln.edu.hk · Phone: 2616-8187 · Office: SEK 201/11, Simon and Eleanor Kwok Building · Consultation: Mon 2–4pm, Wed 10am–12noon (book by email in advance)"],["Other sections' instructors","Mr. Asamoah, Joseph Maxwell · Prof. Wang Rui Erik · Mr. Xie Yinjie"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Assessment Structure"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Component","Weight","Notes"],["Class Participation","5%","Attendance, contribution to discussion, preparedness (see rubric)"],["Online Assignments (Connect)","10%","Weekly; 2 attempts allowed (best score counts); need ≥40% and on-time submission for credit; no credit for late submission"],["Computerized Accounting Project (MYOB)","5%","Full accounting-cycle project; max 3 students/group; recommended to start after the midterm"],["Ethical Dilemma Analysis","5%","Given together with the midterm; based on HKICPA ethical principles"],["Mid-term Examination","25%","25 Oct (Sun), 12:00–14:30, Venue: Sin Cho Ming Function Hall (LCHLG01). MCQ + long problems. Covers Ch.1–4, Ch.6 (Sales Revenue), and Accounting Ethics"],["Final Examination","50%","Cumulative — Ch.1–12 excluding Ch.5. Long problems only. Closed book/notes"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Total: 100%. **Both exams are closed-book and closed-notes. Students absent from an exam receive zero unless valid documentary proof of exceptional circumstances is provided."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Textbook & Connect Access"},{"t":"li","x":"Required textbook: Financial Accounting, McGraw-Hill Education, 12th International Student Edition, by Libby, Libby & Hodge."},{"t":"li","x":"Students must purchase ONE option: (i) E-edition = Connect access code only (with e-book access), or (ii) Bundle = printed book + Connect access code."},{"t":"li","x":"Connect access is required because online assignments (10% of the grade) are completed through it."},{"t":"li","x":"Connect license validity: 180 days from registration; a 14-day free trial access is available in week 1 while waiting for the purchased code to arrive (results carry over once the code is redeemed)."},{"t":"li","x":"Ordering: through SUP Bookshop (Google Form, Visa/Mastercard only) or A&P Bookshop — see the purchase e-mail for the exact links; access-code lead time is 5–7 working days, so order in week 1."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Using Connect (quick reference)"},{"t":"li","x":"Register once via your section's Class URL using your Lingnan student e-mail, student ID, and English full name, then redeem your 20-digit access code (or start the 14-day free trial)."},{"t":"li","x":"The Connect e-book includes a search tool, adjustable font size, highlighting/notes/placemarks, a translation tool, and an \"AI Reader\" that can simplify text, explain graphs/tables, and check your understanding — useful for reviewing dense chapters."},{"t":"li","x":"Assignments: note the due date, number of attempts, and time limit before starting; \"Save & Exit\" preserves progress (but a timed assignment's clock keeps running)."},{"t":"li","x":"Tech support: +80-881-39190 (Asia) or international-asia@mheducation.com."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Weekly Teaching Schedule"},{"t":"p","x":"(Subject to revision at the instructor's discretion based on course progress.)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Week","Ch.","Topic","Class Practice Questions","Online Assignment Due"],["1","1","Financial Statements and Business Decisions","E1-11, E1-12, E1-13","18 Sep"],["2","2","Investing and Financing Decisions and the Accounting System","P2-1, P2-2, P2-3, P2-5","2 Oct"],["3","3","Operating Decisions and the Accounting System","E3-2, E3-3, E3-7, AP3-6","—"],["4","4","Adjustments, Financial Statements, and the Closing Process","E4-8, E4-9, P4-7, COMP4-2","9 Oct"],["5","6","Sales Revenue","E6-3, P6-7","16 Oct"],["6","6","Receivables","E6-15, E6-21, E6-22, P6-3","—"],["7","6","Cash","E6-28, P6-5, P6-6","30 Oct"],["—","—","Midterm Test — 25 Oct (Sun), 12:00–14:30, Sin Cho Ming Function Hall. Covers Ch.1–4, Ch.6 (Sales Revenue), and Ethics","",""],["8","7","Cost of Goods Sold and Inventory","E7-1, E7-13, E7-22","6 Nov"],["9","8","Property, Plant, and Equipment; Intangibles; Natural Resources","E8-5, E8-10, E8-16, E8-19, E8-21, E8-24","13 Nov"],["10","9 & 10","Liabilities & Bond Securities","Ch.9: E9-4, E9-9, P9-2; Ch.10: P10-2","20 Nov"],["11","11","Stockholders' Equity","E11-15, E11-21, E11-22, P11-2, P11-5, P11-8","27 Nov"],["12","12","Statement of Cash Flows","E12-1, E12-4, E12-10, E12-16, AP12-2","30 Nov"],["13","—","Review — Computerized project due 12:00 noon, 2 Dec (Wed)","",""]]},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ This document covers Chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, and 6 (Part 1 — Sales Revenue only), matching the lecture files that were shared on Moodle. Chapters 6 (Receivables & Cash), 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, and 12 were not shared as lecture files at the time of extraction and are NOT covered here."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Chapter 5 — Confirmed Gap"},{"t":"p","x":"The official \"Course Learning Objectives: Exclusions/Additions\" table in the Teaching Schedule explicitly states for Chapter 5: \"All excluded.\" No Chapter 5 lecture exists because the whole chapter is dropped from this course's syllabus — this is not a missing file, it is intentional. The teaching schedule jumps directly from Chapter 4 to Chapter 6."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7. Textbook Content Exclusions/Additions (relative to the standard textbook)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Chapter","Excluded","Added"],["1","N/A","HKICPA code of ethics"],["2","2.5 (current ratio), 2.6","—"],["3","3.6, 3.7","—"],["4","4.2 (earnings per share), 4.3","—"],["5","All excluded","—"],["6","6.3","—"],["7","7.5, 7.6","—"],["8","8.1 (fixed asset turnover ratio), 8.7","—"],["9","9.2, 9.3 (time value of money), 9.5, 9.7, 9.8","—"],["10","10.3 – 10.8","—"],["11","11.2, 11.5, 11.9","—"],["12","12.3, 12.5","—"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Note: Financial ratios and ratio analysis are generally not covered in this course."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8. Learning Outcomes (from the Syllabus)"},{"t":"p","x":"On completion of the course, students should be able to:"},{"t":"li","x":"Discuss the role of accounting in business organizations and capital markets."},{"t":"li","x":"Make judgments on the ethical issues involved in financial reporting."},{"t":"li","x":"Discuss the accounting assumptions, principles and limitations underlying the preparation of financial statements, and apply them to different circumstances."},{"t":"li","x":"Prepare the four essential financial statements, explain their uses and inter-relationships."},{"t":"li","x":"Manually work through the accounting cycle and use accounting software (MYOB) to prepare financial statements from a given set of transactions."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9. Course Policies Worth Remembering"},{"t":"li","x":"Visit Moodle at least 3 times a week — lecture notes, suggested solutions, and announcements are posted there regularly."},{"t":"li","x":"Read the relevant chapter(s) before each class; this is when \"in-class practice\" and \"class practice questions\" get reviewed."},{"t":"li","x":"Homework/online assignments: 2 attempts, higher score counts, unlimited time before the deadline, save-and-exit supported."},{"t":"li","x":"Both exams are closed-book, closed-notes; the final exam is cumulative."},{"t":"li","x":"Academic integrity: written assignments go through Turnitin; plagiarism (including AI-generated text presented as one's own without following the university's GAI usage guidelines) is a serious disciplinary matter."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Computerized Accounting Project — Assignment Brief (MYOB / QuickBooks)"},{"t":"p","x":"Due: Wednesday, 2 December 2026, 12:00 (noon). This is the Computerized Accounting Project referenced in the Course Syllabus; you may complete it using either (A) MYOB or (B) QuickBooks — setup/registration guides for both (A1-A3 MYOB; B1-B2 QuickBooks) are posted on Moodle alongside the assignment questions."},{"t":"p","x":"Scenario: on September 1, 2026, you start a consultancy business named \"Success+[your student ID]+Company\". September 2026 transactions:"},{"t":"li","x":"Sep 1 — Invested $183,000 cash in exchange for common stock."},{"t":"li","x":"Sep 2 — Bought computer equipment for $12,480 cash (cheque 001)."},{"t":"li","x":"Sep 3 — Bought office supplies on credit from Amy Co. for $5,000 (invoice V001)."},{"t":"li","x":"Sep 8 — Earned $154,500 service revenue from Mr. Yu (invoice C001), receiving 2/3 in cash immediately."},{"t":"li","x":"Sep 15 — Paid $6,311 cash sundry expenses to RST Company (cheque 002)."},{"t":"li","x":"Sep 19 — Paid office rent for the month, $35,800 cash (cheque 003)."},{"t":"li","x":"Sep 22 — Received $28,900 cash from a client for consultancy services to be provided in December 2026 (unearned revenue)."},{"t":"li","x":"Sep 27 — Paid $3,210 cash for the September 3 purchase (cheque 004)."},{"t":"p","x":"Required (8 steps, mirrors the full accounting cycle from Chapters 2-4): (1) journalize all September transactions (cash transactions pass through the Bank account); (2) unadjusted trial balance at Sep 30; (3) adjusting entries — all office supplies used up, $260 depreciation expense on the computer equipment — and post to the ledger; (4) adjusted trial balance; (5) Income Statement; (6) Balance Sheet; (7) closing entries, posted to the ledger; (8) post-closing trial balance at Sep 30."},{"t":"p","x":"This is exactly the Chapter 2-4 accounting cycle you already know (journal entries → trial balance → adjustments → financial statements → closing) — the only new skill is entering it into MYOB or QuickBooks instead of by hand. An Honor Declaration Form (naming the student and section) must be signed and submitted with the assignment."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Mixed Exam-Style Practice Set — All Chapters"},{"t":"p","x":"10 questions spanning Chapters 1–4 and 6 (Part 1), mixing multiple choice, short answer, journal entries, and one comprehensive problem — similar in mix and difficulty to the midterm (which covers exactly this material: Ch.1–4, Ch.6 Sales Revenue, and Ethics). Attempt every question before checking the answer key."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Questions"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which of the following would NOT appear on a classified balance sheet?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Accounts Payable   B. Cost of Goods Sold   C. Retained Earnings   D. Prepaid Insurance"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A company receives $9,000 cash on 1 October for services to be rendered evenly over the next 6 months. Under accrual accounting, how much revenue should be recognized by 31 December of the same year?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. $0   B. $1,500   C. $4,500   D. $9,000"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which statement about Accumulated Depreciation is correct?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. It is an expense account closed at year-end.   B. It is a contra-asset account with a normal credit balance.   C. It appears on the income statement.   D. It increases with a debit."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Fern Co. had gross sales of $50,000, sales returns and allowances of $2,000, sales discounts of $800, and credit card discounts of $500 (credit card discounts are SG&A expense). What is net sales?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. $46,700   B. $47,200   C. $48,000   D. $49,500"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Name the accounting assumption or principle illustrated in each: (a) A Hong Kong company reports all figures in HKD without adjusting for inflation. (b) A firm records a machine at the $200,000 it paid, even though it is now believed to be worth $230,000. (c) A firm delays recognizing a $50,000 sale until the goods are actually shipped to the customer, even though the order was placed and paid for earlier."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Journal entries)"},{"t":"p","x":"Journalize the following independent transactions for Willow Corp.: (a) Issued 500 shares of $1 par common stock for $12 cash per share. (b) Purchased $3,000 of supplies on account. (c) Paid $2,000 cash for the current month's rent. (d) Provided $6,000 of services on account. (e) Received $4,500 cash from a customer as an advance for services to be performed next month."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q7 (Adjusting entries)"},{"t":"p","x":"At year-end, Willow Corp. (from Q6) has these additional facts: (i) $800 of the $3,000 supplies purchased in (b) remain on hand. (ii) One month has passed on the advance received in (e) — no services have yet been performed. (iii) The company estimates $250 of interest has accrued on a bank loan, to be paid next year. Prepare the 3 adjusting entries. (Hint for (ii): re-read the deferred revenue rule carefully before answering.)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q8 (Comprehensive problem)"},{"t":"p","x":"Cascade Bikes, Inc. — adjusted trial balance extract at 31 December: Service revenue $84,000; Rental revenue $2,000; Wages expense $31,000; Rent expense $9,600; Depreciation expense $4,400; Utilities expense $1,900; Dividends declared $3,000; Retained Earnings (beginning) $18,500. Required: (a) Compute net income. (b) Prepare the 4 closing journal entries using the Income Summary method. (c) Compute ending Retained Earnings."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q9 (Sales revenue comprehensive)"},{"t":"p","x":"Granite Hardware Ltd. had these June transactions: sold $20,000 of goods on account, terms 3/10, n/30; the customer returned $2,000 of goods 2 days later; the customer paid the remaining balance within the discount period. Separately, the store had $6,000 of credit card sales, with a 2.8% processing fee. Required: (a) journalize all events. (b) compute June net sales."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q10 (Ethics — short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"A junior accountant is told by their manager to record a large customer order as a completed sale in December, even though the goods will not ship until January, in order to hit the year's sales target. Identify (a) the accounting principle being violated, and (b) the most relevant HKICPA ethical principle(s) at stake."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Mixed Practice Set — Full Answer Key"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A1"},{"t":"a","x":"B. Cost of Goods Sold is an income-statement (expense) account, not a balance ssheet item. The other three are all balance sheet accounts (liability, stockholders' equity, and asset, respectively)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A2"},{"t":"a","x":"C, $4,500. $9,000 ÷ 6 months = $1,500/month earned. From 1 Oct to 31 Dec = 3 months (Oct, Nov, Dec) → 3 × $1,500 = $4,500 recognized as revenue; the remaining $4,500 stays as Unearned Revenue (a liability) at year-end."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A3"},{"t":"a","x":"B. Accumulated Depreciation is a contra-asset — it is a permanent balance sheet account (not closed at year-end, unlike expenses) that carries a normal CREDIT balance, the opposite of a regular asset."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A4"},{"t":"a","x":"B, $47,200. Net sales = Gross sales $50,000 − Sales returns and allowances $2,000 − Sales discounts $800 = $47,200. The $500 credit card discount is excluded because it is recorded as SG&A expense, not as a reduction of net sales."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A5"},{"t":"li","x":"(a) Monetary unit assumption."},{"t":"li","x":"(b) Historical cost (measurement) principle."},{"t":"li","x":"(c) Revenue recognition principle (revenue is recognized on delivery/transfer of goods, not on order or cash receipt)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A6"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(a)","Cash (+A)","6,000",""],["","Common Stock (+SE)","","500"],["","Additional Paid-in Capital (+SE)","","5,500"],["","500 shares × $12 = $6,000 total; par value 500 × $1 = $500; the remainder $5,500 is APIC.","500 shares × $12 = $6,000 total; par value 500 × $1 = $500; the remainder $5,500 is APIC.","500 shares × $12 = $6,000 total; par value 500 × $1 = $500; the remainder $5,500 is APIC."],["(b)","Supplies (+A)","3,000",""],["","Accounts Payable (+L)","","3,000"],["(c)","Rent Expense (+E, −SE)","2,000",""],["","Cash (−A)","","2,000"],["(d)","Accounts Receivable (+A)","6,000",""],["","Service Revenue (+R, +SE)","","6,000"],["(e)","Cash (+A)","4,500",""],["","Unearned Revenue (+L)","","4,500"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A7"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(i)","Supplies Expense (+E, −SE)","2,200",""],["","Supplies (−A)","","2,200"],["","3,000 − 800 on hand = 2,200 used.","3,000 − 800 on hand = 2,200 used.","3,000 − 800 on hand = 2,200 used."],["(ii)","— No adjusting entry required.","",""],["(iii)","Interest Expense (+E, −SE)","250",""],["","Interest Payable (+L)","","250"]]},{"t":"p","x":"(ii) explanation: this is a Deferred (Unearned) Revenue. An adjusting entry moving it into revenue is only needed once the service has actually been performed/earned. The scenario explicitly states no services have yet been performed, so the full $4,500 correctly remains as Unearned Revenue (a liability) — no entry is required yet, even though a month has passed. This tests whether you distinguish \"time has passed\" from \"the performance obligation has been satisfied\"."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A8"},{"t":"p","x":"**(a) Net income: **Revenues (84,000 + 2,000) − Expenses (31,000 + 9,600 + 4,400 + 1,900) = 86,000 − 46,900 = $39,100."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Step 1","Service Revenue","84,000",""],["","Rental Revenue","2,000",""],["","Income Summary","","86,000"],["Step 2","Income Summary","46,900",""],["","Wages Expense","","31,000"],["","Rent Expense","","9,600"],["","Depreciation Expense","","4,400"],["","Utilities Expense","","1,900"],["Step 3","Income Summary","39,100",""],["","Retained Earnings","","39,100"],["Step 4","Retained Earnings","3,000",""],["","Dividends Declared","","3,000"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**(c) Ending Retained Earnings: **$18,500 + $39,100 − $3,000 = $54,600."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A9"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Sale","Accounts Receivable (+A)","20,000",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","20,000"],["Return","Sales Returns and Allowances","2,000",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","2,000"],["Collection","Cash (+A)","17,460",""],["","Sales Discounts","540",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","18,000"],["","Discount on remaining balance: (20,000 − 2,000) × 3% = 540.","Discount on remaining balance: (20,000 − 2,000) × 3% = 540.","Discount on remaining balance: (20,000 − 2,000) × 3% = 540."],["Card sales","Cash (+A)","5,832",""],["","SG&A Expense — credit card discount (+E, −SE)","168",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","6,000"],["","Fee = 6,000 × 2.8% = 168.","Fee = 6,000 × 2.8% = 168.","Fee = 6,000 × 2.8% = 168."]]},{"t":"p","x":"**(b) June net sales: **Sales revenue (20,000 + 6,000) − Sales returns and allowances 2,000 − Sales discounts 540 = $23,460. (The $168 card fee is SG&A expense and does not reduce net sales.)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A10"},{"t":"li","x":"(a) Principle violated: the revenue recognition principle — revenue must be recognized when goods/services are actually transferred to the customer, not before. Recording the sale in December when shipment happens in January overstates December's revenue and net income."},{"t":"li","x":"(b) HKICPA principles at stake: Integrity (being party to a materially misleading statement) and Objectivity (resisting pressure from the manager to bend the numbers to hit a target); Professional Behaviour is also relevant if it becomes a pattern that could discredit the profession. The correct action is to decline to record the sale early, explain the applicable principle to the manager, and escalate if the pressure continues."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Appendix — Source Material Index & Known Gaps"},{"t":"p","x":"For transparency and so you can go back to the original Moodle files if needed, here is what every one of the 23 source files became in this document, and a consolidated list of every content gap flagged throughout."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Source File Index"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Original Moodle file","Where it went in this document"],["#Week1 Introduction 1st-Term 2026-27 (S4).txt","Course Overview (instructor contact, class arrangement, assessment overview)"],["(a) [BUS1103 Textbook E-edition Purchase]...txt","Course Overview §4 (Textbook & Connect Access)"],["(b) [User Guide] Connect FDOC LU_BUS1103...txt","Course Overview §4 (Connect quick reference)"],["Course Syllabus and Rubrics...txt","Course Overview §1, §3, §8, §9"],["BUS1103 Teaching Schedule_1st term 2026-27.txt","Course Overview §5, §6, §7 (schedule, Ch.5 gap, exclusions/additions)"],["Lecture-Chapter1.txt","Chapter 1 — full notes + ethics case study"],["Accounting Assumptions and Principles [Q and A].txt","Chapter 1 §1.7 (matching Q&A)"],["ClassPracticeQuestions_E1-11_E1-12_E1-13.txt","Chapter 1 §1.7 (flagged as blank/unextractable)"],["Suggested-solutions_E1-11_E1-12_E1-13.txt","Chapter 1 §1.7 (official answer key)"],["Lecture-Chapter2 (after class).txt","Chapter 2 — full notes"],["Ch2-Worksheet (P2-1_P2-2_P2-3_P2-5).txt","Chapter 2 §2.11 (official question template)"],["ClassPracticeQuestions_P2-1_P2-2_P2-3_P2-5.txt","Chapter 2 §2.11 (flagged as blank/unextractable)"],["Suggested-solutions_P2-1_P2-2_P2-3_P2-5.txt","Chapter 2 §2.11 (official answer key)"],["Lecture-Chapter3 (after class).txt","Chapter 3 — full notes"],["Ch3-Worksheet (E3-2_E3-3_E3-7_AP3-6).txt","Chapter 3 §3.7 (official question template)"],["Suggested-solutions_E3-2_E3-3_E3-7_AP3-6.txt","Chapter 3 §3.7 (official answer key)"],["Lecture-Chapter4 (after class).txt","Chapter 4 — full notes"],["[Note] Closing entries using Income summary.txt","Chapter 4 §4.6 (FastForward worked example)"],["Chapter4-Worksheet (E4-8_E4-9_P4-7).txt","Chapter 4 §4.7 (flagged — no solution found)"],["Chapter4-Worksheet (COMP4-2).txt","Chapter 4 §4.7 (flagged — no solution found)"],["Lecture-Ch6-Part1-Sales Revenue.txt","Chapter 6 (Part 1) — full notes"],["Ch6-Part1_Sales Revenue_Worksheet (E6-3_P6-7).txt","Chapter 6 §6.5 (flagged — no data/solution found)"],["ClassPracticeQuestions_E6-3 (add-requirement).txt","Chapter 6 §6.5 (official instructions)"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Files added on 13 October 2026 (this update):"},{"t":"li","x":"Suggested-solutions_E4-8_E4-9_P4-7_COMP4-2.pdf -> §4.7 (official solutions)"},{"t":"li","x":"[Reference] COMP4-2 (req 6-using Income summary).pdf -> §4.7 (Income Summary variant)"},{"t":"li","x":"Lecture-Ch6-Part2-Receivables (after class).pdf -> §6.8-6.11"},{"t":"li","x":"Ch6-2_Receivables_Worksheet (E6-15_E6-21_E6-22_P6-3).pdf -> §6.12"},{"t":"li","x":"Suggested-solutions_E6-15.pdf -> §6.12"},{"t":"li","x":"Additional Ethics practice question.pdf + (suggested answer).pdf -> §6.13 (C6.9)"},{"t":"li","x":"Lecture-Ch6-Part3-Control-of-Cash.pdf -> §6.15-6.18"},{"t":"li","x":"Ch6-3_Control-of-cash_Worksheet (E6-28_P6-5_P6-6).pdf -> §6.19"},{"t":"li","x":"Lecture-Ch7-COSG-and-Inventory_Part1.pdf -> §7.1-7.7"},{"t":"li","x":"Computerized Accounting Assignment Questions_2026-27T1.pdf -> Computerized Accounting Project section"},{"t":"li","x":"A1/A2/A3 (MYOB) and B1/B2 (QuickBooks) setup & registration guides -> referenced in the Computerized Accounting Project section (software installation guides, not academic content; not reproduced in full)"},{"t":"p","x":"Files added on 3 November 2026 (this update):"},{"t":"li","x":"Suggested-solutions_E6-3_P6-7.pdf -> §6.5 (official solutions)"},{"t":"li","x":"Suggested-solutions_E6-15_E6-21_E6-22_P6-3.pdf -> §6.12 (official solutions for E6-21, E6-22, P6-3)"},{"t":"li","x":"Suggested-solutions_E6-28_P6-5_P6-6_Extra-Qs.pdf -> §6.19-6.20 (official solutions, plus the two official Extra Questions on Bank Reconciliation)"},{"t":"li","x":"Extra Question on COGS and Inventory.pdf -> §7.8 (Beacon Company, official)"},{"t":"li","x":"Extra Questions Worksheets-Bank Reconciliation.pdf -> §6.20 (C6.11, C6.12)"},{"t":"li","x":"Lecture-Ch7-COSG-and-Inventory (Part2).pdf -> §7.9-7.11 (Trekking illustration, financial statement effects, LCNRV)"},{"t":"li","x":"Ch7-Worksheet (E7-1_E7-13_E7-22_Add-Q).pdf -> §7.8 (official template; E7-13/E7-22 remain blank)"},{"t":"li","x":"Suggested-solutions_E7-1.pdf -> §7.8 (official solution)"},{"t":"li","x":"Lecture-Chapter8.pdf -> Chapter 8, full notes (§8.1-8.9)"},{"t":"li","x":"Ch8-Worksheet (E8-5_E8-10_E8-16_E8-19_E8-21_E8-24_Extra-Qs).pdf -> §8.10 (official template; E8-5/E8-10/E8-16/E8-19/E8-21/E8-24 remain blank)"},{"t":"li","x":"Extra Questions on PPE.pdf -> §8.10 (change-in-estimate and sale-of-fixed-assets questions, official, fully solved)"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Consolidated List of Content Gaps"},{"t":"li","x":"Chapter 5 is entirely absent by design — the official \"Exclusions/Additions\" table states \"Chapter 5: All excluded\" from this course's syllabus. This is confirmed, not a missing file."},{"t":"li","x":"Mise a jour (13 oct. 2026) : Chapitre 6 Part 2 (Receivables) et Part 3 (Control of Cash), ainsi que le Chapitre 7 Part 1 (Cost of Goods Sold and Inventory), ont ete ajoutes a ce document. **Mise a jour (3 nov. 2026) : les corriges officiels manquants pour E6-3/P6-7, E6-21/E6-22/P6-3 et E6-28/P6-5/P6-6 ont ete trouves et integres ; le Chapitre 7 a ete complete (methodes de costing chiffrees, LCNRV) ; le Chapitre 8 (Property, Plant & Equipment ; Intangibles ; Natural Resources) a ete ajoute en integralite.** Chapitres 9, 10, 11 et 12 restent non couverts a ce jour, faute de fichiers partages sur Moodle pour ces parties."},{"t":"li","x":"Chapter 1's official class practice questions (E1-11, E1-12, E1-13): the original question-prompt text extracted as blank/image-only pages. Only the official suggested-solutions file was usable, and its content is presented in full in §1.7; the task descriptions there are reconstructed from what the solutions imply."},{"t":"li","x":"Mise a jour (13 oct. 2026) : le fichier officiel de solutions pour E4-8, E4-9, P4-7 et COMP4-2 a ete partage sur Moodle et integre en section 4.7 (y compris la variante Income Summary pour COMP4-2)."},{"t":"li","x":"Chapter 6 Part 1's official class practice questions (E6-3, P6-7): resolved 3 nov. 2026 — the official Suggested-solutions file was found and its full solution is now integrated in §6.5."},{"t":"li","x":"Chapter 6 Part 2's official exercises E6-21, E6-22 and P6-3: resolved 3 nov. 2026 — the official Suggested-solutions file for all three was found and is now integrated in §6.12 (E6-15's solution was already present from the previous update)."},{"t":"li","x":"Chapter 6 Part 3's official exercises E6-28, P6-5 and P6-6: resolved 3 nov. 2026 — the official Suggested-solutions file was found and is now integrated in §6.19, together with two new official \"Extra Question on Bank Reconciliation\" problems (Emporium Company, Presto Ltd) added as C6.11 and C6.12 in §6.20."},{"t":"li","x":"Chapter 7: resolved 3 nov. 2026 — the second lecture deck (inventory costing methods chiffrees via the Trekking Mountain Bikes illustration, financial statement effects, and lower of cost or net realizable value) has now been shared and is integrated in §§7.9-7.11; the official E7-1 solution and the official Beacon Company COGS problem are integrated in §7.8. E7-13 and E7-22 remain blank worksheet templates with no official solutions file found — complementary exercises C7.1-C7.5 (and the Beacon Company problem) cover the same skills with full worked solutions."},{"t":"li","x":"Chapter 8's official practice worksheet (E8-5, E8-10, E8-16, E8-19, E8-21, E8-24) contains only blank answer templates — no official Suggested-solutions file for these six exercises was found on Moodle. Flagged in §8.10; the two official \"Extra Questions on PPE\" (change in depreciation estimate; sale of fixed assets) were found with full official solutions and are integrated in §8.10, and complementary exercises C8.1-C8.4 in §8.11 cover the same six skills (comparing depreciation methods, disposal, intangibles, depletion) with full worked solutions."},{"t":"li","x":"Chapter 6's Concept Check MC#1 and MC#2 (in the lecture slides) were posed without an answer key in the source file — this document computes and shows the correct answer with full reasoning in §6.4; cross-check against the instructor's own key if one is later released."},{"t":"li","x":"Several source files were OCR-extracted from scanned/photographed content (notably the textbook purchase e-mail and the Accounting Assumptions Q&A) and contained minor garbling (broken words, stray characters). Where this affected substance, the content was reconstructed using standard accounting knowledge and cross-checked against the parallel clean sources (e.g., the Chapter 1 lecture slides, which cover the same 9 assumptions/principles cleanly)."},{"t":"p","x":"If your instructor releases official solutions for the Chapter 4 and Chapter 6 exercises flagged above, it is worth updating this document (or your own notes) with the real numbers — the skills and method shown here are correct and exam-ready, but the specific dollar figures for those particular textbook problems could not be verified against an official key."}]},"BUS2108":{"file":"BUS2108_Global_Business_Environment_Synthese_et_Exercices.docx","mtime":"2026-10-11","lessons":{"1":{"title":"Globalization","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.1 What is Globalization?"},{"t":"p","x":"The learning objectives of the chapter: understand what globalization means, recognise its main drivers, describe the changing nature of the global economy, explain the main arguments in the debate on its impact, and understand how it creates opportunities and challenges for management practice."},{"t":"p","x":"Globalization also covers the economic and social changes that have come with trade and technology. People, money, material goods, ideas and even disease travel in greater numbers and with greater speed than ever. Globalization offers business opportunities."},{"t":"p","x":"Globalization: the shift toward a more integrated and interdependent world economy — away from a world of largely self-contained national economies and toward one with far lower barriers to the cross-border flow of goods, services, capital, people and ideas."},{"t":"p","x":"Evidence: trade and foreign investment have grown much faster than world output for over fifty years, alongside the rise of supra-national coordinating bodies (WTO, IMF, World Bank)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Two Facets of Globalization"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Globalization of markets","The merging of historically distinct national markets into one global marketplace, as consumer tastes converge on some global norm. Splits into B2C (e.g. Coca-Cola, McDonald's, IKEA, Starbucks, Apple) and B2B. Most global of all are markets for undifferentiated industrial commodities: oil, aluminium, wheat."],["Globalization of production","Sourcing goods/services from wherever in the world offers the best combination of cost and quality of factors of production (labour, energy, land, capital) — e.g. Boeing's Dreamliner is built from components sourced worldwide. Impediments: formal/informal trade barriers, FDI barriers, transport costs, political/economic risk, and the managerial challenge of coordinating a dispersed supply chain."]]},{"t":"p","x":"The globalization of production aims at a lower overall cost structure and at improving the quality or functionality of a product to compete more effectively. Early outsourcing was mainly for manufacturing; modern communications technology now lets companies outsource services too. Examples in the lecture: Samsung's semiconductor locations worldwide, and Starbucks coffee sold around the world and served through the Nestlé partnership."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Emergence of Global Institutions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Institution","Role"],["WTO","Polices the world trading system; enforces WTO treaty rules; successor to GATT. Covered ~164 nations / 98% of world trade (2017)."],["IMF","Maintains order in the international monetary system; acts as lender of last resort, typically requiring policy reform in return for loans."],["World Bank","Promotes economic development through low-interest loans; generally seen as less politically controversial than the IMF."],["UN","Maintains international peace/security, develops friendly relations among nations, promotes human rights and cooperation; 193 member states."],["G20","Finance ministers/central bank governors of the 19 largest economies + the EU (~90% of global GDP); became the main forum for coordinated crisis response after 2008–09."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Global institutions manage, regulate and police the global marketplace and promote multinational treaties (written agreements governing the global business system), covering sustainable business (climate change), links with corporate social responsibility, digital innovation and consensus at national levels."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Two Drivers of Globalization"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Declining trade and investment barriers**"},{"t":"p","x":"International trade = exporting goods/services to another country's consumers; FDI = investing resources to build/control business activities abroad. 1920s–30s protectionism gave way, after WWII, to progressive liberalization (GATT → Uruguay Round → WTO), and world trade has since grown faster than world production."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Technological change**"},{"t":"li","x":"Communications: the microprocessor drove explosive, low-cost computing power (Moore's Law) and modern telecommunications/the internet, making it far easier for buyers and sellers worldwide to find each other."},{"t":"li","x":"Transportation: commercial jets, containerization and \"superfreighters\" collapsed shipping costs and times."},{"t":"p","x":"Implications: cheaper transport makes geographically-dispersed production economical (globalization of production); cheap communication + transport create an electronic global marketplace, reduce effective \"cultural distance\", and converge consumer tastes (globalization of markets)."},{"t":"p","x":"In the 1920s and 1930s many nations raised barriers to protect domestic industries; after WWII advanced Western countries lowered them through GATT, the Uruguay Round and the WTO. World trade is predicted to grow faster than world production for the foreseeable future. Consequences of fast-growing trade: more companies disperse parts production, national economies become more intertwined, and the world becomes significantly wealthier. Lower transport costs let firms respond better to customer demand, and cheaper communications and transport also reduce cultural distance and converge consumer tastes and preferences. The microprocessor advanced telecommunications as well as computing, and the internet makes it easier for buyers and sellers to find each other."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.2 The Changing Demographics of the Global Economy"},{"t":"p","x":"As the world has globalized, the share of world production and trade has spread more evenly — multinationals from developing economies (and even \"mini-multinationals\") are now common, not just Western giants."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.3 The Globalization Debate: Pros and Cons"},{"t":"p","x":"There is no single right answer on whether globalization is, on balance, good or bad — both sides of the argument contain real truth, and the honest answer depends heavily on which stakeholder's perspective is being considered."},{"t":"p","x":"**Arguments FOR globalization**"},{"t":"li","x":"Allows country specialization → most efficient use of the world's resources (comparative advantage; developed fully in Chapter 6)"},{"t":"li","x":"Has helped lift very large numbers of people out of poverty and hunger, especially in export-oriented developing economies"},{"t":"li","x":"Gives consumers access to a wider range of higher-quality, cheaper goods"},{"t":"li","x":"Faster technology/capital diffusion to developing countries; faster spread of information and cultural exchange"},{"t":"p","x":"**Arguments AGAINST globalization**"},{"t":"li","x":"Environmental harm; the \"race to the bottom\" concern on environmental and labour standards"},{"t":"li","x":"Takes advantage of low-cost labour in developing countries while displacing higher-cost labour in developed ones — the Forbes article ties this to U.S. deindustrialization, a persistent U.S. trade deficit, and estimated job losses (e.g. ~3.2 million U.S. jobs linked to China's most-favoured-nation trading status, per one cited estimate)"},{"t":"li","x":"Erodes national sovereignty; creates unelected supranational \"elites\" (WTO/IMF) with real influence over domestic policy"},{"t":"li","x":"Widens pay inequality — CEOs of global firms see rising pay while many domestic workers face downward wage pressure or job loss"},{"t":"li","x":"Multinationals accused of exploiting tax havens, weak labour/safety standards, and — in some cases — using political influence in ways that raise sovereignty concerns"},{"t":"li","x":"Currency manipulation complaints: some countries are accused of holding their currency artificially low to gain an unfair trade advantage (this becomes a recurring theme in Chapters 10–11)"},{"t":"li","x":"Can spread communicable disease faster via increased travel; creates unrealistic consumption expectations in developing economies; associated with a perceived \"Americanization\" of culture and taste"},{"t":"p","x":"Conclusion (as taught): there is no absolute right or wrong answer — evaluate each claim critically, and recognise both sides have some truth to them."},{"t":"li","x":"Further arguments against, from the study guide: harm to workers' rights, and an \"Americanisation\" of culture and tastes."},{"t":"p","x":"In class, the debate topic was \"Globalisation is a good thing and should be encouraged\", played by a Pro team, a Cons team and a counter-argument team, and it counts toward participation. The Forbes article \"Pros and Cons of Globalization\" was recommended reading, and the teacher stressed there are no absolute pros or cons."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-assessment questions (voluntary, no model answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"1. What does \"international business\" mean, and how does it differ from globalisation?"},{"t":"p","x":"2. What are the major forces pushing globalisation? Why was globalisation difficult to achieve without them?"},{"t":"p","x":"3. Recent meetings of world trade bodies have seen protests against globalisation: who protests and why?"},{"t":"p","x":"4. To what extent do you agree or disagree with the protesters?"}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 1"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A company sources its raw cotton from India, has the fabric woven in Vietnam, and has the finished garments sewn in Bangladesh before selling worldwide. This is best described as an example of:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the globalization of markets"},{"t":"p","x":"B) the globalization of production"},{"t":"p","x":"C) mercantilism"},{"t":"p","x":"D) an optimal currency area"},{"t":"p","x":"E) trade diversion"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nSourcing goods/services from the locations that offer the best combination of cost and quality of factors of production is, by definition, the globalization of production."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Which institution is most accurately described as the \"lender of last resort\" in the global monetary system, typically requiring policy reform in exchange for loans?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) World Bank"},{"t":"p","x":"B) WTO"},{"t":"p","x":"C) IMF"},{"t":"p","x":"D) G20"},{"t":"p","x":"E) United Nations"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nThe IMF's core role is maintaining order in the international monetary system and lending to countries in balance-of-payments difficulty, usually attaching policy conditions."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. Which of the following is most likely to be cited by critics as a COST of globalization, rather than a benefit?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) wider consumer choice of cheaper goods"},{"t":"p","x":"B) faster diffusion of technology to poorer countries"},{"t":"p","x":"C) downward pressure on wages for import-competing workers in developed countries"},{"t":"p","x":"D) specialization according to comparative advantage"},{"t":"p","x":"E) more people lifted out of poverty in export-oriented developing economies"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nOptions A, B, D and E are standard pro-globalization arguments from the study guide/Forbes article; wage pressure on import-competing domestic labour is a standard anti-globalization argument."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Using the EY gaming-industry report discussed in the Lesson 1 participation exercise, explain (with two supporting facts) why the gaming industry is a strong illustration of BOTH the globalization of markets and the globalization of production.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Globalization of markets: gaming content and platforms (e.g. Fortnite, Roblox) attract a converging, worldwide audience of billions of players across mobile, PC and console — a genuinely global consumer market, reinforced by global metaverse ambitions and cross-border in-game payment/asset systems (NFTs).\n• Globalization of production: gaming companies source talent, technology and partnerships across borders (e.g. cross-industry partnerships cited by 43% of surveyed executives) and rely on globally dispersed supply chains for hardware (chips, consoles) — the same underlying forces (falling trade/tech barriers) that drive globalization generally."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. A Hong Kong exporter argues: \"Globalization has been unambiguously good for our business, so it must be good for everyone.\" Using material from this chapter, explain why this reasoning is flawed.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Globalization creates both winners and losers, and their identity depends on whose perspective is taken — a Hong Kong exporter (or a developing-country factory worker) may gain from wider market access and FDI inflows, while an import-competing worker in a developed economy may lose their job to lower-cost overseas production.\n• The course explicitly teaches that there is no single right answer on globalization's net effect; students should evaluate claims from multiple stakeholder perspectives (consumers, workers, governments, the environment) rather than generalising from one party's experience."}]},"2":{"title":"National Differences in Political, Economic and Legal Systems","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"\"Political economy\" refers to a country's political, economic and legal systems together — they are interdependent, and each is shaped by (and shapes) national culture. Never present one country's system as objectively \"better\" than another's; each country has made different choices based on what its government believes benefits its citizens."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.1 Political Systems"},{"t":"p","x":"International business is more complicated than domestic business. Political, legal and economic systems differ, countries vary in economic development and growth trajectories, and cultural, education and skill levels vary. These systems are interdependent, and societal culture affects political economy while political economy shapes societal culture."},{"t":"p","x":"The class rule: do not say that one political system or culture is better than another. Each country has its own political, legal, economic and cultural systems, and there is no absolute best."},{"t":"li","x":"**Political system** — the system of government in a nation, assessed by the degree to which it emphasises individualism as opposed to collectivism, and by the degree to which it is democratic or totalitarian."},{"t":"p","x":"Assessed along two dimensions: (a) collectivism vs. individualism, and (b) democracy vs. totalitarianism."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Collectivism vs. Individualism"},{"t":"li","x":"Collectivism (traced to Plato): the good of society as a whole is emphasised; individual rights may be sacrificed for social harmony/distribution. Socialism (traced to Karl Marx) advocates state ownership of the means of production; split in the early 20th century into Communism (violent revolution, totalitarian dictatorship — in worldwide retreat since the mid-1990s) and Social democracy (same collectivist goals pursued by democratic means)."},{"t":"li","x":"Individualism (traced to Aristotle, refined by Hume, Smith, Mill): individual economic and political freedom is paramount; government's role is limited (chiefly stopping one individual harming another); associated with democratic systems and free markets."},{"t":"p","x":"In a collectivist political system the government is strong and has significant responsibility for the distribution of wealth and support for the less advantaged. Communist China is collectivist, as are the social democracies of northern Europe. In an individualist system the rights of the individual are paramount and government mainly stops one individual directly harming another. The USA is highly individualist: people are generally free to do what they wish, but comparatively little government support is provided (see Reagan's final address, cited in the study guide). Individualism is developed further in the culture chapter."},{"t":"p","x":"Individualism stresses individual freedom and self-expression, and letting people pursue their self-interest to achieve the best overall good for society."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Democracy vs. Totalitarianism"},{"t":"li","x":"Democracy: government by the people, directly or via elected representatives (most common form = representative democracy). Features: free expression/media, regular elections, universal suffrage, limited terms, fair courts, a non-political bureaucracy/police/military, free access to state information. (Case: the U.S. — three branches, legislative/executive/judicial; federal/state/local government layers.)"},{"t":"li","x":"Totalitarianism: one person, party or group exercises absolute control over all spheres of life; opposition parties banned. Features: political repression, no free/fair elections, censored media, denial of civil liberties. Four forms: Communist totalitarianism, Theocratic totalitarianism (religious principles), Tribal totalitarianism (one tribe monopolises power), Right-wing totalitarianism (economic freedom allowed, political freedom restricted to block a communist path)."},{"t":"li","x":"Pseudo-democracies: hybrids where authoritarian elements capture much of the machinery of a nominally democratic state (example given in lecture: Russia under Vladimir Putin)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Representative democracy** — the most common form of democracy, in which elected representatives vote on behalf of their constituents, who can change them in periodic elections."},{"t":"li","x":"**Totalitarian state** — a state controlled by a particular individual, group or party that makes decisions with little room for dissent. China is an example of communist totalitarianism."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.2 Economic systems"},{"t":"p","x":"Closely linked to the political system is the economic system. There are three main forms."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Meaning"],["Command economy","The government controls all factors of production (it owns land, factories and capital and controls labour), decides what is produced and how goods are allocated, aiming to use the country's resources for the maximum benefit of society"],["Free-market economy","The government plays a very limited role; economic activity is governed by corporations and individuals within the market system, and what is produced and how it is allocated is governed by the market and the price mechanism. Hong Kong was traditionally seen as very free-market"],["Mixed economy","Most countries sit between the two extremes: the government keeps production and ownership of some parts of the economy (often healthcare, education, transportation, utilities) while other parts operate in the free market"]]},{"t":"p","x":"In general, more individualist countries lean toward the free-market end of the spectrum and collectivist countries tend to have higher levels of government control."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.3 Legal Systems"},{"t":"p","x":"A country's legal system regulates business practice, defines how transactions are executed, and sets the rights/obligations of the parties involved."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Common law","Based on tradition, precedent and custom; judiciary has flexibility to interpret law based on prior cases. Found in the UK and most former British colonies (incl. the U.S.). Contracts tend to be long and highly detailed, spelling out every contingency, because courts rely heavily on what was actually agreed."],["Civil law","Based on a detailed, codified set of laws. Found in 80+ countries (Germany, France, Japan, Russia, etc.). Contracts can be shorter/less specific because the code itself, not case-by-case precedent, settles most disputes."],["Theocratic law","Based on religious teachings; Islamic law is the most widely practised example."]]},{"t":"li","x":"The UN Convention on Contracts for the International Sale of Goods (CISG) creates a uniform set of rules for cross-border commercial contracts; adopting it signals that a country treats the Convention's rules as part of its own law."},{"t":"p","x":"**Property rights and corruption**"},{"t":"li","x":"Property rights: the legal rights over the use of a resource and any income derived from it. Threatened by private action (theft, piracy, blackmail) and public action (officials extorting income/assets — e.g. excessive taxation, forced licensing costs, uncompensated expropriation)."},{"t":"li","x":"Higher corruption is associated with lower FDI, lower trade, and lower economic growth."},{"t":"p","x":"**Intellectual property protection**"},{"t":"li","x":"Patents (exclusive rights to manufacture/use/sell an invention), Copyrights (rights of authors/artists/publishers over their work), Trademarks (protected names/designs distinguishing a firm's products)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Product safety & product liability**"},{"t":"li","x":"Product safety laws set required safety standards; product liability holds a firm/its officers responsible for injury, death or damage caused by a product. Liability laws are usually least extensive in less-developed countries, forcing firms to choose between home-country and host-country standards."},{"t":"p","x":"Legal systems are harder to categorise because they are idiosyncratic: each country has its own laws, regulations and methods of enforcement. The study guide compares common law and civil law. Common law courts rule largely on precedent (previous court decisions), so the judiciary has more flexibility in interpreting law and contracts need to be specific and detailed so that disputes can be decided. In civil law countries the laws themselves are more specific, courts have less room for interpretation, and contracts may be less detailed because contract law determines how a dispute is settled."},{"t":"p","x":"A business operating abroad must know the local legal system, with particular attention to contract law and property rights. Property rights indicate the degree of protection a firm has over its physical and intellectual assets, against seizure by other firms or by the government itself. Countries with a less established legal system and weak property rights are subject to corruption, which can affect the ability of multinationals to operate."},{"t":"li","x":"**Contract law** — the body of law that governs contract enforcement. A contract specifies the conditions under which an exchange is to occur and details the rights and obligations of the parties."},{"t":"li","x":"**Intellectual property** — property that is the product of intellectual activity (patents, copyrights, trademarks)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Product liability** — the rule that holds a firm and its officers responsible when a product causes injury, death or damage."},{"t":"p","x":"Did you know? It is illegal for Americans to bribe public officials to gain business in a foreign country, even if bribery is commonplace in that nation."},{"t":"p","x":"Discussion in the deck. Read the Guardian report on the big soda industry lobby fighting soda taxes, then, as a PepsiCo CEO, evaluate whether the US soda tax creates an opportunity or a risk for the business, and suggest solutions for sustaining business globally. There is no absolute right or wrong answer; the analysis should use political, legal, economic and globalisation issues."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.4 Managerial implications"},{"t":"li","x":"The political economy a country chooses strongly affects its attractiveness as a trading partner and an investment location."},{"t":"li","x":"Companies generally prefer a free-market system with well-established property rights and a well-functioning legal system. However, most such countries are already developed with strong competition, while countries with more government control and a less established legal system tend to be at an earlier stage of development with greater growth potential. Companies must balance opportunities and risks."},{"t":"li","x":"The political economy can raise ethical concerns for multinationals. High corruption, low legal protection and a lack of respect for worker and human rights may lead firms to redirect investment elsewhere (China remains an attractive location despite a still developing legal system)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.5 Illustrative Cases from the Lecture"},{"t":"li","x":"US political structure: three branches (legislative/Congress, executive/President, judicial/Federal courts) plus federal, state and local government layers; elections every two years (federal) and four years (presidential)."},{"t":"li","x":"North Korea: a highly centralised communist totalitarian state (Korean Workers' Party) — one of the poorest countries in the world yet sustains one of the largest militaries and an illicit nuclear-weapons programme."},{"t":"li","x":"Political factors affecting firms: e.g. President Trump threatening that Canada's Bombardier cannot sell aircraft in the U.S. unless it manufactures there (2026) — a direct example of political risk shaping market access."},{"t":"li","x":"Legal-environment cases: U.S. restrictions on Huawei telecoms/chip technology (national-security-driven legal barriers); the TikTok ban saga in the U.S. (legal/political risk to a foreign-owned platform); product liability cases (IKEA dresser recalls, Samsung Galaxy Note 7 recall, unsafe toys on Amazon)."},{"t":"li","x":"Corruption cases: Hong Kong's ICAC as a comparatively low-corruption benchmark; Singapore's rare political scandals (Action Party) vs. other political scandal examples discussed (Bill Clinton) as illustrations of how corruption/scandal risk varies and is monitored across political systems."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-assessment questions (voluntary, no model answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Choose two countries, one Asian and one European (for example South Korea and France), and find answers to the following."},{"t":"p","x":"1. What is each country's political system? How does it rank on individualism versus collectivism and on totalitarianism versus democratisation? Which party is in power and what policies does it follow? Does the political history still show in business conditions or institutions?"},{"t":"p","x":"2. How would you classify each economic system? Has it changed greatly in recent years?"},{"t":"p","x":"3. How strong are the legal systems? How well respected are property and intellectual property rights? How stringent are product safety and employee safety laws?"},{"t":"p","x":"4. How significant are the political and economic risks of investing in each country?"}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 2"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A country's legal system relies heavily on codified statutes rather than case-by-case judicial precedent, and contracts in that country tend to be relatively short because the code itself resolves most disputes. This country most likely has a:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) common law system"},{"t":"p","x":"B) civil law system"},{"t":"p","x":"C) theocratic law system"},{"t":"p","x":"D) totalitarian system"},{"t":"p","x":"E) collectivist economic system"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nThis is the defining feature of civil law systems (e.g. Germany, France, Japan), in contrast to common law's reliance on precedent and highly detailed contracts."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Which of the following is a defining feature of a totalitarian (rather than democratic) political system?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) universal adult suffrage"},{"t":"p","x":"B) a free and independent media"},{"t":"p","x":"C) opposition political parties are prohibited"},{"t":"p","x":"D) a non-political state bureaucracy"},{"t":"p","x":"E) regular, fair elections"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nBanning opposition parties and concentrating absolute power in one person/party/group is the core defining feature of totalitarianism."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. A foreign investor finds that local officials in a host country routinely demand unofficial payments before approving standard business licences. This is best described as:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) a common law contract dispute"},{"t":"p","x":"B) public-action corruption threatening property rights"},{"t":"p","x":"C) a tariff-rate quota"},{"t":"p","x":"D) an optimal currency area problem"},{"t":"p","x":"E) the Leontief paradox"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nPublic officials extorting payment for services they should provide as of right is the textbook definition of public-action threats to property rights."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Explain why a common law contract for an international sale of goods is typically much longer and more detailed than an equivalent civil law contract, and why this matters for a manager negotiating that contract.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• In common law systems, courts rule largely on precedent and the specific wording the parties agreed to, so anything not explicitly written into the contract may not be enforceable in the way a party expects; the contract itself must anticipate and spell out every likely contingency.\n• In civil law systems, an extensive statutory code already governs most contractual situations by default, so the contract can be shorter because the code — not the document — fills the gaps.\n• Managerial implication: a manager used to civil law norms who signs a \"thin\" contract in a common law jurisdiction risks being unprotected on issues the contract is silent on; conversely, over-specifying a civil law contract wastes negotiation time on points already settled by the code."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. A multinational is deciding whether to expand into two countries: Country X has weak property-rights protection and moderate corruption but very low costs and fast growth; Country Y has strong property-rights protection and low corruption but higher costs and slower growth. Using the framework from this chapter, outline the trade-off the firm faces.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Country Y is the classically \"attractive\" business environment (well-established legal system, protected property rights) but, precisely because it is attractive, is likely to already have strong competition and lower growth potential (a mature market).\n• Country X offers greater growth/first-mover upside precisely because it is less developed and less protected, but the firm faces materially higher political, legal and economic risk (weaker enforcement of contracts/IP, corruption exposure, possible expropriation).\n• The correct answer is not one absolute choice: the firm must weigh the opportunity (growth, lower costs) against the specific risks in Country X, and can partially manage those risks (e.g. joint ventures, insurance, staged investment) rather than avoiding the market outright."}]},"3":{"title":"National Differences in Economic Development","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"A country's political economy choices (Chapter 2) strongly shape its level of economic development and growth — and thus its attractiveness as a trading partner or FDI location."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.1 Measuring Economic Development"},{"t":"p","x":"The level of economic development affects a country's attractiveness as a market or production location. Differences in political, economic and legal systems influence the level of development and the attractiveness of doing business there. There is a general move toward democratic government, market-based economic reforms and legal systems that better enforce property rights."},{"t":"li","x":"**Gross domestic product (GDP)** — the total value of goods and services produced within a country, normally measured annually and per capita (value produced per person), expressed in US dollars. How much a country produces equals what it can consume, so GDP reflects living standards and the purchasing power of citizens."},{"t":"li","x":"**GDP per capita** — GDP divided by population. It shows the standard of living, whereas total GDP does not. The two are not the same thing."},{"t":"li","x":"**Gross national income (GNI)** — the income earned by a country's residents and government, including income earned abroad; the textbook's preferred measure. GNI is not the same as per capita income."},{"t":"li","x":"**Purchasing power parity (PPP)** — an adjustment that allows economists to compare economic productivity and standards of living between countries by correcting for differences in local prices. Some feel it does not reflect reality because of differences in local costs, taxes, tariffs and competition."},{"t":"p","x":"GNI and PPP data give a static picture of development; economic growth rates give a dynamic picture. GDP is a widely accepted measure (high-GDP countries are richer and more developed), but it is imperfect because it does not always reflect differences in living standards, which is why GDP is sometimes adjusted for PPP. Growth is often more important to governments than the level, because fast-growing countries offer growth in sales and profits."},{"t":"li","x":"**Human Development Index (HDI)** — the UN's yearly ranking of countries on a broader measure of development that includes economic measures and lifestyle factors such as health and lifespan."},{"t":"table","rows":[["GDP (Gross Domestic Product)","Total value of goods/services produced within a country in a year, usually expressed per capita in US$. Reflects a country's productivity and (roughly) its citizens' living standards and purchasing power. High-productivity societies (strong access to resources, technology, education, infrastructure) produce higher-value goods/services (chips, pharma, banking) and so have higher GDP per capita."],["GNI (Gross National Income)","The measure the textbook favours: total income earned by a country's residents/government, including income earned abroad (distinct from GDP, which measures output produced within the country's borders)."],["PPP adjustment","Corrects for cross-country differences in local prices. Example: a Hong Kong pizza-maker selling 5,000 pizzas at US$15 each contributes US$75,000 to GDP; a Filipino pizza-maker selling the same 5,000 pizzas at US$5 each (due to lower local prices/incomes) contributes only US$25,000 — despite identical real output. Table 3.1 in the textbook shows the U.S. is ~6× richer than China by nominal GDP but under 4× richer once adjusted for PPP."]]},{"t":"li","x":"Growth rate matters more than the static level: a high-GDP-growth country is far more attractive to international business (rising sales/profit opportunity) than a high-but-flat-GDP country."},{"t":"li","x":"Limitations of GDP: does not capture pollution, health, life expectancy or leisure time. Economist Amartya Sen argued development should be judged by people's capabilities and opportunities, not just material output; the UN's Human Development Index (HDI) blends economic measures with broader \"lifestyle\" indicators."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.2 Government Revenue Sources (Political Economy of Taxation)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Direct taxes**"},{"t":"li","x":"Personal income tax (progressive: higher earners pay a higher rate); Corporate income tax (on net profits); Capital gains tax (on profit from asset sales); Property tax; Inheritance/estate/gift taxes."},{"t":"p","x":"**Indirect taxes**"},{"t":"li","x":"VAT/GST (charged at each stage of the supply chain); Sales tax (single point-of-sale tax, e.g. Hong Kong's hotel-stay tax); Excise tax (alcohol, tobacco, fuel — raises revenue and discourages consumption); Customs/import duties (also a trade-policy tool, covered in Chapter 7)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Environmental/resource taxes**"},{"t":"li","x":"Carbon and environmental taxes on activities/emissions that damage the environment."},{"t":"p","x":"Sales tax examples in the deck include Hong Kong's hotel stay tax and plastic bag tax, vehicle purchase taxes in Singapore and airport departure taxes in many countries. VAT or GST is collected at each stage of the supply chain on the value added (clothing, supermarket products, electronic devices, restaurants in many countries). Excise taxes target alcohol, tobacco, fuel, firearms or gambling, both to raise revenue and to discourage consumption."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.3 What Drives Economic Growth? — The Three I's"},{"t":"li","x":"Incentives: people only work harder, take risks and innovate if they expect to keep the rewards. Free-market, individualist systems (where individuals retain their own returns) are more conducive to innovation and growth than command/collectivist systems where gains flow to the state."},{"t":"li","x":"Institutions: a strong, independent legal system with well-enforced property rights lets innovators protect and profit from their investment (without it being copied or expropriated, directly or via corruption)."},{"t":"li","x":"Innovation: growth ultimately requires people willing to try new processes/products and take on risk and investment — incentives and institutions are what make that risk-taking rational."},{"t":"p","x":"Consensus view: economic growth is highest in market-based economies with strong property rights; \"states in transition\" (e.g. China privatising state-owned enterprises and strengthening its legal system) illustrate countries moving toward this model."},{"t":"p","x":"A country that produces the same amount of the same goods year after year has zero growth. To grow, people must produce more (work harder) or produce items of higher value (work smarter). The three I's explain what leads a country to do so."},{"t":"p","x":"The study guide uses the potato example from the Marginal Revolution video \"Puzzle of Growth: Rich Countries and Poor Countries\": people work harder only if they receive the benefits of their effort. If the benefits of hard work or innovation were spread equally to every member of society, no one would have a reason to work harder, take risks or invest. Low taxes and individual freedom favour innovation, while high government control and heavy taxation discourage investment."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.4 Democracy and Growth: An Unsettled Relationship"},{"t":"p","x":"Observing that the world's richest countries (Japan, the U.S., much of Europe) are democracies does not prove democracy causes growth — it may instead be that development creates pressure for democratization. Some of the fastest-growing economies in modern history (China today; historically Taiwan and South Korea) grew fastest under authoritarian rule. The link between democracy and growth remains genuinely contested, not settled."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.5 Country Attractiveness"},{"t":"p","x":"Countries that open markets and protect property rights are attractive to international business through high growth; but many already-developed economies (with these features already in place) have comparatively low growth, while developing \"catch-up\" markets offer higher growth potential — at the cost of higher political, economic and legal risk. Businesses should not automatically avoid less-developed markets, but must price in and manage that additional risk."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.6 Illustrative Cases"},{"t":"li","x":"Canada's \"per-capita recession\" (2024): GDP can be technically flat/positive while GDP per capita falls if population is growing faster than output — a distinct concept from a technical recession (two consecutive quarters of negative GDP growth)."},{"t":"li","x":"Australia's Great Barrier Reef: government-managed as a national economic + environmental asset — supports 60,000+ tourism-dependent jobs and 2+ million visitors/year, illustrating how political-economy choices (regulation + investment) shape a natural resource's economic contribution."},{"t":"li","x":"U.S. corn agriculture: high domestic GDP contribution and federal subsidies (insurance, income support) support large-scale corn production, processing and export — a live example of government policy shaping a nation's comparative position in a specific product category."},{"t":"p","x":"The per-capita recession case (Canada, 2024) distinguishes GDP from GDP per capita, which shows the standard of living. The reasons it can feel like a recession include inflation, housing costs, shrinking products and higher product prices."},{"t":"p","x":"The Great Barrier Reef case shows political economy at work. The Great Barrier Reef Marine Park Authority reports to the minister responsible for the environment, and the 2015 Intergovernmental Agreement between the Prime Minister and the Queensland Premier provides the framework for the two governments to protect the reef. Marine tourism is the largest economic contributor from reef-dependent activities, supporting more than 60,000 jobs and more than 2 million tourists a year. Tourism is concentrated in about 7% of the park, and 86% of visits occur near Cairns, Port Douglas and the Whitsundays."},{"t":"p","x":"In the corn case, domestic uses include animal feed, processed food (corn oil, syrup) and gasoline or biofuel; farmers' large harvests feed manufacturers that make value-added products exported with state support, and the federal government provides subsidies such as corn insurance and income support programmes."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.7 Managerial implications"},{"t":"li","x":"**The macro environment influences market attractiveness** — a country's political, economic and legal environments clearly influence its attractiveness as a market or investment site. Usually, but not always, a democratic country with a market-based economy, protected property rights and limited corruption is a more attractive place to do business."},{"t":"li","x":"The political, economic and legal systems of a country also raise important ethical issues for international business."},{"t":"li","x":"Countries that do not meet these conditions are not always to be avoided, since money can be made there, but firms must adjust for higher political, economic and legal risk."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-assessment questions (voluntary, no model answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"1. What is the purpose of adjusting a country's GNP figures for purchasing power parity? Which figure matters more to an international businessperson, and why?"},{"t":"p","x":"2. What is the Human Development Index? Why is it not used more often than GDP as a measure of progress?"},{"t":"p","x":"3. Compare the growth rates of India and China over the past thirty years. Given that India is a democracy and China is totalitarian, does this suggest that democracy impedes growth?"},{"t":"p","x":"4. Identify real cases of significant political, economic or legal risk (three country examples, one for each type of risk)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 3"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Country A and Country B have identical nominal GDP per capita, but the cost of a typical basket of goods is 40% lower in Country B. Once adjusted for purchasing power parity, Country B's citizens:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) have a lower real standard of living than Country A's"},{"t":"p","x":"B) have a higher real standard of living than Country A's"},{"t":"p","x":"C) have exactly the same real standard of living"},{"t":"p","x":"D) cannot be compared without knowing the exchange rate regime"},{"t":"p","x":"E) have a lower nominal GDP than Country A"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nThe same nominal income buys more in a lower-price economy, so Country B's PPP-adjusted (real) living standard is higher, even though nominal GDP per capita is identical."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Which of the \"three I's\" of economic growth is most directly undermined when a country has a weak, corrupt judiciary that cannot reliably enforce patents?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Incentives only"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Institutions"},{"t":"p","x":"C) Innovation only, unrelated to institutions"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Indulgence (Hofstede dimension)"},{"t":"p","x":"E) Interest rates"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA weak/corrupt judiciary that cannot enforce property rights (like patents) is a direct institutional failure, which in turn undermines the incentive to innovate — but the textbook categorises the enforcement mechanism itself under \"institutions\"."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. Which statement best reflects the course's position on democracy and economic growth?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Democracy is a proven precondition for economic growth"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Authoritarian government always produces faster growth than democracy"},{"t":"p","x":"C) The direction of causality between democracy and growth is unsettled and contested"},{"t":"p","x":"D) GDP growth and democratization are unrelated phenomena with no historical overlap"},{"t":"p","x":"E) The Human Development Index proves democracy causes growth"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nThe study guide explicitly frames this relationship as \"more difficult, and more contentious\" — citing counterexamples on both sides (democracies that are rich; fast-growing authoritarian states) rather than asserting one direction of causality."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. A government advisor claims: \"GDP per capita is all we need to compare living standards between countries.\" Give two reasons, drawn from this chapter, why this claim is incomplete.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• GDP does not adjust for differences in local price levels, so two countries with the same nominal GDP per capita can have very different real purchasing power (this is exactly what the PPP adjustment corrects for).\n• GDP is a purely material measure and ignores quality-of-life factors such as pollution, health/lifespan, and leisure time — which is why Amartya Sen argued for judging development by capabilities/opportunities, and why the UN created the broader Human Development Index."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Explain, using the \"three I's\" framework, why a resource-rich but institutionally weak developing country might still grow more slowly than a resource-poor country with strong institutions.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Resources alone do not generate sustained growth without the incentives to use them productively and the institutions to protect the returns from doing so.\n• In an institutionally weak country, an entrepreneur who innovates or invests risks having the gains expropriated (via corruption, weak property-rights enforcement, or an unpredictable legal system) — this removes the incentive to take the risk of innovating in the first place.\n• A resource-poor but institutionally strong country instead channels its more limited resources through reliable incentives and enforceable property rights, allowing accumulated innovation and investment to compound over time — which is why the course frames strong institutions/property rights as close to a precondition for sustained growth."}]},"4":{"title":"Differences in Culture","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Culture: \"a system of values and norms shared among a group of people and, when taken together, constitute a design for living\" (Hill). Society: a group of people sharing a common set of values and norms."},{"t":"li","x":"Values provide the underlying beliefs that give context to a society's norms (e.g. a belief in individual free expression, or in filial duty to family)."},{"t":"li","x":"Norms are the social rules governing behaviour, split into folkways (etiquette/manners) and mores (core rules central to how society functions)."},{"t":"li","x":"Culture is learned, not genetic — differences arise from environment, not biology — and it evolves over time (it is not static)."},{"t":"li","x":"Cross-cultural literacy: the ability to understand and respect other cultures without slipping into cultural stereotyping (assuming everyone from a culture behaves identically). Multinationals can themselves be agents of cultural change."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.1 Determinants of Culture"},{"t":"p","x":"Why businesspeople must understand culture: marketing and sales (communicate in line with customers' values), negotiation (know the other side's motivations and style), and management and leadership (direct, motivate and lead local staff compatibly with their values). Managers therefore need a high level of cultural literacy, and cultural diversity must be respected (e.g. Muslim daily prayer times, the Vietnamese afternoon nap). Cultural differences are not absolute good or bad: never write that Culture A is better or more acceptable than Culture B. Multinational enterprises can be engines of cultural change, and there can be a relationship between culture and the cost of doing business in a country or region."},{"t":"p","x":"Culture is also linked to political power and economy: South Korean \"Hallyu\" pop culture (Squid Game, BTS, Blackpink) is backed by the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism to improve the economy, and a South Korean district offered cash subsidies to couples who date and marry (2024), showing culture as part of the political economy. The national political, legal, economic and cultural systems are integrated as the political economy."},{"t":"p","x":"Exam and project tip from the teacher: definitions alone do not earn marks. Apply the theories with reliable, current research in the group work."},{"t":"p","x":"Culture is shaped by — and in turn shapes — a society's prevailing political/economic philosophy, social structure, religion, language and education (a bi-directional relationship). Nation-states do not always map 1:1 onto a single culture (e.g. Canada has English- and French-official-language provinces); societies also contain sub-cultures (e.g. within a religion: Catholic vs. Protestant vs. Orthodox norms on clergy marriage or divorce)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.2 Cultural Distance"},{"t":"p","x":"Cultural distance = the difference between the home and host country's cultures, an important consideration for market-entry strategy. Firms typically enter culturally/geographically closer markets first, and expand to more distant markets as they gain international experience."},{"t":"li","x":"Higher cultural distance → greater difficulty and \"liability of foreignness\", due to weaker knowledge of how the host country works and greater collaboration barriers."},{"t":"li","x":"Effect on entry mode is debated: some studies associate high cultural distance with joint ventures (collaborative, shared-control entry); others find it associated with wholly-owned subsidiaries (full-control entry) — no consensus exists."},{"t":"p","x":"Market entry is not marketing: cultural distance affects how much control and resource commitment a firm accepts when establishing operations abroad (for example when setting up a factory), so MNEs scrutinise their degree of control."},{"t":"li","x":"**Wholly owned subsidiary (WOS)** — a full-control entry mode, favoured by some studies when cultural distance is high."},{"t":"li","x":"**Joint venture** — a collaborative entry mode, favoured by other studies when cultural distance is high; scholars still cannot draw a clear picture of the link between cultural distance and entry-mode preference."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.3 Hofstede's Cultural Dimensions Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"Developed by Geert Hofstede from a large 1960s–70s survey of IBM employees (100,000+ respondents across 40–50+ countries, refined with Bond and Minkov); widely used to understand etiquette and facilitate communication across cultures in business and diplomacy, and still largely valid in cross-cultural psychology, international management and cross-cultural communication. The study guide and the textbook present five dimensions; a sixth, indulgence vs. restraint, was added later. Hofstede's model has been tested and largely verified in many follow-up academic studies and is confirmed by practising managers, but it has limits (below)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"1. Power Distance (PDI)"},{"t":"p","x":"The extent to which less-powerful members of a society accept and expect unequal power distribution."},{"t":"li","x":"High PDI: hierarchy accepted without need for justification; superiors/subordinates not seen as equals; higher-ups decide without soliciting employee input; centralised organisations, large status gaps (examples given: many Asian societies; Malaysia scores 100)."},{"t":"li","x":"Low PDI: flatter organisations; managers consult and expect to be asked for input; more consultative/egalitarian relations (many Western societies)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"2. Individualism vs. Collectivism (IDV)"},{"t":"li","x":"Individualist: self-image = \"I\"; stresses personal achievement and rights; communication tends to be direct (example: an individual celebrity accepting a solo award)."},{"t":"li","x":"Collectivist: self-image = \"We\"; loyalty/harmony with the in-group prioritised over individual needs; communication tends to be more indirect, avoiding public loss of face (example: a group act's member crediting the team on receiving an award). Asian societies tend toward collectivism; Western societies toward individualism."},{"t":"li","x":"Individualist societies focus on the needs of oneself and one's immediate family; collectivist societies stress relationships, loyalty and the well-being of the group. Asian societies tend to be high in collectivism (employee loyalty, workplace harmony), while Western workers demand more individual attention. Examples used in class: an individual celebrity accepting a solo award versus a K-pop member's award; the communication style in the Tom Cruise Oscar clip is a mix."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"3. Uncertainty Avoidance (UAI)"},{"t":"li","x":"High UAI: low tolerance for ambiguity/risk; societies and organisations rely on strict rules/regulations to minimise the unknown; can be more emotionally expressive (example: France/Greece cited as higher-UAI; Greece scores 100)."},{"t":"li","x":"Low UAI: comfortable with ambiguity and change; fewer formal rules; more openness/flexibility. Hong Kong and Singapore are both cited as very low-UAI — reflected in a flexible workforce and nimble, quickly-adapting organisations (Singapore scores 8)."},{"t":"li","x":"Uncertainty avoidance reflects how threatening change is to a culture. In a low-UAI culture, a subordinate is more accepting of a boss saying they do not know the answer; in a high-UAI culture workers demand clarity on duties, reporting lines and career paths, and organisations are bureaucratic and rigid. Hong Kong is very low on UAI (flexible workforce, nimble organisations), while France is cited as high."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"4. Masculinity vs. Femininity (MAS)"},{"t":"li","x":"Masculine societies: value assertiveness, competition, toughness, achievement — expected of both men and women in the workplace (example: Japan, the highest-scoring country at 95; no reigning queens allowed under Japan's monarchy rules is cited as a cultural illustration)."},{"t":"li","x":"Feminine societies: value cooperation, nurturing, quality of life and modesty (example: Sweden, the lowest-scoring country at 5). A society's parental-leave generosity is cited as one femininity indicator (Hong Kong fathers get only 5 days of paternity leave, versus more generous norms in higher-femininity countries)."},{"t":"li","x":"Also called gender role differentiation (gender equality vs. inequality). In a masculine society everyone, male or female, is expected to behave in a tough, competitive, decisive and task-focused way; in a feminine society managers, even male ones, are expected to be supportive, considerate and people-focused. A country with a high femininity score is likely to have better maternity leave and more affordable childcare, whereas a low femininity score goes with more women in leadership and higher rates of female entrepreneurship. Roles are evolving (Hong Kong, South Korea, Japan)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"5. Long-Term vs. Short-Term Orientation (LTO)"},{"t":"li","x":"Long-term orientation: pragmatism, thrift, perseverance, patience for results to materialise — a future focus that delays short-term gratification."},{"t":"li","x":"Short-term orientation: focus on the near future, quicker results, and stronger emphasis on tradition/principles — can result in comparatively higher present-day spending and lower saving (example given: contrasting pension/savings behaviour in Hong Kong vs. the US/UK/Canada/Singapore)."},{"t":"li","x":"The dimension captures attitudes towards time, persistence and delaying gratification (pleasure, life satisfaction). Long-term societies stress persistence, perseverance, saving, long-term growth and adaptability, and value lengthy ongoing relationships and patience for business results. Short-term societies take a more contractual view of relationships and can show uncontrolled spending."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"6. Indulgence vs. Restraint (IVR)"},{"t":"p","x":"Added later (with Minkov); not covered in the textbook, but taught in this course."},{"t":"li","x":"Indulgent societies: allow relatively free gratification of desires and leisure — more optimistic, more spending on leisure/luxuries."},{"t":"li","x":"Restrained societies: suppress gratification via social norms — more pessimistic, more thrift and focus on practical needs (example given: cannabis is legal in Canada but illegal in Hong Kong)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Hofstede's Country Scores — Selected Countries (Textbook Table 4.1)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Country","Power Distance","Uncertainty Avoidance","Individualism","Masculinity","Long-Term Orientation"],["Australia","36","51","90","61","31"],["Brazil","69","76","38","49","65"],["Canada","39","48","80","52","23"],["Germany (F.R.)","35","65","67","66","31"],["United Kingdom","35","35","89","66","25"],["India","77","40","48","56","61"],["Japan","54","92","46","95","80"],["Netherlands","38","53","80","14","44"],["New Zealand","22","49","79","58","30"],["Pakistan","55","70","14","50","0"],["Philippines","94","44","32","64","19"],["Singapore","74","8","20","48","48"],["Sweden","31","29","71","5","33"],["Thailand","64","64","20","34","56"],["United States","40","46","91","62","29"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Note: This table reproduces the textbook's classic scores as presented in class; Hofstede's published scores have been periodically updated since, so verify against the live Hofstede Insights country-comparison tool for a current group project."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Criticisms of Hofstede's Model"},{"t":"li","x":"Assumes a clean one-to-one mapping between nation and culture, though many nations are genuinely multicultural"},{"t":"li","x":"Original survey respondents all worked in a single industry, within a single company (IBM) — limiting generalisability"},{"t":"li","x":"Certain social classes were excluded from the research; respondents were largely male, white-collar, and highly educated"},{"t":"li","x":"Takes a static view of culture and ignores individual personality/life experience, and how culture changes over time"},{"t":"p","x":"Despite these limits, the model remains one of the most widely used and empirically-supported tools for comparing national cultures, and is respected in practice by international managers."},{"t":"li","x":"Other frameworks (self-study, not detailed in class): GLOBE (9 dimensions incl. power distance, uncertainty avoidance, humane/institutional/in-group collectivism, assertiveness, gender egalitarianism, future and performance orientation); the World Values Survey (WVS)."},{"t":"li","x":"Hofstede's model also does not account for individual personality and life experience, and the sample is dated; industries worldwide now advocate cultural diversity and respect for indigenous residents. Whether the textbook scores are still up to date is doubtful, so use the latest data in the group project."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.4 Cultural Relativism vs. Cultural Universalism"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Cultural relativism","The belief that ethics are culturally determined and firms should adopt the ethics of the culture they operate in — \"when in Rome, do as the Romans do.\" (The textbook presents this view; it does not present a counter-argument for universalism.)"],["Cultural universalism","The belief that the same ethical standards should apply everywhere a firm does business, regardless of local culture — considered by the instructor to be an idealistic standard that is rare to find genuinely practised by real firms."]]},{"t":"p","x":"In practice, most multinationals today adopt formal \"Inclusion and Cultural Diversity\" policies that selectively adapt to local norms rather than applying either pure extreme — e.g. HSBC allows nap time and adjusts service hours in Vietnam (respecting local lunch/nap norms) while HSBC Hong Kong keeps branches open through lunch, reflecting different local expectations."},{"t":"li","x":"**Cultural relativism (APA)** — the view that attitudes, behaviours, values, concepts and achievements must be understood in the light of their own cultural milieu and not judged by the standards of a different culture."},{"t":"li","x":"**Cultural universalism (APA)** — the view that the values, concepts and behaviours of diverse cultures can be understood and judged by universal standards. It rejects, at least in part, cultural relativism, and is also called cultural absolutism."},{"t":"p","x":"A company with a universalist approach to business ethics applies the same standards in every country; a relativist company fits in with local culture and may change its behaviour and policies to local ethical standards."},{"t":"p","x":"HSBC Vietnam gives no physical or online bank services between 12 noon and 2 pm on weekdays and none on Saturdays and Sundays, because of nap and lunch time, while HSBC Hong Kong stays open at lunch and offers online self-service 24/7."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.5 Cultural Change"},{"t":"p","x":"Culture evolves — often slowly, and sometimes with social turmoil — and change tends to accelerate as countries grow economically richer (a shift from \"traditional\" to \"secular-rational\" values). Globalization is associated with a convergence hypothesis (cultures becoming more similar), but there is a counter-trend of some societies deliberately re-emphasising their cultural uniqueness."},{"t":"li","x":"Social turmoil is an inevitable outcome of cultural change. In Vietnam, for example, women now run their own businesses and become wealthy."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.6 Applying the Framework — Group Project Template"},{"t":"p","x":"For the group project, apply Hofstede's dimensions systematically: score Hong Kong and each of your two candidate countries on all six dimensions, identify the largest gaps, and translate each meaningful gap into a concrete managerial implication (e.g. a high power-distance gap implies a Hong Kong manager may need to adapt to a more directive style if managing a low-PDI workforce, or vice versa). Support the analysis with current, reliable data rather than definitions alone — the instructor stresses that \"definitions cannot get any marks\"."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-assessment questions (group in-class exercise, no model answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"1. Identify Hong Kong's scores on each Hofstede dimension."},{"t":"p","x":"2. Identify the scores of each of your two assigned countries on each dimension."},{"t":"p","x":"3. Identify the major differences between Hong Kong and each of the two countries."},{"t":"p","x":"4. Discuss how these differences would affect the methods or styles a Hong Kong manager must adopt when managing workers from one of the countries."},{"t":"p","x":"5. How helpful are Hofstede's dimensions in telling you how to manage in another culture, and what are the model's limitations?"}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 4"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A manager notices that in Country X, employees rarely question a supervisor's decision and expect instructions rather than consultation, while pay gaps and status differences between ranks are large and unremarkable. Country X is most likely:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) high in power distance"},{"t":"p","x":"B) high in femininity"},{"t":"p","x":"C) low in uncertainty avoidance"},{"t":"p","x":"D) high in indulgence"},{"t":"p","x":"E) low in long-term orientation"},{"t":"a","x":"A\nAcceptance of unequal, hierarchical power and unquestioning deference to superiors is the defining behaviour of a high power-distance culture."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. A firm launching a marketing campaign in a strongly collectivist culture should generally:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) emphasise individual achievement and personal rewards"},{"t":"p","x":"B) emphasise group belonging, harmony and benefits to the community"},{"t":"p","x":"C) use direct, blunt criticism of competitor products"},{"t":"p","x":"D) assume the same appeal that works in an individualist market will work unchanged"},{"t":"p","x":"E) avoid any reference to family or community"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nCollectivist cultures place a person's self-image and motivation in the context of the group (\"We\"), so campaigns emphasising community/group benefit and harmony tend to resonate better than individual-achievement appeals."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. Which of the following is a documented methodological criticism of Hofstede's original cultural dimensions research?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) It surveyed a representative cross-section of every social class worldwide"},{"t":"p","x":"B) It was based on employees of a single company (IBM) in one industry"},{"t":"p","x":"C) It has never been replicated or tested since 1980"},{"t":"p","x":"D) It excludes any dimension related to individualism"},{"t":"p","x":"E) It was conducted after the year 2000, making it too recent to be tested"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA frequently-cited limitation is that Hofstede's respondents were all IBM employees (one company, one industry, largely white-collar and male) — limiting how representative the national scores are of the whole society."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Your group project assigns you Hong Kong (home) and Sweden (a candidate manufacturing location). Sweden scores very low on Masculinity (5) versus a higher Hong Kong score. Explain one concrete managerial adjustment a Hong Kong manager might need to make when managing a Swedish workforce, and justify it using the Masculinity/Femininity dimension.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Sweden's very low Masculinity score signals a feminine culture that values cooperation, consensus, quality of life and work-life balance over overt competitiveness and status display.\n• A Hong Kong manager used to a more assertive, target-driven, hierarchical management style may need to shift toward a more participative, consensus-based decision-making style, and place real weight on work-life balance policies (e.g. flexible hours) rather than relying on competitive incentives or long-hours culture, which would likely be poorly received and could hurt morale/retention in a low-Masculinity workforce."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Distinguish cultural relativism from cultural universalism and explain, using the HSBC Vietnam/Hong Kong nap-time example, which approach HSBC is actually following in practice.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Cultural relativism holds that ethics/practice should adapt to fit local culture (\"when in Rome...\"); cultural universalism holds that the same standard should apply everywhere regardless of local culture.\n• HSBC allowing nap time and adjusted service hours in its Vietnam branches, while keeping Hong Kong branches open through lunch with no nap policy, shows HSBC adapting its internal practices to local cultural norms in each market — this is an example of (a moderate, business-practical form of) cultural relativism, not universalism, even though HSBC likely maintains universal standards on other matters (e.g. core ethics/anti-bribery policy) across all its branches."}]},"5":{"title":"Ethics, Corporate Social Responsibility, Sustainability & Supply Chain","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.1 Why Business Ethics Matters More Today"},{"t":"li","x":"The internet: unethical behaviour anywhere is quickly exposed and spread worldwide; protest groups have a far more powerful voice than before"},{"t":"li","x":"High-profile scandals: Enron and WorldCom wiped out shareholder value and heightened scrutiny of corporate conduct"},{"t":"li","x":"Tougher legislation: e.g. the U.S. Sarbanes-Oxley Act makes individual company officers personally responsible for ethical misdeeds within their organisation"},{"t":"p","x":"Ethics: accepted principles of right/wrong governing the conduct of a person, profession, or organisation. Business ethics: the same, applied to business conduct. Ethical strategy: a course of action that does not violate these accepted principles. Ethics is a culturally-bound concept (echoing Chapter 4) — what is \"accepted\" varies by country, which is precisely what makes ethical decision-making complex for a multinational."},{"t":"p","x":"Consequences the lecture lists: concern for share price and bankruptcy, negative image and reputation, fine penalties paid by companies and personal penalties for executives. Ethics is culturally bound, as in collectivist societies where children cannot call their parents by name and must respect them strictly."},{"t":"p","x":"Under the study guide, pressure on firms to behave more ethically comes from three forces: the internet, shareholder and customer pressure after Enron and WorldCom (investors lost everything), and tougher laws such as Sarbanes-Oxley. Managers are less sure what behaviour is accepted in the other countries where their firm operates. The textbook's philosophical ethical approaches are not covered in this course, which focuses on the practical actions firms take."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.2 Four Common Ethical Issue Areas"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Employment practices","Wages, benefits, working conditions, hiring/discrimination, health & safety. Example: Hong Kong firms relocating factories to mainland China accused of exploiting weaker local labour protections and inadequate dormitory/safety conditions for staff."],["Human rights","Should a multinational continue operating where basic human rights (association, speech, assembly, movement) are denied? Precedent: the effective withdrawal of multinationals from apartheid-era South Africa is often cited as contributing to that system's collapse. Difficulty: \"human rights\" itself is a partly culturally-bound term."],["Environmental pollution","The \"tragedy of the commons\": a shared resource owned by no one gets over-used/degraded because no individual user bears its full cost. Should an MNC pollute in a developing country if doing so does not break local law? In practice, MNCs sometimes hold HIGHER environmental standards than local competitors, so the picture is not purely one-sided."],["Corruption","Public-level (bribes for licences/market access) vs. private-level (side payments to secure a business contract). Debate: some argue bribery can be welfare-enhancing where it lets business happen despite broken institutions; most economies and firms now reject this — the OECD Anti-Bribery Convention and many MNCs' zero-tolerance policies reflect the view that corruption reduces investment returns and growth."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Additional points from the lecture: employment practices include inferior work conditions in host nations and extended manufacturing in least-developed countries, and MNCs have been accused of exploiting low-wage workers, including under-age workers. Basic human rights taken for granted in the developed world (freedom of association, speech, assembly and movement) are not universal, and child labour is illegal in many countries. Corruption includes marketeering, smuggling and side payments to bureaucrats to speed up approval of business investments. Some argue that when existing political structures distort or limit the market mechanism, corruption may enhance welfare; others argue it reduces returns on investment and leads to low economic growth, and many multinationals have adopted a zero-tolerance policy (the Samsung head jailed for bribery is a lecture example). The environmental ethical issue arises when host-nation regulations are far inferior to the home nation's."},{"t":"p","x":"Hong Kong's Independent Commission Against Corruption (ICAC) was founded in 1974 with three purposes: prevent corruption, enforce anti-corruption laws and educate the community. The ICAC micro-film series on business ethics covers the Prevention of Bribery Ordinance."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.3 Making Ethical Decisions Internationally — Seven Managerial Actions"},{"t":"li","x":"1. Hiring & promotion: favour candidates with a well-grounded sense of personal ethics; consider ethical conduct, not just performance, in promotion decisions"},{"t":"li","x":"2. Organisational culture & leadership: articulate and repeatedly reinforce ethical values (codes of conduct); leaders model the behaviour themselves"},{"t":"li","x":"3. Decision-making processes: explicitly build ethical criteria into decisions (e.g. considering human rights when choosing where to invest, or supplier sustainability when choosing who to buy from). A practical three-question test for whether a decision is ethically acceptable: (i) does it fall within the organisation's accepted values/code of ethics? (ii) would I be comfortable if it were publicised (news, social media)? (iii) would people I respect — family, trusted colleagues — approve?"},{"t":"li","x":"4. Ethics officers: an internal \"ombudsperson\" role to monitor compliance with ethical standards"},{"t":"li","x":"5. Moral courage: the willingness to walk away from a profitable-but-unethical decision, and to support employees who speak up about wrongdoing"},{"t":"li","x":"6. Make CSR a cornerstone of enterprise policy"},{"t":"li","x":"7. Pursue sustainable strategies"},{"t":"p","x":"Detail on the actions. Hiring and promotion: firms often use psychological testing before employment, and prospective employees should also find out about the ethical climate of an organisation. Culture and leadership: senior management makes clear that ethical standards are a prerequisite for continued employment, emphasises this in orientation and training, adopts a code of ethics (also called conduct or practice, depending on the company), and provides incentives and rewards to those who act ethically. The Shell code of conduct for employees and suppliers is the lecture example: no cigarettes at offices, during oil transport, at oil tanks or at stations, otherwise immediate termination, because of fire and explosion risk; the related news of Shell fires and explosions concerns health and safety."},{"t":"p","x":"A manager can accept a decision as ethical if the answer is yes to all three questions: does it fall within the accepted values in the code of ethics, am I willing to see it communicated to all affected stakeholders, and would people I have a significant relationship with approve."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.4 Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR)"},{"t":"p","x":"Dahlsrud (2008): a CSR is the concept that a firm is accountable for its impact on all relevant stakeholders — a continuing commitment to behave responsibly and contribute to economic development while improving the quality of life of the workforce, their families, and the local society and environment."},{"t":"p","x":"Beal (2013) adds practical characteristics: firms try to minimise the risk of negative CSR-related publicity; CSR addresses both internal stakeholders (employees, investors) and external stakeholders (suppliers, retailers, wholesalers, partners, consumers); it should be a long-term, ongoing commitment (not a one-off gesture), often delivered through dedicated foundations/funding; it builds long-term relationships and customer loyalty; and it is typically kept relatively low-profile rather than aggressively self-promoted."},{"t":"p","x":"\"Noblesse oblige\": the idea that those with power/influence have an obligation to use their position to help others — the philosophical underpinning often cited for CSR."},{"t":"p","x":"**Case studies**"},{"t":"li","x":"Disney Conservation Fund and the Disney x Avatar \"Keep Our Oceans Amazing\" campaign"},{"t":"li","x":"Disney's The Lion King \"Protect the Pride\" campaign: movie-goer donations feed the Lion Recovery Fund; funds conservation-expert training in Africa and grants, with employee/expert involvement"},{"t":"li","x":"Rolex.org (the CSR/environmental arm) is deliberately kept distinct from Rolex's commercial marketing website — illustrating that a genuine CSR effort should not be conflated with product marketing"},{"t":"p","x":"The lecture lists three kinds of CSR impact: societal (improving well-being, mental and physical health, self-improvement of people in need), economic development (higher employment, GDP and purchasing power) and environmental sustainability (environmental protection, animal protection, limiting air and water pollution). CSR efforts are long-term, low-profile, run through foundations that fund applications from partners, and build lasting relationships and a good reputation, hence more loyal customers. Multinationals need to give back to the societies that enable them to prosper, and there should be a presumption in favour of decisions with both good economic and good social consequences."},{"t":"p","x":"CSR goes beyond not behaving unethically: firms also do something positively good for the communities in which they operate, and managers are encouraged to take part in community activities (similar to service learning)."},{"t":"p","x":"Tip for group work: a brand's own website and YouTube channel give one-sided, positive information, so cite independent news such as WSJ, Reuters, CNN, CBC and BBC."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.5 Sustainability"},{"t":"p","x":"UN definition (1983): sustainability means meeting our own needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs. A sustainable strategy helps an MNC make profit without harming the environment, while acting responsibly toward all its stakeholders (fairly compensating suppliers so they can keep providing quality inputs; treating employees fairly so the firm can keep attracting talent)."},{"t":"p","x":"The lecture adds that sustainability is often a long-term goal (a more sustainable world), while sustainable development refers to the many processes and pathways to achieve it. A sustainable strategy is good for shareholders, the environment, local communities, employees and customers. Sustainability is most often linked to environmental policy (avoiding depletion of natural resources and the tragedy of the commons), with a wider meaning covering suppliers and employees."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.6 Global Supply Chain Management"},{"t":"p","x":"A global supply chain runs from production/manufacturing/packaging through shipping/delivery to the end consumer."},{"t":"p","x":"**Packaging categories**"},{"t":"li","x":"Primary packaging (holds the product itself); Secondary packaging (bundles several primary packages); Transit packaging (used to physically move primary + secondary packages, e.g. courier boxes) — each layer raises environmental/waste questions."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Sourcing Terminology"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Meaning"],["Outsourcing","A multinational buys products or services from a supplier that produces them somewhere else, whether domestically or globally"],["Insourcing","The multinational stops outsourcing and produces the products or services internally (the opposite of outsourcing)"],["Offshoring","The multinational buys products or services from a supplier that produces them somewhere globally, outside the MNC's home country"],["Offshore outsourcing","The multinational buys from a supplier in a country other than the one in which the product is manufactured or the service is developed"],["Nearshoring","The multinational transfers business or IT processes to suppliers in a nearby country, often one that shares a border with the firm's own country"],["Co-sourcing","The multinational uses both its own employees and an external supplier to perform certain tasks, often in concert; this applies to all four forms of purchasing strategy"]]},{"t":"li","x":"Five levels of global purchasing maturity: (I) domestic purchasing only → (II) international purchasing as needed → (III) international purchasing as part of overall supply-chain strategy → (IV) global purchasing integrated across the firm's worldwide locations → (V) global purchasing integrated across worldwide locations AND functional groups."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Four Pillars of Managing a Global Supply Chain"},{"t":"li","x":"Just-in-time (JIT) inventory: materials arrive just as needed, cutting warehousing/holding costs and helping firms spot defects early — but removes the buffer stock that would otherwise absorb supply disruptions"},{"t":"li","x":"Information technology: systems (EDI, and increasingly blockchain) that optimise production scheduling and traceability"},{"t":"li","x":"Coordination: shared decision-making across the chain targeting responsiveness, variance reduction, inventory reduction, shipment consolidation, quality and life-cycle support"},{"t":"li","x":"Inter-organisational relationships: trust and commitment between supply-chain partners (including the choice between self-owned vs. outsourced transport/logistics)"},{"t":"p","x":"The lecture used Amazon's supply chain as a recurring illustrative (not separately examinable) case bundle spanning unsold-inventory destruction, warehouse-worker injury rates, sourcing from unsafe overseas factories, and privacy/DEI controversies — with the explicit lesson that a company's own website/marketing gives one-sided, positive information, and that independent journalism (WSJ, Reuters, CNN, BBC, etc.) should be used to critically assess a firm's real ethical performance for group-work analysis."},{"t":"p","x":"Quality and costs: the lecture reproduces the relationship between quality and costs from Garvin's \"What Does Product Quality Really Mean?\" (MIT Sloan Management Review, 1984). Efficient supply chain management can have a substantial impact on a firm's profitability. Information technology means EDI (now cheaper and accessible to firms of all sizes) with blockchain as an additional layer. Supply chain coordination is the shared decision-making and operational collaboration of key global supply chain activities, aiming at a more integrated, coherent, efficient and effective chain; its operational objectives are responsiveness, variance reduction, inventory reduction, shipment consolidation, quality and life-cycle support."},{"t":"p","x":"Supply chain strategy terms used in class: more globalised, regionalisation, nearshoring and reshoring. Apple and Samsung have been moving supply chains away from China."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.7 Official In-Class Ethical Case Discussion (Suggested Answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Note: The original case prompt text was not present in the extracted files — only the instructor's \"suggested solution\" was provided. The scenario below is reconstructed from the structure of that answer; please verify the exact wording of the original case against the Moodle file if you need it verbatim."},{"t":"p","x":"Reconstructed scenario: an accountant/finance team member is pressured by a superior to change the useful-life assumption used to calculate depreciation on long-term assets — without any genuine change in facts, events or circumstances — purely to make reported earnings look better."},{"t":"p","x":"**(a) Is this ethical?**"},{"t":"li","x":"No. Depreciation periods must be based on an asset's actual expected useful life; changing the assumption without a real underlying reason understates depreciation expense and overstates net income, producing misleading financial statements."},{"t":"p","x":"**(b) Who is harmed / which stakeholders are affected?**"},{"t":"li","x":"Investors and potential investors, who may hold or buy shares based on artificially inflated (misleading) performance; also banks, creditors, the government, the accounting profession generally, individual accountants, competitors, and the finance director/management themselves (reputational and legal exposure)."},{"t":"p","x":"**(c) Which professional/ethical values are engaged?**"},{"t":"li","x":"Integrity, Objectivity, Due care, and Professional behaviour."},{"t":"p","x":"**(d) Recommended course of action**"},{"t":"li","x":"(i) Refuse the request; explain the reasoning and ask the person requesting it to reconsider"},{"t":"li","x":"(ii) If ignored, escalate the matter to an ethics officer or a higher authority (e.g. the board)"},{"t":"li","x":"(iii) If still ignored, resign"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-assessment questions (voluntary, no model answer): Nike and its sub-contractors"},{"t":"p","x":"1. Should Nike be responsible for the activities of its sub-contractors, or is that the sub-contractors' own responsibility?"},{"t":"p","x":"2. List the ethical problems Nike faces from using these sub-contractors."},{"t":"p","x":"3. Assess the actions Nike is taking: are they enough to overcome the charges?"},{"t":"p","x":"4. What damage has been done to Nike's reputation, and could Nike have behaved differently from the start?"}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 5"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A factory dumps untreated waste into a river that is legally owned by no one and used by many local fishing communities. This scenario is the classic illustration of:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the infant industry argument"},{"t":"p","x":"B) the tragedy of the commons"},{"t":"p","x":"C) trade diversion"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Dunning's eclectic theory"},{"t":"p","x":"E) the Leontief paradox"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA shared resource (the river) owned by no one being over-used/degraded by individual users, with no one bearing the full cost, is precisely the tragedy of the commons."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Which of the following is an example of Just-in-Time (JIT) inventory management's main documented WEAKNESS?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) higher warehousing costs"},{"t":"p","x":"B) reduced ability to catch defective parts"},{"t":"p","x":"C) lack of a buffer stock, making the firm vulnerable to supply disruptions"},{"t":"p","x":"D) inability to use information technology"},{"t":"p","x":"E) requiring more employees on the factory floor"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nJIT deliberately minimises held inventory; its trade-off is that firms lack a buffer to absorb unexpected disruptions in the supply chain."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. According to Beal's (2013) characterisation of CSR discussed in class, which of the following best fits genuine CSR practice?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) a one-off donation heavily promoted in a single ad campaign"},{"t":"p","x":"B) a long-term, relatively low-profile commitment addressing both internal and external stakeholders"},{"t":"p","x":"C) a policy that focuses exclusively on shareholders"},{"t":"p","x":"D) a marketing campaign indistinguishable from the firm's main commercial website"},{"t":"p","x":"E) a policy reviewed once and never updated"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nBeal's framework stresses long-term (not one-off) commitment, attention to both internal and external stakeholders, and typically a lower-profile approach than aggressive self-promotion — as illustrated by Rolex keeping its CSR site distinct from its commercial site."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. A multinational discovers a supplier factory abroad has working conditions that are legal locally but would be illegal in the firm's home country. Applying the ethics framework from this chapter, outline the key considerations the firm's management should weigh before deciding what to do.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Recognise that \"employment practices\" is one of the four core ethical issue areas — legality in the host country does not automatically make a practice ethically acceptable by the firm's own (or its home country's) standards.\n• Apply the three-question ethical decision test: would this fall within the firm's own code of conduct? Would management be comfortable if the conditions were reported in the media? Would people they respect approve?\n• Consider stakeholder impact broadly (workers, local community, the firm's reputation, consumers) and whether continuing to source there without changes could expose the firm to reputational and, potentially, legal risk if reported by independent media.\n• A firm with genuine moral courage and a CSR/sustainability commitment would use its leverage as a customer to require improved conditions (an ethical decision-making process, per action 3 above) rather than simply accepting the host country's minimum legal standard."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Explain the difference between 'outsourcing' and 'offshoring', and give an example of a firm decision that would be BOTH offshore AND outsourced at the same time.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Outsourcing means buying a product/service from an external supplier rather than producing it in-house — the supplier can be domestic or foreign.\n• Offshoring means the firm's OWN operations are relocated/established abroad — the activity stays inside the firm, just in another country.\n• A decision is 'offshore outsourcing' when a firm buys from a third-party supplier located in a different country than where the product is made or used — e.g. a US clothing brand contracting an independent Bangladeshi factory (not its own subsidiary) to manufacture garments for sale in the US."}]},"6":{"title":"International Trade Theory","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"This chapter is described by the instructor as probably the most important class of the whole course: it lays the foundation of the later chapters on trade policy, FDI and regional integration. Classical theories explain WHY trade happens; modern theories explain WHAT goods and services each country trades."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning objectives (6-1 to 6-5)"},{"t":"li","x":"6-1 Understand why nations trade with each other."},{"t":"li","x":"6-2 Summarise the different theories explaining trade flows between nations."},{"t":"li","x":"6-3 Recognise why many economists believe that unrestricted free trade raises the economic welfare of the countries in a free trade system."},{"t":"li","x":"6-4 Explain the arguments of those who maintain that government can play a proactive role in promoting national competitive advantage in certain industries."},{"t":"li","x":"6-5 Understand the implications of international trade theory for management practice."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Overview of trade theory"},{"t":"li","x":"**Free trade** — a situation where a government does not attempt to influence, through quotas or duties, what its citizens can buy from another country or what they can produce and sell to another country."},{"t":"li","x":"**International trade** — the exchange of raw materials and manufactured goods (and services) across national borders."},{"t":"li","x":"Free trade is rarely complete today. For example, Hong Kong banned Japanese seafood imports after the release of radioactive water."},{"t":"li","x":"Theories are proposed by economists, policymakers and academics. There are no absolute good or bad theories: every theory has advantages and drawbacks, and not every theory applies to a real situation (rapid technological change, international relations, developed versus least-developed nations). Some theories may not be fully applicable in practice."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.1 Classical trade theories — \"Trade is good\""},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Mercantilism (mid-16th century; rooted in merchants; the pre-1800 orthodoxy)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Mercantilism** — an economic theory holding that a government should control the economy and that a nation should increase its wealth by selling more than it buys from other nations."},{"t":"li","x":"A country's best interest is to maintain a **trade surplus** (export more than it imports); governments should intervene to promote exports and minimise imports. Exports bring in wealth: gold and silver."},{"t":"li","x":"Mercantilism benefited governments and domestic producers, through tariffs and/or quotas (detailed in Chapter 7), and harmed consumers through higher prices and less choice."},{"t":"li","x":"**Zero-sum game** — a situation in which a gain by one country results in a loss by another. Mercantilism viewed trade as a zero-sum game."},{"t":"li","x":"The Opium Wars, fought largely because Britain tried to force China to buy British goods, led to Britain acquiring Hong Kong (1842/1847). Many people still think this way intuitively."},{"t":"li","x":"Smith, Ricardo and Heckscher–Ohlin criticised mercantilism: limits on imports are often in the interest of domestic producers but not of domestic consumers. They created the theories below."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Absolute advantage (Adam Smith, The Wealth of Nations, 1776)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Absolute advantage** — a country has an absolute advantage in producing a product when it is more efficient than any other country in producing it. Countries differ in their ability to produce goods efficiently (weather, soil, technological equipment, fertiliser, subsidies, and so on)."},{"t":"li","x":"According to Smith, countries should specialise in the goods for which they have an absolute advantage and trade them for goods produced by other countries. **Trade is not a zero-sum game**: imports are not \"bad\" and a country can enrich itself through free trade (no restriction on exports or imports)."},{"t":"li","x":"Examples in the deck (the advantage is not fixed over time): France, wine (now also Italy, China, Australia); Ghana, cocoa (now also Vietnam, Brazil); Korea, rice (now also Thailand, China, Vietnam); the USA, wheat (now also China, Australia, Canada). Study the Ghana and Korea rice and cocoa example and Table 6.1 of the textbook."},{"t":"li","x":"A limit of the theory is that only a country that is the most efficient in at least one product has a reason to specialise and export."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Comparative advantage (David Ricardo, 1817)"},{"t":"li","x":"Ricardo asked what happens when one country has an absolute advantage in the production of all goods."},{"t":"li","x":"**Comparative advantage** — a country should specialise in the goods it produces and exports most efficiently and buy the goods it produces less efficiently from other countries. Equivalently, the country is at less of a disadvantage (lowest opportunity cost) in that product. Example in the deck: the USA produces and exports corn, which Brazil and Hong Kong produce less efficiently; coffee beans work the same way."},{"t":"li","x":"So all countries, even those with no absolute advantage in anything, can gain from exporting and importing. Comparative advantage is the intellectual foundation of international business (see Table 6.2 of the textbook)."},{"t":"li","x":"The WTO puts it this way. All countries, including the poorest, have assets (human, industrial, natural, financial) they can use to produce goods and services for domestic markets or to compete overseas. Countries prosper first by taking advantage of their assets to concentrate on what they produce best, and then by trading these products for the products other countries produce best."},{"t":"li","x":"**Gains from trade** — trade is a **positive-sum game** in which all gain. Potential world production is greater with unrestricted free trade than with restricted trade, which gives a strong rationale for encouraging free trade."},{"t":"li","x":"There is a strong link between the amount of trade a country does and its growth rate (compare China before and after it opened to trade)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Qualifications and assumptions of comparative advantage"},{"t":"li","x":"**Assumptions of comparative advantage** — (1) only two countries and two goods; (2) zero transportation costs; (3) similar prices and values; (4) resources mobile between the production of one good and another within a country; (5) constant returns to scale; (6) fixed stocks of resources; (7) no effects on income distribution within countries."},{"t":"li","x":"The deck asks whether these assumptions are unrealistic in the contemporary world. Despite the limitations, comparative advantage remains one of the most widely accepted principles of economics."},{"t":"li","x":"**Samuelson's counter-view** — for a rich country trading with a poor country, the domestic job losses may be more harmful than the benefit of lower prices to consumers."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.2 Modern trade theories — explaining WHAT countries trade"},{"t":"p","x":"Classical theories did not adequately explain where a country's productivity comes from. Modern trade theory explains why certain countries export certain goods and other countries export other goods."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Heckscher–Ohlin theory (1919): factor endowments"},{"t":"li","x":"**Heckscher–Ohlin theory** — countries export goods that make intensive use of the factors of production they have in relative abundance, and import goods that use their scarce factors intensively."},{"t":"li","x":"**Factor endowments** — comparative advantage reflects differences in national factor endowments: the extent to which a country is endowed (naturally) with resources such as oil, land, soil, weather, labour and capital. Developed by two Scandinavian economists."},{"t":"li","x":"Because different goods use factors in different intensities, clothing is labour-intensive, timber is land-intensive, and televisions and machinery are capital-intensive."},{"t":"li","x":"A country exports goods that make intensive use of the factors that are locally abundant and imports goods that make intensive use of the factors that are locally scarce. The theory has commonsense appeal."},{"t":"li","x":"Rare earth metals (magnets, electronics, aircraft engines, wind turbines, electric vehicles) illustrate a natural endowment. China produced more than two thirds of global rare earth mine production in 2024 (61% in 2023, projected to fall to 54% by 2030); the USA came second with 11.6%."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Leontief paradox (1953)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Leontief paradox** — Leontief expected the capital-abundant USA to export capital-intensive goods and import labour-intensive goods. In reality the USA exported a mix of capital- and labour-intensive goods and imported the same. This \"impossible\" result shows that factor endowments only partly explain trade."},{"t":"li","x":"**Capital-intensive goods** require a relatively large amount of capital (machinery, equipment); they are more technologically advanced and need higher skills. **Labour-intensive goods** require a relatively large amount of labour (textiles, food products, handmade crafts) and less capital."},{"t":"li","x":"Possible explanations of the paradox are that the USA has a special advantage in products made with innovative technologies that are less capital-intensive; differences in technology lead to differences in productivity, which drive trade patterns."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"International product life-cycle theory (Raymond Vernon, mid-1960s)"},{"t":"li","x":"**International product life-cycle theory** — Vernon's theory that new products are first produced and sold in the wealthy home market (the USA) and, as they mature and standardise, production moves to lower-cost countries, which changes the flow and direction of trade."},{"t":"li","x":"For most of the 20th century most new products were produced and sold in the US market: its wealth and size gave US firms a strong incentive to develop new products. At first, demand abroad was limited to high-income groups."},{"t":"li","x":"As the product matures, both the location of sales and the optimal production location change, which affects the flow and direction of trade. The product becomes standardised and price becomes the main competitive weapon."},{"t":"li","x":"The USA switches from exporter to importer as production moves to lower-cost foreign locations (developing countries and, at the commodity stage, less developed countries, which export to everyone). Deck examples: Apple and Samsung."},{"t":"li","x":"Trade flows therefore depend on where the product is in its life cycle (introduction and growth, maturity, decline)."},{"t":"li","x":"In the 21st century the theory is less applicable, because many products are introduced first in Japan or South Korea, many are introduced simultaneously in the USA, Europe and Asia, and firms use globally dispersed production from the start. Many innovations now come from developing nations. Largely discredited."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"New trade theory (Paul Krugman; also called strategic trade theory)"},{"t":"li","x":"**New trade theory** — Krugman's theory that, in industries with economies of scale and first-mover advantages, the countries that dominate world trade in a product are largely those whose firms happened to start first. It is also called strategic trade theory."},{"t":"li","x":"Traditional theories (Smith, Ricardo, Heckscher–Ohlin) could not fully explain real-world trade patterns, so new trade theory was developed to account for the growing importance of economies of scale, the expansion of global industries and intra-industry trade (Germany exports cars to Japan and Japan exports cars to Germany)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Economies of scale** — the reduction of unit production costs that results from making and selling goods in large quantities (for example, buying large amounts of materials at reduced prices). When the output needed to reach economies of scale is a significant proportion of world demand, the global market may support only a small number of firms."},{"t":"li","x":"**First-mover advantages** — the economic and strategic advantages that accrue to early entrants into an industry: firms develop economies of scale and create barriers to entry for other firms. The pattern of trade in the world economy may therefore result from first-mover advantages and economies of scale, and which country dominates is often down to luck."},{"t":"li","x":"**Increasing product variety and reducing costs** — without trade, a small nation may not support the demand needed for economies of scale, so some products are not produced. With trade, a nation specialises in a narrower range of products and buys the rest abroad, so variety rises and costs fall."},{"t":"li","x":"Deck examples include X (formerly Twitter) versus TikTok in the USA, and electric vehicles in Hong Kong (Tesla, BMW, Benz, BYD, Hyundai), where Tesla had more than 40,000 registered vehicles, 47% of the 86,938 electric private cars between August 2017 and July 2024)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Implications of new trade theory** — nations may benefit from trade even when they do not differ in resource endowments or technology; a country may dominate exports of a good because one of its firms happened to be first. This is at variance with Heckscher–Ohlin, is useful in explaining trade patterns, and provides an economic rationale for a proactive trade policy (subsidies, import blocks) in industries where first-mover advantage is important, economies of experience are large and the industry can support only a limited number of competitors."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Porter's diamond of national competitive advantage (Michael Porter, 1990)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Porter's diamond** — Porter's framework explaining why a nation succeeds internationally in a given industry. It rests on four attributes: factor endowment, demand conditions, related and supporting industries, and firm strategy, structure and rivalry. Government policy and chance act on them."},{"t":"li","x":"Porter believed existing trade theories told only part of the story; he wanted to explain why a nation achieves international success in a particular industry. The diamond holds four attributes that shape the environment in which local firms compete; government and chance also influence international trade (the deck treats them as the 5th and 6th diamonds). The order can change; the key is that all six are included."},{"t":"li","x":"According to Porter in the Harvard Business Review, national prosperity is created, not inherited; it does not come from natural endowments, labour pool, interest rates or currency value as classical economics insists. A nation's competitiveness depends on the capacity of its industry to innovate and upgrade: companies gain advantage against the best competitors because of pressure and challenge, from strong domestic rivals, aggressive home-based suppliers and demanding local customers. Deck example: OpenAI versus DeepSeek versus Alibaba. Government experiments (exchange-rate management, trade measures, relaxed antitrust) usually end up undermining competitiveness."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Meaning"],["Factor endowment (factor conditions)","Natural endowments as in Heckscher–Ohlin, plus \"advanced\" factors such as infrastructure and the education system"],["Demand conditions","Quantity and quality of domestic demand: sophisticated, demanding consumers push firms to innovate and produce quality"],["Related and supporting industries","A country is more likely to be competitive where related and supporting industries are internationally competitive and provide competitive inputs"],["Firm strategy, structure and rivalry","Many domestic competitors, none dominant, means intense rivalry and product and process innovation"],["Government policy (added)","Supportive policy (for example protected areas, regulation, subsidies)"],["Chance (added)","Events outside firms' control (for example a UN tourism resolution)"]]},{"t":"li","x":"**Empirical test of Porter's theory** — if correct, the model should predict real-world trade: countries should export products from industries where the diamond is favourable and import products where it is not. There has been little empirical testing of the theory, although it remains the most comprehensive framework available to explain trade patterns."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Worked case: Northern Lights tourism (Canada) with the six diamonds"},{"t":"p","x":"In the Northern Lights case, the factor endowment is the natural dark sky of the Arctic region (Norway, Sweden, Finland, Iceland and Canada are prime destinations)."},{"t":"p","x":"On the demand side, the northern lights tourism market was USD 834.5 million in 2023 and is projected at USD 1,647.9 million by 2030. Europe led with 45.48% of revenue; couples led with 48.23%; the 18–34 age group led with 31.56%. Nordic accommodation stays within the Arctic Circle rose 217% from 2009 to 2016, with airport traffic and tourism expenditure up roughly 280%. An Expedia survey found 42% of Americans will prioritise aurora chasing, ahead of Egypt's pyramids (36%)."},{"t":"p","x":"Government policy matters too. Canada's 13 Dark-Sky Preserves and national parks protect the night and reduce light pollution."},{"t":"p","x":"For firm strategy, structure and rivalry, in Canada the experience is controlled by the government in national parks, partnered with NGOs or commercial firms and guided by the government; in Europe it is commercial and guided."},{"t":"p","x":"Related and supporting industries include hospitality and travel (Klook, Kkday packages), clothing rental, catering, shuttle buses and boats."},{"t":"p","x":"Chance plays a role, for example the UN sustainable and resilient tourism agenda (ecotourism)."},{"t":"p","x":"Participation exercise. Pick an industry of your home country or city and apply Porter's diamond (at least two, ideally all six)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.3 Managerial implications"},{"t":"li","x":"**Managerial use of new trade theory** — a firm should invest heavily to build dominance and first-mover advantage in industries with strong scale or experience economies."},{"t":"li","x":"**Managerial use of Porter's diamond** — a practical tool for deciding where to locate a new plant or business (the same logic as the Weighted Country Attractiveness Matrix exercise)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Why comparative advantage matters** — it underlies the next chapters on government policy, FDI and regional integration: \"trade is good\" is the course's foundational premise. The theories explain why international business happens and how trade makes the best use of world resources and raises living standards."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-assessment questions (voluntary, no model answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"1. What is the difference between absolute and comparative advantage?"},{"t":"p","x":"2. What is mercantilism, and why did this view of trade remain popular for so many years?"},{"t":"p","x":"3. If classical theories explain why trade happens, what is the central question of modern trade theories?"},{"t":"p","x":"4. What is new trade theory? Give an example and explain how companies can use it to make practical decisions."},{"t":"p","x":"Question on Porter's diamond. Identify Hong Kong's internationally successful manufacturing export industries and their common features; explain how the diamond helps explain Hong Kong's success; does the government's ambition to make Hong Kong an information-technology centre fit what the diamond suggests?"},{"t":"p","x":"Suggested material. Video \"A game of no rules: the deceptive promise of free trade\"; articles on Ghana and chocolate (FT, 2021), the downsides of free trade (Businessweek, 2016), Trade in the Balance (The Economist, 2016), World Trade Becalmed (The Economist, 2015), The Miracle of Trade (1996) and Going Global (2001)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.4 Balance of Payments (Self-study: Reserve Bank of Australia and Hong Kong)"},{"t":"p","x":"• Balance of payments (BoP) — a statistical statement summarizing the economic transactions between an economy’s residents and the rest of the world over a period (a quarter or a year): trade in goods and services, investment and income, and transfer payments. It shows how much money flows into and out of the country and is closely related to foreign direct investment."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","What it records"],["Current account","Goods and services trade (the trade balance = exports − imports), primary income (wages, dividends, interest) and secondary income (current transfers such as foreign aid, taxes)"],["Capital account","Capital transfers (debt forgiveness, conditional grants for capital projects) and acquisition or disposal of non-produced, non-financial assets (brand names, rights to use land or water)"],["Financial account","Transactions that change ownership of assets and liabilities: direct investment (10% or more of voting power), portfolio investment (shares and bonds without influence), financial derivatives, reserve assets, other investment (trade credit, currency and deposits)"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Double entry: every transaction has two offsetting entries, so the total BoP should be zero. When value is provided to the rest of the world (an export) a credit is recorded; when value is received (a payment or an import) a debit is recorded. Net errors and omissions is the item that makes the BoP balance."},{"t":"p","x":"• “Current” means the goods, services and income are consumed or received in the current period."},{"t":"p","x":"• Sign convention in Hong Kong’s statistics: in the current and capital accounts a positive balance is a surplus and a negative balance a deficit; exports and income inflows are positive (credit), imports and income outflows negative (debit). In the financial account a positive balance means a net inflow of investment and a negative balance a net outflow; negative figures for investment assets show outflows for Hong Kong residents’ net acquisition of external assets."},{"t":"p","x":"• Hong Kong 2013–2022: the goods and services account generally recorded surpluses because exports exceed imports and Hong Kong is a service economy bridging the Mainland and the world; the balance ranged from a deficit of $6.1 billion (2018) to a surplus of $57.3 billion (2015) between 2013 and 2019, and the surplus rose from $44.4 billion in 2019 to $116.2 billion in 2022 (during COVID-19 imports fell more than exports). The statistics follow the IMF Balance of Payments Manual (sixth edition) and have been compiled since 1998 by the Census and Statistics Department."},{"t":"p","x":"• Australia (RBA): the BoP has two broad accounts, the current account and the combined capital and financial account; the trade balance receives most attention because it forms part of GDP."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-check — Balance of payments"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can name the components of the current, capital and financial accounts."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can classify a transaction as credit or debit and explain why the BoP sums to zero."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can classify a cross-border investment as direct or portfolio."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 6"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Country A can produce both wine and cloth more efficiently than Country B in absolute terms, but is relatively MORE efficient at wine than cloth. According to Ricardo's theory of comparative advantage, Country A should:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) refuse to trade with Country B at all, since it has an absolute advantage in everything"},{"t":"p","x":"B) specialise in cloth, its area of relative disadvantage"},{"t":"p","x":"C) specialise in wine (its comparative advantage) and trade for cloth"},{"t":"p","x":"D) produce equal amounts of both goods for self-sufficiency"},{"t":"p","x":"E) impose a tariff on Country B's cloth exports"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nEven though Country A is absolutely more efficient at both goods, comparative advantage says both countries gain if each specialises in the good where it has the greatest RELATIVE efficiency (lowest opportunity cost) and trades for the rest."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. A researcher tests the Heckscher-Ohlin theory on a capital-abundant country's actual trade data and finds it exports a mix of both capital- and labour-intensive goods, contrary to the theory's prediction. This finding is known as:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the Leontief paradox"},{"t":"p","x":"B) trade diversion"},{"t":"p","x":"C) the optimal currency area problem"},{"t":"p","x":"D) the infant industry argument"},{"t":"p","x":"E) Krugman's new trade theory"},{"t":"a","x":"A\nThis is precisely Wassily Leontief's famous empirical finding that contradicted the Heckscher-Ohlin factor-endowments prediction for U.S. trade."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. A commercial aircraft manufacturer benefits from being one of only two global producers, having entered the market first and built up decades of scale and experience that make it very hard for a new entrant to match. This scenario best illustrates:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) absolute advantage"},{"t":"p","x":"B) the international product life cycle theory"},{"t":"p","x":"C) New Trade Theory / strategic trade theory"},{"t":"p","x":"D) mercantilism"},{"t":"p","x":"E) the Leontief paradox"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nFirst-mover advantage and durable dominance in an industry that can only support a small number of global competitors is the central mechanism described by Krugman's New Trade Theory."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Using Porter's Diamond, briefly analyse why Hong Kong has historically been internationally competitive in trade-related financial and logistics services (you may use general knowledge of Hong Kong to support each point).**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Factor conditions: Hong Kong has advanced factor conditions — world-class port/airport infrastructure, a deep pool of finance/trade professionals, and strong rule of law/property rights supporting financial contracting.\n• Demand conditions: sophisticated local and regional demand for trade-finance and logistics services from firms trading with mainland China and across Asia pushes continual service innovation.\n• Related & supporting industries: Hong Kong has a dense cluster of banks, insurers, shipping firms, and legal/accounting professional services that reinforce each other.\n• Firm strategy, structure & rivalry: intense competition among Hong Kong's many banks/trading houses/logistics firms drives ongoing efficiency and innovation, reinforced by a historically open, pro-competition government policy stance."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. A government minister argues: \"Comparative advantage theory is outdated — my country has no absolute advantage in anything, so free trade will only hurt us.\" Correct this misunderstanding using material from this chapter.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• This confuses absolute advantage with comparative advantage. Ricardo's key insight is that a country with NO absolute advantage in any good STILL gains from trade, as long as it specialises in whichever good it produces with the lowest opportunity cost relative to its other goods, and trades for the rest.\n• The empirical record strongly supports opening to trade being associated with faster growth (e.g. China's growth acceleration after opening to international trade) — though the minister is right to note (per Samuelson's qualification) that domestic job losses in specific import-competing sectors are a real, non-trivial cost that policy should try to address, even while the aggregate national gain from trade remains real."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Extra Practice — Balance of Payments"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1. Classify each as current, capital or financial account: (i) iron ore exports; (ii) debt forgiveness; (iii) foreign aid for emergency food; (iv) buying 15% of a foreign company’s voting shares; (v) dividends received from abroad; (vi) buying a small share of a foreign firm’s stock without influence; (vii) selling a brand name or trademark to a foreign owner."},{"t":"p","x":"Q2. Is each a credit or a debit in the home country’s BoP? (i) an export of wheat; (ii) payment for imported oil; (iii) wages earned by residents abroad; (iv) dividends paid to foreign shareholders."},{"t":"p","x":"Q3. Why should the total BoP equal zero, and what item ensures it does?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. Why does Hong Kong generally record a surplus in the goods and services account?"},{"t":"a","x":"A1. (i) Current (trade balance). (ii) Capital (capital transfer). (iii) Current (secondary income, current transfer). (iv) Financial (direct investment, 10% or more of voting power). (v) Current (primary income). (vi) Financial (portfolio investment). (vii) Capital (non-produced, non-financial asset).\nA2. (i) Credit. (ii) Debit. (iii) Credit (income received from the rest of the world). (iv) Debit.\nA3. Every transaction has two offsetting entries (credit and debit) of equal value, so the sum is zero. Net errors and omissions is added to cover measurement errors and unrecorded transactions.\nA4. Exports exceed imports; as a service economy Hong Kong is competitive as a service provider linking the Mainland and the rest of the world."}]},"7":{"title":"Government Policy and International Trade","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Despite the strong theoretical and empirical case that free trade raises welfare (Chapter 6), virtually every government intervenes in trade to some degree — what mercantilists once did openly is today called \"protectionism.\""},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.1 Instruments of Trade Policy"},{"t":"p","x":"Trade policy in six instruments (as listed in class): tariffs (trade barriers), subsidies (more positive to local businesses), import quotas and voluntary export restraints, local content requirements (relatively mild), administrative policies (trade barriers) and antidumping duties (trade barriers). Each measure restricts imports or supports exports: the winners are local producers protected from international competition, the losers are the general public who are denied access to imported products or pay more for them. Traditional barriers have been greatly reduced by WTO agreements, so some countries turned to less visible forms (local content, administrative policies)."},{"t":"p","x":"Tariffs were historically the most common barrier and are also a revenue source for governments. In the study guide's wording an ad valorem tariff is a percentage of the value of the goods and a specific tariff a fixed amount per item (the guide itself mislabels them; use the lecture definitions)."},{"t":"p","x":"Dumping can lead to misallocation of resources, as more competitive local firms are ousted by less productive dumpers, and firms dump to gain market-share dominance, sometimes supported by government subsidies. Antidumping measures used correctly are not bad in themselves, but they are often misused to penalise lower-cost or more productive nations: the EU put antidumping penalties on Chinese TVs, later overturned by the WTO, which ruled that no dumping had occurred."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Instrument","How it works"],["Tariffs","A tax on imports (ad valorem = % of value, or a fixed amount per unit); protects local producers and raises government revenue, at consumers' expense."],["Subsidies","Government payments/benefits to local producers so they can compete with imports or export more competitively; ultimately funded by taxpayers; especially common in agriculture, where it can hurt poorer countries with a genuine comparative advantage in farming."],["Quotas","A numerical limit on imports of a good; a tariff-rate quota allows a lower tariff up to a threshold, then a higher tariff above it; a Voluntary Export Restraint (VER) is the exporting country \"agreeing\" (usually under pressure) to limit its own exports."],["Local content requirements","Require a set % of a product's value to be produced locally; common in developing countries seeking both import substitution and technology transfer."],["Administrative policies","Bureaucratic/regulatory hurdles that make importing costly or slow without technically breaking trade-agreement commitments — a form of \"hidden\" protectionism."],["Antidumping policies","Countermeasures against \"dumping\" (selling below production cost to seize market share, often subsidised); legitimate when dumping is real, but frequently misused as disguised protectionism — e.g. EU anti-dumping tariffs on Chinese TVs were later overturned by the WTO, which found no dumping had occurred."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.2 Why Governments Intervene"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Political reasons (the dominant real-world driver)"},{"t":"li","x":"Protecting jobs & industries — by far the most common real motive: a small, concentrated group of workers/firms benefits visibly and lobbies/votes accordingly, while the (much larger) cost to the general public is diffuse and largely invisible — even though studies consistently show such tariffs cost the wider economy more than they \"save\" for the protected group."},{"t":"li","x":"Protecting national security — e.g. maintaining domestic aerospace/defence capacity; U.S. restrictions on Huawei telecoms equipment over cybersecurity concerns."},{"t":"li","x":"Retaliation — projects political strength, even though economists show retaliatory barriers make both countries worse off."},{"t":"li","x":"Protecting consumers — e.g. banning unsafe imports (British beef during mad-cow disease); genuine safety justifications and pretextual protectionism can be hard to distinguish."},{"t":"li","x":"Furthering foreign policy objectives — e.g. U.S. sanctions penalising any firm (domestic or foreign) doing business with Iran."},{"t":"li","x":"Protecting human rights — e.g. the historical international trade embargo on apartheid-era South Africa."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Economic reasons (less common, more contested)"},{"t":"li","x":"Infant industry argument: temporary protection lets young domestic industries in developing countries grow to a scale/competence where they can compete internationally."},{"t":"li","x":"Strategic trade policy: following New Trade Theory (Chapter 6), government support for industries with major first-mover/scale advantages — but risky, since it invites retaliation and applies to only a limited set of industries."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Balance of payments (background used in Chapters 7 and 8)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Balance of payments** — the record of all international monetary transactions of a country, private and public sector, showing how much money goes in and out. It summarises transactions in exports and imports of goods, services and financial assets, along with transfer payments such as foreign aid. It is closely related to foreign direct investment (Chapter 8)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Current account** — records the value of the flows (inflows and outflows, i.e. imports and exports) of goods, services and income between residents and the rest of the world; \"current\" because the items traded are consumed or received in the current period (within the quarter)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Capital and financial account** — records capital transfers, where one party transfers ownership of something without receiving anything in return (conditional grants for specific capital projects, such as a foreign aid project to build roads, and forgiveness of debt), and intangible assets such as brand names and rights to use land or water (e.g. for mining or fishing), together with financial assets."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.3 A Brief History of the World Trading System"},{"t":"li","x":"Mercantilism dominant until the mid-19th century, when major powers (influenced by Smith and Ricardo) opened to trade, producing a \"first wave of globalization\" (~1850–1910)."},{"t":"li","x":"Trade rebounded after WWI through the 1920s (a boom era, especially for the U.S.), but the 1929 Wall Street Crash triggered the U.S. Smoot-Hawley Tariff Act (1930), which raised tariffs sharply; other countries retaliated, world trade collapsed, and the resulting economic pain helped drive both the Great Depression and the rise of nationalist movements that led to WWII."},{"t":"li","x":"Post-WWII, the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT, 1947) was created precisely to prevent a repeat of that collapse — committing signatories to gradually negotiate down trade barriers; trade and growth expanded strongly for decades."},{"t":"li","x":"By the 1980s, protectionist pressure resurfaced (partly driven by Japan's rise as a competitor), often via the less-visible \"administrative\" barriers described above — exposing GATT's lack of an effective enforcement mechanism."},{"t":"li","x":"This led to the creation of the World Trade Organization (WTO), with real adjudication/enforcement power. The WTO has since struggled: a persistent developed-vs-developing-country split, a post-2008-financial-crisis backlash against free trade, and — during the Trump administration — the U.S. blocking new appointments to the WTO's appellate body, undermining its ability to resolve disputes. Free trade is described in the study guide as \"at a crossroads\", with meaningful risk of a return to greater protectionism/trade wars."},{"t":"p","x":"The period from about 1850 to 1910 is called the first wave of globalisation, after major trading nations opened their borders under the influence of Smith and Ricardo. The WTO employs judges to adjudicate trade disputes and set timelines for resolving them, and was seen as an effective policeman in its early years. As it expanded, a split emerged between developed countries (wanting more cuts in barriers on advanced manufactures and services) and developing countries (wanting the removal of long-standing barriers to their commodity and agricultural exports). Protests at annual WTO gatherings grew after the 2008–10 financial crisis. Bilateral and multilateral trade agreements (reciprocal agreements between two or more partners) aim to capture gains beyond those available under WTO treaties. New trade theorists believe government intervention in trade can be justified, which classical trade theorists dispute."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.4 Managerial Implications"},{"t":"li","x":"Firms must map exactly which barriers apply on each of their trade routes before committing to a strategy."},{"t":"li","x":"\"Re-shoring\": the U.S.-China trade war's tariffs have, in some cases, made pulling manufacturing back onshore cheaper than staying in China despite higher domestic production costs."},{"t":"li","x":"Firms actively lobby governments: export-oriented firms generally lobby FOR free trade agreements/lower barriers; domestically-oriented, import-competing firms generally lobby FOR protection."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.5 Government Policies on International Trade — Enrichment (Prof. Keung deck)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Tariffs"},{"t":"p","x":"• Tariff — a tax levied on imports that raises the cost of imported products relative to domestic products. Specific tariff: a fixed charge per unit imported. Ad valorem tariff: a proportion of the value of the imported good."},{"t":"p","x":"• Example: a Hong Kong toy costs HKD 100; a 20% tariff makes it HKD 120 before the importers’ own margins, so Country A consumers pay more than HKD 120 and will often prefer domestically produced toys (depending on safety, quality and brand). The exporter loses sales; consumers may suffer."},{"t":"p","x":"• Effects: tariffs raise government revenue, protect domestic producers by raising the cost of foreign goods, and force consumers to pay more. Tariffs are generally pro-producer and anti-consumer, reduce the overall efficiency of the world economy, and are called protectionism."},{"t":"p","x":"• Recent cases: 2025 US tariffs on Canada, Mexico and China, and Canadian retaliation (tariffs on US products, “buy Canadian”); beef (Brazil tariffs, Canadian beef rules); pharmaceuticals (a 100% US tariff threatened Australian exports); the US Supreme Court ruled in February 2026 that the global tariffs were illegal, and more than 1,000 companies (FedEx, Costco, Revlon) sued for refunds; Swissmem reported Swiss exports to the US down 18% in the fourth quarter."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Subsidies"},{"t":"p","x":"• Subsidy — a government payment to a domestic producer. It helps domestic producers compete against low-cost foreign imports and gain export markets, but consumers (as taxpayers) absorb the cost. Examples: agriculture (US corn); government contracts, loans, subsidies and tax credits worth at least $38 billion reported for Elon Musk’s businesses."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Import quotas and voluntary export restraints"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Instrument","Meaning"],["Import quota","A direct restriction on the quantity of a good that may be imported"],["Tariff rate quota","Hybrid: a lower tariff applies to imports within the quota than to imports above it (Vietnam tariff quotas on Cambodian rice)"],["Voluntary export restraint (VER)","A quota imposed by the exporting country, typically at the importing country’s request (Japanese cars to the US)"],["Quota rent","The extra profit producers make when supply is artificially limited by a quota; the additional revenue earned by those allowed to import"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Local content requirements"},{"t":"p","x":"• A requirement that some specific fraction of a good be produced domestically (in physical or value terms). Example: Australia requires streaming services (Netflix, Disney, Amazon Prime) to invest in local Australian content from mid-2024."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Advantages","Disadvantages"],["Job creation and income security","Higher costs (comparative advantage ignored); quality may be poor"],["Industrial diversification","Market misallocation of resources"],["Skills and technology development","Trade retaliation (seen as protectionism)"],["Reliance on local supply chains","Technological limitation; restrictions on and hesitation about foreign direct investment"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Administrative policies and antidumping"},{"t":"p","x":"• Administrative trade policies — bureaucratic rules designed to make it difficult for imports to enter a country; they hurt consumers by denying access to possibly superior foreign products. Example: the US ban on new communications equipment from Huawei, ZTE, Hikvision, Dahua and Hytera on national security grounds."},{"t":"p","x":"• Dumping — selling goods in a foreign market below cost of production or below “fair” market value. Antidumping policies protect domestic producers from unfair foreign competition; if the complaint has merit through the WTO, antidumping duties (also called countervailing duties) may be imposed. Example: China imposed antidumping tariffs on Australian barley in mid-2020 and lifted them after three years (2023)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The debate on government intervention"},{"t":"p","x":"• Political arguments: protecting jobs and industries, national security, consumers (ban unsafe products), foreign policy objectives (preferential terms, punishing “rogue” states), human rights. Economic arguments: the infant industry argument (criticized: useless unless it makes the industry more efficient; firms with potential should raise funds) and strategic trade policy (help domestic firms gain first-mover advantages)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Retaliation causes trade wars (US–Canada, 2025–2026): intervention is used as a bargaining tool, but invites retaliation that hurts international and local businesses (Vermont cheese exports to Canada fell to zero; Canadian electricity exports; furniture stores closing)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Three drawbacks of government intervention: it is self-defeating (protects the inefficient), dangerous (invites retaliation) and unlikely to be well executed (captured by special interest groups). Firms have an incentive to lobby for free trade."},{"t":"p","x":"• Managerial implications: tariffs raise the cost of exporting, quotas may limit a firm’s ability to serve a country, firms may need to meet local content requirements, and antidumping actions limit aggressive pricing to gain market share."},{"t":"p","x":"• WTO: members account for about 98% of world trade; it started strong but has fallen off since the late 1990s; Brexit and the Trump administration suggest a return to greater protectionism. Bilateral and multilateral agreements continue (New Zealand–EU, India–Australia, India–Canada; the US withdrew from the TPP while the other 11 nations continued)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-check — Chapter 7 enrichment"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can name the six instruments of trade policy and classify each as a barrier or a support for local business."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can distinguish specific from ad valorem tariffs and compute a tariff-inclusive price."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can define quota, tariff rate quota, VER and quota rent."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can state the pros and cons of local content requirements and define dumping."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can state three drawbacks of government intervention and explain how retaliation causes a trade war."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-assessment questions (voluntary): video \"China in the WTO\""},{"t":"p","x":"1. What will happen to China's corn production after entry to the WTO, and why?"},{"t":"p","x":"2. Which agricultural products will China be able to produce and compete in successfully after WTO entry?"},{"t":"p","x":"3. Which economic theory from Chapter 6 explains answers 1 and 2?"},{"t":"p","x":"4. An academic says \"you cannot learn to swim on dry land\": what does he mean?"},{"t":"p","x":"5. A worker says \"joining the WTO is good for the country, but bad for the masses\": what does she mean?"},{"t":"p","x":"Suggested reading: \"Zero-sum economics: the destructive logic that threatens globalisation\" (The Economist, January 2023) argues that subsidies, export controls and curbs on FDI are proliferating and threatening the gains from globalisation, in contrast with the positive-sum logic of free trade covered in Chapter 6."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 7"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A government imposes a tariff that raises domestic steel prices, protecting a relatively small number of steelworker jobs while raising costs for the much larger number of firms and consumers that buy steel. Economically, this outcome is best explained by:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the infant industry argument"},{"t":"p","x":"B) a concentrated-benefit, diffuse-cost political dynamic favouring protection despite a net welfare loss"},{"t":"p","x":"C) the Leontief paradox"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Porter's Diamond theory"},{"t":"p","x":"E) an optimal currency area"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nThis is the classic political-economy explanation for protectionism: a small, organised group (steelworkers) has a strong incentive to lobby, while the much larger, diffuse group of consumers bears a smaller individual cost and has little incentive to organise against it — even though the aggregate cost to the economy exceeds the benefit to the protected group."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. An exporting country agrees, under pressure from the importing country, to voluntarily limit how much of a good it will ship there. This is an example of:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) a tariff-rate quota only"},{"t":"p","x":"B) a Voluntary Export Restraint (VER)"},{"t":"p","x":"C) an antidumping duty"},{"t":"p","x":"D) a local content requirement"},{"t":"p","x":"E) an administrative policy"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA VER is defined precisely as an agreement, usually made under duress from the importing country, in which the EXPORTING country limits its own exports of a good."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. The U.S. Smoot-Hawley Tariff Act of 1930 is significant in trade history chiefly because it:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) created the WTO"},{"t":"p","x":"B) triggered retaliatory tariffs that helped collapse world trade and deepen the Great Depression"},{"t":"p","x":"C) eliminated all U.S. import tariffs"},{"t":"p","x":"D) established the Bretton Woods system"},{"t":"p","x":"E) was the first free trade agreement between the U.S. and Canada"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nSmoot-Hawley sharply raised U.S. tariffs after the 1929 crash; other countries retaliated, world trade collapsed, and the resulting economic damage is widely seen as having deepened the Great Depression and fuelled the nationalism that contributed to WWII."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. A trade minister proposes an antidumping duty on imported solar panels, claiming the exporting country is \"dumping\" them. Explain what genuine dumping is, and outline one risk that antidumping policy is often misused.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Dumping is selling a good in a foreign market below its cost of production, typically to seize market share and drive out local competitors (sometimes enabled by government subsidies in the exporting country).\n• Antidumping measures are legitimate when used to counter genuine dumping, but the tool is frequently misused as disguised protectionism against foreign producers who are simply more efficient/lower-cost — not actually dumping. The EU's anti-dumping tariffs on Chinese TVs, later overturned by the WTO after it found no dumping had occurred, is the course's own example of this risk."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Explain why the WTO was created as a successor to GATT, and describe one specific challenge the WTO has faced in recent years.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• GATT lacked an effective enforcement mechanism: when countries flouted their commitments (especially via harder-to-detect 'hidden' barriers like administrative policies), there was no strong way to compel correction or punish offenders.\n• The WTO was created with real judicial/enforcement power — employing judges to adjudicate trade disputes on set timelines.\n• Recent challenges include: a persistent split between developed and developing member priorities (e.g. over agricultural vs. manufactured-goods/services liberalisation); intensified anti-globalisation protest after the 2008 financial crisis; and the Trump-era U.S. blocking of new appellate-body judge appointments, which has hampered the WTO's ability to resolve disputes."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Extra Practice — Government Policies (Moodle deck)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1. A toy costs HKD 100 to export; Country A imposes a 20% ad valorem tariff. What is the tariff-inclusive price? What if the tariff were a specific tariff of HKD 15 per toy?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q2. Classify as tariff, subsidy, quota, VER, local content requirement, administrative policy or antidumping duty: (i) the exporting country caps its own car shipments at the importer’s request; (ii) streaming services must fund local films; (iii) a tax on cheap steel sold below fair value; (iv) cash paid to farmers; (v) rules that delay inspection of imports."},{"t":"p","x":"Q3. What is quota rent and who earns it?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. Give two advantages and two disadvantages of local content requirements."},{"t":"p","x":"Q5. Name three drawbacks of government intervention and two economic arguments for it."},{"t":"p","x":"Q6. Why can a tariff by Country A start a trade war?"},{"t":"a","x":"A1. Ad valorem: 100 × 1.20 = HKD 120. Specific: 100 + 15 = HKD 115.\nA2. (i) VER. (ii) Local content requirement. (iii) Antidumping duty. (iv) Subsidy. (v) Administrative policy.\nA3. Quota rent is the extra profit producers (importers) make when supply is artificially limited by a quota; it is earned by those allowed to import.\nA4. Advantages: job creation; skills and technology development (also industrial diversification, local supply chains). Disadvantages: higher costs; market misallocation (also retaliation, technological limitation, FDI hesitation).\nA5. Drawbacks: self-defeating (protects the inefficient); dangerous (invites retaliation); unlikely to be well executed (special interest groups). Economic arguments: infant industry; strategic trade policy.\nA6. Other countries retaliate with their own tariffs, which spreads to more products and partners and hurts exporters and consumers in both countries."}]},"8":{"title":"Foreign Direct Investment (FDI)","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"FDI is when a firm invests capital earned in its home operations to establish or control a business activity in another (\"host\") country; the home-country headquarters retains management control over the foreign subsidiary and expects to eventually repatriate profits. This distinguishes FDI from portfolio investment (passive cross-border investment, e.g. buying foreign shares, without management control) — the UN/textbook threshold is that a stake above 10% ownership qualifies as FDI."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.1 Measuring FDI"},{"t":"p","x":"Learning objectives of the chapter: recognise current FDI trends, explain the theories of FDI, understand how political ideology shapes a government's attitude to FDI, describe the benefits and costs of FDI to home and host countries, explain the policy instruments governments use to influence FDI, and identify the implications for managers."},{"t":"li","x":"**FDI (lecture definition)** — a firm invests directly in new facilities to produce or market in a foreign country. The US Department of Commerce counts a 10 percent interest in a foreign business entity as FDI. A firm engaged in FDI is a multinational enterprise, with differing degrees of multinationality."},{"t":"li","x":"**Greenfield investment** — FDI with 100% ownership, totally originating from the firm and newly set up in a foreign country."},{"t":"li","x":"**International joint venture** — FDI with less than 100% ownership: majority, equal-share or minority participation."},{"t":"li","x":"**Outflow of FDI** — the flow of FDI out of a country (e.g. South Korean investment going to Vietnam)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Inflow of FDI** — the flow of FDI into a country (e.g. Vietnam allowing investment from South Korea)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Stock of FDI (balance of payments link)** — the total accumulated value of foreign-owned assets at a given time, which relates to the financial assets in the balance of payments. The flow of FDI is the amount undertaken over a given period."},{"t":"li","x":"FDI flow: the net amount of NEW investment from one country to another in a given year — the figure most often quoted in news headlines (\"FDI up X% this year\")."},{"t":"li","x":"FDI stock: the cumulative total value of all FDI investment made to date — arguably the more meaningful long-run measure."},{"t":"li","x":"Production realised from FDI: the total value of goods/services produced by FDI-funded operations — globally, this measure typically EXCEEDS the total value of international trade."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.2 Growth and Direction of FDI"},{"t":"p","x":"FDI is more recent than trade (limited to the very largest firms before ~1950) but has grown far faster than both world trade and world GDP over the past fifty years, driven by the same forces as globalization (Chapter 1): falling trade/investment barriers and technological change (especially telecommunications, which now lets HQs manage/integrate foreign subsidiaries into a genuinely global supply chain)."},{"t":"li","x":"Traditionally, most FDI flowed between developed countries (the U.S., Japan and Britain have historically been the largest outward investors, and the U.S. the largest recipient)."},{"t":"li","x":"Since Deng Xiaoping opened China to FDI in the late 1970s, China and other developing economies have become both major FDI recipients AND, more recently, major outward investors themselves."},{"t":"li","x":"The theoretical link to comparative advantage: e.g. Nike keeps design/marketing (its comparative advantage) in the U.S. but manufactures shoes in Indonesia/Vietnam (their comparative advantage) — FDI, like trade, can raise world efficiency by matching activities to each country's relative strengths."},{"t":"p","x":"The lecture cases: Samsung (home country South Korea) investing in Vietnam (host country) as greenfield investment, approved under national-level agreements (free trade or other agreements) between the two countries; Vietnam is a global manufacturing hub, and Singapore, Japan and Taiwan have also invested there. Cultural considerations matter. Historically most FDI went to developed nations (the US and EU were favourite targets); more recently developing nations in South, East and Southeast Asia (particularly China) and Latin America have received significant inflows."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.3 Types of Outward FDI"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Greenfield investment","Building an entirely new foreign operation from scratch. Time-consuming and can be difficult to establish against already-entrenched local competitors."],["Acquisition (M&A)","Buying an existing foreign firm and taking over its operations. The most common form of FDI by volume — fast to establish, but can be costly to acquire."],["Joint venture / joint development","Partnering with a local firm. Lowers the initial capital outlay and eases entry into an unfamiliar market, but requires profit-sharing and can create partner-management friction."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.4 Why Does FDI Happen? Dunning's Eclectic (OLI) Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"Because a foreign firm competing in an unfamiliar environment suffers a \"liability of foreignness\", FDI could in theory be an inefficient entry mode compared to simply buying from, or licensing to, local firms. Dunning's OLI theory explains why FDI happens anyway: it occurs when all three conditions hold simultaneously."},{"t":"li","x":"Ownership advantage: the firm can do or produce something more efficiently than local competitors"},{"t":"li","x":"Location advantage: producing in the foreign location makes more sense than producing at home"},{"t":"li","x":"Internalization advantage: there is a good reason to keep the activity INSIDE the firm rather than licensing it to a local partner (most often, to protect proprietary technology/know-how from leaking to a potential competitor)"},{"t":"p","x":"Nike example: Nike's ownership advantage (design/brand/marketing capability) stays in the U.S.; Indonesia/Vietnam offer the location advantage (lower-cost, appropriately-skilled manufacturing labour); Nike chooses FDI/tightly-controlled contract manufacturing (an internalization choice) rather than simply licensing its designs to unrelated local factories."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.5 Government Policy Toward FDI"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Free-market view","FDI benefits the world economy via comparative advantage; firms and governments should be free to invest/receive investment without restriction."],["Radical view","FDI is inherently extractive/harmful to the host economy — coming only to exploit cheap labour, the local market, or natural resources — and should be discouraged."],["Pragmatic nationalism (most common today)","Governments evaluate each FDI proposal case-by-case on its net benefits vs. costs, considering: (1) Jobs created and their quality; (2) Technology transfer potential; (3) Local impact (does it use local suppliers and boost local competitiveness, or displace local firms?); (4) Balance-of-payments impact (capital inflow use; subsequent import/export effects); (5) Sovereignty/national-security concerns."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Host country responses range along a spectrum: active encouragement (tax holidays, free land, dedicated investment-promotion agencies) → conditional acceptance (requiring local content or export commitments) → outright rejection for unattractive proposals."},{"t":"p","x":"Home country perspective: generally supportive of outward FDI (extends national influence, revenue and opportunities for domestic firms), but may restrict it when it causes domestic job losses, transfers strategically sensitive technology to a rival, or is seen as a wasteful use of foreign currency."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.6 Benefits and Costs of FDI"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Host-country benefits"},{"t":"li","x":"**Resource-transfer effects** — FDI can bring capital, technology and management resources that would otherwise not be available."},{"t":"li","x":"**Employment effects** — FDI can bring jobs that would otherwise not be created. In Vietnam these are low-skilled jobs in factories, stores and online customer service, plus skilled South Korean staff whose housing, food, education and daily purchases create more Korean businesses there. Opponents say not all new jobs are net additions to employment."},{"t":"li","x":"**Balance-of-payments effects** — the balance-of-payments account records a country's payments to and receipts from other countries, and the current account records exports and imports of goods and services. A surplus is usually favoured over a deficit. FDI helps the host current account when it substitutes for imports, or when the MNE's subsidiary exports goods and services to other countries."},{"t":"li","x":"**Effect on competition and economic growth** — FDI increases competition, drives down prices and improves consumer welfare, which leads to productivity growth, product and process innovation and greater economic growth."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Host-country costs"},{"t":"li","x":"**Adverse effects on competition** — subsidiaries of foreign MNEs may have greater economic power than indigenous competitors, because they are part of a larger organisation and can use funds generated elsewhere to subsidise costs locally. This could drive local competitors out and create a monopoly position (it applies to cellphones, clinics, dental services, supermarkets and banks)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Adverse effects on the balance of payments** — first, capital outflows as foreign subsidiaries repatriate earnings to the parent; second, a debit on the host current account from imports of input products by the foreign subsidiary."},{"t":"li","x":"**Effects on national sovereignty and autonomy** — FDI can mean some loss of economic independence, because key decisions affecting the host economy are made by a foreign parent with no real commitment to the host country and over which its government has no real control."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Home-country benefits"},{"t":"p","x":"Home-country benefits (e.g. South Korea): the capital account of the balance of payments benefits from the inward flow of foreign earnings; outward FDI creates employment effects (e.g. skilled South Korean labour in Vietnam); and the home country gains valuable skills from foreign markets that can be transferred back."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Home-country costs"},{"t":"p","x":"Home-country costs: the balance of payments suffers from the initial capital outflow needed to finance the FDI; the current account is hurt if the FDI serves the home market from a low-cost production location, or if it substitutes for direct exports; and in a home country with unemployment there is concern about the export of jobs."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.7 Government policy instruments"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Home-country policies"},{"t":"li","x":"**Encouraging outward FDI** — government-backed insurance programmes against major types of foreign investment risk; special funds or banks making government loans to firms investing in developing countries; elimination of double taxation of foreign income; and many host nations have relaxed restrictions on inbound FDI (e.g. free rent for Samsung factories in Vietnam)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Restricting outward FDI** — virtually all investor countries, including the United States, have exercised some control from time to time: tax rules are manipulated to favour investing at home, and firms may be barred from investing in certain nations for political reasons (e.g. the US restricting outward FDI in China and Hong Kong)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Host-country policies"},{"t":"li","x":"**Encouraging inward FDI** — governments offer incentives to foreign firms to gain the resource-transfer and employment effects of FDI and to capture FDI away from other potential host countries."},{"t":"li","x":"**Restricting inward FDI** — through ownership restraints (excluding foreign firms from certain sectors on grounds of national security or competition, and because local owners can help maximise the resource-transfer and employment benefits) and performance requirements (used to maximise the benefits and minimise the costs of FDI for the host country)."},{"t":"p","x":"Sanctions case: the EU, the US and Canada imposed sanctions restricting inward FDI from Russia and outward FDI to Russia because of the war between Russia and Ukraine."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.8 Managerial Implications"},{"t":"p","x":"FDI is costly, in both capital and managerial attention (a real opportunity cost); most firms therefore use lower-commitment entry modes (exporting, licensing) first, and only \"graduate\" to FDI once the market/relationship is proven."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.9 In-Class Case: Taiwan FDI Proposal Ranking (Adapted)"},{"t":"p","x":"Three FDI proposals were presented to Taiwan's (fictional) Department of Trade and Industry:"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Ford Motor Co. — $100m greenfield investment for a state-of-the-art assembly plant using advanced Japanese robotics, producing luxury cars for export worldwide."},{"t":"li","x":"B. A small Malaysian computer firm — $4m low-tech assembly plant using local component suppliers, selling mostly to the region (not for export outside Asia)."},{"t":"li","x":"C. A German steel firm — proposes to acquire the loss-making, state-owned Taiwan Steel (the only steelmaker left in the country) for $40m, roughly half of its ~$80m asset value, citing years of losses; output sold almost entirely in the domestic market."},{"t":"p","x":"Applying the pragmatic-nationalism criteria (jobs, technology, local impact, BoP, sovereignty), Proposal A is generally the most attractive: high-value jobs, advanced technology transfer, and export-oriented (strongly positive BoP effect). Proposal B is moderate: modest job creation and some local-supplier benefit, but low-tech and limited to a regional (not export) market. Proposal C is the most complex: it rescues a failing state asset and could bring new technology/management, but also implies job losses/restructuring, sovereignty sensitivities (foreign control of a formerly state-owned strategic industry), and a purely domestic-market focus with no BoP export benefit — a government would likely want conditions attached (e.g. minimum employment guarantees, technology-transfer commitments) before approval."},{"t":"p","x":"Suggested viewing: the film \"American Factory\" (2019 Oscar for best documentary, on Netflix) shows a Chinese firm acquiring a disused factory in the American Midwest to make car windscreens, touching on FDI and culture. The in-class questions were to rank the three proposals, say what Taiwan should do to attract the most attractive one, and what conditions to set before allowing the least attractive one."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 8"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A US investment fund buys a 3% passive stake in a French company's publicly-traded shares, with no management involvement. This is best classified as:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Foreign Direct Investment (FDI)"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Portfolio investment"},{"t":"p","x":"C) A Greenfield investment"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Offshore outsourcing"},{"t":"p","x":"E) A Joint venture"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA passive stake below the ~10% FDI ownership threshold, with no management control, is portfolio investment rather than FDI."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Under Dunning's OLI (eclectic) theory, a firm choosing to manufacture in-house abroad (rather than licensing its technology to a local partner) to prevent that technology being copied is exercising which advantage?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Ownership advantage"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Location advantage"},{"t":"p","x":"C) Internalization advantage"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Comparative advantage only"},{"t":"p","x":"E) Absolute advantage"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nChoosing to keep an activity inside the firm rather than licensing it out — specifically to protect proprietary technology — is the internalization advantage in the OLI framework."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. A host government reviews an incoming FDI proposal by weighing job creation, technology transfer, local-supplier impact, balance-of-payments effects, and sovereignty concerns on a case-by-case basis. This approach is known as:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the radical view of FDI"},{"t":"p","x":"B) the free-market view of FDI"},{"t":"p","x":"C) pragmatic nationalism"},{"t":"p","x":"D) mercantilism"},{"t":"p","x":"E) strategic trade policy"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nCase-by-case weighing of costs and benefits, rather than a blanket welcome (free-market view) or blanket rejection (radical view), defines pragmatic nationalism."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Distinguish FDI flow from FDI stock, and explain which one better answers the question: \"How large is total American economic presence, built up over decades, inside Vietnam today?\"**"},{"t":"a","x":"• FDI flow measures only the NET NEW investment made in a single year (e.g. this year's inflow minus repatriated capital) — a snapshot of recent momentum.\n• FDI stock measures the CUMULATIVE total of all FDI ever made and still held — the running total built up over time.\n• The question asks about total accumulated American economic presence built up over decades, so FDI stock is the correct measure — FDI flow would only show this year's incremental change, not the accumulated total."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. A government official says: \"We should welcome all incoming FDI without exception, since foreign investment always helps our economy.\" Using the free-market vs. radical vs. pragmatic-nationalism framework, evaluate this statement.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• The official's statement reflects the pure free-market view, which does have real support (comparative advantage logic suggests mutual gains from unrestricted investment) but ignores genuine host-country risks the radical view highlights — e.g. an FDI project that displaces local competitors, repatriates most profits with little local benefit, or compromises a strategically sensitive sector.\n• Most real-world governments (and the course's own preferred framework) instead apply pragmatic nationalism: evaluating each proposal on jobs, technology transfer, local impact, balance-of-payments effects and sovereignty/security concerns, encouraging clearly beneficial projects (e.g. via tax incentives) while attaching conditions to or rejecting less beneficial ones — a more balanced position than either extreme."}]},"9":{"title":"Regional Economic Integration","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Note: Per the official Study Guide: this chapter's scope is deliberately narrower than the others. Students do NOT need to memorise the specific details/history of individual regional agreements (NAFTA, EU, ASEAN, etc.) for the mid-term or final exam — only general familiarity with their membership and FORM. The exam-relevant focus is the general framework of integration levels, the economic/political rationale, trade creation vs. trade diversion, and the costs/benefits of a common currency."},{"t":"p","x":"Why do regional/bilateral deals keep proliferating? The WTO's multilateral \"single undertaking\" approach (\"nothing is agreed until everything is agreed\") has largely stalled, because it must reconcile too many divergent national interests at once. It is far easier for a smaller group of geographically-close countries to reach agreement among themselves."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.1 Levels of Economic Integration"},{"t":"p","x":"Each level nests inside the next — a deeper level includes all the features of the levels beneath it, and each step represents a significant loss of national sovereignty."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Level","Defining feature"],["Free Trade Area","Tariff-free trade between members; each member independently sets its own tariffs on non-members."],["Customs Union","Free trade area + a common external tariff — allows border checks on origin to be eliminated."],["Common Market","Customs union + free movement of capital AND labour between members (no work permits needed)."],["Economic Union","Common market + shared macroeconomic policy — e.g. a common currency, harmonised tax rates, common monetary policy."],["Political Union","Economic union + political merger — common social policy and a shared defence policy."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.2 Why Integrate? Economic and Political Rationale"},{"t":"li","x":"Economic: removing internal trade barriers lets the region specialise via comparative advantage, giving consumers wider/cheaper/higher-quality choice and giving firms lower input costs and greater economies of scale, raising international competitiveness."},{"t":"li","x":"Political: reduces tension between historic rivals (part of the EU's founding motivation, after two European wars) and gives small countries greater collective bargaining power than they would have alone."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.3 Impediments to Deeper Integration"},{"t":"li","x":"Even if aggregate/medium-term gains are positive, some sectors face real short-term job losses as internal competition intensifies — a politically painful, immediate cost."},{"t":"li","x":"Each deeper level requires giving up more national sovereignty/decision-making autonomy — a key driver of the UK's vote for Brexit (resentment at \"being told what to do\" by EU institutions)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.4 Is Regional Integration Good for the World Economy? Trade Creation vs. Trade Diversion"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Trade creation","More efficient, LOW-COST producers WITHIN the new trade area replace less-efficient domestic producers — a net welfare GAIN."],["Trade diversion","More efficient producers OUTSIDE the trade area are replaced by less-efficient producers INSIDE the area, purely because of the area's preferential tariffs — a net welfare LOSS."]]},{"t":"p","x":"A regional trade agreement benefits the world economy overall only when the trade creation it generates exceeds the trade diversion it causes — this exact question is directly tested in the official Chapter 9 quiz below."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.5 Costs and Benefits of a Common Regional Currency"},{"t":"li","x":"Benefits: eliminates the transaction costs and exchange-rate uncertainty of cross-border trade/investment within the region (different currencies act like an invisible trade barrier); increases transparency, further boosting cross-border trade and investment."},{"t":"li","x":"Costs: member states lose national monetary autonomy — interest rates and money supply are set jointly (e.g. by the European Central Bank), not independently. This works well only when the members form an \"optimal currency area\" (their economies move in sync and share similar monetary-policy needs). When needs diverge, this becomes a serious problem — the Eurozone crisis arose largely because Greece needed higher interest rates/tighter policy at a time Germany needed the opposite. This is precisely why the UK, Denmark and Sweden chose to stay outside the euro."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.6 Regional Agreements in Asia (light-touch, per Study Guide)"},{"t":"li","x":"ASEAN: the oldest, most established regional agreement in Asia, though still limited in coverage (many goods/services excluded); deepening cooperation with mainland China and moving toward more tariff-free movement."},{"t":"li","x":"APEC: functions mostly as a talking forum, though it has proposed a free trade area of the Asia-Pacific."},{"t":"li","x":"RCEP (Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership): ASEAN + China, Japan, South Korea, Australia and New Zealand (see also the Illustrative Readings section for two contemporary news articles on RCEP's signing)."},{"t":"li","x":"TPP → CPTPP: the U.S.-led Trans-Pacific Partnership was abandoned by President Trump; the remaining 11 countries (Australia, Canada, Japan, Mexico, New Zealand, Vietnam, Singapore, etc.) ratified the narrower Comprehensive and Progressive Agreement for Trans-Pacific Partnership."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.7 Managerial Implications"},{"t":"p","x":"Regional agreements let firms serve a whole harmonised economic region rather than country-by-country (lower compliance costs, harmonised standards, free internal movement of goods) — but can also act as \"fortresses\", easing business within the bloc while raising effective barriers against outside firms. The resulting complexity of managing many overlapping regional/bilateral deals is itself one of the strongest arguments for renewed progress at the (single, universal) WTO level."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.8 Official Chapter 9 Quiz (with Answers)"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Canada and the United States agree to remove all barriers to the trade of goods between them. However, the countries agree that each would be allowed to determine its own trade policies with regard to nonmembers. The economic integration between these countries is called a(n)**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) free trade area."},{"t":"p","x":"B) customs union."},{"t":"p","x":"C) common market."},{"t":"p","x":"D) economic union."},{"t":"p","x":"E) political union."},{"t":"a","x":"A"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Which political factor resulted in the establishment of the European Union (EU)?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) spectacular success of NAFTA"},{"t":"p","x":"B) trade impasse following the 1970s oil crisis"},{"t":"p","x":"C) devastation of Western Europe during two world wars and the desire for lasting peace"},{"t":"p","x":"D) emergence of Japan as an economic/industrial superpower"},{"t":"p","x":"E) rise of communism in Europe"},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. After the formation of a free trade area, the US found its high-cost furniture producers in North Carolina replaced by low-cost producers from another member country. This is an example of**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) value creation."},{"t":"p","x":"B) strategic pricing."},{"t":"p","x":"C) trade creation."},{"t":"p","x":"D) trade diversion."},{"t":"p","x":"E) economic exposure."},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**4. A regional free trade agreement will benefit the world only if**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) it raises the standard of living in one of the member countries."},{"t":"p","x":"B) the amount of trade it creates exceeds the amount it diverts."},{"t":"p","x":"C) the currencies of the nations involved appreciate."},{"t":"p","x":"D) the balance-of-trade situation remains stable in the region."},{"t":"p","x":"E) it creates a trade surplus for one of the countries involved."},{"t":"a","x":"B"},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Iceland and Norway form a free trade area. Iceland begins importing wool from Norway instead of from England, which had produced wool more cheaply than either Iceland or Norway. This is an example of**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) trade creation"},{"t":"p","x":"B) strategic pricing"},{"t":"p","x":"C) synergy"},{"t":"p","x":"D) trade diversion"},{"t":"p","x":"E) protectionism"},{"t":"a","x":"D"},{"t":"p","x":"**6. What was a change proposed by the Single European Act?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) establish frontier controls among EC countries"},{"t":"p","x":"B) increase the resources required for complying with trade bureaucracy"},{"t":"p","x":"C) place barriers to competition in retail banking and insurance"},{"t":"p","x":"D) apply the principle of \"mutual recognition\" to product standards"},{"t":"p","x":"E) reduce costs by not allowing lower-cost suppliers into national economies"},{"t":"a","x":"D"},{"t":"p","x":"**7. A key advantage of adopting the euro is that it**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) helps reduce competition in Europe."},{"t":"p","x":"B) has prevented a highly liquid pan-European capital market."},{"t":"p","x":"C) lowers foreign exchange and hedging costs in Europe."},{"t":"p","x":"D) insulates Europe from international competition."},{"t":"p","x":"E) increases investment options open to institutions only."},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**8. Great Britain, Denmark, and Sweden have stayed out of the euro zone because of the**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) dollar peg advocated by some EU members."},{"t":"p","x":"B) implied loss of national sovereignty to the European Central Bank."},{"t":"p","x":"C) volatility of the euro."},{"t":"p","x":"D) reluctance to compete directly against the U.S. dollar."},{"t":"p","x":"E) reluctance to be considered an optimal currency area."},{"t":"a","x":"B"},{"t":"p","x":"**9. Two neighbouring countries have the same wage rates, tax regimes and business cycles, and have reacted similarly to past economic shocks. These two countries can adopt a common currency because they form a(n)**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) managed currency zone."},{"t":"p","x":"B) open exchange regime."},{"t":"p","x":"C) optimal currency area."},{"t":"p","x":"D) free trade area."},{"t":"p","x":"E) advanced monetary zone."},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**10. An expected consequence of the implementation of NAFTA was that**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) low-skilled jobs would move to Mexico, lowering average wage rates in the U.S. and Canada."},{"t":"p","x":"B) increased imports from Mexico would reduce the U.S./Canada trade deficit."},{"t":"p","x":"C) lower Mexican incomes would decrease demand for U.S./Canadian goods."},{"t":"p","x":"D) many Mexican firms would hire low-skilled U.S. workers."},{"t":"p","x":"E) some U.S. and Canadian firms would move production to Mexico for lower labour costs."},{"t":"a","x":"E"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 9"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Which level of regional economic integration is the FIRST to require free movement of labour between member countries, without work permits?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Free trade area"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Customs union"},{"t":"p","x":"C) Common market"},{"t":"p","x":"D) A simple tariff-rate quota"},{"t":"p","x":"E) An antidumping agreement"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nFree movement of labour (alongside capital) is the defining addition at the Common Market level, beyond the customs union's shared external tariff."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Why might a regional trade bloc's common currency work well for some members but poorly for others?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) because tariffs always rise under a common currency"},{"t":"p","x":"B) because members lose independent monetary policy, which only works smoothly if they form an optimal currency area with synchronised economic needs"},{"t":"p","x":"C) because a common currency always causes trade diversion"},{"t":"p","x":"D) because a common currency abolishes the WTO"},{"t":"p","x":"E) because common currencies are illegal under GATT"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nThe core cost of a common currency is lost national monetary autonomy; this only works well when members' economies move together (an optimal currency area) — otherwise, as in the Eurozone crisis, one common interest rate cannot suit members with very different needs."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. ASEAN member states are considering deepening from a free trade area to a full common market. Identify one specific new benefit and one specific new sovereignty cost this deepening would bring.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• New benefit: free movement of labour and capital across ASEAN would let firms allocate workers/investment to wherever they are most productive within the region, and let workers move to where wages/opportunities are best, raising regional efficiency beyond what a free trade area alone can achieve.\n• New sovereignty cost: individual governments would lose the ability to independently control immigration/labour-market policy and cross-border capital flows — a bigger sovereignty concession than simply agreeing on tariffs, and the kind of concession that, in the EU's case, fed resentment that contributed to Brexit."}]},"10":{"title":"The Foreign Exchange Market","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"The instructor's own framing (not in the textbook, but taught explicitly in class): think of a unit of currency as a measure of VALUE. This reframing makes it much easier to understand what \"overvalued\" and \"undervalued\" actually mean, and why exchange rates move the way they do."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.1 The Foreign Exchange (\"Forex\") Market"},{"t":"p","x":"Operates across multiple financial centres (mainly London, New York, Tokyo), serving the currency-exchange needs of travellers, and of firms/governments engaged in trade and investment. In practice, however, the overwhelming majority of daily FX trading volume comes from short-term currency speculators, not underlying trade/investment activity."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.2 Three Types of Foreign Exchange Risk"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Transaction risk","The exchange rate moves between agreeing a deal and settling payment — e.g. a firm that agrees to pay in a foreign currency in six months may end up paying more (in its own currency) if that currency appreciates in the meantime; a seller who is due to receive a foreign currency later may receive less (in its own currency) if that currency depreciates."],["Translation risk","Consolidating a multinational's foreign revenue/profit into home-currency financial statements can distort the picture — if the HOME currency appreciates, the same foreign earnings translate into a smaller home-currency figure, even though nothing changed in the actual foreign business; this can hurt stock price and the ability to raise capital."],["Economic risk","If a customer country's currency depreciates sharply, demand for the foreign firm's (now relatively more expensive) imported products falls; the firm must choose between accepting lower sales or cutting its price/margin to stay competitive there."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Hedging with forward contracts: a spot rate is today's market exchange rate; a forward rate is a rate locked in TODAY for a currency exchange that will happen at a FUTURE date. A forward contract removes exchange-rate uncertainty for the customer, in exchange for a premium/spread the bank charges for bearing that risk itself."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.3 Explaining Exchange Rate Movements"},{"t":"p","x":"**The Law of One Price / Purchasing Power Parity (PPP)**"},{"t":"p","x":"A given basket of goods should cost the same everywhere, once converted at the market exchange rate. When it doesn't, the exchange rate should adjust until it does; the equilibrium rate where prices equalise is called the PPP rate. Because relative inflation drives these price gaps, a country with persistently higher inflation should see its currency depreciate against lower-inflation countries. Since future inflation is hard to observe directly, relative interest rates are used as a proxy: the International Fisher Effect links interest-rate differences between two countries to the expected movement in their exchange rate. Non-economic factors (market sentiment, the \"bandwagon effect\") also matter, especially in the short run — but PPP remains the most credible tool for MEDIUM- to LONG-term exchange-rate movements."},{"t":"p","x":"**The Big Mac Index (The Economist)**"},{"t":"p","x":"A well-known, tongue-in-cheek but genuinely instructive real-world application of PPP: because a Big Mac is produced almost identically in 100+ countries, comparing its price (converted to USD) across countries gives a simple PPP-based valuation of each currency. A currency is \"undervalued\" if its Big Mac converts to fewer USD than the US price, and \"overvalued\" if it converts to more. The Economist has found that currencies flagged as undervalued by the index tend, ON AVERAGE, to appreciate over the following years — but short (e.g. two-year) back-tests of a naive \"buy the cheapest currency\" strategy have a genuinely mixed record, partly because poorer countries structurally have lower price levels even without currency misvaluation (higher-income countries simply have higher wages/prices even in less-tradable parts of the economy) — which is why The Economist also publishes a GDP-adjusted version of the index."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.4 Can Short-Term Exchange Rate Movements Be Predicted?"},{"t":"p","x":"A genuinely contested question. The efficient-market view (e.g. as expressed by former U.S. Federal Reserve chair Alan Greenspan, quoted in class materials as saying no model beats a coin flip) holds that today's exchange rate already reflects all available information, so short-term moves are essentially unpredictable. The alternative view holds markets are imperfect and can be \"beaten\" using either:"},{"t":"li","x":"Fundamental analysis: regression models built on macroeconomic variables — weakness: correlation is not causation, and any model is necessarily calibrated on past (backward-looking) data."},{"t":"li","x":"Technical analysis: chart patterns/trend-following — has little logical/theoretical grounding, though it remains popular among some traders."},{"t":"p","x":"Course conclusion: short-term currency speculation/day-trading is NOT recommended for individuals or non-specialist firms — many participants lose money (illustrated by the retail-forex boom described in the Newsweek article \"Making Money on Money\" and the U.S. CFTC's long record of fraud-enforcement actions against forex-trading schemes)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.5 Managerial Implications"},{"t":"p","x":"Recognise which of the three risk types apply to a given transaction/investment; use lead/lag strategies or forward-contract hedging to manage exposure; understand PPP and the Fisher effect as the fundamental drivers of exchange rates rather than relying on short-term forecasting, which is unreliable even for specialists."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.6 Official In-Class FX Exercises (worked examples, October 2017 rates as originally set)"},{"t":"p","x":"These are the instructor's own in-class numerical exercises, reproduced here as worked examples (illustrative rates only — always use current live rates for any real calculation):"},{"t":"li","x":"Currency conversion: converting a EUR, GBP or HKD amount into another currency at a given cross-rate is simply multiplying by the appropriate exchange rate — e.g. EUR 800 at a given EUR/USD rate converts directly to USD."},{"t":"li","x":"Forward-contract hedge decision: a Hong Kong importer owes a Thai supplier THB 2,400,000 in six months. If the Thai Baht is expected to strengthen, the importer compares (a) waiting and converting HKD to THB at the future spot rate (risky — costs more if the Baht strengthens as expected) against (b) locking in a forward contract today at a fixed HKD cost. If the forward-contract HKD cost is lower than the expected future spot-rate cost, the hedge is worthwhile — it removes the risk of the Baht appreciating further."},{"t":"li","x":"Big Mac / PPP valuation: given the US Big Mac price and the actual foreign-currency Big Mac price in another country, the implied PPP exchange rate is (foreign price) ÷ (US price); comparing this implied PPP rate to the ACTUAL market exchange rate reveals whether that currency is over- or under-valued, and (per PPP theory) which direction it should be expected to move over the medium/long term."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.7 Official Chapter 10 Quiz (with Answers)"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Steven converted $1,000 to ¥105,000 for a trip to Japan. He spent only ¥50,000. During this period the dollar weakened against the yen. Using a current exchange rate of $1 = ¥100, how many dollars does Steven have left?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) $550"},{"t":"p","x":"B) $523"},{"t":"p","x":"C) $450"},{"t":"p","x":"D) $600"},{"t":"p","x":"E) $500"},{"t":"a","x":"A\n¥105,000 − ¥50,000 = ¥55,000 remaining; at $1 = ¥100, ¥55,000 = $550."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Currency speculation takes place when**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) there is short-term movement of funds from one currency to another hoping to profit from shifts in exchange rates."},{"t":"p","x":"B) the exchange rate a dealer will convert one currency at differs on a particular day."},{"t":"p","x":"C) there is a simultaneous purchase and sale of a given amount of FX for two different value dates."},{"t":"p","x":"D) securities bought in one market are immediately resold in another to profit from a price discrepancy."},{"t":"p","x":"E) money supply growth exceeds output growth, leading to inflation."},{"t":"a","x":"A"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. Currency A trades at 6 to the US$, currency B at 40 to the US$, and currency C at 120 to the US$. Which statement is true?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Of A, B and C, country A has the best economy."},{"t":"p","x":"B) Of A, B and C, currency A is the strongest."},{"t":"p","x":"C) Of A, B and C, currency C is the most undervalued."},{"t":"p","x":"D) A), B) and C) are all true."},{"t":"p","x":"E) It is impossible to say if any of the above are true."},{"t":"a","x":"E\nThe nominal exchange rate alone (how many units per US$) says nothing about which economy is 'best' or which currency is under/overvalued — that requires a PPP-style price comparison, not just the exchange-rate level."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Saturn Tide, a US energy firm, enters an agreement to exchange currency and execute the deal in eighteen months. What is Saturn Tide using to insure itself against foreign exchange risk?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) currency speculation"},{"t":"p","x":"B) carry trade"},{"t":"p","x":"C) hedging"},{"t":"p","x":"D) currency swap"},{"t":"p","x":"E) arbitrage"},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**5. FutureForm (US) imports microprocessors from Japan, owing ¥150,000 per unit at a spot rate of $1 = ¥110, reselling at $1,600 each once the funds from resale arrive. What happens if the rate after 30 days is $1 = ¥90?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Profit of approx. $236 per unit."},{"t":"p","x":"B) Profit of approx. $67 per unit."},{"t":"p","x":"C) Loss of approx. $236 per unit."},{"t":"p","x":"D) Loss of approx. $67 per unit."},{"t":"p","x":"E) Loss of approx. $90 per unit."},{"t":"a","x":"D\nFutureForm must actually pay ¥150,000 to the supplier once it has the resale cash in hand. At $1=¥90, that costs $150,000/90 = $1,666.67; reselling at $1,600 leaves a loss of about $1,666.67 − $1,600 ≈ $67 per microprocessor."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. The current market exchange rate for a currency — the price right now — is called:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) The forward rate"},{"t":"p","x":"B) The constant rate"},{"t":"p","x":"C) The current rate"},{"t":"p","x":"D) The spot rate"},{"t":"p","x":"E) The PPP rate"},{"t":"a","x":"D"},{"t":"p","x":"**7. The idea that currencies will naturally return to the rate at which equal value can be acquired in each country is called:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) currency swap rule"},{"t":"p","x":"B) anti-speculation agenda"},{"t":"p","x":"C) law of one price"},{"t":"p","x":"D) fisher law"},{"t":"p","x":"E) arbitrage equilibrium"},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**8. The yen/dollar rate quoted in London at 3:00pm is ¥115=$1; the rate quoted in New York at 10:00am (=3:00pm London time) is ¥135=$1. Rinaldo buys yen in New York and sells it in London. Rinaldo is engaging in**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) currency swapping."},{"t":"p","x":"B) currency speculation."},{"t":"p","x":"C) carry trade."},{"t":"p","x":"D) arbitrage."},{"t":"p","x":"E) hedging."},{"t":"a","x":"D"},{"t":"p","x":"**9. In countries where inflation is expected to be high, interest rates will also be high, because investors want compensation for the decline in the value of their money. This relationship is called the**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) purchasing power parity puzzle."},{"t":"p","x":"B) lead strategy."},{"t":"p","x":"C) Fisher effect."},{"t":"p","x":"D) bandwagon effect."},{"t":"p","x":"E) international Fisher effect."},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**10. A company's translation exposure is based on the**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) long-run effect of exchange-rate changes on future prices, sales and costs."},{"t":"p","x":"B) impact of currency exchange-rate changes on a company's reported financial statements."},{"t":"p","x":"C) extent to which future international earning power is affected by exchange-rate changes."},{"t":"p","x":"D) extent to which income from individual transactions is affected by FX fluctuations."},{"t":"p","x":"E) obligations for the purchase/sale of goods/services at previously agreed prices."},{"t":"a","x":"B"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 10"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A Hong Kong firm will receive USD 100,000 in three months from a US customer, and is worried the US dollar might weaken against the HKD before then (reducing what it receives in HKD terms). Which of the three FX risk types does this best illustrate, and what could the firm do about it?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Translation risk; the firm should simply restate its financial statements"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Transaction risk; the firm could lock in today's rate using a forward contract"},{"t":"p","x":"C) Economic risk; the firm should raise its prices immediately"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Translation risk; there is no way to manage this risk"},{"t":"p","x":"E) None of the FX risk types apply here"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA single future cash receipt exposed to exchange-rate movement between now and settlement is transaction risk, and the standard tool to remove it is a forward contract locking in today's exchange rate."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. The Big Mac costs the equivalent of $6.00 in Country Z but $5.00 in the US at current market exchange rates. According to Big Mac index / PPP logic, Country Z's currency is:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) undervalued"},{"t":"p","x":"B) overvalued"},{"t":"p","x":"C) at exact PPP equilibrium"},{"t":"p","x":"D) impossible to assess from this information"},{"t":"p","x":"E) pegged to the US dollar"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nIf the identical product costs MORE once converted to USD, the local currency is buying 'too much' in USD terms relative to the US price — it is overvalued relative to PPP, similar to the Swiss franc example in the 2019 Big Mac index article."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. Which of the following best explains why short-term exchange-rate forecasting is described in this course as unreliable even for professionals?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) exchange rates never actually move in the short term"},{"t":"p","x":"B) the efficient-market view holds that today's price already reflects available information, making short-term moves close to a coin flip"},{"t":"p","x":"C) PPP theory perfectly predicts hourly exchange-rate moves"},{"t":"p","x":"D) governments ban short-term currency trading everywhere"},{"t":"p","x":"E) technical analysis is proven to reliably beat the market"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nThis is the efficient-market argument (attributed to Alan Greenspan in the lecture) for why short-term FX forecasting is unreliable — a view the course explicitly favours in recommending against short-term speculation."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. A US Big Mac costs $5.50. In Country Q, a Big Mac costs 220 units of the local currency, and the actual market exchange rate is 50 units per USD. (a) What is the PPP-implied exchange rate? (b) Is Country Q's currency over- or under-valued, and by roughly how much?**"},{"t":"a","x":"• (a) PPP-implied rate = local price ÷ US price = 220 ÷ 5.50 = 40 units per USD.\n• (b) The ACTUAL market rate (50 units per USD) is weaker for the local currency than the PPP-implied rate (40 units per USD) — it takes MORE local currency to buy a dollar than PPP suggests it should — so Country Q's currency is undervalued.\n• Approximate degree of undervaluation = (50 − 40) / 50 ≈ 20% undervalued against the US dollar."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Explain, using the concept of translation risk, why a European company with large profitable operations in the UK could report a FALL in overall group profit (in euros) even though its UK business had an excellent year in pound-sterling terms.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Translation risk arises because the parent company must convert (translate) its foreign subsidiary's local-currency results into the parent's reporting currency for consolidated financial statements.\n• If the euro strengthens significantly against the pound during the year, the same (or even higher) pound-denominated UK profit converts into FEWER euros when translated — so consolidated group profit in euros can fall even though the underlying UK business performed well in its own currency.\n• This is a purely accounting/translation effect, distinct from transaction risk (which affects actual cash flows) and economic risk (which affects competitiveness/demand) — but it can still affect the parent's reported earnings, stock price and ability to raise capital."}]},"11":{"title":"The Global Monetary System","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"This chapter has three components: (1) the history and development of the global monetary system (the gold standard and its successors); (2) fixed vs. floating exchange-rate regimes (plus pegged systems/currency boards); and (3) the IMF's origins, purpose, and its role in major monetary crises. The instructor stresses that the central focus for revision is the fixed-vs-floating comparison — the historical detail mainly provides context."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"11.1 Fixed vs. Floating Exchange Rate Systems"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Fixed exchange rate system","Pros: discourages currency speculation; imposes monetary discipline on governments (they cannot print money freely without breaking the peg). Cons: removes a tool for correcting chronic trade imbalances via currency adjustment; removes national monetary-policy autonomy (interest rates cannot be set independently of defending the peg)."],["Floating exchange rate system","Pros: allows chronic trade imbalances to self-correct through currency movements; preserves national monetary-policy autonomy. Cons: exposed to currency speculation and volatility; provides less automatic fiscal/monetary discipline."]]},{"t":"p","x":"There is no single consensus on which system is superior overall — the right choice depends on a country's specific circumstances. This directly connects back to Chapter 9's discussion of the euro: for its members, the euro functions as an extreme, effectively-permanent fixed-exchange-rate arrangement, which delivered real early benefits (see the euro-area readings below) but also produced the structural vulnerabilities that surfaced in the Eurozone debt crisis when member economies' needs diverged."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"11.2 The Mundell-Fleming \"Impossible Trinity\" (Trilemma)"},{"t":"p","x":"A country can have, at most, TWO of the following three at the same time — never all three simultaneously:"},{"t":"li","x":"1. A fixed exchange rate"},{"t":"li","x":"2. Independent monetary policy"},{"t":"li","x":"3. Free (unrestricted) international capital flows"},{"t":"p","x":"This trilemma is a key organising concept: e.g. a country with a fixed exchange rate and free capital flows (like Hong Kong) must give up independent monetary policy (HK effectively imports US monetary policy via its peg); a country wanting both a fixed rate and independent monetary policy (like China, historically) must restrict free capital flows."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"11.3 Pegged Systems and Currency Boards — Hong Kong's Own System"},{"t":"p","x":"Hong Kong operates a currency board arrangement pegging the HKD to the USD — a STRONGER commitment mechanism than a simple, adjustable peg (the HKMA is obliged to exchange HKD for USD at the fixed rate on demand, backed 1:1 by USD reserves). Removing this peg would have major implications for Hong Kong's monetary autonomy, credibility, and the pricing of HK assets/trade — a natural essay topic given the course's Hong Kong setting (see the official Study Guide self-assessment question on exactly this point, and the illustrative reading on the HKD peg's 30-year history)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"11.4 The International Monetary Fund (IMF)"},{"t":"p","x":"Introduced already in Chapter 1 as a \"global institution\": created (at Bretton Woods) to maintain order in the global monetary system and act as lender of last resort to countries in balance-of-payments difficulty, typically attaching policy-reform conditions (\"conditionality\") to its loans."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"11.5 Course Connections and Historical Threads"},{"t":"p","x":"The Illustrative Real-World Readings section below carries several directly relevant threads for this chapter: the end of the gold standard, the Renminbi's managed currency-basket system, the 30-year history of the HKD peg, and the Eurozone's structural weaknesses 20 years after launch. Students should be able to explain WHY the euro is, in effect, an extreme/permanent fixed-exchange-rate arrangement among its members, and why that explains both its early stability benefits and its debt-crisis vulnerabilities."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 11"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Which of the following is a genuine ADVANTAGE of a fixed exchange rate system, per this chapter?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) it allows persistent trade imbalances to correct automatically"},{"t":"p","x":"B) it discourages currency speculation and imposes monetary discipline on governments"},{"t":"p","x":"C) it guarantees independent national monetary policy"},{"t":"p","x":"D) it eliminates the need for a central bank"},{"t":"p","x":"E) it always produces faster GDP growth than floating rates"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nDiscouraging speculation and forcing monetary discipline (since the government can't simply print money without breaking the peg) are the textbook's cited advantages of a fixed system."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. According to the Mundell-Fleming trilemma, a country that wants BOTH a fixed exchange rate AND full freedom of international capital flows must give up:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) its central bank entirely"},{"t":"p","x":"B) independent monetary policy"},{"t":"p","x":"C) all foreign trade"},{"t":"p","x":"D) its currency altogether"},{"t":"p","x":"E) its membership in the IMF"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nThe trilemma states a country can have at most two of (fixed rate, monetary autonomy, free capital flows) — choosing fixed rate + free capital flows means giving up independent monetary policy, exactly as Hong Kong effectively does under its USD peg."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. Hong Kong's currency board arrangement with the US dollar is best described as:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) a purely floating exchange rate"},{"t":"p","x":"B) a stronger, reserve-backed version of a fixed peg"},{"t":"p","x":"C) a form of portfolio investment"},{"t":"p","x":"D) a customs union"},{"t":"p","x":"E) an example of trade diversion"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA currency board is a stricter commitment than an ordinary adjustable peg: the HKMA must exchange HKD for USD on demand at the fixed rate, backed by full USD reserves."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Explain why removing Hong Kong's USD peg would create both a potential benefit and a potential risk, using the fixed-vs-floating framework from this chapter.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Potential benefit: floating would restore Hong Kong's monetary-policy autonomy, letting the HKMA set interest rates suited to Hong Kong's own economic cycle instead of importing US Federal Reserve policy via the peg, and would let the HKD exchange rate itself absorb/correct any persistent trade or capital-flow imbalances.\n• Potential risk: Hong Kong would lose the discipline and credibility benefits of the peg (which discourages currency speculation and anchors inflation expectations), and — given Hong Kong's very open capital account — would become directly exposed to currency speculation and volatility that the peg currently insulates it from."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Using the Mundell-Fleming trilemma, classify China's historical approach (fixed/managed exchange rate, capital controls, independent monetary policy) and explain which of the three trilemma elements China has traditionally sacrificed.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• China has historically combined (1) a managed/quasi-fixed exchange rate for the Renminbi with (2) an independent domestic monetary policy — the two elements the trilemma says CAN be combined only by giving up the third.\n• The element China has sacrificed is FREE (unrestricted) international capital flows: China has maintained significant capital controls, which is precisely what allows it to keep both a managed exchange rate and independent monetary policy without the trilemma's contradictions forcing a choice."}]}},"extras":[{"t":"p","x":"**BUS2108**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Global Business Environment**"},{"t":"p","x":"Complete Revision Synthesis & Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"Lingnan University — Department of Marketing and International Business"},{"t":"p","x":"Instructor: Prof. Hilda Keung  •  Textbook: Charles Hill, Global Business Today (Evergreen release), McGraw-Hill"},{"t":"p","x":"Compiled from all course materials shared on Moodle (lecture slides, study guides, official quizzes and readings), synthesised into structured revision notes with original complementary practice questions."},{"t":"p","x":"Prepared for personal exam revision — not an official course document."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Table of Contents"},{"t":"p","x":"(Right-click this area in Word and choose \"Update Field\" to generate/refresh the table of contents.)"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Course Overview"},{"t":"p","x":"This section gathers all course-administration material from Moodle: class rules, the participation/gaming warm-up exercise, and the official course outline (Section 4, Wednesday 1:00 p.m., Room LBY G06, Semester 1, 2026-27)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Course Description & Learning Outcomes"},{"t":"p","x":"BUS2108 helps students understand the international business world and the importance of international business to their careers. The course has five parts, each mapped to chapters of the textbook covered later in this document:"},{"t":"li","x":"Part 1 — defines globalization, its drivers, and debates its merits/drawbacks (Chapter 1)"},{"t":"li","x":"Part 2 — national differences in political economy, culture and ethics, and their implications for ethical decision-making (Chapters 2-5)"},{"t":"li","x":"Part 3 — international trade theory and the trade/investment environment (Chapters 6-9)"},{"t":"li","x":"Part 4 — foreign exchange background and the global monetary system (Chapters 10-11)"},{"t":"li","x":"Part 5 — market entry strategy: which markets to enter, and how, when and on what scale (later in the course; only lightly touched in the extracted materials)"},{"t":"p","x":"**On completion, students should be able to:**"},{"t":"li","x":"Understand globalization's impact on jobs, income, labour policy, growth, environment and sovereignty"},{"t":"li","x":"Assess country attractiveness, corruption risk, IP issues and ethical dilemmas across cultures"},{"t":"li","x":"Apply trade/FDI theory and understand government influence on these activities"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain fixed vs floating exchange-rate theories and their relative merits"},{"t":"li","x":"Formulate an international market-entry strategy (which markets, how, when, what scale)"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Assessment Structure"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Component","Weight"],["Attendance & Participation","10%"],["Individual In-class Assessment (Weighted Country Attractiveness Matrix exercise)","10%"],["Mid-term Test (45 MCQs, closed book, covers Chapters 1–6)","15%"],["Group Project & Presentation","25%"],["Continuous Assessment subtotal","60%"],["Final Examination (essay format, covers entire course)","40%"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Note: Lecture 1's slides mention participation as \"5% of the total assessment\", while the official course outline lists Attendance & Participation together at 10%. The course outline is the authoritative document for grading weights — please verify the current split with the instructor if in doubt."},{"t":"p","x":"**Key dates:**"},{"t":"li","x":"In-class individual assessment (WCAM exercise): Wednesday 16 September 2026"},{"t":"li","x":"Mid-term test: covers Chapters 1–6, 45 MCQs, closed book, at MBG22. The outline lists it twice — Thursday 22 October, 8:00–9:00 p.m. (evening) and Wednesday 28 October, 8:00–9:00 a.m. (morning)."},{"t":"li","x":"Group presentations: Wednesday 18 November 2026 (PPT uploaded to Turnitin before 11:00 a.m. that day)"},{"t":"li","x":"Group written report due: Sunday 22 November 2026, 5:00 p.m. (via Turnitin, ~4,000 words)"},{"t":"p","x":"Note: There appear to be two different dates/times listed for the mid-term in the source outline (22 Oct evening vs 28 Oct morning) — please confirm which single sitting applies to Section 4 with the instructor."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Weekly Schedule (Section 4 — Wednesday)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date","Topic","In-class activity","Ch."],["2/9","Course Introduction; The Meaning of Globalization","—","1"],["9/9","National Differences in Political, Economic & Legal Systems (per Study Guide numbering)","Group formation; Globalization Debate; group country/industry selection","2"],["16/9","National Differences in Economic Development","In-class individual assessment: Weighted Country Attractiveness Matrix (WCAM)","3"],["23/9","Differences in Culture","Culture group exercise (Hofstede application)","4"],["30/9","Ethics, CSR & Sustainability","ICAC visit — avoiding corruption in HK and overseas","5"],["7/10","International Trade Theory","New Trade Theory discussion","6"],["14/10","Government Policy and International Trade","Trade wars and impact on international business","7"],["21/10","NO CLASS — mid-term preparation","—","—"],["22 or 28/10","MID-TERM TEST (Chapters 1–6)","—","—"],["28/10","Foreign Direct Investment","Investment proposals discussion (Taiwan FDI case)","8"],["4/11","Regional Economic Integration","Project preparation — researching the global business environment","9"],["11/11","The Foreign Exchange Market","Foreign exchange exercises","10"],["18/11","GROUP PRESENTATIONS","—","—"],["22/11","Group written report due (5:00 p.m.)","—","—"],["25/11","Entering Developed & Emerging Markets; End-of-course review","Return of in-class assessments; classes finish","(12/13)"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Note: The Study Guides used as the backbone of this document number chapters as: Ch.2 = Political/Economic/Legal Systems, Ch.3 = National Differences in Economic Development — the reverse of one row in the course outline table above (which lists 9/9 as \"Economic Development\" = Ch.2). This document follows the Study Guide / textbook numbering consistently throughout, since the Study Guides and the official quizzes both use it."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Class Rules & Protocol"},{"t":"li","x":"Breaks of ~10–15 minutes during each class; classes finish around xx:15–xx:20"},{"t":"li","x":"Phones on vibrate only; step outside to take calls and return quietly"},{"t":"li","x":"Attendance taken every class; minimum 70% attendance required to be allowed to sit the final exam"},{"t":"li","x":"Persistent lateness/absence affects the participation grade; medical/legitimate absences need documentation"},{"t":"li","x":"Generative AI (ChatGPT, etc.) is NOT recommended for assignments; declaration forms required for individual and group work; detected AI use can mean a zero score and referral to the disciplinary committee"},{"t":"li","x":"Plagiarism is strictly prohibited per university regulations; a mandatory online \"Plagiarism Awareness\" tutorial is available; written work must be submitted via Turnitin"},{"t":"li","x":"Tips for doing well: develop a \"business-to-business\" sense; follow current world news; use reliable, evidence-based sources; apply theories/concepts rather than memorising definitions; do not choose China as one of the two comparison countries for the group project; complete every assessment component — missing even one component is very likely to result in failing the course"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Participation / Gaming Warm-Up Exercise"},{"t":"p","x":"The Lesson 1 \"warm-up\" participation activity used the gaming/metaverse industry as a live case of the global business environment, counting toward the participation grade. Students read an EY report, \"What's possible for the gaming industry in the next dimension?\" (Gaming Industry Survey 2022), and answered: \"Could you please share your opinion whether you support a favourable global business environment for the gaming industry in the near future? Explain your reasons.\""},{"t":"p","x":"**Key facts from the EY report (useful supporting evidence for that discussion):**"},{"t":"li","x":"Global gaming revenue exceeded $193 billion in 2021 (2.9 billion players worldwide), growing at a 15.6% CAGR from 2016–2021; forecast to reach $211 billion by 2025 (mobile gaming: $116bn)."},{"t":"li","x":"Key drivers: faster processors/graphics, new hardware, rising global internet access, and the rollout of 5G (enabling cloud gaming and VR/AR)."},{"t":"li","x":"Competitive pressure is seen as healthy: 72% of surveyed executives called increased competition an opportunity; 83% said the industry is under constant pressure to innovate. M&A activity was large ($36.3bn in deals in 2021, incl. Microsoft's proposed $68.7bn Activision Blizzard acquisition) but most executives prioritised organic growth (R&D, new tech) over M&A."},{"t":"li","x":"Data & cyber-security risk is a rising concern: 47% cite cyber risk as a key challenge today, rising to 58% looking three years out."},{"t":"li","x":"The metaverse is seen as gaming's natural next stage (97% of respondents believe gaming is central to the metaverse); NFTs and \"play-to-earn\" models are an emerging but still-unproven revenue source (90% of executives say they lack a viable business model to monetise digital assets yet)."},{"t":"li","x":"Recommended strategic responses: develop a clear metaverse vision, update the operating model (incl. new talent/skills), and design the customer experience around what users want to do in the metaverse (not simply port \"reality\" into it)."},{"t":"p","x":"This exercise links directly into the Chapter 1 globalization debate (see below): the gaming industry is a clear case of the globalization of markets (converging tastes for gaming content worldwide) and, through global supply chains and remote/distributed development teams, the globalization of production."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Group Project Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"Your team works as management consultants for a Hong Kong manufacturing company (40% of sales in Asia, 30% North America, 30% Europe) that currently has one HK factory and wants to build a larger, more modern plant (~1,500 workers) abroad, exporting worldwide (not for the local market). You must recommend which of two candidate countries is the better location."},{"t":"p","x":"**Factors to evaluate for each candidate country:**"},{"t":"li","x":"Wage levels and other economic background"},{"t":"li","x":"Tax rates and investment incentives"},{"t":"li","x":"Availability of appropriately skilled labour"},{"t":"li","x":"Natural factor conditions, infrastructure, and supporting industries"},{"t":"li","x":"Legal environment (labour law, environmental law, etc.)"},{"t":"li","x":"Membership of regional trade agreements and other trade considerations"},{"t":"li","x":"Cultural considerations / ability of the HK management team to work with local staff"},{"t":"li","x":"Political risk"},{"t":"p","x":"Deliverables: a 20-minute group presentation and a ~4,000-word written report, with a clear final recommendation and justification. Do not use China as one of your two comparison countries."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Illustrative Real-World Readings"},{"t":"p","x":"The Moodle materials include a batch of OCR'd newspaper and magazine clippings (mostly from The Economist and the Wall Street Journal, plus one Newsweek and one Financial Times piece) used in class as real-world illustrations of the currency, trade and globalization concepts covered above — chiefly for Chapters 1, 9, 10 and 11. Rather than reproducing the full text of each article (which would make this document unwieldy), each is summarised below with its relevance to the course. The full original OCR'd text of every article remains available in the source files on Moodle / in the extracted materials folder if you want to re-read one in full."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. \"Stronger Euro Good News for Hong Kong Traders\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"South China Morning Post-style report, early 2000s (\"03-04\")"},{"t":"p","x":"Reports that a firmer euro reduced the pressure Hong Kong/China exporters (priced in USD via the HKD peg) faced from European buyers to switch to euro pricing, and gave manufacturers a margin cushion — though the article notes export VOLUMES to Europe were not expected to rise much while the German/French economies stayed weak. A direct, concrete illustration of transaction/economic FX risk for Hong Kong exporters (Chapter 10) and of the HKD's USD-peg mechanics (Chapter 11)."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. \"Making Money on Money\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"Newsweek, 15 March 2004"},{"t":"p","x":"Describes the 2000s boom in retail/individual forex (\"day-trading\") speculation, enabled by high leverage (up to 100:1) and made accessible by new online platforms. Highlights the serious risk of this activity (the U.S. CFTC pursued 57 enforcement cases involving $250m in consumer losses) and quotes Fed Chair Alan Greenspan's view that no model beats a coin flip at predicting currency moves. Directly supports Chapter 10's conclusion that short-term FX speculation is not recommended for non-specialists."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. \"HK Central Banker Says Currency Union Far Off in Asia\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"Asian Wall Street Journal, 22 March 2005"},{"t":"p","x":"HKMA chief Joseph Yam argues an Asian monetary union (modelled on the euro) remains far off, citing much lower real economic convergence among Asian economies than existed in pre-euro Europe, and much greater political diversity. Useful counterpoint for Chapter 9's discussion of common-currency costs/benefits and \"optimal currency areas\" applied to Asia."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. \"Nought to Worry About\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 28 August 2004"},{"t":"p","x":"Covers a wave of currency redenominations (stripping zeroes from inflated currencies) in Turkey, Romania, Bulgaria and Afghanistan, driven by past high inflation. A light illustration of how chronic inflation erodes a currency's practical usability — reinforcing the Chapter 10/11 link between inflation and currency valuation."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. \"Japan's Currency Wonupmanship\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 3 March 2012"},{"t":"p","x":"Explains how a weakening yen against the South Korean won helped Korean firms (Samsung, Hyundai) undercut Japanese rivals (Sony, Honda, and DRAM-chip maker Elpida, which went bankrupt) on price after the 2008 financial crisis. A concrete case of exchange-rate movements directly shaping international competitiveness (Chapter 10's \"economic risk\")."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. \"Currencies' Fair Value Open to Debate\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"Wall Street Journal, 25 February 2013"},{"t":"p","x":"Compares several models (PPP/\"Starbucks latte index\", the IMF's own models, capital-flow-based models) for valuing currencies, showing they often disagree — the euro, yen and Swedish krona are each judged over/undervalued differently by different models. A good companion/contrast to the Big Mac Index for Chapter 10, showing PPP is one tool among several, each imperfect."},{"t":"p","x":"**7. \"World Trade: The Other Conclave\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 16 March 2013"},{"t":"p","x":"Reports on the WTO's stalled Doha Round and the parallel boom in regional/bilateral deals (TPP, TTIP, RCEP-era predecessors) as major economies pursue \"pragmatic\" regional liberalisation while multilateral talks stagnate. Directly illustrates Chapter 7's account of WTO difficulties and Chapter 9's rationale for regional integration."},{"t":"p","x":"**8. \"Trading Places\" (Banyan column)**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 27 January 2018"},{"t":"p","x":"Explains why free trade remains popular in Asia even as the U.S. under President Trump turned protectionist — covering the revival of the TPP as the CPTPP after the U.S. withdrew, and arguing that at Asia's stage of development, trade mostly creates winners (unlike in the West, where it visibly creates concentrated losers too). Reinforces Chapter 1's globalization debate and Chapter 9's regional-integration material."},{"t":"p","x":"**9. \"Watch Your BMI\" (\"Burgernomics\")**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 14 July 2018"},{"t":"p","x":"A Big Mac Index update showing every tracked currency had moved in value; highlights the British pound as ~23% undervalued post-Brexit and notes the U.S. dollar strengthened against nearly every currency as the Fed raised rates. A worked, real-data example of the Big Mac Index / PPP theory for Chapter 10."},{"t":"p","x":"**10. \"The Euro at 20: Undercooked Union\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 5 January 2019"},{"t":"p","x":"A major retrospective on the euro's first 20 years: it survived the 2009-12 debt crisis but remains structurally incomplete — insufficient banking/fiscal integration, low labour mobility, and persistent north-south imbalances (Germany's surpluses vs. Greek/Italian/Portuguese/Spanish debt). Directly supports Chapters 9 and 11's discussion of common-currency costs, optimal currency areas, and the Eurozone crisis."},{"t":"p","x":"**11. \"Burgernomics: Pick of the Menu\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 12 January 2019"},{"t":"p","x":"Shows the U.S. dollar near a 30-year high on the Big Mac Index basis — almost every currency (rich and emerging) looked undervalued against it — and notes that Big-Mac-undervalued currencies have historically tended to appreciate over the following decade, on average. A strong data-driven Chapter 10 companion piece to the theory."},{"t":"p","x":"**12. \"The Meaning of Seven\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 10 August 2019"},{"t":"p","x":"Explains why the Chinese yuan crossing 7-per-dollar in August 2019 triggered a global market sell-off and a formal U.S. Treasury currency-manipulation accusation against China — even though 7.01 vs. 6.99 is a trivial economic difference — because markets had assigned huge symbolic importance to that round-number threshold amid the US-China trade war. Useful for Chapters 7 (trade war/tariffs) and 10 (FX market psychology, \"bandwagon effect\")."},{"t":"p","x":"**13. \"McJobbers and Brokers\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 19 September 2020"},{"t":"p","x":"A rigorous back-test of a naive \"buy the most Big-Mac-undervalued currency\" trading strategy: it LOST money in 10 of 15 completed two-year bets on the standard index, though a GDP-adjusted version of the index fared better. Directly nuances Chapter 10's PPP/Big Mac Index material — a good example of why short-term application of a sound long-run theory can still fail."},{"t":"p","x":"**14. \"Asia-Pacific Nations Sign Historic Accord in 'Victory' for Free Trade\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"Financial Times, 16 November 2020"},{"t":"p","x":"News report on the signing of RCEP (Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership) by 15 Asia-Pacific countries — the first trade deal linking China, Japan and South Korea, covering ~30% of world population/output, expected to add ~$200bn to global GDP by 2030. Directly supports Chapter 9's regional-integration material."},{"t":"p","x":"**15. \"RCEP: Big Deal\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 21 November 2020"},{"t":"p","x":"A more analytical companion to the FT article above: explains RCEP's main practical benefit (a single set of \"rules of origin\" replacing ASEAN's patchwork of separate bilateral deals), notes it is shallower than the TPP/CPTPP (no labour/SOE/environmental disciplines), and flags that India's absence leaves China with outsized influence over the bloc's future rule-setting."},{"t":"p","x":"**16. \"The Big Mac Index: The Happiest Meal\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 24 July 2021"},{"t":"p","x":"A further Big Mac Index update, highlighting Vietnam, Switzerland and Taiwan as flagged by the U.S. Treasury for potentially unfair currency practices, and Lebanon as the cheapest Big Mac in the world amid a currency/economic collapse. Reinforces the PPP/Big Mac Index material and shows real-world currency-manipulation disputes in action (linking Chapters 1, 10 and 11)."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Exam-Style Review Checklist (Chapters 1–11)"},{"t":"p","x":"Use this checklist as a final pass before the mid-term (Chapters 1–6) and the final exam (whole course). For each item, you should be able to explain it in your own words AND apply it to a concrete country/company example — the instructor repeatedly stresses \"application of theories and concepts, no memorization.\""},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 1 — Globalization"},{"t":"li","x":"Define globalization and distinguish the globalization of markets from the globalization of production"},{"t":"li","x":"Name and describe the role of the five global institutions (WTO, IMF, World Bank, UN, G20)"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the two macro drivers of globalization (declining barriers; technological change) and give an example of each"},{"t":"li","x":"Give at least 3 arguments FOR and 3 arguments AGAINST globalization, from different stakeholder perspectives"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 2 — Political, Economic & Legal Systems"},{"t":"li","x":"Place a named country on the collectivism↔individualism and democracy↔totalitarianism spectra"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish common law, civil law and theocratic law, and their effect on contract length/detail"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how corruption and weak property rights affect FDI, trade and growth"},{"t":"li","x":"Define patents, copyrights and trademarks"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 3 — National Differences in Economic Development"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain GDP vs. GNI vs. PPP-adjusted GDP, with a worked numeric example"},{"t":"li","x":"List the \"three I's\" of growth (Incentives, Institutions, Innovation) and connect each to political-economy choices"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain why the democracy–growth relationship is contested, with an example on each side"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain why growth RATE (not just level) matters for country attractiveness"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 4 — Differences in Culture"},{"t":"li","x":"Define culture, values, norms (folkways vs. mores)"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain all 6 Hofstede dimensions, with a country example for each"},{"t":"li","x":"State at least 2 criticisms of Hofstede's methodology"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish cultural relativism from cultural universalism with a business example"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain \"cultural distance\" and its effect on market-entry mode"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 5 — Ethics, CSR, Sustainability & Supply Chain"},{"t":"li","x":"List the four common ethical issue areas (employment practices, human rights, environmental pollution, corruption)"},{"t":"li","x":"List the 7 managerial actions for ethical decision-making, and the 3-question ethical test"},{"t":"li","x":"Define CSR (Dahlsrud/Beal) and sustainability (UN definition), with a company example"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain JIT inventory's core benefit and core risk"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish outsourcing / insourcing / offshoring / offshore outsourcing / nearshoring / co-sourcing"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 6 — International Trade Theory"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain mercantilism and why it's now considered flawed"},{"t":"li","x":"State absolute advantage (Smith) and comparative advantage (Ricardo), and why comparative advantage matters even with no absolute advantage"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the Leontief paradox and why it challenged Heckscher-Ohlin"},{"t":"li","x":"State the four elements of Porter's Diamond and apply them to a real country/industry"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain what New Trade Theory implies for government policy"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 7 — Government Policy and International Trade"},{"t":"li","x":"List the 6 instruments of trade policy (tariffs, subsidies, quotas, local content requirements, administrative policies, antidumping) with a one-line definition of each"},{"t":"li","x":"List the political AND economic reasons governments intervene in trade, and identify the single MOST common real-world reason"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain trade creation vs. this chapter's protectionism history (Smoot-Hawley → GATT → WTO) in sequence"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 8 — Foreign Direct Investment"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish FDI from portfolio investment (with the 10% threshold)"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish FDI flow, FDI stock, and production realised from FDI"},{"t":"li","x":"List the 3 types of outward FDI (Greenfield, Acquisition, Joint venture) with a trade-off for each"},{"t":"li","x":"State Dunning's OLI theory's three conditions and apply them to a real company"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish free-market, radical, and pragmatic-nationalism views of FDI, and list the 5 pragmatic-nationalism evaluation criteria"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 9 — Regional Economic Integration"},{"t":"li","x":"List the 5 levels of integration in order (FTA → customs union → common market → economic union → political union) and what each ADDS"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish trade creation from trade diversion, and state the rule for when integration benefits the world economy"},{"t":"li","x":"List one benefit and one cost of a common regional currency, and define \"optimal currency area\""},{"t":"li","x":"Name the major Asian regional agreements (ASEAN, APEC, RCEP, CPTPP) at a high level only — no need for historical detail"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 10 — The Foreign Exchange Market"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish transaction, translation and economic FX risk with an example of each"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish spot rate from forward rate, and explain how a forward contract hedges risk"},{"t":"li","x":"State the Law of One Price / PPP theory and apply it to a Big Mac-style numeric example"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the International Fisher Effect (inflation/interest-rate link to exchange rates)"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain why short-term FX forecasting is unreliable (efficient market view) and what fundamental vs. technical analysis are"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 11 — The Global Monetary System"},{"t":"li","x":"Compare fixed vs. floating exchange rate systems: 2 pros and 2 cons of each"},{"t":"li","x":"State the Mundell-Fleming trilemma and classify a real country's choice of two-out-of-three"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain Hong Kong's currency board / USD peg and what removing it would imply"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the IMF's core role and link it back to Chapter 1"},{"t":"li","x":"Connect the euro (Ch.9) to this chapter's fixed-vs-floating framework"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Mixed Practice Set — All Chapters (with Full Answer Key)"},{"t":"p","x":"A final, cumulative practice set mixing chapters, in the style of the final examination's essay/application format alongside MCQs. Attempt every question before checking the answer key."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. [Ch.1/6] A firm sources semiconductors from Taiwan (best factor conditions for chip fabrication), assembles phones in Vietnam (lower labour costs), and sells worldwide via converging consumer tastes for smartphones. Which TWO concepts together best describe this?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Mercantilism and the gold standard"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Globalization of production and globalization of markets"},{"t":"p","x":"C) Trade diversion and an optimal currency area"},{"t":"p","x":"D) The Leontief paradox and cultural relativism"},{"t":"p","x":"E) A customs union and a common market"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nSourcing components/assembly from wherever offers the best cost/quality (globalization of production) while selling into a converging worldwide consumer market (globalization of markets) are the two Chapter 1 concepts this describes."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. [Ch.2/3] A country has a civil law system, strong property-rights enforcement, and a free-market economic system. According to the course framework, this country is most likely to:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) have low FDI and low growth"},{"t":"p","x":"B) be an attractive destination for international business, though possibly a mature/competitive market"},{"t":"p","x":"C) automatically become a totalitarian state"},{"t":"p","x":"D) have no legal system at all"},{"t":"p","x":"E) be immune to political risk"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nStrong property rights + free-market economics + a functioning legal system are the classic conditions for an attractive investment/trade destination, though (Chapter 3) such countries are often already developed/competitive, limiting first-mover growth upside."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. [Ch.4] A Hong Kong manager negotiating with a supplier in a high-power-distance, high-collectivism country should most likely expect:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the supplier's junior staff to openly challenge their manager's decisions in the meeting"},{"t":"p","x":"B) decisions to be made by senior figures, with a preference for maintaining group harmony over direct public disagreement"},{"t":"p","x":"C) no hierarchy at all in the supplier organisation"},{"t":"p","x":"D) supplier staff to prioritise individual bonuses over group loyalty"},{"t":"p","x":"E) the supplier to insist on a fully detailed common-law-style contract"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nHigh power distance implies decisions flow from senior figures; high collectivism implies a strong preference for group harmony and avoiding direct public disagreement — together, exactly this expectation."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. [Ch.5] Which of the following actions is NOT one of the seven managerial actions for ethical decision-making covered in class?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) hiring/promoting people with strong personal ethics"},{"t":"p","x":"B) installing ethics officers"},{"t":"p","x":"C) developing moral courage"},{"t":"p","x":"D) maximising short-term profit regardless of stakeholder impact"},{"t":"p","x":"E) making CSR a policy cornerstone"},{"t":"a","x":"D\nThe seven actions are all about embedding ethics into hiring, culture, decision processes, ethics officers, moral courage, CSR and sustainability — maximising short-term profit regardless of stakeholders is the opposite of this framework."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. [Ch.7] A government subsidises its domestic solar panel makers so they can sell below cost abroad and capture global market share. If detected, the importing country could legitimately respond with:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) a Voluntary Export Restraint"},{"t":"p","x":"B) an antidumping duty"},{"t":"p","x":"C) a currency board"},{"t":"p","x":"D) a common market agreement"},{"t":"p","x":"E) an FDI ban only"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nSelling below the cost of production abroad (often subsidy-enabled) is the definition of dumping, and antidumping duties are the legitimate policy response — though as noted in Chapter 7, this tool is sometimes misused."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. [Ch.8/9] A German automaker builds a wholly-new factory in a Central European country that is also an EU member, benefiting from free movement of capital and parts across EU borders. Which TWO concepts does this best combine?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Greenfield FDI and Common Market-level regional integration"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Portfolio investment and a Free Trade Area"},{"t":"p","x":"C) Trade diversion and mercantilism"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Acquisition FDI and a Political Union"},{"t":"p","x":"E) Joint venture FDI and an optimal currency area"},{"t":"a","x":"A\nBuilding an entirely new plant from scratch is Greenfield FDI; benefiting from free movement of capital (and, within the EU, goods/labour) across member borders is a Common Market-level (or deeper) feature of regional integration."},{"t":"p","x":"**7. [Ch.10/11] A country's currency is pegged to the US dollar, and the country also allows fully free cross-border capital flows. Per the Mundell-Fleming trilemma, this country has necessarily given up:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) all foreign trade"},{"t":"p","x":"B) independent monetary policy"},{"t":"p","x":"C) its currency entirely"},{"t":"p","x":"D) WTO membership"},{"t":"p","x":"E) the ability to hedge with forward contracts"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nFixed rate + free capital flows means the third leg (independent monetary policy) must be sacrificed, exactly as the trilemma predicts (and as Hong Kong's own system illustrates)."},{"t":"p","x":"**8. [Ch.9/11] The main reason often given for the euro's structural stress during the 2009-12 debt crisis is that:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the Eurozone was not, in practice, an optimal currency area, so a single monetary policy could not suit members with very different needs"},{"t":"p","x":"B) the euro was a floating exchange-rate system"},{"t":"p","x":"C) the WTO banned the euro during the crisis"},{"t":"p","x":"D) trade creation exceeded trade diversion"},{"t":"p","x":"E) Hofstede's cultural dimensions predicted the crisis"},{"t":"a","x":"A\nThe course repeatedly links the euro crisis to the Eurozone failing to function as a true optimal currency area — e.g. Greece needing higher rates while Germany needed lower ones, a problem a single, shared monetary policy cannot solve for both simultaneously."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Mixed Set — Short-Answer / Case Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**9. A Hong Kong manufacturer is deciding between Vietnam and Poland as the site for a new export-oriented factory (per the group project brief). Name ONE relevant concept from each of three DIFFERENT chapters covered in this document that should inform the decision, and briefly explain why each matters.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Chapter 4 (Culture): Hofstede's cultural-distance framework — comparing Hong Kong's scores against Vietnam's and Poland's on all six dimensions reveals how much management-style adaptation will be needed in each location.\n• Chapter 6 (Trade Theory) / Porter's Diamond: assessing each country's factor conditions, related/supporting industries, and domestic rivalry indicates which location has a stronger underlying competitive base for manufacturing.\n• Chapter 9 (Regional Integration): Poland's EU membership gives tariff-free access to the whole EU common market, while Vietnam's membership of agreements like RCEP/CPTPP gives different regional access — this materially affects where finished goods can be shipped tariff-free.\n• (Any three well-justified, chapter-grounded concepts are acceptable — political/legal risk (Ch.2), economic development/growth rate (Ch.3), and FDI entry-mode choice (Ch.8) are equally valid alternatives.)"},{"t":"p","x":"**10. Using the Big Mac Index / PPP theory (Chapter 10) AND the concept of an optimal currency area (Chapter 9), explain why a country might resist joining a currency union even if doing so would reduce its transaction costs with its main trading partners.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• PPP/Big Mac Index logic shows that a country's currency may currently be systematically under- or over-valued relative to its trading partners' — joining a currency union would lock in a SINGLE conversion rate permanently, removing the ability to let the exchange rate adjust toward its \"fair\" PPP value over time.\n• If the country's economy does not move in sync with the currency union's other members (i.e. it is not part of a genuine optimal currency area), a single shared monetary policy may be poorly suited to its specific needs during an economic shock — exactly the problem that produced the Eurozone debt crisis for Greece and other southern European members.\n• The reduced transaction-cost benefit must therefore be weighed against the loss of monetary-policy autonomy and exchange-rate flexibility — which is precisely why countries like the UK, Denmark and Sweden chose to remain outside the euro despite the EU's common market benefits."},{"t":"p","x":"**11. A multinational is criticised in the press for both (a) using contract manufacturers in a country with weak labour law enforcement, and (b) contributing to environmental damage via its supply chain. Using material from Chapters 5 and 7, outline TWO concrete actions the firm could take, and explain the reasoning behind each.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Action 1 (ethical decision-making process): embed supplier labour standards into sourcing decisions — e.g. requiring supplier audits against the firm's own code of conduct, going beyond the host country's legal minimum, directly following the Chapter 5 principle that ethics is not defined solely by local legality.\n• Action 2 (sustainability strategy): adopt a formal sustainable-sourcing policy addressing the \"tragedy of the commons\" risk in its supply chain (e.g. commitments on emissions, waste, and fair supplier compensation) — both because it reduces real environmental harm and because, per Chapter 5, unaddressed ethical issues are now quickly exposed via the internet/independent media, creating reputational and (per Chapter 7's national-security/consumer-protection logic) potential regulatory risk in the firm's home and host markets alike."}]},"BUS2205":{"file":"BUS2205_Marketing_Management_Synthese_et_Exercices.docx","mtime":"2026-10-11","lessons":{"1":{"title":"Creating Customer Relationships and Value through Marketing","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Week 1 · Learning outcome: Define what marketing is and discuss its role in society; understand the importance of creating value and managing profitable customer relationships."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Define marketing and identify the diverse factors that influence marketing actions."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain how marketing discovers and satisfies consumer needs."},{"t":"p","x":"• Distinguish between marketing mix factors and environmental forces."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain how organizations build strong customer relationships and customer value through marketing."},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe the characteristics of a market orientation."},{"t":"p","x":"Opening case: Bombas, marketing with a mission. \"One purchased = one donated\"; socks are the most requested item at shelters. Bombas has sales of $250 million and has donated more than 100 million articles of clothing. It builds a brand with a purpose, creates better socks, expanded distribution and holds B-Corp certification."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. What Is Marketing?"},{"t":"p","x":"You are already a \"marketing expert\" in the sense that you make thousands of buying decisions and some selling decisions — but doing marketing well is not easy: it affects individuals, organizations, industries, and countries alike."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AMA definition"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Marketing (AMA definition)\n• \"Marketing is the activity, set of institutions, and processes for creating, communicating, delivering, and exchanging offerings that have value for customers, clients, partners, and society at large.\""]]},{"t":"p","x":"Two core objectives sit behind this definition:"},{"t":"li","x":"Discover the needs and wants of customers."},{"t":"li","x":"Satisfy those needs and wants."},{"t":"p","x":"The mechanism that achieves both is exchange."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Exchange"},{"t":"p","x":"Exchange is the trade of things of value between a buyer and a seller so that each is better off after the trade. For marketing (exchange) to occur, four conditions must hold:"},{"t":"li","x":"Two or more parties with unsatisfied needs."},{"t":"li","x":"A desire and ability on their part to have those needs satisfied."},{"t":"li","x":"A way for the parties to communicate."},{"t":"li","x":"Something to exchange."},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing and your career: marketing means delivering value to customers; careers exist in large organizations and in small businesses, and you could start a successful business yourself. The organization (its mission and objectives, management goals, and other departments) and society (customers, owners, suppliers, other organizations) as well as environmental forces all shape marketing actions (Figure 1-2)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. How Marketing Discovers Consumer Needs"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Needs vs. wants"},{"t":"li","x":"**Needs **— necessities: basic requirements a person must satisfy to survive (food, shelter, safety)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Wants **— the form a need takes as shaped by culture and personality (e.g., \"I need food\" becomes \"I want sushi\")."},{"t":"p","x":"A market consists of people with both the desire and the ability to buy a specific offering. Because consumers often cannot articulate what they need, firms use crowdsourcing and innovation tournaments to surface ideas. The challenge is real: it takes roughly 3,000 raw ideas to generate one commercial success, and about 40% of new products still fail. The remedy is to focus relentlessly on the consumer benefit and to learn from past product failures."},{"t":"p","x":"Consumers may not know, or cannot describe, what they need or want. The challenge is meeting needs with new products: it takes about 3,000 raw ideas to generate one commercial success, and 40 percent of new products fail. Focus on the consumer benefit and learn from past mistakes. Class discussion: benefits and showstoppers of Ray-Ban smart glasses, no-sugar soda (Zevia) and a clothing subscription service (Stitch Fix). Question to debate: does marketing persuade people to buy the \"wrong\" things?"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. How Marketing Satisfies Consumer Needs: The Marketing Mix and Environmental Forces"},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing programs are built from two very different sets of factors — one the firm controls, one it does not."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Four P's (controllable marketing mix)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Element","What it covers"],["Product","The good, service, or idea offered — including features, quality, branding, packaging."],["Price","What is exchanged for the product — list price, discounts, payment terms."],["Promotion","Communication between buyer and seller — advertising, personal selling, sales promotion, PR, social media."],["Place","How the product gets to the consumer — distribution channels, retailers, logistics."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Uncontrollable environmental forces"},{"t":"p","x":"These sit largely outside marketing's control and can act as accelerators or brakes on a marketing program:"},{"t":"li","x":"Social forces (demographics, culture)"},{"t":"li","x":"Economic forces (macroeconomic conditions, consumer income)"},{"t":"li","x":"Technological forces (innovation, AI, e-commerce)"},{"t":"li","x":"Competitive forces (structure of competition, rival actions)"},{"t":"li","x":"Regulatory forces (laws and regulations)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Exam-ready distinction\n• Marketing mix (4Ps) = controllable — the firm decides Product, Price, Promotion, Place.\n• Environmental forces = uncontrollable — Social, Economic, Technological, Competitive, Regulatory (mnemonic: \"SET-CR\").\n• A classic exam trap is to list an environmental force as if it were a 4P element, or vice versa — always ask \"can the firm directly set this?\""]]},{"t":"p","x":"The 3M Post-it® Flag case (slides) shows both elements working together: 3M used the SAME core technology but built two different marketing programs (product, price, promotion, place) for two segments — college students and office workers — because their needs differed."},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing program: a plan that integrates the marketing mix to provide a good, service or idea to prospective buyers. Marketing focuses on the needs of the target market, one or more specific groups of potential consumers toward which an organization directs its marketing program."},{"t":"p","x":"Figure 1-4, 3M Post-it Flag launch: two products aimed at two segments."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Marketing mix element","College students (Flag Highlighter)","Office workers (Flag Pen)"],["Product","Flag Highlighter to help students study","Flag Pen to help office workers in day-to-day work"],["Price","About $2.99 to $3.99 for one, $7.99 to $10.99 for a three-pack","About $5.99 to $7.99 for a two-pack; wholesale prices lower"],["Promotion","Limited promotion: TV ad, ads in college newspapers, then student word of mouth","Limited promotion among distributors to get them to stock the product"],["Place","College bookstores, office supply stores, mass merchandisers","Office wholesalers and retailers, mass merchandisers"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Rationale: listen carefully to customer needs and use 3M technology to introduce a useful, innovative product; set prices that provide genuine value; build awareness among users who have never heard of the product; make buying easy at convenient outlets. The product succeeded: a third-generation Flag Highlighter followed, and feedback led to a 3-in-1 highlighter, pen and Post-it flags."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Building Customer Relationships and Value"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Customer value strategies"},{"t":"p","x":"Firms typically compete on gaining loyal customers by emphasizing one dominant value driver:"},{"t":"li","x":"**Best price **— e.g., Target."},{"t":"li","x":"**Best product **— e.g., Starbucks."},{"t":"li","x":"**Best service **— e.g., Nordstrom."},{"t":"p","x":"**Customer value **is the unique combination of benefits received by targeted buyers — quality, convenience, on-time delivery, and both before-sale and after-sale service — at a specific price."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Relationship marketing, CRM, and customer experience"},{"t":"li","x":"**Relationship marketing **links the organization to its individual customers, employees, suppliers, and other partners for mutual long-term benefit. Easy to understand, hard to execute well."},{"t":"li","x":"**Customer Relationship Management (CRM) **is the process of identifying prospective buyers, understanding them intimately, and developing long-term favorable perceptions so they choose the firm and become advocates after purchase."},{"t":"li","x":"**Customer experience **is the internal response customers have to all aspects of an organization and its offering — note the gap that often exists between \"what firms think they offer\" and \"what customers say they receive.\""},{"t":"p","x":"Relationship marketing is easy to understand but hard to do; relationship strategies help discover needs and create products. Customers and organizations build relationships through apps and social media."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. How Marketing Became So Important"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Evolution toward a market orientation (4 eras)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Era","Period","Dominant logic"],["Production era","Up to 1920s","Goods were scarce — focus on making enough product."],["Sales era","1920s–1960s","More goods were being produced than demanded — focus shifted to aggressive selling."],["Marketing concept era","Mid-20th century onward","Meet the needs of the consumer WHILE achieving the organization's own goals — gives the firm a market orientation."],["Customer relationship era","1980s to today","Use new technologies (CRM, data, digital) to increase value for consumers and build long-term relationships."]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Definitions to know cold\n• Marketing concept: an organization should (1) strive to satisfy the needs of consumers, (2) while also trying to achieve the organization's goals.\n• Market orientation: an organization (1) continuously collects information about customers' needs, (2) shares this information across departments, and (3) uses it to create customer value."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ethics and social responsibility"},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing has shifted emphasis from producers' interests to consumers' interests. Firms build codes of ethics, policies, and guidelines (ethics), and are increasingly expected to be accountable to society at large (social responsibility) — including new questions raised by AI and machine learning. This underlies the societal marketing concept: organizations should satisfy consumer needs in a way that also provides for society's well-being."},{"t":"p","x":"Four stages in the life of manufacturing firms: production era (up to the 1920s, goods scarce), sales era (1920s to 1960s, more goods produced than demanded), marketing concept era (meet consumer needs while achieving goals, giving firms a market orientation) and customer relationship era (1980s to today, new technologies increase value for consumers)."},{"t":"p","x":"Ethics and social responsibility also raise new questions about AI and machine learning. **Social responsibility** is the idea that organizations are accountable to a larger society."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Breadth and Depth of Marketing"},{"t":"li","x":"**What is marketed? **Products (goods), services (intangible), and ideas (concepts)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Who buys/uses it? **Ultimate consumers (people buying for personal/household use) vs. organizational buyers (manufacturers, wholesalers, retailers, service firms, nonprofits, government agencies buying for their own use or resale)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Who benefits? **Buying consumers, selling organizations, and society as a whole."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Utility — how consumers benefit"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type of utility","Meaning"],["Form utility","Value created by converting raw materials/components into a finished, desired product."],["Place utility","Value created by making a product available where customers want it."],["Time utility","Value created by making a product available when customers want it."],["Possession utility","Value created by transferring ownership/facilitating the exchange (e.g., financing, delivery)."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Exchange","The trade of things of value between a buyer and a seller so that each is better off after the trade."],["Market","People with both the desire and the ability to buy a specific offering."],["Target market","One or more specific groups of potential consumers toward which an organization directs its marketing program."],["Marketing mix","The controllable factors (product, price, promotion and place) that the marketing manager can use to solve a marketing problem."],["Environmental forces","The uncontrollable forces that affect a marketing decision: social, economic, technological, competitive and regulatory."],["Marketing program","A plan that integrates the marketing mix to provide a good, service or idea to prospective buyers."],["Market segments","Relatively homogeneous groups of prospective buyers that (1) have common needs and (2) will respond similarly to a marketing action."],["Marketing concept","The idea that an organization should (1) strive to satisfy the needs of consumers (2) while also trying to achieve the organization's goals."],["Market orientation","An organization focuses its efforts on (1) continuously collecting information about customers' needs, (2) sharing it across departments and (3) using it to create customer value."],["Societal marketing concept","The view that organizations should satisfy the needs of consumers in a way that provides for society's well-being."],["Product","A good, service or idea consisting of a bundle of tangible and intangible attributes that satisfies consumers' needs and is received in exchange for money or something else of value."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Cases and activities"},{"t":"p","x":"Video case 1, Bombas: creating socks with a purpose. Questions: how the founders Randy Goldberg and David Heath created customer value; how they raised money; the key elements of Bombas's four P's; how the social mission, \"Bee Better\" motto, direct-to-consumer model, Customer Happiness Team and promotion contributed to success; future marketing actions."},{"t":"p","x":"In-class activity 1-1, designing a better candy bar (example: Ghirardelli): specify product, price, promotion, place and points of difference, and why. In-class activity 1-2, the better mousetrap (Emerson quote): the new plastic trap (25 cents each, 90 percent efficiency) failed against the old wooden trap (2 for 15 cents, 50 percent efficiency). Key questions: what triggers the decision to buy, who decides, who buys, where, who uses it and how, which features users want, and why the \"better\" trap missed sales expectations."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 1"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which of the following is the best example of an uncontrollable environmental force affecting a bubble-tea chain, as opposed to an element of its marketing mix?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) The chain lowers its price for a limited-time promotion."},{"t":"li","x":"(B) The government introduces a new sugar-tax regulation on beverages."},{"t":"li","x":"(C) The chain redesigns its cup packaging."},{"t":"li","x":"(D) The chain opens three new outlets in shopping malls."},{"t":"a","x":"(B). A government regulation is an uncontrollable regulatory force. (A), (C) and (D) are all decisions the firm makes itself — Price, Product, and Place respectively — so they are controllable marketing mix elements."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A company that continuously collects information about customer needs, shares it across departments, and uses it to create customer value is demonstrating:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) The production era mindset"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) A market orientation"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) The sales era mindset"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Pure monopoly behavior"},{"t":"a","x":"(B). This is the textbook definition of market orientation: continuously collecting customer information, sharing it across departments, and using it to create value. The production and sales eras predate this customer-information-driven approach, and pure monopoly describes a market structure, not a management philosophy."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Distinguish between a \"need\" and a \"want,\" and give one original example (not from the slides) showing how the same underlying need can produce two very different wants in two different cultures."},{"t":"a","x":"A need is a basic, universal necessity (e.g., the need for nourishment); a want is the culturally and personally shaped form that need takes when a person decides how to satisfy it. Example: the need for nourishment might become a want for \"dim sum with family\" in Hong Kong, but a want for \"a quick protein shake\" in a fitness-focused Western market — same underlying need, very different wants driven by culture and lifestyle."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Name the four types of utility and, for a food-delivery app such as Deliveroo or Foodpanda in Hong Kong, give one concrete way the app creates each type."},{"t":"a","x":"Form utility — the app aggregates restaurant menus into a ready-to-order digital \"product.\" Place utility — food is delivered to the customer's home/office rather than requiring a trip to the restaurant. Time utility — on-demand delivery within a chosen time window. Possession utility — in-app payment and account management make the transaction/ownership transfer seamless."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Explain the difference between the marketing concept and market orientation. Why are they related but not identical?"},{"t":"a","x":"The marketing concept is the underlying philosophy — the idea that a firm should satisfy consumer needs while also achieving its own goals. Market orientation is that philosophy put into organizational practice: it describes the concrete behaviors (continuous customer-information gathering, cross-department sharing, and using the information to create value) that make a firm actually live out the marketing concept. In short: marketing concept = the \"why\"; market orientation = the \"how,\" operationalized."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Applied mini-case)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Mini-case: \"Kumu\" reusable coffee cups\n• A Hong Kong startup, Kumu, sells stainless-steel reusable coffee cups direct-to-consumer online and through pop-up kiosks in office districts. Its founder says: \"We don't want to just sell cups — we want to build a community of people who choose reusable habits.\""]]},{"t":"p","x":"(a) Identify Kumu's likely marketing mix (4Ps) at a basic level. (b) Identify two environmental forces (uncontrollable) that could accelerate or brake Kumu's growth. (c) Using the concept of relationship marketing, explain one action Kumu could take to build long-term customer relationships rather than one-off sales."},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Product — a reusable, durable stainless-steel cup with a sustainability story; Price — likely a premium versus disposable cups but positioned as saving money over time; Promotion — direct-to-consumer digital marketing, community messaging, possibly influencer/sustainability partnerships; Place — online D2C plus pop-up kiosks in office districts for convenience and trial. (b) Social forces (growing environmental consciousness/value consciousness among HK consumers) could accelerate growth; Regulatory forces (e.g., government single-use plastics restrictions) could also accelerate growth; Economic forces (a downturn reducing discretionary spend on a premium reusable cup) could act as a brake. (c) Kumu could build a \"brand community\" or loyalty app that tracks each customer's environmental impact (e.g., \"cups of waste avoided\"), turning a single sale into an ongoing relationship anchored in shared values — consistent with relationship marketing's goal of mutual, long-term benefit rather than a one-off exchange."}]},"2":{"title":"Developing Successful Organizational and Marketing Strategies","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Week 2 · Learning outcome: Discuss the major decisions involved in developing the marketing mix, the implementation challenges, and control activities."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe three kinds of organizations and the three levels of strategy in them."},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe organizational purpose, core values, mission, organizational culture, business, goals and strategies."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain why managers use marketing dashboards and marketing metrics."},{"t":"p","x":"• Discuss how an organization assesses where it is now and where it seeks to be."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain the three steps of the planning phase of the strategic marketing process."},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe the four components of the implementation phase of the strategic marketing process."},{"t":"p","x":"• Discuss how managers identify and act on deviations from plans."},{"t":"p","x":"Opening case: Ben & Jerry's has a three-part mission statement (product mission, economic mission, social mission). Strategies include regenerative farming, sustainable packaging, Free Cone Day, Fairtrade practices and B-Corp certification."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Kinds of Organizations and Levels of Strategy"},{"t":"p","x":"Organizations are legal entities of people who share a common mission and develop offerings (goods, services, or ideas) that create value. Three kinds exist:"},{"t":"li","x":"For-profit organizations (business firms) — exist to earn a profit."},{"t":"li","x":"Nonprofit organizations — pursue a social mission rather than profit."},{"t":"li","x":"Government agencies."},{"t":"p","x":"An industry is a group of companies with similar offerings. Inside an organization, the board of directors oversees three levels of strategy:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Level","Focus"],["Corporate level","Top management (including the CMO) sets overall direction across all businesses/SBUs."],["Strategic Business Unit (SBU) level","Each self-contained business/division with its own mission, competitors, and strategy."],["Functional level","Groups of specialists (e.g., marketing, finance) that create value; cross-functional teams pull departments together around a shared goal."]]},{"t":"li","x":"**Profit** — the money left after a for-profit organization subtracts its total expenses from its total revenues; the reward for the risk it undertakes in marketing its offerings."},{"t":"p","x":"Strategy cannot be \"all things to all people\". The third level is the functional level, where groups of specialists create value; a department groups a function such as finance or marketing, and cross-functional teams bring different departments together toward a performance goal. The corporate level includes the chief marketing officer (CMO)."},{"t":"p","x":"Social entrepreneurship uses business concepts to address important social needs and issues (examples: Earth Brands' compostable Earth Cup; Teach For America, with 10,000 members teaching 750,000 students each year)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Strategy in Visionary Organizations"},{"t":"p","x":"Visionary organizations build (1) a foundation, then (2) set a direction, then (3) choose strategies to execute successfully."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"(1) Organizational foundation — Why does it exist?"},{"t":"li","x":"**Organizational purpose: **why the organization exists, what problems it wants to solve, who it wants to be to those it touches."},{"t":"li","x":"**Core values: **fundamental, passionate, enduring principles that guide conduct over time and inspire stakeholders."},{"t":"li","x":"**Mission (often used interchangeably with vision): **a statement of the organization's function in society — should be clear, concise, and meaningful; often identifies customers, markets, products, technologies."},{"t":"li","x":"**Organizational culture: **the shared values, ideas, attitudes, and norms of behavior learned and shared among members."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"(2) Organizational direction — What will it do?"},{"t":"li","x":"**Business: **the clear, broad, underlying industry or market sector of the organization's offering — \"what business are we really in?\" Business models can and do change over time (e.g., Uber)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Goals/Objectives: **statements of an accomplishment to be achieved, often by a specific time — profit, sales, market share, quality, customer satisfaction, employee welfare, social responsibility, or (for nonprofits) efficiency."},{"t":"li","x":"**Key Performance Indicators (KPIs): **demonstrate how effectively goals are being achieved."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"(3) Organizational strategies — How will it do it?"},{"t":"p","x":"Strategy — an organization's long-term course of action designed to deliver a unique customer experience while achieving its goals — can vary by level (corporate, SBU, functional) and by offering (product, service, idea). The marketing plan is the road map for the marketing actions that implement strategy."},{"t":"li","x":"**Business model** — the strategies an organization develops to provide value to customers. Business models change over time."},{"t":"li","x":"**Marketing plan** — a road map for the marketing actions of an organization for a specified future period, such as one year or five years."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Tracking Performance: Dashboards and Metrics"},{"t":"p","x":"\"You can't manage what you don't measure.\" Data analytics enables data-driven decisions through:"},{"t":"li","x":"**Marketing dashboard: **the visual computer display of essential information related to achieving a marketing objective (graphs, tables in one place)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Marketing metric: **a measure of the quantitative value or trend of a marketing action or result."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Key formulas"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Marketing metrics you must be able to calculate\n• Dollar Sales ($) = Average price × Quantity sold\n• Dollar Market Share (%) = Firm's sales ($) ÷ Total industry sales ($)\n• Market share = the ratio of a firm's sales revenue to total industry sales revenue (including the firm itself)."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Data visualization is used for easy viewing to monitor business performance. A dashboard (Figure 2-3) shows metrics such as website traffic sources, sales performance by SBU and website visits by state, so managers can compare results with goals and with previous years and take action. Ben & Jerry's example: dollar sales = average price × quantity sold; dollar market share = Ben & Jerry's sales ÷ total industry sales. Market share is the ratio of a firm's sales revenue to the total sales revenue of all firms in the industry, including the firm itself."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Setting Strategic Direction: Where Are We Now? Where Do We Want to Go?"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A look around: where are we now?"},{"t":"li","x":"**Core competencies **— what the organization does best."},{"t":"li","x":"**Competitive advantage **— a unique strength relative to competitors."},{"t":"li","x":"Understanding customer preferences."},{"t":"li","x":"Scanning the (global) competitive landscape."},{"t":"p","x":"Customers (what are their preferences?) and competitors (the global marketplace) are examined alongside the firm's own competencies; Lands' End is the class example."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Growth strategies: the BCG (Business Portfolio) Matrix"},{"t":"p","x":"Business portfolio analysis quantifies performance measures and growth targets, treating a firm's SBUs/products like a portfolio of investments, plotted on market growth rate (vertical) vs. relative market share (horizontal):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Quadrant","Market growth","Market share","Description / typical action"],["Stars","High","High","Leaders in fast-growing markets — invest to maintain/grow position."],["Question marks","High","Low","Uncertain potential in attractive markets — invest selectively or divest."],["Cash cows","Low","High","Generate large amounts of cash with little new investment needed — \"milk\" them to fund stars/question marks."],["Dogs","Low","Low","Weak position in an unattractive market — typically harvest or divest."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing Matters, Apple: use your knowledge of Apple products to rank the four SBUs (iPod, iPhone, iPad/iPad mini, Apple Vision Pro) from highest to lowest projected growth to decide where to allocate time and resources (Figure 2-4)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Diversification analysis (the Ansoff-style growth matrix)"},{"t":"p","x":"A second growth framework asks whether to pursue current or new products, in current or new markets:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Strategy","Products","Markets","Example"],["Market penetration","Current","Current","Increase sales of a current product in a current market (e.g., more promotion, loyalty programs)."],["Market development","Current","New","Sell current products to new markets (e.g., new geography, new segment)."],["Product development","New","Current","Sell new products to current, existing markets (e.g., a line extension)."],["Diversification","New","New","Develop new products to sell in new markets — highest risk, potentially highest reward."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Diversification analysis helps a firm search for growth opportunities among current and new markets and current and new products. Ben & Jerry's example: Figure 2-5 shows four market-product strategies to expand sales revenues."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. The Strategic Marketing Process"},{"t":"p","x":"The strategic marketing process is the approach by which an organization allocates its marketing mix resources to reach its target markets. It rests on four guiding principles: customers are different; customers change; competitors change and react; and organizational resources are limited. It has three phases: Planning → Implementation → Evaluation."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Phase 1 — Planning (3 steps)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Conduct a situation (SWOT) analysis. **A situation analysis takes stock of where the firm/product has been, is now, and is headed. A SWOT analysis appraises internal Strengths and Weaknesses and external Opportunities and Threats. For each cell, the generic action is: build on a strength, correct a weakness, exploit an opportunity, avoid a disaster-laden threat."},{"t":"li","x":"**Develop a market-product focus, customer value proposition, and goals. **This step uses market segmentation (aggregating buyers into groups sharing common needs and similar responses to marketing action) and defines a customer value proposition (the cluster of benefits an organization promises to satisfy customer needs) built around clear points of difference (characteristics that make the product superior to competitive substitutes). Customers are increasingly willing to pay a premium for experiences built on efficiency, convenience, knowledge, friendly service, and easy payment."},{"t":"li","x":"**Design a marketing program **— blend the 4Ps (product, price, promotion, place strategies) into one cohesive program."},{"t":"p","x":"Customer value proposition: the cluster of benefits that an organization promises customers to satisfy their needs. Points of difference: the characteristics of a product that make it superior to competitive substitutes. Consumers are willing to pay a premium for certain experiences (Figure 2-8): efficiency, convenience, knowledge, friendly service and easy payment, which are essential to forming and keeping long-term customer relationships."},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing program design (Figure 2-9): product, price, promotion and place (distribution) strategies must be blended into a cohesive program."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Phase 2 — Implementation (4 components)"},{"t":"li","x":"Obtaining resources (people, money, time)."},{"t":"li","x":"Designing the marketing organization."},{"t":"li","x":"**Defining precise tasks, responsibilities, and deadlines **— via an action item list (task / person responsible / deadline / deliverable) and a Gantt chart (a graph of a program schedule showing sequential and concurrent tasks)."},{"t":"li","x":"Executing the marketing program — turning marketing strategy (the means to achieve a goal) into marketing tactics (day-to-day operational actions)."},{"t":"p","x":"The implementation phase carries out the marketing plan. An action item list has four columns: task, person responsible, date to finish, deliverable. A Gantt chart (Figure 2-11) distinguishes sequential and concurrent tasks. Marketing plans are meaningless without strong execution, which requires attention to detail. Figure 2-10 shows the organization of a typical manufacturing firm and its marketing department."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Phase 3 — Evaluation"},{"t":"p","x":"The evaluation phase keeps the marketing program on track by comparing actual results with the plan to identify deviations — the difference between planned and actual results is the planning gap. Managers then act: exploit a positive deviation (things are going better than planned — capitalize on it) or correct a negative deviation (things are going worse than planned — fix it)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Strategic marketing process","An approach whereby an organization allocates its marketing mix resources to reach its target markets."],["Situation analysis","Taking stock of where the firm or product has been recently, where it is now, and where it is headed in terms of marketing plans and the external forces and trends affecting it."],["SWOT analysis","An organization's appraisal of its internal Strengths and Weaknesses and its external Opportunities and Threats."],["Business portfolio analysis","A technique managers use to quantify performance measures and growth targets, analyzing their SBUs as though they were a collection of separate investments."],["Diversification analysis","A technique that helps a firm search for growth opportunities among current and new markets and current and new products."],["Marketing strategy","The means by which a marketing goal is to be achieved, usually characterized by a specified target market and a marketing program to reach it."],["Marketing tactics","The detailed, day-to-day operational marketing actions for each element of the marketing mix that contribute to the success of marketing strategies."],["Planning gap","The difference between the planned and the actual results, found by comparing results with goals."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Cases and activities"},{"t":"p","x":"Video case 2, IBM: putting smart to work. Questions: what IBM's \"Let's Put Smart to Work\" strategy is and how it relates to IBM's values; a SWOT analysis for the initiative and relevant trends; which Chapter 2 marketing tools are prominent in IBM's strategic marketing process; what \"agile\" marketing is at IBM and why IBM uses it."},{"t":"p","x":"In-class activity 2-1, calculating a Fog Index for your own writing: take about 100 words, count words (W), sentences (S) and complex words with 3+ syllables (CW), compute W ÷ S and CW ÷ W × 100; the index estimates readability by years of schooling. In-class activity 2-2, marketing yourself (the Do-It-Myself Marketing Plan worksheet)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 2"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A product with a low share of a slow-growth market, in the BCG matrix, is classified as a:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Star"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Cash cow"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Question mark"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Dog"},{"t":"a","x":"(D) Dog. Low market share + low/slow market growth defines a \"dog\" in the BCG matrix. Stars and question marks both sit in high-growth markets; cash cows have high share but low growth."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A firm that sells its existing skincare product line, unchanged, into a brand-new overseas market for the first time is pursuing:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Market penetration"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Market development"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Product development"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Diversification"},{"t":"a","x":"(B) Market development. Current product, new market = market development. (Current product + current market = penetration; new product + current market = product development; new product + new market = diversification.)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"A firm reports dollar sales of $4,500,000 this quarter, having sold 150,000 units. The total industry sold $30,000,000 in the same quarter. Calculate (a) the firm's average price and (b) its dollar market share. Show your formulas."},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Average price = Dollar sales ÷ Quantity sold = $4,500,000 ÷ 150,000 units = $30 per unit. (b) Dollar market share = Firm's sales ÷ Total industry sales = $4,500,000 ÷ $30,000,000 = 15%."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Explain the difference between marketing strategy and marketing tactics, and give one example of each for a university bubble-tea kiosk trying to boost Friday afternoon sales."},{"t":"a","x":"Marketing strategy is the overall means by which a marketing goal will be achieved — e.g., \"target time-pressed students during their Friday afternoon free period with a value-priced combo to lift footfall.\" Marketing tactics are the specific, day-to-day operational actions that execute that strategy — e.g., \"post an Instagram Story every Friday at 1pm advertising a $5 discount on the classic milk tea from 3–5pm.\" Strategy sets the direction; tactics are the concrete steps that carry it out."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"What is a \"planning gap,\" and what are the two possible managerial responses to it? Give an example of each response."},{"t":"a","x":"A planning gap is the difference between the goals set in the marketing plan and the actual results achieved. The two responses are: (1) exploit a positive deviation — e.g., if sales exceed target because of unexpected viral social-media buzz, the firm could increase production and extend the promotion; (2) correct a negative deviation — e.g., if sales fall short because a new competitor undercut price, the firm could adjust its own pricing or intensify promotion to close the gap."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Applied mini-case)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Mini-case: HKTVmall vs. a new grocery-delivery entrant\n• A new HK-based grocery-delivery startup, \"FreshNow,\" is deciding how to compete against the dominant incumbent, HKTVmall. FreshNow has a small but loyal customer base, a strong same-day delivery technology, limited cash, and no brand recognition outside its current niche of health-conscious young professionals in Kowloon."]]},{"t":"p","x":"(a) Conduct a brief SWOT analysis for FreshNow (one point per quadrant is enough). (b) Where would FreshNow's Kowloon niche business likely sit on the BCG matrix relative to a hypothetical larger, slower-growing division — justify your placement. (c) Recommend ONE of the four diversification-analysis strategies for FreshNow's next 12 months, with a one-sentence justification."},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Strengths — strong same-day delivery technology, loyal niche customer base; Weaknesses — limited cash, low brand recognition outside its niche; Opportunities — growing HK demand for convenient, healthy grocery delivery, potential to expand into adjacent districts; Threats — HKTVmall's scale/brand dominance, risk of being undercut on price or selection. (b) FreshNow's Kowloon niche would likely be a \"question mark\": it sits in an attractive, fast-growing market (grocery delivery/health-conscious segment) but currently has low relative market share versus the dominant HKTVmall — it needs further investment to decide whether it can become a star or should be scaled back. (c) Market development is a reasonable recommendation for the next 12 months: FreshNow should extend its proven same-day delivery model and reputation among health-conscious professionals into adjacent, demographically similar districts (e.g., other white-collar neighborhoods) rather than attempting a completely new product line (diversification) while cash is limited — this leverages its core competency without stretching scarce resources too thin. (Accept product development or penetration if well justified against the resource constraint.)"}]},"3":{"title":"Scanning the Marketing Environment","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Week 3 · Learning outcome: Examine the various environmental forces in which a company operates."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain how environmental scanning provides information about social, economic, technological, competitive and regulatory forces."},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe how social forces such as demographics and culture can have an impact on marketing strategy."},{"t":"p","x":"• Discuss how economic forces such as macroeconomic conditions and consumer income affect marketing."},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe how technological changes can affect marketing."},{"t":"p","x":"• Discuss the forms of competition that exist in a market and the key components of competition."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain the major legislation that ensures competition and regulates the elements of the marketing mix."},{"t":"p","x":"Opening case: Meta Platforms (Facebook, Instagram, WhatsApp, Libra, Oculus) has invested in AI since 2013 and is integrating AI features into its products; all five environmental forces influence it."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Environmental Scanning"},{"t":"p","x":"**Environmental scanning **is the process of continually acquiring information on events occurring outside the organization to identify and interpret potential trends. It covers five environmental forces — the same five \"uncontrollable\" forces from Chapter 1:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Force","Illustrative current trends (from course slides)"],["Social","Rise of micro-influencers; conscious consumerism; growing focus on inclusion and social equity."],["Economic","Creator economy from digital video; shift to subscription business models (Google, Apple, Microsoft); circular economy (reuse over disposal)."],["Technological","AI personalizing customer experience; social commerce on Facebook/Instagram; the Fourth Industrial Revolution (5G, AI, IoT, robotics)."],["Competitive","Sustainability-driven innovation; shift from transactions to ongoing relationships/experiential journeys; machine learning (chatbots, voice search) as competitive strategy."],["Regulatory","Stricter privacy/data laws (GDPR, CPRA, KOSA); antitrust scrutiny of Alphabet, Amazon, Apple, Meta, Microsoft; evolving rules for ridesharing, investing, healthcare."]]},{"t":"p","x":"This chapter is essentially the source of the \"PESTLE-style\" environmental scan referenced in your Group Project brief (Political/Regulatory, Economic, Social, Technological + Competitive)."},{"t":"p","x":"Example: the coffee industry (growers, supermarkets, coffee shops, e.g. Starbucks Reserve Roastery). Marketers track trends in consumption volume, type, location and new brews. Identifying and interpreting trends are both essential to environmental scanning. Environmental forces affect the organization as well as its suppliers and customers (Figure 3-1)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Social Forces"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Demographics"},{"t":"li","x":"World population: ~8.1 billion, projected to reach 9.7 billion by 2050, with most growth in Africa and Asia; India (≈1.67bn) overtakes China (≈1.30bn) as most populous by 2050; population is declining in 61 countries including China, Japan, Russia, and several European states."},{"t":"li","x":"U.S. population trends (context example from slides): growing, aging, and more diverse; ~338 million today; 65+ population growing sharply toward 2050."},{"t":"p","x":"• U.S. population outlook: 338 million today, above 345 million by 2030 and above 360 million by 2050; the population is becoming larger, older and more diverse."},{"t":"p","x":"• Population aged 65+: more than 60 million today, more than 82 million by 2050 (22.7% of the population)."},{"t":"p","x":"• The term \"minority\" as currently used will become obsolete."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Generational cohorts"},{"t":"p","x":"Class activity: identify which generational cohort each ad is reaching (Revlon Age Defying makeup, Delta Premium Select, TOMS Buy One Give One)."},{"t":"p","x":"Responsible decisions: Millennials expect companies to embrace social change, corporate social responsibility and environmental stewardship, and expect sustainable communities. Net Impact is a nonprofit for students who want to use business to improve the world."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Cohort","Birth years","Size and characteristics"],["Baby Boomers","1946–1964","76 million; about 10,000 retire every 24 hours; all will be 65+ by 2030; split into Boomer I and Boomer II cohorts."],["Generation X","1965–1980","55 million; also called the baby bust; self-reliant; support diversity."],["Generation Y (Millennials)","1981–1996 (the course slide shows 1983–1996)","62 million; also called the echo-boom; strong-willed, passionate, optimistic; technologically savvy; expect CSR and sustainability from brands."],["Generation Z","1997–2010","Post-millennial generation; broadest diversity to date; independent, hardworking, financially responsible; embrace diversity."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Population shifts and diversity"},{"t":"p","x":"U.S. population is shifting regionally toward the south and west, driven by three components: natural population change, state-to-state migration, and international migration (metropolitan area = 50,000+ people; micropolitan = 10,000–50,000). Ethnic and racial diversity is increasing, with major economic impact (e.g., Hispanic, African American, and Asian American consumer spending each in the trillions of dollars) — giving rise to multicultural marketing: combinations of the marketing mix reflecting the unique attitudes, ancestry, communication preferences, and lifestyles of different races."},{"t":"p","x":"Racial and ethnic diversity: about one-third of the U.S. population belongs to African American, Native American or Alaska Native, Asian American, or Native Hawaiian or Pacific Islander groups. Spending power: Hispanic consumers about $2.3 trillion, African American about $1.8 trillion, Asian American about $1.5 trillion. This drives multicultural marketing."},{"t":"p","x":"Culture in detail: notable cultural changes cover attitudes and roles, gender stereotypes, fashion, careers and sports. Old commercials targeted women for household products; today advertising moves toward gender-neutral messages, and companies with a primary gender focus prepare for growth from the other gender. 82 percent of shoppers consider themselves environmentally friendly, and consumers commit to brands with a strong link to social action (example: Patagonia Worn Wear)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Culture"},{"t":"p","x":"**Culture **is the set of values, ideas, and attitudes that are learned and shared among members of a group. Notable shifts include changing gender roles and more gender-neutral marketing, and rising sustainability and value consciousness (the concern for obtaining the best quality, features, and performance for a given price) — a large majority of shoppers now describe themselves as environmentally conscious."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Economic Forces"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Macroeconomic conditions"},{"t":"li","x":"**Inflation: **rising production costs and prices (e.g., college tuition has risen far faster than family income)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Recession: **periods of declining economic activity."},{"t":"li","x":"**Consumer sentiment indices **(Consumer Confidence Index; Index of Consumer Sentiment) track consumer expectations about their own future financial situation — higher index = more favorable expectations, closely watched by marketers."},{"t":"li","x":"**Economy** — the income, expenditures and resources that affect the cost of running a business and a household. Macroeconomics studies the performance of the economy (for example GDP, inflation, deflation)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Inflation example: college tuition has increased by 45 percent since 2003, while family income increased by less than 13 percent."},{"t":"p","x":"Consumer sentiment survey question: \"Looking ahead, do you think that a year from now you will be better off financially, worse off, or just about the same as now?\" The answers are used to construct the index."},{"t":"p","x":"Census Bureau (Marketing Insights): marketers use environmental scans to compare data about a segment (population size, household income, education, number of businesses) and to understand consumers, for example by profiling a hometown."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Consumer income (microeconomics)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Income concept","Definition"],["Gross income","Total money earned in one year by a person/household."],["Disposable income","Money left after paying taxes — used for necessities (food, housing, clothing, transportation)."],["Discretionary income","Money left after paying taxes AND necessities — the pool available for non-essential spending."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Illustrative consumer expenditure breakdown from the slides: ~13% of income on food, ~33% on housing, ~3% on clothing, ~25% on transportation and healthcare — the remainder is discretionary."},{"t":"p","x":"Microeconomics concerns the ability of consumers to buy goods and services. The annual Consumer Expenditure Study tracks consumer spending."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Technological Forces"},{"t":"li","x":"**E-commerce **has created new companies, disrupted others, and expanded value through convenience, choice, interactivity, and increased customer control."},{"t":"li","x":"**Internet of Things (IoT): **network of connectivity-enabled smart devices generating rich behavioral data for marketers."},{"t":"li","x":"**Social media: **digital technologies enabling creation/sharing of user-generated content; widely used by marketers for advertising."},{"t":"li","x":"**Artificial Intelligence (AI): **used for personalization, dynamic pricing, chatbots, and generative content (e.g., Coca-Cola's GenAI-developed \"Y3000\" flavor and campaign)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Big data & marketing analytics: **big data = extremely large data sets (social, transactional, voice/natural-language data) requiring sophisticated analysis; marketing analytics studies this data to evaluate performance via descriptive, diagnostic, and predictive analytics."},{"t":"li","x":"**Technology** — the inventions or innovations from applied science or engineering research. It has changed marketing practices."},{"t":"p","x":"• E-commerce also promotes interactivity and relationship building; benefits include convenience, choice and increased customer control; it brings both opportunities and challenges."},{"t":"p","x":"• IoT devices include phones, watches and glasses; the information they track is a boon to marketers."},{"t":"p","x":"• Social media are used to keep in touch, read news, see trends, fill spare time and find inspiration; marketers consider them effective advertising."},{"t":"p","x":"• AI creates customer value, experience and engagement (examples: Siri, Alexa), can adjust prices by time, demand and competition, and powers chatbots that mimic human speech and writing. Generative AI produces personal messages, ads and social media content."},{"t":"p","x":"• Big data examples: social network data, transactional data, natural-language (voice) data. Marketing analytics evaluates performance through metrics."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Competitive Forces"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Forms of competition"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Form","Number of sellers","Example"],["Pure competition","Many, similar/undifferentiated products","Agricultural commodities"],["Monopolistic competition","Many, substitutable products in a price range","Coffee, tea brands"],["Oligopoly","Few sellers control most sales","Telecom carriers"],["Pure monopoly","One seller","Public utilities"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Components of competition"},{"t":"p","x":"Firms must consider the factors that drive competitive intensity in an industry — this maps closely onto Porter's Five Forces logic:"},{"t":"li","x":"Barriers to entry — business practices or conditions that make it hard for new firms to enter."},{"t":"li","x":"Power of buyers and suppliers."},{"t":"li","x":"Existing competitors and substitute products."},{"t":"p","x":"(Context: small businesses are a major competitive force — 33+ million U.S. small businesses employ 46% of employees, generate 66% of new jobs, and represent 44% of GDP.)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Competition** — the alternative firms that could provide a product to satisfy a specific market's needs."},{"t":"li","x":"**Barriers to entry** — business practices or conditions that make it difficult for new firms to enter the market."},{"t":"p","x":"Pure competition: many sellers with similar products (commodities). Oligopoly examples: T-Mobile, AT&T."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Regulatory Forces"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Protecting competition"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Law","Year","Purpose"],["Sherman Antitrust Act","1890","Outlaws monopolistic practices and restraint of trade."],["Clayton Act","1914","Strengthens antitrust law; addresses specific anticompetitive practices (e.g., exclusive dealing)."],["Robinson–Patman Act","1936","Prohibits price discrimination that harms competition."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Other legislative areas (know the categories, not every act)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Product-related: **Patent/Copyright law, Consumer Product Safety Act, labeling laws (e.g., Fair Packaging and Labeling Act), trademark law (Lanham Act)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Pricing & distribution: **laws against price fixing/discounting; distribution issues such as exclusive dealing, exclusive territories, and tying arrangements."},{"t":"li","x":"**Advertising & promotion: **FTC Act (1914); children's privacy (COPPA, 1998); anti-spam law (CAN-SPAM, 2004); telemarketing restrictions."},{"t":"li","x":"**Self-regulation: **industries police themselves (e.g., TV network guidelines on ads for children; the Better Business Bureau, a voluntary alliance promoting fair practices)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Product-related and trademark legislation"},{"t":"li","x":"**Regulation** — the restrictions state and federal laws place on business with regard to the conduct of its activities."},{"t":"p","x":"Product-related laws: patent law; copyright law; Digital Millennium Copyright Act (1998); Child Protection Act (1966); Consumer Product Safety Act (1972); Fair Packaging and Labeling Act (1972); Infant Formula Act (1980); Nutritional Labeling and Education Act (1990); Truth in Labeling Act (2023)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Consumerism** — a grassroots movement started in the 1960s to increase the influence, power and rights of consumers in dealing with institutions."},{"t":"p","x":"Trademark law: the Lanham Act (1946) registers a company's trademark; the Trademark Law Revision Act (1988) lets a company secure rights to a name before actual use; Madrid Protocol (2003); Federal Trademark Dilution Act (1995, 2006)."},{"t":"p","x":"Pricing laws cover two perspectives: price fixing and price discounting. Distribution concerns: exclusive dealing, requirement contracts, exclusive territorial distributorships and tying arrangements."},{"t":"p","x":"Advertising and promotion laws: FTC Act (1914); Telephone Consumer Protection Act (1991); Deceptive Mail Prevention and Enforcement Act (1999); Children's Online Privacy Protection Act (1998); European Union Data Protection Act; CAN-SPAM Act (2004); Trade Facilitation and Trade Enforcement Act (2015)."},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing Matters, does protecting privacy hurt the web? Browsers are tracked to decide which ads appear (online behavioral targeting, OBT); many consumers are unaware of the tracking level; the FTC suggests \"Do Not Track\" options; some browser companies will discontinue third-party cookies. Question: is giving up some privacy a reasonable trade-off for targeted advertising?"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Social forces","The demographic characteristics of the population and its culture."],["Demographics","Describe a population according to selected characteristics such as age, gender, ethnicity, income and occupation."],["Multicultural marketing","Combinations of the marketing mix that reflect the unique attitudes, ancestry, communication preferences and lifestyles of different races."],["Gross income","The total amount of money made in one year by a person, household or family unit (money income at the Census Bureau)."],["Electronic commerce","Any activity that uses electronic communication in the inventory, promotion, distribution, purchase and exchange of products and services (e-commerce)."],["Artificial intelligence","The simulation or approximation of human intelligence in machines."],["Self-regulation","An alternative to government control whereby an industry attempts to police itself."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Cases and activities"},{"t":"p","x":"Video case 3, Geek Squad: a new business for a new environment. Questions: (1) which environmental forces created the opportunity for Robert Stephens; (2) which changes in the purchasing patterns of all consumers and of women made the Best Buy acquisition important; (3) an environmental scan of the five forces for Geek Squad; (4) promotional activities to make users of independent installers switch."},{"t":"p","x":"In-class activity 3-1: environmental scan for Truvía (scan form handout). In-class activity 3-2: competitive intelligence (form handout, Fuld & Company)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 3"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A market with only a handful of large sellers who control most industry sales (e.g., telecom operators in Hong Kong) best describes:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Pure competition"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Monopolistic competition"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Oligopoly"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Pure monopoly"},{"t":"a","x":"(C) Oligopoly. Few sellers controlling the majority of sales is the defining feature of an oligopoly (e.g., T-Mobile/AT&T-style telecom markets)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which of the following is money remaining after BOTH taxes AND necessities (food, housing, clothing, transportation) have been paid for?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Gross income"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Disposable income"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Discretionary income"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Net worth"},{"t":"a","x":"(C) Discretionary income. Disposable income = after taxes only; discretionary income = after taxes AND necessities — this is the pool available for optional purchases."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Explain what \"environmental scanning\" means and why identifying a trend is not, by itself, enough — what else must a marketer do with that trend?"},{"t":"a","x":"Environmental scanning is the continual process of gathering information on events outside the organization to identify potential trends. Identifying a trend is only the first step; the marketer must also interpret/explain the trend — i.e., understand WHY it is happening and what it implies for the organization's customers, competitors, and marketing mix decisions — before it can be turned into a marketing action."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Name the five environmental forces and, for each one, give ONE original current example relevant to Hong Kong (not copied from the slides)."},{"t":"a","x":"Social — HK's aging population increasing demand for health-related products/services; Economic — rising cost of living/rents in HK squeezing discretionary spending; Technological — widespread adoption of e-payment (Octopus, FPS, WeChat Pay HK) reshaping retail and marketing; Competitive — the rise of mainland cross-border e-commerce (e.g., Taobao) intensifying price competition for HK retailers; Regulatory — HK's Personal Data (Privacy) Ordinance shaping how marketers can collect and use customer data."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"What is the purpose of the Sherman Antitrust Act and the Robinson-Patman Act? Why would a large HK retail chain need to be aware of anti-price-discrimination rules when negotiating with suppliers?"},{"t":"a","x":"The Sherman Antitrust Act (1890) outlaws monopolistic practices and restraints of trade to protect competitive markets; the Robinson-Patman Act (1936) specifically prohibits price discrimination between different buyers of the same goods when it harms competition. A large HK retail chain negotiating volume discounts from suppliers needs to be aware that heavily discriminatory pricing that disadvantages smaller competing retailers (in jurisdictions with similar rules) could expose the supplier — and potentially the retailer — to legal risk, so pricing terms must be defensible on objective grounds (e.g., genuine cost differences, volume economies) rather than arbitrary favoritism."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Applied mini-case)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Mini-case: Anitch Barrier Repair Body Cream enters Melbourne\n• (This mirrors your Group Project Option 3.) Anitch, a Hong Kong skincare brand whose R&D is backed by HKU medical/microbiology experts, plans to launch its Barrier Repair Body Cream in Melbourne, Australia."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Using the five environmental forces, identify ONE relevant trend or factor under each of Social, Economic, Technological, Competitive, and Regulatory forces that Anitch's marketing team should scan before entering the Melbourne market."},{"t":"a","x":"Social — Australian consumers' growing interest in \"clean\"/science-backed skincare and sensitive-skin (barrier-repair) products, including for babies/infants (Anitch's product range). Economic — currency exchange rate (HKD/AUD) and Australian consumers' discretionary spending on premium personal-care products. Technological — the strength of e-commerce/D2C skincare distribution and social-commerce (Instagram/TikTok shopping) in Australia. Competitive — established local and global barrier-repair/dermo-cosmetic brands already present in Australian pharmacies and Priceline-style retailers. Regulatory — Australia's cosmetic/therapeutic goods regulations (TGA) and labelling requirements that an imported HK skincare brand must comply with before it can be legally sold."}]},"5":{"title":"Understanding Consumer Behavior","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Week 4 · Learning outcome: Understand the stages of the purchase decision process and the factors influencing this process."},{"t":"p","x":"**Consumer behavior **consists of the actions a person takes in purchasing and using products/services, including the mental and social processes before and after those actions."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe the stages in the consumer purchase decision process."},{"t":"p","x":"• Distinguish among three variations of the consumer purchase decision process: extended, limited and routine problem solving."},{"t":"p","x":"• Identify the major psychological influences on consumer behavior."},{"t":"p","x":"• Identify the major sociocultural influences on consumer behavior."},{"t":"p","x":"Opening case: enlightened carmakers know what \"custom(h)ers\" and \"influenc(h)ers\" value. Women make 62 percent of new-car-buying decisions and influence 85 percent. Key elements: the sense of styling, the need for speed, the substance of safety and the shopping experience."},{"t":"p","x":"Nearly a quarter of U.S. consumers are \"very or extremely loyal\" to brands."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. The Consumer Purchase Decision Process (5 stages)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Stage","What happens","Key idea"],["1. Problem recognition","Perceiving a need","Triggered by a gap between the consumer's ideal and actual situation."],["2. Information search","Seeking value","Internal search (memory) and external search — personal sources (friends/family), public sources (reviews, media), marketer-dominated sources (ads, salespeople)."],["3. Alternative evaluation","Assessing value","Consumer forms evaluative criteria and narrows options to a consideration set (the acceptable brands actually compared)."],["4. Purchase decision","Buying value","Decide from whom to buy and when to buy."],["5. Postpurchase behavior","Realizing value","Satisfaction/dissatisfaction and possible cognitive dissonance (postpurchase psychological tension)."]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Postpurchase behavior — numbers worth remembering\n• Satisfied customers tell about 3 other people; dissatisfied customers tell about 9.\n• About 90% of dissatisfied customers will not buy the product again.\n• Cognitive dissonance = the anxiety/tension a buyer feels after a purchase, especially for high-involvement decisions.\n• Customer retention has outsized profit impact: e.g., a 5% increase in retention can raise profits by 70–80% (illustrative figure from course slides — company-specific, treat as an illustrative order of magnitude)."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Evaluating alternatives (Figure 5-2): common selection criteria for smartwatches and fitness trackers include retail price, display size, weight, operating system, battery life, phone call capability and third-party apps (compared across Apple Watch Series 9, Garmin Venu Sq 2, Fitbit Versa 4 and Samsung Galaxy Watch 6). Evaluative criteria are the features used when comparing brands; the consideration set is the group of acceptable brands."},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing Matters, how much is a satisfied customer worth? Customer satisfaction underlies the marketing concept. Lifetime values: Frito-Lay customer $52.50 a year; Exxon $500 a year; Kleenex $994 over 60 years; GM customer $276,000 over a lifetime of cars, parts and service. GM's retention rate is 65.4 percent, and each 1 percent increase in retention generates an extra $700 million. Satisfied buyers buy from the same seller again."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Consumer Involvement and Problem Solving"},{"t":"p","x":"**Involvement **is the personal, social, and economic significance of the purchase to the consumer. It drives which \"mode\" the 5-stage process runs in:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Problem-solving type","Involvement","Typical marketing strategy implication"],["Extended problem solving","High","Consumers seek lots of information; use comparative ads and personal selling; provide detailed product information."],["Limited problem solving","Medium","Some information search; moderate comparison of a few brands."],["Routine problem solving","Low","Little search — maintain product quality, avoid stockouts, use ads to reduce cognitive dissonance rather than to inform."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Routine problem solving corresponds to low involvement. Strategy: for low-involvement products maintain product quality, avoid stockouts and reduce cognitive dissonance with ads; for high-involvement products consumers seek product information, so use comparative ads and personal selling."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Situational Influences"},{"t":"li","x":"**Purchase task **— the reason behind the purchase (e.g., a gift vs. personal use)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Social surroundings **— the presence and influence of other people."},{"t":"li","x":"**Physical surroundings **— store décor, music, crowding."},{"t":"li","x":"**Temporal effects **— time of day, time pressure/availability."},{"t":"li","x":"**Antecedent states **— the consumer's mood, circumstances, and available money at the moment of decision."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Consumer Touchpoints and Journey Maps"},{"t":"p","x":"**Consumer touchpoints **are a brand's points of contact with a consumer across the process (company channels, review/rating sites, other consumers, social media). A **consumer journey map **visually represents all touchpoints before, during, and after a purchase. AI/GenAI increasingly shapes this journey — smart devices help consumers research products, find deals, and resolve \"pain points\" faster and more efficiently."},{"t":"p","x":"Touchpoints include the company, rating and review services, other consumers and social media (Figure 5-4 shows the Apple journey map for devices sold in Apple stores). AI-driven journeys make the journey more efficient: smart devices equipped with GenAI research products, find deals and promotions and solve consumers' \"pain points\"."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Psychological Influences on Consumer Behavior"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Motivation — Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Level (bottom → top)","Type of need","Marketing example"],["1. Physiological","Food, water, shelter","Basic groceries, utilities."],["2. Safety","Security, protection","Insurance, home security systems."],["3. Social","Belonging, love, friendship","Social apps, community-building brands."],["4. Personal (esteem)","Status, recognition, achievement","Luxury goods, premium memberships."],["5. Self-actualization","Realizing one's full potential","Education, personal development, experiences."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Personality and perception"},{"t":"li","x":"**Personality **— a person's consistent behaviors/responses to recurring situations (traits such as assertiveness, extroversion, compliance, dominance); **self-concept **— how we see ourselves."},{"t":"li","x":"**Perception **— selecting, organizing, and interpreting information into a meaningful picture. The selective perception process filters what we notice: selective exposure (what we pay attention to), selective comprehension (how we interpret it), selective retention (what we remember)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Perceived risk **— anxiety felt because outcomes cannot be anticipated and may be negative; the greater the perceived risk, the more extensive the external search. Firms reduce it via seals of approval, endorsements, free trials/samples, extensive instructions, and warranties/guarantees."},{"t":"p","x":"Subliminal perception is also part of selective perception. Making responsible decisions: the FTC says subliminal messages are deceptive but not illegal; about two-thirds of U.S. consumers think they appear in commercials and half think this makes them buy. Are subliminal messages unethical?"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Learning"},{"t":"p","x":"**Learning **results from (1) repeated experience and (2) reasoning. Behavioral learning builds automatic responses through repeated exposure via four elements:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Element","Meaning"],["Drive","The need that stimulates action."],["Cue","A stimulus or symbol that triggers a response."],["Response","The action taken."],["Reinforcement","The reward for the action, strengthening the future response."]]},{"t":"li","x":"**Stimulus generalization **— response to one stimulus is generalized to a similar stimulus (e.g., trusting a brand extension because you trust the parent brand)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Stimulus discrimination **— ability to perceive differences among similar stimuli (e.g., distinguishing a genuine brand from a knock-off)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Cognitive learning **— making connections between ideas without direct reinforcement."},{"t":"li","x":"**Brand loyalty **— a favorable attitude toward, and consistent repeat purchase of, a single brand over time."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Values, beliefs, and attitudes"},{"t":"p","x":"**Attitude **— a learned predisposition to respond favorably or unfavorably to an object, shaped by values and **beliefs **(a consumer's subjective perception of how a product performs on attributes, based on experience, advertising, and word of mouth). Marketers change attitudes through three approaches:"},{"t":"li","x":"Change beliefs about a brand's attributes."},{"t":"li","x":"Change the perceived importance of attributes."},{"t":"li","x":"Add new product attributes."},{"t":"p","x":"Attitudes are a learned response to objects in a consistent way, either favorable or unfavorable. Beliefs are based on personal experience."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Lifestyle and VALS"},{"t":"p","x":"Lifestyle describes how people spend their time and resources; lifestyle analysis (psychographics) helps target consumers. The VALS framework classifies consumers by primary motivation and resource level:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Primary motivation","Segments","Resource orientation"],["Ideals","Thinkers and Believers","Guided by knowledge and principles."],["Achievement","Achievers and Strivers","Guided by projecting success to others."],["Self-expression","Experiencers and Makers","Guided by desire for activity, variety, risk."]]},{"t":"p","x":"High-resource groups tend to be future-oriented; low-resource groups tend to focus on immediate/basic needs."},{"t":"p","x":"VALS examines the intersection of psychology, demographics and lifestyles. It identifies eight consumer segments (mind-sets) based on primary motivation and resources. Resource levels enhance or constrain motivation. Marketing Matters: examine the VALS types and decide which fits you."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Sociocultural Influences on Consumer Behavior"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Personal influence"},{"t":"li","x":"**Opinion leaders **— individuals who exert direct/indirect social influence over others."},{"t":"li","x":"**Word of mouth **— highly powerful and can be positive or negative (\"buzz\"); underlies influencer marketing."},{"t":"p","x":"Influencer marketing builds on word of mouth. BzzAgent: a volunteer army of 1,000,000+ talkers that captures honest word of mouth; agents receive samples and a manual for creating buzz and keep the products and earn points."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Reference groups"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Relationship to the group"],["Associative group","A group we already belong to."],["Aspiration group","A group we want to belong to."],["Dissociative group","A group we want to distance ourselves from."]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Brand community **— a specialized group of consumers with a structured set of relationships around a particular brand, its fellow customers, and the product in use."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Social class"},{"t":"p","x":"Social class — relatively permanent, homogeneous divisions in society sharing similar values, interests, and behavior, determined by occupation, income source, and education. Three broad categories (each with subcategories): upper, middle, lower."},{"t":"p","x":"Examples of media by class: upper (The New Yorker), middle (Walmart), lower (tabloid magazines)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Family influence"},{"t":"li","x":"**Consumer socialization **— children learn to be consumers by interacting with adults during purchases and through their own experience."},{"t":"li","x":"**Family life cycle **— distinct phases a family progresses through, each with identifiable purchasing behaviors; note that \"traditional\" families are now a minority of U.S. households (~20%)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Decision-making styles: **spouse-dominant vs. joint decisions."},{"t":"li","x":"**Family member roles: **information gatherer, influencer, decision maker, purchaser, user — the same person can play more than one role, and the roles can belong to different family members for the same purchase."},{"t":"p","x":"Traditional families are only 20 percent of U.S. households; the other 80 percent include single parents, unmarried couples, divorced people and more."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Culture and subculture"},{"t":"p","x":"Culture — the set of values, ideas, and attitudes learned and shared among members of a group. Subcultures are subgroups within the larger national culture with their own unique values, ideas, and attitudes. The course slides illustrate this with three major U.S. subcultures (Hispanic/Latino, Black American, Asian American) and their distinct buying-pattern generalizations — useful as an illustration of the concept of multicultural marketing, though such broad generalizations should always be treated cautiously and updated with current local research."},{"t":"p","x":"Subculture buying patterns (course slides, generalizations only):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Subculture","Buying pattern"],["Hispanic (Latino)","Quality and brand conscious; prefer American-made products that cater to Hispanics; preferences influenced by family and peers; advertising seen as a credible source of information; heavy users of social media and streaming services."],["Black American","Spend more on boys' clothing, rental goods, smartphones and audio equipment; price conscious; embrace high-quality, trustworthy brands with a clear social mission; motivated by quality and choice; brands need to understand Black culture."],["Asian American","Fastest growing subculture; very diverse in country of origin, language, customs and tastes, so generalizations are difficult; brand loyal and cost conscious; preserving cultural heritage is important; higher median family income."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Consumer behavior","The actions a person takes in purchasing and using products and services, including the mental and social processes before and after these actions."],["Purchase decision process","The five stages a buyer passes through in choosing products and services: problem recognition, information search, alternative evaluation, purchase decision and postpurchase behavior."],["Involvement","The personal, social and economic significance of the purchase to the consumer."],["Consumer touchpoints","A marketer's product, service or brand points of contact with a consumer from start to finish in the purchase decision process."],["Consumer journey map","A visual representation of all the touchpoints a consumer comes into contact with before, during and after a purchase."],["Motivation","The energizing force that stimulates behavior to satisfy a need."],["Personality","A person's consistent behaviors or responses to recurring situations."],["Reference groups","People to whom an individual looks as a basis for self-appraisal or as a source of personal standards."],["Word of mouth","The influencing of people during conversations."],["Family life cycle","The distinct phases a family progresses through from formation to retirement, each bringing identifiable purchasing behaviors."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Cases and activities"},{"t":"p","x":"Video case 5, Coppertone: creating the leading sun care brand by understanding consumers. Questions: how consumer-behavior understanding helped Coppertone grow at home and abroad; the five-stage decision process for a Coppertone customer; situational, psychological and sociocultural influences; Coppertone's marketing activities; future challenges and recommended actions."},{"t":"p","x":"In-class activity 5-1: the purchase decision process for Starbucks VIA Refreshers instant coffee."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 5"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A consumer who buys the same brand of instant noodles every week without comparing alternatives is demonstrating:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Extended problem solving"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Limited problem solving"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Routine problem solving"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Cognitive dissonance"},{"t":"a","x":"(C) Routine problem solving. Low-involvement, habitual repurchase without active comparison is the hallmark of routine problem solving."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which stage of the purchase decision process involves narrowing options down to a \"consideration set\" using evaluative criteria?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Problem recognition"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Information search"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Alternative evaluation"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Postpurchase behavior"},{"t":"a","x":"(C) Alternative evaluation. This is precisely where evaluative criteria are applied and a consideration set of acceptable brands is formed."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Define cognitive dissonance and explain ONE concrete marketing action a company can take after the sale to reduce it for a high-involvement purchase (e.g., a laptop)."},{"t":"a","x":"Cognitive dissonance is the postpurchase psychological tension/anxiety a buyer feels after making a decision, especially when the purchase was important or involved trade-offs between attractive alternatives. To reduce it, a laptop company could send a reassuring post-purchase email highlighting the product's key strengths, offer a clear warranty/return policy, or provide onboarding support/tutorials that reinforce the buyer's confidence that they made the right choice."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Using Maslow's hierarchy, explain which need level a gym membership app with a social \"workout leaderboard\" feature is primarily appealing to, and justify your answer."},{"t":"a","x":"It appeals primarily to social needs (through the leaderboard/community and comparison with friends) and personal/esteem needs (through status from ranking well) rather than purely physiological needs — the core product (exercise tracking) touches physiological/safety (health), but the leaderboard feature is specifically designed to layer in social belonging and esteem/recognition on top of the base fitness function."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Distinguish between an associative, an aspiration, and a dissociative reference group, using one original brand example for each."},{"t":"a","x":"Associative group — a group the consumer already belongs to, e.g., a student's own university class group buying matching class hoodies. Aspiration group — a group the consumer wants to join, e.g., an amateur runner buying the same shoe brand worn by elite marathoners they admire. Dissociative group — a group the consumer wants to distance from, e.g., a young professional avoiding a clothing brand strongly associated with a demographic or subculture they don't want to be identified with."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Applied mini-case)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Mini-case: Buying a first smartwatch\n• Amy, a 21-year-old university student in Hong Kong, decides to buy her first smartwatch after noticing her running pace has stalled. She asks two friends who own Apple Watches and Garmins, reads reviews on YouTube, and compares an Apple Watch, a Garmin, and a Fitbit in a shopping mall before buying the Apple Watch because her friends and course-mates (whom she wants to fit in with) already use one."]]},{"t":"p","x":"(a) Map Amy's behavior onto the five stages of the purchase decision process. (b) Which level of consumer involvement (extended/limited/routine) does this purchase most resemble, and why? (c) Identify ONE psychological influence and ONE sociocultural influence at work in Amy's decision, and explain each briefly."},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Problem recognition — Amy notices her running pace has stalled (gap between actual and desired performance); Information search — she asks friends (personal source) and watches YouTube reviews (public source); Alternative evaluation — she compares Apple Watch, Garmin, and Fitbit against criteria such as features/brand; Purchase decision — she decides to buy the Apple Watch, and decides where (mall) and when; Postpurchase behavior — not stated, but she may experience cognitive dissonance if the product underperforms versus the social expectations that drove the choice. (b) This resembles extended (or at least limited-to-extended) problem solving: it is a relatively costly, infrequent purchase, she actively searches for information from multiple sources, and she compares several brands before deciding. (c) Psychological influence — perception/social validation is intertwined with perceived risk reduction: comparing multiple sources (reviews, friends' experience) reduces the perceived risk of an unfamiliar, relatively expensive purchase. Sociocultural influence — reference group influence (aspiration/associative): Amy's decision is shaped by wanting to align with her friends and course-mates who already own Apple Watches, illustrating the power of reference groups and word of mouth on purchase decisions."}]},"8":{"title":"Marketing Research: From Customer Insights to Actions","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Week 5 · Learning outcome: Identify the major purposes of marketing research, the major methods by which it is undertaken, and the challenges of organizing market research activities."},{"t":"p","x":"**Marketing research **is the process of defining a marketing problem and opportunity, systematically collecting and analyzing information, and recommending actions."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Identify the reason for conducting marketing research."},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe the five-step marketing research approach that leads to marketing actions."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain how marketing uses secondary and primary data."},{"t":"p","x":"• Discuss the uses of observations, questionnaires, panels, experiments and newer data collection methods."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain how data analytics and data mining lead to marketing actions."},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe three approaches to developing a company's sales forecast."},{"t":"p","x":"Opening case: Hollywood reduces risk through research (movie title testing, concept testing and script assessment, test or preview screening, tracking studies, social listening), for example the Barbie movie."},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing research is used to obtain information that determines consumer demand. A decision is a conscious choice among alternatives; decision-making is a structured approach to choosing (Figure 8-1)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"The Five-Step Marketing Research Approach"},{"t":"p","x":"This is the single most important framework in this chapter — expect to be asked to apply it end-to-end."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 1 — Define the problem"},{"t":"li","x":"Set specific, measurable research objectives."},{"t":"li","x":"Choose the type of research needed:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Purpose"],["Exploratory research","Gathers preliminary information to help define the problem and suggest hypotheses — used when the issue is vague."],["Descriptive research","Describes market characteristics (who, what, where, when, how) — e.g., who buys what, how often."],["Causal research","Tests cause-and-effect relationships — e.g., \"does this price change CAUSE a change in sales?\""]]},{"t":"li","x":"Identify possible marketing actions that could result and define measures of success (criteria used to evaluate proposed solutions)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 2 — Develop the research plan"},{"t":"li","x":"Specify constraints (restrictions placed on potential solutions — budget, time, access)."},{"t":"li","x":"Identify what data are actually needed to support a marketing action."},{"t":"li","x":"Decide how to collect the data: concepts (ideas being tested), methods (data-collection approach), sampling (asking a subset/group for input), and statistical inference (generalizing sample results to the larger population)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Measures of success","Criteria or standards used in evaluating proposed solutions to the problem."],["Constraints","In a decision, the restrictions placed on potential solutions to a problem."],["Data","The facts and figures related to the project, divided into secondary data and primary data."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 3 — Collect relevant information"},{"t":"p","x":"**Data **split into two broad types:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Definition","Examples"],["Secondary data","Facts/figures already recorded prior to this project.","Internal: marketing input data (budgets, sales-call reports), marketing outcome data (actual sales, customer communications). External: Census Bureau reports, trade association studies, business periodicals, syndicated panels (e.g., Nielsen TV ratings, J.D. Power)."],["Primary data","Facts/figures newly collected specifically for this project.","Observational data, questionnaire data, and other primary sources (social media, panels, experiments)."]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Secondary vs. primary data — trade-offs\n• Secondary data — Advantages: time savings, inexpensive. Disadvantages: can be out of date, definitions/categories may not fit, may not be specific enough.\n• Primary data — Advantages: flexible, specific to the problem at hand. Disadvantages: costly, time-consuming."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Information technology includes all of the computing resources that collect, store and analyze data."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Primary data — watching people (observational)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Mechanical methods: **e.g., Nielsen's People Meter, worn like a watch in sampled households, transmitting viewing data nightly."},{"t":"li","x":"**Personal methods: **mystery shoppers, direct observation, ethnographic research (observing consumers in their natural environment)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Neuromarketing: **technologies used to study brain responses to marketing stimuli."},{"t":"li","x":"**Observational data** — the facts and figures obtained by watching how people actually behave, using mechanical, personal or neuromarketing data collection methods."},{"t":"p","x":"Nielsen People Meter details: a device worn like a watch in about 42,000 households that stores and transmits information to Nielsen every night; Nielsen's TV ratings systems cover 90+ countries. Figure 8-3 ranks top primetime shows (rating = percentage of TV homes tuned in)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Primary data — asking people (questionnaires)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Idea generation methods **(coming up with ideas): individual/depth interviews, focus groups (a facilitator guides ~8–12 consumers through ~10 guided questions in an informal, qualitative session; often used by brand marketers and sometimes quoted in advertising, e.g., \"99% of customers agreed...\")."},{"t":"li","x":"**Idea evaluation methods **(testing an idea): personal interview surveys, mail surveys, telephone interviews, online surveys, mall-intercept interviews."},{"t":"li","x":"**Questionnaire data** — the facts and figures obtained by asking people about their attitudes, awareness, intentions and behaviors."},{"t":"p","x":"Idea generation also uses the \"fuzzy front end\" of product development and trend hunting (for example Trend Hunter)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Question formats (know these for survey-design questions)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Format","Example use"],["Open-ended","\"What do you like about this product?\" — free text response."],["Closed-ended / dichotomous","Yes/No or two-option response."],["Multiple-choice","Choose one from several fixed options."],["Attitudinal","Rate agreement/feeling toward a statement."],["Semantic differential scale","Rate between two bipolar adjectives (e.g., Cheap … Expensive)."],["Likert scale","Rate agreement on a numeric scale (e.g., Strongly Disagree → Strongly Agree)."],["Media behavior","Which media/channels the respondent uses."],["Usage behavior","How often/how much the respondent uses the product."],["Demographic","Age, gender, income, etc."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Other formats in the Wendy's survey (Figure 8-4): Likert scale (Q6), media behavior question (Q7), usage behavior question (Q8), demographic question (Q9). Questions must be precise to obtain clear, unambiguous answers."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Other primary sources, big data, and analytics"},{"t":"li","x":"**Social media listening **can surface ideas for new products/services."},{"t":"li","x":"**Panel: **a sample of consumers measured repeatedly over time."},{"t":"li","x":"**Experiment: **manipulates an independent variable (the cause) to observe effects on a dependent variable (the result); **test markets **apply this in small real-world geographies before a full rollout."},{"t":"li","x":"**Big data **— extremely large data sets requiring sophisticated storage/analysis to reveal patterns; **data mining **examines large databases to find statistical relationships between purchasing patterns and marketing actions; **predictive modeling **uses data mining and probability analysis to forecast outcomes."},{"t":"li","x":"**Data visualization **presents analysis results for easy interpretation, feeding an \"intelligent enterprise\" that systematically converts data into decision-ready information."},{"t":"li","x":"**Data mining** — the practice of examining large databases to find statistical relationships between consumer purchasing patterns and marketing actions."},{"t":"li","x":"**Predictive modeling** — statistical models that use data mining and probability analysis to foretell outcomes; predictors (variables) affect the likelihood of future results."},{"t":"p","x":"Applying marketing metrics: are the Carmex social media programs working? Metrics to examine: conversation velocity, Facebook fans, Twitter followers, share of voice, sentiment."},{"t":"p","x":"Making responsible decisions: sophisticated data mining, with data collected through tracking devices such as cookies and apps, reveals personal information about consumers and enables personalization and targeting, so a person's digital life is transparent."},{"t":"p","x":"Advantages of primary data are flexibility and specificity to the problem; disadvantages are cost and time."},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing input data are the efforts expended to make sales; marketing outcome data are the results of the marketing effort. External sources also include the U.S. Census (2020 Census, American Community Survey, 2022 Economic Census, NAICS), trade association studies, universities and internet-based reports. Online resources useful to marketers include LexisNexis, The Wall Street Journal, CNBC, U.S. Census Bureau, Bureau of Economic Analysis, USA.gov and Google."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 4 — Develop findings"},{"t":"p","x":"Analyze the collected data — e.g., using cross-tabulation (relating two variables, such as age of head-of-household against frequency of fast-food patronage) to surface patterns that explain WHY results look the way they do (not just WHAT the results are)."},{"t":"p","x":"Data analysis example: Tony's Pizza. Questions: how are sales doing on a household basis; are fewer households buying, or is each household buying less, or both; what factors explain very flat sales over four years? The marketing dashboards (Figure 8-7) present findings that lead to recommendations. Figure 8-6 shows two forms of a cross tabulation of age of head of household against frequency of fast-food patronage."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 5 — Take marketing action"},{"t":"li","x":"Make action recommendations based on the findings."},{"t":"li","x":"Implement the recommendations."},{"t":"li","x":"Evaluate the results — both the decision itself (did we choose correctly?) and the decision process (was our process for deciding sound?)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Sales Forecasting Techniques"},{"t":"p","x":"**Sales forecast **— the total sales of a product a firm expects to sell in a specified period, under specified environmental conditions and its own marketing efforts. Three main technique families:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Family","Technique","How it works"],["Judgments of the decision maker","Direct forecast","Estimate the value directly, without intervening steps."],["Judgments of the decision maker","Lost-horse forecast","Start from the last known value and list factors that could change it going forward."],["Surveys of knowledgeable groups","Survey of buyers' intentions","Ask prospective customers how likely they are to buy."],["Surveys of knowledgeable groups","Salesforce survey forecast","Ask the sales team to estimate likely sales."],["Statistical methods","Trend extrapolation (incl. linear trend extrapolation)","Extend a past pattern (e.g., a straight-line trend) into the future."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Linear trend extrapolation applies when the past pattern is a straight line (Figure 8-8, Procter & Gamble sales revenues)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Sales forecast** — the total sales of a product that a firm expects to sell during a specified time period under specified environmental conditions and its own marketing efforts."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Cases and activities"},{"t":"p","x":"Video case 8, Golin: using data analytics to understand Walmart shoppers. Questions: the role of marketing research and data analytics in an agency like Golin; how Golin and Walmart defined the problem; how Golin used the customer journey to move customers from \"need\" to \"want\" (the data pointed to an opportunity at the affinity stage); what actions Walmart took."},{"t":"p","x":"In-class activities: 8-1 interpreting Census data (how census data are collected, locating data, sizing market segments, for example children under 5 for a preschool coloring book); 8-2 websites for marketers; 8-3 designing a taste-test survey for Howlin' Coyote Chili; 8-4 Pepsi versus Coke taste test."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 8"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A firm wants to test whether adding a new topping to a menu item CAUSES higher average spend per customer, by running a controlled trial in two comparable stores. This is an example of:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Exploratory research"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Descriptive research"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Causal research"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Secondary research"},{"t":"a","x":"(C) Causal research. A controlled trial designed to test a cause-and-effect relationship (topping → spend) is the definition of causal research."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which of the following is an example of SECONDARY data?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) A brand-new online survey the firm just designed and fielded"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) A focus group the firm just conducted"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) A government census report published two years ago"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) A mystery-shopper visit the firm arranged this week"},{"t":"a","x":"(C). A previously published government report is secondary data — it was collected before and for a different purpose. (A), (B), and (D) are all newly collected specifically for this firm's current needs, i.e., primary data."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Explain the difference between a focus group and an online survey as data-collection methods, including when a marketer would prefer one over the other."},{"t":"a","x":"A focus group is a qualitative method: a facilitator guides 8–12 consumers through open discussion, producing rich, in-depth but non-generalizable insights — useful early on, for idea generation or exploring WHY consumers feel a certain way. An online survey is typically quantitative: it reaches a much larger, more statistically representative sample with structured, closed-ended questions, producing generalizable numeric results (e.g., \"62% would buy this flavor\") but less depth of understanding. A marketer would use a focus group early (exploratory, idea generation) and an online survey later (idea evaluation, to quantify demand across a larger population)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"A start-up has almost no sales history but wants to forecast next year's demand for a brand-new snack product. Which sales-forecasting technique family is most appropriate, and why? Name one specific technique from that family."},{"t":"a","x":"With no sales history, statistical trend extrapolation cannot be used (there is no past pattern to extend). The most appropriate family is surveys of knowledgeable groups — specifically a survey of buyers' intentions, asking a sample of prospective customers how likely they would be to buy the new snack, since this does not require historical company sales data."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"List the five steps of the marketing research approach in order, in one line each."},{"t":"a","x":"(1) Define the problem — set objectives and choose the research type; (2) Develop the research plan — specify constraints and data needed; (3) Collect relevant information — gather primary and/or secondary data; (4) Develop findings — analyze the data; (5) Take marketing action — recommend, implement, and evaluate."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Applied mini-case)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Mini-case: Launching a new bubble-tea flavor\n• A Hong Kong bubble-tea chain wants to decide whether to permanently add a new \"yuzu brown sugar\" flavor to its menu. Management is unsure how big the potential demand is and which age group would like it most."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Design a short marketing research plan using the five-step approach: (a) state the problem and ONE research objective, (b) identify one relevant constraint, (c) recommend ONE primary data-collection method and justify it, (d) state what a supportive finding might look like, and (e) state ONE possible marketing action if the research confirms strong demand."},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Problem: uncertain demand and target age group for a new \"yuzu brown sugar\" flavor. Objective: estimate the % of existing customers who would purchase the new flavor at least once a month, broken down by age group. (b) Constraint: limited marketing research budget/time before the next menu review cycle. (c) Recommended method: an online or in-store intercept survey with a taste-test component — appropriate because it can reach a reasonably large, representative sample of current customers quickly and cheaply relative to a full focus-group program, while still capturing actual reaction to the product (via the taste test) rather than only stated intent. (d) Supportive finding example: \"45% of surveyed customers aged 18–34 say they would buy the flavor at least twice a month, versus 15% of customers aged 35+.\" (e) Marketing action: launch the flavor as a limited-time offer targeted at the 18–34 segment via social media promotion, then use actual sales data to decide on permanent addition to the menu."}]},"9":{"title":"Market Segmentation, Targeting, and Positioning (STP)","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Week 6 · Learning outcome: Explain how attractive market segments are identified and products are positioned for competitive advantage."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Why this chapter matters for your Group Project\n• STP is the analytical backbone of the marketing-plan presentation you must deliver for the Group Project — segmentation, targeting, and a perceptual map / positioning statement are explicitly required deliverables."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain what market segmentation is and when to use it."},{"t":"p","x":"• Identify the five steps involved in segmenting and targeting markets."},{"t":"p","x":"• Recognize the bases used to segment consumer and organizational (business) markets."},{"t":"p","x":"• Develop a market-product grid to identify a target market and recommend resulting marketing actions."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain how marketing managers position products in the marketplace."},{"t":"p","x":"Opening case: Zappos segments the world of soles. Its purpose is to live and deliver WOW; a new segment is customers with disabilities, and its segmentation strategy rests on speed and best price."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Why Segment Markets?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Market segmentation **involves aggregating prospective buyers into groups (segments) that (1) have common needs and (2) will respond similarly to a marketing action. Firms then use **product differentiation **— distinct marketing mix actions — to help consumers see the product as different and better than competitors' offerings."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Three segmentation strategies"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Strategy","Description","Example"],["One product, multiple segments","A single, largely unchanged product is marketed to several different segments.","Books and magazines."],["Multiple products, multiple segments","Different product variants are tailored to different segments.","Automobile model line-ups."],["Segments of one (mass customization)","Build-to-order only when there is an actual order.","Custom-configured products/services."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The segmentation trade-off"},{"t":"li","x":"**Organizational synergy **— a better-functioning organization results from serving multiple segments well."},{"t":"li","x":"**Cannibalization **— risk of a firm's new segment/offer stealing sales from its own existing offer."},{"t":"li","x":"The \"Tiffany/Walmart\" strategy — deliberately selling to both a high-end AND a low-end segment simultaneously, accepting the cannibalization risk for the reach it provides."},{"t":"p","x":"Segment markets when there is an opportunity for increased returns. Segmentation links market needs to the organization's marketing program through marketing mix actions (Figure 9-1). The trade-off is synergies versus cannibalization."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. The Five Steps in Segmenting and Targeting Markets"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 1 — Group potential buyers into segments"},{"t":"p","x":"Good segments should satisfy several criteria: simplicity and cost-effectiveness to identify; potential for increased profit; similarity of needs of buyers WITHIN a segment; difference of needs of buyers ACROSS segments; and the practical potential of a marketing action to actually reach the segment."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Basis (consumer markets)","What it captures","Example variables"],["Geographic","Where customers live or work","Country, region, city, climate"],["Demographic","Objective, measurable classification","Age, gender, income, education, family size"],["Psychographic","Subjective lifestyle/personality attributes","Values, activities, interests, opinions (VALS)"],["Behavioral","Observable actions","Usage rate, brand loyalty, occasion, benefits sought"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["The 80/20 rule and usage rate\n• Usage rate = the quantity consumed or store visits during a specific period; frequency marketing focuses on rewarding usage rate.\n• 80/20 rule = roughly 80% of a firm's sales/profit typically come from 20% of its customers — a core justification for heavy-user targeting."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Organizational (B2B) markets are segmented similarly but with adapted variables: geographic (statistical area), demographic (by industry/NAICS code, by number of employees), and behavioral (usage rate). Marketers also build personas — fictional character descriptions that bring a target segment's data to life (\"a day in the life of...\")."},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing Insights, which flock do you belong to? Nielsen PRIZM classifies 68 distinct neighborhood segments and identifies lifestyle and purchase behavior."},{"t":"p","x":"Usage rate in practice (Figures 9-4 and 9-5, MRI-Simmons): fast-food patronage shows the critical importance of attracting heavy and medium users."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Customer lifetime value (CLV)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Customer lifetime value (CLV)** — the financial worth of a customer to a company over the course of the relationship. Customer patronage has long-term financial consequences, so companies pursue continuing relationships with customers."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Company","Revenue/year","Cost of service","$ margin","Retention rate","Discount rate","CLV"],["McDonald's","$312","$147","$165","88%","4%","$908"],["Burger King","$225","$95","$130","73%","7%","$279"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Wendy's running example, step 1: variables used are geographic, demographic and psychographic. Four student segments (living in dorms, sororities and fraternities; living in apartments; day commuters living outside the area; night commuters living outside the area) and three nonstudent segments (faculty and staff working at the university; residents in the area not connected to the university; workers in the area not connected to the university)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 2 — Group products to be sold into categories"},{"t":"p","x":"Group the firm's individual products/offerings into meaningful categories (e.g., a restaurant grouping menu items into breakfast, lunch, dinner, snacks) so they can be matched against market segments in the next step."},{"t":"p","x":"Wendy's example: individual products are grouped into meals (breakfast, lunch, between-meal snack, dinner, after-dinner snack). Wendy's new products and innovations target segments by gender, needs or university affiliation (Figure 9-6)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 3 — Develop a market-product grid and estimate market sizes"},{"t":"p","x":"**A market-product grid **is a framework relating market segments (rows) to products (columns), with each cell estimating the size of that segment's demand for that product (e.g., on a simple 0–3 scale from no market to large market). This is the analytical tool behind the Group Project's requirement to identify \"4 types of segmentation\" and connect them to specific product/marketing actions."},{"t":"p","x":"In the grid, market segments are the rows and products are the columns; estimated market size ranges from no market (0) to large market (3). The pillow example (Figure 9-2) shows side sleepers as the dominant segment, preferring firm pillows. Wendy's grid next to an urban university is Figure 9-7."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 4 — Select target markets"},{"t":"p","x":"Two categories of criteria come into play: those used to divide the market into segments (above) and those used to actually pick which segment(s) to target:"},{"t":"li","x":"Market size"},{"t":"li","x":"Expected growth"},{"t":"li","x":"Competitive position"},{"t":"li","x":"Cost of reaching the segment"},{"t":"li","x":"Compatibility with the organization's objectives and resources"},{"t":"p","x":"Wendy's decision: no breakfast (market too small and not compatible with the organization's resources); focus on the four student segments only; do not focus on the three nonstudent segments."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 5 — Take marketing actions to reach target markets"},{"t":"p","x":"Translate the chosen target segment(s) into concrete marketing-mix actions (e.g., specific promotions timed to a segment's behavior), while continuously watching competitor moves and market shifts. This is also where Customer Lifetime Value (CLV) becomes relevant — CLV represents the financial worth of a customer to a company over the life of the relationship, and helps justify how much a firm should be willing to spend to acquire/retain a given segment."},{"t":"p","x":"Wendy's immediate actions: day commuters get ads in buses and signs near parking lots; between-meal snacks are promoted in the restaurant's downtime (10 percent off purchases between 2:00 and 4:30 p.m.); dinners go to night commuters (free Frosty with a meal between 5:00 and 8:00 p.m.)."},{"t":"p","x":"Keep an eye on competition: McDonald's, growing hamburger chains (for example Five Guys), \"fast casual\" and non-hamburger chains. Future strategy must consider what headquarters is doing, what competitors are doing and what might change in the area. Wendy's new aggressive marketing includes the \"Global Next Gen\" restaurant (pickup window, dedicated mobile-order shelving, integrated digital technology), a dynamic pricing initiative with an AI-enabled digital menu, and more recycled content."},{"t":"p","x":"Apple's ever-changing segmentation (the \"Apple Product Matrix\"): two types of products (desktops and laptops) and two market segments (consumer and professionals). Market-product synergies are a balancing act between marketing synergies and product synergies; Apple targeted lines of computers at specific segments (\"Camp Runamok no longer\")."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Positioning the Product"},{"t":"p","x":"**Product positioning **is the place a product occupies in consumers' minds, based on important attributes, relative to competing products. **Product repositioning **means deliberately changing that place in the consumer's mind."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Two approaches to positioning"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Approach","Logic"],["Head-to-head positioning","Compete directly against similar products on the same key attributes."],["Differentiation positioning","Seek a less-competitive niche by emphasizing attributes competitors don't own."]]},{"t":"p","x":"A positioning statement is derived from the company's customer value proposition and should clearly state the target segment, the category, and the key point(s) of difference."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Perceptual maps — how to build one"},{"t":"li","x":"Identify the important attributes for the product/brand class (choose two for a 2-D map — these become the X and Y axes)."},{"t":"li","x":"Discover how customers rate competing products/brands on those attributes."},{"t":"li","x":"Discover where customers currently place the company's own product/brand on those attributes."},{"t":"li","x":"Decide whether/how to reposition the company's product in consumers' minds (e.g., moving it toward an attractive, less crowded space on the map)."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Group Project reminder\n• Your Group Project explicitly requires a perceptual map — pick two attributes that are meaningful for your product/market (e.g., Price vs. Perceived Quality, or Traditional vs. Innovative), plot your brand and 2–4 key competitors, and use the map to justify your positioning statement."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Example: dairies reposition chocolate milk for adults. Identify the important attributes for adult drinks, discover how adults see competing drinks, discover how customers see chocolate milk, then reposition chocolate milk to make it more appealing to adults (Figure 9-9). A blank perceptual map has Attribute 1 on the X axis and Attribute 2 on the Y axis."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Market segmentation","Aggregating prospective buyers into groups, or segments, that (1) have common needs and (2) will respond similarly to a marketing action."],["Product differentiation","A marketing strategy in which a firm uses different marketing mix actions to help consumers perceive the product as different and better than competing products."],["Market-product grid","A framework to relate the market segments of potential buyers to products offered or potential marketing actions."],["Usage rate","The quantity consumed or patronage (store visits) during a specific period. Frequency marketing focuses on usage rates."],["80/20 rule","A concept suggesting that 80 percent of a firm's sales are obtained from 20 percent of its customers."],["Personas","Character descriptions of a brand's typical customers; they bring target-market data alive through fictional character narratives."],["Product positioning","The place a product occupies in consumers' minds based on important attributes relative to competitive products."],["Product repositioning","Changing the place a product occupies in a consumer's mind relative to competitive products."],["Perceptual map","A means of displaying in two dimensions the location of products or brands in the minds of consumers, so a manager can see how they perceive competing products and the firm's own product."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Cases and activities"},{"t":"p","x":"Video case 9, Prince Sports: tennis racquets for every segment. Prince segments racquets as Performance (Precision for touring professionals wanting feel, control and spin; Thunder for competitive players wanting a bigger sweet spot and power), Recreational (small head size for control; larger head size for a bigger sweet spot) and Junior (more experienced players aged 8 to 15 with shorter, lighter racquets; beginners aged 5 to 11 with much shorter racquets and slower balls). Case questions: environmental trends for and against Prince; marketing activities to promote tennis in the U.S.; promotions for recreational and junior players; distribution through mass merchandisers (Target, Walmart) and specialty shops; criteria for choosing countries to market aggressively and actions to reach them."},{"t":"p","x":"In-class activity 9-1, Honey Nut Cheerios Milk 'n Cereal Bar: how different people categorize the same objects, why, and why categorization matters for identifying market segments and competitors. In-class activity 9-2, 3M Post-it Flag + Highlighter: suggest consumer and retailer benefits and compose a positioning statement linked to 3M's branding strategy."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 9"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Segmenting customers by \"heavy users who buy 3+ times per week\" versus \"light users who buy once a month\" is an example of which segmentation basis?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Geographic"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Demographic"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Psychographic"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Behavioral"},{"t":"a","x":"(D) Behavioral. Usage rate/frequency of purchase is an observable behavior, the defining trait of behavioral segmentation."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A new energy-drink brand chooses to compete directly against Red Bull on the same \"high-energy, high-performance\" attributes valued by the same core segment. This is:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Head-to-head positioning"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Differentiation positioning"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Market development"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Product repositioning"},{"t":"a","x":"(A) Head-to-head positioning. Competing on the SAME attributes for the SAME core segment as an established rival is the definition of head-to-head positioning (as opposed to seeking a distinct, less-contested space, which would be differentiation positioning)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Explain \"cannibalization\" and give an original example of a brand risking cannibalization by serving two very different segments (a \"Tiffany/Walmart\"-style strategy)."},{"t":"a","x":"Cannibalization is the risk that a firm's own new product/segment/channel steals sales from its EXISTING product/segment/channel rather than generating fully incremental sales. Example: a premium restaurant group launching a budget fast-casual sub-brand risks cannibalizing its own premium restaurant's price-sensitive customers, who might trade down to the cheaper brand instead of buying fully new incremental volume — mirroring the classic \"Tiffany/Walmart\" trade-off of serving both a high-end and a low-end segment under one corporate umbrella."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"List the five criteria used to select which segments to actually target (Step 4), and briefly explain ONE of them."},{"t":"a","x":"(1) Market size, (2) expected growth, (3) competitive position, (4) cost of reaching the segment, (5) compatibility with the organization's objectives and resources. Example explanation: \"cost of reaching the segment\" matters because even a large, fast-growing, attractive segment is not worth targeting if the media/channels needed to reach it are prohibitively expensive relative to the expected return."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"What is a market-product grid, and what specific business question does it help answer?"},{"t":"a","x":"A market-product grid is a matrix that places market segments in rows and products/offerings in columns, with each cell estimating the size of demand that segment represents for that specific product. It helps answer the business question: \"Which combinations of WHO we sell to and WHAT we sell them represent the biggest, most attractive opportunities — and where should we focus (or not focus) our limited marketing resources?\""},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Applied mini-case — Group Project practice)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Mini-case: Positioning Neo's Home Robot for Hong Kong\n• Using the Group Project's Option 1 scenario: Neo's Home Robot (a general-purpose home humanoid robot) is to be introduced into the Hong Kong market."]]},{"t":"p","x":"(a) Propose ONE demographic and ONE psychographic segment that could be a viable target market in Hong Kong for this product, with a one-sentence rationale for each. (b) Write a short positioning statement for Neo's Home Robot aimed at your chosen target segment. (c) Suggest two attributes you would use as the X and Y axes of a perceptual map for this product category, and briefly justify your choice."},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Demographic segment — affluent dual-income households with young children or elderly dependents in HK (household income above a set threshold), since they have both the financial means and a plausible functional need (childcare/eldercare support, household help) for a home robot. Psychographic segment — HK's \"early adopter\" tech-enthusiast segment: consumers who value innovation, status from owning cutting-edge technology, and convenience, regardless of household composition. (b) Example positioning statement: \"For busy, tech-forward Hong Kong households, Neo's Home Robot is the home robot that gives you back time and peace of mind, by reliably handling everyday household tasks — unlike ordinary smart-home gadgets, Neo is a genuine physical helper in your home.\" (c) Suggested axes: X = Price (Affordable ↔ Premium) and Y = Perceived Capability/Autonomy (Basic assistant ↔ Fully autonomous helper) — these two attributes are likely to be the main dimensions HK consumers use to compare a novel, high-involvement product category like home robots, letting the team visually show whether Neo should be positioned as a premium, highly capable option versus cheaper, more limited smart-home devices."}]},"10":{"title":"Developing New Products and Services","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Recognize the terms that pertain to products and services."},{"t":"p","x":"• Identify the ways consumer and business products and services can be classified."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain the significance of “newness” in terms of the degree of consumer learning involved."},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe the factors contributing to the success or failure of a new product or service."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain the purpose of each step of the new-product development process."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"li","x":"**Product** — a good, service or idea consisting of a bundle of tangible and intangible attributes that satisfies consumers’ needs and is received in exchange for money or something else of value."},{"t":"li","x":"**Services** — intangible activities or benefits that an organization provides to satisfy consumers’ needs in exchange for money or something else of value."},{"t":"li","x":"**Consumer products** — products purchased by the ultimate consumer."},{"t":"li","x":"**Convenience products** — items the consumer purchases frequently, conveniently and with minimum shopping effort."},{"t":"li","x":"**Shopping products** — items for which the consumer compares several alternatives on criteria such as price, quality or style."},{"t":"li","x":"**Specialty products** — items a consumer makes a special effort to search out and buy."},{"t":"li","x":"**Unsought products** — items the consumer either does not know about, or knows about but does not initially want."},{"t":"li","x":"**Business products** — products organizations buy that assist in providing other products for resale (B2B or industrial products)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Product item** — a specific product with a unique brand, size or price (stock-keeping unit, SKU)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Product line** — a group of items closely related because they satisfy a class of needs, are used together, are sold to the same customer group, through the same outlets, or within a given price range."},{"t":"li","x":"**Product mix** — all of the product lines offered by an organization."},{"t":"li","x":"**Protocol** — a statement made before development begins that identifies a well-defined target market, customers’ needs, wants and preferences, and what the product will be and do."},{"t":"li","x":"**Open innovation** — practices and processes that encourage the use of external and internal ideas and collaboration when conceiving, producing and marketing new products."},{"t":"li","x":"**New-product development process** — the seven stages an organization goes through to identify opportunities and convert them into salable products or services."},{"t":"li","x":"**Commercialization** — the stage that positions and launches a new product in full-scale production and sales."},{"t":"p","x":"Opening case: Apple, a world-class new-product machine. Successes: Apple II (1977), Macintosh (1984), iPod (2001), iPhone (2007), iPad (2010) and iPad Mini (2012), CarPlay (2014), Apple Watch (2015), AirPods (2017), HomePod (2018), AirTags (2020), Apple Vision headset (2024). Stumbles: Apple III (1980), Lisa (1983), Newton (1987), Macintosh Portable (1989), the \"Hockey Puck\" mouse (1998), Project Titan car (2015 to 2024)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. What Are Products and Services?"},{"t":"p","x":"• Products include goods (nondurable and durable), services and ideas (a thought that leads to action)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Product class is the industry to which a product belongs; product forms are the different versions within the class (recorded music: records, tapes, CDs, MP3s); a product item is a SKU; a product line is a group of closely related items; the product mix is all the lines of the firm."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Classifying consumer products"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Basis","Convenience","Shopping","Specialty","Unsought"],["Examples","Toothpaste, cake mix, hand soap, ATM withdrawal","Cameras, TVs, briefcases, airline tickets","Rolls-Royce cars, Rolex watches, heart surgery","Burial insurance, thesaurus"],["Price","Relatively inexpensive","Fairly expensive","Usually very expensive","Varies"],["Place","Widespread, many outlets","Large number of selective outlets","Very limited","Often limited"],["Promotion","Price, availability and awareness stressed","Differentiation from competitors stressed","Uniqueness of brand and status stressed","Awareness is essential"],["Brand loyalty","Aware of brand but accept substitutes","Prefer specific brands but accept substitutes","Very brand loyal; no substitutes","Accept substitutes"],["Purchase behavior","Frequent; little time and effort","Infrequent; much comparison shopping","Infrequent; extensive search and decision time","Very infrequent; some comparison shopping"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Figure 10-1 compares consumer products on price, place, promotion, brand loyalty and purchase behavior. Convenience: relatively inexpensive, widespread distribution, price/availability/awareness stressed, accept substitutes, frequent purchases with little effort. Shopping: fairly expensive, selective outlets, differentiation stressed, prefer specific brands but accept substitutes, infrequent purchases with much comparison. Specialty: usually very expensive, very limited distribution, uniqueness and status stressed, very brand loyal and accept no substitutes, extensive search. Unsought: price varies, often limited distribution, awareness essential, accept substitutes, very infrequent purchases."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Classifying business products and services"},{"t":"p","x":"• Business product sales result from derived demand (demand that comes from demand for consumer products)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Components are items that become part of the final product. Support products are used to produce other products: installations (buildings, equipment), accessory equipment (tools, office equipment), supplies (stationery, paper clips, brooms) and industrial services (maintenance, repair, legal services)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Services are classified by whether they are delivered by people or equipment, by for-profit or nonprofit organizations, or by government agencies."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. New Products: What Is “New” and Why Do They Succeed or Fail?"},{"t":"p","x":"• Newness compared with existing products: a product is new if it is functionally different; revolutionary newness can create new industries (smartphones and apps). Feature bloat (unnecessary features) and feature fatigue (complex products that do not maximize satisfaction) are risks."},{"t":"p","x":"• Newness from the consumer’s perspective (degree of learning needed): continuous innovation (no new behavior to learn), dynamically continuous innovation (minor behavior changes) and discontinuous innovation (new consumption patterns must be learned)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Newness in legal terms: “new” can be used only for six months after regular distribution."},{"t":"p","x":"• Newness from the organization’s perspective: product line extension (improvement to a product line), brand extension (existing brand name on a new product) and jump in innovation (radical invention)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Success is hard: most consumers buy the same 150 items consistently, and fewer than 3 percent of new consumer packaged goods exceed first-year sales of $50 million."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Marketing reasons for new-product failure (8)"},{"t":"p","x":"• Insignificant point of difference. • Incomplete market and product protocol. • Failure to satisfy customer needs on critical factors. • Bad timing. • No economical access to buyers. • Poor execution of the marketing mix. • Too little market attractiveness. • Poor product quality."},{"t":"p","x":"• Organizational inertia also causes failure: groupthink, and the not-invented-here (NIH) problem. Open innovation counters it by using external and internal ideas."},{"t":"p","x":"• Tracking performance: the New Product Vitality Index (used by 62 percent of large companies) measures the share of revenue from new products; an index between 20 and 30 percent is considered “good”, and it has fallen below that in recent years."},{"t":"p","x":"Class exercise: identify the product class, form, item, line and mix for Crapola! Granola. Feature bloat and fatigue are discussed in Marketing Matters, \"Too much of a good thing\"."},{"t":"p","x":"Google Glass (Marketing Matters): augmented reality glasses whose prototypes were sold to 8,000 qualified \"Glass Explorers\" for $1,500; incomplete testing, the \"Glassholes\" image and withdrawal from the market; the Glass Enterprise Edition 2 also failed."},{"t":"p","x":"Applying marketing metrics: the New Product Vitality Index has fallen below the \"good\" 20 to 30 percent range over the past seven years; what should be done?"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. The New-Product Development Process (7 stages)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Stage","What happens","Examples and tools"],["1. New-product strategy development","Defines the role of the new product in the firm’s overall objectives; protocol and strategic role set","SWOT analysis, environmental scanning; disruptive innovation can occur"],["2. Idea generation","Develops a pool of concepts, internally and externally","Employees, customers, suppliers, crowdsourcing, R&D labs (Apple industrial design, outside labs such as IDEO), competitors, universities, inventors, crowdfunding, generative AI"],["3. Screening and evaluation","Evaluates ideas and eliminates those that warrant no further effort","Internal: customer experience management (CEM); external: concept tests"],["4. Business analysis","Specifies product features and marketing strategy and makes financial projections","Prototype, business fit, financial projections, data analysis"],["5. Development","Turns the idea on paper into a prototype / demonstrable product","Lab and consumer tests"],["6. Market testing","Exposes the product to prospective consumers under realistic conditions","Standard, controlled and simulated test markets"],["7. Commercialization","Full-scale production, positioning and launch","Boeing 787 Dreamliner technical problems; Burger King fries too complicated to get right consistently"],["6. Market testing","Exposes the product to real buyers in test markets before full launch","Standard, controlled and simulated test markets"],["7. Commercialization","Positions and launches the product in full-scale production and sales","Boeing 787 Dreamliner had significant technical problems; Burger King french fries were too complicated to get right consistently"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Test markets: standard (sell through normal distribution in select cities), controlled (contract the test program to an outside service) and simulated (replicate a full-scale test market)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Cases: Apple is a world-class new-product machine (iPhone, iPad, AirPods) with stumbles (Newton, Project Titan car). Google Glass was withdrawn after incomplete testing and a negative image (“Glassholes”); prototypes had been sold to 8,000 “Glass Explorers” for $1,500 each. GoPro succeeded with exciting new products aimed at action-seeking users, supported by content marketing."},{"t":"p","x":"Idea generation sources also include employee and friend suggestions, customer and supplier suggestions, smaller nontraditional firms, and crowdfunding to gather online supporters. Class examples: Life Is Good, Tide Pods."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Cases and activities"},{"t":"p","x":"Video case 10, GoPro: making all of us heroes with exciting new products. Questions: GoPro's points of difference; its primary target markets and the influence of content marketing; GoPro's new-product development process compared with Figure 10-3; which of the eight reasons for failure GoPro avoided; a new-product idea GoPro should evaluate."},{"t":"p","x":"In-class activities: 10-1 focus group for a smartwatch (Pebble); 10-2 what were they thinking? analyzing new-product disasters (Body Smarts, Surge, Wheaties Dunk-A-Balls, Garlic Cake, Special K Plus, Dr. Care); 10-3 Method 6-3-5 to find new-product ideas for Magnetic Poetry."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-check"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can define product, product item, line and mix, and classify products as convenience, shopping, specialty or unsought, and business products as components or support products."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can classify an innovation as continuous, dynamically continuous or discontinuous, and separate line extension, brand extension and jump in innovation."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can list the eight marketing reasons for new-product failure and define the New Product Vitality Index."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can name the seven stages of new-product development in order and state what happens in each."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can name the three kinds of test markets."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1 (MCQ). A consumer buys a Rolex watch only after extensive search and will not accept a substitute. This is a: (a) convenience product; (b) shopping product; (c) specialty product; (d) unsought product."},{"t":"p","x":"Q2 (MCQ). Which stage of the new-product process builds a prototype and includes lab and consumer tests? (a) Business analysis; (b) Development; (c) Market testing; (d) Commercialization."},{"t":"p","x":"Q3. A software firm adds a new color option to an existing app, a second firm adds a feature that requires users to learn gestures, and a third launches a product that requires an entirely new way of consuming. Classify each as continuous, dynamically continuous or discontinuous innovation."},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. A beverage company puts its famous brand name on a new energy bar. A cosmetics firm adds a new shade to an existing foundation line. A lab produces a radically new battery. Classify each from the organization’s perspective."},{"t":"p","x":"Q5. Explain, with an example, the difference between a standard test market, a controlled test market and a simulated test market."},{"t":"p","x":"Q6. A firm launches a new snack that is nearly identical to existing brands, at the same price, in a market where retailers will not give shelf space. Which of the eight reasons for new-product failure apply?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q7. Place these in order: commercialization, screening and evaluation, development, idea generation, market testing, new-product strategy development, business analysis. Then say which stage uses concept tests and which produces financial projections."},{"t":"p","x":"Q8. A shipping company sells forklifts to a factory. Explain why this is a business product, and why demand for the forklifts is derived demand. Give one example each of installations, accessory equipment, supplies and industrial services."},{"t":"a","x":"A1. (c) Specialty product: very brand loyal, no substitutes, extensive search and decision time, very limited distribution.\nA2. (b) Development turns the idea on paper into a prototype, tested in the lab and with consumers.\nA3. New color option: continuous innovation (no new behavior). Gesture feature: dynamically continuous innovation (minor behavior changes). Entirely new way of consuming: discontinuous innovation (new consumption patterns must be learned).\nA4. Brand name on a new product: brand extension. New shade added to an existing line: product line extension. Radically new battery: jump in innovation.\nA5. Standard test market: the product is sold through normal distribution channels in select cities. Controlled test market: the firm contracts an outside service to run the test program. Simulated test market: a test market is replicated (for example in a lab or on computer) without a full-scale launch.\nA6. Insignificant point of difference (nearly identical), no economical access to buyers (no shelf space), and likely too little market attractiveness or incomplete protocol.\nA7. Order: new-product strategy development, idea generation, screening and evaluation, business analysis, development, market testing, commercialization. Concept tests belong to screening and evaluation (external approach); financial projections belong to business analysis.\nA8. Forklifts are bought by an organization to help produce other goods, so they are a business product. Demand is derived because it depends on demand for the factory’s own products. Examples: installations (buildings, heavy equipment), accessory equipment (tools, office equipment), supplies (paper clips, brooms), industrial services (maintenance, repair, legal services)."}]},"11":{"title":"Managing Successful Products, Services, and Brands","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain the product life cycle concept."},{"t":"p","x":"• Identify ways marketing executives manage a product’s life cycle."},{"t":"p","x":"• Recognize the importance of branding and alternative branding strategies."},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe the role of packaging and labeling in the marketing of a product."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"li","x":"**Product life cycle** — the stages a new product goes through in the marketplace: introduction, growth, maturity and decline."},{"t":"li","x":"**Product class** — the entire product category or industry (prerecorded music)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Product form** — variations of a product within the product class (cassettes, CDs, streaming)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Product modification** — altering a product’s quality, performance or appearance to increase its value to customers and sales."},{"t":"li","x":"**Market modification** — finding new customers, increasing a product’s use among existing customers, or creating new use situations."},{"t":"li","x":"**Trading up** — adding value to a product or line through additional features or higher-quality materials."},{"t":"li","x":"**Trading down** — reducing a product’s number of features, quality or price."},{"t":"li","x":"**Branding** — using a name, phrase, design or symbol to identify products and distinguish them from competitors’."},{"t":"li","x":"**Brand personality** — a set of human characteristics associated with a brand name."},{"t":"li","x":"**Brand equity** — the added value a brand name gives a product beyond its functional benefits."},{"t":"li","x":"**Brand purpose** — the reason a brand exists and the role it plays in consumers’ lives and society."},{"t":"li","x":"**Brand licensing** — a contract in which a licensor lets a licensee use its brand name or trademark for a royalty or fee."},{"t":"li","x":"**Multiproduct branding** — using one name for all products in a product class."},{"t":"li","x":"**Multibranding** — giving each product a distinct name, each aimed at a different segment."},{"t":"li","x":"**Private branding** — a manufacturer sells its products under a wholesaler’s or retailer’s brand (private labeling, reseller branding)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Mixed branding** — a firm sells under its own name and under a reseller’s."},{"t":"li","x":"**Packaging** — any container in which a product is offered for sale and on which label information is conveyed."},{"t":"li","x":"**Label** — the part of the package that identifies the product or brand, its maker, use and contents."},{"t":"p","x":"Opening case: Gatorade, bringing science to sweat for 60 years. Concocted in 1965 at the University of Florida; a global multibillion-dollar brand sold in 80+ countries; official drink of NASCAR, NFL, MLB, NBA, NHL, MLS and WNBA. Continuous product development and brand repositioning (G Series, chews, bars, powders, shakes) for the serious athlete."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. The Product Life Cycle"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Marketing objective","Introduction: gain awareness","Growth: stress differentiation","Maturity: maintain brand loyalty","Decline: harvesting, deletion"],["Competition","Few","More","Many","Reduced"],["Product","One","More versions","Full product line","Best-sellers"],["Price","Skimming or penetration","Gain market share, deal","Defend market share, profit","Stay profitable"],["Place","Limited","More outlets","Maximum outlets","Fewer outlets"],["Promotion","Inform, educate","Stress points of difference","Reminder-oriented","Minimal promotion"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Introduction: stimulate trial; primary demand (for the product class) then selective demand (for a brand); skimming (high price) or penetration pricing (low price)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Growth: rapid sales growth, more competitors, profits peak, repeat purchasers, new features, broad distribution."},{"t":"p","x":"• Maturity: sales slow, few new buyers, profits decline, differentiation, fewer competitors (soft drinks, breakfast cereals)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Decline: sales and prices drop. Two strategies: deletion (drop the product) or harvesting (cut marketing costs)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Three aspects: length of the cycle, shape of the curve, and the generalized cycle. High-learning products need significant education; low-learning products need little; a fashion is a style of the times; a fad has a short life cycle."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Diffusion of innovations"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Adopter category","Share of population"],["Innovators","2.5%"],["Early adopters","13.5%"],["Early majority","34%"],["Late majority","34%"],["Laggards","16%"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Barriers to adoption: usage (compatibility with habits), value (incentive to change), risk (physical, economic, social) and psychological (culture or image). Electric cars: only 6% of 2022 sales, held back by usage, value (price premium) and risk (ownership cost) barriers; still in the introductory stage."},{"t":"p","x":"Figure 11-1 relates the stages to total industry sales and profits and to marketing objectives and mix actions. Introduction: advertising informs and educates; growth: advertising for selective demand. Maturity examples: soft drinks and breakfast cereals. The decline stage is also where product life extension is considered. Figure 11-2 shows the smartphone product life cycle (1995 to 2028e); Figure 11-4 shows prerecorded music life cycles by product form; Figure 11-3 shows alternative life-cycle curves by product type. Product class and product form must be distinguished: the life cycle of a form (cassettes, CDs, streaming) differs from that of the class (prerecorded music)."},{"t":"p","x":"Electric cars case (2022): only 6 percent of cars sold were all-electric. Usage barrier: consumers believe electric cars do not fit their driving habits. Value barrier: price premium over hybrid and gas cars. Risk barrier: uncertainty about ownership costs. Electric cars remain in the introductory stage, with a growth stage forecast for 2026 if the barriers can be overcome."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Managing the Product Life Cycle"},{"t":"p","x":"• The product or brand manager manages life-cycle stages, new-product development and marketing program implementation, using data such as the Category Development Index (CDI) and Brand Development Index (BDI)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Modifying the product (modification, bundling, new characteristics) or the market (new customers, increased use, new use situations)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Repositioning changes the place a product occupies in consumers’ minds: changing the value offered, trading up, trading down, downsizing, reacting to a competitor, reaching a new market, catching a rising trend."},{"t":"p","x":"• Downsizing / shrinkflation: less product in the same package at the same price (StarKist can: 6.5 oz to 6.125 oz). Is it ethical?"},{"t":"p","x":"Product modification includes product bundling and new characteristics. The product or brand manager's responsibilities cover the life-cycle stages, new-product development and marketing program implementation, with extensive data analysis (Category Development Index and Brand Development Index)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Branding and Brand Management"},{"t":"p","x":"• Brand equity gives a competitive advantage (consumers pay a premium) and a financial advantage (brands can be bought, sold and licensed)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Creating brand equity: develop positive awareness and association with a product class; establish the brand’s meaning; elicit the proper response; create intense brand loyalty (customer-based brand equity pyramid)."},{"t":"p","x":"• A good brand name suggests benefits; is memorable, distinctive and positive; fits the company or product image; has no legal restrictions; is simple and emotional; has favorable associations in other languages."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Strategy","Meaning","Example"],["Multiproduct branding","One name for all products in a class; includes line extensions, subbranding, brand extensions and co-branding","Corporate or family branding"],["Line extension","Brand name used to enter new markets in its product class","New flavor of an existing brand"],["Subbranding","Corporate brand combined with a new brand",""],["Brand extension","Current brand used to enter a different product class",""],["Co-branding","Two strong brands paired for mutual benefit",""],["Multibranding","Each product has a different name, often by price or quality","Different segments"],["Private branding","Manufacturer sells under the reseller’s name","Store brands"],["Mixed branding","Firm’s own brands plus a reseller’s brand","Reaches a different segment"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Branding questions cover the importance of brand name, symbols, logos and characters, and trademark. Marketing Insights: before starting a business with your own name, check whether it is already registered, since millions of brand and trade names exist and thousands are added each year. Brand licensing example: the NFL licenses team names and logos. Mixed branding is used because the segment attracted to the reseller differs from the firm's own market. Multibranding brands may be based on price and quality."},{"t":"p","x":"Strategies (Figure 11-7): students are recommended to apply at least two strategies in the individual work."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Packaging and Labeling"},{"t":"p","x":"• Packaging and labeling cost about $0.15 of every consumer dollar."},{"t":"p","x":"• Benefits: communication (information), functional (storage, convenience, protection) and perceptual (creates the brand in the consumer’s mind). Example: Pez (1,400 characters, 90 countries, 70 million dispensers)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Challenges: connecting with customers, environmental concerns (reduce waste), health, safety and security, shelf life, and cost reduction."},{"t":"p","x":"Pez: 1,400 characters, 90 countries, licensed character heads; consumers buy 70 million dispensers and 5 billion tablets a year, and Pez has not advertised for years. Shelf life is the time a product can be stored; cost reduction cuts costs while delivering value."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-check"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can describe the objectives and marketing mix of each life-cycle stage."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can distinguish product class from product form, and fad from fashion."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can name the five adopter categories with their shares and the four barriers to adoption."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can separate product modification, market modification and repositioning (trading up, trading down, downsizing)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can classify a brand as multiproduct, multibranded, private or mixed, and define brand equity."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can state three benefits of packaging."},{"t":"li","x":"**Brand name** — any word, device (design, shape, sound or color) or combination used to distinguish a seller's products or services."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Cases and activities"},{"t":"p","x":"Video case 11, Justin's: managing a successful product with passion. Questions: how passion, consciousness and product quality are part of product management; how the life cycle stage influences decisions at Hormel and Justin's; the roles of brand managers and master brand managers; Hormel's branding strategies and Justin's place in the line; the influence of sustainability and corporate responsibility."},{"t":"p","x":"In-class activity 11-1, Breathe Right nasal strips: brainstorming ground rules (quantity over quality, piggybacking allowed, stress creativity, no evaluation or criticism) and the N/3 technique (divide the number of ideas N by 3 to get each student's points; each idea receives 0, 1, 2 or 3 points; tally to find the leaders)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1 (MCQ). Sales slow, profits decline and competitors leave; the firm keeps only best-sellers and cuts promotion. At which life-cycle stage is it? (a) Maturity; (b) Decline; (c) Growth; (d) Introduction."},{"t":"p","x":"Q2. For each, name the stage: (i) competition is few, promotion informs and educates; (ii) maximum outlets, reminder-oriented promotion; (iii) profits peak and more versions appear."},{"t":"p","x":"Q3. Classify as product class or product form: prerecorded music, CDs, streaming, soft drinks, diet cola."},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. A style that is popular for a short time and then disappears, versus a style of the times that returns in cycles. Name each."},{"t":"p","x":"Q5. Name the five adopter categories in order with their percentages, and say which category a person who buys a new phone on release day belongs to."},{"t":"p","x":"Q6. A firm adds new features and a higher-quality material to a product. Another reduces features and price. Name each repositioning move."},{"t":"p","x":"Q7. Identify the branding strategy: (i) Toyota and Lexus names for different segments; (ii) a supermarket sells cereal made by a manufacturer under the supermarket’s name; (iii) the manufacturer also sells its own-brand cereal and the supermarket version; (iv) Gillette uses its name on razors, blades and shaving cream."},{"t":"p","x":"Q8. List the three benefits of packaging and give one example of each."},{"t":"a","x":"A1. (b) Decline: harvesting or deletion, best-sellers only, minimal promotion.\nA2. (i) Introduction. (ii) Maturity. (iii) Growth.\nA3. Product class: prerecorded music, soft drinks. Product form: CDs, streaming, diet cola.\nA4. A fad has a short life cycle; a fashion is a style of the times.\nA5. Innovators 2.5%, early adopters 13.5%, early majority 34%, late majority 34%, laggards 16%. The release-day buyer is an innovator.\nA6. Adding features and higher-quality materials: trading up. Reducing features and price: trading down.\nA7. (i) Multibranding. (ii) Private branding. (iii) Mixed branding. (iv) Multiproduct branding.\nA8. Communication (labels inform consumers), functional (protection, storage, convenience) and perceptual (creates the brand in the consumer’s mind, such as Pez character heads)."}]},"13":{"title":"Building the Price Foundation","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Name the elements of the pricing decision and its role in the marketing mix."},{"t":"p","x":"• Identify pricing objectives and constraints."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain how demand and price elasticity affect revenue."},{"t":"p","x":"• Compute total revenue, total cost and the break-even point."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"li","x":"**Price** — the money or other considerations (including other products and services) exchanged for the ownership or use of a product or service."},{"t":"li","x":"**Value** — the ratio of perceived benefits to price: Value = perceived benefits ÷ price."},{"t":"li","x":"**Value pricing** — simultaneously increasing product and service benefits while maintaining or decreasing price."},{"t":"li","x":"**Pricing objectives** — the role of price in an organization’s marketing and strategic plans."},{"t":"li","x":"**Pricing constraints** — factors that limit the range of prices a firm may set."},{"t":"li","x":"**Demand curve** — a graph relating the quantity sold and price; it shows the maximum number of units sold at a given price."},{"t":"li","x":"**Demand factors** — factors that determine consumers’ willingness and ability to pay: tastes, price and availability of similar products, and income."},{"t":"li","x":"**Price elasticity of demand** — the percentage change in quantity demanded relative to the percentage change in price."},{"t":"li","x":"**Total revenue (TR)** — the total money received from the sale of a product: TR = unit price × quantity sold."},{"t":"li","x":"**Total cost (TC)** — total expense of producing and marketing a product: TC = fixed cost + variable cost."},{"t":"li","x":"**Break-even analysis** — analyzing the relationship between total revenue and total cost to determine profitability at various levels of output."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Nature and Importance of Price"},{"t":"p","x":"• Price is where all other business decisions come together: customers must be willing to pay, it must cover operations, and it must earn profit."},{"t":"p","x":"• Price is what a consumer pays; value is what a consumer receives. As perceived benefit increases, value increases."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Pricing Objectives and Constraints"},{"t":"p","x":"• Constraints: competitors’ prices and consumers’ awareness and ability to compare; consumer-driven pricing actions; seller or retailer-driven actions; legal and ethical considerations."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Estimating Demand and Revenue"},{"t":"p","x":"• Demand factors: consumer tastes, price and availability of similar products, consumer income."},{"t":"p","x":"• A price change causes a movement along the demand curve; a change in a demand factor shifts the curve (Red Baron pizza example)."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Elasticity","Effect of a 1% price decrease","Effect on total revenue"],["Elastic","Quantity rises by more than 1%","Revenue rises when price falls"],["Inelastic","Quantity rises by less than 1%","Revenue falls when price falls"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Necessities are price inelastic. Elasticity affects marketing and public policy decisions."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Total Revenue, Total Cost and Break-Even"},{"t":"p","x":"• TR = P × Q. TC = FC + VC, with VC = unit variable cost × Q."},{"t":"p","x":"• Break-even quantity = fixed cost ÷ (unit price − unit variable cost). Unit contribution = price − unit variable cost."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-check"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can define price and value and compute Value = benefits ÷ price."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can list pricing constraints and the three demand factors."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can tell a movement along the demand curve from a shift."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can classify demand as elastic or inelastic and predict the revenue effect."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can compute TR, TC and the break-even quantity."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1. A product has perceived benefits rated 80 and a price of $20. A rival has benefits rated 90 and a price of $30. Compute the value ratio of each and say which offers better value."},{"t":"p","x":"Q2. Price falls by 10% and quantity demanded rises by 25%. Is demand elastic or inelastic? What happens to revenue?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q3. Price falls by 10% and quantity demanded rises by 4%. Elastic or inelastic? What happens to revenue?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. Consumer income rises and more pizza is bought at every price. Movement along the demand curve or shift?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q5. Fixed cost is $60,000, unit variable cost $12 and price $20. Compute the break-even quantity and the profit at 10,000 units."},{"t":"a","x":"A1. Firm 1: 80 ÷ 20 = 4.0. Rival: 90 ÷ 30 = 3.0. The first offers better value.\nA2. Elasticity = 25% ÷ 10% = 2.5, so elastic; total revenue rises when price falls.\nA3. Elasticity = 4% ÷ 10% = 0.4, so inelastic; total revenue falls when price falls.\nA4. A shift of the demand curve (a demand factor changed), not a movement along it.\nA5. Break-even = 60,000 ÷ (20 − 12) = 7,500 units. At 10,000 units: TR = 200,000; TC = 60,000 + 120,000 = 180,000; profit = 20,000."}]},"14":{"title":"Arriving at the Final Price","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Name the four approaches for selecting an approximate price level."},{"t":"p","x":"• Compute prices with markup, target profit and target return methods."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain special adjustments to the list price and legal aspects of pricing."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"li","x":"**Skimming pricing** — setting the highest initial price that customers who really desire the product will pay for a new or innovative product."},{"t":"li","x":"**Penetration pricing** — setting a low initial price on a new product to appeal immediately to the mass market."},{"t":"li","x":"**Prestige pricing** — a high price so that quality- or status-conscious consumers are attracted."},{"t":"li","x":"**Price lining** — pricing a line of products at a number of specific price points."},{"t":"li","x":"**Odd-even pricing** — setting prices a few dollars or cents under an even number ($999, not $1,000)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Target pricing** — estimating the price consumers would pay, working backward through retailer and wholesaler markups, and adjusting the product to hit that price."},{"t":"li","x":"**Bundle pricing** — two or more products sold in a single package price."},{"t":"li","x":"**Yield management pricing** — charging different prices to maximize revenue for a set amount of capacity."},{"t":"li","x":"**Target profit pricing** — setting an annual target of a specific dollar volume of profit."},{"t":"li","x":"**Target return-on-sales pricing** — setting a price to achieve a profit that is a specified percentage of sales volume."},{"t":"li","x":"**Target return-on-investment pricing** — setting a price to achieve an annual target ROI."},{"t":"li","x":"**Above-, at-, or below-market pricing** — using competitors’ or market prices as a benchmark."},{"t":"li","x":"**Fixed-price policy** — one price for all buyers (one-price policy)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Product line pricing** — setting prices for all items in a line to cover the total cost and produce a profit for the whole line."},{"t":"li","x":"**Price war** — successive price cutting by competitors to increase or maintain unit sales or market share."},{"t":"li","x":"**Price fixing** — a conspiracy among firms to set prices."},{"t":"li","x":"**Price discrimination** — charging different prices to different buyers for products of like grade and quality."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Four Approaches to a Price Level"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Approach","Methods"],["Demand-oriented","Skimming, penetration, prestige, price lining, odd-even, target, bundle, yield management"],["Cost-oriented","Standard markup, cost-plus (percentage of cost or fixed fee), experience curve pricing"],["Profit-oriented","Target profit, target return-on-sales, target return-on-investment"],["Competition-oriented","Customary pricing, above-, at- or below-market pricing, loss-leader pricing"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Loss-leader pricing: a product priced below cost to attract customers into the store. Customary pricing: traditional price and distribution."},{"t":"p","x":"• Surge pricing (Uber, Lyft): fares increase when demand is high, shifting the demand curve to the right; critics call it price gouging."},{"t":"p","x":"• Cost-oriented methods: standard markup pricing (cost plus a markup gives the selling price), cost-plus pricing in two forms (cost-plus percentage-of-cost and cost-plus fixed-fee) and experience curve pricing."},{"t":"p","x":"• Profit-oriented methods: target profit pricing (a specific profit), target return-on-sales pricing (percentage profit on sales volume) and target return-on-investment pricing (prices set to achieve an ROI goal)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Demand-oriented examples: Rolex for prestige pricing; target pricing is the price consumers are willing to pay."},{"t":"p","x":"Discussion question: is surge pricing by Uber and Lyft ethical or price gouging? Video case 14: Carmex [B], setting the price of the number-one lip balm. In-class activity 14-1: extra value meal bundle pricing at McDonald's."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Calculations"},{"t":"p","x":"• Markup on cost = markup $ ÷ cost; markup on selling price = markup $ ÷ selling price; price = cost ÷ (1 − markup % on price)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Target profit price: Price = (fixed cost + variable cost + target profit) ÷ quantity."},{"t":"p","x":"• Target return-on-sales: Price = unit cost ÷ (1 − target return on sales)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Target ROI price: Price = (total cost + ROI × investment) ÷ quantity."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Special Adjustments and Legal Aspects"},{"t":"p","x":"• Not all price differences are illegal, only those that lessen competition. The Robinson-Patman Act allows price differentials with a cost justification defense, changing market conditions or a meet-the-competition defense."},{"t":"p","x":"• Price fixing is illegal; price wars cut margins; price discrimination to different buyers of like grade and quality is regulated."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-check"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can name the four approaches and place each method under one."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can compute a markup price, target profit price and target ROI price."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can distinguish skimming from penetration and bundle from yield management."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can list the three defenses under the Robinson-Patman Act."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1. Classify each method as demand-, cost-, profit- or competition-oriented: skimming; standard markup; target return-on-sales; loss-leader; bundle; target ROI; price lining; customary pricing."},{"t":"p","x":"Q2. A retailer buys an item for $60 and wants a 40% markup on selling price. What is the selling price?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q3. Total cost for 5,000 units is $100,000 (fixed $40,000, variable $60,000) and the firm wants a target profit of $25,000. What price per unit?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. Unit cost is $48 and the target return on sales is 20%. What price?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q5. Total cost is $200,000, investment is $500,000, target ROI is 12% and 10,000 units are expected. What price?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q6. A product is introduced at a very high price aimed at buyers who really want it. Another at a very low price to reach the mass market. Name each. Which gives faster share gains?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q7. A seller charges two buyers different prices for like products. Under what three defenses is this allowed?"},{"t":"a","x":"A1. Demand: skimming, bundle, price lining. Cost: standard markup. Profit: target return-on-sales, target ROI. Competition: loss-leader, customary pricing.\nA2. Price = 60 ÷ (1 − 0.40) = $100.\nA3. Price = (100,000 + 25,000) ÷ 5,000 = $25.\nA4. Price = 48 ÷ (1 − 0.20) = $60.\nA5. Price = (200,000 + 0.12 × 500,000) ÷ 10,000 = (200,000 + 60,000) ÷ 10,000 = $26.\nA6. High price for those who really want it: skimming. Low price for the mass market: penetration, which gives faster share gains.\nA7. Cost justification defense, changing market conditions, and meet-the-competition defense (Robinson-Patman Act)."}]},"15":{"title":"Managing Marketing Channels and Supply Chains","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain what a marketing channel of distribution is and why intermediaries are needed."},{"t":"p","x":"• Distinguish traditional channels, digital channels and the types of vertical marketing systems."},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe the factors considered when selecting and managing a marketing channel."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain what supply chain and logistics management are and how they relate to marketing strategy."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"li","x":"**Marketing channel** — the set of individuals and firms involved in making a product or service available for use by consumers or business users."},{"t":"li","x":"**Intermediaries** — middlemen (agents, brokers, wholesalers, retailers, distributors, dealers) who create value by performing channel functions."},{"t":"li","x":"**Agent or broker** — an intermediary with legal authority to act on behalf of the manufacturer."},{"t":"li","x":"**Wholesaler** — an intermediary who sells to other intermediaries, usually retailers."},{"t":"li","x":"**Retailer** — an intermediary who sells to consumers."},{"t":"li","x":"**Direct channel** — producer sells directly to consumers or users with no intermediary."},{"t":"li","x":"**Dual distribution** — reaching different buyers through different channels."},{"t":"li","x":"**Strategic channel alliance** — one firm’s marketing channel is used to sell another firm’s products."},{"t":"li","x":"**Multichannel (omnichannel) marketing** — blending different communication and delivery channels (BOPUS, BORIS)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Vertical marketing system** — a professionally managed and centrally coordinated channel designed to achieve channel economies and maximum marketing impact."},{"t":"li","x":"**Intensive distribution** — a level of distribution density where a firm tries to place its products and services in as many outlets as possible."},{"t":"li","x":"**Exclusive distribution** — a level of distribution density where only one retailer in a specific geographical area carries the firm's products."},{"t":"li","x":"**Selective distribution** — a level of distribution density where a firm selects a few retailers in a specific geographical area to carry its products."},{"t":"li","x":"**Supply chain** — the firms that perform the activities required to create and deliver a product or service to consumers or industrial users."},{"t":"li","x":"**Logistics** — the organization and flow of goods, services and information to satisfy customer requirements cost-effectively."},{"t":"li","x":"**Vendor-managed inventory (VMI)** — the supplier manages the buyer’s inventory."},{"t":"li","x":"**Middleman** — an intermediary between the manufacturer and end-user markets."},{"t":"li","x":"**Distributor** — an imprecise term, usually describing intermediaries who perform a variety of distribution functions (selling, maintaining inventories, extending credit); more common in business markets, but may also refer to wholesalers."},{"t":"li","x":"**Dealer** — an even more imprecise term than distributor; it can mean the same as distributor, retailer, wholesaler and so forth."},{"t":"li","x":"**Direct-to-consumer (D2C) marketing channel** — lets consumers buy products by interacting with print or electronic media without a face-to-face meeting with a salesperson."},{"t":"li","x":"**Channel conflict** — arises when one channel member believes another channel member is engaged in behavior that prevents it from achieving its goals."},{"t":"li","x":"**Disintermediation** — channel conflict that arises when a channel member bypasses another member and sells or buys products direct."},{"t":"li","x":"**Total logistics cost** — the expenses of transportation, materials handling and warehousing, inventory, stockouts (being out of inventory), order processing and handling of returned products."},{"t":"li","x":"**Customer service (logistics)** — the ability of logistics management to satisfy users in terms of time, dependability, communication and convenience."},{"t":"li","x":"**Bullwhip effect** — the tendency of supply chain managers at different levels to exaggerate the need to increase or decrease inventory in response to variation or lack of predictability in customer demand."},{"t":"li","x":"**Reverse logistics** — reclaiming recyclable and reusable materials, returns and reworks from the point of consumption or use for repair, remanufacturing, redistribution or disposal."},{"t":"li","x":"**Blockchain technology** — a decentralized digital system for recording, documenting and facilitating transactions across all participants in a supply chain."},{"t":"p","x":"Opening case: Eddie Bauer's \"brick, click, and flip\" multichannel strategy (retail stores, website, catalogs). Supply chain dynamics are complex: the store focuses on having items on the shelf, online and catalog focus on a wide product assortment, so the firm balances effectiveness and efficiency."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Nature and Importance of Marketing Channels"},{"t":"p","x":"• Intermediaries create value because products flow through them and they perform functions that producers would otherwise have to perform."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type of function","Activities"],["Transactional","Buying, selling, risk taking"],["Logistical","Assorting, storing, sorting, transporting"],["Facilitating","Financing, grading, marketing information and research"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Channels create four utilities: time (when the consumer wants it), place (where), form (more appealing) and possession (helping the consumer take possession)."},{"t":"p","x":"The nine functions in detail:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Function","Activities"],["Buying","Purchasing products for resale or as an agent for supply of a product."],["Selling","Contacting potential customers, promoting products and seeking orders."],["Risk taking","Assuming business risks in the ownership of inventory that can become obsolete or deteriorate."],["Assorting","Creating product assortments from several sources to serve customers."],["Storing","Assembling and protecting products at a convenient location to offer better customer service."],["Sorting","Purchasing in large quantities and breaking into smaller amounts desired by customers."],["Transporting","Physically moving a product to customers."],["Financing","Extending credit to customers."],["Grading","Inspecting, testing or judging products and assigning them quality grades."],["Marketing information and research","Providing information to customers and suppliers, including competitive conditions and trends."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Channel Structure"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Consumer products","Business products"],["Producer → Consumer (direct)","Producer → Industrial user (direct)"],["Producer → Retailers → Consumers","Producer → Industrial distributor → Industrial user"],["Producer → Wholesalers → Retailers → Consumers","Producer → Agents → Industrial user"],["Producer → Agents → Wholesalers → Retailers → Consumers","Producer → Agents → Industrial distributors → Industrial user"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Digital marketing channels combine electronic and traditional intermediaries and create time, place, form and possession utility (also for services such as car rental, securities and insurance)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Direct-to-consumer (D2C): mail order, direct mail, catalogs, telemarketing, websites, televised home shopping. Multichannel marketing includes BOPUS (buy online, pick up in-store) and BORIS (buy online, return in-store)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Dual distribution and strategic channel alliances: Cereal Partners Worldwide (General Mills makes and markets in the U.S.; Nestlé distributes worldwide; 130+ markets)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Vertical marketing systems"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Meaning","Examples"],["Corporate","One owner at several levels: forward integration (producer owns the intermediary down the chain) or backward integration (retailer owns manufacturing)","Producer-owned stores"],["Contractual","Independent firms integrate efforts by contract; the most popular","Wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chains, retailer-sponsored cooperatives, franchising"],["Administered","Coordination achieved through the size and power of one member","Large manufacturer or retailer"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Franchising types: manufacturer-sponsored retail (car dealers), manufacturer-sponsored wholesale (soft drink bottlers), service-sponsored retail (hotels), service-sponsored (tax services)."},{"t":"p","x":"Contractual systems in detail: wholesaler-sponsored voluntary chains (a wholesaler develops relationships with independent retailers to coordinate buying, merchandising and inventory management); retailer-sponsored cooperatives (independent retailers form an organization to operate a wholesale facility); franchising (an arrangement between a parent company and individual firms for operating a specific business). Administered systems achieve coordination at successive stages of production and distribution."},{"t":"p","x":"Strategic alliances are popular in global marketing because they are easier and cheaper. Cereal Partners Worldwide expects about 10% of the world cereal market."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Channel Choice and Management"},{"t":"p","x":"• Three questions: which channel gives the best coverage of the target market, best satisfies buyers’ requirements and is the most profitable?"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Coverage","Number of outlets","Typical products"],["Intensive","As many as possible","Convenience products"],["Selective","Several","Shopping products"],["Exclusive","One per area","Specialty products"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Buyers’ requirements: information, convenience, variety, pre- or postsale service. Profitability: the margins earned by each channel member; allocate funds on profit, not only on dollar sales."},{"t":"p","x":"Applying marketing metrics, Charlesburg Furniture: review each channel's sales and profit and decide whether to allocate marketing funds on channel dollar sales or on profit."},{"t":"p","x":"Target market coverage means the density of stores in an area (intensive, exclusive, selective)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Logistics and Supply Chain Management"},{"t":"p","x":"• Supply chain management integrates information and logistics across firms to deliver value. A responsive supply chain (Dell: customized systems, faster express transport, higher cost) fits customers who want customization; an efficient supply chain (Walmart: cross-docking, reliable lower prices) fits customers who want low prices."},{"t":"p","x":"• Customer service factors: time (order cycle and replenishment), dependability (consistent replenishment), communication (two-way link, blockchain) and convenience (minimal effort, VMI)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Amazon (video case) creates value through selection and fast delivery from fulfillment centers."},{"t":"p","x":"Definitions in context: a supply chain is the various firms performing the activities required to create and deliver a product or service to ultimate consumers or industrial users. Logistics management organizes the flow of goods to satisfy customer requirements, cost-effectively, with a customer service focus. Supply chain management is the integration of information and logistics across firms. Figure 15-8 shows the automotive supply chain with thousands of firms supplying components, software and parts."},{"t":"p","x":"Time as a service factor involves inventory delivery systems and a quick, efficient response. Walmart's efficient supply chain serves customers who expect reliable, lower-priced items."},{"t":"p","x":"Video case 15, Amazon: delivering the earth's biggest selection. Questions: how logistics and supply chain activities create value; which systems improved the flow from suppliers to fulfillment centers and from fulfillment centers to customers; why logistics will matter for Amazon's future."},{"t":"p","x":"In-class activity 15-1, Apple channels: Apple online store (direct), Apple retail store (direct), Ingram Micro to Best Buy (indirect, wholesaler and retailer) and MacMall or Verizon (indirect, retailer). In-class activity 15-2: channels for Fastenal fasteners (a business-products channel)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-check"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can list the nine channel functions in three groups and the four utilities."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can draw the consumer and business channels."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can distinguish corporate, contractual and administered vertical marketing systems."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can choose intensive, selective or exclusive distribution for a product."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can contrast a responsive with an efficient supply chain."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1 (MCQ). Purchasing products for resale and assuming the risk of obsolete inventory are: (a) logistical functions; (b) transactional functions; (c) facilitating functions; (d) form utilities."},{"t":"p","x":"Q2. Classify as transactional, logistical or facilitating: sorting, financing, selling, transporting, grading, risk taking, assorting, marketing research."},{"t":"p","x":"Q3. A shopper buys a sweater online and picks it up in the nearest store. Which multichannel term applies? What if she returns it in-store?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. Classify the vertical marketing system: (i) a producer buys its own retail stores; (ii) a car maker and its dealers under contract; (iii) a retailer buys a factory; (iv) independent grocers form a cooperative that runs a warehouse."},{"t":"p","x":"Q5. Which coverage strategy fits: chewing gum; a Rolex watch; a TV?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q6. Which supply chain, responsive or efficient, fits Dell and Walmart? Why?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q7. A retailer’s dollar sales are greatest through channel A but channel B earns the largest margin. On which basis should marketing funds be allocated, and why?"},{"t":"a","x":"A1. (b) Buying and risk taking are transactional functions.\nA2. Transactional: selling, risk taking. Logistical: sorting, transporting, assorting. Facilitating: financing, grading, marketing research.\nA3. BOPUS (buy online, pick up in-store); BORIS (buy online, return in-store).\nA4. (i) Corporate (forward integration). (ii) Contractual (franchising). (iii) Corporate (backward integration). (iv) Contractual (retailer-sponsored cooperative).\nA5. Chewing gum: intensive. Rolex: exclusive. TV: selective.\nA6. Dell: responsive, because customers want customized systems and accept waiting and express transport. Walmart: efficient, because customers expect reliable low prices (cross-docking).\nA7. Profit, because the goal is margins earned; the highest sales channel may not be the most profitable."}]},"18":{"title":"Integrated Marketing Communications and Direct Marketing","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe the promotional mix and the five promotional elements."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain integrated marketing communications (IMC)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe the steps of developing an IMC program."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain the value and issues of direct marketing."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"li","x":"**Promotional mix** — the combination of communication tools used to inform buyers about benefits, persuade them to try the product and remind them later."},{"t":"li","x":"**Integrated marketing communications (IMC)** — coordinating all promotional activities (advertising, personal selling, sales promotion, public relations, direct marketing) to provide a consistent message across all audiences."},{"t":"li","x":"**Advertising** — any paid form of nonpersonal communication about an organization, product, service or idea by an identified sponsor."},{"t":"li","x":"**Personal selling** — two-way flow of communication between buyer and seller, often face-to-face, designed to influence a purchase decision."},{"t":"li","x":"**Public relations** — communication management that seeks to influence the feelings, opinions or beliefs held by customers, stockholders, suppliers, employees and other publics."},{"t":"li","x":"**Publicity** — a nonpersonal, indirectly paid presentation of an organization, product or service."},{"t":"li","x":"**Sales promotion** — a short-term inducement of value offered to arouse interest in buying a product or service."},{"t":"li","x":"**Direct marketing** — direct communication with consumers to generate a response: an order, a request for information or a visit to a retail outlet."},{"t":"li","x":"**Online behavioral targeting** — using information about consumers’ online behavior to personalize banner and display ads."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. The Five Promotional Elements"},{"t":"p","x":"Intended learning outcome: discuss the process and advantages of IMC and describe the major decisions involved in developing an IMC campaign."},{"t":"li","x":"Advertising: mass selling (versus customized interaction); paid, nonpersonal and delivered through mass media."},{"t":"li","x":"Personal selling: a two-way flow of communication between buyer and seller, face-to-face, with flexibility; beware of wasted coverage (reaching people outside the target market)."},{"t":"li","x":"Public relations: influencing others about a product through publicity, a nonpersonal, indirectly paid presentation (e.g. McDonald's Twitter)."},{"t":"li","x":"Sales promotion: a short-term offer to help sell a product, offered to intermediaries as well as ultimate consumers."},{"t":"li","x":"Direct marketing: direct communication with customers through direct mail, catalogs, telephone and more."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Element","Mass or customized","Cost","Strengths","Weaknesses"],["Advertising","Mass","Fees for space or time","Efficient way to reach large numbers","High absolute costs; poor feedback"],["Personal selling","Customized","Salaries or commissions","Immediate feedback; very persuasive; selects audience; complex information","Extremely expensive per exposure; messages differ between salespeople"],["Public relations","Mass","No direct payment to media","Often the most credible source","Difficult to get media cooperation"],["Sales promotion","Mass","Wide range of fees","Changes behavior in the short run; flexible","Easily abused; promotion wars; easily duplicated"],["Direct marketing","Customized","Cost of mail, phone or computer communication","Quick to prepare; builds relationship","Declining response; database management is expensive"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Developing an IMC Program"},{"t":"p","x":"• Identify the target audience (demographics, interests, preferences, media use, purchase behaviors) and use online behavioral targeting."},{"t":"p","x":"• Once the budget is set, specify the tools: select the right promotional tools, design the promotion, schedule the promotion."},{"t":"p","x":"• Balance the elements and coordinate a consistent effort; consider consumers, businesses and intermediaries as the target."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Direct marketing"},{"t":"p","x":"• Customizes communication and creates one-to-one interactions; most campaigns use several methods. Value: direct orders, lead generation, traffic generation."},{"t":"p","x":"• Issues: customer databases, matching records with social media activity, e-mail outperforms social media advertising 3:1, mandatory “opt-in” in many countries, and privacy concerns and consumer privacy laws."},{"t":"p","x":"The promotional mix combines tools: determine the balance of the promotional elements, coordinate a consistent promotional effort, and assess the characteristics of the target market (consumers, businesses, intermediaries). Once the budget is set, the five basic IMC tools can be specified: selecting the right promotional tools, designing the promotion and scheduling it (example in class: the Milano Cortina 2026 Olympics). Online behavioral targeting uses information about consumers' online behavior to personalize banners and display ads."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-check"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can name the five elements and state whether each is mass or customized."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can state one strength and one weakness of each element."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can define IMC and explain why consistency matters."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can list the values of direct marketing and its privacy issues."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Case and activity"},{"t":"p","x":"Video case 18, Chipotle Mexican Grill (\"where IMC helps deliver food for real\"): what factors contributed to Chipotle's early success and how does it continue to create customer value; which promotional elements from Figure 18-2 does it use; how does it integrate its message across them; what future marketing actions is it likely to take and should it consider. In-class activity 18-1 (optional): an IMC plan for the Lay's STAX product line, using the Frito-Lay IMC program form."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1. Classify each element as mass or customized: advertising, personal selling, public relations, sales promotion, direct marketing."},{"t":"p","x":"Q2. Which element is extremely expensive per exposure but gives immediate feedback and can handle complex information?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q3. Which element is often the most credible source in the consumer’s mind and requires no direct payment to media?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. A firm uses TV, coupons, social media and a sales team, each with a different message. What principle is violated?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q5. List three values of direct marketing and two issues."},{"t":"a","x":"A1. Mass: advertising, public relations, sales promotion. Customized: personal selling, direct marketing.\nA2. Personal selling.\nA3. Public relations (publicity).\nA4. Integrated marketing communications: all promotional activities must coordinate to provide a consistent message across audiences.\nA5. Values: direct orders, lead generation, traffic generation. Issues: privacy concerns and consumer privacy laws; mandatory opt-in; costly database management."}]},"19":{"title":"Advertising, Sales Promotion, and Public Relations","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe the three forms of product advertisements."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain how to design the advertisement and select the right media."},{"t":"p","x":"• Describe consumer-oriented sales promotions and their pros and cons."},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain publicity tools in public relations."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"li","x":"**Product advertisements** — ads that focus on selling a product or service: pioneering (informational), competitive (persuasive) and reminder."},{"t":"li","x":"**Advertising creativity** — originality and appropriateness of the message."},{"t":"li","x":"**Advertising media** — the means used to communicate the message."},{"t":"li","x":"**Product placement** — a sales promotion tool using a brand-name product in a movie, TV show, video game or another commercial."},{"t":"li","x":"**Coupon** — an offer of a discounted price."},{"t":"li","x":"**Deal** — a short-term price reduction."},{"t":"li","x":"**Premium** — an offer of an item free or at significant savings; self-liquidating premiums are paid for by the consumer."},{"t":"li","x":"**Sweepstakes** — a prize drawing that requires no skill."},{"t":"li","x":"**Publicity tools** — ways of obtaining nonpersonal presentations without direct cost: news releases, news conferences and public service announcements (PSAs)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Advertising"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Form","Purpose","Example"],["Pioneering (informational)","Tell people about a new product","New Lucid car"],["Competitive (persuasive)","Persuade consumers to select this brand","Surface Pro vs. MacBook Pro"],["Reminder","Reinforce previous knowledge","Flowers ad"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Appeals: fear (avoid negative experiences), sex (increase attractiveness), humor (fun and exciting); they can be combined. Most ads include both informational and persuasive elements. GenAI is now used to improve content and creativity (for example Spotify: “Music for Everyone” becomes “Music for Every Moment”)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Producing ads is costly (design, artwork, layout and products can exceed $500,000)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Selecting the media"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Medium","Advantages","Disadvantages"],["Television","Huge audience; picture, sound and motion; reaches 96% of households","High cost; short exposure; complex information is hard; wasted coverage"],["Radio","Low cost; local; quick; reaches 88% of adults","No visual; short exposure"],["Magazines","Targeted; high-quality color; long life; complex information","Long lead time; relatively high cost"],["Newspapers","Local coverage; quick changes; low cost","Short life; poor color"],["Directories","Rural and older segments; long use period","Environmental impact; competes with Google"],["Internet","Video, audio and interactive; paid search; mobile","Complex options; advertising fatigue; hard to measure impact"],["Out-of-home","Low cost; local; repeat exposure","Short message; low selectivity"],["Direct mail","Highly selective; personalized","High cost per contact; junk-mail image"],["Medium","Advantages","Disadvantages"],["Internet","Video and audio capabilities; animation captures attention; interactive ads that link to the advertiser","Complicated with many search, display and payment options; advertising fatigue"],["Out-of-home","Low cost; local focus; high visibility; repeat exposures","Message must be short and simple; low audience selectivity; criticised as a traffic hazard"],["Direct mail","High audience selectivity; complex, personalized messages; high-quality graphics","High cost per contact; poor image (junk mail)"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Selection criteria: media habits of the target audience, product attributes, message complexity, timing and cost. The goal: maximize exposure and minimize cost."},{"t":"p","x":"Advertising creativity is the originality and appropriateness of the message. GenAI example slogans (original versus GenAI-preferred): Spotify \"Music for Everyone\" / \"Music for Every Moment\"; Airbnb \"Belong Anywhere\" / \"Welcome Home, Everywhere\"; Apple \"Think Different\" / \"Innovation at Its Core\"; BMW \"The Ultimate Driving Machine\" / \"Engineered for Elegance\"; GoPro \"Be a Hero\" / \"Life in High Definition\"; Verizon \"5G Built Right\" / \"Making Your World Wireless\". Copywriters create the text of messages and translate it into an advertisement."},{"t":"p","x":"Advertising media are the means to communicate the message (print, radio, television). Media selection considers audience, product, message, objectives, budget and costs."},{"t":"p","x":"Television also uses dynamic ad insertion (ads matched to scenes and viewer demographics), out-of-home TV in hotels, bars and gyms, a shift to live events instead of DVR-recorded programs, and 30-minute infomercials. Radio: 15,000+ US broadcast stations plus webcast and satellite radio; a segmented medium; peak times are drive times; ease of switching stations. Magazines: 7,400+ consumer magazines and about 100 new ones a year, specific topics for segments. Newspapers: local and current events, a call for action, growth in online and mobile editions. Directories (yellow pages): 40 percent of consumers use print directories once a year and 25 percent use online directories monthly; limited accountability and metrics. Internet: rich media, banner/display ads, paid search, mobile marketing; difficult to measure impact because online ads do not always result in a click. Out-of-home: billboards, digital billboards and transit advertising with good reach and frequency. Other media: transit advertising (trains, buses, shelters) and place-based media in locations that attract the target audience, increasingly interactive."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Consumer-Oriented Sales Promotions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Tool","Objective","Advantage","Disadvantage"],["Coupons","Stimulate demand","Encourage retailer support","Consumers delay purchases"],["Deals","Increase trial; retaliate against competitors","Reduce consumer risk","Delay purchases; reduce perceived value"],["Premiums","Build goodwill","Consumers like free merchandise","Consumers buy for the premium"],["Contests","Increase purchases; build business inventory","Encourage involvement","Require creative or analytical thinking"],["Sweepstakes","Encourage present customers to buy more; minimize brand switching","Customers use product and store more often","Sales drop afterwards"],["Samples","Encourage trial","Low risk for consumer","High cost for company"],["Loyalty programs","Encourage repeat purchases","Help create loyalty","High cost"],["Point-of-purchase displays","Increase trial; in-store support","Good visibility","Hard to get high-traffic space"],["Rebates","Encourage purchase; stop sales decline","Stimulate demand","Easily copied; steal future sales"],["Product placements","Introduce new products; demonstrate use","Positive message in noncommercial setting","Little control"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Product placement is worth more than $36 billion annually; disclosure rules are tightening."},{"t":"li","x":"**Samples** — a free product or trial size that encourages consumers to try new things; encourages new-product trial, low risk for consumers, high cost for the company."},{"t":"li","x":"**Loyalty program** — encourages and rewards repeat purchases and offers a premium as purchases accumulate; helps create loyalty; high cost for the company."},{"t":"li","x":"**Point-of-purchase display** — signs or holders that display products in high-traffic areas; increase trial and give good product visibility, but retailers are hard to persuade to allocate high-traffic space."},{"t":"li","x":"**Rebate** — a cash refund with proof of purchase; effective at stimulating demand and stopping a sales decline, but easily copied, steals sales from the future and reduces perceived product value."},{"t":"p","x":"Premiums are items offered free or at significant savings (a self-liquidating premium, such as a free toy, is paid for by the consumer) and encourage frequent buying; contests encourage consumers to apply skills or creative thinking to win a prize; sweepstakes award value-based or experience-based prizes. Product placement introduces new products and demonstrates use in a positive message in a noncommercial setting, but with little control over presentation."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Public Relations"},{"t":"p","x":"• Publicity tools: news release (company announcement), news conference (informational meeting), public service announcement (free media space). Publicity often has stronger impact than advertising."},{"t":"p","x":"• To increase the value of promotion: emphasize long-term relationships and increase self-regulation."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-check"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can classify an ad as pioneering, competitive or reminder."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can state two advantages and two disadvantages of each medium."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can name the consumer sales promotion tools and one drawback of each."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can name the three publicity tools."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1. Name the type of ad: (i) a firm announces a new electric car; (ii) a firm compares its tablet with a rival’s; (iii) a florist ad reminds buyers before Mother’s Day."},{"t":"p","x":"Q2. Which medium: (i) reaches 96% of households but has high cost and short exposure; (ii) targets rural and older segments; (iii) low cost, short message, low audience selectivity; (iv) high-quality color and long ad life?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q3. Which sales promotion tool: (i) cash back with proof of purchase; (ii) free trial-size product; (iii) rewards that accumulate with purchases; (iv) a brand shown in a movie; (v) a short-term price reduction?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. Name three publicity tools."},{"t":"p","x":"Q5. Give two disadvantages of coupons and deals."},{"t":"a","x":"A1. (i) Pioneering. (ii) Competitive. (iii) Reminder.\nA2. (i) Television. (ii) Directories. (iii) Out-of-home. (iv) Magazines.\nA3. (i) Rebate. (ii) Sample. (iii) Loyalty program. (iv) Product placement. (v) Deal.\nA4. News release, news conference, public service announcement (PSA).\nA5. Consumers delay purchases; perceived product value falls."}]},"20":{"title":"Using Social Media and Mobile Marketing to Engage Consumers","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"• Explain a social media marketing program and customer engagement."},{"t":"p","x":"• Select social media by audience and share."},{"t":"p","x":"• Identify types of social media content and how to schedule posts."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"li","x":"**Social media** — online media where users submit comments, photos and videos, often with a feedback process to identify “popular” topics."},{"t":"li","x":"**User-generated content (UGC)** — online media content created by end users and publicly available (consumer-generated content)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Influencer marketing** — identifying and recruiting influencers to advocate a company’s products, services and brands rather than focusing only on prospective buyers."},{"t":"li","x":"**Social media marketing program** — the part of IMC that creates and delivers compelling online content that attracts viewers and encourages them to share it with their networks."},{"t":"li","x":"**Customer engagement** — the interaction and connection between customers and the brand through social media."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Social Media Marketing Programs"},{"t":"p","x":"• Select social media by assessing the characteristics of visitors and the number of unique visitors. Marketing research firms and the social media provide audience data. Compare size of audience with market share."},{"t":"p","x":"• For content ask: is it promotional, entertaining or educational? How does it complement the IMC?"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Content type","Purpose"],["Educational","Teach"],["Inspirational","Motivate"],["Interactive","Invite participation"],["Connecting","Build community"],["Promotional","Sell"],["Newsworthy","Report news"],["Entertaining","Amuse"]]},{"t":"p","x":"• Quality depends largely on the creative. Scheduling: best time is Mondays before 12:00 noon (CST); worst day Sunday; weekend and evening posts get low engagement; best times vary by platform."},{"t":"p","x":"• Carmex case: the Half-Court Hero Contest ($75,000 for a half-court shot) went viral; lesson: watch for opportunities to manage luck and use both social and traditional media."},{"t":"p","x":"A social media marketing program aims at customer engagement. To select social media assess the characteristics of visitors and the number of users or unique visitors; the recent activity on the seven major social media shows size of audience versus market share. Content types also include newsworthy content and entertaining content (e.g. the Pepsi video), in addition to educational, inspirational, interactive, connecting and promotional content."},{"t":"p","x":"Carmex: the company has a partnership with LeBron James; in the Half-Court Hero Contest a viewer, Michael Drysch, made the shot and won, and it went viral."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-check"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can define social media, UGC and influencer marketing."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can list the criteria for selecting social media."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can name the seven content types."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can state the best and worst days to post."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1. Define user-generated content and influencer marketing and give an example of each."},{"t":"p","x":"Q2. Name the two criteria used to select social media."},{"t":"p","x":"Q3. Classify the content: (i) a how-to video; (ii) a motivating story; (iii) a poll; (iv) a discount post; (v) a funny clip."},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. When is the best time and the worst day to post?"},{"t":"a","x":"A1. UGC: content created by end users, such as customer photos or reviews. Influencer marketing: recruiting influencers to advocate the brand, such as a sports star promoting a lip balm.\nA2. Characteristics of visitors and number of unique visitors.\nA3. (i) Educational. (ii) Inspirational. (iii) Interactive. (iv) Promotional. (v) Entertaining.\nA4. Best: Mondays before noon (CST). Worst: Sunday; weekend and evening posts have low engagement."}]}},"extras":[{"t":"p","x":"**BUS2205**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Marketing Management**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Complete Course Synthesis & Practice Exercises**"},{"t":"p","x":"Lingnan University — Department of Marketing & International Business"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on: Kerin, Hartley, Lau & Rudelius — Marketing, 17th Edition (2025 Release, McGraw Hill)"},{"t":"p","x":"Covers Chapters 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 9 and Appendix A — as taught in Section 4, First Term 2026-27"},{"t":"p","x":"Compiled from all Moodle course materials, with original revision notes and practice exercises"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"How to Use This Document"},{"t":"p","x":"This synthesis gathers every substantive piece of content shared on Moodle for BUS2205 Marketing Management (Section 4, First Term 2026-27), reorganized into clean revision notes, plus original practice exercises with full answer keys to help you assimilate the material for the mid-term test, the final exam, and your coursework."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Structure of this document"},{"t":"li","x":"Part 1 – Course Overview: course structure, assessment weighting, key dates, and a brief summary of the Individual Assignment and Group Project (context only, not exam content)."},{"t":"li","x":"Part 2 – Chapter-by-chapter revision notes, in the order taught: Ch.1 → Ch.2 → Ch.3 → Ch.5 → Ch.8 → Ch.9 → Appendix A. Each chapter ends with 4–6 original practice questions and a full answer key."},{"t":"li","x":"Part 3 – Exam-style review checklist across all chapters."},{"t":"li","x":"Part 4 – A mixed practice set (10 questions spanning multiple chapters, including one integrative mini-case) with a full answer key."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Important note on scope\n• Textbook Chapters 4, 6, and 7 are NOT included — they were not covered in the Week 1–6 slide decks shared on Moodle for this course and are not part of the mid-term coverage (Ch. 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 9).\n• The mid-term test (26 Oct 2026) covers Textbook Chapters 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 and 9 only.\n• The Final Exam covers ALL course materials (all topics taught across the term, including Chapters 10-21 taught later, which are outside the scope of the Moodle files provided for this synthesis).\n• Wherever a fact could not be confirmed from the original slides (e.g., an ambiguous figure or a superseded statistic), it is flagged with \"(please verify against original slide)\"."]]},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Part 1 — Course Overview"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Course Information"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Detail"],["Course","BUS2205 Marketing Management — Section 4 (Tuesday)"],["Term","First Term, 2026–27"],["Department","Marketing & International Business, Lingnan University"],["Instructor","Prof. Aihua YAN (aihuayan@ln.edu.hk)"],["Credit hours","3 hours/week, one term (3 hrs class + 3 hrs personal study expected weekly)"],["Textbook","Kerin, Lau, Hartley & Rudelius (2025), Marketing, 17th ed., McGraw-Hill Education"],["Learning platform","McGraw-Hill Connect (self-learning platform; optional online quizzes)"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Course Description & Learning Outcomes"},{"t":"p","x":"This functional core course introduces the fundamental concepts of marketing and equips students with an integrated, strategic approach to developing a marketing plan, structured around the planning → implementation → control phases of the strategic marketing process."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Learning Outcomes (LOs)"},{"t":"li","x":"**LO1: **Apply marketing concepts and theories to situations and make decisions using a strategic marketing perspective."},{"t":"li","x":"**LO2: **Broaden managerial perspective of marketing from a business enterprise's point of view."},{"t":"li","x":"**LO3: **Improve analytical skills; think critically and creatively."},{"t":"li","x":"**LO4: **Present findings/recommendations concisely, convincingly, and logically in written work."},{"t":"li","x":"**LO5: **Make oral presentations with strong communication skills (vocabulary, visual aids, etc.)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Assessment Weighting"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Assessment","Weight","Learning Outcomes Assessed"],["Group Project","20%","LO1, LO2, LO3, LO5"],["Mid-term Test (MCQ, 50 questions)","15%","LO1, LO2"],["Individual Written Assignment","15%","LO1, LO3, LO4"],["Final Exam (case + essay questions)","50%","LO1, LO2"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Dates"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date","Event"],["1 Sep","Course overview; Chapter 1 – Understanding Marketing"],["8 Sep","Chapter 2 – Strategic Marketing Process"],["15 Sep","Chapter 3 – The Marketing Environment; project team formation"],["22 Sep","Chapter 5 – Consumer Behavior; Individual Assignment briefing"],["29 Sep","Chapter 8 – Marketing Research; Group Project briefing"],["6 Oct","Chapter 9 – Segmentation, Targeting & Positioning"],["13 & 20 Oct","Chapters 10–11 – Product Decisions (not covered in this document — see scope note)"],["26 Oct (Mon, 5–6pm, Room MBG22)","Mid-term Test — covers Ch. 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 9"],["27 Oct","Chapter 15 – Channel Decisions"],["3 Nov","Chapters 18–20 – Integrated Marketing Communications"],["8 Nov 2026, 23:59","Individual Written Assignment deadline (via Turnitin/Moodle)"],["10 Nov","Chapters 13–14 – Pricing Decisions"],["17 & 24 Nov","Final project presentations / course review"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Assignment Info (context only — not exam content)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Why this section is here\n• The two items below (Individual Assignment and Group Project) are summarized so you have the full course context in one document.\n• They are coursework deliverables, NOT topics tested on the mid-term or final exam — do not study them as \"content to memorize\", but do note the deadlines and deliverables."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Individual Written Assignment (15% of grade)"},{"t":"p","x":"Brand: GODIVA (Hong Kong boutiques only). Acting as a marketing consultant, each student must visit one physical Godiva boutique in Hong Kong (not online/delivery, not outside HK) and, based on that visit plus outside research, propose at least two distinct strategies to extend Godiva's product life cycle in Hong Kong (e.g., modifying a product, changing served markets, or repositioning). Each strategy must be justified against four criteria: sufficient demand, fit with marketing objectives, company expertise/resources to implement, and ability to differentiate from competitors. Report: 800–1,000 words (in-store experience ≥100 words), submitted via Turnitin/Moodle by 23:59, 8 Nov 2026. Similarity threshold: 25%."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Group Project (20% of grade)"},{"t":"p","x":"Teams of 6–7 act as marketing consultants introducing either (a) a foreign product/service into the China/Hong Kong market, or (b) a China/Hong Kong product/service into a foreign market. Teams choose ONE of three assigned options for this term:"},{"t":"li","x":"Option 1: Neo's Home Robot (1X Technologies) — to be marketed in Hong Kong."},{"t":"li","x":"Option 2: Pop Mart's \"Pop Bakery\" concept — to be marketed in Paris, France."},{"t":"li","x":"Option 3: Anitch's Barrier Repair Body Cream (HK brand) — to be marketed in Melbourne, Australia."},{"t":"p","x":"Deliverables: a PowerPoint marketing plan (max 30 slides, max 50 words/slide) covering environmental scanning (PESTLE) and SWOT, 4 types of segmentation, target segment(s), positioning (with a perceptual map), and a full 4Ps marketing plan — plus a 30-second original video advertisement featuring all team members. A 25-minute team presentation (all members speaking) is delivered near the end of term. Both a peer evaluation and a Generative-AI declaration form are required from every student/team."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Academic integrity & AI use"},{"t":"li","x":"Plagiarism, collusion, and falsified data are treated as dishonest practice and can be referred to the Student Disciplinary Committee."},{"t":"li","x":"All written submissions go through Turnitin."},{"t":"li","x":"Any use of Generative AI tools must be declared on the official declaration form and must follow Lingnan University's GAI guidelines."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Appendix A — Building an Effective Marketing Plan"},{"t":"p","x":"Supplementary content · This appendix is directly useful for your Group Project, since it lays out the exact structure your team's marketing-plan presentation should follow."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Marketing Plans vs. Business Plans"},{"t":"li","x":"**Marketing plan **— a road map for the marketing actions of an organization for a specified future period (e.g., 1 or 5 years)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Business plan **— a road map for the ENTIRE organization over a specified future period (broader than marketing alone: also covers R&D, operations, organization-wide financials)."},{"t":"p","x":"What a plan should contain depends on (1) its target audience and purpose, (2) the kind/complexity of the organization, and (3) the industry. A plan aimed at an INTERNAL audience (to direct the firm) typically differs from one aimed at an EXTERNAL audience (to raise capital) — the external version adds a company description, organizational structure, and biographies of key personnel, since outside readers don't already know the firm."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The questions outside audiences (e.g., investors) always ask"},{"t":"li","x":"Is the marketing/business idea valid?"},{"t":"li","x":"Is the idea distinctive?"},{"t":"li","x":"Is there a real market for the offering?"},{"t":"li","x":"Are the financial projections realistic?"},{"t":"li","x":"Are the key personnel capable of executing the plan?"},{"t":"li","x":"Does the plan clearly describe how investors get their money back?"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Writing and Style Guidelines"},{"t":"li","x":"Use a direct, professional style; be positive and specific."},{"t":"li","x":"Avoid superlatives (\"the best,\" \"revolutionary\") without evidence."},{"t":"li","x":"Use numbers for impact and bullet points for emphasis."},{"t":"li","x":"Use clear A-level and B-level headings so readers can navigate quickly."},{"t":"li","x":"Use visuals — photos, illustrations, graphs, and charts."},{"t":"li","x":"Aim for roughly 15–35 pages."},{"t":"li","x":"Use care in layout, design, and presentation, and cite credible sources of information/data."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Directly transferable to your Group Project slides\n• \"Number of words in each slide is limited to 50\" (your Group Project rule) echoes this chapter's advice to be concise, use bullets, and let numbers/visuals do the work rather than dense paragraphs."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. The Marketing Plan Outline (11 sections)"},{"t":"p","x":"The textbook illustrates this outline using a running example: Paradise Kitchens, Inc., maker of Howlin' Coyote Chili. The structure below is the one to use as your Group Project's mental checklist."},{"t":"table","rows":[["#","Section","What goes here"],["1","Table of Contents","Quick navigation by section/subsection."],["2","Executive Summary","The single most important element — \"sells\" the plan through clarity and brevity (often ~2 pages)."],["3","Company Description","Recent history and successes of the organization."],["4","Strategic Focus and Plan","Mission; Goals (financial & nonfinancial); Core competency and (sustainable) competitive advantage."],["5","Situation Analysis (SWOT)","Internal factors: Strengths & Weaknesses. External factors: social, competitive, technological, economic, legal/regulatory trends. Followed by finer-grained Industry Analysis, Competitor Analysis, Company Analysis, and Customer Analysis (incl. customer characteristics)."],["6","Market-Product Focus","Marketing & product objectives; Target markets; Points of difference; Positioning."],["7","Marketing Program","The 4Ps in detail: Product strategy, Price strategy, Promotion strategy (incl. sales promotion), Place (distribution) strategy."],["8","Financial Data and Projections","Past sales revenues; Five-year projections; Organization (org chart)."],["9","Implementation Plan","How the plan will actually be executed and rolled out over time (often with a schedule/Gantt-style chart)."],["10","Evaluation","How actual results will be compared with targets, and what contingency actions follow."],["11","Appendices","Supporting detail — e.g., biographical sketches of key personnel, detailed financial spreadsheets."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Worked example — SWOT analysis for Paradise Kitchens (Howlin' Coyote Chili)"},{"t":"p","x":"This condensed version of the textbook's worked example shows the level of specificity expected in a real plan's SWOT:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Category","Example points"],["Strengths (internal)","Experienced, entrepreneurial management; unique, high-quality/high-price product; excellent consumer acceptance in existing markets."],["Weaknesses (internal)","Small size limits options; no national awareness/distribution; heavy dependence on key employees; limited financial resources vs. giant competitors."],["Opportunities (external)","Fast-growing Southwestern/Mexican food category; large and growing Hispanic population; food-tech breakthroughs helping small producers compete."],["Threats (external)","Premium price may limit access to mass markets; product not patentable — larger competitors can imitate; more households eating out/ordering takeout instead of cooking."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Notice how each SWOT point is specific and evidence-based (not vague adjectives) — this is exactly the standard your Group Project's SWOT slide should meet."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Worked example — Points of Difference and Positioning"},{"t":"p","x":"Paradise Kitchens' points of difference: (1) unique taste + convenience combination no competitor matches, (2) alignment with the trend toward spicier foods, (3) distinctive premium packaging. Its positioning statement: positioned in consumers' minds as a very high-quality \"authentic Southwestern/Mexican tasting\" chili that is still quick and easy to prepare — i.e., it resolves the old trade-off where chili was either convenient OR tasty, but not both."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Appendix A"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which section of a marketing plan is specifically designed to \"sell\" the plan to a reader through clarity and brevity, often before they read anything else in depth?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Implementation Plan"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Executive Summary"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Appendices"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Organization"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Name two elements that appear in a marketing plan aimed at an EXTERNAL audience (to raise capital) but would typically be unnecessary in a purely INTERNAL plan, and explain why."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"List the four external SWOT categories (beyond simply \"opportunities and threats\") that the Paradise Kitchens example uses to organize external factors."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Applied mini-case — Group Project practice)"},{"t":"p","x":"Using whichever Group Project option your own team has been assigned (Neo's Home Robot / Pop Bakery / Anitch Barrier Repair Body Cream), draft a one-paragraph Executive Summary (3–5 sentences) that a busy reader could use to understand your whole marketing plan at a glance. Then list, in order, the marketing-plan section headings (from the 11-section outline) you would use to structure your team's slide deck."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Answer Key — Appendix A"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1"},{"t":"a","x":"(B) Executive Summary. It is explicitly described as the single most important element in \"selling\" the plan through clarity and brevity."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2"},{"t":"a","x":"(1) Company Description — external readers don't already know the firm's history/background, so it must be spelled out; internal readers already know this. (2) Biographies of key personnel / organizational structure — external investors need reassurance that capable people are running the venture, whereas internal readers already know the team. (Accept any two reasonable, correctly justified elements from the outline, e.g. organizational structure more broadly.)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3"},{"t":"a","x":"Consumer/Social, Competitive, Technological, Economic, and Legal/Regulatory (five categories total, each split into opportunities and threats)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4"},{"t":"p","x":"**Guidance (self-assessed — no single correct answer): **A strong Executive Summary states (1) what the product/service is and where it will be introduced, (2) the core target segment and positioning in one sentence, (3) the headline reason the opportunity is attractive (market size/trend), and (4) the essence of the 4Ps approach — all in plain, confident, non-superlative language. Section order to follow: Company/Product Description → Strategic Focus (objectives) → Situation Analysis (PESTLE/SWOT, Industry & Competitor Analysis) → Market-Product Focus (Segmentation, Targeting, Positioning + perceptual map) → Marketing Program (4Ps) → Implementation/Evaluation notes if time allows. This mirrors both the Appendix A outline and the Key Questions explicitly listed in your Group Project Guide."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Part 3 — Exam-Style Review Checklist (All Chapters)"},{"t":"p","x":"Use this as a final self-test before the mid-term or final exam. For each item, ask yourself: \"Can I explain this out loud, from memory, with an example?\" If not, go back to that chapter's notes."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 1 — Marketing Foundations"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Give the AMA definition of marketing and explain what \"exchange\" means."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 4 conditions required for an exchange/marketing to occur."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish needs vs. wants with an example."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name the 4 P's and the 5 uncontrollable environmental forces, and correctly sort a list of mixed examples into the two categories."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain the 4 eras of market orientation evolution (production → sales → marketing concept → customer relationship)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Define market orientation and the marketing concept, and explain how they relate."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 4 types of utility with an example of each."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 2 — Strategy & the Strategic Marketing Process"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name the 3 levels of organizational strategy (corporate, SBU, functional)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain the 3 elements of a visionary organization's foundation (purpose, core values, mission/culture) and its direction (business, goals, KPIs)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Calculate dollar sales and dollar market share from given numbers."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Correctly classify a business into a BCG matrix quadrant (star/question mark/cash cow/dog) given its market share and market growth."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Apply the 4 diversification-analysis strategies (penetration, development ×2, diversification) to a scenario."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 3 planning-phase steps and the 4 implementation-phase components of the strategic marketing process."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain \"planning gap\" and the two possible responses to a deviation."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 3 — The Marketing Environment"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Define environmental scanning and explain why interpreting a trend matters as much as spotting it."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name the 5 environmental forces and give a current example of each."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish gross, disposable, and discretionary income."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name and distinguish the 4 forms of competition (pure competition, monopolistic competition, oligopoly, pure monopoly)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name at least 3 major pieces of regulatory legislation and what each protects (Sherman Act, Clayton Act, Robinson-Patman Act)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain self-regulation and give an example."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 5 — Consumer Behavior"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 5 stages of the purchase decision process, in order, with what happens at each stage."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish extended, limited, and routine problem solving, and the involvement level behind each."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 5 situational influences."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain Maslow's hierarchy of needs with a marketing example at each level."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain selective perception (exposure/comprehension/retention) and 2 ways to reduce perceived risk."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain the drive–cue–response–reinforcement model of behavioral learning."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish associative, aspiration, and dissociative reference groups."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain the family decision-making roles (gatherer, influencer, decision maker, purchaser, user)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 8 — Marketing Research"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 5 steps of the marketing research approach, in order."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish exploratory, descriptive, and causal research."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish primary vs. secondary data, with the advantages/disadvantages of each."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name at least 4 primary data-collection methods (observational, focus group, survey types, experiment/panel)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish independent vs. dependent variable in an experiment."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name the 3 families of sales-forecasting technique and 1 specific technique from each."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 9 — Segmentation, Targeting & Positioning"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Define market segmentation and the 4 segmentation bases (geographic, demographic, psychographic, behavioral)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain the 80/20 rule and usage rate."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 5 steps in segmenting and targeting markets, in order."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 5 criteria for selecting target markets."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain what a market-product grid shows and how to read one."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish head-to-head vs. differentiation positioning."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain how to build a perceptual map, step by step."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Appendix A — The Marketing Plan"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish a marketing plan from a business plan."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 11 sections of a marketing plan outline, roughly in order."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain how a plan for an external audience differs from one for an internal audience."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name at least 3 writing/style guidelines for a professional marketing plan."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Part 4 — Mixed Practice Set (Cross-Chapter)"},{"t":"p","x":"These questions mix multiple chapters, similar to the case-plus-essay format of the Final Exam. Answer key follows at the end of this section."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q1 (Ch.1, MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A firm changing its packaging design is exercising control over which marketing mix element?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Price"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Product"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Place"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Regulatory force"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q2 (Ch.2, Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"A firm's SBU has 8% share of a market growing at 12% per year, while its main rival has 35% share. Classify this SBU on the BCG matrix and explain your reasoning."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q3 (Ch.3, MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A market with many sellers offering close substitutes that are NOT identical (e.g., independent coffee shops) best fits:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Pure competition"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Monopolistic competition"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Oligopoly"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Pure monopoly"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q4 (Ch.5, Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"A shopper feels regret shortly after buying an expensive winter coat online. Name this phenomenon and suggest one marketing action the retailer could take to address it."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q5 (Ch.8, MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A company mining its own loyalty-card transaction records to look for patterns is using:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Primary data via a mail survey"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Secondary, internal data"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) A focus group"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) An experiment"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q6 (Ch.9, Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"A skincare brand notices that 70% of its profit comes from just 15% of its customers, who buy every month. Name the principle this illustrates and explain one marketing action the brand could take in response."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q7 (Ch.2 + Ch.9, Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Explain how \"market segmentation\" (Ch.9) fits into Step 2 of the strategic marketing process's planning phase (Ch.2) — i.e., why must a firm segment the market BEFORE it can design a marketing program?"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q8 (Ch.3 + Ch.1, MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A new government data-privacy law forcing a retailer to change how it collects customer data for its loyalty app is an example of:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) A controllable marketing-mix decision"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) An uncontrollable regulatory force"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) A behavioral segmentation basis"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) A form of competition"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q9 — Integrative Mini-Case (SWOT + STP + Consumer Behavior)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Case: \"BrewLab\" specialty coffee — expansion decision\n• BrewLab is a small, HK-based specialty coffee roaster known among coffee enthusiasts for single-origin, ethically-sourced beans and a loyal Instagram following. It currently sells through one café in Sai Ying Pun and online direct-to-consumer subscriptions. Its founder is considering opening a second, larger café in a busy Central office district to capture office workers buying takeaway coffee.\n• BrewLab's coffee is more expensive than chain coffee (Pacific Coffee, Starbucks) but noticeably higher quality; the founder personally knows every regular customer at the Sai Ying Pun café. The team has limited capital and no experience running a high-volume takeaway operation."]]},{"t":"p","x":"**(a) SWOT (Ch.2): **Identify one strength, one weakness, one opportunity, and one threat for BrewLab's proposed Central expansion."},{"t":"p","x":"**(b) Segmentation & targeting (Ch.9): **Propose two segmentation bases (from geographic/demographic/psychographic/behavioral) that BrewLab should use to define its target segment in Central, and briefly justify each."},{"t":"p","x":"**(c) Positioning (Ch.9): **Should BrewLab use head-to-head or differentiation positioning against Starbucks/Pacific Coffee in Central? Justify your choice in 1–2 sentences."},{"t":"p","x":"**(d) Consumer behavior (Ch.5): **A Central office worker buying takeaway coffee every weekday morning is most likely engaging in which type of problem solving (extended/limited/routine)? What does this imply for BrewLab's in-store marketing tactics?"},{"t":"p","x":"**(e) Integration: **In 2–3 sentences, explain how your answers to (a)–(d) fit together into a single, coherent argument for (or against) the Central expansion."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Answer Key — Mixed Practice Set"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1"},{"t":"a","x":"(B) Product. Packaging design is a decision the firm makes and controls directly, making it part of the Product element of the marketing mix."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2"},{"t":"a","x":"Question mark. The market growth rate (12%) is high, but the SBU's own market share (8%) is low relative to the rival's 35% — high growth + low relative share is the definition of a question mark, meaning the firm must decide whether to invest further to try to grow share (moving toward \"star\") or scale back."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3"},{"t":"a","x":"(B) Monopolistic competition. Many sellers, similar but not identical/substitutable products within a price range (the textbook's own example is coffee/tea) — this is monopolistic competition, not pure competition (which requires near-identical, commodity-like products)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4"},{"t":"a","x":"This is cognitive dissonance (postpurchase psychological tension, especially likely for a higher-involvement/higher-price purchase). The retailer could send a reassuring confirmation email highlighting the coat's key benefits/reviews, offer a hassle-free return policy, or provide styling tips to reinforce the buyer's confidence in the purchase."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5"},{"t":"a","x":"(B) Secondary, internal data. The loyalty-card data already exists inside the firm (an internal marketing outcome data source) and was collected before this specific analysis — that makes it secondary AND internal, even though data mining is being applied to it."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6"},{"t":"a","x":"This illustrates the 80/20 rule (a small share of heavy users driving most profit) — closely related to usage-rate/behavioral segmentation. The brand should launch a loyalty/frequency-marketing program (e.g., a subscription or rewards tier) specifically to retain and further reward this high-usage-rate segment, since retaining these customers is far more profit-efficient than acquiring new, lower-value ones."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q7"},{"t":"a","x":"Step 2 of the planning phase (\"develop a market-product focus, customer value proposition, and goals\") explicitly requires market segmentation as its foundation: a firm cannot decide which customer value proposition and points of difference to build, nor design a coherent marketing program (Step 3), until it has first identified WHICH group(s) of customers — with common needs that respond similarly to marketing actions — it intends to serve. Segmentation therefore logically precedes and directly feeds the market-product focus step; without it, the subsequent marketing program has no clearly defined audience to be designed around."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q8"},{"t":"a","x":"(B) An uncontrollable regulatory force. A government-imposed privacy law is external to the firm and outside its control — a textbook example of a regulatory environmental force, distinct from a marketing-mix decision, a segmentation basis, or a competitive-structure concept."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q9 — Integrative case answer key"},{"t":"p","x":"**(a) SWOT: **Strength — high perceived product quality and strong brand loyalty/community among coffee enthusiasts. Weakness — no experience running a high-volume takeaway operation, and limited capital to fund the expansion. Opportunity — large volume of office workers in Central who regularly buy takeaway coffee and increasingly value quality/ethical sourcing. Threat — intense, well-resourced competition from established chains (Starbucks, Pacific Coffee) with far greater scale, lower prices, and existing Central footprints."},{"t":"p","x":"**(b) Segmentation & targeting: **Behavioral segmentation — target \"heavy, habitual\" weekday coffee buyers (high usage rate, the 80/20-style core of any café's revenue), since they represent the most reliable, high-frequency revenue base in a business district. Psychographic segmentation — target quality-and-ethics-conscious professionals who are willing to pay a premium for better-sourced, better-tasting coffee (rather than competing purely on convenience/price), since this aligns with BrewLab's existing brand identity and is a segment less well served by the mass-market chains."},{"t":"p","x":"**(c) Positioning: **Differentiation positioning is the better fit: BrewLab cannot out-scale or out-price Starbucks/Pacific Coffee on convenience or cost, but it CAN own a distinct position around specialty quality, ethical sourcing, and an authentic brand story — attributes the mass chains do not credibly claim — making head-to-head competition on the chains' own terms (speed, ubiquity, low price) a weak strategy for a small, capital-constrained player."},{"t":"p","x":"**(d) Consumer behavior: **Most likely routine problem solving (or at most limited): buying a daily takeaway coffee is a low-involvement, high-frequency, habitual purchase for most office workers, who will not extensively research or compare each morning. This implies BrewLab's in-store tactics should focus on maintaining consistent product quality, avoiding stockouts/slow service (since routine buyers are highly sensitive to friction), and building habitual brand loyalty (e.g., a stamp/rewards card) rather than relying on heavy in-store persuasive information, which matters more for high-involvement purchases."},{"t":"p","x":"**(e) Integration: **Because BrewLab's core strength is quality/brand loyalty rather than scale, and because the target segment's daily coffee purchase is a low-involvement, habitual decision, the expansion is more likely to succeed if BrewLab pursues differentiation positioning around quality/ethics aimed at a psychographically-defined \"quality-conscious\" segment of habitual buyers — building fast, reliable, high-quality routine service and loyalty mechanics — rather than trying to out-compete the chains head-to-head on speed and price, which would expose exactly the weaknesses (limited capital, no high-volume experience) identified in the SWOT."}]},"BUS2105":{"file":"BUS2105_Microeconomics_Synthese_et_Exercices.docx","mtime":"2026-10-13","lessons":{"1":{"title":"What Is Economics?","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"After studying this chapter you will be able to:"},{"t":"li","x":"Define economics and distinguish between microeconomics and macroeconomics."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the two big questions of economics."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the key ideas that define the economic way of thinking."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how economists go about their work as social scientists and policy advisers."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Scarcity","Our inability to satisfy all our wants; the fundamental problem that forces choice."],["Incentive","A reward that encourages an action, or a penalty that discourages one."],["Economics","The social science that studies the choices individuals, businesses, governments and societies make as they cope with scarcity, and the incentives that influence and reconcile those choices."],["Microeconomics","The study of choices individuals and businesses make, how those choices interact in markets, and the influence of governments."],["Macroeconomics","The study of the performance of national and global economies (e.g. unemployment, inflation, growth)."],["Factors of production","Productive resources: Land, Labor, Capital, Entrepreneurship."],["Human capital","The knowledge and skill people obtain from education, training and experience."],["Rational choice","A choice that compares costs and benefits and achieves the greatest benefit over cost for the chooser."],["Opportunity cost","The highest-valued alternative that must be given up to get something."],["Marginal benefit / marginal cost","The benefit / opportunity cost of one more (incremental) unit of an activity."],["Positive statement","A statement that can be tested against facts (\"what is\")."],["Normative statement","A statement that expresses an opinion and cannot be tested (\"what ought to be\")."],["Economic model","A simplified description of some aspect of the economic world, including only the features needed for the purpose at hand."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Everything starts from the fact that we want more than we can get. Because of scarcity we must make choices, and the choices we make depend on the incentives we face. Economics divides into two main parts: microeconomics and macroeconomics."},{"t":"p","x":"Microeconomic questions: why are people streaming more movies? Would a tax on online shopping affect Amazon? Macroeconomic questions: why does the unemployment rate fluctuate? Can the monetary authority make unemployment fall by keeping interest rates low?"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Goods and services","The objects that people value and produce to satisfy human wants."],["Factors of production","The productive resources used to produce goods and services, grouped into four categories: land, labor, capital and entrepreneurship."],["Land","The \"gifts of nature\" used to produce goods and services."],["Labor","The work time and work effort that people devote to producing goods and services."],["Human capital","The knowledge and skill that people obtain from education, on-the-job training and work experience; it determines the quality of labor."],["Capital","The tools, instruments, machines, buildings and other constructions that businesses use to produce goods and services."],["Entrepreneurship","The human resource that organizes land, labor and capital."],["Self-interest","Making the choices that you think are best for you."],["Social interest","Choices that are best for society as a whole; it has two dimensions, efficiency and fair shares."],["Efficient resource use","Resource use is efficient if it is not possible to make someone better off without making someone else worse off."],["Globalization","The expansion of international trade, borrowing and lending, and investment."],["Tradeoff","An exchange: giving up one thing to get something else."],["Rational choice","A choice that compares costs and benefits and achieves the greatest benefit over cost for the person making the choice. Only the wants of the person making the choice are relevant to its rationality."],["Benefit","The gain or pleasure that something brings, determined by preferences."],["Preferences","What a person likes and dislikes and the intensity of those feelings."],["Opportunity cost","The highest-valued alternative that must be given up to get something. It has two components: the things you cannot afford to buy and the things you cannot do with your time."],["Marginal benefit","The benefit from pursuing an incremental increase in an activity."],["Marginal cost","The opportunity cost of pursuing an incremental increase in an activity."],["Positive statement","A statement that can be tested by checking it against facts, e.g. \"An increase in the tax rate on cigarettes will decrease the quantity of cigarettes demanded.\""],["Normative statement","A statement that expresses an opinion and cannot be tested, e.g. \"The government should increase the tax on cigarettes to discourage smoking and improve public health.\""],["Economic model","A description of some aspect of the economic world that includes only those features needed for the purpose at hand."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"1. The two big questions of economics"},{"t":"p","x":"• 1. What, how, and for whom are goods and services produced?"},{"t":"p","x":"• 2. When do choices made in self-interest also promote the social interest?"},{"t":"p","x":"The factors of production and what they earn:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Factor","Payment earned"],["Land (natural resources)","Rent"],["Labor (work time and effort)","Wages"],["Capital (tools, machines, buildings)","Interest"],["Entrepreneurship (organizes the other three)","Profit"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Social interest has two dimensions: efficiency (resources used so that no one can be made better off without making someone else worse off) and fair shares (equity). Four themes illustrate tension between self-interest and social interest: globalization, information-age monopolies, climate change, and pandemics (e.g. Covid-19)."},{"t":"p","x":"What? In the United States agriculture accounts for less than 1 percent of production, industry for 11 percent and services for 80 percent; in low-income Ethiopia agriculture is 35 percent, industry 22 percent and services 44 percent. Choices of what to produce end up as the quantity of each item produced."},{"t":"p","x":"How? Goods and services are produced using the factors of production. The quality of labor depends on human capital, and the share of the US population completing higher levels of education has grown since 1900."},{"t":"p","x":"For whom? Who gets the goods and services depends on the incomes people earn, which come from the factors of production."},{"t":"p","x":"Self-interest and social interest: every day billions of people make choices in pursuit of self-interest, and the question is whether these also promote the social interest. Efficiency and fair shares (what is a fair share?) are the two dimensions. Adam Smith's Wealth of Nations was the first systematic attempt to address this question."},{"t":"p","x":"Globalization: in the self-interest of consumers who buy low-cost imports and of multinationals that produce in low-cost regions and sell in high-price regions, but is it in the interest of low-wage workers abroad and of firms that cannot compete with low-cost imports?"},{"t":"p","x":"Information-age monopolies: the information revolution gave us smartphones, laptops and the internet and made Bill Gates and Gordon Moore wealthy, but did it serve the social interest?"},{"t":"p","x":"Climate change: burning fossil fuels pours about 28 billion tons (4 tons per person) of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere each year; two thirds come from the US, China, the EU, Russia and India, with the fastest growth in India and China. Each of us leaves a carbon footprint and can lessen it by walking, cycling or planting a tree, but can individuals be relied on to act in the social interest, and can governments change incentives so self-interested choices are also in the social interest?"},{"t":"p","x":"The Covid pandemic: without social isolation one infected person infects more than one other, so disease spreads exponentially. People choose their degree of isolation in their own interest, so do individual choices serve the social interest?"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"2. The economic way of thinking — six key ideas"},{"t":"p","x":"Six key ideas define the economic way of thinking:"},{"t":"p","x":"1. A choice is a tradeoff: scarcity and choice are at center stage, and every choice means giving up something else (study or have fun on Saturday night)."},{"t":"p","x":"2. People make rational choices by comparing benefits and costs. The idea of rational choice answers \"What goods and services will be produced?\": those that people rationally choose to buy."},{"t":"p","x":"3. Benefit is what you gain from something."},{"t":"p","x":"4. Cost is what you must give up to get something (opportunity cost; for a concert it is the things you cannot buy and the things you cannot do with your time)."},{"t":"p","x":"5. Most choices are \"how-much\" choices made at the margin: you decide how many minutes to allocate to study versus messaging, comparing the benefit of a little more study with its cost. If marginal benefit exceeds marginal cost, do more of that activity."},{"t":"p","x":"6. Choices respond to incentives: a change in marginal cost or marginal benefit changes the incentives we face and leads us to change our choice. The central idea of economics is that we can predict how choices change by looking at changes in incentives, and incentives are the key to reconciling self-interest and the social interest."},{"t":"p","x":"**Important nuance: **Scarcity is not the same as poverty. Even someone with unlimited money still faces scarcity of time — this is why choice and opportunity cost apply to everyone."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"3. Economist as scientist and policy adviser"},{"t":"p","x":"Economists build models to make positive predictions (testable). Policy questions blend positive analysis (what will happen) with normative judgments (what should happen, i.e. the goal) — economics cannot choose the goal, but it can compare the marginal benefits and costs of alternative ways to reach a given goal."},{"t":"p","x":"Economists distinguish positive statements (testable against facts) from normative statements (opinions that cannot be tested). The task of economic science is to discover positive statements consistent with what we observe and that help us understand how the economic world works, by creating and testing economic models. Economics is a toolkit for advising governments and businesses and for personal decisions. All policy questions blend the positive and the normative: economics cannot help with the normative part (the goal), but for a given goal it evaluates alternative solutions by comparing marginal benefits and marginal costs."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Adventure-trip opportunity cost: A student forgoes a summer job paying $6,000 and free room/board at home to go on a trip costing $3,000 (travel/accommodation) + $200 (gasoline) + $1,400 (food)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Opportunity cost = **forgone income ($6,000) + direct trip costs ($3,000 + $200 + $1,400 = $4,600) = $10,600."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **Which of the following is a microeconomic (rather than macroeconomic) question? (a) Why did the national unemployment rate rise to 5%? (b) Why did the price of coffee beans rise this year? (c) Why did GDP growth slow down? (d) Why did the central bank cut interest rates?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Which of the following is a normative statement? (a) An increase in university tuition decreases the quantity of students enrolling. (b) The government should provide free tuition to increase social equity. (c) When the price of textbooks rises, students buy fewer textbooks. (d) A 10% tax on soft drinks reduced sales by 8%."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **Chloé can either work at a café for the summer, earning $5,000 over 10 weeks, or take an unpaid marketing internship. If she takes the internship, she will need to spend $600 on transport and $400 on business attire — costs she would not otherwise incur. What is the opportunity cost of taking the internship?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **Match each factor of production to the income it earns: Land, Labor, Capital, Entrepreneurship ↔ Rent, Wages, Interest, Profit."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **A firm's marginal benefit (extra revenue) from producing one more unit is $50 and its marginal cost is $42. Should it produce that unit? At a higher output level, marginal benefit falls to $30 while marginal cost rises to $35 — should the firm produce that unit?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q6. **Explain, using the idea of scarcity and choice, why \"scarcity\" is not the same as \"poverty.\""},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **(b) — microeconomics concerns choices of individual households, firms, and specific markets; (a), (c) and (d) are economy-wide aggregates studied in macroeconomics.\n**A2. **(b) — it contains the word \"should\" and expresses an opinion that cannot be tested against facts; the other three are testable positive statements.\n**A3. **Opportunity cost = forgone café earnings ($5,000) + extra internship costs ($600 + $400) = $6,000.\n**A4. **Land → Rent; Labor → Wages; Capital → Interest; Entrepreneurship → Profit.\n**A5. **First case: MB ($50) > MC ($42), so the firm should produce the unit (net gain $8). Second case: MB ($30) < MC ($35), so the firm should NOT produce that unit (net loss $5) — it should stop expanding output before that point.\n**A6. **Scarcity means wants always exceed the resources available to satisfy them — it exists for everyone, including the very wealthy, because time itself is always limited. Poverty is a state of having few resources relative to others. Even someone as rich as Bill Gates faces scarcity (of time, attention, etc.) and must make trade-offs, even though he is not poor."}]},"3":{"title":"Demand and Supply","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"After studying this chapter you will be able to:"},{"t":"li","x":"Describe a competitive market and think about a price as an opportunity cost."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the influences on demand."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the influences on supply."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how demand and supply determine prices and quantities bought and sold."},{"t":"li","x":"Use the demand and supply model to make predictions about changes in prices and quantities."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Competitive market","A market with many buyers and sellers, none of whom can influence the price."],["Quantity demanded","The amount consumers plan to buy at a given price in a given period."],["Law of demand","Other things being equal, the higher the price, the smaller the quantity demanded (and vice versa) — due to the substitution effect and income effect."],["Demand curve","The relationship between quantity demanded and price, other things constant; also a willingness-and-ability-to-pay (marginal benefit) curve."],["Change in demand","A shift of the whole demand curve, caused by a change in something other than the good's own price."],["Quantity supplied","The amount producers plan to sell at a given price in a given period."],["Law of supply","Other things being equal, the higher the price, the greater the quantity supplied — because marginal cost rises with output."],["Supply curve","The relationship between quantity supplied and price, other things constant; also a minimum-supply-price (marginal cost) curve."],["Equilibrium price / quantity","The price at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied; the quantity bought and sold at that price."],["Substitute / Complement","A substitute can replace a good in use (demand for the good rises when the substitute's price rises); a complement is used together with it (demand falls when the complement's price rises)."],["Normal / Inferior good","Normal: demand rises as income rises. Inferior: demand falls as income rises."],["Market","Any arrangement that enables buyers and sellers to get information and do business with each other."],["Money price","The amount of money needed to buy a good."],["Relative price","The ratio of the money price of a good to the money price of the next best alternative good; it is the good's opportunity cost."],["Wants","The unlimited desires or wishes people have for goods and services; demand reflects a decision about which wants to satisfy."],["Demand","The entire relationship between the price of a good and the quantity demanded. If you demand something you want it, can afford it and have made a definite plan to buy it."],["Substitution effect","When the relative price (opportunity cost) of a good rises, people seek substitutes, so the quantity demanded decreases."],["Income effect","When the price of a good rises relative to income, people cannot afford all the things they previously bought, so the quantity demanded decreases."],["Substitute","A good that can be used in place of another good."],["Complement","A good that is used in conjunction with another good."],["Normal good","A good for which demand increases as income increases."],["Inferior good","A good for which demand decreases as income increases."],["Quantity supplied","The amount producers plan to sell during a given time period at a particular price."],["Supply","The entire relationship between the quantity supplied and the price of a good. A firm supplies a good if it has the resources and technology to produce it, can profit from producing it and has made a definite plan to produce and sell it."],["Law of supply","Other things remaining the same, the higher the price of a good, the greater the quantity supplied; the lower the price, the smaller the quantity supplied. It results from the tendency for marginal cost to increase as the quantity produced increases."],["Supply curve","The relationship between the quantity supplied of a good and its price when all other influences on producers' planned sales remain the same; it is also a minimum-supply-price curve, where the lowest price at which someone will sell another unit is marginal cost."],["Change in supply","A shift of the supply curve caused by a change in something other than the good's own price: supply increases shifts it rightward, decreases shifts it leftward."],["Substitute in production","Another good that can be produced using the same resources."],["Complements in production","Goods that must be produced together."],["Equilibrium","A situation in which opposing forces balance each other."],["Market equilibrium","Occurs when the price balances the plans of buyers and sellers."],["Equilibrium price","The price at which the quantity demanded equals the quantity supplied."],["Equilibrium quantity","The quantity bought and sold at the equilibrium price."],["Shortage","Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a price below the equilibrium price; the price rises."],["Surplus","Quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded at a price above the equilibrium price; the price falls."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Six factors that shift demand"},{"t":"p","x":"The six main factors that change demand are:"},{"t":"p","x":"1. Prices of related goods: when the price of a substitute for energy bars rises, or the price of a complement falls, the demand for energy bars increases."},{"t":"p","x":"2. Expected future prices: if the price of a good is expected to rise, current demand increases and the demand curve shifts rightward."},{"t":"p","x":"3. Income: when income increases consumers buy more of most goods and the demand curve shifts rightward (normal good); for an inferior good demand decreases as income rises."},{"t":"p","x":"4. Expected future income and credit: if income is expected to rise, or credit is easy to obtain, demand might increase now."},{"t":"p","x":"5. Population: the larger the population, the greater the demand for all goods, especially necessities."},{"t":"p","x":"6. Preferences: people with the same income have different demands if they have different preferences."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Six factors that shift supply"},{"t":"p","x":"The six main factors that change supply are:"},{"t":"p","x":"1. Prices of factors of production: a rise in the price of a factor used to produce a good raises the minimum price the supplier will accept, so supply decreases (shifts leftward)."},{"t":"p","x":"2. Prices of related goods produced: the supply of a good increases if the price of a substitute in production falls, or if the price of a complement in production rises."},{"t":"p","x":"3. Expected future prices: if the price is expected to rise, supply today decreases and the curve shifts leftward."},{"t":"p","x":"4. Number of suppliers: more suppliers means greater supply (rightward shift)."},{"t":"p","x":"5. Technology: advances create new products and lower the cost of producing existing ones, so supply increases (rightward shift)."},{"t":"p","x":"6. State of nature: natural forces such as the weather; a natural disaster decreases supply (leftward shift)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Diagram in original slides: **Figures 3.1–3.6 show the demand curve, a rightward shift from an income increase, the supply curve, and a rightward shift from a technology advance. A movement ALONG a curve is caused only by a change in the good's own price; a SHIFT of the curve is caused by any other relevant factor changing."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Market equilibrium and price adjustment"},{"t":"p","x":"Energy bar example: at $1.00 a bar quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied (a shortage of 9 million bars); at $2.00 quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded (a surplus of 6 million bars); at $1.50 quantity demanded equals quantity supplied, so there is no shortage or surplus. Price regulates buying and selling plans and adjusts when plans do not match; at the equilibrium price the price does not change until an event changes demand or supply."},{"t":"p","x":"A demand curve is also a willingness-and-ability-to-pay curve: as the quantity available decreases, the price someone is willing to pay for another unit rises, and willingness to pay measures marginal benefit. A rise in price brings a movement up along the demand curve (a fall in quantity demanded); a change in anything else shifts the curve."},{"t":"p","x":"At the equilibrium price, quantity demanded = quantity supplied. Below equilibrium price → shortage → price rises. Above equilibrium price → surplus → price falls."},{"t":"p","x":"**Diagram in original slides: **Figures 3.7–3.9 plot demand and supply curves crossing at equilibrium, then a rightward shift of demand raising both price and quantity, and a rightward shift of supply raising quantity but lowering price."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The four-case prediction table (both curves can shift)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Demand","Supply","Equilibrium Price","Equilibrium Quantity"],["Increase","No change","Rises","Rises"],["Decrease","No change","Falls","Falls"],["No change","Increase","Falls","Rises"],["No change","Decrease","Rises","Falls"],["Increase","Increase","Ambiguous","Rises"],["Decrease","Decrease","Ambiguous","Falls"],["Decrease","Increase","Falls","Ambiguous"],["Increase","Decrease","Rises","Ambiguous"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Single shifts: an increase in demand creates a shortage at the original price, so price and quantity both rise; a decrease in demand creates a surplus, so both fall; an increase in supply creates a surplus, so price falls and quantity demanded increases along the demand curve; a decrease in supply creates a shortage, so price rises and quantity demanded decreases."},{"t":"p","x":"When both change in the same direction, quantity moves with them but price is uncertain: an increase in both demand and supply raises quantity (price change uncertain), and a decrease in both lowers quantity (price change uncertain). When they change in opposite directions price is determined but quantity is uncertain: an increase in supply with a decrease in demand lowers price, and a decrease in supply with an increase in demand raises price."},{"t":"p","x":"Method to answer prediction questions: draw the demand-supply diagram with price and quantity on the axes; decide whether the event changes demand, supply, both or neither; decide whether each change is an increase or a decrease; shift the curves in the correct direction (rightward for an increase, leftward for a decrease; do not think \"upward\" and \"downward\" for supply); then compare the new equilibrium with the original."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **Which of the following would shift the supply curve for smartphones rightward? (a) A rise in the price of smartphones. (b) A fall in the wages of factory workers making smartphones. (c) A rise in consumer income. (d) A rise in the price of a substitute in production."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Tea and coffee are substitutes. If the price of tea rises, what happens in the market for coffee?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **The demand and supply schedules for bubble tea (cups per day) are Qd = 1,400 − 200P and Qs = 200P − 200 (P in dollars). (a) Find the equilibrium price and quantity. (b) At a price of $3, is there a shortage or surplus, and of how much? (c) At a price of $5, is there a shortage or surplus, and of how much?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **A health report causes demand for bubble tea to fall by 200 cups at every price (supply is unchanged from Q3). Find the new equilibrium price and quantity."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **If both demand and supply for a good increase at the same time, what happens to the equilibrium quantity? What happens to the equilibrium price?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q6. **Classify each pair as substitutes, complements, or not a demand relationship: (a) butter and margarine; (b) printers and ink cartridges; (c) rice and rainfall in a rice-growing region."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **(b) — a fall in an input price lowers production cost and increases supply (shifts right). (a) is a movement ALONG the curve, not a shift; (c) shifts demand, not supply; (d) would decrease supply of this good (producers switch toward the now more attractively priced substitute).\n**A2. **Coffee demand increases (shifts rightward) because tea and coffee are substitutes, so both the equilibrium price and equilibrium quantity of coffee rise.\n**A3. **(a) Set Qd = Qs: 1,400 − 200P = 200P − 200 → 1,600 = 400P → P = $4; Q = 1,400 − 200(4) = 600 cups/day. (b) At P = $3: Qd = 1,400 − 600 = 800; Qs = 200(3) − 200 = 400 → shortage of 400 cups (price will tend to rise toward $4). (c) At P = $5: Qd = 1,400 − 1,000 = 400; Qs = 200(5) − 200 = 800 → surplus of 400 cups (price will tend to fall toward $4).\n**A4. **New demand: Qd′ = 1,200 − 200P (shifted left by 200 at every price). Set equal to supply: 1,200 − 200P = 200P − 200 → 1,400 = 400P → P = $3.50; Q = 200(3.50) − 200 = 500 cups/day. (Equilibrium price falls from $4.00 to $3.50 and quantity falls from 600 to 500.)\n**A5. **Equilibrium quantity definitely increases (both shifts push quantity up). The equilibrium price change is ambiguous — it depends on which shift is larger, since the demand increase pushes price up while the supply increase pushes price down.\n**A6. **(a) Substitutes (close alternatives in consumption). (b) Complements (must be used together). (c) Not a demand-side relationship — rainfall affects the cost/availability of producing rice, so it is a supply-side (state of nature) factor, not a change in consumer demand."}]},"4":{"title":"Elasticity","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"After studying this chapter you will be able to:"},{"t":"li","x":"Define, calculate and explain the factors that influence the price elasticity of demand."},{"t":"li","x":"Define, calculate and explain the factors that influence the income elasticity of demand and the cross elasticity of demand."},{"t":"li","x":"Define, calculate and explain the factors that influence the elasticity of supply."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions and Formulas"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Price elasticity of demand (PED)","A units-free measure of the responsiveness of the quantity demanded of a good to a change in its price when all other influences on buying plans remain the same. PED = percentage change in quantity demanded ÷ percentage change in price, reported as an absolute value."],["Perfectly inelastic demand","PED = 0: the quantity demanded does not change when the price changes; the demand curve is vertical."],["Inelastic demand","PED between 0 and 1: the percentage change in quantity demanded is smaller than the percentage change in price."],["Unit elastic demand","PED = 1: the percentage change in quantity demanded equals the percentage change in price; the demand curve has an ever-declining slope."],["Elastic demand","PED greater than 1: the percentage change in quantity demanded is greater than the percentage change in price."],["Perfectly elastic demand","PED = infinity: the quantity demanded changes by an infinitely large percentage when the price barely changes; the demand curve is horizontal."],["Total revenue","Price of the good multiplied by the quantity sold."],["Total revenue test","A method of estimating the price elasticity of demand by observing the change in total revenue that results from a price change, other influences on the quantity sold remaining the same."],["Income elasticity of demand","Measures how the quantity demanded of a good responds to a change in income, other things remaining the same: percentage change in quantity demanded ÷ percentage change in income."],["Cross elasticity of demand","Measures the responsiveness of the demand for a good to a change in the price of a substitute or a complement: percentage change in quantity demanded ÷ percentage change in price of the substitute or complement."],["Elasticity of supply","Measures the responsiveness of the quantity supplied to a change in the price of a good when all other influences on selling plans remain the same: percentage change in quantity supplied ÷ percentage change in price."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Why we need elasticity"},{"t":"p","x":"When supply decreases, the equilibrium price rises and quantity decreases, but by how much depends on the responsiveness of the quantity demanded to the price. You could think of responsiveness as the slope of the demand curve, but slope depends on the units in which price and quantity are measured, so we need a measure independent of units. Elasticity is such a measure."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Midpoint (average) method"},{"t":"p","x":"Percentage changes are calculated on the AVERAGE of the initial and new values, so the elasticity is the same whether price rises or falls between the two points, and the change in units of measurement leaves it unchanged (elasticity is a ratio of percentages)."},{"t":"p","x":"PED = [ΔQ ÷ average Q] ÷ [ΔP ÷ average P]"},{"t":"p","x":"The formula yields a negative value because price and quantity move in opposite directions, but it is the magnitude that reveals responsiveness, so we use the absolute value and drop the minus sign."},{"t":"p","x":"Pizza example (Figure 4.1): the price falls from $20.50 to $19.50 (a $1 fall, average price $20) and the quantity demanded rises from 9 to 11 pizzas an hour (a 2-pizza rise, average quantity 10). The percentage change in quantity is (2/10) × 100 = 20% and in price ($1/$20) × 100 = 5%, so PED = 20% ÷ 5% = 4 at the average price of $20."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Factors influencing PED"},{"t":"p","x":"The elasticity of demand for a good depends on:"},{"t":"p","x":"1. The closeness of substitutes: the closer the substitutes, the more elastic the demand. Necessities such as food or housing generally have inelastic demand; luxuries such as exotic vacations generally have elastic demand."},{"t":"p","x":"2. The proportion of income spent on the good: the greater the proportion, the larger the elasticity of demand."},{"t":"p","x":"3. The time elapsed since a price change: the more time consumers have to adjust, or the longer the good can be stored without losing its value, the more elastic the demand."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Elasticity along a linear demand curve"},{"t":"p","x":"PED varies along a downward-sloping linear demand curve. In the pizza example: from $25 to $15 (0 to 20 pizzas, average price $20, average quantity 10) elasticity is (20/10) ÷ (10/20) = 4; from $15 to $10 (20 to 30 pizzas, average price $12.50, average quantity 25) elasticity is (10/25) ÷ (5/12.50) = 1; from $10 to $0 (30 to 50 pizzas, average price $5, average quantity 40) elasticity is (20/40) ÷ (10/5) = 1/4. At the midpoint demand is unit elastic, at prices above the midpoint it is elastic, and at prices below it is inelastic."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Total revenue and elasticity"},{"t":"p","x":"Total revenue = price × quantity sold. A rise in price does not always increase total revenue; the change depends on elasticity:"},{"t":"li","x":"If demand is elastic, a 1 percent price cut increases quantity sold by more than 1 percent, so total revenue increases."},{"t":"li","x":"If demand is inelastic, a 1 percent price cut increases quantity sold by less than 1 percent, so total revenue decreases."},{"t":"li","x":"If demand is unit elastic, a 1 percent price cut increases quantity sold by 1 percent, so total revenue remains unchanged."},{"t":"p","x":"Total revenue test: if a price cut increases total revenue, demand is elastic; if it decreases total revenue, demand is inelastic; if it leaves total revenue unchanged, demand is unit elastic. In Figure 4.4, as the price falls from $25 toward $12.50, demand is elastic and total revenue rises; at $12.50 (25 pizzas an hour) demand is unit elastic and total revenue is at its maximum; below $12.50 demand is inelastic and total revenue falls. The same logic applies to your own expenditure on an item."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Income elasticity of demand"},{"t":"p","x":"If the income elasticity of demand is greater than 1, demand is income elastic and the good is a normal good. If it is greater than zero but less than 1, demand is income inelastic and the good is still a normal good. If it is less than zero (negative), the good is an inferior good."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Cross elasticity of demand"},{"t":"p","x":"The cross elasticity of demand for a substitute is positive and for a complement is negative. In Figure 4.5 a rise in the price of a burger (a substitute for pizza) increases the quantity demanded of pizza, while a rise in the price of a soft drink (a complement of pizza) decreases it."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Elasticity of supply"},{"t":"p","x":"When demand increases, the equilibrium price rises and quantity increases, but by how much depends on the elasticity of supply. Supply is perfectly inelastic if the supply curve is vertical (elasticity 0), unit elastic if the supply curve is linear and passes through the origin, and perfectly elastic if the curve is horizontal (elasticity infinite)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Factors influencing elasticity of supply"},{"t":"p","x":"The elasticity of supply depends on:"},{"t":"p","x":"1. Resource substitution possibilities: the easier it is to substitute among the resources used to produce a good, the greater the elasticity of supply."},{"t":"p","x":"2. The time frame for the supply decision: the longer the time after a price change, the greater the elasticity of supply. Momentary supply is perfectly inelastic, short-run supply is somewhat elastic and long-run supply is the most elastic."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Pizza price falls from $20.50 to $19.50 (average $20); quantity rises from 9 to 11 pizzas/hour (average 10)."},{"t":"p","x":"**%ΔQ = **(2/10) × 100 = 20%.  %ΔP = ($1/$20) × 100 = 5%.  PED = 20% ÷ 5% = 4 (highly elastic)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **The average price of a bag of coffee falls from $12 to $10 (average $11) and the quantity demanded rises from 500 to 700 bags a week (average 600). Calculate the price elasticity of demand and classify it as elastic, inelastic, or unit elastic."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Using the numbers in Q1, calculate total revenue before and after the price change, and confirm your elasticity classification using the total revenue test."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **A consumer's monthly income rises from $3,000 to $3,600 (average $3,300), and their restaurant meals per month rise from 8 to 10 (average 9). Calculate the income elasticity of demand and classify the good."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **The price of butter rises from $4 to $5 a pack (average $4.50); as a result, the quantity demanded of margarine (a substitute) rises from 200 to 240 units (average 220). Calculate the cross elasticity of demand and interpret its sign."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Which good is likely to have the MOST inelastic demand: (a) a particular brand of soft drink; (b) insulin for a diabetic patient; (c) restaurant meals at one specific chain; (d) one specific laptop model?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q6. **Elasticity of supply is likely to be HIGHER when: (a) resources needed are highly specialized; (b) the time frame considered is longer; (c) the good is a service that must be consumed instantly; (d) the firm cannot adjust its production process."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **%ΔQ = (700−500)/600 = 33.3%. %ΔP = (10−12)/11 = −18.2% (use magnitude 18.2%). PED = 33.3% ÷ 18.2% ≈ 1.83 → demand is ELASTIC (PED > 1).\n**A2. **TR before = $12 × 500 = $6,000. TR after = $10 × 700 = $7,000. Total revenue rose when price fell, which confirms elastic demand — consistent with PED ≈ 1.83 found in Q1.\n**A3. **%ΔQ = (10−8)/9 = 22.2%. %ΔY = (3,600−3,000)/3,300 = 18.2%. YED = 22.2% ÷ 18.2% ≈ 1.22. Since YED > 1, restaurant meals are income-elastic — a normal good behaving like a luxury for this consumer.\n**A4. **%ΔQ(margarine) = (240−200)/220 = 18.2%. %ΔP(butter) = (5−4)/4.5 = 22.2%. XED = 18.2% ÷ 22.2% ≈ 0.82. The positive sign confirms butter and margarine are substitutes.\n**A5. **(b) Insulin — a necessity with essentially no substitutes for a diabetic, so demand barely changes with price. The others have closer substitutes and are more elastic.\n**A6. **(b) — the longer the time frame, the more producers can adjust inputs and production methods, making supply more elastic. The other options describe conditions that make supply more INELASTIC."}]},"5":{"title":"Efficiency and Equity","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"After studying this chapter you will be able to:"},{"t":"li","x":"Describe the alternative methods for allocating scarce resources."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the connection between demand and marginal benefit and define consumer surplus; explain the connection between supply and marginal cost and define producer surplus."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the conditions under which markets are efficient and inefficient."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the main ideas about fairness and evaluate claims that markets result in unfair outcomes."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Market price (allocation method)","The people who get the resource are those willing to pay the market price; most scarce resources you supply or consume are allocated this way, and for most goods and services the market does a good job."],["Command","Allocates resources by the order of someone in authority (your labor time at work); works well in organizations with clear lines of authority but badly in an entire economy."],["Majority rule","Allocates resources the way the majority of voters choose (tax rates, defense versus health care); works well when decisions affect lots of people and self-interest must be suppressed to use resources efficiently."],["Contest","Allocates resources to a winner or group of winners (sports, the Oscars); works well when the efforts of the players are hard to monitor and reward directly."],["First-come, first-served","Allocates resources to those first in line (restaurant tables, supermarket checkout); works best when scarce resources can serve just one person at a time in sequence."],["Lottery","Allocates resources to those with the winning number (state lotteries, airport landing slots, marathon places); works well when there is no effective way to distinguish among potential users."],["Personal characteristics","Allocates resources to those with the \"right\" characteristics (choosing marriage partners), but is used in unacceptable ways such as discrimination."],["Force","War and theft allocate resources, but force is also the way the state transfers wealth from the rich to the poor and establishes the legal framework in which voluntary exchange takes place."],["Value","What we get, whereas price is what we pay. The value of one more unit of a good is its marginal benefit, measured by the maximum price a person is willing to pay."],["Marginal benefit / demand curve","A demand curve is a marginal benefit curve, since willingness to pay determines demand."],["Individual demand","The relationship between the price of a good and the quantity demanded by one person."],["Market demand","The relationship between the price of a good and the quantity demanded by all buyers in the market; the market demand curve is the horizontal sum of the individual demand curves."],["Consumer surplus","The excess of the benefit received from a good over the amount paid for it: marginal benefit minus price, summed over the quantity bought. It is the area under the demand curve and above the price paid, up to the quantity bought."],["Marginal cost / supply curve","Cost is what the producer gives up, price is what the producer receives. Marginal cost is the minimum price a firm is willing to accept for an additional unit, so a supply curve is a marginal cost curve."],["Individual supply","The relationship between the price of a good and the quantity supplied by one producer."],["Market supply","The relationship between the price and the quantity supplied by all producers; the market supply curve is the horizontal sum of the individual supply curves."],["Producer surplus","The excess of the amount received from the sale of a good over the cost of producing it: price minus marginal cost summed over the quantity sold. It is the area below the market price and above the supply curve."],["Total surplus","The sum of consumer surplus and producer surplus; it is maximized when the efficient quantity is produced."],["Efficient use of resources","Resources are used efficiently when marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost."],["Invisible hand","Adam Smith's idea in the Wealth of Nations that competitive markets send resources to their highest-valued use: consumers and producers pursue self-interest and market transactions generate an efficient use of resources."],["Market failure","Arises when a market delivers an inefficient outcome, either underproduction or overproduction."],["Deadweight loss","The decrease in total surplus (the gray triangle) when production is inefficient; it is a social loss."],["Externality","A cost or benefit that affects someone other than the seller or the buyer of a good."],["Public good","A good that benefits everyone and from which no one can be excluded."],["Free-rider problem","It is in everyone's self-interest to avoid paying for a public good, which leads to underproduction."],["Common resource","A resource owned by no one but usable by everyone (forests, clean air, water sources)."],["Tragedy of the commons","It is in everyone's self-interest to ignore the costs of their own use of a common resource that fall on others, leading to overproduction."],["Monopoly","A firm that is the sole provider of a good or service."],["Transactions costs","The opportunity cost of making trades in a market."],["Utilitarianism","The principle that we should strive to achieve \"the greatest happiness for the greatest number\"."],["Symmetry principle","The requirement that people in similar situations be treated similarly; in economics it means equality of opportunity, not equality of income."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Resource allocation methods"},{"t":"p","x":"Scarce resources might be allocated by market price, command, majority rule, contest, first-come first-served, lottery, personal characteristics or force. There are many methods and no single one is best in all scenarios; the question is which leads to efficient results."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Benefit, cost and surplus"},{"t":"p","x":"Pizza example for demand: at $1 a slice Lisa demands 30 slices and Nick 10, so market demand is 40 slices. Lisa values her 10th slice at $2, so her consumer surplus from it is $1 (value minus price). At $1 a slice Lisa spends $30, Nick $10, together $40, and consumer surplus is the value in excess of expenditure."},{"t":"p","x":"Pizza example for supply: at $15 a pizza Maria supplies 100 and Max 50, so market supply is 150 pizzas. Maria is willing to produce the 50th pizza for $10, so her surplus on it is $5. Producer surplus is the value of pizza sold in excess of the cost of producing it. Colors used in the slides: consumer surplus green, producer surplus blue, total expenditure blue, total cost red, deadweight loss gray."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Is the competitive market efficient?"},{"t":"p","x":"At competitive equilibrium quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. When production is less than the equilibrium quantity, marginal social benefit exceeds marginal social cost; when it is greater, marginal social cost exceeds marginal social benefit; at the equilibrium quantity they are equal and total surplus is maximized."},{"t":"p","x":"Underproduction: the efficient quantity is 10,000 pizzas a day; restricting production to 5,000 is inefficient and creates a deadweight loss. Overproduction: raising production to 15,000 also creates a deadweight loss, because the cost of the extra units exceeds their benefit."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Sources of market failure"},{"t":"p","x":"In competitive markets underproduction or overproduction arises with:"},{"t":"p","x":"1. Price and quantity regulations: price regulations block price adjustments and quantity regulations (such as farm output limits) lead to underproduction."},{"t":"p","x":"2. Taxes and subsidies: taxes raise the price buyers pay and lower the price sellers receive, so they decrease the quantity produced (underproduction); subsidies do the opposite and lead to overproduction."},{"t":"p","x":"3. Externalities: an electric utility burning coal creates an external cost (acid rain) that it ignores, so overproduction results; an apartment owner who does not install a smoke detector ignores her neighbor's marginal benefit, so underproduction results."},{"t":"p","x":"4. Public goods and common resources: free riding gives underproduction of public goods (public parks, street lighting); the tragedy of the commons gives overproduction of common resources."},{"t":"p","x":"5. Monopoly: a profit-maximizing monopoly sets a price that makes it produce too little, so underproduction results."},{"t":"p","x":"6. High transactions costs: to use the market price as the allocator it must be worth bearing the opportunity cost of establishing a market; some markets are too costly to operate, so the market might underproduce."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Alternatives to the market"},{"t":"p","x":"When a market is inefficient, majority rule is often used but has its own shortcomings: a group pursuing its members' self-interest can become the majority, and votes must be translated into actions by bureaucrats with their own agendas. No one mechanism is efficient everywhere, but supplemented by majority rule, bypassed inside firms by command systems and occasionally using first-come first-served, markets do an amazingly good job."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Is the competitive market fair?"},{"t":"p","x":"Ideas about fairness divide into two groups: it is not fair if the result is not fair, and it is not fair if the rules are not fair."},{"t":"p","x":"It is not fair if the result is not fair (utilitarianism): if everyone gets the same marginal utility from a given amount of income and the marginal benefit of income decreases as income rises, taking a dollar from a richer person (Jerry) and giving it to a poorer person (Tom) increases total benefit, so only equal incomes achieve the greatest happiness. This ignores the cost of making income transfers, which leads to the big tradeoff between efficiency and fairness; because of it, John Rawls proposed that income should be redistributed until the poorest person is as well off as possible."},{"t":"p","x":"It is not fair if the rules are not fair (symmetry principle): Robert Nozick proposed two rules, that the state must create and enforce laws that establish and protect private property, and that private property may be transferred only by voluntary exchange. Hence if resources are allocated efficiently, they may also be allocated fairly."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Pizza market: at $1/slice, Lisa buys 30 slices and Nick buys 10 (market demand = horizontal sum = 40 slices). Consumer surplus is the green triangle under demand and above $1, summed to 40 slices. At $15/pizza, Maria supplies 100 and Max supplies 50 (market supply = 150 pizzas); producer surplus is the blue triangle above the supply curve and below $15."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **The demand and supply schedules for a good are Qd = 100 − 5P and Qs = 5P − 20. (a) Find the competitive equilibrium price and quantity. (b) Calculate consumer surplus and producer surplus at equilibrium. (c) What is total surplus?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Suppose a quota restricts output of the good in Q1 to 30 units. Find (a) the price consumers are willing to pay for the 30th unit, (b) the marginal cost of producing the 30th unit, and (c) the resulting deadweight loss."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **Resource allocation by \"first-come, first-served\" works best when: (a) the resource can serve many people at once; (b) the resource can serve only one person at a time in sequence; (c) there is no way to identify who values the resource most; (d) the government wants to redistribute wealth."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **Using the same demand and supply as Q1, suppose a monopolist restricts output to 20 units (instead of the efficient 40). Is this over- or under-production? Calculate the resulting deadweight loss."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Explain the difference between the \"fair rules\" view (symmetry principle / Nozick) and the \"fair results\" view (utilitarianism / Rawls) of fairness, giving one example of a policy each view would evaluate differently."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **(a) Set 100 − 5P = 5P − 20 → 120 = 10P → P = $12; Q = 100 − 5(12) = 40 units. (b) Consumer surplus = ½ × (demand's price-intercept − equilibrium price) × Q = ½ × (20 − 12) × 40 = $160 (demand hits P-axis at P = $20 when Qd = 0). Producer surplus = ½ × (equilibrium price − supply's price-intercept) × Q = ½ × (12 − 4) × 40 = $160 (supply hits P-axis at P = $4 when Qs = 0). (c) Total surplus = $160 + $160 = $320.\n**A2. **(a) Demand price at Q = 30: 30 = 100 − 5P → P = $14 (buyers' marginal benefit). (b) Marginal cost at Q = 30: 30 = 5P − 20 → P = $10. (c) Deadweight loss = ½ × (40 − 30) × ($14 − $10) = ½ × 10 × 4 = $20.\n**A3. **(b) — e.g. a queue at a checkout or a single ticket window; the resource is used up sequentially by one customer at a time.\n**A4. **Underproduction (20 units is below the efficient 40). Demand price at Q = 20: 20 = 100 − 5P → P = $16. Marginal cost at Q = 20: 20 = 5P − 20 → P = $8. Deadweight loss = ½ × (40 − 20) × ($16 − $8) = ½ × 20 × 8 = $80.\n**A5. **The fair-RULES view judges a policy by whether it preserves voluntary exchange and property rights, regardless of the resulting income distribution — e.g. it would call a high inheritance tax unfair because it interferes with voluntarily-acquired property, even if it reduces inequality. The fair-RESULTS view (utilitarianism/Rawls) judges a policy by the distribution of outcomes it produces — e.g. it would favor progressive income redistribution because it raises the well-being of the worst-off, even though it requires compulsory transfers that the fair-rules view would call unfair."}]},"6":{"title":"Government Actions in Markets","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"After studying this chapter you will be able to:"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how a rent ceiling creates a housing shortage and inefficiency."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how minimum wage laws create unemployment."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the effects of a tax."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the effects of production quotas and subsidies."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how markets for illegal goods work."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Price ceiling (price cap)","A regulation that makes it illegal to charge a price higher than a specified level. Applied to a housing market it is called a rent ceiling."],["Rent ceiling","A price ceiling in the housing market. Set above the equilibrium rent it has no effect; set below, it creates a housing shortage, increased search activity and an illicit (black) market."],["Search activity","The time spent looking for someone with whom to do business; it increases when a price is regulated and there is a shortage."],["Price floor","A regulation that makes it illegal to trade at a price lower than a specified level. Applied to labor markets it is called a minimum wage."],["Minimum wage","A price floor in the labor market. Set below the equilibrium wage it has no effect; set above, it creates a surplus of labor, i.e. unemployment."],["Tax incidence","The division of the burden of a tax between buyers and sellers. The price may rise by the full amount of the tax (buyers pay), by a lesser amount (shared) or not at all (sellers pay)."],["Tax wedge","A tax is like a wedge driven between the price the buyer pays and the price the seller receives; the equilibrium quantity is where the vertical gap between the curves equals the tax."],["Benefits principle","The proposition that people should pay taxes equal to the benefits they receive from the services provided by government."],["Ability-to-pay principle","The proposition that people should pay taxes according to how easily they can bear the burden; it can justify high income tax rates on high incomes."],["Production quota","An upper limit to the quantity of a good that may be produced during a specified period."],["Subsidy","A payment made by the government to a producer."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A housing market with a rent ceiling"},{"t":"p","x":"Example (Figure 6.1): equilibrium rent is $1,400 a month and the rent ceiling is $1,200, so the equilibrium is in the illegal region. At the ceiling quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, a housing shortage. Because the legal price cannot eliminate the shortage, other mechanisms operate: increased search activity (the opportunity cost of housing equals the regulated rent plus the opportunity cost of search, so it exceeds the unregulated rent) and an illicit market (illegal arrangements between renters and landlords at rents above the ceiling, generally above the unregulated rent). With quantity supplied at 60 thousand, someone is willing to pay up to $1,600 a month."},{"t":"p","x":"Inefficiency: the ceiling decreases the quantity of housing supplied below the efficient quantity, so marginal social benefit exceeds marginal social cost and a deadweight loss arises; producer surplus and consumer surplus shrink, with a potential loss from increased search."},{"t":"p","x":"Fairness: on the fair-rules view a rent ceiling is unfair because it blocks voluntary exchange; on the fair-results view it is unfair because it does not generally benefit the poor. Scarce housing is allocated by lottery (e.g. sale of Home Ownership Scheme flats), first-come first-served (those with the most foresight get on the list first, private housing) or discrimination (the owner's self-interest, the second-hand market), and none of these leads to a fair outcome."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A labor market with a minimum wage"},{"t":"p","x":"Example: the equilibrium wage is $14 an hour and the minimum wage is $15, so the equilibrium wage is in the illegal region. The quantity of labor supplied exceeds the quantity demanded, creating unemployment; with only 20 million hours demanded some workers would supply the last hour demanded for $13."},{"t":"p","x":"Fairness: the minimum wage is unfair on both views: the result is unfair because only those who have jobs and keep them benefit, and the rule is unfair because it blocks voluntary exchange, since firms would hire more and people would work more. Other economists disagree and claim the ceteris paribus condition was violated in studies (timing of hiring decisions and regional differences)."},{"t":"p","x":"Inefficiency: the quantity of labor employed is below the efficient quantity. The supply of labor measures the marginal social cost of labor to workers (leisure forgone) and the demand for labor measures the marginal social benefit (value of goods produced). A deadweight loss arises, and the potential loss from increased job search decreases both workers' and firms' surplus."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Taxes and tax incidence"},{"t":"p","x":"Everything you earn and most things you buy are taxed, but it is not obvious who really bears a tax and lawmakers do not decide who pays. A tax on sellers and a tax on buyers have the same effect (equivalence). Cigarette example: with no tax the equilibrium price is $6 a pack and 350 million packs are bought a year. With a $3 tax on sellers, supply decreases, the price buyers pay rises to $8, the price sellers receive falls to $5 and quantity falls to 325 million. A $3 tax on buyers shifts demand down instead, with the same result: buyers pay $2 of the tax and sellers pay $1."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Tax incidence and elasticity"},{"t":"p","x":"The division of the tax depends on the elasticities of demand and supply:"},{"t":"p","x":"1. Perfectly inelastic demand: buyers pay the entire tax. Perfectly elastic demand: sellers pay the entire tax. The more inelastic the demand, the larger the buyers' share."},{"t":"p","x":"2. Perfectly inelastic supply: sellers pay the entire tax. Perfectly elastic supply: buyers pay the entire tax. The more elastic the supply, the larger the buyers' share."},{"t":"p","x":"In practice taxes are levied on goods with inelastic demand or supply. Alcohol, tobacco and gasoline have inelastic demand, so buyers pay most of the tax; labor has a low elasticity of supply, so the worker pays most of the income tax and the social security tax."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Taxes and efficiency"},{"t":"p","x":"Except in the extreme cases of perfectly inelastic demand or supply, a tax creates inefficiency. Example: a $200 tax on tablet computers. With no tax marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost and total surplus is maximized; the tax decreases the quantity bought, raises the buyer's price and lowers the seller's price, marginal social benefit exceeds marginal social cost, the decreased quantity creates a deadweight loss and the tax revenue takes part of total surplus."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Production quotas and subsidies"},{"t":"p","x":"Intervention in markets for farm products takes two main forms: production quotas and subsidies."},{"t":"p","x":"Quota example: with no quota the price is $30 a ton and 60 million tons a year are produced; a quota of 40 million tons raises the price to $50 and marginal cost falls to $20. Production is inefficient (marginal social benefit exceeds marginal social cost) and producers have an incentive to cheat."},{"t":"p","x":"Subsidy example: with no subsidy the price is $40 a ton and 40 million tons are produced; a $20 subsidy lowers the cost of production (the new supply curve is S minus subsidy), the market price falls to $30 and farmers increase output to 60 million tons, but the marginal cost of the 60th ton rises to $50. Farmers receive $30 plus the $20 subsidy, $50 a ton. Marginal social cost exceeds marginal social benefit, so overproduction is inefficient."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Markets for illegal goods"},{"t":"p","x":"The government prohibits trade in some goods such as illegal drugs, yet markets exist for them. The opportunity cost of something is the market price plus any other costs of buying. Starting from a free market for a drug such as marijuana (equilibrium E, price PC, quantity QC):"},{"t":"p","x":"1. Penalties on sellers decrease supply (S plus the cost of breaking the law): the market price rises and the quantity decreases."},{"t":"p","x":"2. Penalties on buyers decrease demand (D minus the cost of breaking the law): the market price falls and the quantity decreases, but the buyer's opportunity cost rises above PC because the buyer pays the market price plus the cost of breaking the law."},{"t":"p","x":"3. Penalties on both decrease demand and supply: the quantity falls to QP, the market price is PC, the buyer pays PB and the seller receives PS."},{"t":"p","x":"Legalizing and taxing drugs: a high enough tax rate would cut consumption to the level that occurs when trade is illegal; arguments that extend beyond economics surround this choice."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Cigarette tax: with no tax, equilibrium price = $6/pack, quantity = 350 million packs/year. A $3/pack tax is imposed. New equilibrium: buyers pay $8/pack, sellers receive $5/pack (an $8 − $5 = $3 wedge, matching the tax), and quantity falls to 325 million packs/year. Buyers bear $2 of the $3 tax and sellers bear $1, because demand for cigarettes is relatively more inelastic than supply."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **The equilibrium rent for an apartment is $1,500/month with 80,000 units rented. A rent ceiling is imposed at $1,200/month; at that price, quantity supplied is 60,000 and quantity demanded is 100,000. Is the ceiling binding? What is the size of the housing shortage?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **The demand for and supply of unskilled labor (in thousands of hours/week) are Qd = 1,300 − 50W and Qs = 50W − 300, where W is the hourly wage. (a) Find the equilibrium wage and quantity of labor hired. (b) The government sets a minimum wage of $18/hour. Find quantities demanded and supplied, and the resulting unemployment. (c) Calculate the deadweight loss created by the minimum wage."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **Using the cigarette-tax example above (no-tax equilibrium $6/pack, 350 million packs; $3 tax → buyers pay $8, sellers receive $5, quantity falls to 325 million): (a) state the buyers' and sellers' shares of the tax per pack; (b) calculate total tax revenue; (c) calculate the deadweight loss."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **If demand for a good is perfectly inelastic and supply is upward-sloping, who bears a tax placed on that good?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Explain, using the concepts of marginal social benefit (MSB) and marginal social cost (MSC), why a subsidy paid to farmers leads to overproduction and a deadweight loss."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **Yes, binding, because $1,200 is below the $1,500 equilibrium rent. Shortage = quantity demanded − quantity supplied = 100,000 − 60,000 = 40,000 units.\n**A2. **(a) Set 1,300 − 50W = 50W − 300 → 1,600 = 100W → W = $16/hour; Q = 1,300 − 50(16) = 500 thousand hours/week. (b) At W = $18: Qd = 1,300 − 50(18) = 400 thousand hours; Qs = 50(18) − 300 = 600 thousand hours. Unemployment = 600 − 400 = 200 thousand hours/week. (c) At Q = 400 (thousand hrs), inverse demand price = (1,300 − 400)/50 = $18 (MSB); inverse supply price = (400 + 300)/50 = $14 (MSC). DWL = ½ × (500 − 400) × ($18 − $14) = ½ × 100 × 4 = 200 (thousand $) = $200,000/week.\n**A3. **(a) Buyers pay $2/pack more ($6→$8); sellers receive $1/pack less ($6→$5) — of the $3 tax, buyers bear two-thirds and sellers bear one-third, because demand is relatively less elastic than supply here. (b) Tax revenue = $3 × 325 million = $975 million/year. (c) DWL = ½ × $3 × (350 − 325) million = ½ × 3 × 25 million = $37.5 million/year.\n**A4. **Buyers bear the entire tax — with perfectly inelastic demand, quantity cannot change, so the price buyers pay rises by the full amount of the tax while the price sellers receive stays the same.\n**A5. **A subsidy lowers the effective marginal cost faced by farmers (MC − subsidy), so they expand output beyond the point where MSB = MSC. Beyond the efficient quantity, MSC (the true resource cost, ignoring the subsidy) exceeds MSB, so each additional unit produced destroys value rather than creating it. The subsidy causes overproduction, and the deadweight loss is the triangle of lost total surplus on units where MSC > MSB, financed at taxpayer expense."}]},"8":{"title":"Utility and Demand","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"After studying this chapter you will be able to:"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the limits to consumption and describe preferences using the concept of utility."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the marginal utility theory of consumer choice."},{"t":"li","x":"Use marginal utility theory to predict the effects of changes in prices and incomes and to explain the paradox of value."},{"t":"li","x":"Describe some new ways of explaining consumer choices."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Consumption possibilities","All the things that a consumer can afford to buy; limited by income and prices."],["Budget line","Shows the limits of a consumer's consumption possibilities, given income and prices. The consumer can afford any point on or inside it and cannot afford any point outside it."],["Preferences","A consumer's likes and dislikes; the choice made depends on preferences."],["Utility","The benefit or satisfaction a consumer gets from consuming a good or service."],["Total utility (TU)","The total benefit a person gets from the consumption of goods; generally more consumption gives more total utility."],["Marginal utility (MU)","The change in total utility that results from a one-unit increase in the quantity of a good consumed."],["Diminishing marginal utility","The principle that marginal utility from a good decreases as the quantity of the good consumed increases."],["Consumer equilibrium","The situation in which the consumer has allocated all available income in the way that maximizes total utility, given prices."],["Marginal utility per dollar","Marginal utility from a good divided by its price (MU/P): the marginal utility from spending one more dollar on it."],["Utility-maximizing rule","A consumer's total utility is maximized by spending all available income and equalizing the marginal utility per dollar for all goods."],["Behavioral economics","Studies the ways in which limits on the human brain's ability to compute and implement rational decisions influence economic behavior, both the decisions people make and the consequences for the way markets work."],["Bounded rationality","Rationality that is bounded by the computing power of the human brain; faced with uncertainty consumers use rules of thumb, the views of others or gut instinct."],["Bounded willpower","Less-than-perfect willpower that prevents us from making a decision that we know, when implementing it, we will later regret."],["Bounded self-interest","Limited self-interest that sometimes results in suppressing our own interests to help others; its main applications are in finance (uncertainty) and savings (the future)."],["Endowment effect","The tendency for people to value something more highly simply because they own it."],["Neuroeconomics","The study of the activity of the human brain when a person makes an economic decision."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Consumption possibilities and the budget line"},{"t":"p","x":"Choices are influenced by consumption possibilities and preferences. Lisa buys only movies and soda, has $40, a movie costs $8 and a case of soda $4; she can afford the combinations at points A to F, whose line is her budget line. Some goods are indivisible and bought in whole units; divisible goods (coffee beans, flour, gasoline) can be bought in any quantity. Consumption possibilities change when income or prices change: a rise in income shifts the budget line outward and leaves its slope unchanged, while a rise in a price changes the slope and shrinks the possibilities. The budget line shows what is possible; preferences determine which possibility is chosen."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Total and marginal utility"},{"t":"p","x":"Table 8.1 shows Lisa's total utility schedule: total utility from a good rises as the quantity rises, while marginal utility falls (diminishing marginal utility). Figure 8.2(a) shows total utility from soda increasing, with the bars showing the extra utility (marginal utility) of each case; Figure 8.2(b) illustrates diminishing marginal utility. The key assumption is that the household chooses the consumption possibility that maximizes total utility."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Utility-maximizing choice: the spreadsheet solution"},{"t":"p","x":"Find the just-affordable combinations, find the total utility of each, and the highest is the consumer's choice. Each row of Table 8.2 exhausts Lisa's $40. For 1 movie and 8 cases of soda she gets 50 units from the movie and 248 from the soda, total 298. Lisa maximizes total utility at 2 movies and 6 cases of soda a month (90 plus 315 units); this is her consumer equilibrium."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Utility-maximizing choice: choosing at the margin"},{"t":"p","x":"A more natural way is to ask whether spending a dollar more or less on a good would bring more total utility. Equalizing marginal utility per dollar across all goods maximizes utility, just as in Chapter 5 competitive equilibrium is efficient because marginal benefit equals marginal cost. Calling the marginal utility of movies MUM and their price PM, and of soda MUS and PS, compare MUM/PM and MUS/PS."},{"t":"p","x":"How the utility-maximizing rule works (Figure 8.3): in row B, MUS/PS < MUM/PM, so Lisa spends too much on soda and too little on movies; if she spends less on soda and more on movies, MUS increases and MUM decreases. In row D, MUS/PS > MUM/PM, so she spends too little on soda and too much on movies, and the adjustment goes the other way. In row C, MUS/PS = MUM/PM and she is maximizing utility."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Predictions from the theory"},{"t":"p","x":"1. A fall in the price of a movie: MUM/PM rises, so before changing quantities MUM/PM > MUS/PS; Lisa sees more movies to drive down MUM and restore equality, so the quantity of movies demanded increases (a movement along the demand curve; the demand curve slopes downward)."},{"t":"p","x":"2. The effect on another good: when the price of a movie falls she also drinks less soda, so the demand for soda decreases (a shift of the soda demand curve) since movies and soda are substitutes here (Figure 8.4)."},{"t":"p","x":"3. A rise in the price of soda: MUS/PS falls so MUS/PS < MUM/PM; Lisa drinks less soda to raise MUS and sees more movies, so the quantity of soda demanded decreases (a movement along the demand curve, Figure 8.5)."},{"t":"p","x":"4. A rise in income: when Lisa's income rises from $40 to $56 a month she buys more of both movies and soda, so both are normal goods (Table 8.5, Figure 8.6)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The paradox of value"},{"t":"p","x":"Water, essential to life, is far cheaper than diamonds, which are not essential. The paradox is resolved by distinguishing total utility from marginal utility. We use so much water that the marginal utility is small but total utility is large; we buy few diamonds so marginal utility is large but total utility is small. For water the price is low, total utility large and marginal utility small; for diamonds the price is high, total utility small and marginal utility high; marginal utility per dollar is equal for both. Value and consumer surplus: the supply of water is perfectly elastic so the quantity consumed and the consumer surplus are large, while diamond supply is perfectly inelastic so the price is high and consumer surplus small."},{"t":"p","x":"Utility is like temperature: both are abstract concepts with arbitrary units of measurement, but utility helps us predict that people buy more of a good when its price falls and more of most goods when income increases, as temperature predicts when water turns to ice or steam. Utility ordering is ordinal, not cardinal, so one person's utility cannot be directly compared with another's."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"New ways of explaining consumer choices"},{"t":"p","x":"Behavioral economics identifies three impediments to rational choice: bounded rationality, bounded willpower and bounded self-interest. The endowment effect is one observed behavior. Neuroeconomics finds that different decisions activate different areas of the brain: the pre-frontal cortex (where memories are stored and data analyzed; decisions there might be deemed rational) and the hippocampus (where memories of anxiety and fear are stored; decisions there might be deemed irrational)."},{"t":"p","x":"The controversy: should economics explain the decisions we observe or what goes on inside people's heads? For most economists the goal is to explain observed decisions, not what goes on inside people's heads."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (original numbers, same method as the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Ben has $32/month for e-books ($8 each) and coffee ($4/cup). Marginal utility: e-books 40, 32, 24, 16 (1st–4th); coffee 20, 16, 14, 12, 10, 8, 6, 4 (1st–8th)."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Combination (E, C)","TU from e-books","TU from coffee","Total utility"],["0 e-books, 8 coffees","0","90","90"],["1 e-book, 6 coffees","40","80","120"],["2 e-books, 4 coffees","72","62","134 ← maximum"],["3 e-books, 2 coffees","96","36","132"],["4 e-books, 0 coffees","112","0","112"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Ben's consumer equilibrium is 2 e-books and 4 cups of coffee (total utility = 134, the maximum among affordable combinations). Check: at this point the next (3rd) e-book has MU = 24 → MU/P = 3; the last (4th) coffee has MU = 12 → MU/P = 3 — equal, confirming equilibrium."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **Using Ben's data above: at the combination 1 e-book and 6 coffees, is Ben maximizing utility? Use marginal utility per dollar to explain, and state which direction he should adjust his spending."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Confirm the principle of diminishing marginal utility using Ben's coffee schedule (20, 16, 14, 12, 10, 8, 6, 4). Does it hold throughout?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **The paradox of value (water is cheap, diamonds are expensive, despite water being essential) is resolved by recognizing that: (a) water has higher total AND marginal utility than diamonds; (b) price reflects marginal utility, not total utility; (c) diamonds have more total utility than water; (d) diamonds are a public good."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **Define behavioral economics' three \"bounds\" on rational choice (bounded rationality, bounded willpower, bounded self-interest) and give one everyday example of each."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **If the price of a good falls so that MUgood/Pgood rises above MUother/Pother, a utility-maximizing consumer will: (a) buy less of the good until MU/P is re-equalized; (b) buy more of the good, which lowers its MU until equality is restored; (c) buy the same amount of both goods; (d) stop buying the other good entirely."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **No. The MU per dollar of the next (2nd) e-book = 32/8 = 4; the MU per dollar of the last (6th) coffee consumed = 8/4 = 2. Since 4 > 2, Ben gains more extra satisfaction per dollar from an additional e-book than from the coffee he is currently buying at the margin — he is not maximizing utility and should buy MORE e-books and LESS coffee (moving toward 2 e-books, 4 coffees, where MU/P is equalized).\n**A2. **Yes — MU falls continuously as quantity rises: 20 → 16 → 14 → 12 → 10 → 8 → 6 → 4, a strictly decreasing sequence with every additional cup, confirming diminishing marginal utility holds throughout this schedule.\n**A3. **(b) — Price is determined by marginal utility (the value of the LAST unit), not total utility. Water has huge total utility but low marginal utility because so much is consumed (its supply is abundant/elastic); diamonds have small total utility but high marginal utility because so little is consumed (supply is scarce/inelastic). At equilibrium, marginal utility per dollar is equalized across goods even though total utilities differ enormously.\n**A4. **Bounded rationality — the human brain has limited computing power, so people use rules of thumb instead of fully optimizing (e.g. picking the first \"good enough\" phone plan rather than comparing every option). Bounded willpower — people know a choice is against their long-term interest but do it anyway (e.g. snoozing the alarm despite planning an early start). Bounded self-interest — people sometimes act to help others at a personal cost (e.g. tipping generously at a restaurant they will never visit again).\n**A5. **(b) — the consumer increases quantity of the now cheaper/more attractive good; diminishing marginal utility means MU falls as more is bought, restoring MU/P equality across goods."}]},"9":{"title":"Possibilities, Preferences, and Choices","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"After studying this chapter you will be able to:"},{"t":"li","x":"Describe a household's budget line and show how it changes when prices or income change."},{"t":"li","x":"Use indifference curves to map preferences and explain the principle of diminishing marginal rate of substitution."},{"t":"li","x":"Predict the effects of changes in prices and income on consumption choices."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Budget line","Describes the limits to a household's consumption choices, given its income and the prices of goods. The household can afford any point on or inside it, but none outside."],["Budget equation","Expenditure = income: PS·QS + PM·QM = Y. Rearranged, QS = Y/PS − (PM/PS)·QM."],["Real income","Income expressed as a quantity of goods the household can afford to buy; Lisa's real income in terms of soda is Y/PS, where the budget line meets the y-axis."],["Relative price","The price of one good divided by the price of another good; it is the magnitude of the slope of the budget line and shows how many cases of soda must be forgone to see an additional movie."],["Indifference curve","A line that shows combinations of goods among which a consumer is indifferent."],["Preference map","A series of indifference curves; the consumer prefers any point on a higher curve to any point on a lower one."],["Marginal rate of substitution (MRS)","The rate at which a person is willing to give up good y to get an additional unit of good x while remaining indifferent (on the same indifference curve); its magnitude is the slope of the indifference curve. A steep curve means a high MRS, a flat curve a low MRS."],["Diminishing marginal rate of substitution","The general tendency for a person to be willing to give up less of good y to get one more unit of good x, while remaining indifferent, as the quantity of x increases; the key assumption of consumer theory."],["Price effect","The effect of a change in the price of a good on the quantity of the good consumed."],["Income effect","The effect of a change in income on buying plans."],["Substitution effect","The effect of a change in price on the quantity bought when the consumer remains on the same indifference curve."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Consumption possibilities and the budget line"},{"t":"p","x":"Lisa has $40, a movie costs $8 and a case of soda $4; she can afford the combinations A to F (movies are an indivisible good, gasoline a divisible one). The budget line is a constraint. Dividing the budget equation by PS gives the line with Y/PS as intercept and PM/PS as slope."},{"t":"p","x":"A change in the price of the good on the x-axis changes the slope of the budget line (Figure 9.2(a): rotation after a change in the relative price of movies). A change in money income brings a parallel shift, with unchanged slope because the relative price does not change (Figure 9.2(b), a fall in income)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Preferences and indifference curves"},{"t":"p","x":"At point C Lisa sees 2 movies and drinks 6 cases of soda a month. She can sort all combinations into those preferred to C, not preferred to C, and just as good as C; the indifference curve joins the points as good as C (G is such a point). She prefers any point above the curve to any on it, and any on it to any below. I0 lies below I1 and I2 above it, so she prefers J on I2 to C or G."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Marginal rate of substitution"},{"t":"p","x":"Figure 9.4: at C Lisa is willing to give up 10 cases of soda to see 5 more movies, an MRS of 2; at G she is willing to give up 4.5 cases to see 9 more movies, an MRS of ½. The MRS diminishes along the curve."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Degree of substitutability"},{"t":"p","x":"The shape of the indifference curves reveals the degree of substitutability between two goods; Figure 9.5 shows ordinary goods, perfect substitutes and perfect complements."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Best affordable choice"},{"t":"p","x":"The consumer's best affordable choice (1) is on the budget line, (2) is on the highest attainable indifference curve and (3) has an MRS equal to the relative price. At F Lisa's MRS exceeds the relative price, at H it is less, and at C it equals PM/PS. Where MRS equals relative price there are no unrealized gains from substituting one good for another."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A change in price and a change in income"},{"t":"p","x":"Price effect (Figure 9.7): when the price of a movie falls from $8 to $4 the budget line rotates outward and Lisa's best affordable point moves from C to J; tracking the quantity of movies against the price generates her demand curve (a movement from A to B along it). Income effect (Figure 9.8): when income decreases with no change in prices the budget line shifts leftward, her best affordable point moves from J to K and her demand for movies decreases, a leftward shift of the demand curve."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Substitution effect and income effect"},{"t":"p","x":"For a normal good a fall in price always increases the quantity consumed. Split the move from C to J into two parts. To isolate the substitution effect give Lisa a hypothetical pay cut so she is back on her original indifference curve with the movie price at $4: her best affordable point is K, and the move from C to K is the substitution effect. The direction of the substitution effect never varies: when the relative price falls the consumer substitutes more of that good for others; it is the first reason the demand curve slopes downward. To isolate the income effect, reverse the pay cut and restore her income, moving from K to J (indifference curve I2). For a normal good the income effect is positive and reinforces the substitution effect, the second reason demand slopes downward."},{"t":"p","x":"For an inferior good, when income increases the quantity bought decreases: the income effect is negative and works against the substitution effect, but as long as the substitution effect dominates the demand curve still slopes downward. If the negative income effect were stronger, a lower price would bring a lower quantity demanded and the demand curve would slope upward; this does not appear to occur in the real world."},{"t":"p","x":"The best affordable choices determine spending patterns, and changes in prices and incomes change the best affordable point and the consumption pattern."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Lisa's budget: $40 income, movie $8, soda $4 → budget line runs from (0 movies, 10 soda) to (5 movies, 0 soda). At point C (2 movies, 6 soda) her MRS = 2 (she'd give up 2 sodas per extra movie), matching the relative price PM/PS = 8/4 = 2 — this is her best affordable point."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **Maya has $60/week to spend on books (price $12) and movies (price $6). Write her budget equation (with movies M on the vertical axis and books B on the horizontal axis), and state the vertical intercept, horizontal intercept, and slope of her budget line."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **If the price of a book in Q1 falls to $6 (movies unchanged at $6), write the new budget equation and describe how the budget line changes (which intercept moves, and how the slope changes)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **At a point on Maya's indifference curve, she is willing to give up 8 movies to get 2 more books while remaining equally satisfied. What is her MRS of books for movies at that point? Using the ORIGINAL prices from Q1 (book $12, movie $6), is she at her best affordable point? If not, what should she do?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **Indifference curves for perfect complements (e.g. left shoes and right shoes) have what shape: (a) straight lines with constant slope; (b) L-shaped (right angles); (c) bowed toward the origin; (d) upward sloping?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Explain why, for a normal good, the substitution effect and income effect of a price decrease work in the SAME direction, and contrast this with an inferior good."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **Budget equation: 12B + 6M = 60, i.e. M = 10 − 2B. Vertical intercept (B = 0): M = 10 movies (her real income in movies). Horizontal intercept (M = 0): B = 5 books. Slope = −2 (the relative price of a book in terms of movies, PB/PM = 12/6 = 2).\n**A2. **New equation: 6B + 6M = 60, i.e. M = 10 − B. The vertical intercept stays at M = 10 (unchanged, since the price of movies and income are unchanged). The horizontal intercept moves outward from B = 5 to B = 10. The slope becomes −1 (flatter than before) — the budget line rotates outward around the unchanged vertical intercept, becoming flatter because books are now relatively cheaper.\n**A3. **MRS = 8 movies ÷ 2 books = 4 (she is willing to give up 4 movies per additional book at this point). The relative price is PB/PM = 12/6 = 2. Since her MRS (4) exceeds the relative price (2), she values an additional book more than what the market requires her to sacrifice — she is NOT at her best affordable point and should buy MORE books and FEWER movies; as she does, diminishing MRS will lower her MRS toward 2, the point where MRS = relative price.\n**A4. **(b) — perfect complements are consumed only in fixed proportions, so the indifference curve is L-shaped: extra units of only one good add no extra utility without the matching quantity of the other.\n**A5. **For a normal good: the substitution effect always pushes the consumer toward the good whose relative price fell (this is universal for any price decrease). The income effect is positive for a normal good — a price fall raises real income, and a consumer with more real income buys more of a normal good — so it reinforces the substitution effect, guaranteeing quantity demanded rises (the law of demand). For an INFERIOR good, the income effect is negative (higher real income → LESS of the inferior good is bought), which works AGAINST the substitution effect. As long as the (positive) substitution effect is larger in magnitude than the (negative) income effect, demand still slopes down overall; only in the theoretical Giffen-good case would the negative income effect dominate and reverse the law of demand."}]},"11":{"title":"Output and Costs","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"After studying this chapter you will be able to:"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish between short run and long run."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain and illustrate a firm's short-run product curves."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain and derive a firm's short-run cost curves."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain and derive a firm's long-run average cost curve."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Short run","A time frame in which the quantity of one or more resources used in production is fixed. For most firms the capital (the plant) is fixed, while labor, raw materials and energy can be changed. Short-run decisions are easily reversed."],["Long run","A time frame in which the quantities of all resources, including the plant size, can be varied. Long-run decisions are not easily reversed."],["Sunk cost","A cost incurred by the firm that cannot be changed; if a plant has no resale value the amount paid for it is a sunk cost. Sunk costs are irrelevant to current decisions."],["Total product (TP)","The total output produced in a given period."],["Marginal product (MP) of labor","The change in total product that results from a one-unit increase in the quantity of labor employed, all other inputs remaining the same."],["Average product (AP) of labor","Total product divided by the quantity of labor employed."],["Increasing marginal returns","Initially the marginal product of a worker exceeds that of the previous worker; it arises from increased specialization and division of labor."],["Diminishing marginal returns","Eventually the marginal product of a worker is less than that of the previous worker; it arises because each additional worker has less access to capital and less space."],["Law of diminishing returns","As a firm uses more of a variable input with a given quantity of fixed inputs, the marginal product of the variable input eventually diminishes."],["Total cost (TC)","The cost of all resources used: TC = TFC + TVC."],["Total fixed cost (TFC)","The cost of the firm's fixed inputs; it does not change with output."],["Total variable cost (TVC)","The cost of the firm's variable inputs; it changes with output."],["Marginal cost (MC)","The increase in total cost that results from a one-unit increase in total product."],["Average fixed cost (AFC)","Total fixed cost per unit of output."],["Average variable cost (AVC)","Total variable cost per unit of output."],["Average total cost (ATC)","Total cost per unit of output: ATC = AFC + AVC."],["Production function","The relationship between the maximum output attainable and the quantities of both capital and labor."],["Marginal product of capital","The increase in output resulting from a one-unit increase in the amount of capital employed, holding labor constant."],["Long-run average cost curve (LRAC)","The relationship between the lowest attainable average total cost and output when both the plant and labor are varied. It is a planning curve that tells the firm the plant that minimizes the cost of producing a given output."],["Economies of scale","Features of a firm's technology that lead to falling long-run average cost as output increases."],["Diseconomies of scale","Features of a firm's technology that lead to rising long-run average cost as output increases."],["Constant returns to scale","Features of a firm's technology that lead to constant long-run average cost as output increases."],["Minimum efficient scale","The smallest quantity of output at which the long-run average cost reaches its lowest level; if LRAC is U-shaped, the minimum point identifies it."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Decision time frames"},{"t":"p","x":"The firm makes many decisions to achieve profit maximization; some are critical to survival, some are irreversible or very costly to reverse, others are easily reversed. All decisions fall in two time frames: the short run and the long run. Two big ideas: long-run flexibility lets firms produce at lower cost than in the short run, and in the short run production costs vary with output in a predictable way linked to input productivity. Cost conditions are similar for all firms; revenue conditions depend on market constraints (the following chapters)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Short-run technology constraint"},{"t":"p","x":"To increase output in the short run a firm must employ more labor. Three concepts describe the link: total product, marginal product and average product. As labor increases total product increases; marginal product increases initially but eventually decreases; average product eventually decreases."},{"t":"p","x":"The total product curve is similar to the PPF: it separates attainable from unattainable output in the short run. Marginal product example: the first worker produces 4 units, the second 6 (total 10), the third 3 (total 13). The height of each bar is the marginal product, and the MP curve passes through the mid-points of the bars. Almost all processes have increasing marginal returns initially and diminishing marginal returns eventually."},{"t":"p","x":"Average product curve (Figure 11.3): when MP exceeds AP, AP increases; when MP is below AP, AP decreases; when MP equals AP, AP is at its maximum."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Short-run cost"},{"t":"p","x":"Three cost concepts and curves: total, marginal and average cost. Fixed costs do not matter in a firm's output decision, although separating them explains why unit cost is U-shaped. Total fixed cost is the same at each output level, total variable cost increases with output, and total cost is their sum. The TVC curve gets its shape from the TP curve: TP becomes steeper at low output and less steep at high output, while TVC becomes less steep at low output and steeper at high output (the TVC curve is the TP curve flipped over, with output plotted against variable cost; e.g. 1 worker costs $25, 2 workers $50)."},{"t":"p","x":"Marginal cost falls as output increases over the range with increasing marginal returns and rises over the range with diminishing marginal returns. The AFC curve falls as output increases, and the AVC and ATC curves are U-shaped. The MC curve is special: where AVC is falling MC is below AVC, where AVC is rising MC is above it, and at minimum AVC, MC equals AVC; the same holds for ATC."},{"t":"p","x":"Why the curves are U-shaped: AVC is U-shaped because initially MP exceeds AP (AP rises, AVC falls) and eventually MP falls below AP (AP falls, AVC rises). ATC is the vertical sum of AFC and AVC, so its U-shape arises from two opposing forces: spreading total fixed cost over a larger output (AFC slopes downward) and eventually diminishing returns (AVC rises more quickly than AFC falls)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Cost curves and product curves"},{"t":"p","x":"The shapes of cost curves are determined by technology. MC is at its minimum at the same output at which MP is at its maximum; when MP is rising MC is falling. AVC is at its minimum at the same output at which AP is at its maximum; when AP is rising AVC is falling."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Shifts in the cost curves"},{"t":"p","x":"The position of the cost curves depends on technology and the prices of factors of production. An increase in productivity shifts product curves upward and cost curves downward. If a technological advance uses more capital and less labor, fixed costs increase and variable costs decrease, so ATC rises at low output and falls at high output. An increase in a fixed cost shifts the TC and ATC curves upward but not the MC curve; an increase in a variable cost shifts TC, ATC and MC upward."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Long-run cost"},{"t":"p","x":"In the long run all inputs are variable and all costs are variable. The production function exhibits diminishing marginal returns to labor (for a given plant) and to capital (for a quantity of labor). A larger plant produces more output with a given quantity of labor, but for each plant diminishing returns occur, producing a set of short-run U-shaped MC, AVC and ATC curves. The larger the plant, the greater the output at which ATC is at a minimum."},{"t":"p","x":"Sweater example: a firm has four plants (1 to 4 knitting machines), each with its own short-run ATC curve. To produce 13 sweaters a day ATC is $7.69 with plant 1, $6.80 with plant 2, $7.69 with plant 3 and $9.50 with plant 4, so the least-cost way is 2 knitting machines. The long-run average cost curve is made up of the lowest ATC for each output; once the firm has chosen its plant it incurs the costs on that plant's ATC curve."},{"t":"p","x":"Economies of scale, diseconomies of scale and constant returns to scale correspond to the downward-sloping, upward-sloping and flat parts of LRAC; a firm has economies of scale up to some output and then moves into constant returns or diseconomies."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Short-run and long-run decisions in the news"},{"t":"p","x":"Nokia's 2013 job cuts and transfer of employees to India, and Peugeot's 2013 closure of its Aulnay-sous-Bois factory with more than 11,000 layoffs, were class examples for distinguishing short-run decisions from long-run decisions. Technology adoption in agriculture: firms hesitate to adopt costly new equipment, but once proven they rush to adopt it, since firms that cannot do so struggle to survive."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Sweater knitting: a firm choosing between 1, 2, 3 or 4 knitting machines to produce 13 sweaters/day finds ATC = $7.69 (1 machine), $6.80 (2 machines), $7.69 (3 machines), $9.50 (4 machines). The least-cost plant for 13 sweaters/day is 2 machines ($6.80) — this point lies on the long-run average cost curve."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **A firm's total product schedule is: Labor (workers) 0,1,2,3,4,5,6 → Total product 0, 8, 20, 30, 36, 40, 42. Compute marginal product (MP) and average product (AP) at each labor level. At which labor level do increasing marginal returns end and diminishing marginal returns begin?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Using the total product schedule above, suppose the daily wage is $50/worker (the only variable cost) and total fixed cost is $100/day. (a) Complete a table of TVC, TC, AFC, AVC, ATC, and MC at each output level (8, 20, 30, 36, 40, 42 units). (b) At what output is AVC lowest? (c) At what output is ATC lowest? (d) Confirm that MC is below ATC while ATC is falling and above ATC while ATC is rising."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **A firm's average total cost is falling as output increases. This implies that: (a) marginal cost must be falling too; (b) marginal cost is below average total cost; (c) marginal cost is above average total cost; (d) marginal cost equals average fixed cost."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **A firm can build a small, medium, or large plant. The short-run ATC for producing 200 units/day is: Small = $25, Medium = $18, Large = $30. Which plant should the firm use to produce 200 units in the long run? What does the pattern of these three values suggest about economies/diseconomies of scale around this output?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Define minimum efficient scale and explain its significance for how many firms can efficiently operate in an industry."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **MP: L1 = 8, L2 = 12, L3 = 10, L4 = 6, L5 = 4, L6 = 2. AP: L1 = 8.0, L2 = 10.0, L3 = 10.0, L4 = 9.0, L5 = 8.0, L6 = 7.0. Marginal product RISES from L1 (8) to L2 (12) — increasing marginal returns — then FALLS from L3 onward (12→10→6→4→2) — diminishing marginal returns begin at the 3rd worker.\n**A2. **(a) TVC = $50 × L, TC = TVC + $100:  Q=8 (L=1): TVC=$50, TC=$150, AFC=$12.50, AVC=$6.25, ATC=$18.75.  Q=20 (L=2): TVC=$100, TC=$200, AFC=$5.00, AVC=$5.00, ATC=$10.00.  Q=30 (L=3): TVC=$150, TC=$250, AFC=$3.33, AVC=$5.00, ATC=$8.33.  Q=36 (L=4): TVC=$200, TC=$300, AFC=$2.78, AVC=$5.56, ATC=$8.33.  Q=40 (L=5): TVC=$250, TC=$350, AFC=$2.50, AVC=$6.25, ATC=$8.75.  Q=42 (L=6): TVC=$300, TC=$400, AFC=$2.38, AVC=$7.14, ATC=$9.52.  Marginal cost (ΔTC/ΔQ between successive labor levels): 0→8: $6.25; 8→20: $4.17; 20→30: $5.00; 30→36: $8.33; 36→40: $12.50; 40→42: $25.00. (b) AVC is lowest ($5.00) at Q = 20 and Q = 30 (tied — a plateau, typical with discrete data). (c) ATC is lowest ($8.33) at Q = 30 and Q = 36 (tied). (d) Over 8→20 (MC=$4.17) ATC falls from $18.75 to $10.00: MC < ATC ✓. Around Q=30–36 (MC=$8.33) ATC is at its $8.33 minimum: MC ≈ ATC ✓. Over 36→40 (MC=$12.50) ATC rises from $8.33 to $8.75: MC > ATC ✓ — confirming the MC/ATC crossing rule.\n**A3. **(b) — while ATC is falling, MC must be pulling it down from below, exactly like a new test score below your GPA average pulls the average down.\n**A4. **Use the Medium plant ($18/unit, the lowest ATC for 200 units) — this point lies on the LRAC curve. Since ATC rises again with the Large plant ($30), the firm has moved from economies of scale (Small→Medium, ATC falls) into diseconomies of scale (Medium→Large, ATC rises) around this output level; the Medium plant is at or near the minimum efficient scale for this output.\n**A5. **Minimum efficient scale (MES) is the smallest output level at which long-run average cost reaches its minimum. If MES is LARGE relative to total market demand, the market can support only a few large, low-cost firms (tending toward oligopoly or natural monopoly). If MES is SMALL relative to market demand, many small firms can each operate at the lowest attainable cost, supporting a competitive market structure with many firms."}]},"12":{"title":"Perfect Competition","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"After studying this chapter you will be able to:"},{"t":"li","x":"Define perfect competition."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how a firm makes its output decision."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how price and output are determined in perfect competition."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain why firms enter and leave a market."},{"t":"li","x":"Predict the effects of technological change in a competitive market."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain why perfect competition is efficient."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Perfect competition","A market in which many firms sell identical products to many buyers, there are no restrictions to entry into the industry, established firms have no advantages over new ones, and sellers and buyers are well informed about prices."],["Price taker","A firm that cannot influence the price of a good or service; it must take the equilibrium market price. Each firm's output is a perfect substitute for the others', so the demand for each firm's output is perfectly elastic."],["Economic profit","Total revenue minus total cost, where total cost is the opportunity cost of production and includes normal profit."],["Normal profit","The value of the entrepreneur's forgone opportunity (the next best activity), included in the opportunity cost. With zero economic profit the entrepreneur earns normal profit and has no incentive to change activity."],["Total revenue (TR)","Price multiplied by quantity sold (P × Q)."],["Marginal revenue (MR)","The change in total revenue that results from a one-unit increase in the quantity sold; for a price taker MR equals the market price, and the MR curve is the demand curve for the firm's product."],["Temporary shutdown","A decision to produce nothing in the short run while remaining in the market; chosen if it minimizes the firm's loss."],["Economic loss","Economic loss = TFC + TVC − TR = TFC + (AVC − P) × Q. If the firm shuts down, Q is 0 and the loss equals TFC, the largest loss the firm must bear."],["Shutdown point","The price and quantity at which the firm is indifferent between producing the profit-maximizing quantity and shutting down; at minimum AVC, where the MC curve crosses the AVC curve, and the loss equals TFC."],["Firm's supply curve","Shows how the firm's profit-maximizing output varies as the market price varies; it is the firm's marginal cost curve above the shutdown point, and the firm produces nothing below it."],["Short-run market supply curve","The quantity supplied by all firms in the market at each price when each firm's plant and the number of firms remain the same."],["Long-run competitive equilibrium","Entry and exit have driven price to equal minimum ATC and every firm earns zero economic profit."],["Efficient use of resources","No one can be made better off without making someone else worse off; arises when marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"What is perfect competition?"},{"t":"p","x":"It arises when the firm's minimum efficient scale is small relative to market demand, so there is room for many firms, and when each firm produces a good with no unique characteristics, so consumers do not care which firm's good they buy. The goal of each firm is to maximize economic profit."},{"t":"p","x":"Example (Figure 12.1): at a market price of $25 a sweater the firm sells 9 sweaters a day for total revenue of $225. The firm can sell any quantity at the market price, so marginal revenue equals the market price of $25, and the MR curve is the demand curve for the firm's product. The demand for a firm's product is perfectly elastic because one firm's sweater is a perfect substitute for another's, while market demand is not perfectly elastic because a sweater is a substitute for some other good."},{"t":"p","x":"The firm must decide how to produce at minimum cost, what quantity to produce, and whether to enter or exit the market."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The firm's output decision"},{"t":"p","x":"Total revenue and total cost: economic profit (TR minus TC) is negative at low output (the firm cannot cover fixed costs), positive at intermediate output and negative again at high output because of steeply rising cost from diminishing returns. The firm maximizes economic profit at 9 sweaters a day."},{"t":"p","x":"Marginal analysis: because marginal revenue is constant and marginal cost eventually increases, profit is maximized at the output where MR = MC. If MR > MC, increasing output raises profit; if MR < MC, increasing output lowers profit; if MR = MC, profit falls if output changes in either direction, so it is maximized."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The temporary shutdown decision"},{"t":"p","x":"If the firm makes an economic loss it must decide whether to exit or stay, and if it stays whether to produce something or shut down temporarily; it chooses what minimizes its loss. Minimum AVC of a sweater is $17; at $17 the profit-maximizing output is 7 sweaters a day, and the loss equals TFC. If the price is between $17 and $20.14, the firm produces where MC equals price, covers all its variable cost and some of its fixed cost, and incurs a loss that is less than TFC."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The firm's supply curve"},{"t":"p","x":"If price equals minimum AVC ($17) the firm is indifferent between producing nothing and producing at the shutdown point T. At $25 it produces 9 sweaters a day (P = MC) and at $31 it produces 10 (P = MC again); the short-run supply curve traces this."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Output, price and profit in the short run"},{"t":"p","x":"Market supply and market demand determine the price. With 1,000 identical firms like Campus Sweaters, the market supply curve is horizontal at the shutdown price of $17, where some firms produce 7 sweaters and others zero. An increase in demand raises price and quantity; a decrease lowers both."},{"t":"p","x":"Profits and losses in the short run: maximum profit is not always a positive economic profit. Compare the firm's ATC at the profit-maximizing output with the market price: price equal to ATC gives zero economic profit (break even, and the entrepreneur earns normal profit), price above ATC gives an economic profit, price below ATC gives an economic loss."},{"t":"p","x":"Short-run decisions are made after irrevocable commitments have created sunk costs; the firm considers only avoidable costs, so to produce its revenue need only exceed avoidable costs, not total costs. Profit maximization does not require a positive economic profit in the short run."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Output, price and profit in the long run"},{"t":"p","x":"In short-run equilibrium a firm may make an economic profit, break even or incur an economic loss; in long-run equilibrium firms break even because firms can enter or exit. New firms enter an industry in which existing firms make an economic profit, and firms exit an industry in which they incur a loss."},{"t":"p","x":"Entry: at a market price of $25 firms make economic profit, so firms enter, market supply increases and the price falls until firms make zero economic profit. Exit: at a price of $17 firms incur economic loss, so firms exit, market supply decreases and the price rises until firms make zero economic profit."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Changes in demand and technological advances"},{"t":"p","x":"A decrease in demand shifts the demand curve leftward, the price falls and each firm decreases its quantity. The price is now below minimum ATC, so firms make economic losses and some exit; market supply decreases, price rises, and economic profit returns to zero. The main difference between the initial and new long-run equilibrium is the number of firms: fewer firms produce the equilibrium quantity. An increase in demand has the opposite effects: price rises, firms make economic profits, entry shifts the short-run supply rightward, price falls to minimum ATC, and a larger number of firms produce the equilibrium quantity."},{"t":"p","x":"Technological advance (Figure 12.11): starting from long-run equilibrium, a new technology lowers production costs and shifts ATC and MC downward. The first firms to adopt it make economic profit, which induces new-technology firms to enter; market supply increases and the price falls. With the lower price, old-technology firms incur economic losses and either exit or switch to the new technology. Eventually all firms use the new technology, market supply has increased and economic profit is zero."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Competition and efficiency"},{"t":"p","x":"Resources are used efficiently when marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost. A consumer's demand curve shows the best budget allocation as price changes, so along the demand curve consumers get the most value from their resources; if only the consumers benefit, the market demand curve is the marginal social benefit curve. A competitive firm's supply curve shows the profit-maximizing quantity at each price, so along it firms get the most value; if firms bear all the costs, the market supply curve is the marginal social cost curve."},{"t":"p","x":"In competitive equilibrium quantity demanded equals quantity supplied, so MSB = MSC; the gain from trade for consumers is consumer surplus and for producers is producer surplus, and in long-run equilibrium total surplus is maximized. In Figure 12.12(b) each firm produces at the lowest possible long-run average total cost and Campus Sweaters makes zero economic profit."},{"t":"p","x":"The pressure of competition forces self-interested firms to produce remarkable long-run results: each firm produces at the lowest possible average total cost (the minimum of the LRAC curve), consumers pay the lowest price that keeps firms in business (P equals minimum ATC), each firm uses the least-cost technology, and firms produce the efficient quantity. The forces of competition, which Adam Smith called the invisible hand, guide firms to maximize the value of scarce resources."},{"t":"p","x":"Class discussion: do firms in perfect competition advertise? (they sell identical products, so there is little reason to). Did Hanjin Shipping make a shutdown or exit decision, and under what conditions does the decision minimize its loss?"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Campus Sweaters (a price-taking firm) faces a market price of $25 a sweater and chooses to produce 9 sweaters a day, the quantity at which MR ($25) = MC. Its minimum AVC is $17 a sweater (its shutdown point, at 7 sweaters a day). At a price of $31, the firm would move up its MC/supply curve and produce 10 sweaters a day (P = MC again, at the new, higher price)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 12"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can explain why a perfectly competitive firm is a price taker and why its demand curve (=MR curve) is horizontal at the market price."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can find the profit-maximizing output using the MR = MC rule (equivalently P = MC for a price taker)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can locate the shutdown point (minimum AVC) and explain the shutdown decision."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can explain how entry and exit drive the long-run equilibrium to zero economic profit (P = minimum ATC)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can explain why perfect competition achieves an efficient (MSB = MSC) outcome."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1. Riverside Fish Co. is a price taker in a perfectly competitive market. At the current market price of $18/kg it produces 400 kg/day, where MC = $18. Its AVC is minimized at $12/kg, at an output of 250 kg/day. (a) Is the firm covering its variable costs? (b) Should it shut down? Explain using the shutdown-point rule."},{"t":"p","x":"Q2. A firm's total fixed cost is $500/day. At a market price of $9, the firm shuts down (produces zero). What is the firm's daily economic loss while shut down? Would this loss be larger or smaller if the firm instead produced the quantity at which P = MC, assuming P exceeds minimum AVC at that price? Explain in words (no calculation needed)."},{"t":"p","x":"Q3 (MCQ). In the long run, a perfectly competitive market moves toward an equilibrium in which: (a) every firm earns a large economic profit; (b) every firm earns zero economic profit (P = minimum ATC); (c) only the largest firms survive; (d) price permanently exceeds marginal cost."},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. The market for notebooks is perfectly competitive. Following a positive demand shock, the short-run price rises above every existing firm's ATC. Describe, step by step, what happens next in the long run to (a) the number of firms, (b) market supply, (c) market price, and (d) each remaining firm's economic profit."},{"t":"p","x":"Q5. Explain, using the P = MC rule and the definitions of marginal social benefit and marginal social cost, why a perfectly competitive market produces the economically efficient quantity of output (assuming no externalities)."},{"t":"a","x":"A1. (a) Yes — at P=$18, the firm is well above its minimum AVC of $12, so it comfortably covers all of its variable costs and some fixed costs. (b) No, it should NOT shut down: the shutdown rule says shut down only if P < minimum AVC; here P ($18) > minimum AVC ($12), so producing 400 kg/day (where P = MC) minimizes the firm's loss (or, as here, lets it earn a profit if P also exceeds ATC) — shutting down would forgo the contribution to fixed costs that continuing to operate provides.\nA2. While shut down, the firm's economic loss equals its total fixed cost, $500/day (it produces nothing, so it has no variable costs and no revenue). If instead it produced where P=MC (with P above minimum AVC), its loss would be SMALLER than $500 — producing lets it cover all variable costs plus part of its fixed costs, reducing (but, since P<ATC, not eliminating) the loss. This is exactly the logic of the shutdown point: the firm shuts down only when even producing at P=MC would not let it cover any part of its fixed costs, i.e. when P falls below minimum AVC.\nA3. (b) — free entry and exit drives economic profit to zero in the long run; surviving firms produce where price equals minimum average total cost.\nA4. (a) The number of firms increases, as the economic profit created by the demand shock attracts new entrants. (b) Market supply increases (shifts rightward) as more firms produce. (c) Market price falls back down as supply increases, moving toward the new long-run equilibrium. (d) Each firm's economic profit falls back toward zero as price falls to equal minimum ATC again — entry continues only as long as economic profit is positive.\nA5. The market demand curve is the marginal social benefit (MSB) curve and the market supply curve is the marginal social cost (MSC) curve. Because each competitive firm produces where P = MC, and market equilibrium occurs where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied, the equilibrium quantity is exactly where MSB (=P=demand) equals MSC (=MC=supply). Producing any other quantity would mean MSB ≠ MSC, so total surplus could be increased by moving toward the competitive quantity — hence the competitive outcome is efficient."}]},"13":{"title":"Monopoly","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"p","x":"After studying this chapter you will be able to:"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how monopoly arises."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how a single-price monopoly determines its output and price."},{"t":"li","x":"Compare the performance and efficiency of single-price monopoly and competition."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how price discrimination increases profit."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how monopoly regulation influences output, price, economic profit and efficiency."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Monopoly","A market that produces a good or service for which no close substitute exists, and in which there is one supplier protected from competition by a barrier preventing the entry of new firms."],["Barrier to entry","A constraint that protects a firm from potential competitors; the three types are natural, ownership and legal."],["Natural monopoly","A market in which economies of scale enable one firm to supply the entire market at the lowest possible cost; the LRAC curve is still sloping downward when it meets the demand curve. In Figure 13.1 one firm produces 4 million units at 5 cents per unit, while two firms would produce them at 10 cents."],["Ownership barrier","Occurs if one firm owns a significant portion of a key resource; during the last century De Beers owned 90 percent of the world's diamonds."],["Legal monopoly","A market in which competition and entry are restricted by a public franchise (postal service, water supply in Hong Kong), a government license (electricity), or a patent or copyright."],["Single-price monopoly","A firm that must sell each unit of its output for the same price to all its customers."],["Price discrimination","The practice of selling different units of a good or service for different prices. Price differences that arise from cost differences are not price discrimination."],["Price setter","A monopoly is a price setter, not a price taker, because the demand for its output is the market demand: to sell a larger output it must set a lower price."],["Marginal revenue (MR)","The change in total revenue that results from a one-unit increase in the quantity sold; for a single-price monopoly MR is less than price at every quantity."],["Economic rent","Any surplus: consumer surplus, producer surplus or economic profit."],["Rent seeking","The pursuit of wealth by capturing economic rent. Rent seekers buy a monopoly (transferring rent to its creator) or create a monopoly (using resources in political activity)."],["Perfect price discrimination","Occurs when a firm sells each unit of output for the highest price someone is willing to pay; marginal revenue equals price and the demand curve is also the marginal revenue curve."],["Regulation","Rules administered by a government agency to influence prices, quantities, entry and other aspects of economic activity."],["Social interest theory","The political and regulatory process relentlessly seeks out inefficiency and regulates to eliminate deadweight loss."],["Capture theory","Regulation serves the self-interest of the producer, who captures the regulator and maximizes economic profit."],["Marginal cost pricing rule","A regulation that sets the price equal to the monopoly's marginal cost; the quantity demanded at that price is the efficient quantity."],["Average cost pricing rule","The regulated firm produces the quantity at which price equals average cost and sets the price equal to average cost (second best)."],["Rate of return regulation","A firm must justify its price by showing that its return on capital does not exceed a specified target rate."],["Price cap regulation","A price ceiling specifying the highest price the firm may charge."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"How monopoly arises"},{"t":"p","x":"A monopoly has two key features: no close substitute (if a good has a close substitute, even one produced by a single firm, that firm effectively faces competition) and barriers to entry (natural, ownership, legal). There are two price-setting strategies: single price or price discrimination (many firms price discriminate, but not all are monopolies)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Price and marginal revenue"},{"t":"p","x":"Because the demand for the monopoly's output is market demand, to sell more it must lower the price on ALL units sold, not just the marginal one. Example (Figure 13.2): at $16 the monopoly sells 2 units; cutting the price to $14 sells 3 units. It loses $4 of total revenue on the 2 units it was selling at $16 and gains $14 on the third unit, so total revenue increases by $10, which is marginal revenue. The MR curve passes midway between 2 and 3 units at $10, so MR < P at each quantity."},{"t":"p","x":"Marginal revenue and elasticity: if demand is elastic, a fall in price increases total revenue and MR is positive; if demand is inelastic, a fall in price decreases total revenue and MR is negative; if demand is unit elastic, a fall in price leaves total revenue unchanged, MR = 0 and total revenue is maximized. In monopoly, demand is always elastic at the chosen output: a single-price monopoly never produces where demand is inelastic, since it could raise total revenue, cut cost and raise economic profit by decreasing output."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Price and output decision"},{"t":"p","x":"The monopoly produces the profit-maximizing quantity, where MR = MC, and sets its price at the highest level at which it can sell that quantity, read off the demand curve (Figure 13.4). Economic profit is the profit per unit multiplied by the quantity produced. The monopoly might make an economic profit even in the long run because barriers to entry protect it from entry, but a monopoly that incurs an economic loss might shut down temporarily in the short run or exit in the long run. The classic diagram first appeared in Joan Robinson's The Economics of Imperfect Competition (1933)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Single-price monopoly and competition compared"},{"t":"p","x":"In perfect competition the market demand curve D is the monopoly's demand, and the market supply S is the horizontal sum of the firms' marginal cost curves, which is the monopoly's marginal cost curve. Competitive equilibrium is where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied (QC, PC). Monopoly equilibrium output QM is where MR = MC, and price PM is on the demand curve at that quantity. Compared with perfect competition, monopoly produces a smaller output and charges a higher price."},{"t":"p","x":"Efficiency: in competition the demand curve is MSB and the supply curve is MSC, so MSB = MSC, total surplus is maximized and the quantity is efficient. In monopoly, because P > MR and MR = MC, P > MC: price exceeds marginal social cost, so marginal social benefit exceeds marginal social cost, the monopoly under-produces and a deadweight loss arises. Some lost consumer surplus goes to the monopoly as producer surplus (a redistribution)."},{"t":"p","x":"Rent seeking uses further resources, so the social cost of monopoly is potentially the deadweight loss plus the economic profit that the monopoly might earn. In the rent-seeking equilibrium, rent-seeking costs shift the ATC curve upward, producer surplus disappears and the deadweight loss increases. Adam Smith: \"People in the same trade seldom meet together, even for merriment and diversion, but the conversation ends in some contrivance to raise prices.\""},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Price discrimination"},{"t":"p","x":"To price discriminate a monopoly must identify and separate different buyer types and sell a product that cannot be resold. It can discriminate among groups of buyers (airline advance-purchase restrictions) or among units of a good (quantity discounts, unless they reflect lower costs at higher volumes). By price discriminating a monopoly captures consumer surplus and converts it into producer surplus: producer surplus equals total revenue minus total variable cost, and economic profit equals producer surplus minus total fixed cost, so more producer surplus means more economic profit."},{"t":"p","x":"Airline example: marginal cost is $40 a trip; with a single price the airline sells 8,000 trips a week at $120, travelers enjoy consumer surplus and producer surplus is $640,000. Discriminating between business travelers and leisure travelers, the airline sells 4,000 leisure trips at $80 a trip (leisure travelers will not pay $120), increasing output to 12,000 trips a week; consumer surplus and producer surplus both increase."},{"t":"p","x":"Perfect price discrimination: the monopoly increases output until the price of the last trip equals marginal cost; producer surplus is maximized when the lowest fare is $40 and 16,000 trips are bought, giving the maximum possible profit. The more perfectly a monopoly can price discriminate, the closer its output is to the competitive output (P = MC) and the more efficient the outcome, but it differs from perfect competition in two ways: the monopoly captures the entire consumer surplus, and the increase in economic profit attracts even more rent-seeking activity that leads to inefficiency."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Monopoly regulation"},{"t":"p","x":"Two theories explain how regulation works: social interest theory and capture theory. When demand and cost conditions create a natural monopoly the quantity produced is less than the efficient quantity. Efficient regulation: the marginal cost pricing rule sets price equal to marginal cost, where MSB = MSC (the demand curve is MSB, the marginal cost curve is MSC), so the quantity produced is efficient. But average cost exceeds price, so the firm incurs an economic loss. To cover the loss the regulated natural monopoly might be permitted to price discriminate, or charge a one-time fee to cover its fixed costs and then price at marginal cost."},{"t":"p","x":"Second-best regulation: the average cost pricing rule (price equal to average cost), or the government pays a subsidy equal to the monopoly's loss. Because regulators cannot be sure what the firm's costs are, they use rate of return regulation or price cap regulation. Under rate of return regulation the firm's managers have an incentive to inflate costs and use more capital than the efficient amount, so it can end up serving the firm's self-interest; under a price cap (Figure 13.12) the firm has an incentive to operate efficiently and keep costs under control, and the cap lowers the price and increases the quantity."},{"t":"p","x":"Class discussion: how average-cost pricing differs from marginal-cost pricing and whether it maximizes social well-being; and how differences in elasticity between business and household phone users explain the old AT&T rate structure (high weekday daytime prices, medium evening prices, low night and weekend prices)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"A single-price monopoly sells at $16 and sells 2 units; cutting price to $14 sells 3 units. It loses $4 of revenue on the original 2 units (now sold at $2 less each) but gains $14 on the 3rd unit, so MR on the 3rd unit = $14 − $4 = $10 — confirming MR < P ($10 < $14). A price-discriminating airline example: at a single price of $120/trip it sells 8,000 trips/week (producer surplus $640,000, marginal cost $40/trip); by separately pricing business travel and leisure travel (e.g. $80/trip for 4,000 extra leisure trips), it expands output to 12,000 trips/week and increases its producer surplus."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 13"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can explain the two features that define a monopoly (no close substitute + barrier to entry) and name the three types of barriers to entry."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can explain why MR < P for a single-price monopoly, and relate the sign of MR to the elasticity of demand."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can find the monopoly's profit-maximizing quantity and price using MR = MC."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can compare monopoly's price/quantity/efficiency outcome against perfect competition and explain the deadweight loss."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can explain how and why price discrimination raises a monopoly's profit and (partially) reduces the deadweight loss."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can explain marginal-cost pricing, average-cost pricing, rate-of-return regulation and price-cap regulation for a natural monopoly."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1 (MCQ). A single-price monopoly will never knowingly produce in the range of its demand curve where demand is: (a) elastic; (b) unit elastic; (c) inelastic; (d) perfectly elastic."},{"t":"p","x":"Q2. HarborWater Co. is the sole water utility in a small town (a natural monopoly: ATC is still falling at the quantity where the demand curve crosses it). The unregulated monopoly price is $6/1,000 litres with MC = $2 at that quantity. The efficient (P=MC) quantity would require a price of $2. (a) Why would requiring HarborWater to set price = MC = $2 likely cause it to incur a loss? (b) Give two different regulatory solutions that could let HarborWater produce the efficient quantity while covering its costs."},{"t":"p","x":"Q3. A monopoly's demand curve is P = 100 − 2Q and its marginal cost is constant at MC = $20. Its marginal revenue curve is MR = 100 − 4Q. (a) Find the profit-maximizing quantity and price (set MR = MC). (b) What quantity and price would prevail under perfect competition (P = MC)? (c) Is the monopoly quantity larger or smaller than the competitive quantity?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. Explain, in your own words, why price discrimination can simultaneously (a) increase a monopoly's profit and (b) increase total output and efficiency compared to single-price monopoly — even though it still leaves consumers worse off than they would be under perfect competition."},{"t":"p","x":"Q5. List the three types of barriers to entry that allow a monopoly to persist, with one real-world example of each (you may reuse the De Beers / public utility / patent examples from the slides, or propose your own)."},{"t":"a","x":"A1. (c) — in the inelastic region, MR is negative, so cutting output there would raise both revenue and profit (by cutting costs while raising revenue); a profit maximizer always operates where MR ≥ 0, i.e. where demand is elastic or unit elastic.\nA2. (a) At the efficient quantity, ATC is still above the $2 marginal-cost price (because ATC is still falling — a natural monopoly has economies of scale not yet exhausted at that output), so price would be below average cost and the firm would make an economic loss. (b) Two valid solutions: (i) the government pays a subsidy equal to the firm's loss at the P=MC price; or (ii) the firm is permitted a one-off fixed access fee / limited price discrimination to recover its fixed costs while still charging $2 per unit at the margin; (iii) alternatively, average-cost pricing (set P = ATC) lets the firm break even, though output is then below the fully efficient quantity — any two of these are acceptable.\nA3. (a) Set MR=MC: 100−4Q=20 → 4Q=80 → Q=20; P=100−2(20)=$60. (b) Competitive: P=MC → 100−2Q=20 → 2Q=80 → Q=40; P=$20. (c) The monopoly quantity (20) is smaller than the competitive quantity (40) — exactly half, in this linear example — and its price ($60) is higher than the competitive price ($20).\nA4. (a) Price discrimination lets the monopoly charge higher prices to buyers willing to pay more (e.g. business travelers) while still selling to price-sensitive buyers at a lower price (e.g. leisure travelers) rather than losing their business altogether — this captures consumer surplus that a single price would have left with consumers, converting it into producer surplus/profit. (b) Because the monopoly no longer has to set ONE price for ALL units, it is willing to sell MORE units (to the price-sensitive segment) than it would under a single price — pushing output closer to the competitive/efficient quantity and shrinking the deadweight loss, even though consumers (collectively) still end up worse off than under perfect competition, since the monopoly — not consumers — captures most of the resulting surplus.\nA5. Natural barrier — economies of scale so large that one firm can supply the whole market at lower cost than two or more firms (example: a local water or electricity distribution network). Ownership barrier — one firm controls a key input (example: De Beers historically owning ~90% of the world's diamonds). Legal barrier — government grants exclusive rights via a public franchise, licence, patent, or copyright (example: a postal service public franchise, or a pharmaceutical patent)."}]},"14":{"title":"Monopolistic Competition","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"li","x":"Define and identify monopolistic competition."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how a firm in monopolistic competition determines its price and output in the short run and the long run."},{"t":"li","x":"Explain why advertising costs are high and why firms in monopolistic competition use brand names."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Monopolistic competition","a market structure with a large number of firms, each producing a slightly differentiated product, competing on quality/price/marketing, with free entry and exit."],["Product differentiation","making a product slightly different from competitors' products (by design, quality, or marketing), which gives the firm a downward-sloping (but highly elastic, since substitutes are close) demand curve."],["Excess capacity","producing less than the quantity at which ATC is minimized; a long-run feature of monopolistic competition (unlike perfect competition), caused by the downward-sloping firm demand curve."],["Markup","the amount by which a firm's price exceeds its marginal cost; positive for a monopolistically competitive firm (vs. zero under perfect competition), again because of the downward-sloping demand curve."],["Signaling (via advertising/brand names)","using a costly, highly visible action (e.g. expensive advertising) to credibly convey unobservable product quality to consumers."],["Market structure of monopolistic competition","Many firms compete, each produces a differentiated product, firms compete on product quality, price and marketing, and firms are free to enter and exit the industry."],["Large number of firms","Each firm has a small market share and limited power over price; it is sensitive to the average market price but pays no attention to the actions of others, so collusion to fix prices is impossible."],["Quality","Includes design, reliability and service; there is a trade-off between price and quality. Marketing takes two main forms: advertising and packaging."],["Efficient scale","The quantity at which ATC is a minimum; in long-run equilibrium monopolistic competition produces less than this (excess capacity), while perfect competition has no excess capacity and no markup."],["Selling costs","Costs such as advertising and fancy retail buildings; they are fixed costs, so they raise average total cost at any quantity but do not change marginal cost."],["Signal","An action taken by an informed person or firm to send a message to uninformed people."],["Brand name","Establishing a brand name provides information about quality and consistency (you are more likely to stay at a Holiday Inn than at Joe's Motel)."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"What is monopolistic competition?"},{"t":"p","x":"A large number of firms means each has only a small market share and so limited power to influence price; each is sensitive to the average market price but not to any single rival's actions, so collusion to fix prices is effectively impossible. Because products are differentiated, firms compete on quality, price, and marketing rather than selling an identical, perfectly substitutable product (unlike perfect competition). There are no significant barriers to entry, so firms cannot sustain economic profit in the long run (unlike monopoly). Examples: audio/video equipment, clothing, jewelry, computers, sporting goods."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Short-run and long-run price/output decision"},{"t":"p","x":"In the short run, a firm in monopolistic competition behaves like a single-price monopoly for its own slightly differentiated product: it produces the quantity at which MR = MC and charges the highest price at which it can sell that quantity, potentially earning an economic profit (if P > ATC) or incurring a loss (if P < ATC) at that output. In the LONG run, economic profit attracts new entrants selling similar (but not identical) products; as new firms enter, each existing firm's market share — and hence the demand for its product — shrinks, which lowers the quantity at which MR = MC and lowers the maximum price it can charge. Entry continues until price falls to equal ATC and every firm earns zero economic profit, exactly as in perfect competition — the key structural difference from perfect competition is that this long-run equilibrium occurs with EXCESS CAPACITY (output below the ATC-minimizing quantity) and a positive MARKUP (P above MC), both driven by the firm's downward-sloping demand curve."},{"t":"p","x":"Profit maximizing might be loss minimizing: a firm might incur an economic loss in the short run (at the profit-maximizing quantity P < ATC). In the long run, entry continues as long as firms make an economic profit (P > ATC). Figure 14.3 shows a firm in long-run equilibrium. In perfect competition the perfectly elastic demand curve means no excess capacity and no markup; in monopolistic competition the downward-sloping demand curve drives both. Class question (Flight Inc., a running shoe producer): does it have excess capacity in the long run, and why doesn't it decrease its capacity?"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Efficiency: monopolistic competition vs. perfect competition"},{"t":"p","x":"Because P > MC at the firm's chosen output, marginal social benefit exceeds marginal social cost in the long run, so (holding product variety fixed) monopolistic competition produces LESS than the efficient quantity. However, the \"loss\" from excess capacity must be weighed against the GAIN consumers get from having a greater degree of product variety — product variety is itself valuable and costly to provide; the efficient degree of variety is where the marginal social benefit of variety equals its marginal social cost."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Product development, advertising, and brand names"},{"t":"p","x":"To keep earning any profit, a firm in monopolistic competition must continuously develop new/improved products, since competitors will imitate any successful innovation. Advertising and brand-building are selling costs (fixed costs — they do not change marginal cost, but they do raise average total cost at any given output); if successful, advertising increases demand for the firm's product and can let it operate at a LARGER output with a LOWER average total cost (spreading the fixed selling cost over more units) — but if all firms in the market advertise and new firms enter, demand for any one firm's product, and hence its markup, tends to shrink again. A firm may also use advertising and brand names to SIGNAL quality it cannot otherwise prove to consumers before purchase: spending a large, highly visible amount on advertising is only worthwhile for a firm confident that customers who try the product will buy again — so the willingness to advertise heavily is itself a credible (if indirect) signal of quality, even when the ad content says little about the product itself."},{"t":"p","x":"Innovation is costly but increases total revenue; firms pursue product development until the marginal revenue from innovation equals its marginal cost. The amount is efficient if the marginal social benefit of an innovation (what consumers will pay for it) equals the marginal social cost to firms. A large proportion of the price we pay covers the cost of selling the good."},{"t":"p","x":"Advertising might also shrink the markup: with no advertising the demand for a firm's output is not very elastic and its markup is large (Figure 14.6a), but if all firms advertise, demand becomes more elastic, the firm produces a larger quantity, its price falls and its markup shrinks (Figure 14.6b)."},{"t":"p","x":"Signalling quality (Coke versus Oke): if Coke is truly good, people who try it after seeing the expensive advertising will like it and keep buying; if Oke is bad, buyers will hate it and stop, so Oke will not waste millions on advertising. The ads need not mention the product; they just need to be flashy and expensive, and consumers read the signal correctly. Brand names likewise tell you what to expect. To the extent that advertising and selling costs give consumers information and services they value more than their cost, they are efficient."},{"t":"p","x":"Class questions: what are the advantages and disadvantages of product differentiation, and can the market itself work out the right amount of differentiation over time?"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"A firm with no advertising produces 25 units at an average total cost of $60. By advertising (raising its fixed selling costs, which shifts the ATC curve upward at each quantity but increases demand), the same firm can produce 100 units at a LOWER average total cost of $40 — the extra output spreads the now-higher fixed costs (including the advertising) over many more units, more than offsetting the direct cost of the advertising itself."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 14"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can list the four defining features of monopolistic competition and contrast them with perfect competition and monopoly."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can describe the short-run price/output decision (MR=MC, like a mini-monopoly for the firm's own variant)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can explain how entry erodes an existing firm's demand curve and drives long-run economic profit to zero."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can define excess capacity and markup, and explain why BOTH are positive in long-run equilibrium (unlike perfect competition)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can explain the efficiency trade-off between excess capacity (a loss) and product variety (a gain)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can explain how advertising affects a firm's ATC and demand, and how advertising/brand names can function as a quality signal."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1 (MCQ). In the long-run equilibrium of monopolistic competition, a typical firm: (a) earns a large economic profit; (b) earns zero economic profit and produces at minimum ATC, exactly like perfect competition; (c) earns zero economic profit but produces at a quantity below the one that minimizes ATC (excess capacity); (d) is protected from entry by a legal barrier."},{"t":"p","x":"Q2. Explain, step by step, what happens to an EXISTING firm's demand curve, profit-maximizing quantity, and price as NEW firms enter a monopolistically competitive market in response to short-run economic profit (assume product differentiation remains constant)."},{"t":"p","x":"Q3. BrewCraft Coffee currently earns zero economic profit, producing 80 cups/hour at an ATC of $3.50/cup, while the ATC-minimizing (efficient) scale for its equipment is 110 cups/hour. (a) Does BrewCraft have excess capacity, and if so how much? (b) Is this consistent with long-run equilibrium in monopolistic competition? Explain why or why not."},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. Two rival smartphone case brands sell nearly identical cases: Brand A spends heavily on advertising and charges $25; Brand B does no advertising and charges $10. Using the signaling idea from the slides, explain why a consumer might rationally infer that Brand A's case is higher quality, even if the advertisements show no technical information about the product."},{"t":"p","x":"Q5. A policymaker argues that monopolistic competition should be regulated like monopoly, because P > MC in the long run for both. Give one argument, based on PRODUCT VARIETY, for why this comparison is misleading."},{"t":"a","x":"A1. (c) — free entry drives economic profit to zero, exactly as in perfect competition, but because each firm's demand curve is downward-sloping (due to product differentiation) rather than horizontal, the zero-profit tangency occurs at an output BELOW the ATC-minimizing quantity — excess capacity — with price above marginal cost — a positive markup.\nA2. As new firms enter selling similar (differentiated) products, each existing firm loses some market share, so the demand for its particular product DECREASES (shifts left). This decrease in demand lowers the quantity at which MR = MC (the firm's profit-maximizing output falls) and lowers the highest price the firm can charge for that smaller quantity. Entry continues — and each firm's demand keeps shrinking — until price falls to equal ATC and economic profit reaches zero for every firm in the market.\nA3. (a) Yes — BrewCraft produces 80 cups/hour but the ATC-minimizing scale is 110 cups/hour, so it has excess capacity of 110−80 = 30 cups/hour (it is operating below its most efficient scale). (b) Yes, this IS consistent with long-run equilibrium in monopolistic competition: zero economic profit (P=ATC) combined with excess capacity (output below the ATC-minimizing quantity) is exactly the textbook long-run outcome, driven by the firm's downward-sloping (not horizontal) demand curve — unlike perfect competition, where long-run equilibrium requires output AT the ATC-minimizing quantity.\nA4. Because advertising is a costly, highly visible action, a consumer can reason: Brand A would only be willing to spend heavily on ads and charge a premium price if it expected repeat purchases from satisfied customers — which requires the product to actually be good. If Brand A's case were low quality, customers who tried it once (lured by the expensive advertising) would not buy again, wasting the large advertising investment. Brand B's lack of advertising investment, by contrast, conveys no such costly signal either way. The spending itself — not its content — is what credibly signals confidence in quality, exactly as in the Coke/Pepsi example from the slides.\nA5. Unlike monopoly (a single firm with no close substitutes), consumers in a monopolistically competitive market benefit from PRODUCT VARIETY — many differentiated options to choose from — which has real value to consumers and is itself costly for firms to provide. The \"excess capacity\" / P>MC outcome is partly the economic price of that variety: any policy that pushed every firm to price at MC (eliminating the markup) would also tend to eliminate the incentive to differentiate products, reducing variety. So the simple deadweight-loss comparison used for monopoly overstates the inefficiency of monopolistic competition, because it ignores the offsetting consumer benefit of variety."}]},"15":{"title":"Oligopoly","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"li","x":"Define and identify oligopoly."},{"t":"li","x":"Use game theory to explain how price and output are determined in oligopoly."},{"t":"li","x":"Use game theory to explain other strategic decisions (advertising, R&D, repeated and sequential games)."},{"t":"li","x":"Describe the antitrust laws that regulate oligopoly."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Oligopoly","a market structure in which natural or legal barriers prevent the entry of new firms and a small number of firms compete."],["Duopoly","a market in which there are only two producers that compete; it captures the essence of oligopoly."],["Cartel","a group of firms acting together to limit output, raise price and increase profit. Cartels are illegal."],["Collusive agreement","an agreement between two or more firms to restrict output, raise the price and increase profits; illegal in the United States, so undertaken in secret."],["Game theory","a tool for studying strategic behavior, that is behavior that takes into account the expected behavior of others and the mutual recognition of interdependence."],["Payoff matrix","a table that shows the payoffs for every possible action by each player for every possible action by the other player."],["Nash equilibrium","the outcome of a game in which each player makes the best choice for himself or herself, given the choice made by the other player (proposed by John Nash)."],["Dominant strategy","an action that is best for a player whatever the other player does."],["Tit-for-tat strategy","a player cooperates this period if the other cooperated in the previous period, and cheats this period if the other cheated in the previous period."],["Trigger strategy","a player cooperates if the other cooperates, but plays the Nash equilibrium strategy forever after if the other cheats."],["Contestable market","a market in which firms can enter and leave so easily that firms in the market face competition from potential entrants."],["Limit pricing","setting the price at the highest level that is consistent with keeping a potential entrant out."],["Antitrust law","the law that regulates oligopolies and prevents them from becoming monopolies or behaving like monopolies."],["Resale price maintenance","a manufacturer agrees with a distributor on the price at which the product will be resold."],["Tying arrangement","an agreement to sell one product only if the buyer agrees to buy another, different product as well."],["Predatory pricing","setting a low price to drive competitors out of business, intending then to set the monopoly price."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. What Is Oligopoly?"},{"t":"p","x":"Two features define it: (1) barriers to entry, natural or legal; (2) a small number of firms."},{"t":"p","x":"• Natural barriers: demand and costs leave room for only a few firms. A natural duopoly has two firms (two firms can meet market demand at the least cost); a natural oligopoly may have three."},{"t":"p","x":"• Legal oligopoly: a legal barrier can keep the number of firms small even when demand and costs would allow more."},{"t":"p","x":"• Interdependence: with a small number of firms each firm’s profit depends on every firm’s actions, so each must think about how rivals will react."},{"t":"p","x":"• Temptation to cooperate: the firms are tempted to form a cartel, which is always illegal."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Game Theory and the Prisoners’ Dilemma"},{"t":"p","x":"All games have four features: Rules (the setting, the actions and the consequences), Strategies (all possible actions of each player), Payoffs (what each player gets in each outcome) and Outcome (the equilibrium reached when players choose rationally)."},{"t":"p","x":"Prisoners’ dilemma. Art and Bob are held in separate cells and cannot communicate. Sentences: if one confesses and the other denies, the confessor gets 1 year and the other 10 years; if both confess, each gets 3 years; if both deny, each gets 2 years (the minor crime only)."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Payoffs (years in prison)","Bob confesses","Bob denies"],["Art confesses","Art 3 · Bob 3","Art 1 · Bob 10"],["Art denies","Art 10 · Bob 1","Art 2 · Bob 2"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Finding the Nash equilibrium: ask each player what is best for him given each action of the other. If Art confesses, Bob’s best reply is to confess (3 years rather than 10). If Art denies, Bob’s best reply is still to confess (1 year rather than 2). Confessing is a dominant strategy for both, so the equilibrium is that both confess and each serves 3 years."},{"t":"p","x":"The dilemma: each knows both would be better off if both denied (2 years each), but each also knows that if he denies the other has an incentive to confess. The equilibrium is therefore a bad outcome for the players, and they cannot reach the better one."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. An Oligopoly Price-Fixing Game (the Duopoly Dilemma)"},{"t":"p","x":"Two firms, Trick and Gear, compete in a natural duopoly. The strategies of firms in a cartel are to comply (produce the agreed output) or cheat (produce more). Four outcomes are possible: both comply, both cheat, Trick complies and Gear cheats, Gear complies and Trick cheats."},{"t":"p","x":"• Collusion: the firms act like a monopoly and maximize joint economic profit by producing where the cartel’s marginal cost (the horizontal sum of the two firms’ MC curves) equals marginal revenue. Each firm agrees to produce 2,000 units and share the profit. At that output price exceeds marginal cost, so each firm is tempted to produce more."},{"t":"p","x":"• One firm cheats: if one firm raises output to 3,000 units, industry output rises to 5,000 and the price falls. The complier’s ATC now exceeds the price so it makes a loss; the cheat’s price still exceeds ATC so its profit rises."},{"t":"p","x":"• Both cheat: each produces 3,000 units, industry output is 6,000 units, the price falls to the competitive level and each firm makes zero economic profit."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Payoffs (economic profit per week)","Gear complies","Gear cheats"],["Trick complies","Trick $2m · Gear $2m","Trick −$1m · Gear $4.5m"],["Trick cheats","Trick $4.5m · Gear −$1m","Trick $0 · Gear $0"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Equilibrium: if Gear complies, Trick earns $2m by complying but $4.5m by cheating, so it cheats. If Gear cheats, Trick loses $1m by complying but earns $0 by cheating, so it cheats. Cheating is a dominant strategy for both. The Nash equilibrium is that both cheat: quantity and price are those of perfect competition and each firm makes zero economic profit."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Other Oligopoly Games"},{"t":"p","x":"• Advertising and R&D games are also prisoners’ dilemmas. Example: Kimberly-Clark (Kleenex) and P&G (Puffs) play a costly R&D game over the quality of facial tissues; each is driven to spend on R&D even though both would be better off if neither did."},{"t":"p","x":"• Game of chicken: it can arise when R&D produces a technology that cannot be patented, so both firms benefit from the R&D of either. Example: either Apple or Nokia would spend $9 million developing a new touch-screen technology that both could then use. If one does the R&D, the other’s best strategy is to do none; if one does none, the other’s best strategy is to do it. The equilibrium is that exactly one firm does the R&D, but the model cannot tell which one."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Repeated Games and Sequential Games"},{"t":"p","x":"• Repeated game: when the same game is played over and over, duopolists can sustain collusion and earn the monopoly profit, because cheating can be punished in later periods. Punishment strategies include tit-for-tat and the more severe trigger strategy; a tit-for-tat strategy is sufficient to produce a cooperative equilibrium in a repeated duopoly game."},{"t":"p","x":"• Price wars: they can result from tit-for-tat when demand is uncertain. A fall in demand lowers the price, which a rival may misread as cheating, and sets off a round of punishment."},{"t":"p","x":"• Sequential entry game in a contestable market: in the first stage the incumbent (Agile) decides whether to set the monopoly price or the competitive price; in the second stage the potential entrant (Wanabe) decides whether to enter or stay out. The game is solved by backward induction (start from the entrant’s decision, then the incumbent’s). Equilibrium: Agile sets the competitive price and makes zero economic profit, because a monopoly price would attract entry. A cheaper alternative for the incumbent is limit pricing: the highest price that still keeps the entrant out."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Antitrust Law"},{"t":"p","x":"Antitrust law regulates oligopolies and prevents them from becoming monopolies or behaving like monopolies."},{"t":"p","x":"• Price fixing is always illegal: if the Justice Department can prove it, there is no defense."},{"t":"p","x":"• Resale price maintenance: inefficient if it promotes monopoly pricing, but efficient if it gives retailers an incentive to provide an efficient level of retail service."},{"t":"p","x":"• Tying arrangements: some argue a firm can raise profit by tying, because where buyers have different willingness to pay for the separate items the firm can price discriminate and capture more consumer surplus (the Microsoft browser case is the classic example; combining products can also be genuine innovation)."},{"t":"p","x":"• Predatory pricing: economists are skeptical it occurs, because a high, certain and immediate loss is a poor exchange for a temporary, uncertain, future gain; no case has been definitively found."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Self-check"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can define oligopoly and explain interdependence and the temptation to cartelize."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can lay out a payoff matrix, find each player’s best reply and the Nash equilibrium, and recognize a dominant strategy."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can explain why the duopolists’ Nash equilibrium (both cheat) gives the competitive outcome with zero profit."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can explain the advertising, R&D and chicken games, and why punishment strategies can sustain collusion in repeated games."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can solve a sequential entry game by backward induction and explain limit pricing."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ I can explain price fixing, resale price maintenance, tying and predatory pricing."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"Q1 (MCQ). Which description fits an oligopoly? (a) Many firms, identical products, free entry; (b) one firm and a barrier to entry; (c) a small number of firms whose profits depend on each other’s decisions, with barriers to entry; (d) many firms selling differentiated products with free entry."},{"t":"p","x":"Q2. Two players who cannot communicate are each asked a question and may answer honestly or lie. If both are honest each receives $100. If one is honest and the other lies, the liar receives $500 and the honest player nothing. If both lie, each receives $50. (a) Construct the payoff matrix. (b) Find the dominant strategy and the equilibrium. (c) Is the equilibrium the best joint outcome?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q3. In the Trick and Gear cartel game, each firm earns $2 million a week if both comply, $0 if both cheat, and if only one cheats the cheat earns $4.5 million and the complier loses $1 million. (a) What is each firm’s best reply if the rival complies? If the rival cheats? (b) What is the Nash equilibrium? (c) Why is a cartel hard to maintain even though both firms would gain from compliance?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q4. Apple and Nokia can each spend $9 million on touch-screen R&D that cannot be patented, so both would use the result whoever pays. Explain the best reply of Nokia to each action of Apple and state the equilibrium. Why can the model not predict which firm does the R&D?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q5. Explain how a tit-for-tat strategy can lead duopolists to cooperate in a repeated game, and why a fall in demand can start a price war."},{"t":"p","x":"Q6. In a contestable market, Agile (incumbent) chooses the monopoly price or the competitive price, then Wanabe chooses to enter or stay out. Using backward induction, explain why Agile may choose the competitive price even though the monopoly price would earn more if Wanabe stayed out. What is limit pricing?"},{"t":"p","x":"Q7. Two company managers speak. Company 1: “We should think twice before changing prices or launching a new marketing strategy; we are in a highly interactive market and our leading position could be endangered.” Company 2: “Raising our price by even 2% meant we could not sell a single unit; we must lower our costs to survive.” Identify the market structure of each firm and justify it."},{"t":"p","x":"Q8. Match each practice to the antitrust issue it raises: (i) two rivals agree on the price they will charge; (ii) a maker sets the price at which retailers must resell; (iii) a seller will only sell product A to buyers who also buy product B; (iv) a firm sets a price below cost to drive rivals out, planning to raise it afterwards."},{"t":"a","x":"A1. (c). An oligopoly has a small number of firms with barriers to entry, and each firm’s profit depends on every firm’s actions.\nA2. (a) Matrix (payoffs, Player 1 · Player 2): both honest 100 · 100; P1 honest and P2 lies 0 · 500; P1 lies and P2 honest 500 · 0; both lie 50 · 50. (b) Whatever the other does, lying is better: if the other is honest, lying gives 500 against 100; if the other lies, lying gives 50 against 0. Lying is a dominant strategy, so the Nash equilibrium is that both lie and each gets $50. (c) No. Both honest gives $100 each, better for both; it is not reached because each has an incentive to lie (a prisoners’ dilemma).\nA3. (a) If the rival complies: complying earns $2m, cheating $4.5m, so cheat. If the rival cheats: complying loses $1m, cheating earns $0, so cheat. (b) Both cheat: each earns zero economic profit, the competitive outcome. (c) Cheating is a dominant strategy; each firm fears being the complier who loses $1m and is tempted by the $4.5m of cheating, so the agreement collapses even though $2m each is better than $0 each.\nA4. If Apple does the R&D, Nokia does better doing none (it uses the technology free and saves $9m). If Apple does no R&D, Nokia’s best reply is to do it (the technology is worth more than $9m to Nokia). The equilibrium is that exactly one firm does the R&D, but there are two such equilibria (Apple does it or Nokia does it), so the model cannot say which one.\nA5. In a repeated game each firm knows cheating today will be punished tomorrow: under tit-for-tat a firm cooperates if the rival cooperated in the previous period and cheats if it cheated. The short-term gain from cheating is outweighed by the loss from later punishment, so both cooperate and share the monopoly profit. When demand is uncertain, a fall in demand lowers the price; a firm may read the fall as cheating by the rival and retaliate, starting a price war.\nA6. Work backward. If Agile sets the monopoly price, Wanabe finds entry profitable and enters, which wipes out Agile’s monopoly profit. If Agile sets the competitive price, Wanabe cannot make a profit and stays out, so Agile earns zero economic profit but keeps the market. Anticipating this, Agile chooses the competitive price. Limit pricing is a less costly alternative: set the highest price that still deters entry, so the incumbent keeps some profit.\nA7. Company 1 is an oligopoly: a few interdependent firms, each worried about rivals’ reactions to a change in price or marketing. Company 2 is perfectly competitive (a price taker): a small price rise loses all sales because identical products are available elsewhere, so its only lever is to cut costs.\nA8. (i) Price fixing: always illegal. (ii) Resale price maintenance: inefficient if it supports monopoly pricing, efficient if it encourages good retail service. (iii) A tying arrangement: may let the seller price discriminate and capture consumer surplus. (iv) Predatory pricing: economists doubt it occurs because the immediate loss is certain and the later gain is not."}]}},"extras":[{"t":"p","x":"**BUS2105**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Microeconomics for Business**"},{"t":"p","x":"Complete Revision Synthesis & Practice Workbook"},{"t":"p","x":"Lingnan University · First Term, 2026–27 · Section 7 (Dr. Albert Wong)"},{"t":"p","x":"Textbook: Parkin, Economics, 14th Global Edition (Pearson / MyLab Economics)"},{"t":"p","x":"Chapters covered: 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11, 12, 13, 14"},{"t":"p","x":"Compiled from all lecture materials shared on Moodle, with original practice exercises and fully worked answer keys."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Table of Contents"},{"t":"p","x":"(Right-click and choose \"Update Field\" in Word to populate/refresh this list.)"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Course Overview"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Course Information"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Details"],["Course","BUS 2105 Microeconomics for Business (Section 7), 2026–27 First Term"],["Instructor","Prof. Wong Man Chung, Albert — SEK101/2, Tel. 2616 7852, albertwong@ln.edu.hk"],["Textbook","Parkin, Michael. Economics, 14th Global Edition (2022) + MyLab Economics with e-text"],["MyLab Course ID (Section L4 & L7)","lingnan52752"],["Course description","Study of the choices individuals and firms make and how these choices interact in markets; characteristics of different market structures; economic environment of business."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Assessment Breakdown"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Component","Weight"],["Final Examination","50%"],["Midterm Test (in-class, MC, covers Ch. 1, 3–6, 8, 9)","20%"],["MyLab Economics assignments*","10%"],["Group Debate","15%"],["Participation","5%"]]},{"t":"p","x":"*If a student does not use MyLab Economics, the midterm weight becomes 30% instead."},{"t":"p","x":"Midterm test: Monday 2 November 2026, in class, 40 MC questions, 1 hour, covering Chapters 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Course Schedule (as shared on Moodle)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Week / Date","Topic","Reading"],["7 Sep","Introduction","Ch. 1"],["14 Sep","Demand and Supply","Ch. 3"],["21 Sep","Elasticity","Ch. 4"],["28 Sep","Efficiency, Equity","Ch. 5"],["5 Oct","Government Actions in Markets; Utility and Demand","Ch. 6, Ch. 8"],["12 Oct","Possibilities, Preferences, and Choices","Ch. 9"],["19 Oct","Chung Yeung Festival — no class","—"],["26 Oct","Output and Costs","Ch. 11"],["2 Nov","Midterm Test; Perfect Competition (not in this synthesis — slides not shared)","Ch. 12"],["9 Nov","Monopoly (not in this synthesis — slides not shared)","Ch. 13"],["16 Nov","Monopolistic Competition (not in this synthesis — slides not shared)","Ch. 14"],["23 Nov","Oligopoly / Course Review (not in this synthesis — slides not shared)","Ch. 15"],["30 Nov","Debate","—"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Note: Chapters 2, 7, 10 and 15 were not among the files shared on Moodle for this synthesis and are therefore not covered in the chapter notes below. Chapters 12 (Perfect Competition) and 13 (Monopoly) were added on 7 October 2026, and Chapter 14 (Monopolistic Competition) on 13 October 2026, once their lecture slides were shared. This document now covers the eleven chapters for which lecture slides have been provided: Chapters 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11, 12, 13 and 14."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"MyLab Economics — Quick Registration Note"},{"t":"p","x":"MyLab Economics is Pearson's online homework/quiz platform used for 10% of the course grade (one quiz per chapter; best 8 of 12 scores count). To register: go to https://mlm.pearson.com/global, enter the Course ID for your section (L4/L7 = lingnan52752), create or sign in to a Pearson account, and redeem your access code (from the textbook bundle). Use Chrome for best results, with pop-ups and cookies enabled. Full step-by-step screenshots are in the separate \"MyLab Econ Student Registration\" file on Moodle — this synthesis does not reproduce them since they contain no economics content to revise."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"How to Use This Document"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Exam-Style Review Checklist (All Chapters)"},{"t":"p","x":"Use this list to self-test before the midterm (Ch. 1, 3–6, 8, 9) and the final (all chapters). Tick each item only once you can explain it out loud without notes."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 1 — What is Economics?"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Define scarcity, incentive, microeconomics, macroeconomics."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ List the 4 factors of production and what each earns (rent/wages/interest/profit)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Distinguish positive vs normative statements with an example of each."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain rational choice and the MB vs MC decision rule."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain opportunity cost, including correctly excluding sunk costs."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 3 — Demand and Supply"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ State the law of demand and the law of supply."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ List all 6 demand shifters and all 6 supply shifters."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Distinguish a movement along a curve from a shift of the curve."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Solve algebraically for equilibrium price and quantity from linear Qd/Qs equations."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Predict price and quantity changes for all 4 basic and 4 combined shift scenarios."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 4 — Elasticity"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Calculate PED, YED, and XED using the midpoint (average) method."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Classify demand as elastic / unit elastic / inelastic / perfectly elastic or inelastic."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Apply the total revenue test correctly (link price change, elasticity, and TR)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ List the 3 determinants of PED and the 2 determinants of elasticity of supply."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Interpret the sign of income elasticity (normal vs inferior) and cross elasticity (substitute vs complement)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 5 — Efficiency and Equity"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ List the 8 resource allocation methods and when each works best."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain why the demand curve is a marginal benefit curve and the supply curve is a marginal cost curve."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Calculate consumer surplus and producer surplus from linear demand/supply."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ State the efficiency condition (MSB = MSC) and calculate deadweight loss for under- and over-production."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ List the sources of market failure and contrast the fair-rules view with the fair-results view."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 6 — Government Actions in Markets"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain why a binding price ceiling creates a shortage, and a binding price floor creates a surplus."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain the tax wedge and calculate the buyer's and seller's share of a per-unit tax."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain how relative elasticities of demand and supply determine tax incidence."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Calculate tax revenue and deadweight loss from a per-unit tax."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Distinguish the effects of a production quota (underproduction) from a subsidy (overproduction)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 8 — Utility and Demand"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Define total utility and marginal utility, and state the principle of diminishing marginal utility."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Apply the utility-maximizing rule (spend all income; equalize MU per dollar across goods)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Solve a consumer-choice problem using a table of just-affordable combinations."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain the paradox of value using total vs marginal utility."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Name the three \"bounds\" on rational choice from behavioral economics."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 9 — Possibilities, Preferences, and Choices"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Write and interpret a budget equation; identify intercepts and slope (relative price)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain how a price change rotates, and an income change shifts, the budget line."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Define MRS and diminishing MRS, and state the 3 conditions for the best affordable point."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Decompose a price effect into substitution effect and income effect."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain why demand slopes down for a normal good and (usually) for an inferior good."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 11 — Output and Costs"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Distinguish the short run from the long run, and identify a sunk cost."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Derive and relate TP, MP, and AP; state the law of diminishing returns."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Derive TFC, TVC, TC, AFC, AVC, ATC, and MC from a cost/output schedule."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ State the MC–AVC–ATC relationship (crossing at each average's minimum)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain economies of scale, diseconomies of scale, and minimum efficient scale using the LRAC curve."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Mixed Practice Set (Spanning Multiple Chapters)"},{"t":"p","x":"A cumulative-style set similar in spirit to the midterm/final. Mixes conceptual, MCQ and quantitative questions across Chapters 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9 and 11."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **[Ch.1] Diego is deciding whether to attend a weekend conference. The conference fee is $150. If he attends, he will miss a day of freelance work worth $300. He has already paid a non-refundable $50 deposit for a photography workshop that overlaps with the conference (he loses this $50 regardless of his choice). What is Diego's opportunity cost of attending the conference?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **[Ch.3] The market for umbrellas has Qd = 500 − 20P and Qs = 20P − 100. (a) Find the equilibrium price and quantity. (b) An unusually rainy season increases demand by 200 units at every price. Find the new equilibrium price and quantity."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **[Ch.4] A cinema cuts ticket prices from $15 to $13 (average $14), and weekly attendance rises from 2,000 to 2,400 (average 2,200). (a) Calculate the price elasticity of demand. (b) What happens to total revenue? (c) Is demand elastic or inelastic over this range?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **[Ch.5] Demand for a good is Qd = 200 − 4P and supply is Qs = 6P − 50. (a) Find the equilibrium price and quantity. (b) Calculate consumer surplus and producer surplus at equilibrium."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **[Ch.6] Before any tax, the equilibrium price of a bottle of wine is $20 with 100,000 bottles sold monthly. A $5/bottle excise tax is imposed. After the tax, consumers pay $23/bottle and quantity falls to 90,000. (a) How much of the tax do consumers pay vs. producers? (b) What is monthly tax revenue? (c) What is the deadweight loss?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q6. **[Ch.8] Priya has $18 to spend on tea ($3/cup) and pastries ($3 each). Her marginal utility schedule (1st–6th unit) is: Tea: 15, 12, 9, 6, 3, 0. Pastries: 18, 15, 12, 9, 6, 3. Find her utility-maximizing combination(s)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q7. **[Ch.9] Sam has $40/week to spend on video games (price $10) and books (price $5). Write his budget equation with games (G) on the vertical axis and books (B) on the horizontal axis, and state the opportunity cost of one more book in terms of games."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q8. **[Ch.11] A firm has total fixed cost of $200. At Q = 50 units, total variable cost is $300. At Q = 100 units, total variable cost is $500. (a) Compute average total cost (ATC) at Q = 50 and Q = 100. (b) Compute the average marginal cost over this range. (c) Is average variable cost (AVC) rising or falling over this range, and is this consistent with your marginal cost answer?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q9. **[Ch.5 + Ch.6] A city imposes a rent ceiling below the market-clearing rent. Using the concepts of consumer surplus, producer surplus, and deadweight loss, explain why this policy is inefficient even though some renters clearly benefit from paying a lower rent."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **Opportunity cost = conference fee ($150) + forgone freelance earnings ($300) = $450. The $50 workshop deposit is a SUNK COST — it is lost either way and does not belong in the opportunity-cost calculation.\n**A2. **(a) 500 − 20P = 20P − 100 → 600 = 40P → P = $15; Q = 500 − 20(15) = 200 units. (b) New demand: Qd′ = 700 − 20P. Set 700 − 20P = 20P − 100 → 800 = 40P → P = $20; Q = 20(20) − 100 = 300 units. (Price rises from $15 to $20 and quantity rises from 200 to 300.)\n**A3. **(a) %ΔQ = (2,400−2,000)/2,200 = 18.2%. %ΔP = (13−15)/14 = −14.3% (magnitude 14.3%). PED = 18.2%/14.3% ≈ 1.27. (b) TR before = $15×2,000 = $30,000; TR after = $13×2,400 = $31,200 — total revenue RISES when price falls. (c) Demand is ELASTIC (PED > 1), consistent with revenue rising after the price cut.\n**A4. **(a) 200 − 4P = 6P − 50 → 250 = 10P → P = $25; Q = 200 − 4(25) = 100 units. (b) Demand's price-intercept (Q=0): 4P=200 → P=$50; CS = ½×(50−25)×100 = $1,250. Supply's price-intercept (Q=0): 6P=50 → P≈$8.33; PS = ½×(25−8.33)×100 ≈ $833.33. Total surplus ≈ $2,083.33.\n**A5. **(a) Consumers pay $3/bottle more ($20→$23); producers receive $2/bottle less ($20→$18, since $23−$5 tax=$18). (b) Tax revenue = $5 × 90,000 = $450,000/month. (c) Deadweight loss = ½ × $5 × (100,000−90,000) = ½×5×10,000 = $25,000/month.\n**A6. **Since tea and pastries have the same price ($3), just-affordable combinations satisfy T + P = 6. Cumulative utility: Tea (0,15,27,36,42,45,45 for 0–6 cups), Pastry (0,18,33,45,54,60,63 for 0–6). Combination totals: (0,6)=63; (1,5)=75; (2,4)=81; (3,3)=81; (4,2)=75; (5,1)=63; (6,0)=45. Maximum total utility = 81, achieved at EITHER 2 tea + 4 pastries OR 3 tea + 3 pastries — a tie, because with equal prices the crossover happens exactly at the boundary between these two combinations (the marginal utilities of the 3rd tea and 4th pastry are both 9, equal to each other).\n**A7. **Budget equation: 10G + 5B = 40 → G = 4 − 0.5B. Vertical intercept: G = 4 games (if B = 0). Horizontal intercept: B = 8 books (if G = 0). The opportunity cost of one more book = 0.5 games (the relative price PB/PG = 5/10 = 0.5, the magnitude of the budget line's slope).\n**A8. **(a) At Q=50: TC = $200+$300 = $500, ATC = $500/50 = $10. At Q=100: TC = $200+$500 = $700, ATC = $700/100 = $7. (b) Average MC = ΔTVC/ΔQ = ($500−$300)/(100−50) = $200/50 = $4/unit. (c) AVC(50) = $300/50 = $6; AVC(100) = $500/100 = $5 — AVC is FALLING over this range, which is consistent with theory because MC ($4) is below both AVC values, pulling the average down.\n**A9. **A rent ceiling below equilibrium restricts the quantity of housing supplied below the efficient quantity, so marginal social benefit (MSB) exceeds marginal social cost (MSC) on the units no longer supplied — this is underproduction. The consumer surplus of the few tenants who obtain housing at the lower regulated rent may rise, but producer surplus (landlords) shrinks substantially, and the units that are no longer built or rented represent a pure deadweight loss (lost surplus that no one captures) — compounded further by the real resource costs of increased search activity and black-market activity. Because total surplus (CS+PS) falls even though a subset of renters individually gains, the policy is inefficient overall, even though it may still be judged \"fair\" or \"unfair\" on separate equity grounds."}]},"BUS2202":{"file":"BUS2202_Organisational_Behaviour_Synthese_et_Exercices.docx","mtime":"2026-10-11","lessons":{"1":{"title":"Introduction to the Field of Organisational Behaviour","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 1   |   Scheduled: 7 Sep 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. What Is Organisational Behaviour?"},{"t":"li","x":"**Organisational behaviour (OB): **the study of what people think, feel and do in and around organisations."},{"t":"li","x":"**Organisations: **groups of people who work interdependently toward some purpose."},{"t":"p","x":"OB matters for individuals (helps you understand and predict workplace events, and get things done by influencing/coordinating with others — employers rate OB skills as highly important) and for organisations (OB theory improves organisational effectiveness)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Organisational Effectiveness"},{"t":"p","x":"An ideal state in which the organisation:"},{"t":"li","x":"Has a good fit with its external environment — organisations are \"open systems\" that take in inputs and transform them into outputs."},{"t":"li","x":"Effectively transforms inputs into outputs, especially through its human capital."},{"t":"li","x":"Satisfies the needs of its key stakeholders."},{"t":"li","x":"**Human capital: **the knowledge, skills, abilities, creative thinking and other valued resources employees bring — essential for survival, difficult to find/copy, and hard to replace with technology."},{"t":"li","x":"**Stakeholders: **any entity that affects or is affected by the firm’s objectives and actions; firms must manage conflicting stakeholder interests with limited resources."},{"t":"li","x":"**Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR): **activities intended to benefit society/environment beyond the firm’s immediate financial or legal obligations — the \"triple bottom line\" of economic, social and environmental performance."},{"t":"li","x":"**Integrative Model of OB: **the course’s big-picture framework, showing how the environment and organisational context flow into individual and team inputs and processes (e.g., MARS, perceptions, teams, leadership — the topics of Lessons 1-10), which in turn drive individual, team and organisational-level outcomes (performance, effectiveness). Every later chapter in this course \"plugs into\" this overall model at the individual, team, or organisational level of analysis."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. The Five Anchors of OB Knowledge"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Anchor","Meaning"],["Systematic research","OB knowledge is built on rigorous research; organisations should practice evidence-based management."],["Practical orientation","OB theories must be usable in practice — application is the true measure of a theory’s impact."],["Multidisciplinary","Many OB concepts are borrowed from psychology, sociology, economics, etc., while OB also develops its own theories."],["Contingency","The same action can have different consequences in different situations — the situation must be diagnosed before choosing an action."],["Multiple levels of analysis","OB topics are usually relevant at the individual, team and organisational levels."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Emerging Workplace Trends"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the Workplace"},{"t":"li","x":"AI = technologies that autonomously simulate human intelligence/cognitive capabilities (predictive, generative, and — potentially — artificial general intelligence)."},{"t":"li","x":"Risks: incomplete/flawed input data causing \"hallucinations\"; error amplification when AI is trained on its own outputs (\"AI poisons itself\"); imperfect prompting produces flawed output."},{"t":"li","x":"Increasingly relevant to OB topics such as decision making, job design and team dynamics."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Inclusive Workplace and Diversity"},{"t":"li","x":"**Surface-level diversity: **observable demographic/physiological differences."},{"t":"li","x":"**Deep-level diversity: **psychological differences (values, beliefs, personality) — includes generational diversity, which is more about life stage than generational cohort."},{"t":"li","x":"**Benefits: **better decisions, attitudes and team performance, more creativity, better representation of community needs, moral/legal imperative."},{"t":"li","x":"**Challenges: **teams take longer to become effective, and diversity can bring more dysfunctional conflict, lower information sharing and lower morale if not managed well."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Work-Life Integration, Remote/Hybrid Work, Employment Relationships"},{"t":"li","x":"**Work-life integration: **being effectively engaged in work and nonwork roles with low role conflict; depleting resources in one role starves the others."},{"t":"li","x":"**Remote work: **performing the job away from the organisation’s premises. Benefits: better work-life integration, lower turnover, higher productivity, environmental and cost benefits. Risks: social isolation, less informal communication, lower team cohesion, weaker organisational culture."},{"t":"li","x":"**Hybrid work: **mixing home and office work; can create tension between employees and executives over expectations."},{"t":"li","x":"**Three employment relationships: **direct employment (higher work quality/innovation/agility, but risk of lower satisfaction among indirect workers on mixed teams); indirect employment (outsourced/agency — generally lower job satisfaction, weaker social networks, ambiguous manager discretion); contract employment (worker serves a client through their own firm)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. The MARS Model of Individual Behaviour and Performance"},{"t":"p","x":"Four factors directly determine individual behaviour and performance — remembered as MARS:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Factor","Definition","Key point"],["Motivation","Internal forces affecting direction, intensity and persistence of voluntary effort.","Determines whether/how hard someone tries."],["Ability","Aptitudes (natural talents) and learned capabilities needed to complete a task.","Improved via person-job matching: selecting, developing, or redesigning jobs."],["Role perceptions","Understanding of the job duties expected of us (tasks, priorities, preferred behaviours/procedures).","Clear role perceptions improve performance, coordination and motivation."],["Situational factors","Context beyond the person’s immediate control.","Can be a constraint, a facilitator, or a cue for behaviour."]]},{"t":"p","x":"All four MARS factors must be present for good performance — a deficiency in any one of them will constrain behaviour and results, regardless of how strong the others are."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Five Types of Individual Behaviour in Organisations"},{"t":"li","x":"**1. Task performance: **voluntary, goal-directed behaviours — proficient (performing known tasks well), adaptive (adjusting to change), and proactive (self-initiated improvement)."},{"t":"li","x":"**2. Organisational citizenship behaviours (OCBs): **cooperative, helpful behaviours that support the work context beyond formal job duties; directed at individuals and/or the organisation; some are discretionary, others near-implicit job requirements; can have negative side effects (e.g., overload) if excessive."},{"t":"li","x":"**3. Counterproductive work behaviours: **voluntary behaviours that may harm the organisation (e.g., theft, harassment, sabotage)."},{"t":"li","x":"**4. Joining and staying with the organisation: **relates to recruitment/retention — skills shortages and high turnover are ongoing challenges."},{"t":"li","x":"**5. Maintaining work attendance: **absenteeism (driven mainly by situational factors and motivation) and presenteeism (attending work while significantly unwell/impaired, reducing effective capacity)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Further points from the slides"},{"t":"p","x":"Globant became a highly successful technology company by applying teamwork, design thinking, an inclusive culture and other organizational behavior practices. Emsisoft, an anti-malware firm, is a completely remote (distributed) organization with no head office; its three dozen staff work from home around the world and the CEO leads from New Zealand (previously Austria)."},{"t":"p","x":"Practicing work-life integration: literally integrate two or more roles; flexible work scheduling; align work and nonwork roles with personal characteristics; boundary management. Life roles are inherently integrated, and depleting personal resources in one role starves the others."},{"t":"p","x":"Course administration (orientation slides): assessments are the individual case study (15%), the group project (25%), class participation (10%) and the final exam (50%, held 8-12 and 14-19 December)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: According to the MARS model, which factor refers to an employee’s understanding of what tasks they are accountable for and which behaviours are preferred?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Motivation"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Ability"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Role perceptions"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Situational factors"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: A completely remote software company with no head office, whose staff work from home worldwide, is an example of which emerging workplace trend?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Hybrid work"},{"t":"p","x":"b) A fully distributed/remote organisation"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Indirect employment"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Presenteeism"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain, using the \"open systems\" idea, why organisational effectiveness requires more than just producing outputs efficiently."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Distinguish surface-level diversity from deep-level diversity, and give one workplace example of each."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A talented graphic designer at a marketing agency consistently misses deadlines. She clearly wants to do well and has the right design skills, but she says she is \"never quite sure what the client actually wants approved by when.\" Using the MARS model, diagnose which factor is most likely deficient here, and recommend one concrete fix."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **c) Role perceptions — the scenario describes understanding of tasks, priorities and preferred behaviours, which is the definition of role perceptions in the MARS model.\n**2. **b) A fully distributed/remote organisation (like Emsisoft in the slides) — the whole company operates with no physical head office; this differs from hybrid work, which mixes home and office time for the same employees.\n**3. **Being an \"open system\" means an organisation depends on a good fit with its external environment (getting the right inputs, meeting stakeholder/market needs) in addition to internally converting inputs to outputs. A firm can be internally efficient yet still fail if it is poorly aligned with its environment or does not satisfy key stakeholders — so effectiveness = good external fit + efficient transformation + stakeholder satisfaction, not efficiency alone.\n**4. **Surface-level diversity refers to observable, demographic or physiological differences (e.g., age, gender, ethnicity, visible disability). Deep-level diversity refers to psychological differences that are less visible (e.g., values, personality, attitudes, beliefs) — for example, two employees of the same age and gender (similar surface-level) might hold very different work values (different deep-level diversity).\n**5. **The most likely deficiency is role perceptions: she has ability (design skill) and apparently motivation (wants to do well), but lacks clarity on tasks/priorities/expected approval process (\"never sure what... approved by when\"). Fix: clarify role perceptions directly — e.g., a written client-approval workflow/checklist specifying who signs off, by when, and in what format, agreed with her manager and the client-facing team, so expectations and priorities are explicit rather than assumed."}]},"2":{"title":"Individual Behaviour, Personality and Values","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 2   |   Scheduled: 14 Sep 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Personality"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **a relatively enduring pattern of thoughts, emotions and behaviours that characterizes a person, plus the psychological processes behind them. Personality traits are categories of behaviour tendencies that are evident across situations."},{"t":"li","x":"**Nature vs. nurture: **heredity explains roughly 50% of behavioural tendencies (nature); the rest is shaped by socialization and learning (nurture). Personality stabilizes in young adulthood as self-concept and executive function (self-regulation) mature, though some traits can still change over the life course."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Big Five (Five-Factor) Personality Model"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Factor","High scorers tend to be...","Workplace relevance"],["Openness to experience","Imaginative, curious, unconventional","May predict creative performance"],["Conscientiousness","Careful, disciplined, organized, achievement-oriented","Best overall predictor of job performance across jobs; weak predictor of adaptive/proactive performance"],["Extraversion","Outgoing, talkative, sociable, assertive","Associated with effective leaders and salespeople"],["Agreeableness","Trusting, cooperative, compassionate","Predicts team-member and customer-service performance; weaker predictor of proficient/proactive performance"],["Neuroticism (emotional stability)","Anxious, insecure (low stability) vs. calm, resilient (high stability)","Low neuroticism linked to better stress tolerance"]]},{"t":"li","x":"**Issues with the Big Five model: **"},{"t":"li","x":"Higher scores are not always \"better\" (e.g., extreme conscientiousness can reduce flexibility)."},{"t":"li","x":"Narrower facets within a factor (e.g., assertiveness within extraversion) sometimes predict better than the broad factor."},{"t":"li","x":"Personality is not perfectly static over time."},{"t":"li","x":"The five-factor model does not capture every perspective on personality (e.g., the Dark Triad and Jungian/MBTI types only partially overlap with it)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"MBTI (Myers-Briggs Type Indicator) / Jungian Theory"},{"t":"li","x":"The most widely used personality test and the most widely studied measure of cognitive style."},{"t":"li","x":"Takes a neutral view of results (no \"good\"/\"bad\" type) and improves self-awareness and mutual understanding."},{"t":"li","x":"Poor at predicting job performance, effective leadership, or team development — caution is advised before using it to select job applicants."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Dark Triad of Personality"},{"t":"p","x":"Three overlapping but distinct \"dark side\" personality traits, discussed alongside leadership because they help explain toxic leadership and workplace misconduct."},{"t":"li","x":"**Machiavellianism: **strong motivation to get what one wants at others’ expense; believes deceit is a natural/acceptable tool; enjoys misleading, outwitting or controlling others; rarely trusts or empathizes with coworkers."},{"t":"li","x":"**Narcissism: **an obsessive belief in one’s own superiority and entitlement, an excessive need for attention/admiration, and intense envy of others."},{"t":"li","x":"**Psychopathy: **ruthless, manipulative \"social predators\"; the \"mask of psychopathy\" is superficial charm hiding selfish self-promotion; impulsive, antisocial, sometimes fraudulent, thrill-seeking behaviour."},{"t":"li","x":"**Workplace consequences: **more organisational politics (influence tactics for personal gain), higher risk of serious white-collar crime, workplace aggression/bullying (linked to psychopathy), poorer decision-making, and typically less effective team behaviour — though the Dark Triad can occasionally correlate with some positive outcomes (e.g., short-term charm, risk-taking), the overall workplace effect is negative."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Values in the Workplace"},{"t":"li","x":"**Values: **stable, evaluative beliefs that guide our preferences and define what is right/wrong or good/bad — what we feel we \"ought\" to do; organized into a value system (a personal hierarchy of values)."},{"t":"p","x":"Compared with personality, values are more evaluative (not just descriptive), can conflict with each other, and are shaped more by nurture than nature."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Schwartz’s Values Model"},{"t":"p","x":"57 values grouped into 10 categories, and further into 4 broader quadrants:"},{"t":"li","x":"Openness to change — motivated to pursue new/innovative ways."},{"t":"li","x":"Conservation — motivated to preserve the status quo/tradition."},{"t":"li","x":"Self-enhancement — motivated by self-interest."},{"t":"li","x":"Self-transcendence — motivated to promote the welfare of others and nature."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"How Values Influence Decisions and Behaviour"},{"t":"li","x":"They affect the relative attractiveness of different choices."},{"t":"li","x":"They frame how we perceive situations."},{"t":"li","x":"They motivate us to act consistently with our self-concept and public image."},{"t":"p","x":"**When values fail to predict behaviour**"},{"t":"li","x":"Situational barriers or lack of opportunity."},{"t":"li","x":"Counter-motivational forces (competing pressures)."},{"t":"li","x":"Lack of values awareness — values are abstract and easy to overlook during routine behaviour."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Values Congruence"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **the similarity between a person’s value hierarchy and that of another source (a team, or the organisation)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Team values congruence: **linked to higher team cohesion and performance."},{"t":"li","x":"**Person-organisation values congruence: **linked to higher job satisfaction, loyalty and organisational citizenship, and lower stress and turnover."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Ethical Values and Behaviour"},{"t":"li","x":"**Ethics: **the study of moral principles/values and whether actions/outcomes are right/wrong, good/bad."},{"t":"p","x":"Four ethical principles:"},{"t":"li","x":"Utilitarianism — the greatest good for the greatest number."},{"t":"li","x":"Individual rights — everyone holds the same natural rights."},{"t":"li","x":"Distributive justice — benefits and burdens should be shared equally or proportionally."},{"t":"li","x":"Ethic of care — a moral obligation to help others."},{"t":"li","x":"**Moral intensity: **the degree an issue demands ethical consideration — higher when consequences are large, there’s wide agreement the outcome is good/bad, the outcome is probable, and many people are affected."},{"t":"li","x":"**Moral sensitivity: **a person’s ability to detect and judge the importance of a moral dilemma — higher with more expertise/knowledge of norms, relevant past experience, empathy, a self-view as an ethical person, and mindfulness."},{"t":"li","x":"**Supporting ethical behaviour: **a corporate code of ethics, educating/testing employees’ ethical knowledge, systems for reporting/investigating wrongdoing, and an ethical culture with ethical leadership."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Values Across Cultures (Cross-Cultural Dimensions)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Dimension","High end","Low end / contrast"],["Individualism","Values personal freedom, self-sufficiency, being recognized for unique qualities (e.g., USA)","Low individualism societies value group ties more (contrast with collectivism)"],["Collectivism","Values group membership and harmonious relationships within the group (e.g., China/Taiwan)","Low collectivism (e.g., some individualist Western societies)"],["Power distance","Accepts unequal power, obedience to authority, formal rules (e.g., China, India)","Expects relatively equal power sharing, interdependent boss relationship (e.g., Sweden, Denmark)"],["Uncertainty avoidance","Feels threatened by ambiguity; values structure and direct communication (e.g., Greece, Belgium)","Tolerates ambiguity and uncertainty (e.g., Singapore, Denmark)"],["Achievement vs. nurturing orientation","Assertiveness, competitiveness, materialism (e.g., Japan, USA)","Values relationships and human interaction (e.g., Netherlands, Sweden)"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Note: individualism and collectivism are conceptually distinct (not simply opposite ends of one scale) — a culture/person can, in principle, score independently on each."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Cases from the slides"},{"t":"p","x":"Bridgewater Associates places considerable weight on the personality, values and other individual differences of its job applicants and employees. IKEA describes itself as a values-driven company, so applicants need to reflect on whether their personal values are congruent with its organizational values. At Beam Suntory, CEO Takeshi Niinami recognized that cross-cultural differences could be a concern when the Japanese beverage company acquired Jim Beam, the Kentucky bourbon maker."},{"t":"p","x":"Schwartz's values circumplex: 57 values clustered into 10 categories and four quadrants. Openness to change: motivated to pursue innovative ways. Conservation: motivated to preserve the status quo. Self-enhancement: motivated by self-interest. Self-transcendence: motivated to promote the welfare of others and nature."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: Which of the Big Five personality factors is generally the strongest overall predictor of job performance across most types of jobs?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Openness to experience"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Extraversion"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Conscientiousness"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Agreeableness"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: An employee who takes pleasure in outwitting and controlling coworkers, believes deceit is an acceptable tool to get ahead, and rarely trusts others, best illustrates which Dark Triad trait?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Narcissism"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Psychopathy"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Machiavellianism"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Neuroticism"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain why values are described as \"more evaluative than descriptive\" compared with personality, and why this matters for predicting workplace behaviour."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Using Hofstede-style cultural values, explain why a manager from a high power-distance culture might struggle to motivate employees using a highly participative decision-making style in a country with much lower power distance (or vice versa)."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A new hire tells his manager during onboarding, \"I really value work-life balance and creative freedom,\" but three months in he is consistently the first to arrive, stays late without being asked, and volunteers for structured, high-pressure client-facing projects. Using the \"values don’t always predict behaviour\" concepts, give two plausible explanations for this apparent values-behaviour gap."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **c) Conscientiousness — it is described in the slides as a weak predictor only of adaptive/proactive performance, but it is the best-established overall predictor of general task performance across most job types.\n**2. **c) Machiavellianism — the specific combination of strategic deceit-as-acceptable, pleasure in manipulation, and low trust/empathy toward coworkers is the defining pattern of Machiavellianism, distinct from narcissism (entitlement/need for admiration) and psychopathy (impulsive, antisocial, superficially charming).\n**3. **Personality traits describe how someone typically behaves (\"what is\"), while values are evaluative — they define what someone believes ought to be done (right/wrong, good/bad) and guide preferences. This matters because two people can share the same value (e.g., \"honesty\") yet act differently depending on situational opportunity, competing motivations, or whether the value is salient/consciously activated in that moment — values are a weaker, more conditional predictor of specific behaviour than trait-behaviour links tend to be.\n**4. **In a high power-distance culture, employees are socialized to expect and be comfortable with unequal power, deference to authority and top-down decisions; being suddenly asked to freely voice opinions or share decision authority can feel uncomfortable, risky, or even like a sign of a weak/incompetent leader. Conversely, in a low power-distance culture, employees expect relatively equal power sharing and an interdependent (not purely dependent) relationship with their boss, so a highly directive, non-participative style can feel demotivating or be seen as illegitimate. Either mismatch between the leader’s style and the culture’s power-distance norms can undermine motivation — this is the same logic behind path-goal theory’s cultural contingencies covered in the leadership lesson.\n**5. **Two plausible explanations: (1) Situational factors/lack of opportunity — e.g., a genuinely demanding early-career workload, an under-resourced team, or a manager who implicitly rewards visibility, may be overriding his stated values as situational barriers. (2) Impression management / self-concept motivation early in a new job — he may be acting to build a strong first impression or prove commitment (consistent with self-enhancement/self-verification motives), with the values-consistent behaviour expected to reappear once he is established. (A third acceptable answer: low values awareness — values are abstract, and in the day-to-day routine of a new job, habitual/reactive behaviour can simply override an abstract stated value that is not consciously \"front of mind.\")"}]},"3":{"title":"Perception and Learning in Organisations","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 3   |   Scheduled: 21 Sep 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Self-Concept"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **our self-beliefs and self-evaluations, comparing our current (perceived) self with our desired (ideal) self, at three levels: individual, relational and collective."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Three Cs of Self-Concept"},{"t":"li","x":"**Complexity: **the number of distinct, important identities a person perceives in themselves; higher when the selves are separate/distinct rather than similar. High complexity = more adaptive, more diverse networks, but more stressful; low complexity = fewer identities to manage, more resources per identity."},{"t":"li","x":"**Consistency: **how compatible one’s multiple self-views are with each other and with one’s actual attributes."},{"t":"li","x":"**Clarity: **how clearly, confidently and stably the self-concept is defined; increases with age, self-reflection and consistency. High clarity is linked to better performance, leadership and career development, and less threat from conflict — though extremely high clarity can cause role inflexibility."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Self-Evaluation and Self-Regulation Processes"},{"t":"li","x":"**Self-enhancement: **the drive to see and present oneself positively (competent, ethical, valued); linked to better mental/physical health and higher \"can-do\" motivation, but also riskier decisions, inflated sense of personal causation, and slower recognition of one’s own mistakes."},{"t":"li","x":"**Self-verification: **the motivation to confirm and maintain our existing self-concept; we communicate our self-view to others, seek confirming feedback, and tend to dismiss feedback that contradicts our self-concept."},{"t":"li","x":"**Self-evaluation: **covers self-esteem (liking/respecting/being satisfied with oneself — high self-esteem = less swayed by others, more persistent, clearer thinking), self-efficacy (belief we can successfully perform a specific task — a MARS-model \"ability\" input), and locus of control (general belief about how much control we have over life events — an internal locus of control is linked to higher self-evaluation)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Social Self and Social Identity Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"We balance two opposing needs: to be distinctive/unique (personal identity) and to belong/be included with others (social identity). We define ourselves partly through the groups we identify with — especially groups we are easily identified with, that carry high status, or where we hold minority status in a given situation."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. The Perceptual Process"},{"t":"li","x":"**Perception: **the process of receiving information about, and making sense of, the world around us."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Selective Attention"},{"t":"p","x":"We select some sensory information and ignore the rest, influenced by characteristics of both the perceiver and the object; selected information is tagged with emotional markers. Biases include assumptions/expectations and confirmation bias (favouring information that fits what we already believe)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Perceptual Organization and Interpretation"},{"t":"li","x":"Categorical thinking — organizing people/things into categories to reduce complexity."},{"t":"li","x":"Perceptual grouping principles — similarity/proximity, closure (filling in missing pieces), and perceiving trends."},{"t":"li","x":"Mental models — knowledge structures (visual \"maps\" or cause-effect \"relational\" models) that help us describe, explain and predict the world; the risk is that mental models make it hard to see the world differently, so we must actively question them."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Perceptual Biases and Problems"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Stereotyping"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **assigning traits to people based on their membership in a social category — stereotypes may contain a \"kernel of truth\" but are embellished/distorted."},{"t":"li","x":"**Why it happens: **categorical thinking, the drive to comprehend/predict others’ behaviour, and support for self-enhancement/social identity, reinforced through three sub-processes: categorization (sorting people into groups), homogenization (assuming similarity within a group, difference between groups), and differentiation (favouring one’s own group)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Problems: **overgeneralization, stereotype threat, and it is a foundation of systemic/intentional discrimination. Stereotype activation is hard to prevent, but its application can be minimized."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Attribution Theory"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **the process of deciding whether an observed behaviour/event is caused mainly by internal factors (the person) or external factors (the situation, beyond the person’s control)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Self-serving bias: **attributing our own failures to external causes and our successes to internal causes (a product of self-enhancement)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Fundamental attribution error (correspondence bias): **the tendency to over-emphasize internal causes when explaining other people’s behaviour, because we find it harder to see the external/situational forces acting on them (a fairly modest-sized error, but still commonly discussed)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Self-Fulfilling Prophecy"},{"t":"p","x":"A cycle: (1) a supervisor forms expectations about an employee; (2) those expectations affect the supervisor’s own behaviour toward the employee; (3) the supervisor’s behaviour affects the employee’s ability/motivation (self-confidence); (4) the employee’s behaviour becomes more consistent with the supervisor’s original expectations."},{"t":"li","x":"**Strongest: **at the start of a relationship, when several people share the same expectation, and when the employee has low prior achievement."},{"t":"li","x":"**Minimizing it: **awareness (limited benefit on its own), a supportive/learning organisational culture, and hiring supervisors who are inherently optimistic about staff."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Other Perceptual Effects"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Effect","Description"],["Halo effect","A general impression from one trait colours our perception of a person’s other, unrelated traits."],["False-consensus effect","Overestimating how much others share our own beliefs, values or traits."],["Primacy effect","Quickly forming an opinion of someone based on the first information received — hard to change afterward."],["Recency effect","The most recent information dominates our overall perception/judgement."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Improving Perceptions"},{"t":"li","x":"**Awareness of biases: **a starting point, but on its own gives only limited bias reduction and can even reinforce stereotypes."},{"t":"li","x":"**Improving self-awareness: **e.g., the Implicit Association Test, and the Johari Window (a 2x2 model of self/others’ awareness: open, blind, hidden and unknown areas) to expand mutual understanding."},{"t":"li","x":"**Meaningful interaction: **based on the \"contact hypothesis\" — people working together on valued, shared activities reduces perceptual bias of one another and builds empathy (understanding and being sensitive to others’ feelings, thoughts and situations)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Global Mindset"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **the ability to adopt a global perspective, empathize and act effectively across cultures, process complex information about novel environments, and develop new, multilevel mental models."},{"t":"li","x":"**Developing it: **begins with self-awareness, comparing your own mental models against those from other cultures/regions, and building knowledge of other people/cultures — ideally through direct immersion (e.g., an exchange programme)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Cases from the slides"},{"t":"p","x":"Perceptions in engineering: stereotypes, discrimination and other misperceptions are among the reasons women are under-represented in the engineering profession. Nando's (South African restaurant chain) launched an advertisement, \"You People\", poking fun at our tendency to stereotype people who are different from us."},{"t":"p","x":"Self-serving bias examples: after the pandemic, Expedia blamed falling sales on travel restrictions and search engine optimisation, but the real reason was operational inefficiency; Netflix blamed lost subscribers on customers switching to chip-based credit cards and forgetting to update their accounts. Attribution theory rules are shown in Exhibit 3.4; the Johari Window is Exhibit 3.6."},{"t":"p","x":"Global mindset at EY: the firm encourages and supports temporary transfers (secondments) to other countries so employees develop a global mindset by working with people from different backgrounds and cultures."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: A manager rates an employee highly on \"leadership potential\" mainly because the employee is very articulate in meetings, and then (without further evidence) assumes the same person must also be highly organized and technically skilled. This is best explained by:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Fundamental attribution error"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Halo effect"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Self-fulfilling prophecy"},{"t":"p","x":"d) False-consensus effect"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: According to attribution theory, when we explain our own failures by pointing to bad luck or an unfair boss, but explain our own successes by pointing to our talent and hard work, we are showing:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) The fundamental attribution error"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Self-serving bias"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Stereotype threat"},{"t":"p","x":"d) The halo effect"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain the difference between the fundamental attribution error and self-serving bias, using one workplace example for each."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Using the Johari Window and the contact hypothesis, explain two concrete things an organisation could do to reduce stereotyping between two departments that rarely interact."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A new supervisor is told, before ever meeting her team, that \"the night-shift team is difficult and unmotivated.\" Within a few weeks, the night-shift team’s performance and engagement have genuinely declined. Using the self-fulfilling prophecy model, explain step-by-step how this could have happened, and suggest one action that could have prevented it."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Halo effect — a single positive trait (articulateness) is being used to infer unrelated positive traits (organization, technical skill) without direct evidence.\n**2. **b) Self-serving bias — internal attribution for success, external attribution for failure, specifically about one’s own behaviour/outcomes (this is distinct from the fundamental attribution error, which is about how we judge other people’s behaviour).\n**3. **Fundamental attribution error: judging other people’s behaviour by over-emphasizing internal/personal causes and under-weighting situational causes — e.g., assuming a colleague who missed a deadline is simply \"lazy,\" without considering that they were pulled onto an urgent client fire drill. Self-serving bias: how we explain our own behaviour/outcomes, attributing our own failures to external causes and our own successes to internal causes — e.g., a salesperson who hits target says \"I worked hard and built great client relationships\" (internal) but who misses target says \"the market was terrible this quarter\" (external).\n**4. **Two acceptable actions: (1) Run a joint, shared-goal project or workshop that puts members of both departments together on a valued task (contact hypothesis) — genuine cooperative interaction toward a common outcome reduces perceptual bias and builds empathy between the groups. (2) Use a structured Johari Window exercise in a cross-department session, where each side shares how they see themselves and receives feedback on their \"blind spot\" from the other department — this expands the shared \"open\" area of mutual understanding and can surface and correct inaccurate stereotypes on both sides.\n**5. **Step-by-step: (1) The new supervisor forms a negative expectation about the team before even meeting them, based only on hearsay. (2) This expectation shapes her own behaviour toward the team — e.g., less trust, more controlling supervision, less positive feedback, possibly less delegation. (3) Her behaviour affects the team’s actual motivation and confidence (they may feel micromanaged, undervalued or mistrusted). (4) Over time the team’s engagement and performance genuinely decline, appearing to \"confirm\" the original expectation, even though the expectation itself helped cause the outcome. Prevention: the supervisor (or whoever briefed her) should avoid transmitting unverified second-hand expectations before first-hand contact, and instead approach the team with a deliberately positive-but-realistic expectation and gather her own direct evidence before forming judgements — consistent with the guidance that leaders should maintain positive, realistic expectations toward all employees."}]},"4":{"title":"Workplace Emotions, Attitudes and Stress","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 4   |   Scheduled: 28 Sep 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Emotions vs. Attitudes"},{"t":"table","rows":[["","Attitudes","Emotions"],["Nature","Cluster of beliefs, feelings and behavioural intentions","Experiences tied to a specific attitude object"],["Basis","Judgments involving conscious reasoning","Operate as events, often nonconscious"],["Duration","More stable over time","Brief episodes"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Emotions-Attitudes-Behaviour Model"},{"t":"p","x":"Beliefs (established perceptions about the attitude object, e.g., \"this job involves long hours\") lead to feelings (conscious positive/negative evaluations formed from the valence of those beliefs), which lead to behavioural intentions (motivation to act), which are the best predictor of actual behaviour. However, the links between each stage are only \"contingencies\" — not guarantees — because personality, past experience, and situational constraints can break the chain at any step (e.g., two people share a feeling but form different intentions; or share an intention but only one has the situational freedom to act on it)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Cognitive Dissonance"},{"t":"p","x":"An emotional experience caused by a perceived inconsistency among our beliefs, feelings and behaviour — it threatens our self-image as a rational person and motivates us toward consistency. Because it is hard to undo behaviour already taken, we typically resolve dissonance by changing our beliefs/feelings about the attitude object, or by recalling previous decisions consistent with the current one."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Emotional Labor and Emotional Intelligence"},{"t":"li","x":"**Emotional labor: **the effort, planning and control needed to display organisationally desired emotions during interpersonal interactions. It is most stressful when the required display differs sharply from what is actually felt, or contradicts one’s self-concept. Emotional display norms also vary across cultures (e.g., Russian employees report more stress from being expected to smile at customers, since smiling carries a different cultural meaning there)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Surface acting: **consciously faking the expected emotion through verbal/nonverbal behaviour — stressful and hard to sustain."},{"t":"li","x":"**Deep acting: **regulating one’s actual felt emotions (changing/modifying the situation, suppressing or amplifying emotion, shifting attention, or reframing the situation) so the displayed emotion becomes more genuine."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Emotional Intelligence (EI) Model"},{"t":"table","rows":[["","Self","Others"],["Recognition of emotions","Awareness of own emotions","Awareness of others’ emotions"],["Regulation of emotions","Management of own emotions","Management of others’ emotions"]]},{"t":"p","x":"EI outcomes: better teamwork, better handling of emotional labor, more effective leadership, better decisions involving others, and a more positive mindset for creativity. EI can be developed through training, coaching, practice and feedback, and tends to increase with age."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Workplace Attitudes: Job Satisfaction and Commitment"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"EVLN: Responses to Job Dissatisfaction"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Response","Meaning"],["Exit","Leaving the situation — quitting, transferring, being absent."],["Voice","Trying to change the situation — problem-solving, complaining constructively."],["Loyalty","Patiently waiting for the situation to improve."],["Neglect","Reducing effort/quality, increasing absenteeism."]]},{"t":"li","x":"**Job satisfaction and performance: **the relationship is only moderately positive, because general attitudes are poor predictors of specific behaviours, employees often have limited control over their own performance, and causation can run the other way — good performance (if rewarded) increases satisfaction, rather than the reverse."},{"t":"li","x":"**Service profit chain model: **explains why job satisfaction raises customer satisfaction and profit — via (1) employee emotions transferring to customer emotions, and (2) more satisfied, experienced employees delivering better service."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Organisational Commitment"},{"t":"li","x":"**Affective commitment: **emotional attachment to and identification with the organisation — linked to lower turnover and higher motivation/OCB. Built through organisational justice and support, shared values, trust, organisational comprehension (understanding how the firm works), and employee involvement/psychological ownership."},{"t":"li","x":"**Continuance commitment: **a calculative attachment — staying because leaving would be costly or because there are no good alternatives; associated with lower turnover but also lower performance, OCB and cooperation."},{"t":"li","x":"**Normative commitment: **a felt obligation/moral duty to stay, based on the norm of reciprocity."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Workplace Stress"},{"t":"li","x":"**Stress: **an adaptive response to a situation perceived as challenging or threatening to our well-being; it prepares us to adapt to a hostile environment. Stress can be \"eustress\" (a positive, motivating challenge) or \"distress\" (negative strain)."},{"t":"li","x":"**General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS): **the body’s stress response unfolds in three stages — alarm reaction, resistance, and (if the stressor persists) exhaustion."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Four Main Workplace Stressors"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Stressor","Description"],["Organizational constraints","Lack of resources needed to do the job — indirectly threatens rewards and status (\"situation\" in the MARS model)."],["Interpersonal conflict","Others’ behaviour interferes with our goals or feels threatening; includes workplace harassment such as bullying and sexual harassment."],["Work overload","More hours and/or more intensive work than can reasonably be sustained."],["Low task control","Lack of control over how and when tasks are performed; more stressful as personal responsibility for outcomes increases."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Individual Differences in Stress"},{"t":"p","x":"People experience less stress, or fewer negative stress outcomes, when they have better physical health/lifestyle, appropriate coping strategies, a personality profile of lower neuroticism and higher extraversion, and a positive self-concept."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Managing Work-Related Stress — Five Strategies"},{"t":"li","x":"**1. Remove the stressor: **e.g., person-job matching, reducing noise, a complaint system, giving employees more control over their work, work-life integration practices."},{"t":"li","x":"**2. Withdraw from the stressor: **permanent (e.g., transfer) or temporary (e.g., vacation) solutions."},{"t":"li","x":"**3. Change stress perceptions: **e.g., strengthening a positive self-concept, personal goal setting, using humour."},{"t":"li","x":"**4. Control stress consequences: **e.g., exercise, meditation, wellness programmes, Employee Assistance Programmes (EAPs)."},{"t":"li","x":"**5. Receive social support: **emotional and informational support from others (supervisors, coworkers, family)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Gap note: **This chapter connects directly to the Individual Case assignment (Janet Wong, stress management) summarized in the Course Overview — the four stressors and five stress-management strategies above are the core theory to apply in that report."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Further points from the slides"},{"t":"p","x":"Opening case: Hilton became the number one place to work in America through its \"Heart of House\" and other initiatives focused on improving employee emotions and attitudes."},{"t":"p","x":"Emotions defined: psychological, behavioral and physiological episodes that create a state of readiness. They are brief episodes, subtle and nonconscious, experienced, and they motivate. The circumplex model of emotions (Exhibit 4.1) maps core affect (Russell)."},{"t":"p","x":"Attitude-behavior contingencies: (1) beliefs-feelings: two people hold the same belief but attach different valences to it (challenging work is positive for some, negative for others); (2) feelings-behavioral intentions: the same feelings lead to different intentions because of past experience or personality (two employees dislike long hours, one complains, the other does not because complaining did not work before); (3) behavioral intentions-behavior: the same intention, but situation or skills let only one act (an employee wants to quit but cannot find a suitable job)."},{"t":"p","x":"How emotions influence attitudes and behavior: emotional markers attach to incoming sensory information; emotional experiences occur when information is first received and later when we think about it; feelings and beliefs are shaped by cumulative emotional episodes; we \"listen in\" on our emotions; emotions also affect behavior directly."},{"t":"p","x":"Emotion display norms differ across cultures: some strongly discourage emotional expression, others encourage open display of true emotions."},{"t":"p","x":"Service profit chain (Exhibit 4.5): job satisfaction raises customer satisfaction and profits because employee emotions affect customer emotions and experienced employees provide better service."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: An employee genuinely believes long working hours are \"part of ambition and drive,\" but after months of 70-hour weeks he privately starts to resent the job and feels physically exhausted, yet keeps telling colleagues \"I love the hustle.\" This best illustrates:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) The self-fulfilling prophecy"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Cognitive dissonance"},{"t":"p","x":"c) The halo effect"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Distributive justice"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: A flight attendant who does not personally feel cheerful but consciously forces a smile and an upbeat tone with every passenger, without changing how she actually feels inside, is engaging in:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Deep acting"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Surface acting"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Emotional intelligence regulation of others"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Affective commitment"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Using the EVLN model, describe what \"neglect\" looks like in practice, and explain why it is often harder for a manager to detect early than \"voice\" or \"exit.\""},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Explain why job satisfaction and job performance are only moderately correlated, giving two of the three reasons discussed in class."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A customer-service team member has recently been given a heavier caseload (work overload), has very little say in how she schedules her calls (low task control), and has started missing minor errors and calling in sick more often. Using stress theory, identify which two of the four main workplace stressors are most clearly present, and recommend one management action from each of two different stress-management strategy categories to help her."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Cognitive dissonance — there is a perceived inconsistency between his behaviour/feelings (resentment, exhaustion) and his stated belief/public identity (\"I love the hustle\"); rather than changing the behaviour (reducing hours), he is likely to keep reinforcing the consonant belief to reduce the emotional discomfort.\n**2. **b) Surface acting — she is faking the expected emotional display (smiling, upbeat tone) without regulating or changing her actual inner feelings, which is the definition of surface acting (and the more stressful of the two acting strategies).\n**3. **Neglect means quietly reducing work effort or quality and/or increasing absenteeism, rather than actively complaining (voice) or leaving (exit). It is harder to detect early because it is passive and low-visibility — there is no single triggering event or conversation for a manager to notice, and small drops in effort or slightly more sick days can easily be attributed to other causes (workload, personal life) rather than recognized as a dissatisfaction signal, unlike a formal complaint (voice) or a resignation (exit), which are unmistakable events.\n**4. **Any two of: (1) General attitudes (like overall job satisfaction) are poor predictors of specific behaviours (like a particular performance outcome) — attitudes and behaviours are best matched when both are measured at a similarly specific/general level. (2) Employees often have limited personal control over their performance (e.g., depends on equipment, teammates, market conditions), which weakens the link from how they feel to what they can actually produce. (3) The causal arrow may run in reverse — good performance, when it is recognized and rewarded, increases satisfaction — but if performance is not rewarded, this reverse loop does not strengthen the correlation.\n**5. **Stressors present: Work overload (heavier caseload) and Low task control (little say in scheduling calls). Recommended actions — one per category: (1) Remove the stressor: redistribute part of her caseload or give her more autonomy over how she sequences/schedules her calls (directly reduces overload and increases task control). (2) Control stress consequences / receive social support: offer access to the company’s wellness programme or EAP, and ensure her supervisor checks in supportively and offers informational support (e.g., coaching on prioritization) rather than simply monitoring the missed errors and sick days."}]},"5":{"title":"Motivation","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 5   |   Scheduled: 5 Oct 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Motivation and Engagement Defined"},{"t":"li","x":"**Employee motivation: **the forces within a person that affect the direction, intensity and persistence of effort for voluntary behaviour (same \"M\" as in the MARS model)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Employee engagement: **an employee’s emotional and cognitive motivation — a focused, intense, persistent and purposive effort toward work-related goals."},{"t":"li","x":"**Why motivation is harder today: **a multigenerational, multicultural workforce; rapid workplace change; and less motivation achievable simply through direct supervision."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Drives, Needs and Four-Drive Theory"},{"t":"li","x":"**Drives: **hardwired, innate, universal brain activity that corrects deficiencies and produces emotions that energize action (\"prime movers\" of behaviour)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Needs: **goal-directed forces we experience, channeling our emotions toward specific goals shaped by self-concept, social norms and experience."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Four-Drive Theory (Lawrence & Nohria)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Drive","Meaning"],["Drive to acquire","Seek, acquire, control and retain objects or experiences."],["Drive to bond","Form social relationships and mutual caring commitments with others."],["Drive to comprehend","Satisfy curiosity; know and understand ourselves and our environment."],["Drive to defend","Protect ourselves physically and socially."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Mechanism: drives determine which emotions get attached to incoming information; emotions become conscious when strong enough or when drives conflict; our mental skill set then uses social norms, personal values and experience to convert these drive-based emotions into goal-directed choices and effort."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Content Theories of Motivation"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Maslow’s Needs Hierarchy Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"Five (of seven) needs are arranged in a hierarchy (physiological, safety, belongingness/love, esteem, self-actualization); the lowest unmet need dominates until satisfied, then the next-higher need becomes the top motivator. The model lacks strong empirical support — its main problem is that people do not actually have a universal, identical needs hierarchy."},{"t":"li","x":"**Maslow’s contributions despite the weak evidence: **a holistic perspective (study needs together, not in isolation), a humanistic perspective (social dynamics, not just instinct, shape motivation), and a positive perspective (emphasis on self-actualization/growth, foundational to \"positive OB\")."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Intrinsic vs. Extrinsic Motivation"},{"t":"li","x":"**Intrinsic motivation: **seeking fulfilment from the activity itself, not as a means to another outcome — anchored in innate drives for competence and autonomy."},{"t":"li","x":"**Extrinsic motivation: **being motivated by an instrumental outcome (pay, recognition) that indirectly fulfils needs. Extrinsic rewards can sometimes undermine intrinsic motivation, but the research shows this effect is minimal and often absent."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Learned Needs Theory (McClelland)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Need for achievement (nAch): **wants to accomplish challenging goals, values clear feedback, prefers moderate-risk tasks."},{"t":"li","x":"**Need for affiliation (nAff): **seeks approval from others, tends to conform, avoids conflict."},{"t":"li","x":"**Need for power (nPow): **seeks power/influence for social or personal purposes."},{"t":"p","x":"These needs are \"learned\" — they can be strengthened or weakened through self-concept, social norms and experience, and training can shift a person’s need strength through reinforcement."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Process Theories of Motivation"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Expectancy Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"Motivation = a function of three beliefs, multiplied together:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Link","Belief","How to increase it"],["Effort → Performance (E-to-P)","My effort will lead to the required performance level","Train/select for fit; provide time, resources, coaching and behavioural modeling to build self-efficacy"],["Performance → Outcome (P-to-O)","My performance will lead to a specific outcome/reward","Measure performance accurately; clearly explain the reward link; show real examples of others being rewarded for performance"],["Outcome valence","That outcome/reward is something I actually value","Ensure rewards are genuinely valued; individualize rewards; minimize \"countervalent\" (negatively valued) side-effects"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Because the three components multiply, a \"zero\" on any one of them (e.g., a reward the employee does not value at all) drives overall motivation toward zero, regardless of how strong the other two beliefs are."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Organizational Behaviour Modification (OB Mod) — the A-B-Cs"},{"t":"p","x":"Antecedent (a cue that triggers behaviour) → Behavior → Consequence (what follows the behaviour and shapes whether it recurs)."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Consequence type","What happens","Effect on behaviour"],["Positive reinforcement","A desired consequence is introduced after the behaviour","Behaviour increases / is maintained"],["Negative reinforcement","An undesired consequence is removed after the behaviour","Behaviour increases"],["Punishment","An undesired consequence is introduced after the behaviour","Behaviour decreases"],["Extinction","A previously reinforcing consequence is withheld","Behaviour decreases (fades out)"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Social Cognitive Theory"},{"t":"li","x":"Learning from consequences — observing what happens to others and anticipating similar consequences for ourselves in other situations."},{"t":"li","x":"Behaviour modeling — learning by observing and imitating others’ behaviour."},{"t":"li","x":"Self-regulation — purposive action: setting our own goals/standards, anticipating consequences, and self-reinforcing our own behaviour."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Goal Setting, Feedback and Coaching"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Effective Goal-Setting Features"},{"t":"p","x":"Specific, Measurable, Achievable (yet challenging), Relevant (within the employee’s control), Time-framed, Exciting (builds real commitment, not just compliance), Reviewed (feedback/recognition on progress)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Characteristics of Effective Feedback"},{"t":"p","x":"Specific (identifiable behaviours/outcomes), Relevant (within the employee’s control), Timely, Credible (from a knowledgeable, unbiased source), and Sufficiently Frequent (more for learners; otherwise matched to the task cycle)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Strengths-Based Coaching"},{"t":"p","x":"Maximizes potential by focusing on strengths, not weaknesses: the employee identifies an area of strength, the coach helps them discover how to leverage it, and they discuss situational barriers and solutions together — motivating because people prefer feedback on their strengths, and core traits/interests tend to stabilize in adulthood."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Organizational Justice and Equity Theory"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Three Types of Organizational Justice"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Focus"],["Distributive justice","Fairness of the outcomes themselves (based on equality, equity, or need)."],["Procedural justice","Fairness of the decision-making process used to reach the outcome."],["Interactional justice","Fairness/respect in how people are treated during the process."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Equity Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"People compare their own outcome-to-input ratio with a comparison other’s ratio. Perceived inequity (under- or over-reward) creates tension that motivates people to restore balance by: reducing their own inputs, increasing their own outcomes, increasing the other’s inputs, reducing the other’s outcomes, changing their perceptions (cognitively re-evaluating), changing their comparison other, or leaving the field (e.g., quitting, transferring)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Procedural Justice Rules (selected)**"},{"t":"li","x":"Decision makers have no self-interest and set aside restrictive doctrines."},{"t":"li","x":"Decisions use complete, accurate information and consider all affected groups."},{"t":"li","x":"Criteria/procedures are applied consistently and ethically."},{"t":"li","x":"Employees can voice evidence/opinions, and questionable decisions can be appealed."},{"t":"p","x":"**Interactional Justice Rules**"},{"t":"li","x":"Employees are treated politely and with respect."},{"t":"li","x":"Employees receive thorough, well-justified explanations."},{"t":"li","x":"Employees receive honest, candid and timely information."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Further points from the slides"},{"t":"p","x":"Opening case: many companies are improving motivation by turning old performance appraisal systems into coaching and developmental conversations with more responsive goal setting and feedback. KPMG applied OB Mod principles through gamification: an app to improve employee knowledge of the firm's global capabilities."},{"t":"p","x":"Sources of feedback: nonsocial sources are not conveyed directly by people (for example electronic displays); social sources come directly from others, including multisource feedback from the full circle of people around the employee. Preferred use: nonsocial feedback for goal progress, social sources for conveying positive feedback."},{"t":"p","x":"Class case: BUS2202 term 23-24 Part A, Q1 (case discussion)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: An employee believes that no matter how hard she works, her performance will never actually be measured accurately or rewarded by her manager. According to expectancy theory, which link is broken?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Effort-to-performance (E-to-P) expectancy"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Performance-to-outcome (P-to-O) expectancy"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Outcome valence"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Distributive justice"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: A manager removes an annoying, repetitive email alert every time an employee submits her report on time, which increases the employee’s tendency to submit reports on time going forward. This is an example of:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Positive reinforcement"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Punishment"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Negative reinforcement"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Extinction"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Using equity theory, list three different ways an employee who feels under-rewarded relative to a coworker might try to restore equity, other than quitting."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Explain why Maslow’s needs hierarchy is considered to lack strong empirical support, and name one lasting contribution it still made to the study of motivation."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A junior analyst has strong technical skills and clearly wants to do well (high E-to-P belief — he is confident his effort will produce good work), and he knows that strong performance reviews lead to bonuses at his firm (strong P-to-O belief). However, he tells his mentor \"I honestly don’t care about the bonus — what I want is more interesting, varied project work, and nobody’s offering me that.\" Using expectancy theory, diagnose the motivational gap and recommend a fix consistent with the theory."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Performance-to-outcome (P-to-O) expectancy — her belief is specifically that good performance will not be measured or linked to a reward, which is a breakdown in the performance→outcome link, not in whether her effort produces performance (E-to-P) or whether she values the reward (valence).\n**2. **c) Negative reinforcement — an aversive stimulus (the annoying alert) is removed contingent on the desired behaviour (on-time submission), which increases that behaviour. This differs from positive reinforcement, where something desirable would be added, not an unpleasant stimulus taken away.\n**3. **Any three of: reduce her own inputs (e.g., put in less effort/time); increase her own outcomes (e.g., ask for a raise, take more perks); try to increase the coworker’s inputs (e.g., expect them to take on more work); try to reduce the coworker’s outcomes; cognitively change her perception of the situation (re-evaluate what \"fair\" means here, downplay the comparison); or change her comparison other (compare herself to a different, less favourably-treated colleague instead).\n**4. **Maslow’s theory lacks strong empirical support mainly because it assumes a single, universal hierarchy of needs and a strict \"one need dominates until satisfied, then the next takes over\" progression — but research shows people differ substantially in their personal needs hierarchies, so the model does not hold up as a precise, testable framework. Lasting contributions (any one): its holistic perspective (studying multiple needs together rather than in isolation), its humanistic perspective (recognizing social dynamics, not just instinct, shape motivation), or its positive perspective (highlighting self-actualization/growth needs, which helped found \"positive OB\").\n**5. **The motivational gap is in outcome valence: his E-to-P and P-to-O beliefs are both strong, but the specific outcome on offer (a bonus) has low personal valence for him — he simply does not value it much, so overall motivation toward \"chasing the bonus\" stays low even though the other two expectancy components are intact (recall the three components multiply, so a low-valence outcome caps the overall effect). Fix, consistent with expectancy theory’s guidance to \"individualize rewards\" and \"ensure rewards are valued\": his manager should offer an outcome he genuinely values instead — e.g., link strong performance reviews to a say in choosing more varied/interesting project assignments, rather than (or in addition to) a cash bonus."}]},"6":{"title":"Team Dynamics","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 8   |   Scheduled: 12 Oct 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. What Is a Team?"},{"t":"p","x":"Five defining features: (1) two or more people; (2) exist to fulfil a purpose; (3) interdependence and need for collaboration; (4) mutual accountability — members influence each other; (5) members perceive themselves to be a team."},{"t":"li","x":"**Types of teams vary by: **permanence (how long the team exists), skill diversity (variety of member skills/knowledge), and authority dispersion (how decision-making is distributed)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Informal Groups"},{"t":"p","x":"Groups that exist primarily for the benefit of their members, driven by the innate drive to bond, social identity, goal accomplishment, and emotional support — informal groups can also benefit the organisation."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Advantages and Challenges of Teams"},{"t":"li","x":"**Advantages: **better decisions/products, better information sharing and coordination, higher motivation (fulfils the drive to bond, creates felt obligation to the group), mutual accountability (members monitor each other), and coworkers act as moving performance benchmarks."},{"t":"li","x":"**Challenges: **process losses, and social loafing (individuals exert less effort in a group than they would alone)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Social Loafing — Causes and Remedies**"},{"t":"li","x":"**More likely when: **individual performance is hidden/indistinguishable, the work itself is low in intrinsic motivation, due to certain individual characteristics, or when team dynamics undermine motivation."},{"t":"li","x":"**Minimizing it: **form smaller teams, specialize tasks, measure individual performance, increase job enrichment, and raise awareness of social loafing and team obligations."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. The Team Effectiveness Model"},{"t":"p","x":"Team effectiveness is shaped by the organisational/team environment, team design, and team processes."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Organisation and Team Environment"},{"t":"p","x":"Reward systems, organisational structure, communication systems, organisational leadership, and physical space (e.g., shared \"obeya\" collaboration rooms) all shape how well a team can function."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Team Design: Task, Size and Composition"},{"t":"li","x":"**Best task characteristics for teams: **complex tasks that can be divided into specialized roles, well-structured tasks (low variability, high analyzability), and high task interdependence."},{"t":"p","x":"**Levels of Task Interdependence**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Description","Example"],["Pooled interdependence","Each member’s output is combined, with minimal direct interaction between members.","Several people sharing one photocopier"],["Sequential interdependence","One member’s output becomes the next member’s input, in one direction.","An assembly line"],["Reciprocal interdependence","Work flows back and forth between members in both directions.","A medical team treating a patient"]]},{"t":"li","x":"**Team size: **smaller teams generally perform better (less process loss, more individual engagement, faster development) — but the team must still be large enough to complete the task."},{"t":"li","x":"**Team composition: **members must engage in both task work (behaviours that complete the assigned task) and teamwork (the \"5 Cs\" behaviours that support the team as a unit — e.g., coordinating, communicating, cooperating)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Diversity in Team Composition**"},{"t":"li","x":"**Advantages: **multiple perspectives on problems/alternatives, a broader knowledge base, and better representation of constituents."},{"t":"li","x":"**Disadvantages: **slower team development, and greater susceptibility to \"faultlines\" (subgroup splits along visible demographic or value lines)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Team Processes"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Stages of Team Development"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Stage","What happens"],["Forming","Members learn about each other and evaluate their membership in the team."],["Storming","Conflict emerges; members act proactively and compete for roles/influence."],["Norming","Roles become established; the team builds consensus around objectives and a shared mental model."],["Performing","Efficient coordination, high cooperation and trust, strong commitment to team goals, identification with the team."],["Adjourning","The team disbands; focus shifts from task to relationships (wrapping up, closure)."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Two central underlying processes across these stages: developing team identification, and developing shared team mental models and coordinating routines."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Team Roles and Team Building"},{"t":"li","x":"**Team roles: **expected, purposive behaviour patterns with perceived value to the team/organisation, attached to one or more members, acquired formally or informally; divided into task-work roles and team-work roles."},{"t":"li","x":"**Team building activities: **goal setting, problem-solving, role clarification, and interpersonal relations work — effective only under specific supportive conditions."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Team Norms"},{"t":"p","x":"Informal rules and shared expectations that regulate behaviour, developing through initial team experiences, critical events in the team’s history, and the values/experience members bring. Preventing or changing dysfunctional norms: state desired norms explicitly when forming the team, select members with compatible values, openly discuss counter-productive norms, use team-based rewards to counter bad norms, or (as a last resort) disband a team with entrenched dysfunctional norms."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Team Cohesion"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **the degree of attraction members feel toward the team and their motivation to remain in it."},{"t":"li","x":"**Stronger/faster with: **higher member similarity, smaller team size, regular/frequent interaction, somewhat difficult entry, higher team success, and more external competition/challenge."},{"t":"li","x":"**Cohesion boosts performance when: **task interdependence is high and team norms align with organisational objectives — but high cohesion combined with norms that are NOT aligned with organisational goals can actually hurt performance (e.g., a very close-knit team can collectively agree to cut corners)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Trust in Teams"},{"t":"p","x":"Three levels, from lowest to highest: calculus-based trust (based on consistent rewards/punishments), knowledge-based trust (based on predictability from experience with the person), and identification-based trust (based on mutual understanding/shared identity). \"Swift trust\" is an initial moderate-to-high trust extended to new teammates just because they joined the team."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Self-Directed and Remote Teams"},{"t":"li","x":"**Self-directed teams: **cross-functional groups organised around whole work processes, with autonomy over task decisions. Success factors: responsibility for an entire work process, high interdependence within the team, low interdependence with other teams, autonomy to organise/coordinate work, and a work site/technology that supports team communication and job enrichment."},{"t":"li","x":"**Remote (virtual/distributed) teams: **vary by geographic dispersion and how much members work apart. Success factors: strong use of the \"5 Cs\" teamwork behaviours, freedom to choose from a toolkit of communication channels, fairly high task structure, and some opportunities to meet face-to-face."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Team Decision Making"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Four Constraints on Team Decisions"},{"t":"li","x":"Time constraints — production blocking (only one person can effectively speak/contribute at a time) and coordination difficulty."},{"t":"li","x":"Evaluation apprehension — reluctance to voice unusual ideas due to self-presentation concerns."},{"t":"li","x":"Peer pressure to conform — suppresses dissenting opinions."},{"t":"li","x":"Overconfidence (inflated team efficacy) — caused by self-enhancement, cohesion, external threats, and mutually reinforcing beliefs."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Guidelines for Better Team Decisions"},{"t":"li","x":"Build in checks and balances so no single member dominates."},{"t":"li","x":"Maintain an optimal (not oversized) team size."},{"t":"li","x":"Encourage confidence, but stay alert to overconfidence."},{"t":"li","x":"Foster norms that encourage critical thinking."},{"t":"li","x":"Develop and maintain psychological safety."},{"t":"li","x":"Use team structures that specifically encourage creativity."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Creative Team Structures"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Structure","Description"],["Brainstorming","Rules: speak freely, no criticism, generate as many ideas as possible, build on others’ ideas. Works best with skilled facilitators, confident members and psychological safety; success = idea quality, not quantity. Limited by production blocking and conformity/fixation effects."],["Brainwriting","Brainstorming done individually/in writing rather than through conversation — reduces production blocking."],["Electronic brainstorming","Brainwriting supported by technology — reduces production blocking, evaluation apprehension and conformity even further."],["Nominal group technique","Brainwriting combined with a subsequent verbal discussion stage."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Cases from the slides"},{"t":"p","x":"The U.S. Army instills in every soldier and staff member the belief that success occurs through teamwork. The Rijksmuseum reorganized its public display areas around historical periods and formed diverse teams of staff from the specialized collections for each era. Automattic is a completely distributed organization with remote teams, but ensures they meet physically at least twice a year, including the all-hands Grand Meetup."},{"t":"p","x":"Past exam question (T1 23-24, Part B): Thomas launches an online shoe-reselling business with three friends; as it grows they face task-management problems, interpersonal conflicts, competition for team roles and relational influence. Apply the stages of team development and team concepts to the situation."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: A hospital surgical team, where information and adjustments flow back and forth constantly between the surgeon, anaesthetist and nurses during an operation, best illustrates which type of task interdependence?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Pooled interdependence"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Sequential interdependence"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Reciprocal interdependence"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Independent tasks"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: A close-knit team has very high cohesion, but its shared informal norm is \"we don’t report each other’s mistakes to management.\" What is the most accurate conclusion?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) High cohesion always improves performance regardless of norms"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Cohesion improves performance only when team norms align with organisational goals — here they do not, so performance/quality may suffer"},{"t":"p","x":"c) This is an example of social loafing"},{"t":"p","x":"d) This is an example of swift trust"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Name and briefly describe the five stages of team development, in order."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Explain two of the four constraints on effective team decision-making, and for each, name one structural remedy (e.g., a creative-team structure) that helps address it."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A newly formed venture team (four co-founder friends) has just moved from a period of open disagreement and jockeying for roles into a phase where responsibilities are becoming clearer and the group is building a shared understanding of how to work together. (a) Which two stages of team development does this describe (the one just completed and the one now beginning)? (b) Suggest one concrete team-building action appropriate for consolidating the new stage."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **c) Reciprocal interdependence — work and information flow back and forth in both directions between team members in real time, unlike the one-directional flow of sequential interdependence (assembly line) or the minimal-interaction pooled interdependence (shared photocopier).\n**2. **b) Cohesion improves performance specifically when task interdependence is high and team norms are consistent with organisational objectives; here the norm actively undermines an organisational goal (accountability/quality), so despite high cohesion, performance or compliance outcomes are likely to suffer rather than improve.\n**3. **Forming (members learn about each other, evaluate membership) → Storming (conflict emerges, members compete for roles) → Norming (roles settle, consensus builds around objectives and a shared mental model) → Performing (efficient, cooperative, high-trust coordination, strong commitment) → Adjourning (the team disbands, focus shifts from task to relationships).\n**4. **Any two, e.g.: (1) Time constraints/production blocking — remedy: brainwriting or electronic brainstorming, which let members contribute simultaneously in writing instead of waiting to speak. (2) Evaluation apprehension — remedy: electronic brainstorming (anonymity reduces fear of judgement) or strong brainstorming ground rules (\"no criticism\") enforced by a skilled facilitator. (3) Peer pressure to conform — remedy: nominal group technique, which separates individual idea generation (in writing, before group discussion) from the group conformity pressure of live debate. (4) Overconfidence/inflated team efficacy — remedy: building in checks and balances/critical-thinking norms so no single narrative dominates unchecked.\n**5. **(a) The team has just completed Storming (open disagreement, competing for roles) and is now entering Norming (roles clarifying, building a shared team mental model and consensus on objectives). (b) A concrete team-building action: a facilitated role-clarification session where each founder explicitly states their intended responsibilities and decision rights, and the group agrees and documents them together — this is exactly the \"role clarification\" type of team building that helps consolidate the Norming stage (this maps onto the actual exam-style question on this same case in the Chapter 8 slides)."}]},"7":{"title":"Leadership in Organisational Settings","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 12   |   Scheduled: 26 Oct 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Leadership Defined"},{"t":"li","x":"**Leadership: **the ability to influence, motivate and enable others to contribute toward the effectiveness of the organisations of which they are members. Leaders influence and motivate others, and act as enablers."},{"t":"li","x":"**Shared leadership: **the view that leadership is a role, not a position held by one person — employees lead each other (e.g., championing new ideas). It flourishes where formal leaders are willing to delegate power, the culture is collaborative (not competitive), and employees have developed effective influence skills."},{"t":"p","x":"Four main perspectives of leadership covered in this chapter: transformational, managerial, implicit leadership, and leader attributes."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Transformational Leadership"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Elements of Transformational Leadership"},{"t":"li","x":"**1. Develop and communicate a strategic vision: **an aspirational, higher-purpose future state — linked to personal values, challenging, abstract, and unifying. Communicated with symbols, metaphors, framing, and delivered with humility, sincerity and passion."},{"t":"li","x":"**2. Model the vision: **the leader \"walks the talk\" — their own behaviour symbolizes and demonstrates the vision, which both legitimizes the vision and builds employee trust in the leader."},{"t":"li","x":"**3. Encourage experimentation: **encourage employees to question current practices and support a learning orientation."},{"t":"li","x":"**4. Build commitment to the vision: **built cumulatively through communicating, modeling and encouraging experimentation, and reinforced through rewards, recognition and celebration."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Charisma vs. Transformational Leadership"},{"t":"li","x":"**Charisma: **a personal trait that produces referent power and tends to create dependent followers."},{"t":"li","x":"**Transformational leadership: **a set of behaviours that engage followers through persuasion and trust, empowering (rather than creating dependent) followers. There is a risk that leaders become \"intoxicated\" by their own charisma."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Evaluating Transformational Leadership"},{"t":"li","x":"**Strengths: **linked to higher satisfaction, commitment, performance, OCBs, better decisions and more creativity."},{"t":"li","x":"**Limitations: **circular logic (it is sometimes defined by its own effects on employees), mixed models that blur leader traits with leader behaviours (confusing cause and effect), and uncertainty about whether it is a truly universal theory across cultures (some specific elements may fit some cultures better than others)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Managerial Leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"Daily activities that support and guide employees’ performance/well-being to sustain current objectives and practices — assumes a relatively stable environment and is micro-focused, whereas transformational leadership assumes a dynamic environment and is macro-focused. Managerial leadership is \"doing things right\"; transformational leadership is \"doing the right things.\" The two are interdependent, not substitutes for each other."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Task-Oriented vs. People-Oriented Behaviours"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Task-oriented behaviours","People-oriented behaviours"],["Assign work, clarify responsibilities, set goals/deadlines, give feedback, establish procedures, plan future work.","Show concern for employee needs, make the workplace pleasant, recognize contributions, listen to employees."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Both styles are necessary, but they have different effects on outcomes; most effective leaders use both, situationally."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Servant Leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"Leadership focused on serving followers’ need fulfilment, personal development and growth — selfless, egalitarian, humble, nurturing, empathetic, and ethical. Characteristics: a natural calling to serve others; a humble, egalitarian, accepting relationship style; and ethical decisions/actions. (Note: it is essentially an extension/variation of people-oriented leadership.)"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Contingency Perspectives: Path-Goal Theory and Substitutes"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Path-Goal Leadership Model"},{"t":"p","x":"A contingency model: leaders vary their style to clarify performance-to-outcome (P-to-O) expectancies, influence outcome valences, and facilitate goal achievement. Four styles:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Style","What it looks like","Best when..."],["Directive","Clarifies goals, methods, and performance standards (= task-oriented).","Employees are inexperienced/unskilled; task is nonroutine/ambiguous; employees have an external locus of control; team norms are dysfunctional."],["Supportive","Provides psychological support (= people-oriented).","Employees are low-skilled (combined with directive); team cohesion is low; employees have an external locus of control."],["Participative","Involves employees in decisions.","Employees have an internal locus of control; task is nonroutine."],["Achievement-oriented","Sets challenging goals and shows confidence employees can reach them.","Employees have an internal locus of control and are skilled/experienced."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Leadership Substitutes Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"Contingencies that limit a leader’s influence or make a particular style unnecessary — e.g., strong training/experience can substitute for task-oriented leadership; a highly cohesive team can substitute for supportive leadership. Evidence: substitutes help, but they do not completely replace the need for real leadership."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Implicit Leadership Perspective"},{"t":"li","x":"**Leadership prototypes: **our preconceived beliefs about the features/behaviours of an effective leader; we evaluate leaders more favourably when they fit our prototype."},{"t":"li","x":"**Romance of leadership effect: **the tendency to distort/inflate a leader’s perceived effect on organisational success — driven by our need to simplify complex explanations and our need for a sense of situational control."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Personal Attributes of Effective Leaders and Authentic Leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"Eight commonly identified attributes: personality (especially extraversion and conscientiousness, though other Big Five factors also matter), self-concept (complex, internally consistent, clear self-view as a leader, positive self-evaluation), leadership motivation (strong need for socialized power), drive (moderately high need for achievement, inquisitiveness, boldness), integrity (truthfulness, consistent words/actions, applying one’s values), knowledge of the business, cognitive and practical intelligence, and emotional intelligence."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Authentic Leadership"},{"t":"li","x":"**Know yourself: **self-reflection, seeking feedback from trusted sources, understanding your inner purpose."},{"t":"li","x":"**Be yourself: **developing your own style, self-discipline anchored in personal values, maintaining a strong, positive self-evaluation."},{"t":"p","x":"**Limitations of the Leader-Attributes Perspective**"},{"t":"li","x":"Treats leadership as a universal formula, when effective combinations of attributes can differ."},{"t":"li","x":"Different combinations of attributes may work equally well."},{"t":"li","x":"Views leadership as residing purely \"within the person,\" when it is arguably relational."},{"t":"li","x":"The attributes-effectiveness link is muddied by implicit leadership (prototype fit)."},{"t":"li","x":"Attributes indicate leadership potential only, not guaranteed performance."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7. Cultural and Gender Issues in Leadership"},{"t":"li","x":"**Culture: **societal values/practices shape a leader’s own values and decisions, and shape followers’ prototypes of what an effective leader looks like. Some styles appear near-universal (e.g., \"charismatic visionary\"); others vary — e.g., participative leadership is seen as more characteristic of effective leadership in low power-distance cultures than in high power-distance ones."},{"t":"li","x":"**Gender: **male and female leaders show broadly similar levels of task- and people-oriented leadership; women tend to use more participative leadership. Gender stereotypes and leader prototypes distort followers’ evaluations — women are evaluated more negatively when applying directive/autocratic styles, yet rated more favourably than men on emerging leadership qualities such as coaching and teamwork."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Dark Side of Leadership — Cross-Reference to the Dark Triad (Chapter 2)"},{"t":"p","x":"This lecture also recapped the Dark Triad of personality (Machiavellianism, narcissism, psychopathy — see Lesson 2 for full definitions) because these traits help explain destructive or toxic leadership: a leader high in narcissism may pursue a compelling \"vision\" mainly for self-glorification rather than genuine follower benefit (undermining authentic transformational leadership); Machiavellian or psychopathic tendencies in a leader are linked to more organisational politics, unethical influence tactics, and poorer team outcomes, even when such leaders can appear charismatic in the short term."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8. Case Study: \"The Real Leadership Lessons of Steve Jobs\" (Isaacson, HBR 2012)"},{"t":"p","x":"Summary: Isaacson argues that the real lessons from Steve Jobs come from what he actually built (Apple, Pixar, and the transformation of computing, music, phones, tablets, retail and publishing), not merely from his famously abrasive personality. He distills Jobs’s approach into repeated behaviours: an obsessive focus (cutting product lines to a handful of priorities); relentless simplification (of design and user interfaces); taking end-to-end responsibility for the whole user experience; leapfrogging competitors instead of just catching up; prioritizing great products over short-term profit; trusting instinct/intuition over focus groups; a \"reality distortion field\" that pushed teams to achieve what they thought was impossible; deliberately shaping first impressions (\"impute\"); relentless perfectionism (delaying launches until a design felt right); demanding only \"A player\" talent; insisting on face-to-face collaboration over slides and email; holding both big-picture vision and granular detail simultaneously; fusing the humanities with technology; and staying \"hungry and foolish\" (a nonconformist, restless drive to keep disrupting)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Linking the Case to Leadership Theory (2-3 concepts)"},{"t":"li","x":"**1. Transformational leadership: **Jobs repeatedly built and communicated an aspirational, higher-purpose vision (e.g., \"insanely great\" products, \"put a dent in the universe\"), modeled that vision through his own obsessive standards (\"walking the talk\" on quality/simplicity), and pushed relentless experimentation (delaying the iPhone and iPad launches to get the design right) — a textbook illustration of the \"develop/communicate vision, model the vision, encourage experimentation\" elements, though the case itself frames the question \"Is Steve Jobs charismatic or transformational?\" as genuinely debatable."},{"t":"li","x":"**2. Charisma vs. transformational leadership (and its risks): **Jobs’s \"reality distortion field\" is a clear example of referent power/charisma producing highly dependent, intensely loyal followers who would push past what they believed possible — illustrating both the potential upside of charisma (extraordinary achievement, e.g., Gorilla Glass in under six months) and the theoretical risk flagged in the slides that leaders can become \"intoxicated\" by their own charisma, and that charismatic influence can shade into control rather than empowerment."},{"t":"li","x":"**3. Personal attributes of effective leaders (drive, self-concept, knowledge of the business): **Jobs displays an extreme version of \"drive\" (boldness, action-orientation, relentless achievement need), a very strong, internally consistent self-concept/self-view as a leader-innovator (\"that’s not who I am\"), and deep knowledge of the business/product across both the big picture and fine details — while also showing the limitation that attributes indicate potential, not guaranteed universal effectiveness: his \"tough on people\" management style is explicitly debated in the case as something that worked in his specific context, but that other CEOs who copy the harshness without his ability to also inspire genuine loyalty \"make a dangerous mistake.\""},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Leader examples from the slides"},{"t":"p","x":"David Ossip, Ceridian chairman and CEO, is among the highest-rated leaders thanks to an inspiring vision, effective communication and role modeling of desired behavior. Keith Krach, a serial entrepreneur who cofounded or led Ariba and DocuSign, developed and communicated an inspirational strategic vision at each firm. Class question: is Steve Jobs charismatic or transformational? ITV CEO Carolyn McCall says effective leadership requires emotional intelligence, drive and integrity, and that knowing yourself and being yourself are crucial."},{"t":"p","x":"Dark Triad: Machiavellianism (strong motivation to get what one wants at others' expense; believes deceit is natural and acceptable; takes pleasure in misleading, outwitting and controlling others; seldom empathizes with or trusts coworkers) and psychopathy (social predators who may ruthlessly dominate and manipulate others; a mask of superficial charm but selfish self-promotion; antisocial, impulsive and often fraudulent thrill-seeking behavior)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9. Complementary Practice Exercises (New Questions)"},{"t":"p","x":"These are original questions (not from Moodle), in addition to the official quiz reproduced in Section 10 below."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: A startup founder tells the team \"we are going to change how people commute forever,\" works alongside engineers late into the night on the smallest UI details, and publicly celebrates any team member who challenges the current product roadmap with a better idea. Which perspective of leadership does this best illustrate?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Managerial leadership only"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Transformational leadership (vision + modeling + encouraging experimentation)"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Leadership substitutes theory"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Implicit leadership only"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: According to path-goal theory, which leadership style pairing is most appropriate for a highly skilled, experienced team with an internal locus of control who wants to be stretched further?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Directive and supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Achievement-oriented (and participative)"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Directive only"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Supportive only"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain the difference between managerial leadership and transformational leadership, and why the slides describe them as \"interdependent\" rather than substitutes for one another."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: What is \"leadership substitutes theory,\" and give one workplace example of a factor that could substitute for supportive leadership."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A newly promoted team lead has excellent technical knowledge and a clear personal vision for improving the product, but her team says she \"micromanages every decision and never lets us push back.\" Using authentic leadership and the leader-attributes perspective, identify what seems to be missing, and recommend one concrete change."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Transformational leadership — the vision statement (\"change how people commute forever\"), personally modeling high standards, and celebrating challenges to the status quo map directly onto the three elements: develop/communicate a vision, model the vision, and encourage experimentation.\n**2. **b) Achievement-oriented (often combined with participative) — path-goal theory recommends achievement-oriented and participative styles for skilled, experienced employees with an internal locus of control, since directive leadership would be seen as excessive, unwanted control for this group.\n**3. **Managerial leadership is the daily, micro-focused work of supporting current performance and well-being in a relatively stable environment (\"doing things right\"); transformational leadership is the macro-focused work of communicating and driving a shift toward a new future state in a dynamic environment (\"doing the right things\"). They are interdependent because an organisation needs both a compelling future vision (transformational) and disciplined day-to-day execution and support (managerial) — a leader who is purely visionary but never manages daily operations well (or vice versa) will struggle to sustain performance.\n**4. **Leadership substitutes theory identifies contingencies that either limit how much a leader can actually influence outcomes, or make a specific leadership style unnecessary. Example: a highly cohesive team with strong mutual social support can substitute for supportive leadership, because members already provide each other the psychological support a leader would otherwise need to supply.\n**5. **What seems to be missing is the \"be yourself\"/self-discipline balance of authentic leadership, combined with an over-reliance on directive control rather than participative/achievement-oriented leadership suited to a presumably capable team; from the leader-attributes perspective, she may have strong knowledge/drive but is not yet demonstrating the relational, empowering side of effective leadership (also connects to servant/shared leadership — trusting the team to lead in areas of their own expertise). Concrete change: deliberately delegate one or two recurring decisions fully to the team, explicitly inviting dissent/challenge in the next meeting (moving toward a more participative or achievement-oriented style), and seek trusted feedback on how her behaviour is being perceived."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10. Official MCQ Quiz — Chapter 12 Leadership (with Answers)"},{"t":"p","x":"Reproduced from the course-provided quiz bank. Use this to self-test recall of exact terminology before the exam."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"True / False"},{"t":"p","x":"**T/F 1. **The directive style is unnecessary when employees have routine and simple tasks."},{"t":"p","x":"**T/F 2. **One concern of path-goal theory is that it assumes leaders can change managerial styles to fit the situation."},{"t":"p","x":"**T/F 3. **Research indicates that male and female leaders widely differ in their levels of task-oriented and people-oriented leadership."},{"t":"p","x":"**T/F 4. **Charismatic visionary is a universally recognized leadership category."},{"t":"p","x":"**T/F 5. **According to path-goal leadership theory, directive leadership should never be applied to inexperienced employees."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Multiple Choice"},{"t":"p","x":"**6. **Leaders who create, communicate, and model a shared vision for the team are using the ________ perspective."},{"t":"p","x":"a) Transformational leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Path-goal leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Implicit leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Managerial leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Substitute leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"**7. **________ leadership is mostly a variation of people-oriented leadership."},{"t":"p","x":"a) Path-goal"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Shared"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Managerial"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Transformational"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Servant"},{"t":"p","x":"**8. **Which of the following leadership theories or perspectives explicitly includes the participative and the directive styles?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Competency (trait) perspective"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Transformational leadership theory"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Implicit leadership perspective"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Path-goal theory"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Managerial leadership perspective"},{"t":"p","x":"**9. **________ exists when employees persuade coworkers to try out a new work activity or introduce new technology."},{"t":"p","x":"a) Servant leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"b) The romance of leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Shared leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Directive leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Supportive leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"**10. **The \"romance of leadership\" is important for which of the following approaches?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Transformational leadership perspective"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Managerial leadership perspective"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Implicit leadership perspective"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Contingency perspective of leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Servant perspective of leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"**11. **Which of these concepts discusses the tendency of employees to overestimate the leader’s influence on organizational outcomes?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) The romance of leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Servant leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Path-goal leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Transactional leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Managerial leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"**12. **How do leadership prototypes develop?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Defined by organizational policy"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Defined by leaders"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Through culture, as all cultures share a universal prototype"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Through the romance of leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Through socialization within families and society"},{"t":"p","x":"**13. **Why do people tend to evaluate female leaders slightly less favorably than male leaders?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Females tend to adopt a directive style of leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"b) People have more trust in authoritarian leadership than participative leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"c) People tend to rely on gender stereotypes and prototypes of leaders"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Females tend to use one leadership style, whereas effective leaders use many styles"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Females are more people-oriented than task-oriented"},{"t":"p","x":"**14. **Which of the following statements about culture and leadership is true?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Participative leadership is perceived as characteristic of effective leadership in low power-distance cultures"},{"t":"p","x":"b) There are no similarities and only differences in leadership across cultures"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Transformational leadership theory explains differences in leadership practices across cultures"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Employees in high power-distance cultures prefer a participative leadership style"},{"t":"p","x":"e) People’s expectations from their leaders are universal and do not differ across countries"},{"t":"p","x":"**15. **How do females differ from males in their use of leadership styles?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Females tend to use more of the people-oriented leadership style than do males"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Females have much lower interpersonal skills than males"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Females tend to use more of the task-oriented leadership style than do males"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Females tend to use more of the participative leadership style than do males"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Females tend to use directive styles of leadership in organizations"},{"t":"p","x":"**16. **How do cultural values relate to leader behavior?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Leaders define cultural values"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Cultural values have no impact on a leader’s actions"},{"t":"p","x":"c) There is a universal set of cultural values all leaders need to adopt"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Cultural values have no impact on leader behaviors"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Culture shapes the leader’s values"},{"t":"p","x":"**17. **Studies have found that females, when compared to males, are evaluated negatively as leaders when they:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Empower employees"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Use a participative style"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Use a directive style"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Use coaching"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Behave according to their followers’ expectations"},{"t":"p","x":"**18. **________ is a leadership style identified in path-goal theory?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Achievement-oriented leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Servant leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Transformational leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Implicit leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Authentic leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"**19. **Which of the following leadership styles in path-goal theory is the same as task-oriented leadership?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Directive"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Participative"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Achievement-oriented"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Collaborative"},{"t":"p","x":"**20. **Which of the following leadership styles in path-goal theory is the same as people-oriented leadership?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Directive"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Participative"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Achievement-oriented"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Telling"},{"t":"p","x":"**21. **According to path-goal theory, a combination of ________ leadership is best for employees who are (or perceive themselves to be) inexperienced and unskilled."},{"t":"p","x":"a) Achievement-oriented and directive"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Participative and directive"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Directive and supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Supportive and achievement-oriented"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Supportive and participative"},{"t":"p","x":"**22. **People with an external locus of control tend to be more satisfied with ________ leadership styles."},{"t":"p","x":"a) Achievement-oriented and directive"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Participative and directive"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Directive and supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Supportive and achievement-oriented"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Supportive and participative"},{"t":"p","x":"**23. **When team cohesiveness is low, which of the following leadership styles should be used?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Directive"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Participative"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Achievement-oriented"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Authoritative"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Short Answer (Official Quiz)"},{"t":"p","x":"**SA1. **Briefly describe the implicit leadership theory."},{"t":"p","x":"**SA2. **Is the following statement accurate? Why or why not? \"Females are less effective than males in leadership positions because they tend to rely too much on the supportive style of leadership.\""},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**True/False:**\n**1. **TRUE — the directive style is ineffective (unnecessary) with routine/simple tasks because the manager’s guidance serves no purpose and can be seen as unnecessarily close control.\n**2. **TRUE — a real concern with path-goal theory is that it assumes leaders can readily switch styles to fit the situation, when in reality this takes considerable effort because leaders tend to prefer one style consistent with their own personality/values.\n**3. **FALSE — field studies generally find male and female leaders do not differ much in task-oriented or people-oriented leadership levels.\n**4. **TRUE — \"charismatic visionary\" (visionary, inspirational, high performance orientation, integrity, decisiveness) is recognized across cultures by middle managers worldwide as characteristic of effective leaders.\n**5. **FALSE — a combination of directive and supportive leadership is actually recommended for inexperienced/unskilled employees; directive leadership is only detrimental for skilled, experienced employees (too much supervisory control).\n**Multiple Choice:**\n**6. **a) Transformational leadership — creating, communicating and modeling a shared vision is the defining behaviour set of transformational leaders.\n**7. **e) Servant leadership — it is essentially an extension/variation of people-oriented leadership, defined as serving others’ need fulfilment, development and growth.\n**8. **d) Path-goal theory — its four styles explicitly include directive, supportive, participative and achievement-oriented leadership.\n**9. **c) Shared leadership — exists when employees persuade coworkers to try new activities/technology, or support each other through influence/OCBs.\n**10. **c) Implicit leadership perspective — the \"romance of leadership\" (inflating perceived leader influence on outcomes) is one of its two core components, alongside leadership prototypes.\n**11. **a) The romance of leadership — this is precisely the tendency to inflate a leader’s perceived effect on organisational success.\n**12. **e) Through socialization within the family and society — leadership prototypes are learned expectations built up from early life experience, not imposed by policy.\n**13. **c) People tend to rely on gender stereotypes and prototypes of leaders — this shapes (distorts) evaluation of female leaders independent of their actual competence.\n**14. **a) Participative leadership is perceived as characteristic of effective leadership in low power-distance cultures (but less so in high power-distance cultures).\n**15. **d) Females tend to use more of the participative leadership style than males (linked in the slides to socialization toward being more egalitarian/less status-oriented).\n**16. **e) Culture shapes the leader’s values and norms, which in turn shape the leader’s decisions/actions, and also shapes followers’ expectations of leaders.\n**17. **c) Use a directive style — studies find females are evaluated negatively when they apply a directive/autocratic style, because this conflicts with followers’ more participative, communal prototype of a female leader, even though the same behaviour is judged less harshly when a male leader does it.\n**18. **a) Achievement-oriented leadership — one of the four path-goal styles (directive, supportive, participative, achievement-oriented).\n**19. **a) Directive — directive leadership is the same as task-oriented leadership (clarifying goals, methods, and performance standards).\n**20. **b) Supportive — supportive leadership is the same as people-oriented leadership (providing psychological support).\n**21. **c) Directive and supportive — directive leadership gives task information, supportive leadership helps them cope with uncertainty, which together best fit inexperienced/unskilled employees.\n**22. **c) Directive and supportive — people with an external locus of control believe outcomes depend on luck/fate rather than their own effort, so they respond better to more directive/supportive guidance.\n**23. **b) Supportive — when team cohesion is low, leaders should use the supportive style to help build morale and cohesion.\n**Short Answer:**\n**SA1. **The implicit leadership perspective explains that followers’ own perceptions shape how \"effective\" a leader is judged to be. It has two components: (1) leadership prototypes — preconceived beliefs about the features/behaviours of an effective leader, developed through socialization within family and society, which shape our willingness to accept someone as a leader; and (2) the romance of leadership — followers tend to inflate a leader’s perceived influence on organisational success, partly because it simplifies complex events, and partly (in many cultures) because people prefer to believe outcomes are driven by people rather than uncontrollable forces.\n**SA2. **The statement is false. Research indicates females are about as effective as males in leadership roles (so the premise that they are \"less effective\" is incorrect), and females do not, in fact, disproportionately rely on the supportive style — they tend to adopt more of a participative style than male leaders. Females are often rated higher than males on emerging leadership qualities like coaching, teamwork and empowerment, but they can still be evaluated unfairly (negatively) when applying more directive/autocratic styles, because of gender stereotypes and follower leader-prototypes — so any leadership effectiveness gap is more about biased perception than actual differences in ability or style."}]},"8":{"title":"Power and Influence in the Workplace","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 10   |   Scheduled: 2 Nov 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. The Meaning of Power"},{"t":"li","x":"**Power: **the capacity of a person, team or organisation to influence others — a potential to change attitudes/behaviour, not necessarily an actual attempt to do so. It depends on the target’s perception that the power-holder controls a valuable resource, requires a minimum level of trust between both parties, and involves unequal dependence between the parties."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Five Sources of Power"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Source","Description"],["Legitimate power","Agreement that people in specific positions can request certain behaviours from others; bounded by each follower’s \"zone of indifference\" (their willingness to defer to authority in a given domain); connected to the norm of reciprocity and to information control (legitimate power lets you distribute/gatekeep information)."],["Reward power","Control over rewards valued by others, or the ability to remove negative sanctions."],["Coercive power","The ability to apply punishment."],["Expert power","The capacity to influence others through valued knowledge or skill; risk of \"mindless deference\" to expertise. A key form is managing uncertainty — preventing, forecasting, or absorbing environmental changes for the organisation."],["Referent power","The capacity to influence others through identification with, and respect for, the power-holder; associated with charisma."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Contingencies of Power"},{"t":"li","x":"**Nonsubstitutability: **power increases when a resource has few/no other sources and few/no substitutes; can be increased by differentiating the resource or controlling access to it."},{"t":"li","x":"**Centrality: **how interdependent others are with the power-holder — how many people, and how quickly, are affected by that person’s actions/resources."},{"t":"li","x":"**Visibility: **being known as the holder of a valued resource — built through social interaction and visible \"power symbols.\""},{"t":"li","x":"**Discretion: **freedom to exercise independent judgement — formal rules reduce discretion and, with it, power."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Power Through Social Networks"},{"t":"li","x":"**Social networks: **connect people through interdependence, serving instrumental (task-related) and expressive (emotional/social) functions; cultural variants exist (e.g., \"guanxi\" in Chinese business networks)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Social capital: **the goodwill (mutual support, trust, reciprocity, coordination) that lets people in a network actually share knowledge, opportunities and other resources."},{"t":"li","x":"**Strong ties: **close-knit relationships — offer resources quickly and plentifully, but the resources tend to be less unique (everyone in a tight circle already has similar information)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Weak ties: **acquaintances — offer more unique resources, but more slowly."},{"t":"li","x":"**Network centrality: **a person’s overall importance in a network, based on betweenness (sitting between other people/connecting them), degree centrality (sheer number of connections), and closeness (strength of connections)."},{"t":"p","x":"Networks provide three power resources: information (expert power), visibility, and referent power."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Consequences of Power"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type of power experience","Typical effect"],["Feeling empowered (power over oneself / freedom from others’ influence)","Higher motivation, but can bring less mindful thinking, less empathy, and more reliance on stereotypes."],["Having power over others","Tends to produce a sense of duty/responsibility, less stereotyping, more empathy, and more mindfulness of how one’s actions affect others."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Influencing Others — Eight Influence Tactics"},{"t":"p","x":"Influence: any behaviour that attempts to alter another person’s attitudes or behaviour, applying one or more power bases, conditional on the power contingencies above. Influence is essential to coordination, and is central to the very definition of leadership."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Tactic","Description"],["Silent authority","The mere presence or request of the power-holder influences behaviour (subtle use of legitimate power)."],["Assertiveness","\"Vocal authority\" — reminding, checking, or applying pressure (legitimate/coercive power)."],["Information control","Manipulating others’ access to information to shape their attitudes/behaviour."],["Coalition formation","Pooling members’ resources and power to influence others; symbolizes the issue as legitimate/important and adds influence via social identity."],["Upward appeal","Claiming (or showing evidence of) support from higher authority."],["Persuasion","Logical arguments and/or emotional appeals; effectiveness depends on the persuader, the message, the channel, and the audience."],["Impression management","Actively shaping others’ perceptions of us — includes self-presentation/personal branding and ingratiation (building liking/perceived similarity)."],["Exchange","Trading resources for a desired behaviour — central to negotiation, reciprocity, and social networks."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Contingencies of Influence"},{"t":"li","x":"\"Soft\" tactics (e.g., persuasion) are generally more acceptable than \"hard\" tactics (e.g., assertiveness)."},{"t":"li","x":"The best tactic depends on the influencer’s power base (e.g., experts succeed with persuasion; strong legitimate power holders succeed with silent authority)."},{"t":"li","x":"It also depends on organisational position (e.g., being too assertive with one’s boss can carry consequences) and on personal, organisational and cultural values (e.g., ingratiation is more common in the US than in Hong Kong, possibly linked to power distance)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7. Organisational Politics"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **using influence tactics for personal gain at the perceived expense of others or the organisation — generally has negative, not beneficial, consequences."},{"t":"li","x":"**Minimizing organisational politics: **provide sufficient resources, clarify resource-allocation rules, manage change effectively, and actively discourage political behaviour."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Further points from the slides"},{"t":"p","x":"Opening case: by developing a personal brand and expert power, Ann Hand attained managerial and executive positions in the energy, fast food and online gaming industries. A personal brand leverages power through visibility and nonsubstitutability."},{"t":"p","x":"Expert power's problem is mindless deference to expertise. Managing uncertainty is an important form of expert power: people prevent environmental changes (finance experts prevent cash shortages), forecast them (predicting emerging consumer trends) or absorb them (maintenance crews fix equipment breakdowns)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: A finance manager is highly valued because she is the only person in the company who fully understands the firm’s complex tax structure, and no one else has time to learn it. This mainly illustrates which power contingency?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Visibility"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Nonsubstitutability"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Discretion"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Centrality"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: An employee mentions in a meeting, \"the VP already agrees with this plan, she told me so yesterday,\" in order to get buy-in from a hesitant colleague. This is an example of which influence tactic?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Coalition formation"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Upward appeal"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Silent authority"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Exchange"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain the difference between \"strong ties\" and \"weak ties\" in a social network, and why weak ties are often described as valuable for finding a new job."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Distinguish \"feeling empowered\" from \"having power over others\" in terms of their typical psychological and behavioural consequences."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A newly hired specialist has deep technical expertise that nobody else in the department has (expert power), but she has almost no visibility — most senior leaders don’t know her by name, and she rarely interacts outside her immediate team. Using the power contingencies framework, explain why her actual influence in the organisation may be lower than her expertise alone would suggest, and recommend one action to increase her power."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Nonsubstitutability — her power comes from being the only source of a needed, non-substitutable resource (specialized tax knowledge), not from being widely known (visibility) or having discretion/centrality per se.\n**2. **b) Upward appeal — claiming or showing evidence of support from a higher authority (the VP) to influence the colleague’s decision.\n**3. **Strong ties are close-knit relationships (e.g., close colleagues, family) that provide resources quickly and abundantly, but because everyone in a tight circle tends to share similar information, the resources are less unique. Weak ties are more distant acquaintances who provide resources more slowly, but because they move in different circles, they are more likely to offer genuinely new, unique information — such as a job opening you would never hear about through your close inner circle. This is why weak ties are valuable for job searching: they act as bridges to information you don’t already have.\n**4. **\"Feeling empowered\" (power over oneself, freedom from others’ influence) tends to raise motivation but can reduce mindful thinking and empathy and increase reliance on stereotypes about others. \"Having power over others,\" by contrast, tends to create a sense of duty/responsibility and is linked to less stereotyping, more empathy, and more mindfulness of how one’s actions affect other people — i.e., power directed at others, when experienced as a responsibility, tends to produce more careful, other-aware behaviour than power experienced purely as personal freedom.\n**5. **Even strong expert power delivers limited real influence if the contingencies of power are not also present: without visibility (being known as the holder of that expertise) and without centrality (being connected to, and depended on by, many people across the organisation), her expert power stays \"dormant\" because decision-makers simply don’t know to draw on it or trust it. Recommended action: deliberately build visibility and network centrality — e.g., presenting her findings at cross-departmental meetings, contributing to visible cross-functional projects, or building an internal professional profile/personal brand — so that her expertise becomes known and accessible (also connecting expertise to information/referent power through the social network)."}]},"9":{"title":"Conflict and Negotiation in the Workplace","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 11   |   Scheduled: 9 Nov 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Is Conflict Good or Bad?"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Negative outcomes","Positive outcomes"],["Lower performance; higher stress, dissatisfaction, turnover; less information sharing/coordination; more organisational politics; wasted resources; weaker team cohesion (conflict within the team)","Better decision making; tests the logic of arguments; questions assumptions; generates creative thinking; more responsiveness to a changing environment; stronger team cohesion (conflict with an outside opponent)"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Task vs. Relationship Conflict"},{"t":"li","x":"**Task (constructive) conflict: **focuses on the quality of ideas/arguments (logic, evidence, assumptions), shows respect for all participants, and avoids attacking the opponent’s competence or authority."},{"t":"li","x":"**Relationship conflict: **focuses on the opponent’s character, tries to win by undermining their credibility, and relies on status/assertiveness — dysfunctional because it triggers defense mechanisms, reduces trust, and reduces motivation to communicate, escalating the conflict."},{"t":"li","x":"**Minimizing relationship conflict during task conflict: **emotional intelligence (regulating emotions, reframing hostility), team development (mutual understanding, cohesion motivating de-escalation), and psychological safety norms (safe to disagree constructively, with respect and open-mindedness)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. The Conflict Process and Its Structural Sources"},{"t":"p","x":"The conflict process model traces how sources of conflict lead to perceptions/emotions, then to manifest behaviour, then to outcomes/resolution attempts."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Six Structural Sources of Conflict"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Source","Description"],["Incompatible goals","One party’s goals appear to interfere with another’s."],["Differentiation","Different training, values, beliefs and experience between parties (e.g., generational diversity)."],["Interdependence","All conflict requires interdependence; conflict risk rises as interdependence increases."],["Scarce resources","Creates direct competition for the resource."],["Ambiguous rules","Creates uncertainty and opens the door to office politics."],["Communication problems","Poorly stated messages escalate conflict; conflict itself causes more emotive messaging, and reduced communication then amplifies the conflict further."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Five Interpersonal Conflict-Handling Styles"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Style","Best used when...","Main risk"],["Problem solving","Interests are not perfectly opposed; parties trust each other/are open; the issue is complex.","Takes time; sharing information can increase the other party’s power."],["Forcing","A quick resolution is required; your position has stronger logical/moral grounds; the other party would otherwise exploit cooperation.","Relationship damage; harms long-term relations."],["Avoiding","The conflict is emotionally charged (relationship conflict); parties want to preserve harmony; resolution costs exceed its benefits.","The conflict remains unresolved, causing ongoing frustration/uncertainty."],["Yielding","The issue matters less to you than the other party; your position is logically weaker; the other party has much more power; harmony matters.","Raises the other party’s future expectations."],["Compromising","A single issue with opposing interests; parties lack time/trust for problem solving; roughly equal power; harmony matters.","Can produce a sub-optimal outcome where a larger mutual gain was possible."]]},{"t":"li","x":"**Cultural/gender differences: **collectivist cultures tend to show more avoidance; men tend to use more forcing; women tend to use somewhat more avoiding (especially female managers), problem solving, compromising and yielding — often linked to differing motivations/expectations around protecting relationships."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Structural Approaches to Conflict Management"},{"t":"li","x":"Emphasize superordinate goals — focus on shared objectives, which reduces goal incompatibility and may reduce differentiation."},{"t":"li","x":"Reduce differentiation — create common experiences (meaningful interaction, staff rotation, building a strong shared culture)."},{"t":"li","x":"Improve communication and understanding — e.g., Johari Window and contact-hypothesis-based activities (only effective once differentiation has been reduced first)."},{"t":"li","x":"Reduce interdependence — use buffers (e.g., inventory), use integrator roles, or combine jobs into one."},{"t":"li","x":"Increase resources — weigh the cost of extra resources against the cost of ongoing conflict."},{"t":"li","x":"Clarify rules/procedures — establish clear rules, roles, responsibilities and schedules."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Negotiation"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **trying to resolve divergent goals by redefining the terms of interdependence between parties."},{"t":"li","x":"**Distributive approach: **a win-lose orientation, most common with a single item to resolve."},{"t":"li","x":"**Integrative (mutual gains) approach: **a win-win orientation, works better with multiple issues of different value to each party (allowing trade-offs)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"BATNA and Power in Negotiation"},{"t":"p","x":"BATNA = Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement — it represents your estimated cost of walking away. Having more than one BATNA increases your negotiating power. Negotiation power overall rises with favourable sources and contingencies of power (Chapter 10), which also tend to improve your BATNA."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Negotiation Process"},{"t":"li","x":"**Gather information: **listen closely, ask questions, watch nonverbal cues, summarize the other party’s statements, and share your own reactions — the goal is to discover the needs behind their stated positions."},{"t":"li","x":"**Manage concessions: **make fewer, smaller, clearly labeled concessions; explicitly ask for reciprocity; use multi-issue offers rather than resolving one issue at a time."},{"t":"li","x":"**Manage time and relationships: **be aware of deadline effects, exploding offers and escalating time commitments; build trustworthiness by finding common ground, managing first impressions, signalling trustworthiness, and using emotional intelligence."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Negotiation Setting"},{"t":"li","x":"Location — easier to negotiate on your own turf."},{"t":"li","x":"Physical setting — seating, spacing, and formality all matter."},{"t":"li","x":"Audience — negotiators become more competitive and make fewer concessions when being watched."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Gender and Negotiation"},{"t":"p","x":"Compared with men, women in negotiations tend to set lower target points and accept offers closer to their resistance point, rely less on alternatives to improve outcomes, avoid initiating negotiation (accepting first offers more often), receive more deceitful tactics from counterparts, and be judged less favourably when they do use effective, assertive negotiation tactics. With training and experience, women negotiate about as well as men."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Further points from the slides"},{"t":"p","x":"Opening case: overt conflict is rare among commercial airline crews, but when clashes occur the consequences are costly for the airline and inconvenient for passengers."},{"t":"p","x":"Third-party intervention (Exhibit 11.5): managers prefer the inquisitional strategy because it fits the decision-oriented nature of managerial jobs, but it is usually the least effective method and conflicts with procedural justice. Mediation offers the highest potential satisfaction with process and outcomes. Arbitration is used when mediation fails and has procedural justice because of predetermined rules and processes."},{"t":"p","x":"Bargaining zone model (Exhibit 11.6): negotiators have a target point and a resistance point; an agreement is possible only where the parties' resistance points overlap, which is the bargaining zone."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Third-Party Dispute Resolution"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Key features"],["Inquisition","The third party (often a manager) controls both the investigation process and the final decision. Popular with managers (fits a decision-oriented role) but usually the least effective method, and can conflict with procedural justice principles."],["Mediation","The third party controls the process but not the outcome, helping the parties reach their own solution. Generally has the highest potential satisfaction with both process and outcome."],["Arbitration","The third party controls the outcome via predetermined rules/processes (used when mediation fails). Provides procedural justice because rules and process are set in advance."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: Two departments are fighting over the same limited annual training budget, with no clear rule for how it should be split. Which two structural sources of conflict are most clearly present?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Differentiation and interdependence"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Scarce resources and ambiguous rules"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Communication problems and incompatible goals"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Interdependence and communication problems"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: In a negotiation, a job candidate says, \"I have two other strong offers on the table,\" giving her more confidence to hold firm on salary. This illustrates the importance of:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) The bargaining zone model"},{"t":"p","x":"b) BATNA (and having more than one)"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Distributive justice"},{"t":"p","x":"d) The conflict process model"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain why \"avoiding\" is sometimes the appropriate conflict-handling style, giving one situation where it would be the best choice and one risk of overusing it."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Distinguish mediation from arbitration, and explain why mediation typically produces higher satisfaction with the outcome than arbitration or inquisition."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: Two team members are in an escalating dispute over how to approach a shared project. What started as a disagreement about the best method (task conflict) has turned personal — they are now openly questioning each other’s competence and credibility in front of the team (relationship conflict). As their manager, using conflict theory, describe two specific actions you would take to de-escalate the relationship conflict while still allowing the useful task conflict (differing ideas about method) to continue productively."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Scarce resources and ambiguous rules — a limited shared budget is a classic scarce-resource conflict, and the absence of a clear allocation rule directly matches the \"ambiguous rules\" structural source.\n**2. **b) BATNA (having more than one) — her alternative offers represent her Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement; having multiple strong BATNAs increases her negotiating power and confidence to hold her position.\n**3. **Avoiding is appropriate when the conflict is emotionally charged (relationship conflict), when parties want to preserve harmony, or when the cost of resolving the issue outweighs the benefit — e.g., a minor stylistic disagreement between two colleagues who otherwise work well together might best be left alone rather than escalated. The risk of overusing it is that the underlying issue remains unresolved, which can cause ongoing frustration, uncertainty, and (if used repeatedly on substantive issues) allow real problems to fester.\n**4. **In mediation, the third party controls the process (e.g., structuring the conversation, ensuring both sides are heard) but the parties themselves control the outcome/solution. In arbitration, the third party controls the actual outcome, based on predetermined rules. Mediation typically produces the highest satisfaction because the parties retain ownership of the solution (voice and self-determination, consistent with procedural justice), whereas arbitration and especially inquisition impose an outcome on the parties, reducing their sense of control and voice even if the process itself feels reasonably fair.\n**5. **Two acceptable actions: (1) Explicitly separate the person from the problem — redirect the conversation back to the specific task/method being debated, and set (or reinforce) a team norm of psychological safety that disagreement about ideas is welcome but attacking a colleague’s competence or character is not; this uses \"structural approaches to conflict management\" (clarifying norms/rules) and protects task conflict while suppressing relationship conflict. (2) Act as a mediator (not an arbitrator) in a private conversation with both parties — control the process (structured turn-taking, active listening, summarizing) without dictating the final method decision, so both retain ownership of the eventual solution; this both rebuilds trust (reducing relationship conflict) and keeps the substantive method discussion (task conflict) alive."}]},"10":{"title":"Culture and Organisational Change","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 14 (+ Chapter 15, pp. 215-218, 220-221)   |   Scheduled: 16 Nov 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Organisational Culture Defined"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **the values and assumptions shared within an organisation, providing direction toward the \"right way\" of doing things — often described as the company’s (largely invisible) \"DNA,\" a powerful template for employee behaviour."},{"t":"li","x":"**The \"iceberg\" idea: **visible artifacts sit above the surface, while the deeper values and shared assumptions that actually drive behaviour sit unseen below it — you must look at multiple artifacts to accurately decipher a culture, since culture is often fuzzy, fragmented into subcultures, and lives within individuals rather than as one single, unified entity."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Organisational Culture Profile — Content Dimensions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Dimension","Characteristics"],["Innovation","Experimenting, opportunity-seeking, risk-taking, few rules."],["Stability","Predictability, security, rule-oriented."],["Respect for people","Fairness, tolerance."],["Outcome orientation","Action-oriented, high expectations, results-oriented."],["Attention to detail","Precise, analytic."],["Team orientation","Collaboration, people-oriented."],["Aggressiveness","Competitive, low emphasis on social responsibility."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Subcultures"},{"t":"li","x":"**Dominant culture: **the values/assumptions shared most consistently and widely across the organisation."},{"t":"li","x":"**Subcultures: **exist throughout the organisation; countercultures are subcultures that oppose parts of the dominant culture — they serve two useful functions: surveillance/critical review, and acting as a source of emerging (future) values."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Culture Artifacts"},{"t":"p","x":"Observable symbols/signs that maintain and transmit culture — you typically need several artifacts together to accurately decipher a company’s real culture."},{"t":"li","x":"**Stories and legends: **social prescriptions of desired (or dysfunctional) behaviour; most effective when they describe real people, are believed to be true, are known throughout the organisation, and are prescriptive."},{"t":"li","x":"**Language: **how employees address each other/outsiders, express emotion and describe stakeholders; leaders use language to anchor or shift culture; language also differentiates subcultures."},{"t":"li","x":"**Rituals: **programmed routines (e.g., how visitors are greeted)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Ceremonies: **planned activities for an audience (e.g., award ceremonies)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Physical structures/symbols: **building design, office layout, furniture and other physical cues that both shape and reflect culture."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Culture Strength and Effectiveness"},{"t":"li","x":"**Culture strength: **how widely and deeply employees hold the organisation’s dominant values/assumptions. In strong cultures, most employees understand and embrace the dominant values, these are institutionalized through well-established artifacts, and the culture is long-lasting (often traceable to the founder)."},{"t":"p","x":"Three functions of a strong culture: (1) control system — an \"automatic pilot\" guiding decisions/behaviour; (2) social glue — bonds people together, fulfilling the need for social identity; (3) sense-making — helps employees understand what is expected of them (role clarity). Culture also shapes ethical conduct."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Contingencies of Culture Strength"},{"t":"li","x":"Culture content must be aligned with the environment — a misaligned culture guides the wrong decisions for stakeholder relations."},{"t":"li","x":"Culture strength should not reach the level of a cult — cults lock people into rigid mental models and suppress dissenting subcultures."},{"t":"li","x":"The culture should be an adaptive culture — externally focused, supporting continuous improvement and a learning orientation."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Merging Organisational Cultures"},{"t":"li","x":"**Bicultural audit: **part of merger due diligence — (1) identify cultural differences between the merging firms; (2) analyze which differences will cause conflict and which provide common ground; (3) identify strategies and action plans to bridge the two cultures."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Strategy","Description","Best when..."],["Assimilation","The acquired firm’s staff embrace the acquiring firm’s culture.","The acquired culture is weak/dysfunctional and the acquiring culture is strong and successful. (Rare.)"],["Deculturation","The acquiring firm imposes its culture and practices.","The acquired firm’s culture clearly does not work; often meets employee resistance."],["Integration","A composite culture preserves the best of both.","Both firms have relatively weak cultures, values overlap, or staff are motivated to adopt new shared values. (Slow, potentially risky.)"],["Separation","Each firm keeps its own culture/practices.","The merging firms are in unrelated industries or different countries."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Changing and Strengthening Organisational Culture"},{"t":"li","x":"Actions of founders/leaders — a founder’s values/personality shape early culture; transformational leaders can reshape culture during organisational change."},{"t":"li","x":"Aligning artifacts — since artifacts keep culture \"in place,\" changing them (stories, symbols, language) helps change the culture itself."},{"t":"li","x":"Introducing culturally consistent rewards — rewards are powerful artifacts that reinforce desired values."},{"t":"li","x":"Supporting workforce stability and communication — it takes time to build shared understanding of a culture, and strong cultures depend on frequent, open communication."},{"t":"li","x":"Attracting, selecting and socializing employees — via the Attraction-Selection-Attrition (ASA) framework and formal socialization practices."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Attraction-Selection-Attrition (ASA) Theory"},{"t":"li","x":"**Attraction: **applicants self-select toward organisations whose values feel compatible with their own."},{"t":"li","x":"**Selection: **firms select applicants whose values seem compatible with the organisation."},{"t":"li","x":"**Attrition: **employees whose values are incompatible eventually quit or are removed."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Organisational Socialization"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **the process by which individuals learn the values, expected behaviours and social knowledge needed to take on their role in the organisation."},{"t":"li","x":"**Learning process: **newcomers build organisational comprehension — learning expectations, power dynamics, culture and jargon — and form social relationships."},{"t":"li","x":"**Adjustment process: **newcomers adapt to the new work setting, develop new work roles/social identity, and adopt new team norms/behaviours; those with more diverse prior work experience tend to adjust better."},{"t":"li","x":"**Psychological contracts: **an individual’s beliefs about the terms of a reciprocal exchange with the organisation — transactional contracts are short-term, well-defined economic exchanges; relational contracts are long-term attachments with a broad range of mutual obligations."},{"t":"li","x":"**Improving socialization: **realistic job previews (RJPs — giving a balanced mix of positive and negative information about the job/context before hiring), and socialization agents such as supervisors (technical information, feedback, job duties) and co-workers (role models, ideally accessible, tolerant and supportive)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7. Organisational Change (Chapter 15 — Supplementary Content)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Gap note: **The slide deck for Chapter 15 (Organisational Change) was not among the Moodle files available for this synthesis, even though the exam-revision summary explicitly lists Lewin’s force field analysis, why people resist change, and the unfreezing-changing-refreezing stages as examinable. The notes below use standard, well-established OB theory on this exact topic so your coverage is complete — please verify the precise terminology, examples and any extra detail against your own lecture slides once you can access them."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Lewin’s Force Field Analysis"},{"t":"p","x":"Kurt Lewin proposed that, at any point, an organisation is in a state of relative equilibrium held in place by two opposing sets of forces:"},{"t":"li","x":"**Driving forces: **pressures pushing the organisation toward change (e.g., new technology, competitive threats, leadership pressure, new regulations)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Restraining forces: **pressures maintaining the status quo (e.g., habit, fear, vested interests, group norms, structural inertia)."},{"t":"p","x":"Change happens when driving forces outweigh restraining forces. Simply increasing driving forces can raise tension and resistance; the model suggests it is often more effective (and more sustainable) to reduce restraining forces as well as, or instead of, only pushing harder."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Lewin’s Three-Step Change Model"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Step","What happens"],["1. Unfreezing","Creating the motivation and readiness for change — building dissatisfaction with the current state, communicating why change is needed, and reducing restraining forces."],["2. Changing (moving)","Actually implementing the new attitudes, behaviours, processes or structures — often through training, new routines, and role modeling by leaders."],["3. Refreezing","Stabilizing the new state so it becomes the new normal — reinforcing new behaviours through rewards, aligned artifacts/culture, and consistent reinforcement, so the organisation does not drift back to the old way."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Why People Resist Change (Standard Reasons)"},{"t":"li","x":"Fear of the unknown / uncertainty about how change will personally affect them."},{"t":"li","x":"Loss of something valued — status, power, job security, established relationships, or a sense of competence and mastery (\"saving face\" — not wanting to look incompetent under new methods)."},{"t":"li","x":"Breaking established habits and routines, which feel effortful and uncomfortable."},{"t":"li","x":"Lack of trust in leadership, or poor/insufficient communication about the reasons for and nature of the change."},{"t":"li","x":"Disruption to social relationships and existing team dynamics."},{"t":"li","x":"A genuine (not merely self-interested) belief that the proposed change is the wrong direction."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"General Strategies for Managing Resistance to Change"},{"t":"li","x":"Communication and education — explain the need for and nature of the change clearly and early."},{"t":"li","x":"Participation and involvement — let affected employees help shape the change, increasing buy-in and voice."},{"t":"li","x":"Facilitation and support — training, counselling, and resources to ease the transition."},{"t":"li","x":"Negotiation and agreement — where some parties will clearly lose out, negotiate to offset their losses."},{"t":"li","x":"Coercion — used sparingly, when speed is essential and other approaches have failed; carries relationship risk."},{"t":"p","x":"This connects directly back to organisational culture: successful, lasting organisational change usually requires \"unfreezing\" old cultural assumptions and then \"refreezing\" new ones using the same culture-change levers covered in Section 5 above (leader actions, aligned artifacts, rewards, and socialization)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Cases and extra points from the slides"},{"t":"p","x":"Uber's CEO Dara Khosrowshahi set out to replace the company's \"toxic culture\" with values aligned with more productive behavior and societal expectations. Satya Nadella, whose career has been mostly at Microsoft, transformed its culture to team-oriented cooperation, continuous learning and far-reaching innovation."},{"t":"p","x":"Bicultural audit (part of a merger's due diligence; it minimizes cultural collision): (1) identify cultural differences between the merging companies; (2) analyze the data to determine which differences will cause conflict and which values provide common ground; (3) identify strategies and prepare action plans to bridge the two cultures."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: A company’s founder personally tells a well-known internal story, in every new-hire orientation, about the time she refunded an angry customer out of her own pocket before the company had a formal refund policy. This is primarily an example of which type of culture artifact?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) A ritual"},{"t":"p","x":"b) A ceremony"},{"t":"p","x":"c) A story/legend"},{"t":"p","x":"d) A physical symbol"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: Two companies with very different, incompatible national cultures merge, but the acquiring firm decides to let each unit keep running under its own existing culture and management practices. This is an example of which culture-merging strategy?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Assimilation"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Deculturation"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Integration"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Separation"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain, using Lewin’s force field analysis, why simply \"pushing harder\" for change (increasing driving forces) without addressing restraining forces can sometimes backfire."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Using the Attraction-Selection-Attrition (ASA) framework, explain how a strong culture can become self-reinforcing over time, for better or worse."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A company is rolling out a new company-wide software system. Several long-tenured employees are quietly avoiding training sessions and continuing to use the old manual process \"just in case,\" even though management has announced the change. Using Lewin’s model and the standard reasons for resisting change, diagnose what stage of change this suggests the organisation is stuck at, identify the most likely reason(s) for resistance, and recommend one action for each of the unfreezing and refreezing stages."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **c) A story/legend — it describes a real person, is presented as true, is known throughout the organisation (told at every orientation), and is prescriptive (it signals desired customer-first behaviour) — matching all four features of an effective story/legend.\n**2. **d) Separation — each firm keeps its own corporate culture and practices, which the slides identify as the best approach when merging firms are in unrelated industries or very different countries/cultures.\n**3. **Force field analysis models an organisation as held in equilibrium by driving forces (for change) and restraining forces (against change). Simply increasing driving forces (e.g., more pressure, mandates, deadlines) without reducing restraining forces (fear, habit, distrust, loss of status) raises overall tension in the system: employees may comply superficially while resistance and anxiety build \"underground,\" or resistance may intensify to counterbalance the added pressure, producing more conflict and less durable change than an approach that also actively reduces restraining forces (e.g., addressing fears, building trust, easing the habit-breaking process).\n**4. **Under ASA, applicants are attracted to organisations whose values feel compatible with their own (Attraction), firms tend to hire people who fit the existing culture (Selection), and employees whose values clash with the culture tend to leave or be pushed out over time (Attrition). Because all three forces point the same direction, they compound: the culture becomes progressively more homogeneous and strongly held over time. This is positive when the culture is genuinely adaptive and ethical (reinforcing a healthy strong culture), but it can also entrench a dysfunctional or overly homogeneous culture (e.g., filtering out genuinely useful diversity of thought), since ASA does not discriminate between \"good\" and \"bad\" cultural content — it just reinforces whatever content is already dominant.\n**5. **This organisation appears stuck at the unfreezing stage — the announcement alone has not created enough real motivation/readiness for change, since restraining forces (habit, likely some fear of looking incompetent with the new system, and possibly distrust that the change is really permanent) are still stronger than the driving forces for these employees. Likely resistance reasons: breaking established habits/routines, and fear of the unknown or loss of a sense of competence/mastery (\"saving face\") with the unfamiliar new system. Recommended actions: Unfreezing — more direct communication of why the change matters (e.g., linking it to real business risks of the old process) plus reducing the specific fear driving resistance (e.g., reassurance that mistakes during the learning period are expected and won’t be penalized); Refreezing — once employees do adopt the new system, reinforce and lock in the new behaviour by removing access to the old manual process, updating official procedures/artifacts to reference only the new system, and rewarding/recognizing early adopters so the new way becomes the visible norm rather than something that can quietly slide back."}]}},"extras":[{"t":"p","x":"**BUS2202**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Organisational Behaviour**"},{"t":"p","x":"Complete Revision Synthesis & Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"Lingnan University — Term 1, AY2026-27"},{"t":"p","x":"All Moodle content for Lessons 1-10 (Chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 10, 11, 12, 14), synthesized and cross-checked against the instructor’s exam revision summary, with original practice exercises per lesson and a mixed final review set."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"How to Use This Document"},{"t":"p","x":"This synthesis gathers every substantive piece of course content posted on Moodle for BUS2202 Organisational Behaviour and organizes it lesson by lesson, in the order actually taught (Lessons 1-10, following the official course schedule). For each lesson you will find: (1) clean revision notes covering every theory, model and concept from the slides, and (2) a \"Complementary Practice Exercises\" section with brand-new self-test questions and a full answer key. The document ends with a cross-lesson exam review checklist and a mixed practice set."},{"t":"li","x":"**Course admin (this section): **syllabus highlights, full schedule, and a short, clearly-labeled summary of the Individual Assignment and Group Presentation — for context only, not exam content."},{"t":"li","x":"**Lessons 1-10: **revision notes + 5 practice questions with answer key each."},{"t":"li","x":"**Leadership (Lesson 7): **also includes the official MCQ quiz (with answers) and a summary of the Steve Jobs case study linked to leadership theory."},{"t":"li","x":"**End of document: **exam-style review checklist across all lessons + one mixed practice set (10 questions) with full answer key."},{"t":"p","x":"**Gap note: **One topic listed in the instructor’s exam revision summary — Chapter 15, Organisational Change (Lewin’s force field analysis, unfreezing/changing/refreezing, resistance to change) — was not among the 18 files extracted from Moodle for this course. It has been added at the end of Lesson 10 using standard, correct OB theory so your coverage is complete; please verify the specifics (e.g., exact terminology used in your lecture) against the original slide deck once you can access it."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Course Overview (Admin Information)"},{"t":"p","x":"The material below is course administration, drawn from the syllabus, rubrics, schedule and assignment briefs. It is summarized here for context and is clearly separated from the examinable content that starts at Lesson 1."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Syllabus Highlights"},{"t":"li","x":"**Course: **BUS2202 Organisational Behaviour, 3 credits, 3-hour lecture per week, Functional Core course."},{"t":"li","x":"**Course description: **the study of what people think, feel and do in organisations, and how this affects organisational performance; aims to give students conceptual tools to work more effectively in the workplace."},{"t":"li","x":"**Learning outcomes (LOs): **"},{"t":"li","x":"LO1 — Explain and discuss concepts/theories of individual and organisational behaviour and their workplace impacts."},{"t":"li","x":"LO2 — Apply OB knowledge to diagnose work-related issues and develop solutions."},{"t":"li","x":"LO3 — Demonstrate critical thinking on behaviour-related problems."},{"t":"li","x":"LO4 — Develop effective teamwork skills in a business context."},{"t":"li","x":"**Indicative content (4 blocks): **Introduction to OB; Individual Behaviour and Processes (values/ethics, personality, perception/learning, emotions/attitudes, job satisfaction, motivation, stress); Group Processes (team effectiveness, conflict, power/politics, leadership); Organisational Processes (culture, organisational change)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Required textbook: **McShane, S.L. & Von Glinow, M.A., Organizational Behavior (9th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2020) — the individual assignment brief references the newer 10th ed., Organizational Behavior: Emerging Knowledge, Global Reality (2024)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Assessment weighting: **50% continuous assessment (25% group/team work, 15% individual case, 10% individual class participation) + 50% final examination."},{"t":"li","x":"**Academic integrity: **Turnitin submission required for written work; GAI (generative AI) use must be declared per Lingnan’s GAI guidelines; plagiarism and fabricated references are treated as serious academic offences (fabricated/falsified references can result in a grade of zero)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Workload expectation: **about 9 hours/week total (3 contact + 6 personal study)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Full Course Schedule (Term 1, AY2026-27: 1 Sep - 30 Nov 2026)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Lesson","Date","Topic","Chapter","Notes"],["1","7 Sep","Introduction to the Field of OB","Ch 1","Ice-breaking, course overview"],["2","14 Sep","Individual Behaviour, Personality and Values","Ch 2","Recap of assessments; group exercises"],["3","21 Sep","Perception and Learning in Organisations","Ch 3","Perception/learning psychology tests"],["4","28 Sep","Workplace Emotions, Attitudes and Stress","Ch 4","Submit group members’ name list"],["5","5 Oct","Motivation","Ch 5","Case discussion; individual assignment checkpoint"],["6","12 Oct","Team Dynamics","Ch 8","Case discussion"],["—","19 Oct","Holiday — No class","—","Individual Assignment due 20 Oct, 23:59 (15%)"],["7","26 Oct","Leadership in Organisational Settings","Ch 12","Submit group topic (1-page PPT)"],["8","2 Nov","Power and Influence","Ch 10","In-class exercises"],["9","9 Nov","Conflict and Negotiation","Ch 11","In-class exercises"],["10","16 Nov","Culture and Organisational Change","Ch 14 & 15","In-class exercises"],["11","23 Nov","Group Presentation","—","25% of grade"],["12","30 Nov","Course Summary and Exam Revision","—","Instructor’s topic-by-chapter summary"],["Exam","8-12 & 14-19 Dec","Final Examination","All","50% of grade"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Assignment Info (Context Only — Not Exam Content)"},{"t":"p","x":"The two items below are continuous-assessment deliverables, summarized briefly so you know what is expected alongside your revision. They are not part of the examinable theory content."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Individual Case Report — Stress Management (15%)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Case: **\"The Human Element: The Case of Senior Project Manager, Janet Wong\" — a senior project manager overriding an AI risk-monitoring system’s warnings under fatigue, leading to a costly production incident, while also neglecting family responsibilities."},{"t":"li","x":"**Task: **a 1,400-word report (incl. references) answering four questions (25 marks each): (1) causes of Janet’s stress with case evidence; (2) work and personal consequences of that stress; (3) whether other team members would react the same way to similar stressors (individual differences); (4) what Janet and her supervisor could have done differently, including around the human-in-the-loop AI interventions."},{"t":"li","x":"**Practical requirements: **submit via Turnitin by 23:59, 20 Oct 2026; English quality counts for 10% of the mark; GAI use must be declared (GAI content should not exceed 20%, similarity not exceed 10%, excluding references); late submission = 10% deduction per day; fabricated/falsified references = automatic zero."},{"t":"p","x":"**Gap note: **This case maps directly onto the Chapter 4 stress content (stressors, individual differences in stress, stress management strategies) covered in Lesson 4 of this document — revisit that section when preparing the report."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Group Presentation — OB/HR Issue Analysis (25%)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Format: **groups of 4-5 members; 15-minute oral presentation + 5-minute Q&A (20 minutes total); hardcopy PowerPoint (in notes form) submitted in Lecture 12; no written report required."},{"t":"li","x":"**Task: **identify and critically analyse a real, recent OB/HR issue related to Employee Motivation, Team Dynamics, or Leadership, applying course theories/concepts (not just describing the case), evaluate the effectiveness of actions taken, and recommend solutions."},{"t":"li","x":"**Process: **submit a one-page PowerPoint project proposal (title, issue, area of investigation) before the group consultation (before Lecture 7); presentation structure = cover page, introduction, analysis, recommendations, conclusion, APA references."},{"t":"li","x":"**Assessment criteria: **comprehension/relevance/accuracy of content, evidence of research, application of OB/HR theory, critical evaluation, logical flow, creativity/feasibility of recommendations, presentation style, referencing."},{"t":"li","x":"**Other rules: **peer evaluation adjusts individual marks for uneven contribution; Turnitin required; GAI use must be declared and cited; English quality assessed separately."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Rubric Snapshot (For Awareness)"},{"t":"p","x":"All four rubrics (class participation, individual case, group presentation, final exam) score on a similar structure: understanding of concepts, problem diagnosis / stakeholder analysis, application of concepts to the problem, clarity/logic of expression, and English quality. The final exam rubric weighs concept understanding, problem diagnosis, stakeholder analysis and application of theory at 20% each, plus 20% for clear/logical/accurate expression — i.e., knowing the theory is necessary but not sufficient: you must also apply it precisely to the scenario given and reference multiple stakeholder perspectives."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Final Exam Review Checklist (All Lessons)"},{"t":"p","x":"Use this as a self-test: for each item, check that you can define it, explain it in your own words, and apply it to a short scenario. This checklist is cross-checked against the instructor’s \"Exam Revision — Summary of key topics by Chapter\" so every listed exam topic is represented somewhere in this document."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 1 — Introduction to OB"},{"t":"li","x":"Definition of OB and of an organisation; organisational effectiveness (open systems, human capital, stakeholders)."},{"t":"li","x":"The Integrative Model of OB (big-picture framework)."},{"t":"li","x":"The five OB anchors (systematic research, practical orientation, multidisciplinary, contingency, multiple levels of analysis)."},{"t":"li","x":"Emerging workplace trends: AI, inclusive workplace/diversity, work-life integration, remote/hybrid work, employment relationships."},{"t":"li","x":"The MARS model (Motivation, Ability, Role perceptions, Situational factors) and the five types of individual behaviour (task performance, OCBs, counterproductive behaviour, joining/staying, attendance)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 2 — Personality and Values"},{"t":"li","x":"Big Five personality factors and their workplace links; issues with the model."},{"t":"li","x":"MBTI/Jungian theory — strengths and (important) limitations."},{"t":"li","x":"The Dark Triad: Machiavellianism, narcissism, psychopathy, and workplace consequences."},{"t":"li","x":"Schwartz’s values model; values congruence; when values fail to predict behaviour."},{"t":"li","x":"Four ethical principles; moral intensity vs. moral sensitivity; supporting ethical behaviour."},{"t":"li","x":"Cross-cultural values: individualism, collectivism, power distance, uncertainty avoidance, achievement-nurturing."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 3 — Perception and Learning"},{"t":"li","x":"Self-concept: the 3 Cs (complexity, consistency, clarity); self-enhancement, self-verification, self-evaluation (self-esteem, self-efficacy, locus of control); social self/social identity theory."},{"t":"li","x":"The perceptual process: selective attention, perceptual organization, mental models."},{"t":"li","x":"Stereotyping (categorization, homogenization, differentiation); attribution theory (self-serving bias, fundamental attribution error); self-fulfilling prophecy; halo, false-consensus, primacy and recency effects."},{"t":"li","x":"Improving perceptions: awareness, self-awareness (Johari Window), meaningful interaction/contact hypothesis, empathy."},{"t":"li","x":"Global mindset."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 4 — Emotions, Attitudes and Stress"},{"t":"li","x":"Attitudes vs. emotions; the beliefs → feelings → behavioural intentions → behaviour model; cognitive dissonance."},{"t":"li","x":"Emotional labor (surface vs. deep acting); the Emotional Intelligence model (recognition/regulation x self/others)."},{"t":"li","x":"EVLN model of dissatisfaction; job satisfaction-performance link; the service profit chain; affective/continuance/normative commitment."},{"t":"li","x":"Stress definition, GAS; four major stressors (constraints, interpersonal conflict, overload, low task control); individual differences in stress; five stress-management strategies."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 5 — Motivation"},{"t":"li","x":"Four-drive theory; Maslow’s needs hierarchy (and its weaknesses/contributions); intrinsic vs. extrinsic motivation; learned needs theory (nAch, nAff, nPow)."},{"t":"li","x":"Expectancy theory (E-to-P, P-to-O, valence) and its practical levers."},{"t":"li","x":"OB Mod / A-B-Cs (positive/negative reinforcement, punishment, extinction); social cognitive theory (modeling, self-regulation)."},{"t":"li","x":"Effective goal setting and feedback; strengths-based coaching."},{"t":"li","x":"Organizational justice (distributive, procedural, interactional); equity theory and ways to correct perceived inequity."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 6 — Team Dynamics"},{"t":"li","x":"Definition of a team; informal groups; advantages/challenges of teams (incl. social loafing and remedies)."},{"t":"li","x":"Team effectiveness model: organisation/team environment, task interdependence types (pooled/sequential/reciprocal), team size, team composition/diversity."},{"t":"li","x":"Stages of team development (forming-storming-norming-performing-adjourning); team roles, norms, cohesion, trust (calculus/knowledge/identification-based, swift trust)."},{"t":"li","x":"Self-directed teams and remote teams — success factors."},{"t":"li","x":"Team decision-making constraints and remedies; creative structures (brainstorming, brainwriting, electronic brainstorming, nominal group technique)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 7 — Leadership"},{"t":"li","x":"Leadership definition; shared leadership."},{"t":"li","x":"Transformational leadership (4 elements) vs. charisma; managerial leadership vs. transformational; task- vs. people-oriented; servant leadership."},{"t":"li","x":"Path-goal theory (4 styles + contingencies); leadership substitutes theory."},{"t":"li","x":"Implicit leadership (prototypes + romance of leadership)."},{"t":"li","x":"8 personal attributes of effective leaders; authentic leadership; limitations of the attributes perspective."},{"t":"li","x":"Cultural and gender issues in leadership; Dark Triad and destructive leadership."},{"t":"li","x":"Steve Jobs case — link to transformational leadership, charisma, and leader attributes; official MCQ quiz content."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 8 — Power and Influence"},{"t":"li","x":"Definition of power; five sources (legitimate, reward, coercive, expert, referent)."},{"t":"li","x":"Four power contingencies (nonsubstitutability, centrality, visibility, discretion); consequences of power (empowerment vs. power-over)."},{"t":"li","x":"Social networks: social capital, strong/weak ties, network centrality (betweenness, degree, closeness), guanxi."},{"t":"li","x":"Eight influence tactics; contingencies of influence (soft vs. hard tactics)."},{"t":"li","x":"Organisational politics and how to minimize it."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 9 — Conflict and Negotiation"},{"t":"li","x":"Positive/negative outcomes of conflict; task vs. relationship conflict and how to minimize the latter."},{"t":"li","x":"The conflict process model; six structural sources of conflict."},{"t":"li","x":"Five conflict-handling styles and their contingencies; cultural/gender differences."},{"t":"li","x":"Six structural approaches to conflict management; three types of third-party intervention (inquisition, mediation, arbitration)."},{"t":"li","x":"Distributive vs. integrative negotiation; BATNA; the negotiation process (information, concessions, time/relationships); negotiation setting; gender and negotiation."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 10 — Culture and Organisational Change"},{"t":"li","x":"Elements of culture (the \"iceberg\"); organisational culture profile dimensions; subcultures/countercultures."},{"t":"li","x":"Culture artifacts (stories, language/rituals/ceremonies, physical structures); culture strength and its three functions; contingencies of culture strength."},{"t":"li","x":"Four strategies for merging cultures (assimilation, deculturation, integration, separation) and the bicultural audit."},{"t":"li","x":"Five strategies for changing/strengthening culture; ASA theory; organisational socialization (learning/adjustment, psychological contracts, RJPs, socialization agents)."},{"t":"li","x":"Lewin’s force field analysis; unfreezing-changing-refreezing; reasons people resist change; strategies for managing resistance. (Supplementary — verify against original Ch.15 slide.)"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Mixed Practice Exam Set (All Lessons)"},{"t":"p","x":"10 original questions spanning multiple lessons, in a style similar to the final exam (mix of MCQ, short-answer, and applied/case questions). Attempt all questions before checking the answer key."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: A manager assumes a new hire from a well-regarded university must automatically be an excellent performer, and rates his (still unobserved) work more favourably than it deserves. Which concept from Lesson 3 best explains this?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Fundamental attribution error"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Halo effect"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Equity theory"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Social loafing"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: Which combination of path-goal leadership styles (Lesson 7) is generally recommended for employees who are inexperienced and unskilled?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Achievement-oriented and participative"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Directive and supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Participative only"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Achievement-oriented only"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **MCQ: According to expectancy theory (Lesson 5), if an employee is fully confident her effort will lead to strong performance, and she knows strong performance is measured and rewarded, but she genuinely does not care about the specific reward on offer, her overall motivation will be:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Very high, since two of three components are strong"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Low, because the components multiply and a near-zero valence drags down the whole expectancy chain"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Unaffected by valence, since it only matters for extrinsic rewards"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Determined only by her locus of control"},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **MCQ: A team that has just resolved its early competition for roles and is now building consensus on objectives and a shared way of working, but is not yet performing at peak efficiency, is in which stage of team development (Lesson 6)?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Forming"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Storming"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Norming"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Performing"},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Short answer: A company wants to reduce dysfunctional relationship conflict on its cross-functional teams (Lesson 9) while still encouraging healthy task conflict. Name two concrete practices it could adopt, drawing on both the conflict lesson and the team dynamics lesson."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. **Short answer: Using both the power (Lesson 8) and culture (Lesson 10) lessons, explain how a strong organisational culture can itself function as a form of legitimate power over employees, even without any single manager exercising direct authority."},{"t":"p","x":"**7. **Short answer: A long-serving employee scores very high on organisational commitment, but exit interviews later reveal she stayed mainly \"because I’d lose my pension and couldn’t easily find another job at my age\" rather than because she loved the company. Which type of organisational commitment (Lesson 4) does this describe, and why does this type typically NOT predict strong performance or organisational citizenship behaviour?"},{"t":"p","x":"**8. **Applied scenario (integrates Lessons 4, 5 and 9): A high-performing employee has recently become irritable, is missing small details in his work, and has twice raised his voice at a colleague during a routine planning meeting — a level of task disagreement that used to stay perfectly professional between them. He mentions in passing that his workload has \"basically doubled\" since a teammate left three months ago and was never replaced. (a) Using stress theory, identify the most likely primary stressor. (b) Using motivation theory, explain one likely secondary effect on his motivation (pick one theory and justify). (c) Using conflict theory, classify what is now happening with his colleague and recommend one action to prevent it from escalating into full relationship conflict."},{"t":"p","x":"**9. **Applied scenario (integrates Lessons 6 and 7): A newly appointed team leader inherited a team that already had strong informal norms and high cohesion before she arrived, but those norms currently tolerate cutting corners on quality checks. Using team dynamics and leadership theory, recommend a specific leadership approach (naming a theory/style) and a specific team-level action to shift the team’s norms without destroying its existing cohesion."},{"t":"p","x":"**10. **Applied scenario (integrates Lessons 2, 3 and 8): A senior employee with high Machiavellian tendencies has built an extensive internal network and is skilled at persuasion and impression management, but is known to selectively share information (\"information control\") to keep colleagues dependent on him. (a) Name the specific power source(s) and influence tactic(s) most clearly at play. (b) Explain, using attribution theory, why colleagues might be slow to correctly identify what he is doing. (c) Suggest one structural/organisational response (not just \"tell him to stop\") that would reduce his ability to keep using this tactic."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Halo effect — one favourable trait/cue (a prestigious university) is being used to infer unrelated positive qualities (performance) without direct evidence; this is distinct from the fundamental attribution error, which concerns explaining someone’s behaviour by internal vs. external causes.\n**2. **b) Directive and supportive — directive leadership provides needed task guidance, and supportive leadership helps inexperienced/unskilled employees cope with the uncertainty of the unfamiliar task; achievement-oriented and participative styles fit skilled, internally-motivated employees instead.\n**3. **b) Low — because expectancy theory’s three components (E-to-P, P-to-O, valence) combine multiplicatively, not additively, a valence close to zero pulls overall motivation toward zero even when the other two beliefs are strong.\n**4. **c) Norming — competition for roles (a storming behaviour) has been resolved, and the team is now building shared consensus/mental models, but has not yet reached the smooth, high-trust efficiency of performing.\n**5. **Any two reasonable practices, e.g.: (1) Establish explicit psychological-safety team norms at formation (Lesson 6) that separate critiquing ideas from critiquing people, reinforced by the team leader modeling this distinction. (2) Use a structured third-party approach — e.g., a peer facilitator/mediator (Lesson 9) for recurring cross-functional disagreements, so disputes are processed through a process that controls the conversation (mediation) rather than escalating into personal attacks. (3) Build emotional intelligence and team development/mutual understanding early (e.g., a kickoff session where members share working styles), which the conflict lesson identifies as reducing relationship conflict during task conflict.\n**6. **A strong organisational culture functions like a form of legitimate/normative power because widely shared values and assumptions (Lesson 10) become an internalized \"automatic pilot\" that guides what employees believe is the appropriate way to behave — similar to how legitimate power works through people’s agreement that certain requests/expectations are appropriate to follow (Lesson 8), except here the \"authority\" is the collective culture and its artifacts (stories, rituals, language) rather than a specific person’s position. Because the control is largely self-imposed and reinforced through social identity (\"social glue\") and sense-making, it can be even more powerful and pervasive than a single manager’s direct legitimate or reward power, since employees are, in effect, both the source and the target of the influence.\n**7. **This is continuance commitment — a calculative attachment based on the (high) cost of leaving (losing her pension) and a perceived lack of good alternatives, rather than affective commitment (genuine emotional attachment) or normative commitment (felt moral obligation). Continuance commitment does not predict strong performance or OCB because the employee’s motivation to stay is not connected to caring about the organisation’s success — she is present because leaving is costly, not because she is emotionally invested in going \"above and beyond,\" so her discretionary effort and citizenship behaviour remain low even though her tenure/loyalty (in the narrow sense of \"stays a long time\") looks strong.\n**8. **(a) Primary stressor: work overload (his workload effectively doubled with no replacement hire) — this is one of the four major workplace stressors. (b) One acceptable answer: under expectancy theory, sustained overload likely erodes his effort-to-performance (E-to-P) expectancy — he may increasingly doubt that his effort can still produce the standard of work he used to deliver, given an unsustainable workload, which would reduce his overall motivation and could explain the missed details (alternatively, equity theory is also acceptable: he may perceive his input/outcome ratio has worsened — much higher inputs for the same outcomes/reward — creating inequity tension that shows up as reduced effort or irritability). (c) This looks like an early sign of relationship conflict developing out of what was previously professional task conflict (raised voice during a routine disagreement is a relationship-conflict warning sign, not just task disagreement). Recommended action: the manager should intervene early and privately — address the underlying stressor (redistribute workload or get approval to backfill the missing role) while also having a supportive, non-judgemental conversation with him about the recent friction, to prevent the (stress-driven) irritability from further damaging the working relationship with his colleague.\n**9. **Leadership approach: the situation calls for a directive element of path-goal leadership (clarifying that the quality-check standard is non-negotiable and setting clear expectations), rather than a purely supportive or laissez-faire style, since the existing team norm is actively counter-productive; this can be paired with elements of transformational leadership (clearly articulating why quality matters — connecting it to a larger shared purpose/vision the team can rally around, rather than simply issuing a rule). Team-level action: rather than disbanding the team (an extreme last resort) or ignoring the cohesion, the leader should explicitly and openly discuss the counter-productive norm with the team (a specific technique from the Lesson 6 norms content), invite the team’s own input on a better process, and introduce a team-based reward or recognition tied to quality outcomes — this leverages the team’s existing high cohesion in service of the new norm rather than fighting against it.\n**10. **(a) Power sources: expert power (network/persuasion skill) and especially legitimate/gatekept information control combined with referent power built through impression management; the influence tactics at play are clearly information control and impression management (and likely coalition-building, given his extensive network). (b) Attribution theory explanation: colleagues are likely committing something close to a fundamental attribution error in reverse — because he is skilled at impression management, they may attribute his helpfulness/charm to genuine positive character (an internal, dispositional attribution: \"he’s just a well-connected, helpful person\") rather than correctly attributing his selective information-sharing to a strategic, self-interested tactic; his persuasive skill actively shapes their perception, making the real (situational/strategic) cause harder to see. (c) Structural response: reduce his structural monopoly on information rather than relying on confronting him directly — e.g., implement shared documentation/knowledge-management systems so that critical information is not exclusively channeled through him, and formally distribute the information-brokering role across more than one person; this directly reduces his nonsubstitutability and discretion (two power contingencies from Lesson 8) without needing to rely on changing his personality."}]}}};
